    100: "אַגְמֹון (ʼagmôwn | ag-mone')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H98 אֲגַם ; a marshy pool (others from a different root, a kettle); by implication\nStrong's: a bulrush (as growing there); collectively a rope of bulrushes\nKJV: bulrush, caldron, hook, rush.\n\nCognate Group: H100 (bulrush), H98 (pond), H99 (pond)\nGreek Equivalents: G440 ἄνθραξ",
    101: "אַגָּן (ʼaggân | ag-gawn')\n\nDerivation: probably from H5059 נָגַן\nStrong's: a bowl (as pounded out hollow)\nKJV: basin, cup, goblet.\n\nCognate Group: H101 (basin)",
    102: "אַגָּף (ʼaggâph | ag-gawf')\n\nDerivation: probably from H5062 נָגַף (through the idea of impending); a cover or heap; i.e.\nStrong's: (only plural) wings of an army, or crowds of troops\nKJV: bands.\n\nCognate Group: H102 (bands)",
    103: "אָגַר (ʼâgar | aw-gar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to harvest\nKJV: gather.\n\nCognate Group: H103 (gather)",
    104: "אִגְּרָא (ʼiggᵉrâʼ | ig-er-aw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) of Persian origin\nStrong's: an epistle (as carried by a state courier or postman)\nKJV: letter.\n\nCognate Group: H104 (letter), H116 (now), H149 (diligently), H69 (stone), H147 (threshingfloor), H2 (father), H148 (judge), H144 (Adar)\nGreek Equivalents: G1992 ἐπιστολή, G29 ἀγγαρεύω",
    105: "אֲגַרְטָל (ʼăgarṭâl | ag-ar-tawl')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a basin\nKJV: charger.\n\nCognate Group: H105 (charger)",
    106: "אֶגְרֹף (ʼegrôph | eg-rofe')\n\nDerivation: from H1640 גָּרַף (in the sense of grasping)\nStrong's: the clenched hand\nKJV: fist.\n\nCognate Group: H4053 (clod), H1640 (sweep away), H106 (fist)\nGreek Equivalents: G4435 πυγμή",
    107: "אִגֶּרֶת (ʼiggereth | ig-eh'-reth)\n\nDerivation: feminine of H104 אִגְּרָא\nStrong's: an epistle\nKJV: letter.\n\nCognate Group: H107 (letter), H95 (piece silver), H94 (Agur)\nGreek Equivalents: G1992 ἐπιστολή",
    108: "אֵד (ʼêd | ade)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H181 אוּד (in the sense of enveloping)\nStrong's: a fog\nKJV: mist, vapor.\n\nCognate Group: H182 (cause), H343 (calamity), H181 (brand), H108 (mist)",
    109: "אָדַב (ʼâdab | aw-dab')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to languish\nKJV: grieve.\n\nCognate Group: H109 (grieve), H110 (Adbeel)",
    110: "אַדְבְּאֵל (ʼAdbᵉʼêl | ad-beh-ale')\n\nDerivation: probably from H109 אָדַב (in the sense of chastisement) and H410 אֵל ; disciplined of God\nStrong's: Adbeel, a son of Ishmael\nKJV: Adbeel.\n\nCognate Group: H109 (grieve), H110 (Adbeel)",
    111: "אֲדַד (ʼĂdad | ad-ad')\n\nDerivation: probably an orthographical variation for H2301 חֲדַד\nStrong's: Adad (or Hadad), an Edomite\nKJV: Hadad.\n\nCognate Group: H1906 (sounding again), H1908 (Hadad), H1959 (shout), H1909 (Hadadezer), H1928 (Hadarezer), H111 (Hadad), H1910 (Hadad-rimmon)",
    112: "אִדֹּו (ʼIddôw | id-do)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Iddo, an Israelite\nKJV: Iddo.\n\nCognate Group: H118 (Adalia), H112 (Iddo)",
