    654: "אֶפְלָל (ʼEphlâl | ef-lawl')\n\nDerivation: from H6419 פָּלַל ; judge\nStrong's: Ephlal, an Israelite\nKJV: Ephlal.\n\nCognate Group: H6415 (judgment), H6397 (Pelonite), H6414 (judge), H654 (Ephlal), H8605 (prayer), H6421 (Pelaliah), H6419 (intreat), H6417 (judgment), H6420 (Palal), H6416 (judge)",
    655: "אֹפֶן (ʼôphen | o'-fen)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to revolve\nStrong's: a turn, i.e. a season\nKJV: [phrase] fitly.\n\nCognate Group: H655 (fitly), H212 (wheel)\nGreek Equivalents: G718 ἁρμόζω",
    656: "אָפֵס (ʼâphêç | aw-face')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to disappear, i.e. cease\nKJV: be clean gone (at an end, brought to nought), fail.\n\nCognate Group: H656 (be clean gone), H6450 (Pas-dammim), H659 (of nought), H657 (ankle), H658 (Ephes-dammim)\nGreek Equivalents: G142 αἴρω, G609 ἀποκόπτω, G1587 ἐκλείπω",
    657: "אֶפֶס (ʼepheç | eh'-fes)\n\nDerivation: from H656 אָפֵס ; (sometimes like H6466 פָּעַל)\n\nStrong's: cessation, i.e. an end (especially of the earth); often used adverb, no further; also the ankle (in the dual), as being the extremity of the leg or foot\nKJV: ankle, but (only), end, howbeit, less than nothing, nevertheless (where), no, none (beside), not (any, -withstanding), thing of nought, save(-ing), there, uttermost part, want, without (cause).\n\nCognate Group: H656 (be clean gone), H6450 (Pas-dammim), H659 (of nought), H657 (ankle), H658 (Ephes-dammim)\nGreek Equivalents: G206 ἄκρον, G684 ἀπώλεια, G859 ἄφεσις, G970 βία, G2078 ἔσχατος, G2756 κενός, G3641 ὀλίγος, G3765 οὐκέτι, G4009 πέρας, G4133 πλήν",
    658: "אֶפֶס דַּמִּים (ʼEpheç Dammîym | eh'-fes dam-meem')\n\nDerivation: from H657 אֶפֶס and the plural of H1818 דָּם ; boundary of blood-drops\nStrong's: Ephes-Dammim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Ephes-dammim.\n\nCognate Group: H656 (be clean gone), H6450 (Pas-dammim), H659 (of nought), H657 (ankle), H658 (Ephes-dammim)",
    659: "אֵפַע (ʼêphaʻ | eh'-fah)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root probably meaning to breathe\nStrong's: properly, a breath, i.e. nothing\nKJV: of nought.\n\nCognate Group: H656 (be clean gone), H6450 (Pas-dammim), H659 (of nought), H657 (ankle), H658 (Ephes-dammim)",
    660: "אֶפְעֶה (ʼephʻeh | ef-eh')\n\nDerivation: from H659 אֵפַע (in the sense of hissing)\nStrong's: an asp or other venomous serpent\nKJV: viper.\n\nCognate Group: H6463 (cry), H660 (viper), H6464 (Pai)\nGreek Equivalents: G785 ἀσπίς, G935 βασιλεύς, G1795 ἐντυπόω, G3789 ὄφις",
    661: "אָפַף (ʼâphaph | aw-faf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to surround\nKJV: compass.\n\nCognate Group: H661 (compass)\nGreek Equivalents: G4023 περιέχω",
    662: "אָפַק (ʼâphaq | aw-fak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to contain, i.e. (reflex.) abstain\nKJV: force (oneself), restrain.\n\nCognate Group: H663 (Aphek), H650 (brook), H664 (Aphekah), H662 (force)\nGreek Equivalents: G430 ἀνέχομαι, G1467 ἐγκρατεύομαι",
    663: "אֲפֵק (ʼĂphêq | af-ake')\n\nDerivation: or אֲפִיק; from H662 אָפַק (in the sense of strength); fortress\nStrong's: Aphek (or Aphik), the name of three places in Palestine\nKJV: Aphek, Aphik.\n\nCognate Group: H663 (Aphek), H650 (brook), H664 (Aphekah), H662 (force)\nGreek Equivalents: G859 ἄφεσις, G2949 κῦμα, G4077 πηγή, G4125 πλευρά, G4138 πλήρωμα, G5327 φάραγξ, G5493 χείμαῤῥος",
    664: "אֲפֵקָה (ʼĂphêqâh | af-ay-kaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H663 אֲפֵק ; fortress\nStrong's: Aphekah, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Aphekah.\n\nCognate Group: H663 (Aphek), H650 (brook), H664 (Aphekah), H662 (force)",
    665: "אֵפֶר (ʼêpher | ay'-fer)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to bestrew\nStrong's: ashes\nKJV: ashes.\n\nCognate Group: H665 (ashes)\nGreek Equivalents: G2874 κοπρία, G4700 σποδός",
    666: "אֲפֵר (ʼăphêr | af-ayr')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H665 אֵפֶר (in the sense of covering)\nStrong's: a turban\nKJV: ashes.\n\nCognate Group: H668 (chariot), H3613 (Caleb-ephrathah), H672 (once perhaps for Ephraim; also of an Israelitish woman; Ephrath), H673 (Ephraimite), H669 (Ephraim), H666 (ashes)",
    667: "אֶפְרֹחַ (ʼephrôach | ef-ro'-akh)\n\nDerivation: from H6524 פָּרַח (in the sense of bursting the shell)\nStrong's: the brood of a bird\nKJV: young (one).\n\nCognate Group: H6524 ([idiom] abroad), H667 (young), H6526 (youth), H6525 (blossom)\nGreek Equivalents: G3502 νεοσσός, G3556 νοσσίον",
    668: "אַפִּרְיֹון (ʼappiryôwn | ap-pir-yone')\n\nDerivation: probably of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: a palanquin\nKJV: chariot.\n\nCognate Group: H668 (chariot), H3613 (Caleb-ephrathah), H672 (once perhaps for Ephraim; also of an Israelitish woman; Ephrath), H673 (Ephraimite), H669 (Ephraim), H666 (ashes)\nGreek Equivalents: G2930 κρύσταλλος",
    669: "אֶפְרַיִם (ʼEphrayim | ef-rah'-yim)\n\nDerivation: dual of masculine form of H672 אֶפְרָת ; double fruit\nStrong's: Ephrajim, a son of Joseph; also the tribe descended from him, and its territory\nKJV: Ephraim, Ephraimites.\n\nCognate Group: H668 (chariot), H3613 (Caleb-ephrathah), H672 (once perhaps for Ephraim; also of an Israelitish woman; Ephrath), H673 (Ephraimite), H669 (Ephraim), H666 (ashes)\nGreek Equivalents: G2187 Ἐφραίμ",
    670: "אֲפָרְסַי (ʼĂphârᵉçay | af-aw-re-sah'ee)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) of foreign origin (only in the plural)\nStrong's: an Apherasite or inhabitant of an unknown region of Assyria\nKJV: Apharsite.\n\nCognate Group: H711 (purple), H674 (revenue), H670 (Apharsite), H638 (also), H671 (Apharsachites)",
    671: "אֲפַרְסְכַי (ʼĂpharçᵉkay | af-ar-sek-ah'ee)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) or אֲפַרְסַתְכַי; (Aramaic), of foreign origin (only in the plural)\nStrong's: an Apharsekite or Apharsathkite, an unknown Assyrian tribe\nKJV: Apharsachites, Apharasthchites.\n\nCognate Group: H711 (purple), H674 (revenue), H670 (Apharsite), H638 (also), H671 (Apharsachites)",
    672: "אֶפְרָת (ʼEphrâth | ef-rawth')\n\nDerivation: or אֶפְרָתָה; from H6509 פָּרָה ; fruitfulness\nStrong's: Ephrath, another name for Bethlehem\nKJV: once (Psalm 132:6) perhaps for Ephraim; also of an Israelitish woman; Ephrath, Ephratah.\n\nCognate Group: H668 (chariot), H3613 (Caleb-ephrathah), H672 (once perhaps for Ephraim; also of an Israelitish woman; Ephrath), H673 (Ephraimite), H669 (Ephraim), H666 (ashes)",
    673: "אֶפְרָתִי (ʼEphrâthîy | ef-rawth-ee')\n\nDerivation: patrial from H672 אֶפְרָת\nStrong's: an Ephrathite or an Ephraimite\nKJV: Ephraimite, Ephrathite.\n\nCognate Group: H668 (chariot), H3613 (Caleb-ephrathah), H672 (once perhaps for Ephraim; also of an Israelitish woman; Ephrath), H673 (Ephraimite), H669 (Ephraim), H666 (ashes)",
    674: "אַפְּתֹם (ʼappᵉthôm | ap-pe-thome')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) of Persian origin\nStrong's: revenue; others at the last\nKJV: revenue.\n\nCognate Group: H711 (purple), H674 (revenue), H670 (Apharsite), H638 (also), H671 (Apharsachites)",
    675: "אֶצְבֹּון (ʼEtsbôwn | ets-bone')\n\nTypo: lemma אֶצְבֹּו missing final nun, corrected to אֶצְבֹּון\n\nDerivation: or אֶצְבֹּן; of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Etsbon, the name of two Israelites\nKJV: Ezbon.\n\nCognate Group: H4159 (miracle), H675 (Ezbon)",
    676: "אֶצְבַּע (ʼetsbaʻ | ets-bah')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6648 צֶבַע (in the sense of grasping)\nStrong's: something to sieze with, i.e. a finger; by analogy, a toe\nKJV: finger, toe.\n\nCognate Group: H676 (finger)\nGreek Equivalents: G1147 δάκτυλος",
    677: "אֶצְבַּע (ʼetsbaʻ | ets-bah')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H676 אֶצְבַּע \nStrong's: something to sieze with, i.e. a finger; by analogy, a toe\nKJV: finger, toe.\n\nCognate Group: H6676 (neck), H6655 (against), H6665 (righteousness), H6656 (true), H677 (finger)",
    678: "אָצִיל (ʼâtsîyl | aw-tseel')\n\nDerivation: from H680 אָצַל (in its secondary sense of separation)\nStrong's: an extremity (Isaiah 41:9), also a noble\nKJV: chief man, noble.\n\nCognate Group: H681 (at), H679 (hole), H680 (keep), H683 (Azaliah), H682 (Azal), H678 (chief man), H1018 (Beth-ezel)",
