    1606: "גְּעָרָה (gᵉʻârâh | gheh-aw-raw')\n\nDerivation: from H1605 גָּעַר\nStrong's: a chiding\nKJV: rebuke(-ing), reproof.\n\nCognate Group: H1606 (rebuke), H1605 (corrupt), H4045 (rebuke)\nGreek Equivalents: G547 ἀπειλή, G1650 ἔλεγχος",
    1607: "גָּעַשׁ (gâʻash | gaw-ash')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to agitate violently\nKJV: move, shake, toss, trouble.\n\nCognate Group: H1608 (Gaash), H1609 (Gatam), H1607 (move)\nGreek Equivalents: G4531 σαλεύω, G4682 σπαράσσω, G5015 ταράσσω",
    1608: "גַּעַשׁ (Gaʻash | ga'-ash)\n\nDerivation: from H1607 גָּעַשׁ ; a quaking\nStrong's: Gaash, a hill in Palestine\nKJV: Gaash.\n\nCognate Group: H1608 (Gaash), H1609 (Gatam), H1607 (move)",
    1609: "גַּעְתָּם (Gaʻtâm | gah-tawm')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Gatam, an Edomite\nKJV: Gatam.\n\nCognate Group: H1608 (Gaash), H1609 (Gatam), H1607 (move)",
    1610: "גַּף (gaph | gaf)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to arch\nStrong's: the back; by extension the body or self\nKJV: [phrase] highest places, himself.\n\nCognate Group: H1617 (Gera), H1610 (highest places), H1614 (brimstone), H1613 (gopher)\nGreek Equivalents: G3440 μόνον, G3441 μόνος",
    1611: "גַּף (gaph | gaf)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H1610 גַּף\nStrong's: a wing\nKJV: wing.\n\nCognate Group: H1611 (wing), H1596 (treasure), H1635 (bone), H1585 (perfect), H1655 (body)",
    1612: "גֶּפֶן (gephen | gheh'-fen)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to bend\nStrong's: a vine (as twining), especially the grape\nKJV: vine, tree.\n\nCognate Group: H1612 (vine)\nGreek Equivalents: G288 ἄμπελος, G290 ἀμπελών",
    1613: "גֹּפֶר (gôpher | go'-fer)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root, probably meaning to house in\nStrong's: a kind of tree or wood (as used for building), apparently the cypress\nKJV: gopher.\n\nCognate Group: H1617 (Gera), H1610 (highest places), H1614 (brimstone), H1613 (gopher)",
    1614: "גׇּפְרִית (gophrîyth | gof-reeth')\n\nDerivation: probably feminine of H1613 גֹּפֶר\nStrong's: properly, cypress-resin; by analogy, sulphur (as equally inflammable)\nKJV: brimstone.\n\nCognate Group: H1617 (Gera), H1610 (highest places), H1614 (brimstone), H1613 (gopher)\nGreek Equivalents: G2303 θεῖον",
    1615: "גִּר (gir | gheer)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from H3564 כּוּר\nStrong's: lime (from being burned in a kiln)\nKJV: chalk(-stone).\n\nCognate Group: H1529 (Geshan), H1615 (chalk)",
    1616: "גֵּר (gêr | gare)\n\nDerivation: or (fully) geyr (gare); from H1481 גּוּר\nStrong's: properly, a guest; by implication, a foreigner\nKJV: alien, sojourner, stranger.\n\nCognate Group: H1485 (Gur-baal), H3017 (Jagur), H1616 (alien), H1628 (habitation), H4460 (barn), H1483 (Gur), H4035 (barn), H4033 (dwelling), H1481 (abide)\nGreek Equivalents: G3581 ξένος, G3941 πάροικος, G4339 προσήλυτος",
    1617: "גֵּרָא (Gêrâʼ | gay-raw')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from H1626 גֵּרָה ; a grain\nStrong's: Gera, the name of six Israelites\nKJV: Gera.\n\nCognate Group: H1617 (Gera), H1610 (highest places), H1614 (brimstone), H1613 (gopher)",
    1618: "גָּרָב (gârâb | gaw-rawb')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to scratch\nStrong's: scurf (from itching)\nKJV: scab, scurvy.\n\nCognate Group: H1618 (scab), H1619 (Gareb), H1622 (Girgashite)",
    1619: "גָּרֵב (Gârêb | gaw-rabe')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H1618 גָּרָב ; scabby\nStrong's: Gareb, the name of an Israelite, also of a hill near Jerusalem\nKJV: Gareb.\n\nCognate Group: H1618 (scab), H1619 (Gareb), H1622 (Girgashite)",
    1620: "גַּרְגַּר (gargar | gar-gar')\n\nDerivation: by reduplication from H1641 גָּרַר\nStrong's: a berry (as if a pellet of rumination)\nKJV: berry.\n\nCognate Group: H1641 (catch), H1625 (cud), H4050 (axe), H1642 (Gerar), H1626 (gerah), H1621 (neck), H1620 (berry)",
    1621: "גַּרְגְּרֹות (gargᵉrôwth | gar-gher-owth')\n\nDerivation: feminine plural from H1641 גָּרַר\nStrong's: the throat (as used in rumination)\nKJV: neck.\n\nCognate Group: H1641 (catch), H1625 (cud), H4050 (axe), H1642 (Gerar), H1626 (gerah), H1621 (neck), H1620 (berry)\nGreek Equivalents: G5137 τράχηλος",
    1622: "גִּרְגָּשִׁי (Girgâshîy | ghir-gaw-shee')\n\nDerivation: patrial from an unused name (of uncertain derivation)\nStrong's: a Girgashite, one of the native tribes of Canaan\nKJV: Girgashite, Girgasite.\n\nCognate Group: H1618 (scab), H1619 (Gareb), H1622 (Girgashite)\nGreek Equivalents: G1086 Γεργεσηνός",
    1623: "גָּרַד (gârad | gaw-rad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to abrade\nKJV: scrape.\n\nCognate Group: H1623 (scrape)",
    1624: "גָּרָה (gârâh | gaw-raw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to grate, i.e. (figuratively) to anger\nKJV: contend, meddle, stir up, strive.\n\nCognate Group: H1624 (contend), H8409 (blow), H1627 ([idiom] aloud)\nGreek Equivalents: G436 ἀνθίστημι, G2042 ἐρεθίζω, G2051 ἐρίζω, G3736 ὀρύσσω, G3903 παρασκευάζω, G4171 πόλεμος",
    1625: "גֵּרָה (gêrâh | gay-raw')\n\nDerivation: from H1641 גָּרַר\nStrong's: the cud (as scraping the throat)\nKJV: cud.\n\nCognate Group: H1641 (catch), H1625 (cud), H4050 (axe), H1642 (Gerar), H1626 (gerah), H1621 (neck), H1620 (berry)",
    1626: "גֵּרָה (gêrâh | gay-raw')\n\nDerivation: from H1641 גָּרַר (as in H1625 גֵּרָה); properly, (like H1620 גַּרְגַּר) a kernel (round as if scraped), i.e.\nStrong's: a gerah or small weight (and coin)\nKJV: gerah.\n\nCognate Group: H1641 (catch), H1625 (cud), H4050 (axe), H1642 (Gerar), H1626 (gerah), H1621 (neck), H1620 (berry)",
    1627: "גָּרֹון (gârôwn | gaw-rone')\n\nDerivation: or (shortened) גָּרֹן; from H1641 גָּרַר ; (compare H1621 גַּרְגְּרֹות)\n\nStrong's: the throat (as roughened by swallowing)\nKJV: [idiom] aloud, mouth, neck, throat.\n\nCognate Group: H1624 (contend), H8409 (blow), H1627 ([idiom] aloud)\nGreek Equivalents: G2479 ἰσχύς, G2995 λάρυγξ, G5137 τράχηλος",
    1628: "גֵּרוּת (gêrûwth | gay-rooth')\n\nDerivation: from H1481 גּוּר\nStrong's: a (temporary) residence\nKJV: habitation.\n\nCognate Group: H1485 (Gur-baal), H3017 (Jagur), H1616 (alien), H1628 (habitation), H4460 (barn), H1483 (Gur), H4035 (barn), H4033 (dwelling), H1481 (abide)",
    1629: "גָּרַז (gâraz | gaw-raz')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to cut off\nKJV: cut off.\n\nCognate Group: H1631 (ax), H1630 (Gerizim), H1629 (cut off)\nGreek Equivalents: G641 ἀποῤῥίπτω",
    1630: "גְּרִזִים (Gᵉrizîym | gher-ee-zeem')\n\nDerivation: plural of an unused noun from H1629 גָּרַז (compare H1511 גִּזְרִי), cut up (i.e. rocky)\nStrong's: Gerizim, a mountain of Palestine\nKJV: Gerizim.\n\nCognate Group: H1631 (ax), H1630 (Gerizim), H1629 (cut off)",
    1631: "גַּרְזֶן (garzen | gar-zen')\n\nDerivation: from H1629 גָּרַז\nStrong's: an axe\nKJV: ax.\n\nCognate Group: H1631 (ax), H1630 (Gerizim), H1629 (cut off)\nGreek Equivalents: G513 ἀξίνη, G4604 σίδηρος",
    1632: "גָּרֹל (gârôl | gaw-role')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H1486 גֹּורָל\nStrong's: harsh\nKJV: man of great (as in the margin which reads H1419 גָּדֹול).\n\nCognate Group: H1486 (lot), H1632 (man of great)",
    1633: "גָּרַם (gâram | gaw-ram')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; to be spare or skeleton-like; used only as a denominative from H1634 גֶּרֶם\nStrong's: (causative) to bone, i.e. denude (by extensive, craunch) the bones\nKJV: gnaw the bones, break.\n\nCognate Group: H1633 (gnaw the bones), H1634 (bone), H1636 (Garmite)\nGreek Equivalents: G5275 ὑπολείπω",
    1634: "גֶּרֶם (gerem | gheh'-rem)\n\nDerivation: from H1633 גָּרַם\nStrong's: a bone (as the skeleton of the body); hence, self, i.e. (figuratively) very\nKJV: bone, strong, top.\n\nCognate Group: H1633 (gnaw the bones), H1634 (bone), H1636 (Garmite)\nGreek Equivalents: G3747 ὀστέον",
    1635: "גֶּרֶם (gerem | gheh'-rem)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H1634 גֶּרֶם\nStrong's: a bone\nKJV: bone.\n\nCognate Group: H1611 (wing), H1596 (treasure), H1635 (bone), H1585 (perfect), H1655 (body)",
    1636: "גַּרְמִי (Garmîy | gar-mee')\n\nDerivation: from H1634 גֶּרֶם\nStrong's: bony, i.e. strong\nKJV: Garmite.\n\nCognate Group: H1633 (gnaw the bones), H1634 (bone), H1636 (Garmite)",
    1637: "גֹּרֶן (gôren | go'-ren)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to smooth\nStrong's: a threshing-floor (as made even); by analogy, any open area\nKJV: (barn, corn, threshing-) floor, (threshing-, void) place.\n\nCognate Group: H1637 (floor)\nGreek Equivalents: G2149 εὐρύχωρος",
    1638: "גָּרַס (gâraç | gaw-ras')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to crush; also (intransitively and figuratively) to dissolve\nKJV: break.\n\nCognate Group: H1638 (break), H1643 (beaten corn)\nGreek Equivalents: G1544 ἐκβάλλω, G1971 ἐπιποθέω",
    1639: "גָּרַע (gâraʻ | gaw-rah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to scrape off; by implication, to shave, remove, lessen, withhold\nKJV: abate, clip, (di-) minish, do (take) away, keep back, restrain, make small, withdraw.\n\nCognate Group: H4052 (narrowed rest), H1639 (abate)\nGreek Equivalents: G620 ἀπολείπω, G650 ἀποστερέω, G683 ἀπωθέομαι, G851 ἀφαιρέω, G864 ἀφικνέομαι, G1808 ἐξαίρω, G1813 ἐξαλείφω, G3587 ξυράω, G4931 συντελέω, G5302 ὑστερέω",
    1640: "גָּרַף (gâraph | gaw-raf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to bear off violently\nKJV: sweep away.\n\nCognate Group: H4053 (clod), H1640 (sweep away), H106 (fist)\nGreek Equivalents: G1544 ἐκβάλλω",
    1641: "גָּרַר (gârar | gaw-rar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to drag off roughly; by implication, to bring up the cud (i.e. ruminate); by analogy, to saw\nKJV: catch, chew, [idiom] continuing, destroy, saw.\n\nCognate Group: H1641 (catch), H1625 (cud), H4050 (axe), H1642 (Gerar), H1626 (gerah), H1621 (neck), H1620 (berry)\nGreek Equivalents: G321 ἀνάγω, G1670 ἑλκύω, G4762 στρέφω",
    1642: "גְּרָר (Gᵉrâr | gher-awr')\n\nDerivation: probably from H1641 גָּרַר ; a rolling country\nStrong's: Gerar, a Philistine city\nKJV: Gerar.\n\nCognate Group: H1641 (catch), H1625 (cud), H4050 (axe), H1642 (Gerar), H1626 (gerah), H1621 (neck), H1620 (berry)",
    1643: "גֶּרֶשׂ (geres | gheh'-res)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to husk\nStrong's: a kernel (collectively), i.e. grain\nKJV: beaten corn.\n\nCognate Group: H1638 (break), H1643 (beaten corn)",
    1644: "גָּרַשׁ (gârash | gaw-rash')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to drive out from a possession; especially to expatriate or divorce\nKJV: cast up (out), divorced (woman), drive away (forth, out), expel, [idiom] surely put away, trouble, thrust out.\n\nCognate Group: H1647 (Gershom), H4054 (cast out), H1645 (put forth), H1646 (exaction), H1648 (Gershon), H1644 (cast up), H1649 (Gershonite)\nGreek Equivalents: G630 ἀπολύω, G641 ἀποῤῥίπτω, G683 ἀπωθέομαι, G853 ἀφανίζω, G1544 ἐκβάλλω, G1546 ἐκβολή, G1610 ἐκριζόω, G1808 ἐξαίρω, G1821 ἐξαποστέλλω, G2831 κλυδωνίζομαι, G3351 μετοικίζω",
    1645: "גֶּרֶשׁ (geresh | gheh'-resh)\n\nDerivation: from H1644 גָּרַשׁ\nStrong's: produce (as if expelled)\nKJV: put forth.\n\nCognate Group: H1647 (Gershom), H4054 (cast out), H1645 (put forth), H1646 (exaction), H1648 (Gershon), H1644 (cast up), H1649 (Gershonite)",
    1646: "גְרֻשָׁה (gᵉrushâh | gher-oo-shaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine passive participle of H1644 גָּרַשׁ\nStrong's: (abstractly) dispossession\nKJV: exaction.\n\nCognate Group: H1647 (Gershom), H4054 (cast out), H1645 (put forth), H1646 (exaction), H1648 (Gershon), H1644 (cast up), H1649 (Gershonite)",
    1647: "גֵּרְשֹׁם (Gêrᵉshôm | gay-resh-ome')\n\nDerivation: for H1648 גֵּרְשֹׁון\nStrong's: Gereshom, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Gershom.\n\nCognate Group: H1647 (Gershom), H4054 (cast out), H1645 (put forth), H1646 (exaction), H1648 (Gershon), H1644 (cast up), H1649 (Gershonite)",
    1648: "גֵּרְשֹׁון (Gêrᵉshôwn | gay-resh-one')\n\nDerivation: or גֵּרְשֹׁום; from H1644 גָּרַשׁ ; a refugee\nStrong's: Gereshon or Gereshom, an Israelite\nKJV: Gershon, Gershom.\n\nCognate Group: H1647 (Gershom), H4054 (cast out), H1645 (put forth), H1646 (exaction), H1648 (Gershon), H1644 (cast up), H1649 (Gershonite)",
    1649: "גֵּרְשֻׁנִּי (Gêrᵉshunnîy | gay-resh-oon-nee')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from H1648 גֵּרְשֹׁון\nStrong's: a Gereshonite or descendant of Gereshon\nKJV: Gershonite, sons of Gershon.\n\nCognate Group: H1647 (Gershom), H4054 (cast out), H1645 (put forth), H1646 (exaction), H1648 (Gershon), H1644 (cast up), H1649 (Gershonite)",
    1650: "גְּשׁוּר (Gᵉshûwr | ghesh-oor')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root (meaning to join); bridge\nStrong's: Geshur, a district of Syria\nKJV: Geshur, Geshurite.\n\nCognate Group: H1650 (Geshur), H1651 (Geshuri)",
    1651: "גְּשׁוּרִי (Gᵉshûwrîy | ghe-shoo-ree')\n\nDerivation: patrial from H1650 גְּשׁוּר\nStrong's: a Geshurite (also collectively) or inhabitants of Geshur\nKJV: Geshuri, Geshurites.\n\nCognate Group: H1650 (Geshur), H1651 (Geshuri)",
    1652: "גָּשַׁם (gâsham | gaw-sham')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to shower violently\nKJV: (cause to) rain.\n\nCognate Group: H1658 (Gispa), H1652 (rain), H1656 (rained upon), H1654 (Geshem), H1657 (Goshen), H1653 (rain)\nGreek Equivalents: G2597 καταβαίνω, G5205 ὑετός",
    1653: "גֶּשֶׁם (geshem | gheh'-shem)\n\nDerivation: from H1652 גָּשַׁם\nStrong's: a shower\nKJV: rain, shower.\n\nCognate Group: H1658 (Gispa), H1652 (rain), H1656 (rained upon), H1654 (Geshem), H1657 (Goshen), H1653 (rain)\nGreek Equivalents: G1028 βροχή, G5205 ὑετός, G5494 χειμών",
    1654: "גֶּשֶׁם (Geshem | gheh'-shem)\n\nDerivation: or (prolonged) גַּשְׁמוּ; the same as H1653 גֶּשֶׁם\nStrong's: Geshem or Gashmu, an Arabian\nKJV: Geshem, Gashmu.\n\nCognate Group: H1658 (Gispa), H1652 (rain), H1656 (rained upon), H1654 (Geshem), H1657 (Goshen), H1653 (rain)",
    1655: "גֶּשֶׁם (geshem | gheh'-shem)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) apparently the same as H1653 גֶּשֶׁם\nStrong's: used in a peculiar sense, the body (probably for the (figuratively) idea of a hard rain)\nKJV: body.\n\nCognate Group: H1611 (wing), H1596 (treasure), H1635 (bone), H1585 (perfect), H1655 (body)\nGreek Equivalents: G4983 σῶμα",
    1656: "גֹּשֶׁם (gôshem | go'-shem)\n\nDerivation: from H1652 גָּשַׁם ; equivalent to H1653 גֶּשֶׁם \nStrong's: a shower\nKJV: rained upon.\n\nCognate Group: H1658 (Gispa), H1652 (rain), H1656 (rained upon), H1654 (Geshem), H1657 (Goshen), H1653 (rain)",
    1657: "גֹּשֶׁן (Gôshen | go'-shen)\n\nDerivation: probably of Egyptian origin\nStrong's: Goshen, the residence of the Israelites in Egypt; also a place in Palestine\nKJV: Goshen.\n\nCognate Group: H1658 (Gispa), H1652 (rain), H1656 (rained upon), H1654 (Geshem), H1657 (Goshen), H1653 (rain)",
    1658: "גִּשְׁפָּא (Gishpâʼ | ghish-paw')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Gishpa, an Israelite\nKJV: Gispa.\n\nCognate Group: H1658 (Gispa), H1652 (rain), H1656 (rained upon), H1654 (Geshem), H1657 (Goshen), H1653 (rain)",
    1659: "גָּשַׁשׁ (gâshash | gaw-shash')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; apparently\nStrong's: to feel about\nKJV: grope.\n\nCognate Group: H1666 (Gether), H1659 (grope)\nGreek Equivalents: G5584 ψηλαφάω",
    1660: "גַּת (gath | gath)\n\nDerivation: probably from H5059 נָגַן (in the sense of treading out grapes)\nStrong's: a wine-press (or vat for holding the grapes in pressing them)\nKJV: (wine-) press (fat).\n\nCognate Group: H1667 (Gath-rimmon), H1665 (Gittith), H1662 (Gath-kephr), H1660 (press), H1663 (Gittite), H1661 (Gath), H1664 (Gittaim)\nGreek Equivalents: G1068 Γεθσημανῆ, G3025 ληνός",
    1661: "גַּת (Gath | gath)\n\nDerivation: the same as H1660 גַּת\nStrong's: Gath, a Philistine city\nKJV: Gath.\n\nCognate Group: H1667 (Gath-rimmon), H1665 (Gittith), H1662 (Gath-kephr), H1660 (press), H1663 (Gittite), H1661 (Gath), H1664 (Gittaim)",
    1662: "גַּת־הַחֵפֶר (Gath-ha-Chêpher | gath-hah-khay'-fer)\n\nDerivation: or (abridged) גִּתָּה־חֵפֶר; from H1660 גַּת and H2658 חָפַר with the article inserted; wine-press of (the) well\nStrong's: Gath-Chepher, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Gath-kephr, Gittah-kephr.\n\nCognate Group: H1667 (Gath-rimmon), H1665 (Gittith), H1662 (Gath-kephr), H1660 (press), H1663 (Gittite), H1661 (Gath), H1664 (Gittaim)",
