    2834: "חָשַׂף (châsaph | khaw-saf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to strip off, i.e. generally to make naked (for exertion or in disgrace), to drain away or bail up (a liquid)\nKJV: make bare, clean, discover, draw out, take, uncover.\n\nCognate Group: H4286 (made appear), H2834 (make bare), H2835 (little flock), H2817 (Hashupha)\nGreek Equivalents: G343 ἀνακαλύπτω, G601 ἀποκαλύπτω, G2045 ἐρευνάω, G2045 ἐρευνάω, G2694 κατασύρω",
    2835: "חָשִׂף (châsiph | khaw-seef')\n\nDerivation: from H2834 חָשַׂף\nStrong's: properly, drawn off, i.e. separated; hence, a small company (as divided from the rest)\nKJV: little flock.\n\nCognate Group: H4286 (made appear), H2834 (make bare), H2835 (little flock), H2817 (Hashupha)\nGreek Equivalents: G4168 ποίμνιον",
    2836: "חָשַׁק (châshaq | khaw-shak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; also interchangeable for H2820 חָשַׂךְ \nStrong's: to cling, i.e. join, (figuratively) to love, delight in; elliptically; to deliver\nKJV: have a delight, (have a) desire, fillet, long, set (in) love.\n\nCognate Group: H2838 (fillet), H2836 (have a delight), H2839 (felloe), H2837 (desire)\nGreek Equivalents: G138 αἱρέομαι, G1679 ἐλπίζω, G1760 ἐνθυμέομαι, G1937 ἐπιθυμέω, G4231 πραγματεύομαι, G4255 προαιρέομαι",
    2837: "חֵשֶׁק (chêsheq | khay'-shek)\n\nDerivation: from H2836 חָשַׁק\nStrong's: delight\nKJV: desire, pleasure.\n\nCognate Group: H2838 (fillet), H2836 (have a delight), H2839 (felloe), H2837 (desire)\nGreek Equivalents: G1939 ἐπιθυμία",
    2838: "חָשֻׁק (châshuq | khaw-shook')\n\nDerivation: or חָשׁוּק; past participle of H2836 חָשַׁק\nStrong's: attached, i.e. a fence-rail or rod connecting the posts or pillars\nKJV: fillet.\n\nCognate Group: H2838 (fillet), H2836 (have a delight), H2839 (felloe), H2837 (desire)",
    2839: "חִשֻּׁק (chishshuq | khish-shook')\n\nDerivation: from H2836 חָשַׁק\nStrong's: conjoined, i.e. a wheel-spoke or rod connecting the hub with the rim\nKJV: felloe.\n\nCognate Group: H2838 (fillet), H2836 (have a delight), H2839 (felloe), H2837 (desire)",
    2840: "חִשֻּׁר (chishshur | khish-shoor')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to bind together\nStrong's: combined, i.e. the nave or hub of awheel (as holding the spokes together)\nKJV: spoke.\n\nCognate Group: H2840 (spoke), H2841 (dark)",
    2841: "חַשְׁרָה (chashrâh | khash-raw')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H2840 חִשֻּׁר\nStrong's: properly, a combination or gathering, i.e. of watery clouds\nKJV: dark.\n\nCognate Group: H2840 (spoke), H2841 (dark)",
    2842: "חָשַׁשׁ (châshash | khaw-shash')\n\nDerivation: by variation for H7179 קַשׁ\nStrong's: dry grass\nKJV: chaff.\n\nCognate Group: H2845 (Heth), H2850 (Hittite), H2842 (chaff)",
    2843: "חֻשָׁתִי (Chushâthîy | khoo-shaw-thee')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from H2364 חוּשָׁה\nStrong's: a Chushathite or descendant of Chushah\nKJV: Hushathite.\n\nCognate Group: H2366 (Hushim), H2843 (Hushathite), H2364 (Hushah), H2365 (Hushai), H2363 (haste), H2367 (Husham)",
    2844: "חַת (chath | khath)\n\nDerivation: from H2865 חָתַת\nStrong's: concretely, crushed; also afraid; abstractly, terror\nKJV: broken, dismayed, dread, fear.\n\nCognate Group: H2847 (terror), H2865 (abolish), H2866 (casting down), H2867 (cathath), H2851 (terror), H4288 (destruction), H2844 (broken), H2849 (fear)\nGreek Equivalents: G770 ἀσθενέω, G5401 φόβος",
    2845: "חֵת (Chêth | khayth)\n\nDerivation: from H2865 חָתַת ; terror\nStrong's: Cheth, an aboriginal Canaanite\nKJV: Heth.\n\nCognate Group: H2845 (Heth), H2850 (Hittite), H2842 (chaff)",
    2846: "חָתָה (châthâh | khaw-thaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to lay hold of; especially to pick up fire\nKJV: heap, take (away).\n\nCognate Group: H4289 (censer), H3189 (Jahath), H2852 (determine), H4287 (Mahath), H2846 (heap)\nGreek Equivalents: G142 αἴρω, G4987 σωρεύω",
    2847: "חִתָּה (chittâh | khit-taw')\n\nDerivation: from H2865 חָתַת\nStrong's: fear\nKJV: terror.\n\nCognate Group: H2847 (terror), H2865 (abolish), H2866 (casting down), H2867 (cathath), H2851 (terror), H4288 (destruction), H2844 (broken), H2849 (fear)",
    2848: "חִתּוּל (chittûwl | khit-tool')\n\nDerivation: from H2853 חָתַל\nStrong's: swathed, i.e. a bandage\nKJV: roller.\n\nCognate Group: H2848 (roller), H2855 (Hethlon), H2853 ([idiom] at all), H2854 (swaddling band)",
    2849: "חַתְחַת (chathchath | khath-khath')\n\nDerivation: from H2844 חַת\nStrong's: terror\nKJV: fear.\n\nCognate Group: H2847 (terror), H2865 (abolish), H2866 (casting down), H2867 (cathath), H2851 (terror), H4288 (destruction), H2844 (broken), H2849 (fear)\nGreek Equivalents: G2285 θάμβος",
    2850: "חִתִּי (Chittîy | khit-tee')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from H2845 חֵת\nStrong's: a Chittite, or descendant of Cheth\nKJV: Hittite, Hittities.\n\nCognate Group: H2845 (Heth), H2850 (Hittite), H2842 (chaff)",
    2851: "חִתִּית (chittîyth | khit-teeth')\n\nDerivation: from H2865 חָתַת\nStrong's: fear\nKJV: terror.\n\nCognate Group: H2847 (terror), H2865 (abolish), H2866 (casting down), H2867 (cathath), H2851 (terror), H4288 (destruction), H2844 (broken), H2849 (fear)\nGreek Equivalents: G1629 ἐκφοβέω",
    2852: "חָתַךְ (châthak | khaw-thak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to cut off, i.e. (figuratively) to decree\nKJV: determine.\n\nCognate Group: H4289 (censer), H3189 (Jahath), H2852 (determine), H4287 (Mahath), H2846 (heap)\nGreek Equivalents: G4932 συντέμνω",
    2853: "חָתַל (châthal | khaw-thal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to swathe\nKJV: [idiom] at all, swaddle.\n\nCognate Group: H2848 (roller), H2855 (Hethlon), H2853 ([idiom] at all), H2854 (swaddling band)",
    2854: "חֲתֻלָּה (chăthullâh | khath-ool-law')\n\nDerivation: from H2853 חָתַל\nStrong's: a swathing cloth (figuratively)\nKJV: swaddling band.\n\nCognate Group: H2848 (roller), H2855 (Hethlon), H2853 ([idiom] at all), H2854 (swaddling band)",
    2855: "חֶתְלֹן (Chethlôn | kheth-lone')\n\nDerivation: from H2853 חָתַל ; enswathed\nStrong's: Chethlon, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Hethlon.\n\nCognate Group: H2848 (roller), H2855 (Hethlon), H2853 ([idiom] at all), H2854 (swaddling band)",
    2856: "חָתַם (châtham | khaw-tham')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to close up; especially to seal\nKJV: make an end, mark, seal (up), stop.\n\nCognate Group: H2368 (seal), H2369 (Hotham), H2856 (make an end), H2858 (signet)\nGreek Equivalents: G1863 ἐπάγω, G2696 κατασφραγίζω, G4921 συνιστάω, G4931 συντελέω, G4972 σφραγίζω",
    2857: "חֲתַם (chătham | khath-am')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) a root corresponding to H2856 חָתַם\nStrong's: to seal\nKJV: seal.\n\nCognate Group: H2857 (seal)",
    2858: "חֹתֶמֶת (chôthemeth | kho-the-meth)\n\nDerivation: feminine active participle of H2856 חָתַם\nStrong's: a seal\nKJV: signet.\n\nCognate Group: H2368 (seal), H2369 (Hotham), H2856 (make an end), H2858 (signet)",
    2859: "חָתַן (châthan | khaw-than')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to give (a daughter) away in marriage; hence (generally) to contract affinity by marriage\nKJV: join in affinity, father in law, make marriages, mother in law, son in law.\n\nCognate Group: H2861 (espousal), H2860 (bridegroom), H2859 (join in affinity)\nGreek Equivalents: G1918 ἐπιγαμβρεύω, G3994 πενθερά, G3995 πενθερός",
    2860: "חָתָן (châthân | khaw-thawn')\n\nDerivation: from H2859 חָתַן\nStrong's: a relative by marriage (especially through the bride); figuratively, a circumcised child (as a species of religious espousal)\nKJV: bridegroom, husband, son in law.\n\nCognate Group: H2861 (espousal), H2860 (bridegroom), H2859 (join in affinity)\nGreek Equivalents: G79 ἀδελφή, G1135 γυνή, G3566 νυμφίος",
    2861: "חֲתֻנָּה (chăthunnâh | khath-oon-naw')\n\nDerivation: from H2859 חָתַן\nStrong's: a wedding\nKJV: espousal.\n\nCognate Group: H2861 (espousal), H2860 (bridegroom), H2859 (join in affinity)",
    2862: "חָתַף (châthaph | khaw-thaf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to clutch\nKJV: take away.\n\nCognate Group: H2862 (take away), H2863 (prey)\nGreek Equivalents: G726 ἁρπάζω",
    2863: "חֶתֶף (chetheph | kheh'-thef)\n\nDerivation: from H2862 חָתַף\nStrong's: properly, rapine; figuratively, robbery\nKJV: prey.\n\nCognate Group: H2862 (take away), H2863 (prey)",
    2864: "חָתַר (châthar | khaw-thar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to force a passage, as by burglary; figuratively, with oars\nKJV: dig (through), row.\n\nCognate Group: H4290 (breaking up), H2864 (dig)\nGreek Equivalents: G1358 διορύσσω, G3736 ὀρύσσω, G3849 παραβιάζομαι",
    2865: "חָתַת (châthath | khaw-thath')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to prostrate; hence, to break down, either (literally) by violence, or (figuratively) by confusion and fear\nKJV: abolish, affright, be (make) afraid, amaze, beat down, discourage, (cause to) dismay, go down, scare, terrify.\n\nCognate Group: H2847 (terror), H2865 (abolish), H2866 (casting down), H2867 (cathath), H2851 (terror), H4288 (destruction), H2844 (broken), H2849 (fear)\nGreek Equivalents: G772 ἀσθενής, G1168 δειλιάω, G1587 ἐκλείπω, G1839 ἐξίστημι, G2274 ἡττάω, G2644 καταλλάσσω, G3860 παραδίδωμι, G3886 παραλύω, G4422 πτοέω, G4724 στέλλω, G4937 συντρίβω, G5399 φοβέω",
    2866: "חֲתַת (chăthath | khath-ath')\n\nDerivation: from H2865 חָתַת\nStrong's: dismay\nKJV: casting down.\n\nCognate Group: H2847 (terror), H2865 (abolish), H2866 (casting down), H2867 (cathath), H2851 (terror), H4288 (destruction), H2844 (broken), H2849 (fear)",
    2867: "חֲתַת (Chăthath | khath-ath')\n\nDerivation: the same as H2866 חֲתַת\nStrong's: Chathath, an Israelite\nKJV: cathath. f\n\nCognate Group: H2847 (terror), H2865 (abolish), H2866 (casting down), H2867 (cathath), H2851 (terror), H4288 (destruction), H2844 (broken), H2849 (fear)",
    2868: "טְאֵב (ṭᵉʼêb | teh-abe')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to rejoice\nKJV: be glad\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)",
    2869: "טָב (ṭâb | teh-abe')\n\nDerivation: the same as H2896 טֹוב\nStrong's: good\nKJV: fine, good.\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)\nGreek Equivalents: G18 ἀγαθός",
    2870: "טָבְאֵל (ṭâbᵉʼêl | taw-beh-ale')\n\nDerivation: from H2895 טֹוב and H410 אֵל ; pleasing (to) God\nStrong's: Tabeel, the name of a Syrian and of a Persian\nKJV: Tabeal, Tabeel.\n\nCognate Group: H4292 (besom), H2894 (sweep), H2870 (Tabeal)",
    2871: "טָבוּל (ṭâbûwl | taw-bool')\n\nDerivation: passive participle of H2881 טָבַל\nStrong's: properly, dyed, i.e. a turban (probably as of colored stuff)\nKJV: dyed attire.\n\nCognate Group: H2871 (dyed attire)",
    2872: "טַבּוּר (ṭabbûwr | tab-boor')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to pile up\nStrong's: properly, accumulated; i.e. (by implication) a summit\nKJV: middle, midst.\n\nCognate Group: H2887 (Tebeth), H2872 (middle), H2886 (Tabrimmon), H2888 (Tabbath)",
    2873: "טָבַח (ṭâbach | taw-bakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to slaughter (animals or men)\nKJV: kill, (make) slaughter, slay.\n\nCognate Group: H2879 (cook), H2878 (flesh), H2873 (kill), H2875 (Tebah), H4293 (slaughter), H2880 (Tibhath), H2874 ([idiom] beast), H2876 (cook)\nGreek Equivalents: G2380 θύω, G4967 σφαγή, G4969 σφάζω",
    2874: "טֶבַח (ṭebach | teh'-bakh)\n\nDerivation: from H2873 טָבַח\nStrong's: properly, something slaughtered; hence, a beast (or meat, as butchered); abstractly butchery (or concretely, a place of slaughter)\nKJV: [idiom] beast, slaughter, [idiom] slay, [idiom] sore.\n\nCognate Group: H2879 (cook), H2878 (flesh), H2873 (kill), H2875 (Tebah), H4293 (slaughter), H2880 (Tibhath), H2874 ([idiom] beast), H2876 (cook)\nGreek Equivalents: G4968 σφάγιον",
    2875: "טֶבַח (Ṭebach | teh'-bakh)\n\nDerivation: the same as H2874 טֶבַח ; massacre\nStrong's: Tebach, the name of a Mesopotamian and of an Israelite\nKJV: Tebah.\n\nCognate Group: H2879 (cook), H2878 (flesh), H2873 (kill), H2875 (Tebah), H4293 (slaughter), H2880 (Tibhath), H2874 ([idiom] beast), H2876 (cook)",
    2876: "טַבָּח (ṭabbâch | tab-bawkh')\n\nDerivation: from H2873 טָבַח\nStrong's: properly, a butcher; hence, a lifeguardsman (because he was acting as an executioner); also a cook (usually slaughtering the animal for food)\nKJV: cook, guard.\n\nCognate Group: H2879 (cook), H2878 (flesh), H2873 (kill), H2875 (Tebah), H4293 (slaughter), H2880 (Tibhath), H2874 ([idiom] beast), H2876 (cook)",
    2877: "טַבָּח (ṭabbâch | tab-bawkh')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) the same as H2876 טַבָּח\nStrong's: a lifeguardsman\nKJV: guard.\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)",
    2878: "טִבְחָה (ṭibchâh | tib-khaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H2874 טֶבַח and meaning the same\nStrong's: properly, something slaughtered; hence, a beast (or meat, as butchered); abstractly butchery (or concretely, a place of slaughter)\nKJV: flesh, slaughter.\n\nCognate Group: H2879 (cook), H2878 (flesh), H2873 (kill), H2875 (Tebah), H4293 (slaughter), H2880 (Tibhath), H2874 ([idiom] beast), H2876 (cook)",
    2879: "טַבָּחָה (ṭabbâchâh | tab-baw-khaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H2876 טַבָּח\nStrong's: a female cook\nKJV: cook.\n\nCognate Group: H2879 (cook), H2878 (flesh), H2873 (kill), H2875 (Tebah), H4293 (slaughter), H2880 (Tibhath), H2874 ([idiom] beast), H2876 (cook)",
    2880: "טִבְחַת (Ṭibchath | tib-khath')\n\nTypo: lemma טבְחַת missing vowel, corrected to טִבְחַת\n\nDerivation: from H2878 טִבְחָה ; slaughter\nStrong's: Tibchath, a place in Syria\nKJV: Tibhath.\n\nCognate Group: H2879 (cook), H2878 (flesh), H2873 (kill), H2875 (Tebah), H4293 (slaughter), H2880 (Tibhath), H2874 ([idiom] beast), H2876 (cook)",
    2881: "טָבַל (ṭâbal | taw-bal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to dip, to immerse\nKJV: dip, plunge.\n\nCognate Group: H2881 (dip), H2882 (Tebaliah)\nGreek Equivalents: G907 βαπτίζω, G911 βάπτω, G3435 μολύνω",
    2882: "טְבַלְיָהוּ (Ṭᵉbalyâhûw | teb-al-yaw'-hoo)\n\nDerivation: from H2881 טָבַל and H3050 יָהּ ; Jah has dipped\nStrong's: Tebaljah, an Israelite\nKJV: Tebaliah.\n\nCognate Group: H2881 (dip), H2882 (Tebaliah)",
    2883: "טָבַע (ṭâbaʻ | taw-bah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to sink\nKJV: drown, fasten, settle, sink.\n\nCognate Group: H2883 (drown), H2884 (Tabaoth), H2885 (ring)\nGreek Equivalents: G2647 καταλύω, G2666 καταπίνω, G2670 καταποντίζω, G4078 πήγνυμι",
    2884: "טַבָּעֹות (Ṭabbâʻôwth | tab-baw-othe')\n\nDerivation: plural of H2885 טַבַּעַת ; rings\nStrong's: Tabbaoth, one of the Nethinim\nKJV: Tabaoth.\n\nCognate Group: H2883 (drown), H2884 (Tabaoth), H2885 (ring)",
    2885: "טַבַּעַת (ṭabbaʻath | tab-bah'-ath)\n\nDerivation: from H2883 טָבַע\nStrong's: properly, a seal (as sunk into the wax), i.e. signet (for sealing); hence (generally) a ring of any kind\nKJV: ring.\n\nCognate Group: H2883 (drown), H2884 (Tabaoth), H2885 (ring)\nGreek Equivalents: G1146 δακτύλιος",
    2886: "טַבְרִמֹּון (Ṭabrimmôwn | tab-rim-mone')\n\nDerivation: from H2895 טֹוב and H7417 רִמֹּון ; pleasing (to) Rimmon\nStrong's: Tabrimmon, a Syrian\nKJV: Tabrimmon.\n\nCognate Group: H2887 (Tebeth), H2872 (middle), H2886 (Tabrimmon), H2888 (Tabbath)",
    2887: "טֵבֶת (Ṭêbeth | tay'-beth)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign derivation\nStrong's: Tebeth, the tenth Hebrew month\nKJV: Tebeth.\n\nCognate Group: H2887 (Tebeth), H2872 (middle), H2886 (Tabrimmon), H2888 (Tabbath)",
    2888: "טַבַּת (Ṭabbath | tab-bath')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Tabbath, a place East of the Jordan\nKJV: Tabbath.\n\nCognate Group: H2887 (Tebeth), H2872 (middle), H2886 (Tabrimmon), H2888 (Tabbath)",
    2889: "טָהֹור (ṭâhôwr | taw-hore')\n\nDerivation: or טָהֹר; from H2891 טָהֵר\nStrong's: pure (in a physical, chemical, ceremonial or moral sense)\nKJV: clean, fair, pure(-ness).\n\nCognate Group: H2890 (pureness), H2893 ([idiom] is cleansed), H2891 (be clean), H2892 (clearness), H2889 (clean)\nGreek Equivalents: G39 ἅγιον, G53 ἁγνός, G1342 δίκαιος, G1384 δόκιμος, G2513 καθαρός, G3741 ὅσιος",
    2890: "טְהֹור (ṭᵉhôwr | teh-hore')\n\nDerivation: from H2891 טָהֵר\nStrong's: purity\nKJV: pureness.\n\nCognate Group: H2890 (pureness), H2893 ([idiom] is cleansed), H2891 (be clean), H2892 (clearness), H2889 (clean)",
    2891: "טָהֵר (ṭâhêr | taw-hare')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; properly, to be bright; i.e. (by implication)\n\nStrong's: to be pure (physical sound, clear, unadulterated; Levitically, uncontaminated; morally, innocent or holy)\nKJV: be (make, make self, pronounce) clean, cleanse (self), purge, purify(-ier, self).\n\nCognate Group: H2890 (pureness), H2893 ([idiom] is cleansed), H2891 (be clean), H2892 (clearness), H2889 (clean)\nGreek Equivalents: G48 ἁγνίζω, G49 ἁγνισμός, G169 ἀκάθαρτος, G273 ἄμεμπτος, G1026 βρέχω, G2511 καθαρίζω, G2512 καθαρισμός, G2513 καθαρός",
    2892: "טֹהַר (ṭôhar | to'-har)\n\nDerivation: from H2891 טָהֵר\nStrong's: literally brightness; ceremonially purification\nKJV: clearness, glory, purifying.\n\nCognate Group: H2890 (pureness), H2893 ([idiom] is cleansed), H2891 (be clean), H2892 (clearness), H2889 (clean)\nGreek Equivalents: G2514 καθαρότης",
    2893: "טׇהֳרָה (ṭohŏrâh | toh-or-aw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H2892 טֹהַר\nStrong's: ceremonial purification; moral purity\nKJV: [idiom] is cleansed, cleansing, purification(-fying).\n\nCognate Group: H2890 (pureness), H2893 ([idiom] is cleansed), H2891 (be clean), H2892 (clearness), H2889 (clean)\nGreek Equivalents: G47 ἁγνεία, G2512 καθαρισμός",
    2894: "טוּא (ṭûwʼ | too)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to sweep away\nKJV: sweep.\n\nCognate Group: H4292 (besom), H2894 (sweep), H2870 (Tabeal)",
    2895: "טֹוב (ṭôwb | tobe)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root,\nStrong's: to be (transitively, do or make) good (or well) in the widest sense\nKJV: be (do) better, cheer, be (do, seem) good, (make) goodly, [idiom] please, (be, do, go, play) well.\n\nCognate Group: H2900 (Tobiah), H2897 (Tob), H2896 (beautiful), H2899 (Tob-adonijah), H2898 (fair), H2895 (be better)\nGreek Equivalents: G700 ἀρέσκω, G1013 βούλημα, G1380 δοκέω, G1653 ἐλεέω, G2573 καλῶς, G4851 συμφέρω",
    2896: "טֹוב (ṭôwb | tobe)\n\nDerivation: from H2895 טֹוב\nStrong's: good (as an adjective) in the widest sense; used likewise as a noun, both in the masculine and the feminine, the singular and the plural (good, a good or good thing, a good man or woman; the good, goods or good things, good men or women), also as an adverb (well)\nKJV: beautiful, best, better, bountiful, cheerful, at ease, [idiom] fair (word), (be in) favour, fine, glad, good (deed, -lier, -liest, -ly, -ness, -s), graciously, joyful, kindly, kindness, liketh (best), loving, merry, [idiom] most, pleasant, [phrase] pleaseth, pleasure, precious, prosperity, ready, sweet, wealth, welfare, (be) well(-favoured).\n\nCognate Group: H2900 (Tobiah), H2897 (Tob), H2896 (beautiful), H2899 (Tob-adonijah), H2898 (fair), H2895 (be better)\nGreek Equivalents: G18 ἀγαθός, G19 ἀγαθωσύνη, G19 ἀγαθωσύνη, G39 ἅγιον, G227 ἀληθής, G228 ἀληθινός, G701 ἀρεστός, G791 ἀστεῖος, G957 βελτίον, G1343 δικαιοσύνη, G1588 ἐκλεκτός, G2234 ἡδέως, G2908 κρεῖσσον, G2909 κρείττων, G4096 πιότης, G5485 χάρις, G5543 χρηστός, G5544 χρηστότης, G5611 ὡραῖος",
    2897: "טֹוב (Ṭôwb | tobe)\n\nDerivation: the same as H2896 טֹוב ; good\nStrong's: Tob, a region apparently East of the Jordan\nKJV: Tob.\n\nCognate Group: H2900 (Tobiah), H2897 (Tob), H2896 (beautiful), H2899 (Tob-adonijah), H2898 (fair), H2895 (be better)",
    2898: "טוּב (ṭûwb | toob)\n\nDerivation: from H2895 טֹוב\nStrong's: good (as a noun), in the widest sense, especially goodness (superlative concretely, the best), beauty, gladness, welfare\nKJV: fair, gladness, good(-ness, thing, -s), joy, go well with.\n\nCognate Group: H2900 (Tobiah), H2897 (Tob), H2896 (beautiful), H2899 (Tob-adonijah), H2898 (fair), H2895 (be better)\nGreek Equivalents: G19 ἀγαθωσύνη, G1391 δόξα, G5544 χρηστότης",
    2899: "טֹוב אֲדֹנִיָּהוּ (Ṭôwb ʼĂdônîyâhûw | tobe ado-nee-yah'-hoo)\n\nTypo: xlit Ădônîyâhûw corrected to ʼĂdônîyâhûw\n\nDerivation: from H2896 טֹוב and H138 אֲדֹנִיָּה ; pleasing (to) Adonijah\nStrong's: Tob-Adonijah, an Israelite\nKJV: Tob-adonijah.\n\nCognate Group: H2900 (Tobiah), H2897 (Tob), H2896 (beautiful), H2899 (Tob-adonijah), H2898 (fair), H2895 (be better)",
    2900: "טֹובִיָּה (Ṭôwbîyâh | to-bee-yaw')\n\nDerivation: or טֹובִיָּהוּ; from H2896 טֹוב and H3050 יָהּ ; goodness of Jehovah\nStrong's: Tobijah, the name of three Israelites and of one Samaritan\nKJV: Tobiah, Tobijah.\n\nCognate Group: H2900 (Tobiah), H2897 (Tob), H2896 (beautiful), H2899 (Tob-adonijah), H2898 (fair), H2895 (be better)",
    2901: "טָוָה (ṭâvâh | taw-vaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to spin\nKJV: spin.\n\nCognate Group: H4299 (spun), H2901 (spin)\nGreek Equivalents: G3514 νήθω",
    2902: "טוּחַ (ṭûwach | too'-akh)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to smear, especially with lime\nKJV: daub, overlay, plaister, smut.\n\nCognate Group: H2910 (inward parts), H2902 (daub), H2915 (daubing)\nGreek Equivalents: G1813 ἐξαλείφω",
    2903: "טֹופָפָה (ṭôwphâphâh | to-faw-faw')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to go around or bind\nStrong's: a fillet for the forehead\nKJV: frontlet.\n\nCognate Group: H2903 (frontlet)\nGreek Equivalents: G761 ἀσάλευτος",
    2904: "טוּל (ṭûwl | tool)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to pitch over or reel; hence (transitively) to cast down or out\nKJV: carry away, (utterly) cast (down, forth, out), send out.\n\nCognate Group: H2904 (carry away), H2925 (captivity)\nGreek Equivalents: G142 αἴρω, G641 ἀποῤῥίπτω, G906 βάλλω, G1544 ἐκβάλλω, G1546 ἐκβολή, G1685 ἐμβάλλω, G1825 ἐξεγείρω, G1869 ἐπαίρω, G1904 ἐπέρχομαι, G2296 θαυμάζω, G3860 παραδίδωμι",
    2905: "טוּר (ṭûwr | toor)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to range in a regular manner\nStrong's: a row; hence, a wall\nKJV: row.\n\nCognate Group: H2905 (row), H3195 (Jetur), H2918 (castle)\nGreek Equivalents: G1085 γένος, G3735 ὄρος",
    2906: "טוּר (ṭûwr | toor)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6697 צוּר\nStrong's: a rock or hill\nKJV: mountain.\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)",
    2907: "טוּשׂ (ṭûws | toos)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to pounce as a bird of prey\nKJV: haste.\n\nCognate Group: H2907 (haste)\nGreek Equivalents: G4072 πέτομαι",
    2908: "טְוָת (ṭᵉvâth | tev-awth')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from a root corresponding to H2901 טָוָה\nStrong's: hunger (as twisting)\nKJV: fasting.\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)\nGreek Equivalents: G2218 ζυγός, G3523 νῆστις",
    2909: "טָחָה (ṭâchâh | taw-khaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to stretch a bow, as an archer\nKJV: (bow-) shot.\n\nCognate Group: H2909 (shot)\nGreek Equivalents: G1000 βολή",
    2910: "טֻחָה (ṭuchâh | too-khaw')Typo: xlit ṭuwchâh corrected to ṭuchâh\n\nDerivation: from H2909 טָחָה (or H2902 טוּחַ) in the sense of overlaying\nStrong's: (in the plural only) the kidneys (as being covered); hence (figuratively) the inmost thought\nKJV: inward parts.\n\nCognate Group: H2910 (inward parts), H2902 (daub), H2915 (daubing)",
    2911: "טְחֹון (ṭᵉchôwn | tekh-one')\n\nDerivation: from H2912 טָחַן\nStrong's: a hand mill; hence, a millstone\nKJV: to grind.\n\nCognate Group: H2912 (grind), H2902 (daub), H2913 (grinding), H2911 (to grind)",
    2912: "טָחַן (ṭâchan | taw-khan')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to grind meal; hence, to be aconcubine (that being their employment)\nKJV: grind(-er).\n\nCognate Group: H2912 (grind), H2902 (daub), H2913 (grinding), H2911 (to grind)",
    2913: "טַחֲנָה (ṭachănâh | takh-an-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H2912 טָחַן\nStrong's: a hand mill; hence (figuratively) chewing\nKJV: grinding.\n\nCognate Group: H2912 (grind), H2902 (daub), H2913 (grinding), H2911 (to grind)",
    2914: "טְחֹר (ṭᵉchôr | tekh-ore')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to burn\nStrong's: a boil or ulcer (from the inflammation), especially a tumorin the anus or pudenda (the piles)\nKJV: emerod.\n\nCognate Group: H2914 (emerod)\nGreek Equivalents: G3491 ναῦς",
    2915: "טִיחַ (ṭîyach | tee'akh)\n\nDerivation: from (the equiv. of) H2902 טוּחַ\nStrong's: mortar or plaster\nKJV: daubing.\n\nCognate Group: H2910 (inward parts), H2902 (daub), H2915 (daubing)",
    2916: "טִיט (ṭîyṭ | teet)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning apparently to be sticky (rather perb. a demonstrative); from H2894 טוּא, through the idea of dirt to be swept away)\nStrong's: mud or clay; figuratively, calamity\nKJV: clay, dirt, mire.\n\nCognate Group: H2916 (clay)\nGreek Equivalents: G1004 βόρβορος, G4081 πηλός",
    2917: "טִין (ṭîyn | teen)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) perhaps, by interchange, for a word corresponding to H2916 טִיט\nStrong's: clay\nKJV: miry.\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)",
    2918: "טִירָה (ṭîyrâh | tee-raw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of (an equivalent to) H2905 טוּר\nStrong's: a wall; hence, a fortress or a hamlet\nKJV: (goodly) castle, habitation, palace, row.\n\nCognate Group: H2905 (row), H3195 (Jetur), H2918 (castle)\nGreek Equivalents: G1886 ἔπαυλις, G2968 κώμη",
    2919: "טַל (ṭal | tal)\n\nDerivation: from H2926 טָלַל\nStrong's: dew (as covering vegetation)\nKJV: dew.\n\nCognate Group: H2919 (dew)",
    2920: "טַל (ṭal | tal)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) the same as H2919 טַל \nStrong's: dew (as covering vegetation)\nKJV: dew.\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)\nGreek Equivalents: G2386 ἴαμα",
    2921: "טָלָא (ṭâlâʼ | taw-law')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to cover with pieces; i.e. (by implication) to spot or variegate (as tapestry)\nKJV: clouted, with divers colours, spotted.\n\nCognate Group: H2923 (Telaim), H2921 (clouted)",
    2922: "טְלָא (ṭᵉlâʼ | tel-aw')\n\nDerivation: apparently from H2921 טָלָא in the (orig.) sense of covering (for protection); (compare H2924 טָלֶה)\n\nStrong's: a lamb\nKJV: lamb.\n\nCognate Group: H2922 (lamb), H2924 (lamb)",
    2923: "טְלָאִים (Ṭᵉlâʼîym | tel-aw-eem')\n\nDerivation: from the plural of H2922 טְלָא ; lambs\nStrong's: Telaim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Telaim.\n\nCognate Group: H2923 (Telaim), H2921 (clouted)",
    2924: "טָלֶה (ṭâleh | taw-leh')\n\nDerivation: by variation for H2922 טְלָא\nStrong's: a lamb\nKJV: lamb.\n\nCognate Group: H2922 (lamb), H2924 (lamb)\nGreek Equivalents: G5008 ταλιθά",
    2925: "טַלְטֵלָה (ṭalṭêlâh | tal-tay-law')\n\nDerivation: from H2904 טוּל\nStrong's: overthrow or rejection\nKJV: captivity.\n\nCognate Group: H2904 (carry away), H2925 (captivity)",
    2926: "טָלַל (ṭâlal | taw-lal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to strew over, i.e. (by implication) to cover in or plate (with beams)\nKJV: cover.\n\nCognate Group: H2926 (cover)\nGreek Equivalents: G538 ἀπατάω, G1818 ἐξαπατάω, G3884 παραλογίζομαι, G4105 πλανάω",
    2927: "טְלַל (ṭᵉlal | tel-al')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H2926 טָלַל\nStrong's: to cover with shade\nKJV: have a shadow.\n\nCognate Group: H2877 (guard), H2920 (dew), H2869 (fine), H2908 (fasting), H2906 (mountain), H2927 (have a shadow), H2868 (be glad), H2917 (miry)\nGreek Equivalents: G2681 κατασκηνόω",
    2928: "טֶלֶם (Ṭelem | teh'-lem)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to break up or treat violently; oppression\nStrong's: Telem, the name of a place in Idumaea, also of a temple doorkeeper\nKJV: Telem.\n\nCognate Group: H2929 (Talmon), H2928 (Telem)",
    2929: "טַלְמֹון (Ṭalmôwn | tal-mone')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H2728 חָרְגֹּל ; oppressive\nStrong's: Talmon, a temple doorkeeper\nKJV: Talmon.\n\nCognate Group: H2929 (Talmon), H2928 (Telem)",
    2930: "טָמֵא (ṭâmêʼ | taw-may')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be foul, especially in a ceremial or moral sense (contaminated)\nKJV: defile (self), pollute (self), be (make, make self, pronounce) unclean, [idiom] utterly.\n\nCognate Group: H2932 (filthiness), H2930 (defile), H2931 (defiled)\nGreek Equivalents: G1808 ἐξαίρω, G3392 μιαίνω",
    2931: "טָמֵא (ṭâmêʼ | taw-may')\n\nDerivation: from H2930 טָמֵא\nStrong's: foul in a religious sense\nKJV: defiled, [phrase] infamous, polluted(-tion), unclean.\n\nCognate Group: H2932 (filthiness), H2930 (defile), H2931 (defiled)\nGreek Equivalents: G167 ἀκαθαρσία, G169 ἀκάθαρτος, G4509 ῥύπος, G5090 Τιμαῖος, G924 Βαρτιμαῖος",
    2932: "טֻמְאָה (ṭumʼâh | toom-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H2930 טָמֵא\nStrong's: religious impurity\nKJV: filthiness, unclean(-ness).\n\nCognate Group: H2932 (filthiness), H2930 (defile), H2931 (defiled)\nGreek Equivalents: G167 ἀκαθαρσία, G266 ἁμαρτία",
    2933: "טָמָה (ṭâmâh | taw-maw')\n\nDerivation: a collateral form of H2930 טָמֵא\nStrong's: to be impure in a religious sense\nKJV: be defiled, be reputed vile.\n\nCognate Group: H2933 (be defiled)",
    2934: "טָמַן (ṭâman | taw-man')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to hide (by covering over)\nKJV: hide, lay privily, in secret.\n\nCognate Group: H4301 (hidden riches), H2934 (hide)\nGreek Equivalents: G1470 ἐγκρύπτω, G1607 ἐκπορεύομαι, G2928 κρύπτω, G5278 ὑπομένω",
    2935: "טֶנֶא (ṭeneʼ | teh'-neh)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root probably meaning to weave\nStrong's: a basket (of interlaced osiers)\nKJV: basket.\n\nCognate Group: H2935 (basket)\nGreek Equivalents: G596 ἀποθήκη",
    2936: "טָנַף (ṭânaph | taw-naf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to soil\nKJV: defile.\n\nCognate Group: H2936 (defile)\nGreek Equivalents: G3435 μολύνω",
    2937: "טָעָה (ṭâʻâh | taw-aw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to wander; causatively to lead astray\nKJV: seduce.\n\nCognate Group: H2937 (seduce)",
    2938: "טָעַם (ṭâʻam | taw-am')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to taste; figuratively, to perceive\nKJV: [idiom] but, perceive, taste.\n\nCognate Group: H4303 (dainty), H2938 ([idiom] but), H2940 (advice)\nGreek Equivalents: G1089 γεύομαι",
    2939: "טְעַם (ṭᵉʻam | teh-am')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H2938 טָעַם\nStrong's: to taste; causatively to feed\nKJV: make to eat, feed.\n\nCognate Group: H2953 (nail), H2941 (account), H2939 (make to eat), H2942 (chancellor)",
    2940: "טַעַם (ṭaʻam | tah'-am)\n\nDerivation: from H2938 טָעַם\nStrong's: properly, a taste, i.e. (figuratively) perception; by implication, intelligence; transitively, a mandate\nKJV: advice, behaviour, decree, discretion, judgment, reason, taste, understanding.\n\nCognate Group: H4303 (dainty), H2938 ([idiom] but), H2940 (advice)\nGreek Equivalents: G1106 γνώμη, G2237 ἡδονή, G3056 λόγος, G3809 παιδεία, G4907 σύνεσις, G5158 τρόπος",
    2941: "טַעַם (ṭaʻam | tah'-am)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from H2939 טְעַם ; compare H2940 טַעַם)\n\nStrong's: properly, a taste, i.e. ajudicial sentence\nKJV: account, [idiom] to be commanded, commandment, matter.\n\nCognate Group: H2953 (nail), H2941 (account), H2939 (make to eat), H2942 (chancellor)",
    2942: "טְעֵם (ṭᵉʻêm | teh-ame')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from H2939 טְעַם, and equivalent to H2941 טַעַם\nStrong's: properly, flavor; figuratively, judgment (both subjective and objective); hence, account (both subjectively and objectively)\nKJV: [phrase] chancellor, [phrase] command, commandment, decree, [phrase] regard, taste, wisdom.\n\nCognate Group: H2953 (nail), H2941 (account), H2939 (make to eat), H2942 (chancellor)\nGreek Equivalents: G1106 γνώμη, G1378 δόγμα, G1785 ἐντολή",
    2943: "טָעַן (ṭâʻan | taw-an')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to load a beast\nKJV: lade.\n\nCognate Group: H2943 (lade)\nGreek Equivalents: G1072 γεμίζω",
    2944: "טָעַן (ṭâʻan | taw-an')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to stab\nKJV: thrust through.\n\nCognate Group: H2944 (thrust through)",
    2945: "טַף (ṭaph | taf)\n\nDerivation: from H2952 טָפַף (perhaps referring to the tripping gait of children)\nStrong's: a family (mostly used collectively in the singular)\nKJV: (little) children (ones), families.\n\nCognate Group: H2952 (mince), H2945 (children), H2955 (Taphath), H2954 (be fat)\nGreek Equivalents: G1549 ἔκγονον, G2992 λαός, G3516 νήπιος, G3793 ὄχλος, G3813 παιδίον, G4772 συγγένεια, G4983 σῶμα, G5043 τέκνον",
    2946: "טָפַח (ṭâphach | taw-fakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; or perhaps a denominative from H2947 טֵפַח, from dandling on the palms\nStrong's: to flatten out or extend (as a tent); figuratively, to nurse achild (as promotive of growth)\nKJV: span, swaddle.\n\nCognate Group: H2949 (span long), H2946 (span), H2948 (hand-breadth), H4304 (vail), H2947 (coping)\nGreek Equivalents: G4732 στερεόω",
    2947: "טֵפַח (ṭêphach | tay'-fakh)\n\nDerivation: from H2946 טָפַח\nStrong's: a spread of the hand, i.e. a palm-breadth (not 'span' of the fingers); architecturally, a corbel (as a supporting palm)\nKJV: coping, hand-breadth.\n\nCognate Group: H2949 (span long), H2946 (span), H2948 (hand-breadth), H4304 (vail), H2947 (coping)",
    2948: "טֹפַח (ṭôphach | to'-fakh)\n\nDerivation: from H2946 טָפַח (the same as H2947 טֵפַח)\n\nStrong's: a spread of the hand, i.e. a palm-breadth (not 'span' of the fingers); architecturally, a corbel (as a supporting palm)\nKJV: hand-breadth (broad).\n\nCognate Group: H2949 (span long), H2946 (span), H2948 (hand-breadth), H4304 (vail), H2947 (coping)",
    2949: "טִפֻּח (ṭippuch | tip-pookh')\n\nDerivation: from H2946 טָפַח\nStrong's: nursing\nKJV: span long.\n\nCognate Group: H2949 (span long), H2946 (span), H2948 (hand-breadth), H4304 (vail), H2947 (coping)\nGreek Equivalents: G2337 θηλάζω",
    2950: "טָפַל (ṭâphal | taw-fal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to stick on as apatch; figuratively, to impute falsely\nKJV: forge(-r), sew up.\n\nCognate Group: H2950 (forge), H2951 (captain)",
    2951: "טִפְסַר (ṭiphçar | tif-sar')\n\nDerivation: of foreign derivation\nStrong's: a military governor\nKJV: captain.\n\nCognate Group: H2950 (forge), H2951 (captain)",
    2952: "טָפַף (ṭâphaph | taw-faf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; apparently\nStrong's: to trip (with short steps) coquettishly\nKJV: mince.\n\nCognate Group: H2952 (mince), H2945 (children), H2955 (Taphath), H2954 (be fat)",
    2953: "טְפַר (ṭᵉphar | tef-ar')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from a root corresponding to H6852 צָפַר, and meaning the same as H6856 צִפֹּרֶן\nStrong's: a finger-nail; also a hoof or claw\nKJV: nail.\n\nCognate Group: H2953 (nail), H2941 (account), H2939 (make to eat), H2942 (chancellor)",
    2954: "טָפַשׁ (ṭâphash | taw-fash')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, apparently to be thick; figuratively, to be stupid\nKJV: be fat.\n\nCognate Group: H2952 (mince), H2945 (children), H2955 (Taphath), H2954 (be fat)",
    2955: "טָפַת (Ṭâphath | taw-fath')\n\nDerivation: probably from H5197 נָטַף ; a dropping (of ointment)\nStrong's: Taphath, an Israelitess\nKJV: Taphath.\n\nCognate Group: H2952 (mince), H2945 (children), H2955 (Taphath), H2954 (be fat)",
    2956: "טָרַד (ṭârad | taw-rad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to drive on; figuratively, to follow close\nKJV: continual.\n\nCognate Group: H4308 (Matred), H2956 (continual)\nGreek Equivalents: G1544 ἐκβάλλω",
    2957: "טְרַד (ṭᵉrad | ter-ad')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H2956 טָרַד\nStrong's: to expel\nKJV: drive.\n\nCognate Group: H2967 (Tarpelites), H2957 (drive)\nGreek Equivalents: G520 ἀπάγω, G1559 ἐκδιώκω",
    2958: "טְרֹום (ṭᵉrôwm | ter-ome')\n\nDerivation: a variation of H2962 טֶרֶם\nStrong's: not yet\nKJV: before.\n\nCognate Group: H2958 (before), H2962 (before)",
    2959: "טָרַח (ṭârach | taw-rakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to overburden\nKJV: weary.\n\nCognate Group: H2960 (cumbrance), H2959 (weary)",
    2960: "טֹרַח (ṭôrach | to'-rakh)\n\nDerivation: from H2959 טָרַח\nStrong's: a burden\nKJV: cumbrance, trouble.\n\nCognate Group: H2960 (cumbrance), H2959 (weary)\nGreek Equivalents: G2873 κόπος, G4140 πλησμονή",
    2961: "טָרִי (ṭârîy | taw-ree')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root apparently meaning to be moist\nStrong's: properly, dripping; hence, fresh (i.e. recently made such)\nKJV: new, putrefying.\n\nCognate Group: H2961 (new)",
    2962: "טֶרֶם (ṭerem | teh'-rem)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root apparently meaning to interrupt or suspend\nStrong's: properly, non-occurrence; used adverbially, not yet or before\nKJV: before, ere, not yet.\n\nCognate Group: H2958 (before), H2962 (before)\nGreek Equivalents: G3764 οὐδέπω",
    2963: "טָרַף (ṭâraph | taw-raf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to pluck off or pull to pieces; causatively to supply with food (as in morsels)\nKJV: catch, [idiom] without doubt, feed, ravin, rend in pieces, [idiom] surely, tear (in pieces).\n\nCognate Group: H2964 (leaf), H2966 (ravin), H2963 (catch), H2965 (pluckt off)\nGreek Equivalents: G726 ἁρπάζω, G727 ἅρπαξ, G2339 θήρα, G2340 θηρεύω, G2595 κάρφος, G4929 συντάσσω, G4937 συντρίβω",
    2964: "טֶרֶף (ṭereph | teh'-ref)\n\nDerivation: from H2963 טָרַף\nStrong's: something torn, i.e. a fragment, e.g. a fresh leaf, prey, food\nKJV: leaf, meat, prey, spoil.\n\nCognate Group: H2964 (leaf), H2966 (ravin), H2963 (catch), H2965 (pluckt off)\nGreek Equivalents: G1033 βρῶμα, G2339 θήρα, G5160 τροφή",
    2965: "טָרָף (ṭârâph | taw-rawf')\n\nDerivation: from H2963 טָרַף\nStrong's: recently torn off, i.e. fresh\nKJV: pluckt off.\n\nCognate Group: H2964 (leaf), H2966 (ravin), H2963 (catch), H2965 (pluckt off)",
    2966: "טְרֵפָה (ṭᵉrêphâh | ter-ay-faw')\n\nDerivation: feminine (collectively) of H2964 טֶרֶף\nStrong's: prey, i.e. flocks devoured by animals\nKJV: ravin, (that which was) torn (of beasts, in pieces).\n\nCognate Group: H2964 (leaf), H2966 (ravin), H2963 (catch), H2965 (pluckt off)\nGreek Equivalents: G724 ἁρπαγή",
    2967: "טַרְפְּלַי (Ṭarpᵉlay | tar-pel-ah'-ee)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from a name of foreign derivation\nStrong's: a Tarpelite (collectively) or inhabitants of Tarpel, a place in Assyria\nKJV: Tarpelites. y\n\nCognate Group: H2967 (Tarpelites), H2957 (drive)",
    2968: "יָאַב (yâʼab | yaw-ab')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to desire\nKJV: long.\n\nCognate Group: H2968 (long)\nGreek Equivalents: G1971 ἐπιποθέω",
    2969: "יָאָה (yâʼâh | yaw-aw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be suitable\nKJV: appertain.\n\nCognate Group: H2969 (appertain)",
    2970: "יַאֲזַנְיָה (Yaʼăzanyâh | yah-az-an-yaw')\n\nDerivation: or יַאֲזַנְיָהוּ; from H238 אָזַן and H3050 יָהּ ; heard of Jah\nStrong's: Jaazanjah, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Jaazaniah. Compare H3153 יְזַנְיָה .\n\nCognate Group: H238 (give ear), H240 (weapon), H243 (Aznoth-tabor), H2970 (Jaazaniah), H244 (Ozni), H241 (advertise), H245 (Azaniah), H3153 (Jezaniah)",
    2971: "יָאִיר (Yâʼîyr | yaw-ere')\n\nDerivation: from H215 אֹור ; enlightener\nStrong's: Jair, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Jair.\n\nCognate Group: H2971 (Jair), H222 (Uriel), H3975 (den), H221 (Uri), H218 (Ur), H216 (bright), H224 (Urim), H223 (Uriah), H2972 (Jairite), H215 ([idiom] break of day), H3974 (bright), H217 (fire), H219 (herb)\nGreek Equivalents: G2383 Ἰάειρος",
    2972: "יָאִרִי (Yâʼirîy | yaw-ee-ree')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from H2971 יָאִיר\nStrong's: a Jairite or descendant of Jair\nKJV: Jairite.\n\nCognate Group: H2971 (Jair), H222 (Uriel), H3975 (den), H221 (Uri), H218 (Ur), H216 (bright), H224 (Urim), H223 (Uriah), H2972 (Jairite), H215 ([idiom] break of day), H3974 (bright), H217 (fire), H219 (herb)",
    2973: "יָאַל (yâʼal | yaw-al')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to be slack, i.e. (figuratively) to be foolish\nKJV: dote, be (become, do) foolish(-ly).\n\nCognate Group: H2973 (dote)\nGreek Equivalents: G50 ἀγνοέω, G1410 δύναμαι, G1587 ἐκλείπω",
    2974: "יָאַל (yâʼal | yaw-al')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (probably rather the same as H2973 יָאַל through the idea of mental weakness)\nStrong's: properly, to yield, especially assent; hence (pos.) to undertake as an act of volition\nKJV: assay, begin, be content, please, take upon, [idiom] willingly, would.\n\nCognate Group: H2974 (assay), H2975 (brook)\nGreek Equivalents: G71 ἄγω, G757 ἄρχω, G2192 ἔχω, G2650 καταμένω, G2872 κοπιάω, G3786 ὄφελος, G4160 ποιέω, G5622 ὠφέλεια, G5623 ὠφελέω",
    2975: "יְאֹר (yᵉʼôr | yeh-ore')\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian origin\nStrong's: a channel, e.g. a fosse, canal, shaft; specifically the Nile, as the one river of Egypt, including its collateral trenches; also the Tigris, as the main river of Assyria\nKJV: brook, flood, river, stream.\n\nCognate Group: H2974 (assay), H2975 (brook)\nGreek Equivalents: G4215 ποταμός",
