    3646: "כַּמֹּן (kammôn | kam-mone')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to store up or preserve\nStrong's: 'cummin' (from its use as a condiment)\nKJV: cummin.\n\nCognate Group: H4362 (treasure), H3646 (cummin)\nGreek Equivalents: G2951 κύμινον",
    3647: "כָּמַס (kâmaç | kaw-mas')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to store away, i.e. (figuratively) in the memory\nKJV: lay up in store.\n\nCognate Group: H4363 (Mikmas), H3647 (lay up in store)\nGreek Equivalents: G4863 συνάγω",
    3648: "כָּמַר (kâmar | kaw-mar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to intertwine or contract, i.e. (by implication) to shrivel (as with heat); figuratively, to be deeply affected with passion (love or pity)\nKJV: be black, be kindled, yearn.\n\nCognate Group: H3648 (be black)\nGreek Equivalents: G4962 συστρέφω, G5015 ταράσσω",
    3649: "כָּמָר (kâmâr | kaw-mawr')\n\nDerivation: from H3648 כָּמַר\nStrong's: properly, an ascetic (as if shrunk with self-maceration), i.e. an idolatrous priest (only in plural)\nKJV: Chemarims (idolatrous) priests.\n\nCognate Group: H3649 (Chemarims priests), H4365 (drag), H4364 (net)",
