    4990: "מִתְרְדָת (Mithrᵉdâth | mith-red-awth')\n\nDerivation: of Persian origin\nStrong's: Mithredath, the name of two Persians\nKJV: Mithredath.\n\nCognate Group: H4990 (Mithredath), H4987 (sweetness), H4988 (feed sweetly), H4985 (be sweet), H4986 (sweetness), H4477 (sweet), H4966 (sweet), H4989 (Mithcah)",
    4991: "מַתָּת (mattâth | mat-tawth')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H4976 מַתָּן abbreviated form\nStrong's: a present\nKJV: gift.\n\nCognate Group: H4982 (Mattenai), H5417 (Nethaneel), H5416 (Nathan), H4977 (Mattan), H4979 (gift), H5418 (Nethaniah), H4991 (gift), H4976 (gift), H4983 (Mattaniah), H5414 (add), H5419 (Nathanmelech), H4992 (Mattathah), H4980 (Mattanah), H4993 (Mattithiah), H5411 (Nethinims)\nGreek Equivalents: G1394 δόσις, G3158 Ματθάτ",
    4992: "מַתַּתָּה (Mattattâh | mat-tat-taw')\n\nDerivation: for H4993 מַתִּתְיָה ; gift of Jah\nStrong's: Mattattah, an Israelite\nKJV: Mattathah.\n\nCognate Group: H4982 (Mattenai), H5417 (Nethaneel), H5416 (Nathan), H4977 (Mattan), H4979 (gift), H5418 (Nethaniah), H4991 (gift), H4976 (gift), H4983 (Mattaniah), H5414 (add), H5419 (Nathanmelech), H4992 (Mattathah), H4980 (Mattanah), H4993 (Mattithiah), H5411 (Nethinims)\nGreek Equivalents: G3160 Ματταθά",
    4993: "מַתִּתְיָה (Mattithyâh | mat-tith-yaw')\n\nDerivation: or מַתִּתְיָהוּ; from H4991 מַתָּת and H3050 יָהּ ; gift of Jah\nStrong's: Mattithjah, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Mattithiah. n\n\nCognate Group: H4982 (Mattenai), H5417 (Nethaneel), H5416 (Nathan), H4977 (Mattan), H4979 (gift), H5418 (Nethaniah), H4991 (gift), H4976 (gift), H4983 (Mattaniah), H5414 (add), H5419 (Nathanmelech), H4992 (Mattathah), H4980 (Mattanah), H4993 (Mattithiah), H5411 (Nethinims)\nGreek Equivalents: G3161 Ματταθίας",
    4994: "נָא (nâʼ | naw)\n\nDerivation: a primitive particle of incitement and entreaty, which may usually be rendered\nStrong's: 'I pray', 'now', or 'then'; added mostly to verbs (in the Imperative or Future), or to interjections, occasionally to an adverb or conjunction\nKJV: I beseech (pray) thee (you), go to, now, oh.\n\nCognate Group: H4994 (I beseech thee), H4997 (bottle), H4996 (No)\nGreek Equivalents: G515 ἀξιόω, G3568 νῦν, G5614 ὡσαννά",
    4995: "נָא (nâʼ | naw)\n\nDerivation: apparently from H5106 נוּא in the sense of harshness from refusal\nStrong's: properly, tough, i.e. uncooked (flesh)\nKJV: raw.\n\nCognate Group: H4995 (raw), H5212 (Nisan), H5210 (Nineveh)",
    4996: "נֹא (Nôʼ | no)\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian origin\nStrong's: No (i.e. Thebes), the capital of Upper Egypt\nKJV: No. Compare H528 אָמֹון .\n\nCognate Group: H4994 (I beseech thee), H4997 (bottle), H4996 (No)",
    4997: "נֹאד (nôʼd | node)\n\nDerivation: or נאֹוד\n\nTypo: lemma נֹאוד vowel, corrected to נאֹוד\n\nTypo: xlit nôʼwd corrected to nʼôwd\n\n; also (feminine) נֹאדָה; from an unused root of uncertain signification\nStrong's: a (skin or leather) bag (for fluids)\nKJV: bottle.\n\nCognate Group: H4994 (I beseech thee), H4997 (bottle), H4996 (No)\nGreek Equivalents: G779 ἀσκός, G2222 ζωή",
    4998: "נָאָה (nâʼâh | naw-aw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to be at home, i.e. (by implication) to be pleasant (or suitable), i.e. beautiful\nKJV: be beautiful, become, be comely.\n\nCognate Group: H4998 (be beautiful), H5000 (becometh)\nGreek Equivalents: G2379 θυσιαστήριον, G4241 πρέπω, G4486 ῥήγνυμι",
    4999: "נָאָה (nâʼâh | naw-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H4998 נָאָה\nStrong's: a home; figuratively, a pasture\nKJV: habitation, house, pasture, pleasant place.\n\nCognate Group: H5116 (comely), H5115 (keept at home), H4999 (habitation), H5121 (Naioth)\nGreek Equivalents: G3542 νομή, G3624 οἶκος, G5117 τόπος, G5610 ὥρα, G5611 ὡραῖος, G3484 Ναΐν",
    5000: "נָאוֶה (nâʼveh | naw-veh')\n\nDerivation: from H4998 נָאָה or H5116 נָוֶה\nStrong's: suitable, or beautiful\nKJV: becometh, comely, seemly.\n\nCognate Group: H4998 (be beautiful), H5000 (becometh)",
    5001: "נָאַם (nâʼam | naw-am')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to whisper, i.e. (by implication) to utter as aoracle\nKJV: say.\n\nCognate Group: H5001 (say), H5002 (said)\nGreek Equivalents: G3004 λέγω, G5346 φημί",
    5002: "נְאֻם (nᵉʼum | neh-oom')\n\nDerivation: from H5001 נָאַם\nStrong's: an oracle\nKJV: (hath) said, saith.\n\nCognate Group: H5001 (say), H5002 (said)\nGreek Equivalents: G3004 λέγω",
    5003: "נָאַף (nâʼaph | naw-af')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to commit adultery; figuratively, to apostatize\nKJV: adulterer(-ess), commit(-ing) adultery, woman that breaketh wedlock.\n\nCognate Group: H5003 (adulterer), H5004 (adultery), H5005 (adultery)\nGreek Equivalents: G3429 μοιχάω, G3430 μοιχεία, G3431 μοιχεύω, G3432 μοιχός",
    5004: "נִאֻף (niʼuph | nee-oof')\n\nDerivation: from H5003 נָאַף\nStrong's: adultery\nKJV: adultery.\n\nCognate Group: H5003 (adulterer), H5004 (adultery), H5005 (adultery)\nGreek Equivalents: G3430 μοιχεία",
    5005: "נַאֲפוּף (naʼăphûwph | nah-af-oof')\n\nDerivation: from H5003 נָאַף\nStrong's: adultery\nKJV: adultery.\n\nCognate Group: H5003 (adulterer), H5004 (adultery), H5005 (adultery)\nGreek Equivalents: G3430 μοιχεία",
    5006: "נָאַץ (nâʼats | naw-ats')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; in Ecclesiastes 12:5, by interchange for H5132 נוּץ, to bloom\nStrong's: to scorn\nKJV: abhor, (give occasion to) blaspheme, contemn, despise, flourish, [idiom] great, provoke.\n\nCognate Group: H5007 (blasphemy), H5006 (abhor)\nGreek Equivalents: G114 ἀθετέω, G683 ἀπωθέομαι, G987 βλασφημέω, G1578 ἐκκλίνω, G2206 ζηλόω, G3456 μυκτηρίζω, G3947 παροξύνω, G3949 παροργίζω",
    5007: "נְאָצָה (nᵉʼâtsâh | neh-aw-tsaw')\n\nDerivation: or נֶאָצָה; from H5006 נָאַץ\nStrong's: scorn\nKJV: blasphemy.\n\nCognate Group: H5007 (blasphemy), H5006 (abhor)\nGreek Equivalents: G988 βλασφημία, G1650 ἔλεγχος, G3709 ὀργή, G3950 παροργισμός",
    5008: "נָאַק (nâʼaq | naw-ak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to groan\nKJV: groan.\n\nCognate Group: H5008 (groan), H5009 (groaning)",
    5009: "נְאָקָה (nᵉʼâqâh | neh-aw-kaw')\n\nDerivation: from H5008 נָאַק\nStrong's: a groan\nKJV: groaning.\n\nCognate Group: H5008 (groan), H5009 (groaning)\nGreek Equivalents: G4726 στεναγμός",
    5010: "נָאַר (nâʼar | naw-ar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to reject\nKJV: abhor, make void.\n\nCognate Group: H5011 (Nob), H5010 (abhor)",
    5011: "נֹב (Nôb | nobe)\n\nDerivation: the same as H5108 נֹוב ; fruit\nStrong's: Nob, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Nob.\n\nCognate Group: H5011 (Nob), H5010 (abhor)",
    5012: "נָבָא (nâbâʼ | naw-baw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to prophesy, i.e. speak (or sing) by inspiration (in prediction or simple discourse)\nKJV: prophesy(-ing), make self a prophet.\n\nCognate Group: H5030 (prophecy), H5031 (prophetess), H5016 (prophecy), H5012 (prophesy)\nGreek Equivalents: G669 ἀποφθέγγομαι, G2980 λαλέω, G4395 προφητεύω",
    5013: "נְבָא (nᵉbâʼ | neb-aw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5012 נָבָא \nStrong's: to prophesy, i.e. speak (or sing) by inspiration (in prediction or simple discourse) to prophesy, i.e. speak (or sing) by inspiration (in prediction or simple discourse)\nKJV: prophesy.\n\nCognate Group: H5023 (reward), H5013 (prophesy), H5017 (prophesying), H5020 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5043 (candlestick), H5029 (prophet)",
    5014: "נָבַב (nâbab | naw-bab')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to pierce; to be hollow, or (figuratively) foolish\nKJV: hollow, vain.\n\nCognate Group: H5018 (Nebuzaradan), H5015 (Nebo), H5021 (Nebushazban), H5022 (Naboth), H5019 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5014 (hollow)",
    5015: "נְבֹו (Nᵉbôw | neb-o')\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign derivation\nStrong's: Nebo, the name of a Babylonian deity, also of a mountain in Moab, and of a place in Palestine\nKJV: Nebo.\n\nCognate Group: H5018 (Nebuzaradan), H5015 (Nebo), H5021 (Nebushazban), H5022 (Naboth), H5019 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5014 (hollow)",
    5016: "נְבוּאָה (nᵉbûwʼâh | neb-oo-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H5012 נָבָא\nStrong's: a prediction (spoken or written)\nKJV: prophecy.\n\nCognate Group: H5030 (prophecy), H5031 (prophetess), H5016 (prophecy), H5012 (prophesy)\nGreek Equivalents: G4394 προφητεία",
    5017: "נְבוּאָה (nᵉbûwʼâh | neb-oo-aw)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5016 נְבוּאָה\nStrong's: inspired teaching\nKJV: prophesying.\n\nCognate Group: H5023 (reward), H5013 (prophesy), H5017 (prophesying), H5020 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5043 (candlestick), H5029 (prophet)\nGreek Equivalents: G3056 λόγος",
    5018: "נְבוּזַרְאֲדָן (Nᵉbûwzarʼădân | neb-oo-zar-ad-awn')\n\nTypo:  xlit Nᵉbûwzarădân corrected to Nᵉbûwzarʼădân\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin\nStrong's: Nebuzaradan, a Babylonian general\nKJV: Nebuzaradan.\n\nCognate Group: H5018 (Nebuzaradan), H5015 (Nebo), H5021 (Nebushazban), H5022 (Naboth), H5019 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5014 (hollow)",
    5019: "נְבוּכַדְנֶאצַּר (Nᵉbûwkadneʼtstsar | neb-oo-kad-nets-tsar')\n\nDerivation: or נְבֻּכַדְנֶאצַּר; (2 Kings 24:1,10), or נְבוּכַדְנֶצַּר; (Esther 2:6; Daniel 1:18), or נְבוּכַדְרֶאצַּר; or נְבוּכַדְרֶאצֹּור; (Ezra 2:1; Jeremiah 49:28), or foreign derivation\nStrong's: Nebukadnetstsar (or -retstsar, or -retstsor), king of Babylon\nKJV: Nebuchadnezzar, Nebuchadrezzar.\n\nCognate Group: H5018 (Nebuzaradan), H5015 (Nebo), H5021 (Nebushazban), H5022 (Naboth), H5019 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5014 (hollow)",
    5020: "נְבוּכַדְנֶצַּר (Nᵉbûwkadnetstsar | neb-oo-kad-nets-tsar')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5019 נְבוּכַדְנֶאצַּר \nStrong's: Nebukadnetstsar (or -retstsar, or -retstsor), king of Babylon\nKJV: Nebuchadnezzar.\n\nCognate Group: H5023 (reward), H5013 (prophesy), H5017 (prophesying), H5020 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5043 (candlestick), H5029 (prophet)",
    5021: "נְבוּשַׁזְבָּן (Nᵉbûwshazbân | neb-oo-shaz-bawn')\n\nDerivation: of foreign derivation\nStrong's: Nebushazban, Nebuchadnezzar's chief eunuch\nKJV: Nebushazban.\n\nCognate Group: H5018 (Nebuzaradan), H5015 (Nebo), H5021 (Nebushazban), H5022 (Naboth), H5019 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5014 (hollow)",
    5022: "נָבֹות (Nâbôwth | naw-both')\n\nDerivation: feminine plural from the same as H5011 נֹב ; fruits\nStrong's: Naboth, an Israelite\nKJV: Naboth.\n\nCognate Group: H5018 (Nebuzaradan), H5015 (Nebo), H5021 (Nebushazban), H5022 (Naboth), H5019 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5014 (hollow)",
    5023: "נְבִזְבָּה (nᵉbizbâh | neb-iz-baw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a largess\nKJV: reward.\n\nCognate Group: H5023 (reward), H5013 (prophesy), H5017 (prophesying), H5020 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5043 (candlestick), H5029 (prophet)\nGreek Equivalents: G1431 δωρεά",
    5024: "נָבַח (nâbach | naw-bakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to bark (as a dog)\nKJV: bark.\n\nCognate Group: H5025 (Nobah), H5024 (bark), H5026 (Nibhaz)",
    5025: "נֹבַח (Nôbach | no'-bach)\n\nDerivation: from H5024 נָבַח ; a bark\nStrong's: Nobach, the name of an Israelite, and of a place East of the Jordan\nKJV: Nobah.\n\nCognate Group: H5025 (Nobah), H5024 (bark), H5026 (Nibhaz)",
    5026: "נִבְחַז (Nibchaz | nib-khaz')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin\nStrong's: Nibchaz, a deity of the Avites\nKJV: Nibhaz.\n\nCognate Group: H5025 (Nobah), H5024 (bark), H5026 (Nibhaz)",
    5027: "נָבַט (nâbaṭ | naw-bat')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to scan, i.e. look intently at; by implication, to regard with pleasure, favor or care\nKJV: (cause to) behold, consider, look (down), regard, have respect, see.\n\nCognate Group: H5027 (behold), H5032 (Nebaioth), H4007 (expectation), H5028 (Nebat), H5033 (spring)\nGreek Equivalents: G308 ἀναβλέπω, G991 βλέπω, G1567 ἐκζητέω, G1689 ἐμβλέπω, G1914 ἐπιβλέπω, G1994 ἐπιστρέφω, G2657 κατανοέω, G4017 περιβλέπω, G4334 προσέρχομαι",
    5028: "נְבָט (Nᵉbâṭ | neb-awt')\n\nDerivation: from H5027 נָבַט ; regard\nStrong's: Nebat, the father of Jeroboam I\nKJV: Nebat.\n\nCognate Group: H5027 (behold), H5032 (Nebaioth), H4007 (expectation), H5028 (Nebat), H5033 (spring)",
    5029: "נְבִיא (nᵉbîyʼ | neb-ee')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5030 נָבִיא\nStrong's: a prophet\nKJV: prophet.\n\nCognate Group: H5023 (reward), H5013 (prophesy), H5017 (prophesying), H5020 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5043 (candlestick), H5029 (prophet)\nGreek Equivalents: G921 Βαρνάβας",
    5030: "נָבִיא (nâbîyʼ | naw-bee')\n\nDerivation: from H5012 נָבָא\nStrong's: a prophet or (generally) inspired man\nKJV: prophecy, that prophesy, prophet.\n\nCognate Group: H5030 (prophecy), H5031 (prophetess), H5016 (prophecy), H5012 (prophesy)\nGreek Equivalents: G5578 ψευδοπροφήτης",
    5031: "נְבִיאָה (nᵉbîyʼâh | neb-ee-yaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H5030 נָבִיא\nStrong's: a prophetess or (generally) inspired woman; by implication, a poetess; by association a prophet's wife\nKJV: prophetess.\n\nCognate Group: H5030 (prophecy), H5031 (prophetess), H5016 (prophecy), H5012 (prophesy)\nGreek Equivalents: G4398 προφῆτις",
    5032: "נְבָיֹות (Nᵉbâyôwth | neb-aw-yoth')\n\nDerivation: or נְבָיֹת; feminine plural from H5107 נוּב ; fruitfulnesses\nStrong's: Nebajoth, a son of Ismael, and the country settled by him\nKJV: Nebaioth, Nebajoth.\n\nCognate Group: H5027 (behold), H5032 (Nebaioth), H4007 (expectation), H5028 (Nebat), H5033 (spring)",
    5033: "נֵבֶךְ (nêbek | nay'-bek)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to burst forth\nStrong's: a fountain\nKJV: spring.\n\nCognate Group: H5027 (behold), H5032 (Nebaioth), H4007 (expectation), H5028 (Nebat), H5033 (spring)\nGreek Equivalents: G4077 πηγή",
    5034: "נָבֵל (nâbêl | naw-bale')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to wilt; generally, to fall away, fail, faint; figuratively, to be foolish or (morally) wicked; causatively, to despise, disgrace\nKJV: disgrace, dishounour, lightly esteem, fade (away, -ing), fall (down, -ling, off), do foolishly, come to nought, [idiom] surely, make vile, wither.\n\nCognate Group: H5037 (Nabal), H5034 (disgrace), H5040 (lewdness), H5039 (folly), H5036 (fool)\nGreek Equivalents: G167 ἀκαθαρσία, G577 ἀποβάλλω, G622 ἀπόλλυμι, G634 ἀποπίπτω, G641 ἀποῤῥίπτω, G818 ἀτιμάζω, G868 ἀφίστημι, G2704 καταφθείρω, G3822 παλαιόω, G4098 πίπτω, G5351 φθείρω, G5356 φθορά",
    5035: "נֶבֶל (nebel | neh'-bel)\n\nDerivation: or נֵבֶל; from H5034 נָבֵל\nStrong's: a skin-bag for liquids (from collapsing when empty); hence, a vase (as similar in shape when full); also a lyre (as having a body of like form)\nKJV: bottle, pitcher, psaltery, vessel, viol.\n\nCognate Group: H5035 (bottle)\nGreek Equivalents: G30 ἀγγεῖον, G779 ἀσκός, G2167 εὐφροσύνη, G2788 κιθάρα, G5568 ψαλμός",
    5036: "נָבָל (nâbâl | naw-bawl')\n\nDerivation: from H5034 נָבֵל\nStrong's: stupid; wicked (especially impious)\nKJV: fool(-ish, -ish man, -ish woman), vile person.\n\nCognate Group: H5037 (Nabal), H5034 (disgrace), H5040 (lewdness), H5039 (folly), H5036 (fool)\nGreek Equivalents: G521 ἀπαίδευτος, G801 ἀσύνετος, G878 ἄφρων, G3474 μωρός",
    5037: "נָבָל (Nâbâl | naw-bawl')\n\nDerivation: the same as H5036 נָבָל ; dolt\nStrong's: Nabal, an Israelite\nKJV: Nabal.\n\nCognate Group: H5037 (Nabal), H5034 (disgrace), H5040 (lewdness), H5039 (folly), H5036 (fool)",
    5038: "נְבֵלָה (nᵉbêlâh | neb-ay-law')\n\nDerivation: from H5034 נָבֵל\nStrong's: a flabby thing, i.e. a carcase or carrion (human or bestial, often collectively); figuratively, an idol\nKJV: (dead) body, (dead) carcase, dead of itself, which died, (beast) that (which) dieth of itself.\n\nCognate Group: H5038 (body), H5041 (Neballat), H5034 (disgrace)\nGreek Equivalents: G3498 νεκρός, G4983 σῶμα",
    5039: "נְבָלָה (nᵉbâlâh | neb-aw-law')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H5036 נָבָל\nStrong's: foolishness, i.e. (morally) wickedness; concretely, a crime; by extension, punishment\nKJV: folly, vile, villany.\n\nCognate Group: H5037 (Nabal), H5034 (disgrace), H5040 (lewdness), H5039 (folly), H5036 (fool)\nGreek Equivalents: G94 ἄδικος, G458 ἀνομία, G809 ἀσχήμων, G877 ἀφροσύνη, G1135 γυνή",
    5040: "נַבְלוּת (nablûwth | nab-looth')\n\nDerivation: from H5036 נָבָל\nStrong's: properly, disgrace, i.e. the (female) pudenda\nKJV: lewdness.\n\nCognate Group: H5037 (Nabal), H5034 (disgrace), H5040 (lewdness), H5039 (folly), H5036 (fool)",
    5041: "נְבַלָּט (Nᵉballâṭ | neb-al-lawt')\n\nDerivation: apparently from H5036 נָבָל and H3909 לָט ; foolish secrecy\nStrong's: Neballat, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Neballat.\n\nCognate Group: H5038 (body), H5041 (Neballat), H5034 (disgrace)",
    5042: "נָבַע (nâbaʻ | naw-bah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to gush forth; figuratively, to utter (good or bad words); specifically, to emit (a foul odor)\nKJV: belch out, flowing, pour out, send forth, utter (abundantly).\n\nCognate Group: H4002 (fountain), H5042 (belch out)\nGreek Equivalents: G312 ἀναγγέλλω, G611 ἀποκρίνομαι, G669 ἀποφθέγγομαι, G2044 ἐρεύγομαι, G5350 φθέγγομαι",
    5043: "נֶבְרְשָׁא (nebrᵉshâʼ | neb-reh-shaw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from an unused root meaning to shine\nStrong's: a light; plural (collectively) a chandelier\nKJV: candlestick.\n\nCognate Group: H5023 (reward), H5013 (prophesy), H5017 (prophesying), H5020 (Nebuchadnezzar), H5043 (candlestick), H5029 (prophet)\nGreek Equivalents: G2985 λαμπάς, G5457 φῶς",
    5044: "נִבְשָׁן (Nibshân | nib-shawn')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Nibshan, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Nibshan.\n\nCognate Group: H5044 (Nibshan), H1316 (Bashan)",
    5045: "נֶגֶב (negeb | neh'-gheb)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to be parched\nStrong's: the south (from its drought); specifically, the Negeb or southern district of Judah, occasionally, Egypt (as south to Palestine)\nKJV: south (country, side, -ward).\n\nCognate Group: H5045 (south)\nGreek Equivalents: G2048 ἔρημος, G3047 λίψ, G3314 μεσημβρία, G3558 νότος",
    5046: "נָגַד (nâgad | naw-gad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to front, i.e. stand boldly out opposite; by implication (causatively), to manifest; figuratively, to announce (always by word of mouth to one present); specifically, to expose, predict, explain, praise\nKJV: bewray, [idiom] certainly, certify, declare(-ing), denounce, expound, [idiom] fully, messenger, plainly, profess, rehearse, report, shew (forth), speak, [idiom] surely, tell, utter.\n\nCognate Group: H5046 (bewray), H5048 (about), H5057 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G31 ἀγγελία, G312 ἀναγγέλλω, G518 ἀπαγγέλλω, G601 ἀποκαλύπτω, G1097 γινώσκω, G1166 δεικνύω, G2980 λαλέω, G4302 προλέγω, G4591 σημαίνω, G5263 ὑποδείκνυμι",
    5047: "נְגַד (nᵉgad | neg-ad')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5046 נָגַד\nStrong's: to flow (through the idea of clearing the way)\nKJV: issue.\n\nCognate Group: H5049 (toward), H5053 (morning), H5047 (issue)\nGreek Equivalents: G1670 ἑλκύω",
    5048: "נֶגֶד (neged | neh'-ghed)\n\nDerivation: from H5046 נָגַד\nStrong's: a front, i.e. part opposite; specifically a counterpart, or mate; usually (adverbial, especially with preposition) over against or before\nKJV: about, (over) against, [idiom] aloof, [idiom] far (off), [idiom] from, over, presence, [idiom] other side, sight, [idiom] to view.\n\nCognate Group: H5046 (bewray), H5048 (about), H5057 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G991 βλέπω, G1726 ἐναντίον, G1726 ἐναντίον, G1727 ἐναντίος, G1727 ἐναντίος, G2064 ἔρχομαι, G2112 εὐθέως, G2117 εὐθύς, G2192 ἔχω, G3664 ὅμοιος, G3775 οὖς",
    5049: "נֶגֶד (neged | neh'-ghed)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5048 נֶגֶד\nStrong's: opposite\nKJV: toward.\n\nCognate Group: H5049 (toward), H5053 (morning), H5047 (issue)",
    5050: "נָגַהּ (nâgahh | naw-gah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to glitter; causatively, to illuminate\nKJV: (en-) lighten, (cause to) shine.\n\nCognate Group: H5054 (brightness), H5052 (Nogah), H5050 (lighten), H5051 (bright)\nGreek Equivalents: G305 ἀναβαίνω, G1325 δίδωμι, G1584 ἐκλάμπω, G2989 λάμπω, G5461 φωτίζω",
    5051: "נֹגַהּ (nôgahh | no'-gah)\n\nDerivation: from H5050 נָגַהּ\nStrong's: brilliancy (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: bright(-ness), light, (clear) shining.\n\nCognate Group: H5054 (brightness), H5052 (Nogah), H5050 (lighten), H5051 (bright)\nGreek Equivalents: G395 ἀνατολή, G2987 λαμπρότης, G5338 φέγγος, G5457 φῶς",
    5052: "נֹגַהּ (Nôgahh | no'-gah)\n\nDerivation: the same as H5051 נֹגַהּ\nStrong's: Nogah, a son of David\nKJV: Nogah.\n\nCognate Group: H5054 (brightness), H5052 (Nogah), H5050 (lighten), H5051 (bright)\nGreek Equivalents: G3477 Ναγγαί",
    5053: "נֹגַהּ (nôgahh | no'-gah)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5051 נֹגַהּ\nStrong's: dawn\nKJV: morning.\n\nCognate Group: H5049 (toward), H5053 (morning), H5047 (issue)",
    5054: "נְגֹהָה (nᵉgôhâh | neg-o-haw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H5051 נֹגַהּ\nStrong's: splendor\nKJV: brightness.\n\nCognate Group: H5054 (brightness), H5052 (Nogah), H5050 (lighten), H5051 (bright)\nGreek Equivalents: G827 αὐγή",
    5055: "נָגַח (nâgach | naw-gakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to butt with the horns; figuratively, to war against\nKJV: gore, push (down, -ing).\n\nCognate Group: H5055 (gore), H5056 (used to push)",
    5056: "נַגָּח (naggâch | nag-gawkh')\n\nDerivation: from H5055 נָגַח\nStrong's: butting, i.e. vicious\nKJV: used (wont) to push.\n\nCognate Group: H5055 (gore), H5056 (used to push)",
    5057: "נָגִיד (nâgîyd | naw-gheed')\n\nDerivation: or נָגִד; from H5046 נָגַד\nStrong's: a commander (as occupying the front), civil, military or religious; generally (abstractly, plural), honorable themes\nKJV: captain, chief, excellent thing, (chief) governor, leader, noble, prince, (chief) ruler.\n\nCognate Group: H5046 (bewray), H5048 (about), H5057 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G758 ἄρχων, G935 βασιλεύς, G1988 ἐπιστάτης, G2233 ἡγέομαι, G4586 σεμνός",
    5058: "נְגִינָה (nᵉgîynâh | neg-ee-naw')\n\nDerivation: or נְגִינַת; (Psalm 61:title), from H5059 נָגַן\nStrong's: properly, instrumental music; by implication, a stringed instrument; by extension, a poem set to music; specifically, an epigram\nKJV: stringed instrument, musick, Neginoth (plural), song.\n\nCognate Group: H4485 (music), H5058 (stringed instrument), H5059 (player on instruments)\nGreek Equivalents: G2788 κιθάρα, G5215 ὕμνος, G5603 ᾠδή",
    5059: "נָגַן (nâgan | naw-gan')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to thrum, i.e. beat atune with the fingers; expectation. to play on astringed instrument; hence (generally), to make music\nKJV: player on instruments, sing to the stringed instruments, melody, ministrel, play(-er, -ing).\n\nCognate Group: H4485 (music), H5058 (stringed instrument), H5059 (player on instruments)\nGreek Equivalents: G2127 εὐλογέω, G2789 κιθαρίζω, G5567 ψάλλω, G5568 ψαλμός",
    5060: "נָגַע (nâgaʻ | naw-gah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to touch, i.e. lay the hand upon (for any purpose; euphemistically, to lie with a woman); by implication, to reach (figuratively, to arrive, acquire); violently, to strike (punish, defeat, destroy, etc.)\nKJV: beat, ([idiom] be able to) bring (down), cast, come (nigh), draw near (nigh), get up, happen, join, near, plague, reach (up), smite, strike, touch.\n\nCognate Group: H5061 (plague), H5060 (beat)\nGreek Equivalents: G378 ἀναπληρόω, G402 ἀναχωρέω, G680 ἅπτομαι, G681 ἅπτω, G864 ἀφικνέομαι, G1096 γίνομαι, G1448 ἐγγίζω, G1620 ἐκτίθημι, G1651 ἐλέγχω, G2345 θιγγάνω, G2480 ἰσχύω, G2597 καταβαίνω, G2609 κατάγω, G2853 κολλάω, G3146 μαστιγόω, G3854 παραγίνομαι, G3918 πάρειμι, G4192 πόνος, G4317 προσάγω, G4331 προσεγγίζω, G4363 προσπίπτω, G4404 πρωΐ, G4863 συνάγω, G4876 συναντάω, G5087 τίθημι, G5348 φθάνω",
    5061: "נֶגַע (negaʻ | neh'-gah)\n\nDerivation: from H5060 נָגַע\nStrong's: a blow (figuratively, infliction); also (by implication) a spot (concretely, a leprous person or dress)\nKJV: plague, sore, stricken, stripe, stroke, wound.\n\nCognate Group: H5061 (plague), H5060 (beat)\nGreek Equivalents: G860 ἁφή, G3148 μάστιξ, G3601 ὀδύνη, G4127 πληγή",
    5062: "נָגַף (nâgaph | naw-gaf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to push, gore, defeat, stub (the toe), inflict (a disease)\nKJV: beat, dash, hurt, plague, slay, smite (down), strike, stumble, [idiom] surely, put to the worse.\n\nCognate Group: H4046 (plague), H5063 (plague), H5062 (beat)\nGreek Equivalents: G2352 θραύω, G2871 κοπή, G2875 κόπτω, G3811 παιδεύω, G3817 παίω, G3960 πατάσσω, G4098 πίπτω, G4141 πλήσσω, G4350 προσκόπτω, G4417 πταίω, G4937 συντρίβω, G5180 τύπτω",
    5063: "נֶגֶף (negeph | neh'-ghef)\n\nDerivation: from H5062 נָגַף\nStrong's: a trip (of the foot); figuratively, an infliction (of disease)\nKJV: plague, stumbling.\n\nCognate Group: H4046 (plague), H5063 (plague), H5062 (beat)\nGreek Equivalents: G4127 πληγή, G4348 πρόσκομμα, G4431 πτῶσις",
    5064: "נָגַר (nâgar | naw-gar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to flow; figuratively, to stretch out; causatively, to pour out or down; figuratively, to deliver over\nKJV: fall, flow away, pour down (out), run, shed, spilt, trickle down.\n\nCognate Group: H5064 (fall)\nGreek Equivalents: G1904 ἐπέρχομαι, G2666 καταπίνω, G2702 καταφέρω, G3860 παραδίδωμι, G4867 συναθροίζω",
    5065: "נָגַשׂ (nâgas | naw-gas')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to drive (an animal, a workman, a debtor, an army); by implication, to tax, harass, tyrannize\nKJV: distress, driver, exact(-or), oppress(-or), [idiom] raiser of taxes, taskmaster.\n\nCognate Group: H5065 (distress)\nGreek Equivalents: G523 ἀπαιτέω, G1325 δίδωμι, G1643 ἐλαύνω, G1985 ἐπίσκοπος, G1988 ἐπιστάτης, G4238 πράσσω, G4317 προσάγω",
    5066: "נָגַשׁ (nâgash | naw-gash')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be or come (causatively, bring) near (for any purpose); euphemistically, to lie with a woman; as an enemy, to attack; religious to worship; causatively, to present; figuratively, to adduce an argument; by reversal, to stand back\nKJV: (make to) approach (nigh), bring (forth, hither, near), (cause to) come (hither, near, nigh), give place, go hard (up), (be, draw, go) near (nigh), offer, overtake, present, put, stand.\n\nCognate Group: H5066 (approach)\nGreek Equivalents: G654 ἀποστρέφω, G868 ἀφίστημι, G1011 βουλεύω, G1448 ἐγγίζω, G1525 εἰσέρχομαι, G1994 ἐπιστρέφω, G2638 καταλαμβάνω, G2853 κολλάω, G2961 κυριεύω, G4198 πορεύομαι, G4233 πράκτωρ, G4254 προάγω, G4317 προσάγω, G4331 προσεγγίζω, G4334 προσέρχομαι, G4365 προσπορεύομαι, G4374 προσφέρω, G5117 τόπος, G5342 φέρω",
    5067: "נֵד (nêd | nade)\n\nDerivation: from H5110 נוּד in the sense of piling up\nStrong's: a mound, i.e. wave\nKJV: heap.\n\nCognate Group: H5067 (heap)",
    5068: "נָדַב (nâdab | naw-dab')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to impel; hence, to volunteer (as a soldier), to present spontaneously\nKJV: offer freely, be (give, make, offer self) willing(-ly).\n\nCognate Group: H5070 (Nadab), H5072 (Nedabiah), H5071 (free offering), H5068 (offer freely), H5114 (Nodab), H5082 (soul), H5081 (free)\nGreek Equivalents: G1380 δοκέω, G1413 δυνάστης, G1595 ἑκούσιον, G5342 φέρω",
    5069: "נְדַב (nᵉdab | ned-ab')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5068 נָדַב\nStrong's: be (or give) liberal(-ly)\nKJV: (be minded of...own) freewill (offering), offer freely (willingly).\n\nCognate Group: H5069 (freewill), H5073 (row)",
    5070: "נָדָב (Nâdâb | naw-dawb')\n\nDerivation: from H5068 נָדַב ; liberal\nStrong's: Nadab, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Nadab.\n\nCognate Group: H5070 (Nadab), H5072 (Nedabiah), H5071 (free offering), H5068 (offer freely), H5114 (Nodab), H5082 (soul), H5081 (free)",
    5071: "נְדָבָה (nᵉdâbâh | ned-aw-baw')\n\nDerivation: from H5068 נָדַב\nStrong's: properly (abstractly) spontaneity, or (adjectively) spontaneous; also (concretely) a spontaneous or (by inference, in plural) abundant gift\nKJV: free(-will) offering, freely, plentiful, voluntary(-ily, offering), willing(-ly), offering).\n\nCognate Group: H5070 (Nadab), H5072 (Nedabiah), H5071 (free offering), H5068 (offer freely), H5114 (Nodab), H5082 (soul), H5081 (free)\nGreek Equivalents: G139 αἵρεσις, G1390 δόμα, G1595 ἑκούσιον, G3671 ὁμολογία, G4968 σφάγιον",
    5072: "נְדַבְיָה (Nᵉdabyâh | ned-ab-yaw')\n\nDerivation: from H5068 נָדַב and H3050 יָהּ ; largess of Jah\nStrong's: Nedabjah, an Israelite\nKJV: Nedabiah.\n\nCognate Group: H5070 (Nadab), H5072 (Nedabiah), H5071 (free offering), H5068 (offer freely), H5114 (Nodab), H5082 (soul), H5081 (free)",
    5073: "נִדְבָּךְ (nidbâk | nid-bawk')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from a root meaning to stick\nStrong's: a layer (of building materials)\nKJV: row.\n\nCognate Group: H5069 (freewill), H5073 (row)",
    5074: "נָדַד (nâdad | naw-dad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to wave to and fro (rarely to flap up and down); figuratively, to rove, flee, or (causatively) to drive away\nKJV: chase (away), [idiom] could not, depart, flee ([idiom] apace, away), (re-) move, thrust away, wander (abroad, -er, -ing).\n\nCognate Group: H5076 (tossing to and fro), H5074 (chase), H5206 (removed), H5079 ([idiom] far)\nGreek Equivalents: G868 ἀφίστημι, G948 βδελύσσω, G1309 διαφεύγω, G1839 ἐξίστημι, G1856 ἐξωθέω, G2064 ἔρχομαι, G2172 εὔχομαι, G2597 καταβαίνω, G4072 πέτομαι, G4107 πλανήτης, G4422 πτοέω, G5015 ταράσσω, G5343 φεύγω",
    5075: "נְדַד (nᵉdad | ned-ad')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5074 נָדַד\nStrong's: to depart\nKJV: go from.\n\nCognate Group: H5103 (river), H5085 (body), H5075 (go from), H5094 (light)\nGreek Equivalents: G1096 γίνομαι",
    5076: "נָדֻד (nâdud | naw-dood')\n\nDerivation: passive participle of H5074 נָדַד\nStrong's: properly, tossed; abstractly, a rolling (on the bed)\nKJV: tossing to and fro.\n\nCognate Group: H5076 (tossing to and fro), H5074 (chase), H5206 (removed), H5079 ([idiom] far)\nGreek Equivalents: G3601 ὀδύνη",
    5077: "נָדָה (nâdâh | naw-daw')\n\nDerivation: or נָדָא; (2 Kings 17:21), a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to toss; figuratively, to exclude, i.e. banish, postpone, prohibit\nKJV: cast out, drive, put far away.\n\nCognate Group: H5077 (cast out)\nGreek Equivalents: G1856 ἐξωθέω",
    5078: "נֵדֶה (nêdeh | nay'-deh)\n\nDerivation: from H5077 נָדָה in the sense of freely flinging money\nStrong's: a bounty (for prostitution)\nKJV: gifts.\n\nCognate Group: H5078 (gifts)\nGreek Equivalents: G3410 μίσθωμα",
    5079: "נִדָּה (niddâh | nid-daw')\n\nDerivation: from H5074 נָדַד\nStrong's: properly, rejection; by implication, impurity, especially personal (menstruation) or moral (idolatry, incest)\nKJV: [idiom] far, filthiness, [idiom] flowers, menstruous (woman), put apart, [idiom] removed (woman), separation, set apart, unclean(-ness, thing, with filthiness).\n\nCognate Group: H5076 (tossing to and fro), H5074 (chase), H5206 (removed), H5079 ([idiom] far)\nGreek Equivalents: G49 ἁγνισμός, G167 ἀκαθαρσία, G3334 μετακινέω, G4473 ῥαντισμός",
    5080: "נָדַח (nâdach | naw-dakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to push off; used in a great variety of applications, literally and figuratively (to expel, mislead, strike, inflict, etc.)\nKJV: banish, bring, cast down (out), chase, compel, draw away, drive (away, out, quite), fetch a stroke, force, go away, outcast, thrust away (out), withdraw.\n\nCognate Group: H5083 (gift), H4065 (cause of banishment), H5084 (sheath), H5080 (banish)\nGreek Equivalents: G548 ἄπειμι, G622 ἀπόλλυμι, G635 ἀποπλανάω, G683 ἀπωθέομαι, G868 ἀφίστημι, G1287 διασκορπίζω, G1289 διασπείρω, G1290 διασπορά, G1544 ἐκβάλλω, G1856 ἐξωθέω, G1911 ἐπιβάλλω, G4105 πλανάω, G4687 σπείρω, G5343 φεύγω",
    5081: "נָדִיב (nâdîyb | naw-deeb')\n\nDerivation: from H5068 נָדַב\nStrong's: properly, voluntary, i.e. generous; hence, magnanimous; as noun, a grandee (sometimes a tyrant)\nKJV: free, liberal (things), noble, prince, willing (hearted).\n\nCognate Group: H5070 (Nadab), H5072 (Nedabiah), H5071 (free offering), H5068 (offer freely), H5114 (Nodab), H5082 (soul), H5081 (free)\nGreek Equivalents: G757 ἄρχω, G758 ἄρχων, G935 βασιλεύς, G1342 δίκαιος, G2152 εὐσεβής, G4289 πρόθυμος",
    5082: "נְדִיבָה (nᵉdîybâh | ned-ee-baw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H5081 נָדִיב\nStrong's: properly, nobility, i.e. reputation\nKJV: soul.\n\nCognate Group: H5070 (Nadab), H5072 (Nedabiah), H5071 (free offering), H5068 (offer freely), H5114 (Nodab), H5082 (soul), H5081 (free)\nGreek Equivalents: G1680 ἐλπίς, G4908 συνετός",
    5083: "נָדָן (nâdân | naw-dawn')\n\nDerivation: probably from an unused root meaning to give\nStrong's: a present (for prostitution)\nKJV: gift.\n\nCognate Group: H5083 (gift), H4065 (cause of banishment), H5084 (sheath), H5080 (banish)\nGreek Equivalents: G3410 μίσθωμα",
    5084: "נָדָן (nâdân | naw-dawn')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a sheath (of a sword)\nKJV: sheath.\n\nCognate Group: H5083 (gift), H4065 (cause of banishment), H5084 (sheath), H5080 (banish)",
    5085: "נִדְנֶה (nidneh | nid-neh')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from the same as H5084 נָדָן\nStrong's: a sheath; figuratively, the body (as the receptacle of the soul)\nKJV: body.\n\nCognate Group: H5103 (river), H5085 (body), H5075 (go from), H5094 (light)",
    5086: "נָדַף (nâdaph | naw-daf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to shove asunder, i.e. disperse\nKJV: drive (away, to and fro), thrust down, shaken, tossed to and fro.\n\nCognate Group: H5086 (drive)\nGreek Equivalents: G1377 διώκω, G1587 ἐκλείπω, G1856 ἐξωθέω, G2795 κινέω, G3679 ὀνειδίζω, G3947 παροξύνω, G5342 φέρω",
    5087: "נָדַר (nâdar | naw-dar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to promise (pos., to do or give something to God)\nKJV: (make a) vow.\n\nCognate Group: H5088 (vow), H5087 (vow)\nGreek Equivalents: G2171 εὐχή, G2172 εὔχομαι, G3670 ὁμολογέω",
    5088: "נֶדֶר (neder | neh'-der)\n\nDerivation: or נֵדֶר; from H5087 נָדַר\nStrong's: a promise (to God); also (concretely) a thing promised\nKJV: vow(-ed).\n\nCognate Group: H5088 (vow), H5087 (vow)\nGreek Equivalents: G1435 δῶρον, G2171 εὐχή, G3671 ὁμολογία",
    5089: "נֹהַּ (nôahh | no'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to lament\nStrong's: lamentation\nKJV: wailing.\n\nCognate Group: H5089 (wailing)",
    5090: "נָהַג (nâhag | naw-hag')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to drive forth (a person, an animal or chariot), i.e. lead, carry away; reflexively, to proceed (i.e. impel or guide oneself); also (from the panting induced by effort), to sigh\nKJV: acquaint, bring (away), carry away, drive (away), lead (away, forth), (be) guide, lead (away, forth).\n\nCognate Group: H4491 (driving), H5090 (acquaint)\nGreek Equivalents: G71 ἄγω, G162 αἰχμαλωτεύω, G321 ἀνάγω, G520 ἀπάγω, G1521 εἰσάγω, G1863 ἐπάγω, G3870 παρακαλέω, G3880 παραλαμβάνω, G4165 ποιμαίνω",
    5091: "נָהָה (nâhâh | naw-haw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to groan, i.e. bewail; hence (through the idea of crying aloud), to assemble (as if on proclamation)\nKJV: lament, wail.\n\nCognate Group: H5204 (wailing), H5091 (lament), H5093 (doleful), H5092 (lamentation)\nGreek Equivalents: G1914 ἐπιβλέπω, G2354 θρηνέω",
    5092: "נְהִי (nᵉhîy | neh-hee')\n\nDerivation: from H5091 נָהָה\nStrong's: an elegy\nKJV: lamentation, wailing.\n\nCognate Group: H5204 (wailing), H5091 (lament), H5093 (doleful), H5092 (lamentation)\nGreek Equivalents: G2355 θρῆνος, G2870 κοπετός",
    5093: "נִהְיָה (nihyâh | nih-yaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H5092 נְהִי\nStrong's: lamentation\nKJV: doleful.\n\nCognate Group: H5204 (wailing), H5091 (lament), H5093 (doleful), H5092 (lamentation)",
    5094: "נְהִיר (nᵉhîyr | neh-heere')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) or נְהִירוּ; (Aramaic), from the same as H5105 נְהָרָה\nStrong's: illumination, i.e. (figuratively) wisdom\nKJV: light.\n\nCognate Group: H5103 (river), H5085 (body), H5075 (go from), H5094 (light)\nGreek Equivalents: G5457 φῶς",
    5095: "נָהַל (nâhal | naw-hal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to run with asparkle, i.e. flow; hence (transitively), to conduct, and (by inference) to protect, sustain\nKJV: carry, feed, guide, lead (gently, on).\n\nCognate Group: H5097 (bush), H5095 (carry), H5096 (Nahalal)\nGreek Equivalents: G71 ἄγω, G482 ἀντιλαμβάνομαι, G1625 ἐκτρέφω, G1765 ἐνισχύω, G2664 καταπαύω, G3870 παρακαλέω",
    5096: "נַהֲלָל (Nahălâl | nah-hal-awl')\n\nDerivation: or נַהֲלֹל; the same as H5097 נַהֲלֹל\nStrong's: Nahalal or Nahalol, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Nahalal, Nahallal, Nahalol.\n\nCognate Group: H5097 (bush), H5095 (carry), H5096 (Nahalal)",
    5097: "נַהֲלֹל (nahălôl | nah-hal-ole')\n\nDerivation: from H5095 נָהַל\nStrong's: pasture\nKJV: bush.\n\nCognate Group: H5097 (bush), H5095 (carry), H5096 (Nahalal)\nGreek Equivalents: G3586 ξύλον",
    5098: "נָהַם (nâham | naw-ham')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to growl\nKJV: mourn, roar(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H5099 (roaring), H5100 (disquietness), H5098 (mourn)\nGreek Equivalents: G994 βοάω, G1030 βρυγμός, G1980 ἐπισκέπτομαι, G2372 θυμός, G3338 μεταμέλλομαι, G3983 πεινάω",
    5099: "נַהַם (naham | nah'-ham)\n\nDerivation: from H5098 נָהַם\nStrong's: a snarl\nKJV: roaring.\n\nCognate Group: H5099 (roaring), H5100 (disquietness), H5098 (mourn)",
    5100: "נְהָמָה (nᵉhâmâh | neh-haw-maw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H5099 נַהַם\nStrong's: snarling\nKJV: disquietness, roaring.\n\nCognate Group: H5099 (roaring), H5100 (disquietness), H5098 (mourn)\nGreek Equivalents: G4726 στεναγμός, G5456 φωνή",
    5101: "נָהַק (nâhaq | naw-hak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to bray (as an ass), scream (from hunger)\nKJV: bray.\n\nCognate Group: H5101 (bray)\nGreek Equivalents: G994 βοάω, G2896 κράζω",
    5102: "נָהַר (nâhar | naw-har')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to sparkle, i.e. (figuratively) be cheerful; hence (from the sheen of a running stream) to flow, i.e. (figuratively) assemble\nKJV: flow (together), be lightened.\n\nCognate Group: H5104 (flood), H5102 (flow)\nGreek Equivalents: G2240 ἥκω, G4863 συνάγω, G5461 φωτίζω",
    5103: "נְהַר (nᵉhar | neh-har')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from a root corresponding to H5102 נָהַר\nStrong's: a river, especially the Euphrates\nKJV: river, stream.\n\nCognate Group: H5103 (river), H5085 (body), H5075 (go from), H5094 (light)",
    5104: "נָהָר (nâhâr | naw-hawr')\n\nDerivation: from H5102 נָהַר\nStrong's: a stream (including the sea; expectation the Nile, Euphrates, etc.); figuratively, prosperity\nKJV: flood, river.\n\nCognate Group: H5104 (flood), H5102 (flow)\nGreek Equivalents: G4215 ποταμός",
    5105: "נְהָרָה (nᵉhârâh | neh-haw-raw')\n\nDerivation: from H5102 נָהַר in its original sense\nStrong's: daylight\nKJV: light.\n\nCognate Group: H4492 (den), H5105 (light), H5102 (flow)\nGreek Equivalents: G5338 φέγγος",
    5106: "נוּא (nûwʼ | noo)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to refuse, forbid, dissuade, or neutralize\nKJV: break, disallow, discourage, make of none effect.\n\nCognate Group: H5106 (break), H8569 (breach of promise)\nGreek Equivalents: G114 ἀθετέω, G868 ἀφίστημι, G1294 διαστρέφω, G3310 μερίς",
    5107: "נוּב (nûwb | noob)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to germinate, i.e. (figuratively) to (causatively, make) flourish; also (of words), to utter\nKJV: bring forth (fruit), make cheerful, increase.\n\nCognate Group: H5107 (bring forth), H8570 (fruit), H5109 (Nebai), H5108 (fruit)\nGreek Equivalents: G4129 πληθύνω, G4482 ῥέω",
    5108: "נֹוב (nôwb | nobe)\n\nDerivation: or נֵיב; from H5107 נוּב\nStrong's: produce, literally or figuratively\nKJV: fruit.\n\nCognate Group: H5107 (bring forth), H8570 (fruit), H5109 (Nebai), H5108 (fruit)\nGreek Equivalents: G2007 ἐπιτίθημι",
    5109: "נֹובַי (Nôwbay | no-bah'ee)\n\nDerivation: from H5108 נֹוב ; fruitful\nStrong's: Nobai, an Israelite\nKJV: Nebai (from the margin).\n\nCognate Group: H5107 (bring forth), H8570 (fruit), H5109 (Nebai), H5108 (fruit)",
    5110: "נוּד (nûwd | nood)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to nod, i.e. waver; figuratively, to wander, flee, disappear; also (from shaking the head in sympathy), to console, deplore, or (from tossing the head in scorn) taunt\nKJV: bemoan, flee, get, mourn, make to move, take pity, remove, shake, skip for joy, be sorry, vagabond, way, wandering.\n\nCognate Group: H4493 (shaking), H5205 (moving), H5112 (wandering), H5113 (Nod), H5110 (bemoan)\nGreek Equivalents: G526 ἀπαλλοτριόω, G1168 δειλιάω, G2354 θρηνέω, G2795 κινέω, G3996 πενθέω, G4531 σαλεύω, G4727 στενάζω",
    5111: "נוּד (nûwd | nood)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H5116 נָוֶה\nStrong's: to flee\nKJV: get away.\n\nCognate Group: H5111 (get away), H5122 (dunghill), H5135 (fiery)",
    5112: "נֹוד (nôwd | node)Typo: lemma נֹודּ extra dagesh, corrected to נֹוד\n\nDerivation: (only defective נֹד); from H5110 נוּד\nStrong's: exile\nKJV: wandering.\n\nCognate Group: H4493 (shaking), H5205 (moving), H5112 (wandering), H5113 (Nod), H5110 (bemoan)",
    5113: "נֹוד (Nôwd | node)\n\nDerivation: the same as H5112 נֹוד \n\nTypo: lemma נֹודּ extra dagesh, corrected to נֹוד\n\n; vagrancy\nStrong's: Nod, the land of Cain\nKJV: Nod.\n\nCognate Group: H4493 (shaking), H5205 (moving), H5112 (wandering), H5113 (Nod), H5110 (bemoan)",
    5114: "נֹודָב (Nôwdâb | no-dawb')\n\nDerivation: from H5068 נָדַב ; noble\nStrong's: Nodab, an Arab tribe\nKJV: Nodab.\n\nCognate Group: H5070 (Nadab), H5072 (Nedabiah), H5071 (free offering), H5068 (offer freely), H5114 (Nodab), H5082 (soul), H5081 (free)",
    5115: "נָוָה (nâvâh | naw-vaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; (compare H5116 נָוֶה)\n\nStrong's: to rest (as at home); causatively (through the implied idea of beauty), to celebrate (with praises)\nKJV: keept at home, prepare an habitation.\n\nCognate Group: H5115 (keept at home)\nGreek Equivalents: G1392 δοξάζω",
    5116: "נָוֶה (nâveh | naw-veh')\n\nDerivation: or (feminine) נָוָה; from H5115 נָוָה\nStrong's: (adjectively) at home; hence (by implication of satisfaction) lovely; also (noun) a home, of God (temple), men (residence), flocks (pasture), or wild animals (den)\nKJV: comely, dwelling (place), fold, habitation, pleasant place, sheepcote, stable, tarried.\n\nCognate Group: H5116 (comely), H5115 (keept at home), H4999 (habitation), H5121 (Naioth)\nGreek Equivalents: G718 ἁρμόζω, G1886 ἔπαυλις, G2143 εὐπρέπεια, G2167 εὐφροσύνη, G2570 καλός, G2646 κατάλυμα, G3542 νομή, G4851 συμφέρω, G5117 τόπος",
    5117: "נוּחַ (nûwach | noo'-akh)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to rest, i.e. settle down; used in a great variety of applications, literal and figurative, intransitive, transitive and causative (to dwell, stay, let fall, place, let alone, withdraw, give comfort, etc.)\nKJV: cease, be confederate, lay, let down, (be) quiet, remain, (cause to, be at, give, have, make to) rest, set down. Compare H3241 יָנִים .\n\nCognate Group: H2680 (half of the Manahethites), H5183 (lighting down), H5118 (rest), H4494 (rest), H5119 (Nohah), H5146 (Noah), H5207 (sweet), H4495 (Manoah), H3240 (bestow), H5117 (cease), H4496 (comfortable), H2010 (release), H4506 (Manahath), H2679 (half of the Manahethites), H3239 (Janoah)\nGreek Equivalents: G372 ἀνάπαυσις, G372 ἀνάπαυσις, G373 ἀναπαύω, G373 ἀναπαύω, G659 ἀποτίθημι, G863 ἀφίημι, G1325 δίδωμι, G1439 ἐάω, G1705 ἐμπίπλημι, G1879 ἐπαναπαύομαι, G1950 ἐπιλανθάνομαι, G2007 ἐπιτίθημι, G2523 καθίζω, G2524 καθίημι, G2525 καθίστημι, G2641 καταλείπω, G2663 κατάπαυσις, G2664 καταπαύω, G3860 παραδίδωμι, G4855 συμφύω, G4863 συνάγω, G5087 τίθημι",
    5118: "נוּחַ (nûwach | noo'-akh)\n\nDerivation: or נֹוחַ; from H5117 נוּחַ\nStrong's: quiet\nKJV: rest(-ed, -ing place).\n\nCognate Group: H2680 (half of the Manahethites), H5183 (lighting down), H5118 (rest), H4494 (rest), H5119 (Nohah), H5146 (Noah), H5207 (sweet), H4495 (Manoah), H3240 (bestow), H5117 (cease), H4496 (comfortable), H2010 (release), H4506 (Manahath), H2679 (half of the Manahethites), H3239 (Janoah)",
    5119: "נֹוחָה (Nôwchâh | no-chaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H5118 נוּחַ ; quietude\nStrong's: Nochah, an Israelite\nKJV: Nohah.\n\nCognate Group: H2680 (half of the Manahethites), H5183 (lighting down), H5118 (rest), H4494 (rest), H5119 (Nohah), H5146 (Noah), H5207 (sweet), H4495 (Manoah), H3240 (bestow), H5117 (cease), H4496 (comfortable), H2010 (release), H4506 (Manahath), H2679 (half of the Manahethites), H3239 (Janoah)",
    5120: "נוּט (nûwṭ | noot)Cognate Group: H5120 (be moved)\n\nGreek Equivalents: G4531 σαλεύω \nStrong's: to quake\nKJV: be moved.",
    5121: "נָוִית (Nâvîyth | naw-veeth')\n\nDerivation: from H5115 נָוָה ; residence\nStrong's: Navith, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Naioth (from the margin).\n\nCognate Group: H5116 (comely), H5115 (keept at home), H4999 (habitation), H5121 (Naioth)",
    5122: "נְוָלוּ (nᵉvâlûw | nev-aw-loo')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) or נְוָלִי; (Aramaic), from an unused root probably meaning to be foul\nStrong's: a sink\nKJV: dunghill.\n\nCognate Group: H5111 (get away), H5122 (dunghill), H5135 (fiery)",
    5123: "נוּם (nûwm | noom)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to slumber (from drowsiness)\nKJV: sleep, slumber.\n\nCognate Group: H5123 (sleep), H5124 (drowsiness), H8572 (slumber), H3241 (Janum)\nGreek Equivalents: G402 ἀναχωρέω, G1265 διαμένω, G3573 νυστάζω, G4579 σείω",
    5124: "נוּמָה (nûwmâh | noo-maw')\n\nDerivation: from H5123 נוּם\nStrong's: sleepiness\nKJV: drowsiness.\n\nCognate Group: H5123 (sleep), H5124 (drowsiness), H8572 (slumber), H3241 (Janum)",
    5125: "נוּן (nûwn | noon)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to resprout, i.e. propagate by shoots; figuratively, to be perpetual\nKJV: be continued.\n\nCognate Group: H5125 (be continued), H5126 (Non), H5209 (son)",
    5126: "נוּן (Nûwn | noon)\n\nDerivation: or נֹון; (1 Chronicles 7:27), from H5125 נוּן ; perpetuity,\nStrong's: Nun or Non, the father of Joshua\nKJV: Non, Nun.\n\nCognate Group: H5125 (be continued), H5126 (Non), H5209 (son)",
    5127: "נוּס (nûwç | noos)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to flit, i.e. vanish away (subside, escape; causatively, chase, impel, deliver)\nKJV: [idiom] abate, away, be displayed, (make to) flee (away, -ing), put to flight, [idiom] hide, lift up a standard.\n\nCognate Group: H5127 ([idiom] abate), H4498 ([idiom] apace), H5211 (that fleeth), H4499 (fleeing)\nGreek Equivalents: G1309 διαφεύγω, G1377 διώκω, G1628 ἐκφεύγω, G2703 καταφεύγω, G2795 κινέω, G3334 μετακινέω, G4863 συνάγω, G5343 φεύγω, G5351 φθείρω, G5437 φυγή",
    5128: "נוּעַ (nûwaʻ | noo'-ah)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to waver, in a great variety of applications, literally and figuratively (as subjoined)\nKJV: continually, fugitive, [idiom] make, to (go) up and down, be gone away, (be) move(-able, -d), be promoted, reel, remove, scatter, set, shake, sift, stagger, to and fro, be vagabond, wag, (make) wander (up and down).\n\nCognate Group: H5128 (continually), H5269 (Neah), H4517 (cornet), H5270 (Noah)\nGreek Equivalents: G757 ἄρχω, G1287 διασκορπίζω, G1839 ἐξίστημι, G1869 ἐπαίρω, G2795 κινέω, G2827 κλίνω, G3039 λικμάω, G3334 μετακινέω, G4531 σαλεύω, G4579 σείω, G4867 συναθροίζω, G5399 φοβέω",
    5129: "נֹועַדְיָה (Nôwʻadyâh | no-ad-yaw')\n\nDerivation: from H3259 יָעַד and H3050 יָהּ ; convened of Jah\nStrong's: Noadjah, the name of an Israelite, and a false prophetess\nKJV: Noadiah.\n\nCognate Group: H5129 (Noadiah), H4151 (appointed time), H4150 (appointed), H3259 (agree), H4152 (appointed), H5712 (assembly), H3260 (Iddo See H3035)",
    5130: "נוּף (nûwph | noof)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to quiver (i.e. vibrate up and down, or rock to and fro); used in a great variety of applications (including sprinkling, beckoning, rubbing, bastinadoing, sawing, waving, etc.)\nKJV: lift up, move, offer, perfume, send, shake, sift, strike, wave.\n\nCognate Group: H5130 (lift up), H8573 (offering), H5299 (border)\nGreek Equivalents: G142 αἴρω, G399 ἀναφέρω, G591 ἀποδίδωμι, G851 ἀφαιρέω, G873 ἀφορίζω, G1670 ἑλκύω, G1869 ἐπαίρω, G1911 ἐπιβάλλω, G2007 ἐπιτίθημι, G2018 ἐπιφέρω, G3870 παρακαλέω, G5342 φέρω",
    5131: "נֹוף (nôwph | nofe)\n\nDerivation: from H5130 נוּף\nStrong's: elevation\nKJV: situation. Compare H5297 נֹף .\n\nCognate Group: H5316 (country), H5299 (border), H5131 (situation)",
    5132: "נוּץ (nûwts | noots)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to flash; hence, to blossom (from the brilliancy of color); also, to fly away (from the quickness of motion)\nKJV: flee away, bud (forth).\n\nCognate Group: H5133 (feather), H5132 (flee away)",
    5133: "נֹוצָה (nôwtsâh | no-tsaw')\n\nDerivation: or נֹצָה; feminine active participle of H5327 נָצָה in the sense of flying\nStrong's: a pinion (or wing feather); often (collectively) plumage\nKJV: feather(-s), ostrich.\n\nCognate Group: H5133 (feather), H5132 (flee away)",
    5134: "נוּק (nûwq | nook)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to suckle\nKJV: nurse.\n\nCognate Group: H5134 (nurse)",
    5135: "נוּר (nûwr | noor)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from an unused root (corresponding to that of H5216 נִיר) meaning to shine\nStrong's: fire\nKJV: fiery, fire.\n\nCognate Group: H5111 (get away), H5122 (dunghill), H5135 (fiery)\nGreek Equivalents: G4442 πῦρ",
    5136: "נוּשׁ (nûwsh | noosh)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be sick, i.e. (figuratively) distressed\nKJV: be full of heaviness.\n\nCognate Group: H5136 (be full of heaviness)\nGreek Equivalents: G5004 ταλαιπωρία",
    5137: "נָזָה (nâzâh | naw-zaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to spirt, i.e. besprinkle (especially in expiation)\nKJV: sprinkle.\n\nCognate Group: H3150 (Jeziah), H5137 (sprinkle)\nGreek Equivalents: G2296 θαυμάζω, G2609 κατάγω, G4472 ῥαντίζω",
    5138: "נָזִיד (nâzîyd | naw-zeed')\n\nDerivation: from H2102 זוּד\nStrong's: something boiled, i.e. soup\nKJV: pottage.\n\nCognate Group: H2121 (proud), H2132 (olive), H5138 (pottage), H2133 (Zethan), H2087 (presumptuously), H2102 (be proud), H2128 (Ziph), H2086 (presumptuous), H2129 (Ziphah), H2130 (Ziphim)",
    5139: "נָזִיר (nâzîyr | naw-zeer')\n\nDerivation: or נָזִר; from H5144 נָזַר\nStrong's: separate, i.e. consecrated (as prince, a Nazirite); hence (figuratively from the latter) an unpruned vine (like an unshorn Nazirite)\nKJV: Nazarite (by a false alliteration with Nazareth), separate(-d), vine undressed.\n\nCognate Group: H5139 (Nazarite), H5145 (consecration), H4502 (crowned), H5144 (consecrate)\nGreek Equivalents: G37 ἁγιάζω, G38 ἁγιασμός, G39 ἅγιον, G47 ἁγνεία, G1392 δοξάζω, G2233 ἡγέομαι",
    5140: "נָזַל (nâzal | naw-zal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to drip, or shed by trickling\nKJV: distil, drop, flood, (cause to) flow(-ing), gush out, melt, pour (down), running water, stream.\n\nCognate Group: H5140 (distil)\nGreek Equivalents: G1806 ἐξάγω, G1831 ἐξέρχομαι, G2597 καταβαίνω, G4482 ῥέω, G5080 τήκω, G5204 ὕδωρ",
    5141: "נֶזֶם (nezem | neh'-zem)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root of uncertain meaning\nStrong's: a nose-ring\nKJV: earring, jewel.\n\nCognate Group: H5143 (damage), H5141 (earring)",
    5142: "נְזַק (nᵉzaq | nez-ak')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to the root of H5143 נֵזֶק\nStrong's: to suffer (causatively, inflict) loss\nKJV: have (en-) damage, hurt(-ful).\n\nCognate Group: H5142 (have damage), H5174 (brass)\nGreek Equivalents: G2554 κακοποιέω",
    5143: "נֵזֶק (nêzeq | nay'zek)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to injure\nStrong's: loss\nKJV: damage.\n\nCognate Group: H5143 (damage), H5141 (earring)",
    5144: "נָזַר (nâzar | naw-zar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to hold aloof, i.e. (intransitivey) abstain (from food and drink, from impurity, and even from divine worship (i.e. apostatize)); specifically, to set apart (to sacred purposes), i.e. devote\nKJV: consecrate, separate(-ing, self).\n\nCognate Group: H5139 (Nazarite), H5145 (consecration), H4502 (crowned), H5144 (consecrate)\nGreek Equivalents: G37 ἁγιάζω, G48 ἁγνίζω, G526 ἀπαλλοτριόω, G2126 εὐλαβής, G2171 εὐχή, G2172 εὔχομαι, G4337 προσέχω",
    5145: "נֶזֶר (nezer | neh'-zer)\n\nDerivation: or נֵזֶר; from H5144 נָזַר\nStrong's: properly, something set apart, i.e. (abstractly) dedication (of a priet or Nazirite); hence (concretely) unshorn locks; also (by implication) a chaplet (especially of royalty)\nKJV: consecration, crown, hair, separation.\n\nCognate Group: H5139 (Nazarite), H5145 (consecration), H4502 (crowned), H5144 (consecrate)\nGreek Equivalents: G39 ἅγιον, G47 ἁγνεία, G49 ἁγνισμός",
    5146: "נֹחַ (Nôach | no'-akh)\n\nDerivation: the same as H5118 נוּחַ ; rest\nStrong's: Noach, the patriarch of the flood\nKJV: Noah.\n\nCognate Group: H2680 (half of the Manahethites), H5183 (lighting down), H5118 (rest), H4494 (rest), H5119 (Nohah), H5146 (Noah), H5207 (sweet), H4495 (Manoah), H3240 (bestow), H5117 (cease), H4496 (comfortable), H2010 (release), H4506 (Manahath), H2679 (half of the Manahethites), H3239 (Janoah)\nGreek Equivalents: G3575 Νῶε",
    5147: "נַחְבִּי (Nachbîy | nakh-bee')\n\nDerivation: from H2247 חָבָה ; occult\nStrong's: Nachbi, an Israelite\nKJV: Nakbi.\n\nCognate Group: H2252 (Habaiah), H2247 (hide), H2253 (hiding), H5147 (Nakbi)",
    5148: "נָחָה (nâchâh | naw-khaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to guide; by implication, to transport (into exile, or as colonists)\nKJV: bestow, bring, govern, guide, lead (forth), put, straiten.\n\nCognate Group: H5148 (bestow)\nGreek Equivalents: G71 ἄγω, G1166 δεικνύω, G1863 ἐπάγω, G2137 εὐοδόω, G3343 μεταπέμπω, G3870 παρακαλέω, G5087 τίθημι",
    5149: "נְחוּם (Nᵉchûwm | neh-khoom')\n\nDerivation: from H5162 נָחַם ; comforted\nStrong's: Nechum, an Israelite\nKJV: Nehum.\n\nCognate Group: H5164 (repentance), H5165 (comfort), H4505 (Menahem), H8575 (comfort), H5163 (Naham), H5151 (Nahum), H5149 (Nehum), H5150 (comfort), H5162 (comfort), H5167 (Nahamani), H5166 (Nehemiah), H8576 (Tanhumeth)",
    5150: "נִחוּם (nichûwm | nee-khoom')\n\nDerivation: or נִחֻם; from H5162 נָחַם\nStrong's: properly, consoled; abstractly, solace\nKJV: comfort(-able), repenting.\n\nCognate Group: H5164 (repentance), H5165 (comfort), H4505 (Menahem), H8575 (comfort), H5163 (Naham), H5151 (Nahum), H5149 (Nehum), H5150 (comfort), H5162 (comfort), H5167 (Nahamani), H5166 (Nehemiah), H8576 (Tanhumeth)",
    5151: "נַחוּם (Nachûwm | nakh-oom')\n\nDerivation: from H5162 נָחַם ; comfortable\nStrong's: Nachum, an Israelite prophet\nKJV: Nahum.\n\nCognate Group: H5164 (repentance), H5165 (comfort), H4505 (Menahem), H8575 (comfort), H5163 (Naham), H5151 (Nahum), H5149 (Nehum), H5150 (comfort), H5162 (comfort), H5167 (Nahamani), H5166 (Nehemiah), H8576 (Tanhumeth)\nGreek Equivalents: G3486 Ναούμ, G2584 Καπερναούμ",
    5152: "נָחֹור (Nâchôwr | naw-khore')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H5170 נַחַר ; snorer\nStrong's: Nochor, the name of the grandfather and a brother of Abraham\nKJV: Nahor.\n\nCognate Group: H5156 (nostrils), H5170 (nostrils), H5152 (Nahor), H5171 (Naharai)\nGreek Equivalents: G3493 Ναχώρ",
    5153: "נָחוּשׁ (nâchûwsh | naw-khoosh')\n\nDerivation: apparently passive participle of H5172 נָחַשׁ (perhaps in the sense of ringing), i.e. bell-metal; or from the red color of the throat of a serpent (H5175 נָחָשׁ, as denominative) when hissing)\nStrong's: coppery, i.e. (figuratively) hard\nKJV: of brass.\n\nCognate Group: H5178 (brasen), H5153 (of brass), H5180 (Nehushtan), H5154 (brass), H5179 (Nehushta)\nGreek Equivalents: G5470 χάλκεος",
    5154: "נְחוּשָׁה (nᵉchûwshâh | nekh-oo-shaw')\n\nDerivation: or נְחֻשָׁה; feminine of H5153 נָחוּשׁ\nStrong's: copper\nKJV: brass, steel. Compare H5176 נָחָשׁ .\n\nCognate Group: H5178 (brasen), H5153 (of brass), H5180 (Nehushtan), H5154 (brass), H5179 (Nehushta)\nGreek Equivalents: G5475 χαλκός",
    5155: "נְחִילָה (nᵉchîylâh | nekh-ee-law')\n\nDerivation: probably denominative from H2485 חָלִיל\nStrong's: a flute\nKJV: (plural) Nehiloth.\n\nCognate Group: H5157 (divide), H5159 (heritage), H5155 (Nehiloth)",
    5156: "נְחִיר (nᵉchîyr | nekh-eer')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H5170 נַחַר\nStrong's: a nostril\nKJV: (dual) nostrils.\n\nCognate Group: H5156 (nostrils), H5170 (nostrils), H5152 (Nahor), H5171 (Naharai)",
    5157: "נָחַל (nâchal | naw-khal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to inherit (as a (figurative) mode of descent), or (generally) to occupy; causatively, to bequeath, or (generally) distribute, instate\nKJV: divide, have (inheritance), take as a heritage, (cause to, give to, make to) inherit, (distribute for, divide (for, for an, by), give for, have, leave for, take (for)) inheritance, (have in, cause to, be made to) possess(-ion).\n\nCognate Group: H5157 (divide), H5159 (heritage), H5155 (Nehiloth)\nGreek Equivalents: G1244 διαιρέω, G1266 διαμερίζω, G1330 διέρχομαι, G1687 ἐμβατεύω, G1842 ἐξολοθρεύω, G2624 κατακληροδοτέω, G2624 κατακληροδοτέω, G2722 κατέχω, G2816 κληρονομέω, G2932 κτάομαι, G3307 μερίζω",
    5158: "נַחַל (nachal | nakh'-al)\n\nDerivation: or (feminine) נַחְלָה; (Psalm 124:4), or נַחֲלָה; (Ezekiel 47:19; Ezekiel 48:28), from H5157 נָחַל in its original sense\nStrong's: a stream, especially a winter torrent; (by implication) a (narrow) valley (in which a brook runs); also a shaft (of a mine)\nKJV: brook, flood, river, stream, valley.\n\nCognate Group: H5161 (Nehelamite), H5158 (brook), H5160 (Nahaliel)\nGreek Equivalents: G4215 ποταμός, G5327 φάραγξ, G5493 χείμαῤῥος",
    5159: "נַחֲלָה (nachălâh | nakh-al-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H5157 נָחַל (in its usual sense)\nStrong's: properly, something inherited, i.e. (abstractly) occupancy, or (concretely) an heirloom; generally an estate, patrimony or portion\nKJV: heritage, to inherit, inheritance, possession. Compare H5158 נַחַל .\n\nCognate Group: H5157 (divide), H5159 (heritage), H5155 (Nehiloth)\nGreek Equivalents: G1243 διαίρεσις, G2697 κατάσχεσις, G2817 κληρονομία, G2819 κλῆρος, G2820 κληρόω, G2933 κτῆμα, G3310 μερίς, G3313 μέρος, G3624 οἶκος, G5117 τόπος",
    5160: "נַחֲלִיאֵל (Nachălîyʼêl | nakh-al-ee-ale')\n\nDerivation: from H5158 נַחַל and H410 אֵל ; valley of God\nStrong's: Nachaliel, a place in the Desert\nKJV: Nahaliel.\n\nCognate Group: H5161 (Nehelamite), H5158 (brook), H5160 (Nahaliel)",
    5161: "נֶחֱלָמִי (Nechĕlâmîy | nekh-el-aw-mee')\n\nDerivation: apparently a patronymic from an unused name (apparently passive participle of H2492 חָלַם); dreamed\nStrong's: a Nechelamite, or descendant of Nechlam\nKJV: Nehelamite.\n\nCognate Group: H5161 (Nehelamite), H5158 (brook), H5160 (Nahaliel)",
    5162: "נָחַם (nâcham | naw-kham')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to sigh, i.e. breathe strongly; by implication, to be sorry, i.e. (in a favorable sense) to pity, console or (reflexively) rue; or (unfavorably) to avenge (oneself)\nKJV: comfort (self), ease (one's self), repent(-er,-ing, self).\n\nCognate Group: H5164 (repentance), H5165 (comfort), H4505 (Menahem), H8575 (comfort), H5163 (Naham), H5151 (Nahum), H5149 (Nehum), H5150 (comfort), H5162 (comfort), H5167 (Nahamani), H5166 (Nehemiah), H8576 (Tanhumeth)\nGreek Equivalents: G373 ἀναπαύω, G447 ἀνίημι, G1653 ἐλεέω, G1760 ἐνθυμέομαι, G2233 ἡγέομαι, G2296 θαυμάζω, G2373 θυμόω, G2433 ἱλάσκομαι, G2436 ἵλεως, G3338 μεταμέλλομαι, G3340 μετανοέω, G3870 παρακαλέω, G3874 παράκλησις, G3973 παύω",
    5163: "נַחַם (Nacham | nakh'-am)\n\nDerivation: from H5162 נָחַם ; consolation\nStrong's: Nacham, an Israelite\nKJV: Naham.\n\nCognate Group: H5164 (repentance), H5165 (comfort), H4505 (Menahem), H8575 (comfort), H5163 (Naham), H5151 (Nahum), H5149 (Nehum), H5150 (comfort), H5162 (comfort), H5167 (Nahamani), H5166 (Nehemiah), H8576 (Tanhumeth)",
    5164: "נֹחַם (nôcham | no'-kham)\n\nDerivation: from H5162 נָחַם\nStrong's: ruefulness, i.e. desistance\nKJV: repentance.\n\nCognate Group: H5164 (repentance), H5165 (comfort), H4505 (Menahem), H8575 (comfort), H5163 (Naham), H5151 (Nahum), H5149 (Nehum), H5150 (comfort), H5162 (comfort), H5167 (Nahamani), H5166 (Nehemiah), H8576 (Tanhumeth)",
    5165: "נֶחָמָה (nechâmâh | nekh-aw-maw')\n\nDerivation: from H5162 נָחַם\nStrong's: consolation\nKJV: comfort.\n\nCognate Group: H5164 (repentance), H5165 (comfort), H4505 (Menahem), H8575 (comfort), H5163 (Naham), H5151 (Nahum), H5149 (Nehum), H5150 (comfort), H5162 (comfort), H5167 (Nahamani), H5166 (Nehemiah), H8576 (Tanhumeth)",
