    6301: "פְּדָהצוּר (Pᵉdâhtsûwr | ped-aw-tsoor')\n\nDerivation: from H6299 פָּדָה and H6697 צוּר ; a rock (i.e. God) has ransomed\nStrong's: Pedahtsur, an Israelite\nKJV: Pedahzur.\n\nCognate Group: H6305 (Pedaiah), H6307 (Padan), H6306 (ransom), H6303 (Padon), H6299 ([idiom] at all), H6304 (division), H6301 (Pedahzur), H6302 (to be redeemed), H6300 (Pedahel)",
    6302: "פָּדוּי (pâdûwy | paw-doo'ee)\n\nDerivation: passive participle of H6299 פָּדָה . ransomed (and so occurring under H6299 פָּדָה)\nStrong's: as abstractly (in plural masculine) a ransom\nKJV: (that are) to be (that were) redeemed.\n\nCognate Group: H6305 (Pedaiah), H6307 (Padan), H6306 (ransom), H6303 (Padon), H6299 ([idiom] at all), H6304 (division), H6301 (Pedahzur), H6302 (to be redeemed), H6300 (Pedahel)",
    6303: "פָּדֹון (Pâdôwn | paw-done')\n\nDerivation: from H6299 פָּדָה ; ransom\nStrong's: Padon, one of the Nethinim.\nKJV: Padon.\n\nCognate Group: H6305 (Pedaiah), H6307 (Padan), H6306 (ransom), H6303 (Padon), H6299 ([idiom] at all), H6304 (division), H6301 (Pedahzur), H6302 (to be redeemed), H6300 (Pedahel)",
    6304: "פְּדוּת (pᵉdûwth | ped-ooth')\n\nDerivation: or פְּדֻת; from H6929 קֵדְמָה \n\nTypo: lemma קדְמָה missing vowel, corrected to קֵדְמָה\n\nStrong's: distinction; also deliverance\nKJV: division, redeem, redemption.\n\nCognate Group: H6305 (Pedaiah), H6307 (Padan), H6306 (ransom), H6303 (Padon), H6299 ([idiom] at all), H6304 (division), H6301 (Pedahzur), H6302 (to be redeemed), H6300 (Pedahel)\nGreek Equivalents: G1293 διαστολή, G3085 λύτρωσις",
    6305: "פְּדָיָה (Pᵉdâyâh | ped-aw-yaw')\n\nDerivation: or פְּדָיָהוּ; from H6299 פָּדָה and H3050 יָהּ ; Jah has ransomed\nStrong's: Pedajah, the name of six Israelites\nKJV: Pedaiah.\n\nCognate Group: H6305 (Pedaiah), H6307 (Padan), H6306 (ransom), H6303 (Padon), H6299 ([idiom] at all), H6304 (division), H6301 (Pedahzur), H6302 (to be redeemed), H6300 (Pedahel)\nGreek Equivalents: G2090 ἑτοιμάζω",
    6306: "פִּדְיֹום (pidyôwm | pid-yome')\n\nDerivation: or פִּדְיֹם; also פִּדְיֹון; or פִּדְיֹן; from H6299 פָּדָה\nStrong's: a ransom\nKJV: ransom, that were redeemed, redemption.\n\nCognate Group: H6305 (Pedaiah), H6307 (Padan), H6306 (ransom), H6303 (Padon), H6299 ([idiom] at all), H6304 (division), H6301 (Pedahzur), H6302 (to be redeemed), H6300 (Pedahel)\nGreek Equivalents: G3083 λύτρον, G3085 λύτρωσις",
    6307: "פַּדָּן (Paddân | pad-dawn')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to extend; a plateau; or פַּדַּן אֲרָם; from the same and H758 אֲרָם ; the table-land of Aram\nStrong's: Paddan or Paddan-Aram, a region of Syria\nKJV: Padan, Padan-aram.\n\nCognate Group: H6305 (Pedaiah), H6307 (Padan), H6306 (ransom), H6303 (Padon), H6299 ([idiom] at all), H6304 (division), H6301 (Pedahzur), H6302 (to be redeemed), H6300 (Pedahel)",
    6308: "פָּדַע (pâdaʻ | paw-dah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to retrieve\nKJV: deliver.\n\nCognate Group: H6308 (deliver), H6309 (fat)\nGreek Equivalents: G472 ἀντέχομαι",
    6309: "פֶּדֶר (peder | peh'der)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to be greasy\nStrong's: suet\nKJV: fat.\n\nCognate Group: H6308 (deliver), H6309 (fat)",
    6310: "פֶּה (peh | peh)\n\nDerivation: from H6284 פָּאָה\nStrong's: the mouth (as the means of blowing), whether literal or figurative (particularly speech); specifically edge, portion or side; adverbially (with preposition) according to\nKJV: accord(-ing as, -ing to), after, appointment, assent, collar, command(-ment), [idiom] eat, edge, end, entry, [phrase] file, hole, [idiom] in, mind, mouth, part, portion, [idiom] (should) say(-ing), sentence, skirt, sound, speech, [idiom] spoken, talk, tenor, [idiom] to, [phrase] two-edged, wish, word.\n\nCognate Group: H6374 (tooth), H6310 (accord), H6366 (edge)\nGreek Equivalents: G1035 βρῶσις, G1100 γλῶσσα, G1411 δύναμις, G2372 θυμός, G3056 λόγος, G4383 πρόσωπον, G4487 ῥῆμα, G4750 στόμα, G5408 φόνος, G5456 φωνή, G5491 χεῖλος",
    6311: "פֹּה (pôh | po)\n\nDerivation: or פֹּא; (Job 38:11), or פֹּו; probably from a primitive inseparable particle 'p' (of demonstrative force) and H1931 הוּא\nStrong's: this place (French ici), i.e. here or hence\nKJV: here, hither, the one (other, this, that) side.\n\nCognate Group: H6312 (Phuvah), H6311 (here), H6324 (Punites)\nGreek Equivalents: G847 αὐτοῦ, G1782 ἐντεῦθεν, G1782 ἐντεῦθεν, G1782 ἐντεῦθεν, G5602 ὧδε",
    6312: "פּוּאָה (Pûwʼâh | poo-aw')\n\nDerivation: or פֻּוָּה; from H6284 פָּאָה ; a blast\nStrong's: Puah or Puvvah, the name of two Israelites\nKJV: Phuvah, Pua, Puah.\n\nCognate Group: H6312 (Phuvah), H6311 (here), H6324 (Punites)",
    6313: "פּוּג (pûwg | poog)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be sluggish\nKJV: cease, be feeble, faint, be slacked.\n\nCognate Group: H6314 (rest), H6313 (cease), H2014 (intermission)\nGreek Equivalents: G1839 ἐξίστημι, G2559 κακόω",
    6314: "פּוּגָה (pûwgâh | poo-gaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6313 פּוּג\nStrong's: intermission\nKJV: rest.\n\nCognate Group: H6314 (rest), H6313 (cease), H2014 (intermission)",
    6315: "פּוּחַ (pûwach | poo'akh)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to puff, i.e. blow with the breath or air; hence, to fan (as a breeze), to utter, to kindle (a fire), to scoff\nKJV: blow (upon), break, puff, bring into a snare, speak, utter.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)\nGreek Equivalents: G393 ἀνατέλλω, G1458 ἐγκαλέω, G1572 ἐκκαίω, G1720 ἐμφυσάω, G1925 ἐπιδείκνυμι, G2634 κατακυριεύω",
    6316: "פּוּט (Pûwṭ | poot)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin\nStrong's: Put, a son of Ham, also the name of his descendants or their region, and of a Persian tribe\nKJV: Phut, Put.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)",
    6317: "פּוּטִיאֵל (Pûwṭîyʼêl | poo-tee-ale')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root (probably meaning to disparage) and H410 אֵל ; contempt of God\nStrong's: Putiel, an Israelite\nKJV: Putiel.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)",
    6318: "פֹּוטִיפַר (Pôwṭîyphar | po-tee-far')\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Potiphar, an Egyptian\nKJV: Potiphar.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)",
    6319: "פֹּוטִי פֶרַע (Pôwṭîy Pheraʻ | po-tee feh'-rah)\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Poti-Phera, an Egyptian\nKJV: Poti-pherah.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)",
    6320: "פּוּךְ (pûwk | pook)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to paint\nStrong's: dye (specifically, stibium for the eyes)\nKJV: fair colours, glistering, paint(-ed) (-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)\nGreek Equivalents: G4185 πολυτελής",
    6321: "פֹּול (pôwl | pole)Typo: lemma פֹול missing dagesh, corrected to פֹּול\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to be thick\nStrong's: a bean (as plump)\n\nKJV: beans.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)",
    6322: "פּוּל (Pûwl | pool)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin\nStrong's: Pul, the name of an Assyrian king and of an Ethiopian tribe\nKJV: Pul.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)",
    6323: "פּוּן (pûwn | poon)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root meaning to turn, i.e.\nStrong's: be perplexed\nKJV: be distracted.\n\nCognate Group: H6323 (be distracted), H6326 (Puah), H6325 (Punon)\nGreek Equivalents: G1820 ἐξαπορέομαι",
    6324: "פּוּנִי (Pûwnîy | poo-nee')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from an unused name meaning a turn\nStrong's: a Punite (collectively) or descendants of an unknown Pun\nKJV: Punites.\n\nCognate Group: H6312 (Phuvah), H6311 (here), H6324 (Punites)",
    6325: "פּוּנֹן (Pûwnôn | poo-none')\n\nDerivation: from H6323 פּוּן ; perplexity\nStrong's: Punon, a place in the Desert\nKJV: Punon.\n\nCognate Group: H6323 (be distracted), H6326 (Puah), H6325 (Punon)",
    6326: "פּוּעָה (Pûwʻâh | poo-aw')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to glitter; brilliancy\nStrong's: Puah, an Israelitess\nKJV: Puah.\n\nCognate Group: H6323 (be distracted), H6326 (Puah), H6325 (Punon)",
    6327: "פּוּץ (pûwts | poots)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to dash in pieces, literally or figuratively (especially to disperse)\nKJV: break (dash, shake) in (to) pieces, cast (abroad), disperse (selves), drive, retire, scatter (abroad), spread abroad.\n\nCognate Group: H8600 (dispersion), H6327 (break in pieces), H4650 (maul)\nGreek Equivalents: G649 ἀποστέλλω, G1272 διανοίγω, G1287 διασκορπίζω, G1288 διασπάω, G1289 διασπείρω, G1720 ἐμφυσάω, G2875 κόπτω, G4650 σκορπίζω, G4687 σπείρω",
    6328: "פּוּק (pûwq | pook)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to waver\nKJV: stumble, move.\n\nCognate Group: H6330 (grief), H6328 (stumble), H6375 (smite together)\nGreek Equivalents: G1459 ἐγκαταλείπω",
    6329: "פּוּק (pûwq | pook)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (identical with H6328 פּוּק through the idea of dropping out; compare H5312 נְפַק)\nStrong's: to issue, i.e. furnish; causatively, to secure; figuratively, to succeed\nKJV: afford, draw out, further, get, obtain.\n\nCognate Group: H6332 (Pur), H6329 (afford)\nGreek Equivalents: G1325 δίδωμι, G2090 ἑτοιμάζω, G2147 εὑρίσκω, G2795 κινέω, G2983 λαμβάνω",
    6330: "פּוּקָה (pûwqâh | poo-kaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6328 פּוּק\nStrong's: a stumbling-block\nKJV: grief.\n\nCognate Group: H6330 (grief), H6328 (stumble), H6375 (smite together)",
    6331: "פּוּר (pûwr | poor)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to crush\nKJV: break, bring to nought, [idiom] utterly take.\n\nCognate Group: H6565 ([idiom] any ways), H6331 (break)",
    6332: "פּוּר (Pûwr | poor)\n\nDerivation: also (plural) פּוּרִים; or פֻּרִים; from H6331 פּוּר\nStrong's: a lot (as by means of a broken piece)\nKJV: Pur, Purim.\n\nCognate Group: H6332 (Pur), H6329 (afford)",
    6333: "פּוּרָה (pûwrâh | poo-raw')\n\nDerivation: from H6331 פּוּר\nStrong's: a wine-press (as crushing the grapes)\nKJV: winepress.\n\nCognate Group: H6333 (winepress), H6517 (pan)",
    6334: "פֹּורָתָא (Pôwrâthâʼ | po-raw-thaw')\n\nDerivation: of Persian origin\nStrong's: Poratha, a son of Haman\nKJV: Poratha.\n\nCognate Group: H6334 (Poratha)",
    6335: "פּוּשׁ (pûwsh | poosh)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to spread; figuratively, act proudly\nKJV: grow up, be grown fat, spread selves, be scattered.\n\nCognate Group: H6335 (grow up)\nGreek Equivalents: G522 ἀπαίρω, G4640 σκιρτάω",
    6336: "פּוּתִי (Pûwthîy | poo-thee')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from an unused name meaning a hinge\nStrong's: a Puthite (collectively) or descendants of an unknown Puth\nKJV: Puhites (as if from H6312 פּוּאָה).\n\nCognate Group: H6335 (grow up), H6336 (Puhites)",
    6337: "פָּז (pâz | pawz)\n\nDerivation: from H6338 פָּזַז\nStrong's: pure (gold); hence, gold itself (as refined)\nKJV: fine (pure) gold.\n\nCognate Group: H6337 (fine gold), H6338 (best)\nGreek Equivalents: G3037 λίθος, G5093 τίμιος, G5116 τοπάζιον, G5552 χρύσεος, G5553 χρυσίον",
    6338: "פָּזַז (pâzaz | paw-zaz')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to refine (gold)\nKJV: best (gold).\n\nCognate Group: H6337 (fine gold), H6338 (best)\nGreek Equivalents: G1384 δόκιμος",
    6339: "פָּזַז (pâzaz | paw-zaz')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (identical with H6338 פָּזַז)\nStrong's: to solidify (as if by refining); also to spring (as if separating the limbs)\nKJV: leap, be made strong.\n\nCognate Group: H6339 (leap)\nGreek Equivalents: G1590 ἐκλύω, G3738 ὀρχέομαι",
    6340: "פָּזַר (pâzar | paw-zar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to scatter, whether in enmity or bounty\nKJV: disperse, scatter (abroad).\n\nCognate Group: H6340 (disperse)\nGreek Equivalents: G1287 διασκορπίζω, G1289 διασπείρω, G4105 πλανάω, G4650 σκορπίζω, G4687 σπείρω",
    6341: "פַּח (pach | pakh)\n\nDerivation: from H6351 פָּחַח\nStrong's: a (metallic) sheet (as pounded thin); also a spring net (as spread out like a lamina)\nKJV: gin, (thin) plate, snare.\n\nCognate Group: H6341 (gin), H6351 (be snared)\nGreek Equivalents: G3013 λεπίς, G3803 παγίς, G5132 τράπεζα",
    6342: "פָּחַד (pâchad | paw-kkad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be startled (by a sudden alarm); hence, to fear in general\nKJV: be afraid, stand in awe, (be in) fear, make to shake.\n\nCognate Group: H6343 (dread), H6342 (be afraid), H6344 (stone), H6345 (fear), H6346 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G868 ἀφίστημι, G1168 δειλιάω, G1286 διασείω, G1788 ἐντρέπω, G1839 ἐξίστημι, G2125 εὐλαβέομαι, G3871 παρακαλύπτω, G4422 πτοέω, G5399 φοβέω, G5431 φροντίζω",
    6343: "פַּחַד (pachad | pakh'-ad)\n\nDerivation: from H6342 פָּחַד\nStrong's: a (sudden) alarm (properly, the object feared, by implication, the feeling)\nKJV: dread(-ful), fear, (thing) great (fear, -ly feared), terror.\n\nCognate Group: H6343 (dread), H6342 (be afraid), H6344 (stone), H6345 (fear), H6346 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G870 ἀφόβως, G1611 ἔκστασις, G2285 θάμβος, G2351 θόρυβος, G3639 ὄλεθρος, G4423 πτόησις, G5156 τρόμος, G5401 φόβος",
    6344: "פַּחַד (pachad | pakh'-ad)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6343 פַּחַד\nStrong's: a testicle (as a cause of shame akin to fear)\nKJV: stone.\n\nCognate Group: H6343 (dread), H6342 (be afraid), H6344 (stone), H6345 (fear), H6346 (captain)",
    6345: "פַּחְדָּה (pachdâh | pakh-daw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6343 פַּחַד\nStrong's: alarm (i.e. awe)\nKJV: fear.\n\nCognate Group: H6343 (dread), H6342 (be afraid), H6344 (stone), H6345 (fear), H6346 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G2106 εὐδοκέω",
    6346: "פֶּחָה (pechâh | peh-khaw')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin\nStrong's: a prefect (of a city or small district)\nKJV: captain, deputy, governor.\n\nCognate Group: H6343 (dread), H6342 (be afraid), H6344 (stone), H6345 (fear), H6346 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G758 ἄρχων, G2232 ἡγεμών, G2233 ἡγέομαι",
    6347: "פֶּחָה (pechâh | peh-khaw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6346 פֶּחָה \nStrong's: a prefect (of a city or small district)\nKJV: captain, governor.\n\nCognate Group: H6361 (hose), H6353 (potter), H6347 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G3623 οἰκονόμος",
    6348: "פָּחַז (pâchaz | paw-khaz')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to bubble up or froth (as boiling water), i.e. (figuratively) to be unimportant\nKJV: light.\n\nCognate Group: H6350 (lightness), H6349 (unstable), H6348 (light)\nGreek Equivalents: G2284 θαμβέω",
    6349: "פַּחַז (pachaz | pakh'-az)\n\nDerivation: from H6348 פָּחַז\nStrong's: ebullition, i.e. froth (figuratively, lust)\nKJV: unstable.\n\nCognate Group: H6350 (lightness), H6349 (unstable), H6348 (light)",
    6350: "פַּחֲזוּת (pachăzûwth | pakh-az-ooth')\n\nDerivation: from H6348 פָּחַז\nStrong's: frivolity\nKJV: lightness.\n\nCognate Group: H6350 (lightness), H6349 (unstable), H6348 (light)",
    6351: "פָּחַח (pâchach | paw-khakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; to batter out; but used only as denominative from H6341 פַּח,\nStrong's: to spread a net\nKJV: be snared.\n\nCognate Group: H6341 (gin), H6351 (be snared)",
    6352: "פֶּחָם (pechâm | peh-khawm')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from an unused root probably meaning to be black\nStrong's: a coal, whether charred or live\nKJV: coals.\n\nCognate Group: H6352 (coals)\nGreek Equivalents: G440 ἄνθραξ",
    6353: "פֶּחָר (pechâr | peh-khawr')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from an unused root probably meaning to fashion\nStrong's: a potter\nKJV: potter.\n\nCognate Group: H6361 (hose), H6353 (potter), H6347 (captain)\nGreek Equivalents: G2764 κεραμικός",
    6354: "פַּחַת (pachath | pakh'-ath)\n\nDerivation: probably from an unused root apparently meaning to dig\nStrong's: a pit, especially forcatching animals\nKJV: hole, pit, snare.\n\nCognate Group: H6360 (hammer), H6357 (topaz), H6355 (Pahathmoab), H6356 (fret inward), H6354 (hole)\nGreek Equivalents: G999 βόθυνος, G5490 χάσμα",
    6355: "פַּחַת מֹואָב (Pachath Môwʼâb | pakh'-ath mo-awb')\n\nDerivation: from H6354 פַּחַת and H4124 מֹואָב ; pit of Moab\nStrong's: Pachath-Moab, an Israelite\nKJV: Pahathmoab.\n\nCognate Group: H6360 (hammer), H6357 (topaz), H6355 (Pahathmoab), H6356 (fret inward), H6354 (hole)",
    6356: "פְּחֶתֶת (pᵉchetheth | pekh-eh'-theth)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6354 פַּחַת\nStrong's: a hole (by mildew in a garment)\nKJV: fret inward.\n\nCognate Group: H6360 (hammer), H6357 (topaz), H6355 (Pahathmoab), H6356 (fret inward), H6354 (hole)",
    6357: "פִּטְדָה (piṭdâh | pit-daw')\n\nDerivation: of foreign derivation\nStrong's: a gem, probably the topaz\nKJV: topaz.\n\nCognate Group: H6360 (hammer), H6357 (topaz), H6355 (Pahathmoab), H6356 (fret inward), H6354 (hole)\nGreek Equivalents: G5116 τοπάζιον",
    6358: "פָּטוּר (pâṭûwr | paw-toor')\n\nDerivation: passive participle of H6362 פָּטַר\nStrong's: opened, i.e. (as noun) a bud\nKJV: open.\n\nCognate Group: H6362 (dismiss), H6358 (open), H6367 (Pi-hahiroth), H6363 (firstling), H6364 (Pi-beseth), H6359 (free)",
    6359: "פָּטִיר (pâṭîyr | paw-teer')\n\nDerivation: from H6362 פָּטַר\nStrong's: open, i.e. unoccupied\nKJV: free.\n\nCognate Group: H6362 (dismiss), H6358 (open), H6367 (Pi-hahiroth), H6363 (firstling), H6364 (Pi-beseth), H6359 (free)",
    6360: "פַּטִּישׁ (paṭṭîysh | pat-teesh')\n\nDerivation: intensively from an unused root meaning to pound\nStrong's: a hammer\nKJV: hammer.\n\nCognate Group: H6360 (hammer), H6357 (topaz), H6355 (Pahathmoab), H6356 (fret inward), H6354 (hole)",
    6361: "פַּטִּישׁ (paṭṭîysh | pat-teesh')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from a root corresponding to that of H6360 פַּטִּישׁ\nStrong's: a gown (as if hammered out wide)\nKJV: hose.\n\nCognate Group: H6361 (hose), H6353 (potter), H6347 (captain)",
    6362: "פָּטַר (pâṭar | paw-tar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to cleave or burst through, i.e. (causatively) to emit, whether literal or figurative (gape)\nKJV: dismiss, free, let (shoot) out, slip away.\n\nCognate Group: H6362 (dismiss), H6358 (open), H6367 (Pi-hahiroth), H6363 (firstling), H6364 (Pi-beseth), H6359 (free)",
    6363: "פֶּטֶר (peṭer | peh'-ter)\n\nDerivation: or פִּטְרָה; from H6362 פָּטַר\nStrong's: a fissure, i.e. (concretely) firstling (as opening the matrix)\nKJV: firstling, openeth, such as open.\n\nCognate Group: H6362 (dismiss), H6358 (open), H6367 (Pi-hahiroth), H6363 (firstling), H6364 (Pi-beseth), H6359 (free)\nGreek Equivalents: G1272 διανοίγω, G4416 πρωτότοκος",
    6364: "פִּי־בֶסֶת (Pîy-Beçeth | pee beh'-seth)\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian origin\nStrong's: Pi-Beseth, a place in Egypt\nKJV: Pi-beseth.\n\nCognate Group: H6362 (dismiss), H6358 (open), H6367 (Pi-hahiroth), H6363 (firstling), H6364 (Pi-beseth), H6359 (free)",
    6365: "פִּיד (pîyd | peed)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root probably meaning to pierce\nStrong's: (figuratively) misfortune\nKJV: destruction, ruin.\n\nCognate Group: H6369 (Phichol), H6365 (destruction)\nGreek Equivalents: G4430 πτῶμα, G5098 τιμωρία",
    6366: "פֵּיָה (pêyâh | pay-aw')\n\nDerivation: or פִּיָּה; feminine of H6310 פֶּה\nStrong's: an edge\nKJV: (two-) edge(-d).\n\nCognate Group: H6374 (tooth), H6310 (accord), H6366 (edge)\nGreek Equivalents: G1366 δίστομος",
    6367: "פִּי הַחִירֹת (Pîy ha-Chîyrôth | pee hah-khee-roth')\n\nTypo: lemma פִּי הַחִרֹת yod, corrected to פִּי הַחִירֹת\n\nTypo: xlit Pi corrected to Pîy\n\nDerivation: from H6310 פֶּה and the feminine plural of a noun (from the same root as H2356 חֹור), with the article interpolated; mouth of the gorges\nStrong's: Pi-ha-Chiroth, a place in Egypt\nKJV: Pi-hahiroth. (In Numbers 14:19 without Pi-.)\n\nCognate Group: H6362 (dismiss), H6358 (open), H6367 (Pi-hahiroth), H6363 (firstling), H6364 (Pi-beseth), H6359 (free)",
    6368: "פִּיחַ (pîyach | pee'-akh)\n\nDerivation: from H6315 פּוּחַ\nStrong's: a powder (as easily puffed away), i.e. ashes or dust\nKJV: ashes.\n\nCognate Group: H6368 (ashes), H6315 (blow), H6322 (Pul), H6319 (Poti-pherah), H6317 (Putiel), H6321 (beans), H6320 (fair colours), H6318 (Potiphar), H6316 (Phut)",
    6369: "פִּיכֹל (Pîykôl | pee-kole')\n\nDerivation: apparently from H6310 פֶּה and H3605 כֹּל ; mouth of all\nStrong's: Picol, a Philistine\nKJV: Phichol.\n\nCognate Group: H6369 (Phichol), H6365 (destruction)",
    6370: "פִּילֶגֶשׁ (pîylegesh | pee-leh'-ghesh)\n\nDerivation: or פִּלֶגֶשׁ; of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a concubine; also (masculine) a paramour\nKJV: concubine, paramour.\n\nCognate Group: H6391 (division), H6385 (divide), H6388 (river), H4653 (division), H6394 (Pildash), H6393 (torch), H6370 (concubine), H6390 (division), H6389 (Peleg)\nGreek Equivalents: G1135 γυνή",
    6371: "פִּימָה (pîymâh | pee-maw')\n\nDerivation: probably from an unused root meaning to be plump\nStrong's: obesity\nKJV: collops.\n\nCognate Group: H6372 (Phinehas), H6373 (Pinon), H6377 (Pithon), H6376 (Pison), H6371 (collops)",
    6372: "פִּינְחָס (Pîynᵉchâç | pee-nekh-aws')\n\nDerivation: apparently from H6310 פֶּה and a variation of H5175 נָחָשׁ ; mouth of a serpent\nStrong's: Pinechas, the name of three Israelites\nKJV: Phinehas.\n\nCognate Group: H6372 (Phinehas), H6373 (Pinon), H6377 (Pithon), H6376 (Pison), H6371 (collops)",
    6373: "פִּינֹן (pîynôn | pee-none')\n\nDerivation: probably the same as H6325 פּוּנֹן\nStrong's: Pinon, an Idumaean\nKJV: Pinon.\n\nCognate Group: H6372 (Phinehas), H6373 (Pinon), H6377 (Pithon), H6376 (Pison), H6371 (collops)",
