    6524: "פָּרַח (pârach | paw-rakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to break forth as a bud, i.e. bloom; generally, to spread; specifically, to fly (as extending the wings); figuratively, to flourish\nKJV: [idiom] abroad, [idiom] abundantly, blossom, break forth (out), bud, flourish, make fly, grow, spread, spring (up).\n\nCognate Group: H6524 ([idiom] abroad), H667 (young), H6526 (youth), H6525 (blossom)\nGreek Equivalents: G330 ἀναθάλλω, G393 ἀνατέλλω, G985 βλαστάνω, G1817 ἐξανίστημι, G2476 ἵστημι, G2592 καρποφορέω",
    6525: "פֶּרַח (perach | peh'-rakh)\n\nDerivation: from H6524 פָּרַח\nStrong's: a calyx (natural or artificial); generally, bloom\nKJV: blossom, bud, flower.\n\nCognate Group: H6524 ([idiom] abroad), H667 (young), H6526 (youth), H6525 (blossom)\nGreek Equivalents: G438 ἄνθος, G2918 κρίνον",
    6526: "פִּרְחַח (pirchach | pir-khakh')\n\nDerivation: from H6524 פָּרַח\nStrong's: progeny, i.e. a brood\nKJV: youth.\n\nCognate Group: H6524 ([idiom] abroad), H667 (young), H6526 (youth), H6525 (blossom)",
    6527: "פָּרַט (pâraṭ | paw-rat')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to scatter words, i.e. prate (or hum)\nKJV: chant.\n\nCognate Group: H6528 (grape), H6527 (chant)",
    6528: "פֶּרֶט (pereṭ | peh'-ret)\n\nDerivation: from H6527 פָּרַט\nStrong's: a stray or single berry\nKJV: grape.\n\nCognate Group: H6528 (grape), H6527 (chant)",
    6529: "פְּרִי (pᵉrîy | per-ee')\n\nDerivation: from H6509 פָּרָה\nStrong's: fruit (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: bough, (first-)fruit(-ful), reward.\n\nCognate Group: H6500 (be fruitful), H6518 (village), H6513 (Phurah), H6509 (bear), H6516 (Parvaim), H6503 (Parbar), H6529 (bough)\nGreek Equivalents: G1549 ἔκγονον, G1588 ἐκλεκτός, G2129 εὐλογία, G2590 καρπός, G2593 καρποφόρος, G5043 τέκνον",
    6530: "פְּרִיץ (pᵉrîyts | per-eets')\n\nDerivation: from H6555 פָּרַץ \n\nTypo: lemma פּרַץ missing vowel, corrected to פָּרַץ\n\nStrong's: violent, i.e. a tyrant\nKJV: destroyer, ravenous, robber.\n\nCognate Group: H6530 (destroyer), H6558 (Pharzites), H6560 (Perezuzza), H6557 (Perez), H6559 (Perazim), H6556 (breach), H6555 ([idiom] abroad)\nGreek Equivalents: G3027 λῃστής, G3061 λοιμός, G4642 σκληρός",
    6531: "פֶּרֶךְ (perek | peh'-rek)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to break apart\nStrong's: fracture, i.e. severity\nKJV: cruelty, rigour.\n\nCognate Group: H6531 (cruelty)\nGreek Equivalents: G970 βία, G3449 μόχθος",
    6532: "פֹּרֶכֶת (pôreketh | po-reh'-keth)\n\nDerivation: feminine active participle of the same as H6531 פֶּרֶךְ\nStrong's: a separatrix, i.e. (the sacred) screen\nKJV: vail.\n\nCognate Group: H6532 (vail)\nGreek Equivalents: G2665 καταπέτασμα",
    6533: "פָּרַם (pâram | paw-ram')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to tear\nKJV: rend.\n\nCognate Group: H6542 (Persian), H6535 (Parnach), H6534 (Parmasta), H6533 (rend), H6539 (Persia)\nGreek Equivalents: G3886 παραλύω",
    6534: "פַּרְמַשְׁתָּא (Parmashtâʼ | par-mash-taw')\n\nDerivation: of Persian origin\nStrong's: Parmashta, a son of Haman\nKJV: Parmasta.\n\nCognate Group: H6542 (Persian), H6535 (Parnach), H6534 (Parmasta), H6533 (rend), H6539 (Persia)",
    6535: "פַּרְנַךְ (Parnak | par-nak')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Parnak, an Israelite\nKJV: Parnach.\n\nCognate Group: H6542 (Persian), H6535 (Parnach), H6534 (Parmasta), H6533 (rend), H6539 (Persia)",
    6536: "פָּרַס (pâraç | paw-ras')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to break in pieces, i.e. (usually without violence) to split, distribute\nKJV: deal, divide, have hoofs, part, tear.\n\nCognate Group: H6536 (deal), H6541 (claw), H6538 (claw)",
    6537: "פְּרַס (pᵉraç | per-as')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6536 פָּרַס\nStrong's: to split up\nKJV: divide, (U-) pharsin.\n\nCognate Group: H6540 (Persia), H6537 (divide), H6543 (Persian)\nGreek Equivalents: G1244 διαιρέω",
    6538: "פֶּרֶס (pereç | peh'-res)\n\nDerivation: from H6536 פָּרַס\nStrong's: a claw; also a kind of eagle\nKJV: claw, ossifrage.\n\nCognate Group: H6536 (deal), H6541 (claw), H6538 (claw)\nGreek Equivalents: G5329 Φάρες",
    6539: "פָּרַס (Pâraç | paw-ras')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin\nStrong's: Paras (i.e. Persia), an Eastern country, including its inhabitants\nKJV: Persia, Persians.\n\nCognate Group: H6542 (Persian), H6535 (Parnach), H6534 (Parmasta), H6533 (rend), H6539 (Persia)",
    6540: "פָּרַס (Pâraç | paw-ras')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6539 פָּרַס \nStrong's: Paras (i.e. Persia), an Eastern country, including its inhabitants\nKJV: Persia, Persians.\n\nCognate Group: H6540 (Persia), H6537 (divide), H6543 (Persian)",
    6541: "פַּרְסָה (parçâh | par-saw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6538 פֶּרֶס\nStrong's: a claw or split hoof\nKJV: claw, (cloven-) footed, hoof.\n\nCognate Group: H6536 (deal), H6541 (claw), H6538 (claw)\nGreek Equivalents: G2487 ἴχνος, G4228 πούς",
    6542: "פַּרְסִי (Parçîy | par-see')\n\nDerivation: patrial from H6539 פָּרַס\nStrong's: a Parsite (i.e. Persian), or inhabitant of Peres\nKJV: Persian.\n\nCognate Group: H6542 (Persian), H6535 (Parnach), H6534 (Parmasta), H6533 (rend), H6539 (Persia)",
    6543: "פַּרְסִי (Parçîy | par-see')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6542 פַּרְסִי \nStrong's: a Parsite (i.e. Persian), or inhabitant of Peres\nKJV: Persian.\n\nCognate Group: H6540 (Persia), H6537 (divide), H6543 (Persian)",
    6544: "פָּרַע (pâraʻ | paw-rah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to loosen; by implication, to expose, dismiss; figuratively, absolve, begin\nKJV: avenge, avoid, bare, go back, let, (make) naked, set at nought, perish, refuse, uncover.\n\nCognate Group: H6552 (Pirathon), H6544 (avenge), H6546 (avenging), H6553 (Pirathonite)\nGreek Equivalents: G177 ἀκατακάλυπτος, G601 ἀποκαλύπτω, G683 ἀπωθέομαι, G757 ἄρχω, G851 ἀφαιρέω, G1291 διαστέλλομαι, G1291 διαστέλλομαι, G1294 διαστρέφω",
    6545: "פֶּרַע (peraʻ | peh'-rah)\n\nDerivation: from H6544 פָּרַע\nStrong's: the hair (as dishevelled)\nKJV: locks.\n\nCognate Group: H6545 (locks)\nGreek Equivalents: G747 ἀρχηγός, G758 ἄρχων, G2864 κόμη",
    6546: "פַּרְעָה (parʻâh | par-aw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6545 פֶּרַע (in the sense of beginning)\nStrong's: leadership (plural concretely, leaders)\nKJV: [phrase] avenging, revenge.\n\nCognate Group: H6552 (Pirathon), H6544 (avenge), H6546 (avenging), H6553 (Pirathonite)",
    6547: "פַּרְעֹה (Parʻôh | par-o')\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Paroh, a general title of Egyptian kings\nKJV: Pharaoh.\n\nCognate Group: H6547 (Pharaoh), H6554 (Pharpar), H6544 (avenge), H6551 (Parosh), H6550 (flea)\nGreek Equivalents: G5328 Φαραώ",
    6548: "פַּרְעֹה חׇפְרַע (Parʻôh Chophraʻ | par-o' khof-rah')\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Paroh-Chophra, an Egyptian king\nKJV: Pharaoh-hophra.\n\nCognate Group: H6548 (Pharaoh-hophra), H2659 (be ashamed)",
    6549: "פַּרְעֹה נְכֹה (Parʻôh Nᵉkôh | par-o' nek-o')\n\nDerivation: or פַּרְעֹה נְכֹו; of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Paroh-Nekoh (or -Neko), an Egyptian king\nKJV: Pharaoh-necho, Pharaohnechoh.\n\nCognate Group: H5223 (contrite), H5222 (abject), H6549 (Pharaoh-necho), H5221 (beat), H3559 (certain), H5224 (Necho), H4347 (beaten)",
    6550: "פַּרְעֹשׁ (parʻôsh | par-oshe')\n\nDerivation: probably from H6544 פָּרַע and H6211 עָשׁ\nStrong's: a flea (as the isolated insect)\nKJV: flea.\n\nCognate Group: H6547 (Pharaoh), H6554 (Pharpar), H6544 (avenge), H6551 (Parosh), H6550 (flea)",
    6551: "פַּרְעֹשׁ (Parʻôsh | par-oshe')\n\nDerivation: the same as H6550 פַּרְעֹשׁ\nStrong's: Parosh, the name of our Israelite\nKJV: Parosh, Pharosh.\n\nCognate Group: H6547 (Pharaoh), H6554 (Pharpar), H6544 (avenge), H6551 (Parosh), H6550 (flea)",
    6552: "פִּרְעָתֹון (Pirʻâthôwn | pir-aw-thone')\n\nDerivation: from H6546 פַּרְעָה ; chieftaincy\nStrong's: Pirathon, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Pirathon.\n\nCognate Group: H6552 (Pirathon), H6544 (avenge), H6546 (avenging), H6553 (Pirathonite)",
    6553: "פִּרְעָתֹונִי (Pirʻâthôwnîy | pir-aw-tho-nee')\n\nDerivation: or פִּרְעָתֹנִי; patrial from H6552 פִּרְעָתֹון\nStrong's: a Pirathonite or inhabitant of Pirathon\nKJV: Pirathonite.\n\nCognate Group: H6552 (Pirathon), H6544 (avenge), H6546 (avenging), H6553 (Pirathonite)",
    6554: "פַּרְפַּר (Parpar | par-par')\n\nDerivation: probably from H6565 פָּרַר in the sense of rushing; rapid\nStrong's: Parpar, a river of Syria\nKJV: Pharpar.\n\nCognate Group: H6547 (Pharaoh), H6554 (Pharpar), H6544 (avenge), H6551 (Parosh), H6550 (flea)",
    6555: "פָּרַץ (pârats | paw-rats')Typo: lemma פּרַץ missing vowel, corrected to פָּרַץ\n\nGreek Equivalents: G402 ἀναχωρέω, G622 ἀπόλλυμι, G837 αὐξάνω, G971 βιάζω, G1291 διαστέλλομαι, G1600 ἐκπετάννυμι, G2137 εὐοδόω, G2352 θραύω, G2480 ἰσχύω, G2507 καθαιρέω, G2598 καταβάλλω, G2679 κατασκάπτω, G2720 κατευθύνω, G4115 πλατύνω, G4129 πληθύνω, G4147 πλουτέω \n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to break out (in many applications, direct and indirect, literal and figurative)\n\nKJV: [idiom] abroad, (make a) breach, break (away, down, -er, forth, in, up), burst out, come (spread) abroad, compel, disperse, grow, increase, open, press, scatter, urge.\n\nCognate Group: H6530 (destroyer), H6558 (Pharzites), H6560 (Perezuzza), H6557 (Perez), H6559 (Perazim), H6556 (breach), H6555 ([idiom] abroad)",
    6556: "פֶּרֶץ (perets | peh'-rets)\n\nDerivation: from H6555 פָּרַץ \n\nTypo: lemma פּרַץ missing vowel, corrected to פָּרַץ\n\nStrong's: a break (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: breach, breaking forth (in), [idiom] forth, gap.\n\nCognate Group: H6530 (destroyer), H6558 (Pharzites), H6560 (Perezuzza), H6557 (Perez), H6559 (Perazim), H6556 (breach), H6555 ([idiom] abroad)\nGreek Equivalents: G4098 πίπτω, G4430 πτῶμα, G5418 φραγμός",
    6557: "פֶּרֶץ (Perets | peh'-rets)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6556 פֶּרֶץ\nStrong's: Perets, the name of two Israelites\nKJV: Perez, Pharez.\n\nCognate Group: H6530 (destroyer), H6558 (Pharzites), H6560 (Perezuzza), H6557 (Perez), H6559 (Perazim), H6556 (breach), H6555 ([idiom] abroad)\nGreek Equivalents: G5329 Φάρες",
    6558: "פַּרְצִי (Partsîy | par-tsee')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from H6557 פֶּרֶץ\nStrong's: a Partsite (collectively) or descendants of Perets\nKJV: Pharzites.\n\nCognate Group: H6530 (destroyer), H6558 (Pharzites), H6560 (Perezuzza), H6557 (Perez), H6559 (Perazim), H6556 (breach), H6555 ([idiom] abroad)",
    6559: "פְּרָצִים (pᵉrâtsîym | per-aw-tseem')\n\nDerivation: plural of H6556 פֶּרֶץ ; breaks\nStrong's: Peratsim, a mountain in Palestine\nKJV: Perazim.\n\nCognate Group: H6530 (destroyer), H6558 (Pharzites), H6560 (Perezuzza), H6557 (Perez), H6559 (Perazim), H6556 (breach), H6555 ([idiom] abroad)",
    6560: "פֶּרֶץ עֻזָּא (Perets ʻUzzâʼ | peh'-rets ooz-zaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6556 פֶּרֶץ and H5798 עֻזָּא ; break of Uzza\nStrong's: Perets-Uzza, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Perezuzza.\n\nCognate Group: H6530 (destroyer), H6558 (Pharzites), H6560 (Perezuzza), H6557 (Perez), H6559 (Perazim), H6556 (breach), H6555 ([idiom] abroad)",
    6561: "פָּרַק (pâraq | paw-rak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to break off or crunch; figuratively, to deliver\nKJV: break (off), deliver, redeem, rend (in pieces), tear in pieces.\n\nCognate Group: H6561 (break), H6563 (crossway), H4665 (neck), H6564 (broth)\nGreek Equivalents: G1262 διαλύω, G1590 ἐκλύω, G1612 ἐκστρέφω, G3084 λυτρόω, G4014 περιαιρέω",
    6562: "פְּרַק (pᵉraq | per-ak')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6561 פָּרַק\nStrong's: to discontinue\nKJV: break off.\n\nCognate Group: H6562 (break off)",
    6563: "פֶּרֶק (pereq | peh'-rek)\n\nDerivation: from H6561 פָּרַק\nStrong's: rapine; also a fork (in roads)\nKJV: crossway, robbery.\n\nCognate Group: H6561 (break), H6563 (crossway), H4665 (neck), H6564 (broth)\nGreek Equivalents: G93 ἀδικία",
    6564: "פָּרָק (pârâq | paw-rawk')\n\nDerivation: from H6561 פָּרַק\nStrong's: soup (as full of crumbed meat)\nKJV: broth. See also H4832 מַרְפֵּא .\n\nCognate Group: H6561 (break), H6563 (crossway), H4665 (neck), H6564 (broth)",
    6565: "פָּרַר (pârar | paw-rar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to break up (usually figuratively), i.e. to violate, frustrate\nKJV: [idiom] any ways, break (asunder), cast off, cause to cease, [idiom] clean, defeat, disannul, disappoint, dissolve, divide, make of none effect, fail, frustrate, bring (come) to nought, [idiom] utterly, make void.\n\nCognate Group: H6565 ([idiom] any ways), H6331 (break)\nGreek Equivalents: G142 αἴρω, G639 ἀπορέω, G640 ἀπορία, G654 ἀποστρέφω, G1259 διαλλάσσω, G1696 ἐμμένω, G2819 κλῆρος, G2901 κραταιόω, G3306 μένω, G3845 παραβαίνω, G4014 περιαιρέω",
    6566: "פָּרַשׂ (pâras | paw-ras')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to break apart, disperse, etc.\nKJV: break, chop in pieces, lay open, scatter, spread (abroad, forth, selves, out), stretch (forth, out).\n\nCognate Group: H6566 (break), H6576 (spread), H4666 (that which)\nGreek Equivalents: G142 αἴρω, G380 ἀναπτύσσω, G447 ἀνίημι, G455 ἀνοίγω, G906 βάλλω, G1272 διανοίγω, G1289 διασπείρω, G1291 διαστέλλομαι, G1600 ἐκπετάννυμι, G1614 ἐκτείνω, G1911 ἐπιβάλλω, G1943 ἐπικαλύπτω, G2806 κλάω, G3935 παρίημι, G4016 περιβάλλω",
    6567: "פָּרָשׁ (pârâsh | paw-rash')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to separate, literally (to disperse) or figuratively (to specify); also (by implication) to wound\nKJV: scatter, declare, distinctly, shew, sting.\n\nCognate Group: H6567 (scatter), H6575 (declaration)\nGreek Equivalents: G1252 διακρίνω, G1316 διαχωρίζομαι, G1321 διδάσκω, G2447 ἰός, G5330 Φαρισαῖος",
    6568: "פְּרַשׁ (pᵉrash | per-ash')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6567 פָּרָשׁ\nStrong's: to specify\nKJV: distinctly.\n\nCognate Group: H6573 (copy), H6568 (distinctly)",
    6569: "פֶּרֶשׁ (peresh | peh'-resh)\n\nDerivation: from H6567 פָּרָשׁ\nStrong's: excrement (as eliminated)\nKJV: dung.\n\nCognate Group: H6570 (Peresh), H6569 (dung)",
    6570: "פֶּרֶשׁ (Peresh | peh'-resh)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6569 פֶּרֶשׁ\nStrong's: Peresh, an Israelite\nKJV: Peresh.\n\nCognate Group: H6570 (Peresh), H6569 (dung)",
    6571: "פָּרָשׁ (pârâsh | paw-rawsh')\n\nDerivation: from H6567 פָּרָשׁ ; (compare H5483 סוּס)\n\nStrong's: a steed (as stretched out to a vehicle, not single nor for mounting); also (by implication) a driver (in a chariot), i.e. (collectively) cavalry\nKJV: horseman.\n\nCognate Group: H6571 (horseman), H6578 (Euphrates), H6574 (dirt), H6579 (noble), H6572 (copy), H6577 (Parshandatha)\nGreek Equivalents: G2460 ἱππεύς, G2462 ἵππος",
    6572: "פַּרְשֶׁגֶן (parshegen | par-sheh'-ghen)\n\nDerivation: or פַּתְשֶׁגֶן; of foreign origin\nStrong's: a transcript\nKJV: copy.\n\nCognate Group: H6571 (horseman), H6578 (Euphrates), H6574 (dirt), H6579 (noble), H6572 (copy), H6577 (Parshandatha)\nGreek Equivalents: G1296 διαταγή",
    6573: "פַּרְשֶׁגֶן (parshegen | par-sheh'-ghen)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6572 פַּרְשֶׁגֶן \nStrong's: a transcript\nKJV: copy.\n\nCognate Group: H6573 (copy), H6568 (distinctly)",
    6574: "פַּרְשְׁדֹן (parshᵉdôn | par-shed-one')\n\nDerivation: perhaps by compounding H6567 פָּרָשׁ and H6504 פָּרַד (in the sense of straddling) (compare H6576 פַּרְשֵׁז)\nStrong's: the crotch (or anus)\nKJV: dirt.\n\nCognate Group: H6571 (horseman), H6578 (Euphrates), H6574 (dirt), H6579 (noble), H6572 (copy), H6577 (Parshandatha)",
    6575: "פָּרָשָׁה (pârâshâh | paw-raw-shaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6567 פָּרָשׁ\nStrong's: exposition\nKJV: declaration, sum.\n\nCognate Group: H6567 (scatter), H6575 (declaration)\nGreek Equivalents: G1860 ἐπαγγελία",
    6576: "פַּרְשֵׁז (parshêz | par-shaze')\n\nDerivation: a root apparently formed by compounding H6567 פָּרָשׁ and that of H6518 פָּרָז (compare H6574 פַּרְשְׁדֹן)\nStrong's: to expand\nKJV: spread.\n\nCognate Group: H6566 (break), H6576 (spread), H4666 (that which)\nGreek Equivalents: G1600 ἐκπετάννυμι",
    6577: "פַּרְשַׁנְדָּתָא (Parshandâthâʼ | par-shan-daw-thaw')\n\nDerivation: of Persian origin\nStrong's: Parshandatha, a son of Haman\nKJV: Parshandatha.\n\nCognate Group: H6571 (horseman), H6578 (Euphrates), H6574 (dirt), H6579 (noble), H6572 (copy), H6577 (Parshandatha)",
    6578: "פְּרָת (Pᵉrâth | per-awth')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to break forth; rushing\nStrong's: Perath (i.e. Euphrates), a river of the East\nKJV: Euphrates.\n\nCognate Group: H6571 (horseman), H6578 (Euphrates), H6574 (dirt), H6579 (noble), H6572 (copy), H6577 (Parshandatha)\nGreek Equivalents: G2166 Εὐφράτης",
    6579: "פַּרְתַּם (partam | par-tam')\n\nDerivation: of Persian origin\nStrong's: a grandee\nKJV: (most) noble, prince.\n\nCognate Group: H6571 (horseman), H6578 (Euphrates), H6574 (dirt), H6579 (noble), H6572 (copy), H6577 (Parshandatha)\nGreek Equivalents: G1741 ἔνδοξος",
    6580: "פַּשׁ (pash | pash)\n\nDerivation: probably from an unused root meaning to disintegrate\nStrong's: stupidity (as a result of grossness or of degeneracy)\nKJV: extremity.\n\nCognate Group: H6580 (extremity), H6589 (open)",
    6581: "פָּשָׂה (pâsâh | paw-saw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to spread\nKJV: spread.\n\nCognate Group: H6581 (spread)\nGreek Equivalents: G3328 μεταβάλλω",
    6582: "פָּשַׁח (pâshach | paw-shakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to tear in pieces\nKJV: pull in pieces.\n\nCognate Group: H6582 (pull in pieces), H6583 (Pashur)",
    6583: "פַּשְׁחוּר (Pashchûwr | pash-khoor')\n\nDerivation: probably from H6582 פָּשַׁח ; liberation\nStrong's: Pashchur, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Pashur.\n\nCognate Group: H6582 (pull in pieces), H6583 (Pashur)",
    6584: "פָּשַׁט (pâshaṭ | paw-shat')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to spread out (i.e. deploy in hostile array); by analogy, to strip (i.e. unclothe, plunder, flay, etc.)\nKJV: fall upon, flay, invade, make an invasion, pull off, put off, make a road, run upon, rush, set, spoil, spread selves (abroad), strip (off, self).\n\nCognate Group: H6584 (fall upon)\nGreek Equivalents: G851 ἀφαιρέω, G1194 δέρω, G1562 ἐκδύω, G1614 ἐκτείνω, G1632 ἐκχέω, G2007 ἐπιτίθημι, G2944 κυκλόω, G3729 ὁρμάω",
    6585: "פָּשַׂע (pâsaʻ | paw-sah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to stride (from spreading the legs), i.e. rush upon\nKJV: go.\n\nCognate Group: H6585 (go), H4667 (buttocks), H6587 (step)",
    6586: "פָּשַׁע (pâshaʻ | paw-shah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (identical with through the idea of expansion)\nStrong's: to break away (from just authority), i.e. trespass, apostatize, quarrel\nKJV: offend, rebel, revolt, transgress(-ion, -or).\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)\nGreek Equivalents: G91 ἀδικέω, G114 ἀθετέω, G264 ἁμαρτάνω, G266 ἁμαρτία, G458 ἀνομία, G459 ἄνομος, G764 ἀσεβέω, G765 ἀσεβής, G868 ἀφίστημι, G3845 παραβαίνω, G4105 πλανάω, G4160 ποιέω",
    6587: "פֶּשַׂע (pesaʻ | peh'-sah)\n\nDerivation: from H6585 פָּשַׂע\nStrong's: a stride\nKJV: step.\n\nCognate Group: H6585 (go), H4667 (buttocks), H6587 (step)",
    6588: "פֶּשַׁע (peshaʻ | peh'-shah)\n\nDerivation: from H6586 פָּשַׁע\nStrong's: a revolt (national, moral or religious)\nKJV: rebellion, sin, transgression, trespass.\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)\nGreek Equivalents: G52 ἄγνοια, G92 ἀδίκημα, G93 ἀδικία, G115 ἀθέτησις, G265 ἁμάρτημα, G266 ἁμαρτία, G458 ἀνομία, G763 ἀσέβεια, G3900 παράπτωμα, G4106 πλάνη",
    6589: "פָּשַׂק (pâsaq | paw-sak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to dispart (the feet or lips), i.e. become licentious\nKJV: open (wide).\n\nCognate Group: H6580 (extremity), H6589 (open)\nGreek Equivalents: G71 ἄγω, G1236 διάγω, G4312 προπετής",
    6590: "פְּשַׁר (pᵉshar | pesh-ar')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6622 פָּתַר\nStrong's: to interpret\nKJV: make (interpretations), interpreting.\n\nCognate Group: H6590 (make), H6591 (interpretation), H6600 (answer)",
    6591: "פְּשַׁר (pᵉshar | pesh-ar')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from H6590 פְּשַׁר\nStrong's: an interpretation\nKJV: interpretation.\n\nCognate Group: H6590 (make), H6591 (interpretation), H6600 (answer)\nGreek Equivalents: G1538 ἕκαστος, G2917 κρίμα, G2920 κρίσις",
    6592: "פֵּשֶׁר (pêsher | pay'-sher)\n\nDerivation: corresponding to H6591 פְּשַׁר \nStrong's: an interpretation\nKJV: interpretation.\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)\nGreek Equivalents: G3080 λύσις",
    6593: "פִּשְׁתֶּה (pishteh | pish-teh')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6580 פַּשׁ as in the sense of comminuting\nStrong's: linen (i.e. the thread, as carded)\nKJV: flax, linen.\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)\nGreek Equivalents: G3043 λίνον, G3608 ὀθόνιον",
    6594: "פִּשְׁתָּה (pishtâh | pish-taw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6593 פִּשְׁתֶּה\nStrong's: flax; by implication, a wick\nKJV: flax, tow.\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)\nGreek Equivalents: G3043 λίνον",
    6595: "פַּת (path | path)\n\nDerivation: from H6626 פָּתַת\nStrong's: a bit\nKJV: meat, morsel, piece.\n\nCognate Group: H6626 (participle x), H6595 (meat)\nGreek Equivalents: G740 ἄρτος, G1163 δεῖ, G1173 δεῖπνον, G2801 κλάσμα",
    6596: "פֹּת (pôth | pohth)\n\nDerivation: or פֹּתָה; (Ezekiel 13:19), from an unused root meaning to open\nStrong's: a hole, i.e. hinge or the female pudenda\nKJV: hinge, secret participle\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)\nGreek Equivalents: G4976 σχῆμα",
    6597: "פִּתְאֹום (pithʼôwm | pith-ome')\n\nDerivation: or פִּתְאֹם; from H6621 פֶּתַע\nStrong's: instantly\nKJV: straightway, sudden(-ly).\n\nCognate Group: H6621 (at an instant), H6597 (straightway)\nGreek Equivalents: G869 ἄφνω, G1810 ἐξαίφνης, G1819 ἐξάπινα, G1904 ἐπέρχομαι, G2112 εὐθέως, G3916 παραχρῆμα",
    6598: "פַּתְבַּג (pathbag | pathbag')\n\nDerivation: of Persian origin\nStrong's: a dainty\nKJV: portion (provision) of meat.\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)",
    6599: "פִּתְגָּם (pithgâm | pith-gawm')\n\nDerivation: of Persian origin\nStrong's: a (judicial) sentence\nKJV: decree, sentence.\n\nCognate Group: H6593 (flax), H6599 (decree), H6596 (hinge), H6592 (interpretation), H6586 (offend), H6588 (rebellion), H6598 (portion of meat), H6594 (flax)",
    6600: "פִּתְגָּם (pithgâm | pith-gawm')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6599 פִּתְגָּם\nStrong's: a word, answer, letter or decree\nKJV: answer, letter, matter, word.\n\nCognate Group: H6590 (make), H6591 (interpretation), H6600 (answer)\nGreek Equivalents: G2003 ἐπιταγή, G3056 λόγος, G3551 νόμος, G4487 ῥῆμα",
    6601: "פָּתָה (pâthâh | paw-thaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to open, i.e. be (causatively, make) roomy; usually figuratively (in a mental or moral sense) to be (causatively, make) simple or (in a sinister way) delude\nKJV: allure, deceive, enlarge, entice, flatter, persuade, silly (one).\n\nCognate Group: H6615 (simple), H6604 (Pethor), H6602 (Pethuel), H6601 (allure), H6612 (foolish), H3315 (Japheth)\nGreek Equivalents: G25 ἀγαπάω, G538 ἀπατάω, G1811 ἐξακολουθέω, G4105 πλανάω, G4115 πλατύνω",
    6602: "פְּתוּאֵל (Pᵉthûwʼêl | peth-oo-ale')\n\nDerivation: from H6601 פָּתָה and H410 אֵל ; enlarged of God\nStrong's: Pethuel, an Israelite\nKJV: Pethuel.\n\nCognate Group: H6615 (simple), H6604 (Pethor), H6602 (Pethuel), H6601 (allure), H6612 (foolish), H3315 (Japheth)",
    6603: "פִּתּוּחַ (pittûwach | pit-too'-akh)\n\nDerivation: or פִּתֻּחַ ; passive participle of H6605 פָּתַח\nStrong's: sculpture (in low or high relief or even intaglio)\nKJV: carved (work) (are, en-) grave(-ing, -n).\n\nCognate Group: H6605 (appear), H6614 (stomacher), H6603 (carved grave)",
    6604: "פְּתֹור (Pᵉthôwr | peth-ore')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin\nStrong's: Pethor, a place in Mesopotamia\nKJV: Pethor.\n\nCognate Group: H6615 (simple), H6604 (Pethor), H6602 (Pethuel), H6601 (allure), H6612 (foolish), H3315 (Japheth)",
    6605: "פָּתַח (pâthach | paw-thakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to open wide (literally or figuratively); specifically, to loosen, begin, plough, carve\nKJV: appear, break forth, draw (out), let go free, (en-) grave(-n), loose (self), (be, be set) open(-ing), put off, ungird, unstop, have vent.\n\nCognate Group: H6608 (entrance), H5318 (Neptoah), H4668 (key), H6605 (appear), H6607 (door), H4669 (opening), H6611 (Pethakiah), H3317 (Jiphthah-el), H6609 (drawn sword), H6610 (open), H3316 (Jephthah)\nGreek Equivalents: G191 ἀκούω, G393 ἀνατέλλω, G455 ἀνοίγω, G851 ἀφαιρέω, G863 ἀφίημι, G1272 διανοίγω, G1562 ἐκδύω, G1572 ἐκκαίω, G1590 ἐκλύω, G3089 λύω, G3736 ὀρύσσω, G3886 παραλύω, G4685 σπάω",
    6606: "פְּתַח (pᵉthach | peth-akh')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6605 פָּתַח\nStrong's: to open\nKJV: open.\n\nCognate Group: H6606 (open)\nGreek Equivalents: G2188 ἐφφαθά",
    6607: "פֶּתַח (pethach | peh'-thakh)\n\nDerivation: from H6605 פָּתַח\nStrong's: an opening (literally), i.e. door (gate) or entrance way\nKJV: door, entering (in), entrance (-ry), gate, opening, place.\n\nCognate Group: H6608 (entrance), H5318 (Neptoah), H4668 (key), H6605 (appear), H6607 (door), H4669 (opening), H6611 (Pethakiah), H3317 (Jiphthah-el), H6609 (drawn sword), H6610 (open), H3316 (Jephthah)\nGreek Equivalents: G833 αὐλή, G1529 εἴσοδος, G2374 θύρα, G2376 θυρίς, G4439 πύλη, G4440 πυλών",
    6608: "פֵּתַח (pêthach | pay'-thakh)\n\nDerivation: from H6605 פָּתַח\nStrong's: opening (figuratively) i.e. disclosure\nKJV: entrance.\n\nCognate Group: H6608 (entrance), H5318 (Neptoah), H4668 (key), H6605 (appear), H6607 (door), H4669 (opening), H6611 (Pethakiah), H3317 (Jiphthah-el), H6609 (drawn sword), H6610 (open), H3316 (Jephthah)",
    6609: "פְּתִחָה (pᵉthichâh | peth-ee-khaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6605 פָּתַח\nStrong's: something opened, i.e. a drawn sword\nKJV: drawn sword.\n\nCognate Group: H6608 (entrance), H5318 (Neptoah), H4668 (key), H6605 (appear), H6607 (door), H4669 (opening), H6611 (Pethakiah), H3317 (Jiphthah-el), H6609 (drawn sword), H6610 (open), H3316 (Jephthah)\nGreek Equivalents: G1002 βολίς",
    6610: "פִּתְחֹון (pithchôwn | pith-khone')\n\nDerivation: from H6605 פָּתַח\nStrong's: opening (the act)\nKJV: open(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H6608 (entrance), H5318 (Neptoah), H4668 (key), H6605 (appear), H6607 (door), H4669 (opening), H6611 (Pethakiah), H3317 (Jiphthah-el), H6609 (drawn sword), H6610 (open), H3316 (Jephthah)",
    6611: "פְּתַחְיָה (Pᵉthachyâh | peth-akh-yaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6605 פָּתַח and H3050 יָהּ ; Jah has opened\nStrong's: Pethachjah, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Pethakiah.\n\nCognate Group: H6608 (entrance), H5318 (Neptoah), H4668 (key), H6605 (appear), H6607 (door), H4669 (opening), H6611 (Pethakiah), H3317 (Jiphthah-el), H6609 (drawn sword), H6610 (open), H3316 (Jephthah)",
    6612: "פְּתִי (pᵉthîy | peth-ee')\n\nDerivation: or פֶּתִי; or פְּתָאִי; from H6601 פָּתָה\nStrong's: silly (i.e. seducible)\nKJV: foolish, simple(-icity, one).\n\nCognate Group: H6615 (simple), H6604 (Pethor), H6602 (Pethuel), H6601 (allure), H6612 (foolish), H3315 (Japheth)\nGreek Equivalents: G172 ἄκακος, G877 ἀφροσύνη, G878 ἄφρων, G1343 δικαιοσύνη, G3516 νήπιος",
    6613: "פְּתַי (pᵉthay | peth-ah'-ee)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from a root corresponding to H6601 פָּתָה\nStrong's: open, i.e. (as noun) width\nKJV: breadth.\n\nCognate Group: H6613 (breadth)\nGreek Equivalents: G4114 πλάτος",
    6614: "פְּתִיגִיל (pᵉthîygîyl | peth-eeg-eel')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: probably a figured mantle forholidays\nKJV: stomacher.\n\nCognate Group: H6605 (appear), H6614 (stomacher), H6603 (carved grave)",
    6615: "פְּתַיּוּת (pᵉthayûwth | peth-ah-yooth')\n\nDerivation: from H6612 פְּתִי\nStrong's: silliness (i.e. seducibility)\nKJV: simple.\n\nCognate Group: H6615 (simple), H6604 (Pethor), H6602 (Pethuel), H6601 (allure), H6612 (foolish), H3315 (Japheth)",
    6616: "פָּתִיל (pâthîyl | paw-theel')\n\nDerivation: from H6617 פָּתַל\nStrong's: twine\nKJV: bound, bracelet, lace, line, ribband, thread, wire.\n\nCognate Group: H5319 (wrestling), H6617 (froward), H5321 (Naphtali), H6619 (Pithom), H6618 (crooked), H6616 (bound)\nGreek Equivalents: G2355 θρῆνος, G4912 συνέχω",
    6617: "פָּתַל (pâthal | paw-thal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to twine, i.e. (literally) to struggle or (figuratively) be (morally) tortuous\nKJV: (shew self) froward, shew self unsavoury, wrestle.\n\nCognate Group: H5319 (wrestling), H6617 (froward), H5321 (Naphtali), H6619 (Pithom), H6618 (crooked), H6616 (bound)\nGreek Equivalents: G1294 διαστρέφω, G4646 σκολιός, G4761 στρεβλόω",
    6618: "פְּתַלְתֹּל (pᵉthaltôl | peth-al-tole')\n\nDerivation: from H6617 פָּתַל\nStrong's: tortuous (i.e. crafty)\nKJV: crooked.\n\nCognate Group: H5319 (wrestling), H6617 (froward), H5321 (Naphtali), H6619 (Pithom), H6618 (crooked), H6616 (bound)\nGreek Equivalents: G995 βοή, G1162 δέησις, G2906 κραυγή, G5456 φωνή",
    6619: "פִּתֹם (Pithôm | pee-thome')\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Pithom, a place in Egypt\nKJV: Pithom.\n\nCognate Group: H5319 (wrestling), H6617 (froward), H5321 (Naphtali), H6619 (Pithom), H6618 (crooked), H6616 (bound)",
    6620: "פֶּתֶן (pethen | peh'-then)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to twist\nStrong's: an asp (from its contortions)\nKJV: adder.\n\nCognate Group: H4670 (threshold), H6620 (adder)\nGreek Equivalents: G785 ἀσπίς, G935 βασιλεύς, G1404 δράκων",
    6621: "פֶּתַע (pethaʻ | peh'-thah)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to open (the eyes); (compare H6597 פִּתְאֹום)\n\nStrong's: a wink, i.e. moment(used only (with or without preposition) adverbially, quickly or unexpectedly\nKJV: at an instant, suddenly, [idiom] very.\n\nCognate Group: H6621 (at an instant), H6597 (straightway)\nGreek Equivalents: G1810 ἐξαίφνης, G1819 ἐξάπινα, G3916 παραχρῆμα, G4743 στιγμή",
    6622: "פָּתַר (pâthar | paw-thar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to open up, i.e. (figuratively) interpret (a dream)\nKJV: interpret(-ation, -er).\n\nCognate Group: H6623 (interpretation), H6622 (interpret), H6625 (Pathrusim), H6624 (Pathros), H6572 (copy)\nGreek Equivalents: G518 ἀπαγγέλλω, G868 ἀφίστημι, G1299 διατάσσω, G2647 καταλύω",
    6623: "פִּתְרֹון (pithrôwn | pith-rone')\n\nDerivation: or פִּתְרֹן; from H6622 פָּתַר\nStrong's: interpretation (of a dream)\nKJV: interpretation.\n\nCognate Group: H6623 (interpretation), H6622 (interpret), H6625 (Pathrusim), H6624 (Pathros), H6572 (copy)",
    6624: "פַּתְרֹוס (Pathrôwç | path-roce')\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Pathros, a part of Egypt\nKJV: Pathros.\n\nCognate Group: H6623 (interpretation), H6622 (interpret), H6625 (Pathrusim), H6624 (Pathros), H6572 (copy)",
    6625: "פַּתְרֻסִי (Pathruçîy | path-roo-see')\n\nDerivation: patrial from H6624 פַּתְרֹוס\nStrong's: a Pathrusite, or inhabitant of Pathros\nKJV: Pathrusim.\n\nCognate Group: H6623 (interpretation), H6622 (interpret), H6625 (Pathrusim), H6624 (Pathros), H6572 (copy)",
    6626: "פָּתַת (pâthath | paw-thath')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to open, i.e. break\nKJV: participle x\n\nCognate Group: H6626 (participle x), H6595 (meat)",
    6627: "צָאָה (tsâʼâh | tsaw-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H3318 יָצָא\nStrong's: issue, i.e. (human) excrement\nKJV: that (which) cometh from (out).\n\nCognate Group: H6678 (Zoba), H6674 (filthy), H2578 (Hamath-Zobah), H6675 (dung), H6627 (that cometh from)\nGreek Equivalents: G808 ἀσχημοσύνη",
    6628: "צֶאֶל (tseʼel | tseh'-el)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to be slender\nStrong's: the lotus tree\nKJV: shady tree.\n\nCognate Group: H6628 (shady tree)\nGreek Equivalents: G1186 δένδρον",
    6629: "צֹאן (tsôʼn | tsone)\n\nDerivation: or צאֹון; (Psalm 144:13), from an unused root meaning to migrate\nStrong's: a collective name for a flock (of sheep or goats); also figuratively (of men)\nKJV: (small) cattle, flock ([phrase] -s), lamb ([phrase] -s), sheep(-cote, -fold, -shearer, -herds).\n\nCognate Group: H6629 (cattle), H6630 (Zaanan), H6799 (Zenan)\nGreek Equivalents: G286 ἀμνός, G2934 κτῆνος, G2992 λαός, G4168 ποίμνιον, G4262 προβατικός, G4263 πρόβατον",
    6630: "צַאֲנָן (Tsaʼănân | tsah-an-awn')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6629 צֹאן used denominatively; sheep pasture\nStrong's: Zaanan, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zaanan.\n\nCognate Group: H6629 (cattle), H6630 (Zaanan), H6799 (Zenan)",
    6631: "צֶאֱצָא (tseʼĕtsâʼ | tseh-ets-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H3318 יָצָא\nStrong's: issue, i.e. produce, children\nKJV: that which cometh forth (out), offspring.\n\nCognate Group: H4161 (brought out), H6631 (that which cometh forth), H8444 (border), H4163 (draught house; going forth), H3329 (those that came forth), H3318 ([idiom] after), H4162 (Moza)\nGreek Equivalents: G1549 ἔκγονον, G5043 τέκνον",
    6632: "צָב (tsâb | tsawb)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to establish\nStrong's: a palanquin or canopy (as a fixture); also a species of lizard (probably as clinging fast)\nKJV: covered, litter, tortoise.\n\nCognate Group: H6632 (covered)",
    6633: "צָבָא (tsâbâʼ | tsaw-baw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to mass (an army or servants)\nKJV: assemble, fight, perform, muster, wait upon, war.\n\nCognate Group: H6633 (assemble), H6635 (appointed time)\nGreek Equivalents: G1754 ἐνεργέω, G1831 ἐξέρχομαι, G3008 λειτουργέω, G3936 παρίστημι, G4757 στρατιώτης",
    6634: "צְבָא (tsᵉbâʼ | tseb-aw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6623 פִּתְרֹון in the figurative sense of summoning one's wishes\nStrong's: to please\nKJV: will, would.\n\nCognate Group: H6640 (purpose), H6634 (will)\nGreek Equivalents: G1013 βούλημα, G1380 δοκέω, G2212 ζητέω, G2307 θέλημα, G2309 θέλω, G923 Βαρσαβᾶς",
    6635: "צָבָא (tsâbâʼ | tsaw-baw')\n\nDerivation: or (feminine) צְבָאָה; from H6633 צָבָא\nStrong's: a mass of persons (or figuratively, things), especially reg. organized forwar (an army); by implication, a campaign, literally or figuratively (specifically, hardship, worship)\nKJV: appointed time, ([phrase]) army, ([phrase]) battle, company, host, service, soldiers, waiting upon, war(-fare).\n\nCognate Group: H6633 (assemble), H6635 (appointed time)\nGreek Equivalents: G39 ἅγιον, G706 ἀριθμός, G792 ἀστήρ, G798 ἄστρον, G979 βίος, G1411 δύναμις, G1484 ἔθνος, G2889 κόσμος, G3009 λειτουργία, G3163 μάχη, G4128 πλῆθος, G4171 πόλεμος, G4752 στρατεία, G4756 στρατιά, G4519 σαβαώθ",
    6636: "צְבֹאִים (Tsᵉbôʼîym | tseb-o-eem')\n\nDerivation: or (more correctly) צְבִיִּים; or צְבִיִּם; plural of H6643 צְבִי ; gazelles\nStrong's: Tseboim or Tsebijim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zeboiim, Zeboim.\n\nCognate Group: H6646 (roe), H6645 (Zibiah), H6644 (Zibia), H6643 (beautiful), H6636 (Zeboiim)",
    6637: "צֹבֵבָה (Tsôbêbâh | tso-bay-baw')\n\nDerivation: feminine active participle of the same as H6632 צָב ; the canopier (with the article)\nStrong's: Tsobebah, an Israelitess\nKJV: Zobebah.\n\nCognate Group: H6632 (covered), H6637 (Zobebah)",
    6638: "צָבָה (tsâbâh | tsaw-baw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to amass, i.e. grow turgid; specifically, to array an army against\nKJV: fight swell.\n\nCognate Group: H6638 (fight swell), H6639 (swell)",
    6639: "צָבֶה (tsâbeh | tsaw-beh')\n\nDerivation: from H6638 צָבָה\nStrong's: turgid\nKJV: swell.\n\nCognate Group: H6638 (fight swell), H6639 (swell)",
    6640: "צְבוּ (tsᵉbûw | tseb-oo')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from H6634 צְבָא\nStrong's: properly, will; concretely, an affair (as a matter of determination)\nKJV: purpose.\n\nCognate Group: H6640 (purpose), H6634 (will)\nGreek Equivalents: G4229 πρᾶγμα",
    6641: "צָבוּעַ (tsâbûwaʻ | tsaw-boo'-ah)\n\nDerivation: passive participle of the same as H6648 צֶבַע\nStrong's: dyed (in stripes), i.e. the hyena\nKJV: speckled.\n\nCognate Group: H6641 (speckled), H6648 (divers)",
    6642: "צָבַט (tsâbaṭ | tsaw-bat')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to grasp, i.e. hand out\nKJV: reach.\n\nCognate Group: H6642 (reach)",
    6643: "צְבִי (tsᵉbîy | tseb-ee')\n\nDerivation: from H6638 צָבָה in the sense of prominence\nStrong's: splendor (as conspicuous); also a gazelle (as beautiful)\nKJV: beautiful(-ty), glorious (-ry), goodly, pleasant, roe(-buck).\n\nCognate Group: H6643 (beautiful)\nGreek Equivalents: G1391 δόξα, G1588 ἐκλεκτός, G1680 ἐλπίς, G1741 ἔνδοξος, G2308 θέλησις",
    6644: "צִבְיָא (Tsibyâʼ | tsib-yaw')\n\nDerivation: for H6645 צִבְיָּה\nStrong's: Tsibja, an Israelite\nKJV: Zibia.\n\nCognate Group: H6646 (roe), H6645 (Zibiah), H6644 (Zibia), H6643 (beautiful), H6636 (Zeboiim)",
    6645: "צִבְיָּה (Tsibyâh | tsib-yaw')\n\nDerivation: for H6646 צְבִיָּה\nStrong's: Tsibjah, an Israelite\nKJV: Zibiah.\n\nCognate Group: H6646 (roe), H6645 (Zibiah), H6644 (Zibia), H6643 (beautiful), H6636 (Zeboiim)",
    6646: "צְבִיָּה (tsᵉbîyâh | tseb-ee-yaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6643 צְבִי\nStrong's: a female gazelle\nKJV: roe.\n\nCognate Group: H6646 (roe), H6645 (Zibiah), H6644 (Zibia), H6643 (beautiful), H6636 (Zeboiim)\nGreek Equivalents: G5000 Ταβιθά",
    6647: "צְבַע (tsᵉbaʻ | tseb-ah')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) a root corresponding to that of H6648 צֶבַע\nStrong's: to dip\nKJV: wet.\n\nCognate Group: H6647 (wet)\nGreek Equivalents: G835 αὐλίζομαι, G911 βάπτω",
    6648: "צֶבַע (tsebaʻ | tseh'-bah)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to dip (into coloring fluid)\nStrong's: a dye\nKJV: divers, colours.\n\nCognate Group: H6641 (speckled), H6648 (divers)",
    6649: "צִבְעֹון (Tsibʻôwn | tsib-one')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6648 צֶבַע ; variegated\nStrong's: Tsibon, an Idumaean\nKJV: Zibeon.\n\nCognate Group: H6649 (Zibeon), H6650 (Zeboim)",
    6650: "צְבֹעִים (Tsᵉbôʻîym | tseb-o-eem')\n\nDerivation: plural of H6641 צָבוּעַ ; hyenas\nStrong's: Tseboim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zeboim.\n\nCognate Group: H6649 (Zibeon), H6650 (Zeboim)",
    6651: "צָבַר (tsâbar | tsaw-bar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to aggregate\nKJV: gather (together), heap (up), lay up.\n\nCognate Group: H6651 (gather), H6652 (heap)\nGreek Equivalents: G906 βάλλω, G2343 θησαυρίζω, G4863 συνάγω",
    6652: "צִבֻּר (tsibbur | tsib-boor')\n\nDerivation: from H6551 פַּרְעֹשׁ\nStrong's: a pile\nKJV: heap.\n\nCognate Group: H6651 (gather), H6652 (heap)\nGreek Equivalents: G1015 βουνός",
    6653: "צֶבֶת (tsebeth | tseh'-beth)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root apparently meaning to grip\nStrong's: a lock of stalks\nKJV: handful.\n\nCognate Group: H6653 (handful)",
    6654: "צַד (tsad | tsad)\n\nDerivation: contr. from an unused root meaning to sidle off\nStrong's: a side; figuratively, an adversary\nKJV: (be-) side.\n\nCognate Group: H6657 (Zedad), H6654 (side), H6661 (Ziddim)\nGreek Equivalents: G3313 μέρος, G4125 πλευρά, G5606 ὦμος",
    6655: "צַד (tsad | tsad)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6654 צַד\nStrong's: used adverbially (with preposition) at or upon the side of\nKJV: against, concerning.\n\nCognate Group: H6676 (neck), H6655 (against), H6665 (righteousness), H6656 (true), H677 (finger)",
    6656: "צְדָא (tsᵉdâʼ | tsed-aw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) from an unused root corresponding to H6658 צָדָה in the sense of intentness\nStrong's: a (sinister) design\nKJV: true.\n\nCognate Group: H6676 (neck), H6655 (against), H6665 (righteousness), H6656 (true), H677 (finger)\nGreek Equivalents: G230 ἀληθῶς",
    6657: "צְדָד (Tsᵉdâd | tsed-awd')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6654 צַד ; a siding\nStrong's: Tsedad, a place near Palestine\nKJV: Zedad.\n\nCognate Group: H6657 (Zedad), H6654 (side), H6661 (Ziddim)",
    6658: "צָדָה (tsâdâh | tsaw-daw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to chase; by implication, to desolate\nKJV: destroy, hunt, lie in wait.\n\nCognate Group: H6658 (destroy), H6660 (lying in wait)\nGreek Equivalents: G1195 δεσμεύω, G1587 ἐκλείπω",
    6659: "צָדֹוק (Tsâdôwq | tsaw-doke')\n\nDerivation: from H6663 צָדַק ; just\nStrong's: Tsadok, the name of eight or nine Israelites\nKJV: Zadok.\n\nCognate Group: H6659 (Zadok), H6666 (justice), H6662 (just), H6667 (Zedekiah), H3072 (the Lord our righteousness), H6663 (cleanse), H6664 ([idiom] even)\nGreek Equivalents: G4524 Σαδώκ",
    6660: "צְדִיָּה (tsᵉdîyâh | tsed-ee-yaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6658 צָדָה ; (compare H6656 צְדָא)\n\nStrong's: design\nKJV: lying in wait.\n\nCognate Group: H6658 (destroy), H6660 (lying in wait)",
    6661: "צִדִּים (Tsiddîym | tsid-deem')\n\nDerivation: plural of H6654 צַד ; sides\nStrong's: Tsiddim (with the article), a place in Palestine\nKJV: Ziddim.\n\nCognate Group: H6657 (Zedad), H6654 (side), H6661 (Ziddim)",
    6662: "צַדִּיק (tsaddîyq | tsad-deek')\n\nDerivation: from H6663 צָדַק\nStrong's: just\nKJV: just, lawful, righteous (man).\n\nCognate Group: H6659 (Zadok), H6666 (justice), H6662 (just), H6667 (Zedekiah), H3072 (the Lord our righteousness), H6663 (cleanse), H6664 ([idiom] even)\nGreek Equivalents: G95 ἀδίκως, G227 ἀληθής, G1342 δίκαιος, G1343 δικαιοσύνη, G2152 εὐσεβής, G4103 πιστός",
    6663: "צָדַק (tsâdaq | tsaw-dak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be (causatively, make) right (in a moral or forensic sense)\nKJV: cleanse, clear self, (be, do) just(-ice, -ify, -ify self), (be turn to) righteous(-ness).\n\nCognate Group: H6659 (Zadok), H6666 (justice), H6662 (just), H6667 (Zedekiah), H3072 (the Lord our righteousness), H6663 (cleanse), H6664 ([idiom] even)\nGreek Equivalents: G273 ἄμεμπτος, G398 ἀναφαίνω, G1342 δίκαιος, G1342 δίκαιος, G1342 δίκαιος, G1344 δικαιόω, G2511 καθαρίζω",
    6664: "צֶדֶק (tsedeq | tseh'-dek)\n\nDerivation: from H6663 צָדַק\nStrong's: the right (natural, moral or legal); also (abstractly) equity or (figuratively) prosperity\nKJV: [idiom] even, ([idiom] that which is altogether) just(-ice), (un-)right(-eous) (cause, -ly, -ness).\n\nCognate Group: H6659 (Zadok), H6666 (justice), H6662 (just), H6667 (Zedekiah), H3072 (the Lord our righteousness), H6663 (cleanse), H6664 ([idiom] even)\nGreek Equivalents: G1342 δίκαιος, G1343 δικαιοσύνη, G1344 δικαιόω, G1346 δικαίως, G2920 κρίσις",
    6665: "צִדְקָה (tsidqâh | tsid-kaw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6666 צְדָקָה\nStrong's: beneficence\nKJV: righteousness.\n\nCognate Group: H6676 (neck), H6655 (against), H6665 (righteousness), H6656 (true), H677 (finger)",
    6666: "צְדָקָה (tsᵉdâqâh | tsed-aw-kaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6663 צָדַק\nStrong's: rightness (abstractly), subjectively (rectitude), objectively (justice), morally (virtue) or figuratively (prosperity)\nKJV: justice, moderately, right(-eous) (act, -ly, -ness).\n\nCognate Group: H6659 (Zadok), H6666 (justice), H6662 (just), H6667 (Zedekiah), H3072 (the Lord our righteousness), H6663 (cleanse), H6664 ([idiom] even)\nGreek Equivalents: G1342 δίκαιος, G1343 δικαιοσύνη, G1344 δικαιόω, G1345 δικαίωμα, G1654 ἐλεημοσύνη, G2167 εὐφροσύνη, G2917 κρίμα",
    6667: "צִדְקִיָּה (Tsidqîyâh | tsid-kee-yaw')\n\nDerivation: or צִדְקִיָּהוּ; from H6664 צֶדֶק and H3050 יָהּ ; right of Jah\nStrong's: Tsidkijah, the name of six Israelites\nKJV: Zedekiah, Zidkijah.\n\nCognate Group: H6659 (Zadok), H6666 (justice), H6662 (just), H6667 (Zedekiah), H3072 (the Lord our righteousness), H6663 (cleanse), H6664 ([idiom] even)",
    6668: "צָהַב (tsâhab | tsaw-hab')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to glitter, i.e. be golden in color\nKJV: [idiom] fine.\n\nCognate Group: H6668 ([idiom] fine), H6669 (yellow)\nGreek Equivalents: G4744 στίλβω",
    6669: "צָהֹב (tsâhôb | tsaw-obe')\n\nDerivation: from H6668 צָהַב\nStrong's: golden in color\nKJV: yellow.\n\nCognate Group: H6668 ([idiom] fine), H6669 (yellow)",
    6670: "צָהַל (tsâhal | tsaw-hal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to gleam, i.e. (figuratively) be cheerful; by transference to sound clear (of various animal or human expressions)\nKJV: bellow, cry aloud (out), lift up, neigh, rejoice, make to shine, shout.\n\nCognate Group: H4684 (neighing), H6670 (bellow)\nGreek Equivalents: G21 ἀγαλλιάω, G994 βοάω, G5015 ταράσσω",
    6671: "צָהַר (tsâhar | tsaw-har')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; to glisten; used only as denominative from H3323 יִצְהָר,\nStrong's: to press out oil\nKJV: make oil.\n\nCognate Group: H3324 (Izhar), H3325 (Izeharites), H6672 (midday), H6671 (make oil), H3323 (anointed oil)",
    6672: "צֹהַר (tsôhar | tso'-har)\n\nDerivation: from H6671 צָהַר\nStrong's: a light (i.e. window); dual double light, i.e. noon\nKJV: midday, noon(-day, -tide), window.\n\nCognate Group: H3324 (Izhar), H3325 (Izeharites), H6672 (midday), H6671 (make oil), H3323 (anointed oil)\nGreek Equivalents: G3314 μεσημβρία",
    6673: "צַו (tsav | tsav)\n\nDerivation: or צָו; from H6680 צָוָה\nStrong's: an injunction\nKJV: commandment, precept.\n\nCognate Group: H6673 (commandment), H4687 (commanded), H6725 (sign), H6680 (appoint)",
    6674: "צֹוא (tsôwʼ | tso)\n\nDerivation: or צֹא; from an unused root meaning to issue\nStrong's: soiled (as if excrementitious)\nKJV: filthy.\n\nCognate Group: H6678 (Zoba), H6674 (filthy), H2578 (Hamath-Zobah), H6675 (dung), H6627 (that cometh from)\nGreek Equivalents: G4508 ῥυπαρός",
    6675: "צֹואָה (tsôwʼâh | tso-aw')\n\nDerivation: or צֹאָה ; feminine of H6674 צֹוא\nStrong's: excrement; generally, dirt; figuratively, pollution\nKJV: dung, filth(-iness). Marg. for H2716 חֶרֶא .\n\nCognate Group: H6678 (Zoba), H6674 (filthy), H2578 (Hamath-Zobah), H6675 (dung), H6627 (that cometh from)\nGreek Equivalents: G1841 ἔξοδος, G4509 ῥύπος",
    6676: "צַוַּאר (tsavvaʼr | tsav-var')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6677 צַוָּאר \nStrong's: the back of the neck (as that on which burdens are bound)\nKJV: neck.\n\nCognate Group: H6676 (neck), H6655 (against), H6665 (righteousness), H6656 (true), H677 (finger)\nGreek Equivalents: G5137 τράχηλος",
    6677: "צַוָּאר (tsavvâʼr | tsav-vawr')\n\nDerivation: or צַוָּר; (Nehemiah 3:5), or צַוָּרֹן\n\nTypo: lemma צַוָּרן missing vowel, corrected to צַוָּרֹן\n\n; (Song of Solomon 4:9), or (feminine) צַוָּארָה; (Micah 2:3), intensively from H6696 צוּר in the sense of binding\nStrong's: the back of the neck (as that on which burdens are bound)\nKJV: neck.\n\nCognate Group: H6677 (neck)",
    6678: "צֹובָא (Tsôwbâʼ | tso-baw')\n\nDerivation: or צֹובָה; or צֹבָה; from an unused root meaning to station; a station\nStrong's: Zoba or Zobah, a region of Syria\nKJV: Zoba, Zobah.\n\nCognate Group: H6678 (Zoba), H6674 (filthy), H2578 (Hamath-Zobah), H6675 (dung), H6627 (that cometh from)",
    6679: "צוּד (tsûwd | tsood)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; also denominative from H6718 צַיִד \nStrong's: to lie alongside (i.e. in wait); by implication, to catch an animal (figuratively, men); to victual (for a journey)\nKJV: chase, hunt, sore, take (provision).\n\nCognate Group: H6719 (hunter), H4685 (bulwark), H6679 (chase), H4686 (castle), H6718 ([idiom] catcheth), H4679 (castle)\nGreek Equivalents: G1294 διαστρέφω, G1612 ἐκστρέφω, G2340 θηρεύω",
    6680: "צָוָה (tsâvâh | tsaw-vaw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: (intensively) to constitute, enjoin\nKJV: appoint, (for-) bid, (give a) charge, (give a, give in, send with) command(-er, -ment), send a messenger, put, (set) in order.\n\nCognate Group: H6673 (commandment), H4687 (commanded), H6725 (sign), H6680 (appoint)\nGreek Equivalents: G649 ἀποστέλλω, G1303 διατίθεμαι, G1325 δίδωμι, G1627 ἐκφέρω, G1760 ἐνθυμέομαι, G1781 ἐντέλλομαι, G2004 ἐπιτάσσω, G2525 καθίστημι, G2729 κατισχύω, G2980 λαλέω, G4367 προστάσσω, G4921 συνιστάω, G4929 συντάσσω, G5021 τάσσω, G5087 τίθημι",
    6681: "צָוַח (tsâvach | tsaw-vakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to screech (exultingly)\nKJV: shout.\n\nCognate Group: H6682 (cry), H6681 (shout)\nGreek Equivalents: G994 βοάω",
    6682: "צְוָחָה (tsᵉvâchâh | tsev-aw-khaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6681 צָוַח\nStrong's: a screech (of anguish)\nKJV: cry(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H6682 (cry), H6681 (shout)\nGreek Equivalents: G2906 κραυγή, G3649 ὀλολύζω",
    6683: "צוּלָה (tsûwlâh | tsoo-law')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to sink\nStrong's: an abyss (of the sea)\nKJV: deep.\n\nCognate Group: H6683 (deep), H4699 (bottom), H4688 (bottom)\nGreek Equivalents: G12 ἄβυσσος",
    6684: "צוּם (tsûwm | tsoom)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to cover over (the mouth), i.e. to fast\nKJV: [idiom] at all, fast.\n\nCognate Group: H6685 (fast), H6684 ([idiom] at all)\nGreek Equivalents: G3522 νηστεύω",
    6685: "צֹום (tsôwm | tsome)\n\nDerivation: or צֹם; from H6684 צוּם\nStrong's: a fast\nKJV: fast(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H6685 (fast), H6684 ([idiom] at all)\nGreek Equivalents: G3521 νηστεία",
    6686: "צוּעָר (Tsûwʻâr | tsoo-awr')\n\nDerivation: from H6819 צָעַר ; small\nStrong's: Tsuar, an Israelite\nKJV: Zuar.\n\nCognate Group: H4706 (Mizar), H4705 (little one), H6730 (Zior), H6811 (Zair), H4704 (little), H6686 (Zuar), H6810 (least), H6819 (be brought low), H6820 (Zoar), H6812 (youth)",
    6687: "צוּף (tsûwph | tsoof)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to overflow\nKJV: (make to over-) flow, swim.\n\nCognate Group: H6824 ([idiom] swimmest), H6687 (flow), H6688 (honeycomb), H7436 (Ramathaimzophim), H6689 (Zophai)\nGreek Equivalents: G2346 θλίβω, G2716 κατεργάζομαι, G3926 παρενοχλέω",
    6688: "צוּף (tsûwph | tsoof)\n\nDerivation: from H6687 צוּף\nStrong's: comb of honey (from dripping)\nKJV: honeycomb.\n\nCognate Group: H6824 ([idiom] swimmest), H6687 (flow), H6688 (honeycomb), H7436 (Ramathaimzophim), H6689 (Zophai)\nGreek Equivalents: G2781 κηρίον",
    6689: "צוּף (Tsûwph | tsoof)\n\nDerivation: or צֹופַי; or צִיף; from H6688 צוּף ; honey-comb\nStrong's: Tsuph or Tsophai or Tsiph, the name of an Israelite and of a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zophai, Zuph.\n\nCognate Group: H6824 ([idiom] swimmest), H6687 (flow), H6688 (honeycomb), H7436 (Ramathaimzophim), H6689 (Zophai)",
    6690: "צֹופַח (Tsôwphach | tso-fakh')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to expand, breadth\nStrong's: Tsophach, an Israelite\nKJV: Zophah.\n\nCognate Group: H6835 (cruse), H6690 (Zophah), H6838 (wafer)",
    6691: "צֹופַר (Tsôwphar | tso-far')\n\nDerivation: from H6852 צָפַר ; departing\nStrong's: Tsophar, a friend of Job\nKJV: Zophar.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)",
    6692: "צוּץ (tsûwts | tsoots)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to twinkle, i.e. glance; by analogy, to blossom (figuratively, flourish)\nKJV: bloom, blossom, flourish, shew self.\n\nCognate Group: H6692 (bloom), H6731 (blossom), H6733 (flower)\nGreek Equivalents: G985 βλαστάνω",
    6693: "צוּק (tsûwq | tsook)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to compress, i.e. (figuratively) oppress, distress\nKJV: constrain, distress, lie sore, (op-) press(-or), straiten.\n\nCognate Group: H4164 (anguish), H6695 (anguish), H6693 (constrain), H4691 (anguish), H4689 (anguish)",
    6694: "צוּק (tsûwq | tsook)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (identical with through the idea of narrowness (of orifice))\nStrong's: to pour out, i.e. (figuratively) smelt, utter\nKJV: be molten, pour.\n\nCognate Group: H6694 (be molten), H4690 (pillar)",
    6695: "צֹוק (tsôwq | tsoke)\n\nDerivation: or (feminine) צוּקָה; from H6693 צוּק\nStrong's: a strait, i.e. (figuratively) distress\nKJV: anguish, [idiom] troublous.\n\nCognate Group: H4164 (anguish), H6695 (anguish), H6693 (constrain), H4691 (anguish), H4689 (anguish)\nGreek Equivalents: G4730 στενοχωρία",
    6696: "צוּר (tsûwr | tsoor)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to cramp, i.e. confine (in many applications, literally and figuratively, formative or hostile)\nKJV: adversary, assault, beset, besiege, bind (up), cast, distress, fashion, fortify, inclose, lay siege, put up in bags.\n\nCognate Group: H6696 (adversary), H6887 (adversary), H4694 (fenced), H4692 (besieged)\nGreek Equivalents: G64 ἀγρεύω, G480 ἀντίκειμαι, G906 βάλλω, G1210 δέω, G2523 καθίζω, G2983 λαμβάνω, G4111 πλάσσω, G4776 συγκαθίζω, G4912 συνέχω",
    6697: "צוּר (tsûwr | tsoor)\n\nDerivation: or צֻר; from H6696 צוּר\nStrong's: properly, a cliff (or sharp rock, as compressed); generally, a rock or boulder; figuratively, a refuge; also an edge (as precipitous)\nKJV: edge, [idiom] (mighty) God (one), rock, [idiom] sharp, stone, [idiom] strength, [idiom] strong. See also H1049 בֵּית צוּר .\n\nCognate Group: H6698 (Zur), H6700 (Zuriel), H6697 (edge), H6701 (Zurishaddai)\nGreek Equivalents: G39 ἅγιον, G998 βοηθός, G2316 θεός, G2962 κύριος, G3173 μέγας, G3735 ὄρος, G4073 πέτρα, G4731 στερεός, G5441 φύλαξ",
    6698: "צוּר (Tsûwr | tsoor)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6697 צוּר ; rock\nStrong's: Tsur, the name of a Midianite and of an Israelite\nKJV: Zur.\n\nCognate Group: H6698 (Zur), H6700 (Zuriel), H6697 (edge), H6701 (Zurishaddai)",
    6699: "צוּרָה (tsûwrâh | tsoo-raw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6697 צוּר\nStrong's: a rock (Job 28:10); also a form (as if pressed out)\nKJV: form, rock.\n\nCognate Group: H6696 (adversary), H6736 (beauty), H6699 (form)\nGreek Equivalents: G1345 δικαίωμα",
    6700: "צוּרִיאֵל (Tsûwrîyʼêl | tsoo-ree-ale')\n\nDerivation: from H6697 צוּר and H410 אֵל ; rock of God\nStrong's: Tsuriel, an Israelite\nKJV: Zuriel.\n\nCognate Group: H6698 (Zur), H6700 (Zuriel), H6697 (edge), H6701 (Zurishaddai)",
    6701: "צוּרִישַׁדַּי (Tsûwrîyshadday | tsoo-ree-shad-dah'-ee)\n\nDerivation: from H6697 צוּר and H7706 שַׁדַּי ; rock of (the) Almighty\nStrong's: Tsurishaddai, an Israelite\nKJV: Zurishaddai.\n\nCognate Group: H6698 (Zur), H6700 (Zuriel), H6697 (edge), H6701 (Zurishaddai)",
    6702: "צוּת (tsûwth | tsooth)\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to blaze\nKJV: burn.\n\nCognate Group: H6702 (burn), H3341 (burn)",
    6703: "צַח (tsach | tsakh)\n\nDerivation: from H6705 צָחַח\nStrong's: dazzling, i.e. sunny, bright, (figuratively) evident\nKJV: clear, dry, plainly, white.\n\nCognate Group: H6710 (drought), H6703 (clear), H6705 (be whiter), H6708 (higher place), H6707 (dry land), H6706 (higher place)\nGreek Equivalents: G1515 εἰρήνη, G3022 λευκός",
    6704: "צִחֶה (tsicheh | tsee-kheh')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to glow\nStrong's: parched\nKJV: dried up.\n\nCognate Group: H6704 (dried up)",
    6705: "צָחַח (tsâchach | tsaw-khakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to glare, i.e. be dazzling white\nKJV: be whiter.\n\nCognate Group: H6710 (drought), H6703 (clear), H6705 (be whiter), H6708 (higher place), H6707 (dry land), H6706 (higher place)\nGreek Equivalents: G2989 λάμπω",
    6706: "צְחִיחַ (tsᵉchîyach | tsekh-ee'-akh)\n\nDerivation: from H6705 צָחַח\nStrong's: glaring, i.e. exposed to the bright sun\nKJV: higher place, top.\n\nCognate Group: H6710 (drought), H6703 (clear), H6705 (be whiter), H6708 (higher place), H6707 (dry land), H6706 (higher place)",
    6707: "צְחִיחָה (tsᵉchîychâh | tsekh-ee-khaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6706 צְחִיחַ\nStrong's: a parched region, i.e. the desert\nKJV: dry land.\n\nCognate Group: H6710 (drought), H6703 (clear), H6705 (be whiter), H6708 (higher place), H6707 (dry land), H6706 (higher place)",
    6708: "צְחִיחִי (tsᵉchîychîy | tsekh-ee-khee')\n\nDerivation: from H6706 צְחִיחַ\nStrong's: bare spot, i.e. in the glaring sun\nKJV: higher place.\n\nCognate Group: H6710 (drought), H6703 (clear), H6705 (be whiter), H6708 (higher place), H6707 (dry land), H6706 (higher place)",
    6709: "צַחֲנָה (tsachănâh | tsakh-an-aw')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to putrefy\nStrong's: stench\nKJV: ill savour.\n\nCognate Group: H6709 (ill savour)",
    6710: "צַחְצָחָה (tsachtsâchâh | tsakh-tsaw-khaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6705 צָחַח\nStrong's: a dry place, i.e. desert\nKJV: drought.\n\nCognate Group: H6710 (drought), H6703 (clear), H6705 (be whiter), H6708 (higher place), H6707 (dry land), H6706 (higher place)",
    6711: "צָחַק (tsâchaq | tsaw-khak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to laugh outright (in merriment or scorn); by implication, to sport\nKJV: laugh, mock, play, make sport.\n\nCognate Group: H6712 (laugh), H3327 (Isaac), H3446 (Isaac), H6711 (laugh)\nGreek Equivalents: G1070 γελάω, G1702 ἐμπαίζω, G3815 παίζω",
    6712: "צְחֹק (tsᵉchôq | tsekh-oke')\n\nDerivation: from H6711 צָחַק\nStrong's: laughter (in pleasure or derision)\nKJV: laugh(-ed to scorn).\n\nCognate Group: H6712 (laugh), H3327 (Isaac), H3446 (Isaac), H6711 (laugh)\nGreek Equivalents: G1071 γέλως",
    6713: "צַחַר (tsachar | tsakh'-ar)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to dazzle\nStrong's: sheen, i.e. whiteness\nKJV: white.\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)",
    6714: "צֹחַר (Tsôchar | tso'-khar)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6713 צַחַר ; whiteness\nStrong's: Tsochar, the name of a Hittite and of an Israelite\nKJV: Zohar. Compare H3328 יִצְחַר .\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)",
    6715: "צָחֹר (tsâchôr | tsaw-khore')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6713 צַחַר\nStrong's: white\nKJV: white.\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)",
    6716: "צִי (tsîy | tsee)\n\nDerivation: from H6680 צָוָה\nStrong's: a ship (as a fixture)\nKJV: ship.\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)\nGreek Equivalents: G4143 πλοῖον",
    6717: "צִיבָא (Tsîybâʼ | tsee-baw')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6678 צֹובָא ; station\nStrong's: Tsiba, an Israelite\nKJV: Ziba.\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)",
    6718: "צַיִד (tsayid | tsah'-yid)\n\nDerivation: from a form of H6679 צוּד and meaning the same\nStrong's: the chase; also game (thus taken); (generally) lunch (especially for a journey)\nKJV: [idiom] catcheth, food, [idiom] hunter, (that which he took in) hunting, venison, victuals.\n\nCognate Group: H6719 (hunter), H4685 (bulwark), H6679 (chase), H4686 (castle), H6718 ([idiom] catcheth), H4679 (castle)\nGreek Equivalents: G2339 θήρα",
    6719: "צַיָּד (tsayâd | tsah'-yawd)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6718 צַיִד\nStrong's: a huntsman\nKJV: hunter.\n\nCognate Group: H6719 (hunter), H4685 (bulwark), H6679 (chase), H4686 (castle), H6718 ([idiom] catcheth), H4679 (castle)\nGreek Equivalents: G966 Βηθσαϊδά",
    6720: "צֵידָה (tsêydâh | tsay-daw')\n\nDerivation: or צֵדָה; feminine of H6718 צַיִד\nStrong's: food\nKJV: meat, provision, venison, victuals.\n\nCognate Group: H6737 (make as if), H6679 (chase), H6720 (meat), H6718 ([idiom] catcheth)\nGreek Equivalents: G1979 ἐπισιτισμός",
    6721: "צִידֹון (Tsîydôwn | tsee-done')\n\nDerivation: or צִידֹן; from H6679 צוּד in the sense of catching fish; fishery\nStrong's: Tsidon, the name of a son of Canaan, and of a place in Palestine\nKJV: Sidon, Zidon.\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)\nGreek Equivalents: G4605 Σιδών",
    6722: "צִידֹנִי (Tsîydônîy | tsee-do-nee')\n\nDerivation: patrial from H6721 צִידֹון\nStrong's: a Tsidonian or inhabitant of Tsidon\nKJV: Sidonian, of Sidon, Zidonian.\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)",
    6723: "צִיָּה (tsîyâh | tsee-yaw')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to parch\nStrong's: aridity; concretely, a desert\nKJV: barren, drought, dry (land, place), solitary place, wilderness.\n\nCognate Group: H6727 (Ziha), H6734 (fringe), H6723 (barren), H6732 (Ziz), H6731 (blossom), H6724 (dry place), H6726 (Zion)\nGreek Equivalents: G504 ἄνυδρος, G1372 διψάω, G2048 ἔρημος, G3584 ξηρός",
    6724: "צִיֹון (tsîyôwn | tsee-yone')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6723 צִיָּה\nStrong's: a desert\nKJV: dry place.\n\nCognate Group: H6727 (Ziha), H6734 (fringe), H6723 (barren), H6732 (Ziz), H6731 (blossom), H6724 (dry place), H6726 (Zion)",
    6725: "צִיּוּן (tsîyûwn | tsee-yoon')Typo: lemma צִיוּן missing dagesh, corrected to צִיּוּן\n\nGreek Equivalents: G4592 σημεῖον \n\nDerivation: from the same as H6723 צִיָּה in the sense of conspicuousness (compare H5329 נָצַח)\n\nStrong's: a monumental or guiding pillar\nKJV: sign, title, waymark.\n\nCognate Group: H6673 (commandment), H4687 (commanded), H6725 (sign), H6680 (appoint)",
    6726: "צִיֹּון (Tsîyôwn | tsee-yone')\n\nDerivation: the same (regularly) as H6725 צִיּוּן \n\nTypo: lemma צִיוּן missing dagesh, corrected to צִיּוּן\n\nStrong's: Tsijon (as a permanent capital), a mountain of Jerusalem\nKJV: Zion.\n\nCognate Group: H6727 (Ziha), H6734 (fringe), H6723 (barren), H6732 (Ziz), H6731 (blossom), H6724 (dry place), H6726 (Zion)\nGreek Equivalents: G4622 Σιών",
    6727: "צִיחָא (Tsîychâʼ | tsee-khaw')\n\nDerivation: or צִחָא; as if feminine of H6704 צִחֶה ; drought\nStrong's: Tsicha, the name of two Nethinim\nKJV: Ziha.\n\nCognate Group: H6727 (Ziha), H6734 (fringe), H6723 (barren), H6732 (Ziz), H6731 (blossom), H6724 (dry place), H6726 (Zion)",
    6728: "צִיִּי (tsîyîy | tsee-ee')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6723 צִיָּה\nStrong's: a desert-dweller, i.e. nomad or wild beast\nKJV: wild beast of the desert, that dwell in (inhabiting) the wilderness.\n\nCognate Group: H6716 (ship), H6715 (white), H3328 (and Zehoar), H6714 (Zohar), H6713 (white), H6717 (Ziba), H6728 (wild beast of the desert), H6722 (Sidonian), H6721 (Sidon)\nGreek Equivalents: G1140 δαιμόνιον, G2342 θηρίον",
    6729: "צִינֹק (tsîynôq | tsee-noke')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to confine\nStrong's: the pillory\nKJV: stocks.\n\nCognate Group: H6729 (stocks)",
    6730: "צִיעֹר (Tsîyʻôr | tsee-ore')\n\nDerivation: from H6819 צָעַר ; small\nStrong's: Tsior, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zior.\n\nCognate Group: H4706 (Mizar), H4705 (little one), H6730 (Zior), H6811 (Zair), H4704 (little), H6686 (Zuar), H6810 (least), H6819 (be brought low), H6820 (Zoar), H6812 (youth)",
    6731: "צִיץ (tsîyts | tseets)\n\nDerivation: or צִץ; from H6692 צוּץ\nStrong's: properly, glistening, i.e. a burnished plate; also a flower (as bright-colored); a wing (as gleaming in the air)\nKJV: blossom, flower, plate, wing.\n\nCognate Group: H6692 (bloom), H6731 (blossom), H6733 (flower)\nGreek Equivalents: G438 ἄνθος",
    6732: "צִיץ (Tsîyts | tseets)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6731 צִיץ ; bloom\nStrong's: Tsits, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Ziz.\n\nCognate Group: H6727 (Ziha), H6734 (fringe), H6723 (barren), H6732 (Ziz), H6731 (blossom), H6724 (dry place), H6726 (Zion)",
    6733: "צִיצָה (tsîytsâh | tsee-tsaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6731 צִיץ\nStrong's: a flower\nKJV: flower.\n\nCognate Group: H6692 (bloom), H6731 (blossom), H6733 (flower)",
    6734: "צִיצִת (tsîytsith | tsee-tseeth')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6731 צִיץ\nStrong's: a floral or wing-like projection, i.e. a forelock of hair, a tassel\nKJV: fringe, lock.\n\nCognate Group: H6727 (Ziha), H6734 (fringe), H6723 (barren), H6732 (Ziz), H6731 (blossom), H6724 (dry place), H6726 (Zion)\nGreek Equivalents: G2899 κράσπεδον",
    6735: "צִיר (tsîyr | tseer)\n\nDerivation: from H6696 צוּר\nStrong's: a hinge (as pressed in turning); also a throe (as a phys. or mental pressure); also a herald or erranddoer (as constrained by the principal)\nKJV: ambassador, hinge, messenger, pain, pang, sorrow. Compare H6736 צִיר .\n\nCognate Group: H6735 (ambassador)\nGreek Equivalents: G32 ἄγγελος, G5604 ὠδίν",
    6736: "צִיר (tsîyr | tseer)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6735 צִיר\nStrong's: a form (of beauty; as if pressed out, i.e. carved); hence, an (idolatrous) image\nKJV: beauty, idol.\n\nCognate Group: H6696 (adversary), H6736 (beauty), H6699 (form)",
    6737: "צָיַר (tsâyar | tsaw-yar')\n\nDerivation: a denominative from H6735 צִיר in the sense of ambassador\nStrong's: to make an errand, i.e. betake oneself\nKJV: make as if...had been ambassador.\n\nCognate Group: H6737 (make as if), H6679 (chase), H6720 (meat), H6718 ([idiom] catcheth)",
    6738: "צֵל (tsêl | tsale)\n\nDerivation: from H6751 צָלַל\nStrong's: shade, whether literal or figurative\nKJV: defence, shade(-ow).\n\nCognate Group: H6751 (begin to be dark), H6741 (Zillah), H6738 (defence), H6753 (Hazelelponi), H6769 (Zilthai), H6752 (shadow), H6757 (shadow of death)\nGreek Equivalents: G803 ἀσφάλεια, G2540 καιρός, G4639 σκιά, G4721 στέγη",
    6739: "צְלָא (tsᵉlâʼ | tsel-aw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) probably corresponding to H6760 צָלַע in the sense of bowing\nStrong's: pray\nKJV: pray.\n\nCognate Group: H6739 (pray)\nGreek Equivalents: G4336 προσεύχομαι",
    6740: "צָלָה (tsâlâh | tsaw-law')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to roast\nKJV: roast.\n\nCognate Group: H6740 (roast), H6748 (roast)",
    6741: "צִלָּה (Tsillâh | tsil-law')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6738 צֵל\nStrong's: Tsillah, an antediluvian woman\nKJV: Zillah.\n\nCognate Group: H6751 (begin to be dark), H6741 (Zillah), H6738 (defence), H6753 (Hazelelponi), H6769 (Zilthai), H6752 (shadow), H6757 (shadow of death)",
    6742: "צְלוּל (tsᵉlûwl | tsel-ool')\n\nDerivation: from H6749 צָלַל in the sense of rolling\nStrong's: a (round or flattened) cake\nKJV: cake.\n\nCognate Group: H6742 (cake)",
    6743: "צָלַח (tsâlach | tsaw-lakh')\n\nDerivation: or צָלֵחַ; a primitive root\nStrong's: to push forward, in various senses (literal or figurative, transitive or intransitive)\nKJV: break out, come (mightily), go over, be good, be meet, be profitable, (cause to, effect, make to, send) prosper(-ity, -ous, -ously).\n\nCognate Group: H6743 (break out)\nGreek Equivalents: G242 ἅλλομαι, G837 αὐξάνω, G2013 ἐπιτυγχάνω, G2106 εὐδοκέω, G2137 εὐοδόω, G2177 ἐφάλλομαι, G2720 κατευθύνω, G4098 πίπτω, G5530 χράομαι, G5539 χρήσιμος",
    6744: "צְלַח (tsᵉlach | tsel-akh')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6743 צָלַח\nStrong's: to advance (transitive or intransitive)\nKJV: promote, prosper.\n\nCognate Group: H6841 (he), H6755 (form), H6744 (promote), H6853 (bird)\nGreek Equivalents: G758 ἄρχων",
    6745: "צֵלָחָה (tsêlâchâh | tsay-law-khaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6743 צָלַח\nStrong's: something protracted or flattened out, i.e. a platter\nKJV: pan.\n\nCognate Group: H6746 (cruse), H6745 (pan), H6747 (bosom)",
    6746: "צְלֹחִית (tsᵉlôchîyth | tsel-o-kheeth')\n\nDerivation: from H6743 צָלַח\nStrong's: something prolonged or tall, i.e. a vial or salt-cellar\nKJV: cruse.\n\nCognate Group: H6746 (cruse), H6745 (pan), H6747 (bosom)",
    6747: "צַלַּחַת (tsallachath | tsal-lakh'-ath)\n\nDerivation: from H6743 צָלַח\nStrong's: something advanced or deep, i.e. a bowl; figuratively, the bosom\nKJV: bosom, dish.\n\nCognate Group: H6746 (cruse), H6745 (pan), H6747 (bosom)\nGreek Equivalents: G2859 κόλπος",
    6748: "צָלִי (tsâlîy | tsaw-lee')\n\nDerivation: passive participle of H6740 צָלָה\nStrong's: roasted\nKJV: roast.\n\nCognate Group: H6740 (roast), H6748 (roast)\nGreek Equivalents: G3702 ὀπτός",
    6749: "צָלַל (tsâlal | tsaw-lal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to tumble down, i.e. settle by awaving motion\nKJV: sink. Compare H6750 צָלַל, H6751 צָלַל .\n\nCognate Group: H6749 (sink)",
    6750: "צָלַל (tsâlal | tsaw-lal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (identical with through the idea of vibration)\nStrong's: to tinkle, i.e. rattle together (as the ears in reddening with shame, or the teeth in chattering with fear)\nKJV: quiver, tingle.\n\nCognate Group: H6767 (cymbal), H4698 (bell), H4700 (cymbals), H6750 (quiver)\nGreek Equivalents: G1416 δύνω, G2278 ἠχέω, G2525 καθίστημι",
    6751: "צָלַל (tsâlal | tsaw-lal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (identical with through the idea of hovering over (compare H6754 צֶלֶם))\nStrong's: to shade, as twilight or an opaque object\nKJV: begin to be dark, shadowing.\n\nCognate Group: H6751 (begin to be dark), H6741 (Zillah), H6738 (defence), H6753 (Hazelelponi), H6769 (Zilthai), H6752 (shadow), H6757 (shadow of death)",
    6752: "צֵלֶל (tsêlel | tsay'-lel)\n\nDerivation: from H6751 צָלַל\nStrong's: shade\nKJV: shadow.\n\nCognate Group: H6751 (begin to be dark), H6741 (Zillah), H6738 (defence), H6753 (Hazelelponi), H6769 (Zilthai), H6752 (shadow), H6757 (shadow of death)",
    6753: "צְלֶלְפֹּונִי (Tsᵉlelpôwnîy | tsel-el-po-nee')\n\nDerivation: from H6752 צֵלֶל and the active participle of H6437 פָּנָה ; shade-facing\nStrong's: Tselelponi, an Israelitess\nKJV: Hazelelponi (including the article).\n\nCognate Group: H6751 (begin to be dark), H6741 (Zillah), H6738 (defence), H6753 (Hazelelponi), H6769 (Zilthai), H6752 (shadow), H6757 (shadow of death)",
    6754: "צֶלֶם (tselem | tseh'-lem)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to shade\nStrong's: a phantom, i.e. (figuratively) illusion, resemblance; hence, a representative figure, especially an idol\nKJV: image, vain shew.\n\nCognate Group: H6754 (image), H6756 (Zalmon), H6758 (Zalmonah), H6759 (Zalmunna)\nGreek Equivalents: G1497 εἴδωλον, G1504 εἰκών, G3444 μορφή, G3667 ὁμοίωμα, G3799 ὄψις, G5179 τύπος",
    6755: "צֶלֶם (tselem | tseh'-lem)\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) or צְלֶם; (Aramaic), corresponding to H6754 צֶלֶם\nStrong's: an idolatrous figure\nKJV: form, image.\n\nCognate Group: H6841 (he), H6755 (form), H6744 (promote), H6853 (bird)",
    6756: "צַלְמֹון (Tsalmôwn | tsal-mone')\n\nDerivation: from H6754 צֶלֶם ; shady\nStrong's: Tsalmon, the name of a place in Palestine and of an Israelite\nKJV: Zalmon.\n\nCognate Group: H6754 (image), H6756 (Zalmon), H6758 (Zalmonah), H6759 (Zalmunna)",
    6757: "צַלְמָוֶת (tsalmâveth | tsal-maw'-veth)\n\nDerivation: from H6738 צֵל and H4194 מָוֶת\nStrong's: shade of death, i.e. the grave (figuratively, calamity)\nKJV: shadow of death.\n\nCognate Group: H6751 (begin to be dark), H6741 (Zillah), H6738 (defence), H6753 (Hazelelponi), H6769 (Zilthai), H6752 (shadow), H6757 (shadow of death)\nGreek Equivalents: G86 ᾅδης, G175 ἄκαρπος, G2288 θάνατος, G4639 σκιά, G4655 σκότος",
    6758: "צַלְמֹנָה (Tsalmônâh | tsal-mo-naw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6757 צַלְמָוֶת ; shadiness\nStrong's: Tsalmonah, a place in the Desert\nKJV: Zalmonah.\n\nCognate Group: H6754 (image), H6756 (Zalmon), H6758 (Zalmonah), H6759 (Zalmunna)",
    6759: "צַלְמֻנָּע (Tsalmunnâʻ | tsal-moon-naw')\n\nDerivation: from H6738 צֵל and H4513 מָנַע ; shade has been denied\nStrong's: Tsalmunna, a Midianite\nKJV: Zalmunna.\n\nCognate Group: H6754 (image), H6756 (Zalmon), H6758 (Zalmonah), H6759 (Zalmunna)",
    6760: "צָלַע (tsâlaʻ | tsaw-lah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root; probably to curve; used only as denominative from H6763 צֵלָע,\nStrong's: to limp (as if one-sided)\nKJV: halt.\n\nCognate Group: H6768 (Zelek), H6766 (Zelzah), H6764 (Zalaph), H6765 (Zelophehad), H6761 (adversity), H6760 (halt)\nGreek Equivalents: G4937 συντρίβω",
    6761: "צֶלַע (tselaʻ | tseh'-lah)\n\nDerivation: from H6760 צָלַע\nStrong's: a limping or full (figuratively)\nKJV: adversity, halt(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H6768 (Zelek), H6766 (Zelzah), H6764 (Zalaph), H6765 (Zelophehad), H6761 (adversity), H6760 (halt)",
    6762: "צֶלַע (Tselaʻ | tseh'-lah)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6761 צֶלַע\nStrong's: Tsela, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zelah.\n\nCognate Group: H6763 (beam), H6762 (Zelah)",
    6763: "צֵלָע (tsêlâʻ | tsay-law')\n\nDerivation: or (feminine) צַלְעָה; from H6760 צָלַע\nStrong's: a rib (as curved), literally (of the body) or figuratively (of a door, i.e. leaf); hence, a side, literally (of a person) or figuratively (of an object or the sky, i.e. quarter); architecturally, a (especially floor or ceiling) timber or plank (single or collective, i.e. a flooring)\nKJV: beam, board, chamber, corner, leaf, plank, rib, side (chamber).\n\nCognate Group: H6763 (beam), H6762 (Zelah)\nGreek Equivalents: G3148 μάστιξ, G3313 μέρος, G3586 ξύλον, G4009 πέρας, G4125 πλευρά",
    6764: "צָלָף (Tsâlâph | tsaw-lawf')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root of unknown meaning\nStrong's: Tsalaph, an Israelite\nKJV: Zalaph.\n\nCognate Group: H6768 (Zelek), H6766 (Zelzah), H6764 (Zalaph), H6765 (Zelophehad), H6761 (adversity), H6760 (halt)",
    6765: "צְלׇפְחָד (Tsᵉlophchâd | tsel-of-chawd')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6764 צָלָף and H259 אֶחָד\nStrong's: Tselophchad, an Israelite\nKJV: Zelophehad.\n\nCognate Group: H6768 (Zelek), H6766 (Zelzah), H6764 (Zalaph), H6765 (Zelophehad), H6761 (adversity), H6760 (halt)",
    6766: "צֶלְצַח (Tseltsach | tsel-tsakh')\n\nDerivation: from H6738 צֵל and H6703 צַח ; clear shade\nStrong's: Tseltsach, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zelzah.\n\nCognate Group: H6768 (Zelek), H6766 (Zelzah), H6764 (Zalaph), H6765 (Zelophehad), H6761 (adversity), H6760 (halt)",
    6767: "צְלָצַל (tsᵉlâtsal | tsel-aw-tsal')\n\nDerivation: from H6750 צָלַל reduplicated\nStrong's: a clatter, i.e. (abstractly) whirring (of wings); (concretely) a cricket; also a harpoon (as rattling), a cymbal (as clanging)\nKJV: cymbal, locust, shadowing, spear.\n\nCognate Group: H6767 (cymbal), H4698 (bell), H4700 (cymbals), H6750 (quiver)\nGreek Equivalents: G836 αὐλός, G2950 κύμβαλον",
    6768: "צֶלֶק (Tseleq | tseh'-lek)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to split; fissure\nStrong's: Tselek, an Israelite\nKJV: Zelek.\n\nCognate Group: H6768 (Zelek), H6766 (Zelzah), H6764 (Zalaph), H6765 (Zelophehad), H6761 (adversity), H6760 (halt)",
    6769: "צִלְּתַי (Tsillᵉthay | tsil-leth-ah'-ee)\n\nDerivation: from the feminine of H6738 צֵל ; shady\nStrong's: Tsillethai, the name of two Israelites\nKJV: Zilthai.\n\nCognate Group: H6751 (begin to be dark), H6741 (Zillah), H6738 (defence), H6753 (Hazelelponi), H6769 (Zilthai), H6752 (shadow), H6757 (shadow of death)",
    6770: "צָמֵא (tsâmêʼ | tsaw-may')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to thirst (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: (be a-, suffer) thirst(-y).\n\nCognate Group: H6771 (thirst), H6770 (thirst), H6773 (thirst), H6772 (thirst), H6774 (drought)\nGreek Equivalents: G1372 διψάω",
    6771: "צָמֵא (tsâmêʼ | tsaw-may')\n\nDerivation: from H6770 צָמֵא\nStrong's: thirsty (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: (that) thirst(-eth, -y).\n\nCognate Group: H6771 (thirst), H6770 (thirst), H6773 (thirst), H6772 (thirst), H6774 (drought)\nGreek Equivalents: G361 ἀναμάρτητος, G1373 δίψος",
    6772: "צָמָא (tsâmâʼ | tsaw-maw')\n\nDerivation: from H6770 צָמֵא\nStrong's: thirst (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: thirst(-y).\n\nCognate Group: H6771 (thirst), H6770 (thirst), H6773 (thirst), H6772 (thirst), H6774 (drought)\nGreek Equivalents: G1373 δίψος",
    6773: "צִמְאָה (tsimʼâh | tsim-aw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6772 צָמָא\nStrong's: thirst (figuratively, of libidinousnes)\nKJV: thirst.\n\nCognate Group: H6771 (thirst), H6770 (thirst), H6773 (thirst), H6772 (thirst), H6774 (drought)",
    6774: "צִמָּאֹון (tsimmâʼôwn | tsim-maw-one')\n\nDerivation: from H6771 צָמֵא\nStrong's: a thirsty place, i.e. desert\nKJV: drought, dry ground, thirsty land.\n\nCognate Group: H6771 (thirst), H6770 (thirst), H6773 (thirst), H6772 (thirst), H6774 (drought)",
    6775: "צָמַד (tsâmad | tsaw-mad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to link, i.e. gird; figuratively, to serve, (mentally) contrive\nKJV: fasten, frame, join (self).\n\nCognate Group: H6781 (bracelet), H6776 (acre), H6775 (fasten)\nGreek Equivalents: G5055 τελέω",
    6776: "צֶמֶד (tsemed | tseh'-med)\n\nDerivation: a yoke or team (i.e. pair)\nStrong's: hence, an acre (i.e. day's task for a yoke of cattle to plough)\nKJV: acre, couple, [idiom] together, two (donkeys), yoke (of oxen).\n\nCognate Group: H6781 (bracelet), H6776 (acre), H6775 (fasten)\nGreek Equivalents: G1016 βοῦς, G1091 γεώργιον, G2201 ζεῦγος",
    6777: "צַמָּה (tsammâh | tsam-maw')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to fasten on\nStrong's: a veil\nKJV: locks.\n\nCognate Group: H6777 (locks), H6782 (robber)",
    6778: "צַמּוּק (tsammûwq | tsam-mook')\n\nDerivation: from H6784 צָמַק\nStrong's: a cake of dried grapes\nKJV: bunch (cluster) of raisins.\n\nCognate Group: H6778 (bunch of raisins), H6784 (dry)",
    6779: "צָמַח (tsâmach | tsaw-makh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to sprout (transitive or intransitive, literal or figurative)\nKJV: bear, bring forth, (cause to, make to) bud (forth), (cause to, make to) grow (again, up), (cause to) spring (forth, up).\n\nCognate Group: H6780 (branch), H6779 (bear)\nGreek Equivalents: G393 ἀνατέλλω, G985 βλαστάνω, G1816 ἐξανατέλλω, G5453 φύω",
    6780: "צֶמַח (tsemach | tseh'-makh)\n\nDerivation: from H6779 צָמַח\nStrong's: a sprout (usually concrete), literal or figurative\nKJV: branch, bud, that which (where) grew (upon), spring(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H6780 (branch), H6779 (bear)\nGreek Equivalents: G395 ἀνατολή, G438 ἄνθος, G2479 ἰσχύς",
    6781: "צָמִיד (tsâmîyd | tsaw-meed')\n\nDerivation: or צָמִד; from H6775 צָמַד\nStrong's: a bracelet or arm-clasp; generally, a lid\nKJV: bracelet, covering.\n\nCognate Group: H6781 (bracelet), H6776 (acre), H6775 (fasten)\nGreek Equivalents: G1199 δεσμόν, G2611 καταδέω",
    6782: "צַמִּים (tsammîym | tsam-meem')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6777 צַמָּה\nStrong's: a noose (as fastening); figuratively, destruction\nKJV: robber.\n\nCognate Group: H6777 (locks), H6782 (robber)",
    6783: "צְמִיתֻת (tsᵉmîythuth | tsem-ee-thooth')\n\nDerivation: or צְמִתֻת; from H6789 צָמַת\nStrong's: excision, i.e. destruction; used only (adverbially) with prepositional prefix to extinction, i.e. perpetually\nKJV: ever.\n\nCognate Group: H6783 (ever), H6792 (sheep), H6790 (Zin), H6789 (consume)\nGreek Equivalents: G951 βεβαίωσις",
    6784: "צָמַק (tsâmaq | tsaw-mak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to dry up\nKJV: dry.\n\nCognate Group: H6778 (bunch of raisins), H6784 (dry)\nGreek Equivalents: G3584 ξηρός",
    6785: "צֶמֶר (tsemer | tseh'-mer)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root probably meaning to be shaggy\nStrong's: wool\nKJV: wool(-len).\n\nCognate Group: H6787 (Zemaraim), H6788 (highest branch), H6786 (Zemarite), H6785 (wool)\nGreek Equivalents: G2053 ἔριον, G2440 ἱμάτιον",
    6786: "צְמָרִי (Tsᵉmârîy | tsem-aw-ree')\n\nDerivation: patrial from an unused name of a place in Palestine\nStrong's: a Tsemarite or branch of the Canaanites\nKJV: Zemarite.\n\nCognate Group: H6787 (Zemaraim), H6788 (highest branch), H6786 (Zemarite), H6785 (wool)",
    6787: "צְמָרַיִם (Tsᵉmârayim | tsem-aw-rah'-yim)\n\nDerivation: dual of H6785 צֶמֶר ; double fleece\nStrong's: Tsemarajim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zemaraim.\n\nCognate Group: H6787 (Zemaraim), H6788 (highest branch), H6786 (Zemarite), H6785 (wool)",
    6788: "צַמֶּרֶת (tsammereth | tsam-meh'-reth)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6785 צֶמֶר\nStrong's: fleeciness, i.e. foliage\nKJV: highest branch, top.\n\nCognate Group: H6787 (Zemaraim), H6788 (highest branch), H6786 (Zemarite), H6785 (wool)\nGreek Equivalents: G746 ἀρχή, G1588 ἐκλεκτός",
    6789: "צָמַת (tsâmath | tsaw-math')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to extirpate (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: consume, cut off, destroy, vanish.\n\nCognate Group: H6783 (ever), H6792 (sheep), H6790 (Zin), H6789 (consume)\nGreek Equivalents: G615 ἀποκτείνω, G853 ἀφανίζω, G1559 ἐκδιώκω, G1842 ἐξολοθρεύω, G2190 ἐχθρός, G2289 θανατόω, G5015 ταράσσω",
    6790: "צִן (Tsin | tseen)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to prick; a crag\nStrong's: Tsin, a part of the Desert\nKJV: Zin.\n\nCognate Group: H6783 (ever), H6792 (sheep), H6790 (Zin), H6789 (consume)",
    6791: "צֵן (tsên | tsane)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to be prickly\nStrong's: a thorn; hence, a cactus-hedge\nKJV: thorn.\n\nCognate Group: H6791 (thorn), H6796 (thorn), H6793 (buckler)\nGreek Equivalents: G1002 βολίς, G5146 τρίβολος",
    6792: "צֹנֵא (tsônêʼ | tso-nay')\n\nDerivation: or צֹנֶה; for H6629 צֹאן\nStrong's: a flock\nKJV: sheep.\n\nCognate Group: H6783 (ever), H6792 (sheep), H6790 (Zin), H6789 (consume)\nGreek Equivalents: G2934 κτῆνος",
    6793: "צִנָּה (tsinnâh | tsin-naw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6791 צֵן\nStrong's: a hook (as pointed); also a (large) shield (as if guarding by prickliness); also cold (as piercing)\nKJV: buckler, cold, hook, shield, target.\n\nCognate Group: H6791 (thorn), H6796 (thorn), H6793 (buckler)\nGreek Equivalents: G1841 ἔξοδος, G2375 θυρεός, G3696 ὅπλον",
    6794: "צִנּוּר (tsinnûwr | tsin-noor')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root perhaps meaning to be hollow\nStrong's: a culvert\nKJV: gutter, water-spout.\n\nCognate Group: H6804 (pipe), H6794 (gutter)",
    6795: "צָנַח (tsânach | tsaw-nakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to alight; (transitive) to cause to descend, i.e. drive down\nKJV: fasten, light (from off).\n\nCognate Group: H6795 (fasten)",
    6796: "צָנִין (tsânîyn | tsaw-neen')\n\nDerivation: or צָנִן; from the same as H6791 צֵן\nStrong's: a thorn\nKJV: thorn.\n\nCognate Group: H6791 (thorn), H6796 (thorn), H6793 (buckler)",
    6797: "צָנִיף (tsânîyph | tsaw-neef')\n\nDerivation: or צָנֹוף; or (feminine) צָנִיפָה; from H6801 צָנַף\nStrong's: a head-dress (i.e. piece of cloth wrapped around)\nKJV: diadem, hood, mitre.\n\nCognate Group: H6797 (diadem), H6801 (be attired), H6802 ([idiom] toss), H4701 (diadem)\nGreek Equivalents: G1238 διάδημα",
    6798: "צָנַם (tsânam | tsaw-nam')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to blast or shrink\nKJV: withered.\n\nCognate Group: H6798 (withered)",
    6799: "צְנָן (Tsᵉnân | tsen-awn')\n\nDerivation: probably for H6630 צַאֲנָן\nStrong's: Tsenan, a place near Palestine\nKJV: Zenan.\n\nCognate Group: H6629 (cattle), H6630 (Zaanan), H6799 (Zenan)",
    6800: "צָנַע (tsânaʻ | tsaw-nah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to humiliate\nKJV: humbly, lowly.\n\nCognate Group: H6800 (humbly)\nGreek Equivalents: G2092 ἕτοιμος, G5011 ταπεινός",
    6801: "צָנַף (tsânaph | tsaw-naf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to wrap, i.e. roll or dress\nKJV: be attired, [idiom] surely, violently turn.\n\nCognate Group: H6797 (diadem), H6801 (be attired), H6802 ([idiom] toss), H4701 (diadem)\nGreek Equivalents: G4060 περιτίθημι",
    6802: "צְנֵפָה (tsᵉnêphâh | tsen-ay-faw')\n\nDerivation: from H6801 צָנַף\nStrong's: a ball\nKJV: [idiom] toss.\n\nCognate Group: H6797 (diadem), H6801 (be attired), H6802 ([idiom] toss), H4701 (diadem)",
    6803: "צִנְצֶנֶת (tsintseneth | tsin-tseh'-neth)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6791 צֵן\nStrong's: a vase (probably a vial tapering at the top)\nKJV: pot.\n\nCognate Group: H6803 (pot), H6793 (buckler)\nGreek Equivalents: G4713 στάμνος",
    6804: "צַנְתָּרָה (tsantârâh | tsan-taw-raw')\n\nDerivation: probably from the same as H6794 צִנּוּר\nStrong's: a tube\nKJV: pipe.\n\nCognate Group: H6804 (pipe), H6794 (gutter)",
    6805: "צָעַד (tsâʻad | tsaw-ad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to pace, i.e. step regularly; (upward) to mount; (along) to march; (down and causatively) to hurl\nKJV: bring, go, march (through), run over.\n\nCognate Group: H4703 (going), H6807 (going), H6805 (bring), H6806 (pace)\nGreek Equivalents: G318 ἀνάγκη, G522 ἀπαίρω, G1224 διαβαίνω, G1910 ἐπιβαίνω, G4198 πορεύομαι",
    6806: "צַעַד (tsaʻad | tsah'-ad)\n\nDerivation: from H6804 צַנְתָּרָה\nStrong's: a pace or regular step\nKJV: pace, step.\n\nCognate Group: H4703 (going), H6807 (going), H6805 (bring), H6806 (pace)\nGreek Equivalents: G2487 ἴχνος, G4197 πορεία",
    6807: "צְעָדָה (tsᵉʻâdâh | tseh-aw-daw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6806 צַעַד\nStrong's: a march; (concretely) an (ornamental) ankle-chain\nKJV: going, ornament of the legs.\n\nCognate Group: H4703 (going), H6807 (going), H6805 (bring), H6806 (pace)",
    6808: "צָעָה (tsâʻâh | tsaw-aw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to tip over (for the purpose of spilling or pouring out), i.e. (figuratively) depopulate; by implication, to imprison or conquer; (reflexive) to lie down (for coitus)\nKJV: captive exile, travelling, (cause to) wander(-er).\n\nCognate Group: H6808 (captive exile)\nGreek Equivalents: G2827 κλίνω",
    6809: "צָעִיף (tsâʻîyph | tsaw-eef')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to wrap over\nStrong's: a veil\nKJV: vail.\n\nCognate Group: H6809 (vail)",
    6810: "צָעִיר (tsâʻîyr | tsaw-eer')\n\nDerivation: or צָעֹור; from H6819 צָעַר\nStrong's: little; (in number) few; (in age) young, (in value) ignoble\nKJV: least, little (one), small (one), [phrase] young(-er, -est).\n\nCognate Group: H4706 (Mizar), H4705 (little one), H6730 (Zior), H6811 (Zair), H4704 (little), H6686 (Zuar), H6810 (least), H6819 (be brought low), H6820 (Zoar), H6812 (youth)\nGreek Equivalents: G721 ἀρνίον, G1640 ἐλάσσων, G3398 μικρός, G3501 νέος",
    6811: "צָעִיר (Tsâʻîyr | tsaw-eer')\n\nDerivation: the same as H6810 צָעִיר\nStrong's: Tsair, a place in Idumaea\nKJV: Zair.\n\nCognate Group: H4706 (Mizar), H4705 (little one), H6730 (Zior), H6811 (Zair), H4704 (little), H6686 (Zuar), H6810 (least), H6819 (be brought low), H6820 (Zoar), H6812 (youth)",
    6812: "צְעִירָה (tsᵉʻîyrâh | tseh-ee-raw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6810 צָעִיר\nStrong's: smallness (of age), i.e. juvenility\nKJV: youth.\n\nCognate Group: H4706 (Mizar), H4705 (little one), H6730 (Zior), H6811 (Zair), H4704 (little), H6686 (Zuar), H6810 (least), H6819 (be brought low), H6820 (Zoar), H6812 (youth)\nGreek Equivalents: G3503 νεότης",
    6813: "צָעַן (tsâʻan | tsaw-an')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to load up (beasts), i.e. to migrate\nKJV: be taken down.\n\nCognate Group: H6813 (be taken down), H6815 (Zaannannim), H6814 (Zoan)\nGreek Equivalents: G4531 σαλεύω, G4579 σείω",
    6814: "צֹעַן (Tsôʻan | tso'-an)\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Tsoan, a place in Egypt\nKJV: Zoan.\n\nCognate Group: H6813 (be taken down), H6815 (Zaannannim), H6814 (Zoan)",
    6815: "צַעֲנַנִּים (Tsaʻănannîym | tsah-an-an-neem')\n\nDerivation: or (dual) צַעֲנַיִם; plural from H6813 צָעַן ; removals\nStrong's: Tsaanannim or Tsaanajim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zaannannim, Zaanaim.\n\nCognate Group: H6813 (be taken down), H6815 (Zaannannim), H6814 (Zoan)",
    6816: "צַעְצֻעַ (tsaʻtsuaʻ | tsah-tsoo'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to bestrew with carvings\nStrong's: sculpture\nKJV: image (work).\n\nCognate Group: H6816 (image)",
    6817: "צָעַק (tsâʻaq | tsaw-ak')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to shriek; (by implication) to proclaim (an assembly)\nKJV: [idiom] at all, call together, cry (out), gather (selves) (together).\n\nCognate Group: H6817 ([idiom] at all), H6818 (cry)\nGreek Equivalents: G305 ἀναβαίνω, G310 ἀναβοάω, G994 βοάω, G2896 κράζω, G3853 παραγγέλλω, G4863 συνάγω",
    6818: "צַעֲקָה (tsaʻăqâh | tsah-ak-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H6817 צָעַק\nStrong's: a shriek\nKJV: cry(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: H6817 ([idiom] at all), H6818 (cry)",
    6819: "צָעַר (tsâʻar | tsaw-ar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be small, i.e. (figuratively) ignoble\nKJV: be brought low, little one, be small.\n\nCognate Group: H4706 (Mizar), H4705 (little one), H6730 (Zior), H6811 (Zair), H4704 (little), H6686 (Zuar), H6810 (least), H6819 (be brought low), H6820 (Zoar), H6812 (youth)\nGreek Equivalents: G1642 ἐλαττόω, G3641 ὀλίγος",
    6820: "צֹעַר (Tsôʻar | tso'ar)\n\nDerivation: from H6819 צָעַר ; little\nStrong's: Tsoar, a place East of the Jordan\nKJV: Zoar.\n\nCognate Group: H4706 (Mizar), H4705 (little one), H6730 (Zior), H6811 (Zair), H4704 (little), H6686 (Zuar), H6810 (least), H6819 (be brought low), H6820 (Zoar), H6812 (youth)",
    6821: "צָפַד (tsâphad | tsaw-fad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to adhere\nKJV: cleave.\n\nCognate Group: H6821 (cleave)\nGreek Equivalents: G4078 πήγνυμι",
    6822: "צָפָה (tsâphâh | tsaw-faw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: properly, to lean forward, i.e. to peer into the distance; by implication, to observe, await\nKJV: behold, espy, look up (well), wait for, (keep the) watch(-man).\n\nCognate Group: H6837 (Ziphion), H6836 (watching), H4709 (Mitspah), H6827 (Zephon), H6831 (Zephonites), H4707 (watch tower), H6822 (behold), H6825 (Zephi), H4708 (Mizpeh), H6839 (Zophim)\nGreek Equivalents: G1914 ἐπιβλέπω, G2572 καλύπτω, G2657 κατανοέω, G2885 κοσμέω, G4023 περιέχω, G4649 σκοπός, G5442 φυλάσσω",
    6823: "צָפָה (tsâphâh | tsaw-faw')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root (probably identical with H6822 צָפָה through the idea of expansion in outlook, transferring to action)\nStrong's: to sheet over (especially with metal)\nKJV: cover, overlay.\n\nCognate Group: H6826 (covering), H6858 (chapiter), H6823 (cover), H6844 (watchtower)\nGreek Equivalents: G5558 χρυσόω",
    6824: "צָפָה (tsâphâh | tsaw-faw')\n\nDerivation: from H6823 צָפָה\nStrong's: an inundation (as covering)\nKJV: [idiom] swimmest.\n\nCognate Group: H6824 ([idiom] swimmest), H6687 (flow), H6688 (honeycomb), H7436 (Ramathaimzophim), H6689 (Zophai)",
    6825: "צְפֹו (Tsᵉphôw | tsef-o')\n\nDerivation: or צְפִי; from H6822 צָפָה ; observant\nStrong's: Tsepho or Tsephi, an Idumaean\nKJV: Zephi, Zepho.\n\nCognate Group: H6837 (Ziphion), H6836 (watching), H4709 (Mitspah), H6827 (Zephon), H6831 (Zephonites), H4707 (watch tower), H6822 (behold), H6825 (Zephi), H4708 (Mizpeh), H6839 (Zophim)",
    6826: "צִפּוּי (tsippûwy | tsip-poo'-ee)\n\nDerivation: from H6823 צָפָה\nStrong's: encasement (with metal)\nKJV: covering, overlaying.\n\nCognate Group: H6826 (covering), H6858 (chapiter), H6823 (cover), H6844 (watchtower)",
    6827: "צְפֹון (Tsᵉphôwn | tsef-one')\n\nDerivation: probably for H6837 צִפְיֹון\nStrong's: Tsephon, an Israelite\nKJV: Zephon.\n\nCognate Group: H6837 (Ziphion), H6836 (watching), H4709 (Mitspah), H6827 (Zephon), H6831 (Zephonites), H4707 (watch tower), H6822 (behold), H6825 (Zephi), H4708 (Mizpeh), H6839 (Zophim)",
    6828: "צָפֹון (tsâphôwn | tsaw-fone')\n\nDerivation: or צָפֹן; from H6845 צָפַן\nStrong's: properly, hidden, i.e. dark; used only of the north as aquarter (gloomy and unknown)\nKJV: north(-ern, side, -ward, wind).\n\nCognate Group: H6830 (northern), H6846 (Zephaniah), H4710 (hidden thing), H6829 (Zaphon), H6828 (north), H6845 (esteem), H6840 (hid), H6847 (Zaphnath-paaneah)\nGreek Equivalents: G1005 βοῤῥᾶς, G2176 εὐώνυμος",
    6829: "צָפֹון (Tsâphôwn | tsaw-fone')\n\nDerivation: the same as H6828 צָפֹון ; boreal\nStrong's: Tsaphon, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zaphon.\n\nCognate Group: H6830 (northern), H6846 (Zephaniah), H4710 (hidden thing), H6829 (Zaphon), H6828 (north), H6845 (esteem), H6840 (hid), H6847 (Zaphnath-paaneah)",
    6830: "צְפֹונִי (tsᵉphôwnîy | tsef-o-nee')\n\nDerivation: from H6828 צָפֹון\nStrong's: northern\nKJV: northern.\n\nCognate Group: H6830 (northern), H6846 (Zephaniah), H4710 (hidden thing), H6829 (Zaphon), H6828 (north), H6845 (esteem), H6840 (hid), H6847 (Zaphnath-paaneah)",
    6831: "צְפֹונִי (Tsᵉphôwnîy | tsef-o-nee')\n\nDerivation: patronymically from H6827 צְפֹון\nStrong's: a Tsephonite, or (collectively) descendants of Tsephon\nKJV: Zephonites.\n\nCognate Group: H6837 (Ziphion), H6836 (watching), H4709 (Mitspah), H6827 (Zephon), H6831 (Zephonites), H4707 (watch tower), H6822 (behold), H6825 (Zephi), H4708 (Mizpeh), H6839 (Zophim)",
    6832: "צְפוּעַ (tsᵉphûwaʻ | tsef-oo'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6848 צֶפַע\nStrong's: excrement (as protruded)\nKJV: dung.\n\nCognate Group: H6832 (dung)",
    6833: "צִפֹּור (tsippôwr | tsip-pore')\n\nDerivation: or צִפֹּר; from H6852 צָפַר\nStrong's: a little bird (as hopping)\nKJV: bird, fowl, sparrow.\n\nCognate Group: H6833 (bird), H6855 (Zipporah), H6834 (Zippor)\nGreek Equivalents: G3732 ὄρνεον, G4071 πετεινόν, G4765 στρουθίον",
    6834: "צִפֹּור (Tsippôwr | tsip-pore')\n\nDerivation: the same as H6833 צִפֹּור\nStrong's: Tsippor, a Moabite\nKJV: Zippor.\n\nCognate Group: H6833 (bird), H6855 (Zipporah), H6834 (Zippor)",
    6835: "צַפַּחַת (tsappachath | tsap-pakh'-ath)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to expand\nStrong's: a saucer (as flat)\nKJV: cruse.\n\nCognate Group: H6835 (cruse), H6690 (Zophah), H6838 (wafer)",
    6836: "צְפִיָּה (tsᵉphîyâh | tsef-ee-yaw')\n\nDerivation: from H6822 צָפָה\nStrong's: watchfulness\nKJV: watching.\n\nCognate Group: H6837 (Ziphion), H6836 (watching), H4709 (Mitspah), H6827 (Zephon), H6831 (Zephonites), H4707 (watch tower), H6822 (behold), H6825 (Zephi), H4708 (Mizpeh), H6839 (Zophim)",
    6837: "צִפְיֹון (Tsiphyôwn | tsif-yone')\n\nDerivation: from H6822 צָפָה ; watch-tower\nStrong's: Tsiphjon, an Israelite\nKJV: Ziphion. Compare H6827 צְפֹון .\n\nCognate Group: H6837 (Ziphion), H6836 (watching), H4709 (Mitspah), H6827 (Zephon), H6831 (Zephonites), H4707 (watch tower), H6822 (behold), H6825 (Zephi), H4708 (Mizpeh), H6839 (Zophim)",
    6838: "צַפִּיחִת (tsappîychith | tsap-pee-kheeth')\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6835 צַפַּחַת\nStrong's: a flat thin cake\nKJV: wafer.\n\nCognate Group: H6835 (cruse), H6690 (Zophah), H6838 (wafer)",
    6839: "צֹפִים (Tsôphîym | tso-feem')\n\nDerivation: plural of active participle of H6822 צָפָה ; watchers\nStrong's: Tsophim, a place East of the Jordan\nKJV: Zophim.\n\nCognate Group: H6837 (Ziphion), H6836 (watching), H4709 (Mitspah), H6827 (Zephon), H6831 (Zephonites), H4707 (watch tower), H6822 (behold), H6825 (Zephi), H4708 (Mizpeh), H6839 (Zophim)",
    6840: "צָפִין (tsâphîyn | tsaw-feen')\n\nDerivation: from H6845 צָפַן\nStrong's: a treasure (as hidden)\nKJV: hid.\n\nCognate Group: H6830 (northern), H6846 (Zephaniah), H4710 (hidden thing), H6829 (Zaphon), H6828 (north), H6845 (esteem), H6840 (hid), H6847 (Zaphnath-paaneah)",
    6841: "צְפִיר (tsᵉphîyr | tsef-eer')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6842 צָפִיר\nStrong's: a he-goat\nKJV: he (goat).\n\nCognate Group: H6841 (he), H6755 (form), H6744 (promote), H6853 (bird)",
    6842: "צָפִיר (tsâphîyr | tsaw-feer')\n\nDerivation: from H6852 צָפַר\nStrong's: a male goat (as prancing)\nKJV: (he) goat.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)\nGreek Equivalents: G5131 τράγος",
    6843: "צְפִירָה (tsᵉphîyrâh | tsef-ee-raw')\n\nDerivation: feminine formed like H6842 צָפִיר\nStrong's: a crown (as encircling the head); also a turn of affairs (i.e. mishap)\nKJV: diadem, morning.\n\nCognate Group: H6843 (diadem)\nGreek Equivalents: G4120 πλέκω",
    6844: "צָפִית (tsâphîyth | tsaw-feeth')\n\nDerivation: from H6822 צָפָה\nStrong's: a sentry\nKJV: watchtower.\n\nCognate Group: H6826 (covering), H6858 (chapiter), H6823 (cover), H6844 (watchtower)",
    6845: "צָפַן (tsâphan | tsaw-fan')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to hide (by covering over); by implication, to hoard or reserve; figuratively to deny; specifically (favorably) to protect, (unfavorably) to lurk\nKJV: esteem, hide(-den one, self), lay up, lurk (be set) privily, (keep) secret(-ly, place).\n\nCognate Group: H6830 (northern), H6846 (Zephaniah), H4710 (hidden thing), H6829 (Zaphon), H6828 (north), H6845 (esteem), H6840 (hid), H6847 (Zaphnath-paaneah)\nGreek Equivalents: G39 ἅγιον, G578 ἀποβλέπω, G1325 δίδωμι, G1470 ἐγκρύπτω, G1587 ἐκλείπω, G1984 ἐπισκοπή, G2192 ἔχω, G2343 θησαυρίζω, G2928 κρύπτω, G5083 τηρέω",
    6846: "צְפַנְיָה (Tsᵉphanyâh | tsef-an-yaw')\n\nDerivation: or צְפַנְיָהוּ; from H6845 צָפַן and H3050 יָהּ ; Jah has secreted\nStrong's: Tsephanjah, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: Zephaniah.\n\nCognate Group: H6830 (northern), H6846 (Zephaniah), H4710 (hidden thing), H6829 (Zaphon), H6828 (north), H6845 (esteem), H6840 (hid), H6847 (Zaphnath-paaneah)",
    6847: "צׇפְנַת פַּעְנֵחַ (Tsophnath Paʻnêach | tsof-nath' pah-nay'-akh)\n\nDerivation: of Egyptian derivation\nStrong's: Tsophnath-Paneach, Joseph's Egyptian name\nKJV: Zaphnath-paaneah.\n\nCognate Group: H6830 (northern), H6846 (Zephaniah), H4710 (hidden thing), H6829 (Zaphon), H6828 (north), H6845 (esteem), H6840 (hid), H6847 (Zaphnath-paaneah)",
    6848: "צֶפַע (tsephaʻ | tseh'-fah or tsiphoniy tsif-o-nee')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to extrude\nStrong's: a viper (as thrusting out the tongue, i.e. hissing)\nKJV: adder, cockatrice.\n\nCognate Group: H6848 (adder)\nGreek Equivalents: G785 ἀσπίς, G2289 θανατόω",
    6849: "צְפִעָה (tsᵉphiʻâh | tsef-ee-aw')\n\nDerivation: feminine from the same as H6848 צֶפַע\nStrong's: an outcast thing\nKJV: issue.\n\nCognate Group: H6849 (issue)",
    6850: "צָפַף (tsâphaph | tsaw-faf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to coo or chirp (as a bird)\nKJV: chatter, peep, whisper.\n\nCognate Group: H6850 (chatter), H6851 (willow tree)\nGreek Equivalents: G471 ἀντέπω, G770 ἀσθενέω, G1093 γῆ, G5455 φωνέω",
    6851: "צַפְצָפָה (tsaphtsâphâh | tsaf-tsaw-faw')\n\nDerivation: from H6687 צוּף\nStrong's: a willow (as growing in overflowed places)\nKJV: willow tree.\n\nCognate Group: H6850 (chatter), H6851 (willow tree)\nGreek Equivalents: G1914 ἐπιβλέπω",
    6852: "צָפַר (tsâphar | tsaw-far')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to skip about, i.e. return\nKJV: depart early.\n\nCognate Group: H6852 (depart early)\nGreek Equivalents: G1633 ἐκχωρέω",
    6853: "צְפַר (tsᵉphar | tsef-ar')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6833 צִפֹּור\nStrong's: a bird.\nKJV: bird.\n\nCognate Group: H6841 (he), H6755 (form), H6744 (promote), H6853 (bird)",
    6854: "צְפַרְדֵּעַ (tsᵉphardêaʻ | tsef-ar-day'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from H6852 צָפַר and a word elsewhere unused meaning a swamp\nStrong's: a marsh-leaper, i.e. frog\nKJV: frog.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)\nGreek Equivalents: G944 βάτραχος",
    6855: "צִפֹּרָה (Tsippôrâh | tsip-po-raw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6833 צִפֹּור ; bird\nStrong's: Tsipporah, Moses' wife\nKJV: Zipporah.\n\nCognate Group: H6833 (bird), H6855 (Zipporah), H6834 (Zippor)",
    6856: "צִפֹּרֶן (tsippôren | tsip-po'-ren)\n\nDerivation: from H6852 צָפַר (in the denominative sense (from H6833 צִפֹּור) of scratching)\nStrong's: properly, a claw, i.e. (human) nail; also the point of astyle (or pen, tipped with adamant)\nKJV: nail, point.\n\nCognate Group: H6856 (nail)",
    6857: "צְפַת (Tsᵉphath | tsef-ath')\n\nDerivation: from H6822 צָפָה ; watch-tower\nStrong's: Tsephath, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zephath.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)",
    6858: "צֶפֶת (tsepheth | tseh'-feth)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to encircle\nStrong's: a capital of a column\nKJV: chapiter.\n\nCognate Group: H6826 (covering), H6858 (chapiter), H6823 (cover), H6844 (watchtower)",
    6859: "צְפָתָה (Tsᵉphâthâh | tsef-aw'-thaw)\n\nDerivation: the same as H6857 צְפַת\nStrong's: Tsephathah, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zephathah.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)",
    6860: "צִקְלַג (Tsiqlag | tsik-lag')\n\nDerivation: or צִיקלַג; (1 Chronicles 12:1,20), of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Tsiklag or Tsikelag, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Ziklag.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)",
    6861: "צִקְלֹן (tsiqlôn | tsik-lone')\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to wind\nStrong's: a sack (as tied at the mouth)\nKJV: husk.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)",
    6862: "צַר (tsar | tsar)\n\nDerivation: or צָר; from H6887 צָרַר ; compare as in H6864 צֹר \nStrong's: narrow; (as a noun) a tight place (usually figuratively, i.e. trouble); also a pebble ; (transitive) an opponent (as crowding)\nKJV: adversary, afflicted(-tion), anguish, close, distress, enemy, flint, foe, narrow, small, sorrow, strait, tribulation, trouble.\n\nCognate Group: H6862 (adversary), H3334 (be distressed), H6872 (bag), H4712 (distress), H6887 (adversary), H6869 (adversary), H1222 (gold)\nGreek Equivalents: G318 ἀνάγκη, G972 βίαιος, G1228 διάβολος, G1587 ἐκλείπω, G2190 ἐχθρός, G2346 θλίβω, G2347 θλῖψις, G2556 κακός, G4728 στενός, G4731 στερεός, G5227 ὑπεναντίος",
    6863: "צֵר (Tsêr | tsare)\n\nDerivation: from H6887 צָרַר ; rock\nStrong's: Tser, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zer.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)",
    6864: "צֹר (tsôr | tsore)\n\nDerivation: from H6696 צוּר\nStrong's: a stone (as if pressed hard or to a point); (by implication, of use) a knife\nKJV: flint, sharp stone.\n\nCognate Group: H6872 (bag), H6864 (flint), H6862 (adversary), H6890 (Zareth-shahar), H6889 (Zereth), H6888 (Zererath), H6891 (Zarthan)\nGreek Equivalents: G5586 ψῆφος",
    6865: "צֹר (Tsôr | tsore)\n\nDerivation: or צֹור; the same as H6864 צֹר ; a rock\nStrong's: Tsor, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Tyre, Tyrus.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)\nGreek Equivalents: G5184 Τύρος, G4947 Συρία",
    6866: "צָרַב (tsârab | tsaw-rab')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to burn\nKJV: burn.\n\nCognate Group: H6868 (Zereda), H6867 (burning), H6866 (burn)\nGreek Equivalents: G2618 κατακαίω",
    6867: "צָרֶבֶת (tsârebeth | tsaw-reh'-beth)\n\nDerivation: from H6686 צוּעָר\nStrong's: conflagration (of fire or disease)\nKJV: burning, inflammation.\n\nCognate Group: H6868 (Zereda), H6867 (burning), H6866 (burn)\nGreek Equivalents: G5481 χαρακτήρ",
    6868: "צְרֵדָה (Tsᵉrêdâh | tser-ay-daw')\n\nDerivation: or צְרֵדָתָה; apparently from an unused root meaning to pierce; puncture\nStrong's: Tseredah, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zereda, Zeredathah.\n\nCognate Group: H6868 (Zereda), H6867 (burning), H6866 (burn)",
    6869: "צָרָה (tsârâh | tsaw-raw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6862 צַר\nStrong's: tightness (i.e. figuratively, trouble); transitively, a female rival:\nKJV: adversary, adversity, affliction, anguish, distress, tribulation, trouble.\n\nCognate Group: H1226 (dearth), H1223 (Bozrah), H1208 (vintage), H1221 (Bezer), H1220 (gold defence), H1224 (Bozrah), H4013 (fenced), H1230 (Bakbakkar), H6869 (adversary), H1219 (cut off), H1225 (stronghold), H1210 (vintage)\nGreek Equivalents: G640 ἀπορία, G2347 θλῖψις, G4726 στεναγμός",
    6870: "צְרוּיָה (Tsᵉrûwyâh | tser-oo-yaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine passive participle from the same as H6875 צְרִי ; wounded\nStrong's: Tserujah, an Israelitess\nKJV: Zeruiah.\n\nCognate Group: H6874 (Zeri), H6870 (Zeruiah), H6875 (balm)",
    6871: "צְרוּעָה (Tsᵉrûwʻâh | tser-oo-aw')\n\nDerivation: feminine passive participle of H6879 צָרַע ; leprous\nStrong's: Tseruah, an Israelitess\nKJV: Zeruah.\n\nCognate Group: H6880 (hornet), H6881 (Zareah), H6883 (leprosy), H6879 (leper), H6871 (Zeruah), H6882 (Zorites)",
    6872: "צְרֹור (tsᵉrôwr | tser-ore')\n\nDerivation: or (shorter) צְרֹר; from H6887 צָרַר\nStrong's: a parcel (as packed up); also a kernel or particle (as if a package)\nKJV: bag, [idiom] bendeth, bundle, least grain, small stone.\n\nCognate Group: H6862 (adversary), H3334 (be distressed), H6872 (bag), H4712 (distress), H6887 (adversary), H6869 (adversary), H1222 (gold)\nGreek Equivalents: G905 βαλάντιον, G1199 δεσμόν, G3037 λίθος",
    6873: "צָרַח (tsârach | tsaw-rakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to be clear (in tone, i.e. shrill), i.e. to whoop\nKJV: cry, roar.\n\nCognate Group: H6873 (cry)\nGreek Equivalents: G994 βοάω",
    6874: "צְרִי (Tsᵉrîy | tser-ee')\n\nDerivation: the same as H6875 צְרִי\nStrong's: Tseri, an Israelite\nKJV: Zeri. Compare H3340 יִצְרִי .\n\nCognate Group: H6874 (Zeri), H6870 (Zeruiah), H6875 (balm)",
    6875: "צְרִי (tsᵉrîy | tser-ee')\n\nDerivation: or צֳרִי; from an unused root meaning to crack (as by pressure), hence, to leak\nStrong's: distillation, i.e. balsam\nKJV: balm.\n\nCognate Group: H6874 (Zeri), H6870 (Zeruiah), H6875 (balm)",
    6876: "צֹרִי (Tsôrîy | tso-ree')\n\nDerivation: patrial from H6865 צֹר\nStrong's: a Tsorite or inhabitant of Tsor (i.e. Syrian)\nKJV: (man) of Tyre.\n\nCognate Group: H6863 (Zer), H6857 (Zephath), H6854 (frog), H6876 (of Tyre), H6865 (Tyre), H6691 (Zophar), H6860 (Ziklag), H6859 (Zephathah), H6842 (goat), H6861 (husk)",
    6877: "צְרִיחַ (tsᵉrîyach | tser-ee'-akh)\n\nDerivation: from H6873 צָרַח in the sense of clearness of vision\nStrong's: a citadel\nKJV: high place, hold.\n\nCognate Group: H6877 (high place)\nGreek Equivalents: G3794 ὀχύρωμα",
    6878: "צֹרֶךְ (tsôrek | tso'-rek)\n\nDerivation: from an unused root meaning to need\nStrong's: need\nKJV: need.\n\nCognate Group: H6878 (need)\nGreek Equivalents: G5532 χρεία",
    6879: "צָרַע (tsâraʻ | tsaw-rah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to scourge, i.e. (intransitive and figurative) to be stricken with leprosy\nKJV: leper, leprous.\n\nCognate Group: H6880 (hornet), H6881 (Zareah), H6883 (leprosy), H6879 (leper), H6871 (Zeruah), H6882 (Zorites)\nGreek Equivalents: G3015 λεπρός",
    6880: "צִרְעָה (tsirʻâh | tsir-aw')\n\nDerivation: from H6879 צָרַע\nStrong's: a wasp (as stinging)\nKJV: hornet.\n\nCognate Group: H6880 (hornet), H6881 (Zareah), H6883 (leprosy), H6879 (leper), H6871 (Zeruah), H6882 (Zorites)",
    6881: "צׇרְעָה (Tsorʻâh | tsor-aw')\n\nDerivation: apparently another form for H6880 צִרְעָה\nStrong's: Tsorah, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zareah, Zorah, Zoreah.\n\nCognate Group: H6880 (hornet), H6881 (Zareah), H6883 (leprosy), H6879 (leper), H6871 (Zeruah), H6882 (Zorites)",
    6882: "צׇרְעִי (Tsorʻîy | tsor-ee')\n\nDerivation: or צׇרְעָתִי; patrial from H6881 צׇרְעָה\nStrong's: a Tsorite or Tsorathite, i.e. inhabitants of Tsorah\nKJV: Zorites, Zareathites, Zorathites.\n\nCognate Group: H6880 (hornet), H6881 (Zareah), H6883 (leprosy), H6879 (leper), H6871 (Zeruah), H6882 (Zorites)",
    6883: "צָרַעַת (tsâraʻath | tsaw-rah'-ath)\n\nDerivation: from H6879 צָרַע\nStrong's: leprosy\nKJV: leprosy.\n\nCognate Group: H6880 (hornet), H6881 (Zareah), H6883 (leprosy), H6879 (leper), H6871 (Zeruah), H6882 (Zorites)\nGreek Equivalents: G3014 λέπρα",
    6884: "צָרַף (tsâraph | tsaw-raf')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to fuse (metal), i.e. refine (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: cast, (re-) fine(-er), founder, goldsmith, melt, pure, purge away, try.\n\nCognate Group: H6886 (Zarephath), H4715 (fining pot), H6885 (goldsmith's), H6884 (cast)\nGreek Equivalents: G695 ἀργυροκόπος, G1381 δοκιμάζω, G1571 ἐκκαθαίρω, G2511 καθαρίζω, G2680 κατασκευάζω, G4448 πυρόω, G5471 χαλκεύς",
    6885: "צֹרְפִי (Tsôrᵉphîy | tso-ref-ee')\n\nDerivation: from H6884 צָרַף ; refiner\nStrong's: Tsorephi (with the article), an Israelite\nKJV: goldsmith's.\n\nCognate Group: H6886 (Zarephath), H4715 (fining pot), H6885 (goldsmith's), H6884 (cast)",
    6886: "צָרְפַת (Tsârᵉphath | tsaq-ref-ath')\n\nDerivation: from H6884 צָרַף ; refinement\nStrong's: Tsarephath, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zarephath.\n\nCognate Group: H6886 (Zarephath), H4715 (fining pot), H6885 (goldsmith's), H6884 (cast)\nGreek Equivalents: G4558 Σάρεπτα",
    6887: "צָרַר (tsârar | tsaw-rar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to cramp, literally or figuratively, transitive or intransitive\nKJV: adversary, (be in) afflict(-ion), beseige, bind (up), (be in, bring) distress, enemy, narrower, oppress, pangs, shut up, be in a strait (trouble), vex.\n\nCognate Group: H6696 (adversary), H6887 (adversary), H4694 (fenced), H4692 (besieged)\nGreek Equivalents: G436 ἀνθίστημι, G480 ἀντίκειμαι, G639 ἀπορέω, G1195 δεσμεύω, G1228 διάβολος, G2190 ἐχθρός, G2346 θλίβω, G2662 καταπατέω, G3404 μισέω, G3960 πατάσσω, G4170 πολεμέω, G4729 στενοχωρέω, G4912 συνέχω, G4962 συστρέφω, G4963 συστροφή, G5605 ὠδίνω",
    6888: "צְרֵרָה (Tsᵉrêrâh | tser-ay-raw')\n\nDerivation: apparently by erroneous transcription for H6868 צְרֵדָה\nStrong's: Tsererah for Tseredah\nKJV: Zererath.\n\nCognate Group: H6872 (bag), H6864 (flint), H6862 (adversary), H6890 (Zareth-shahar), H6889 (Zereth), H6888 (Zererath), H6891 (Zarthan)",
    6889: "צֶרֶת (Tsereth | tseh'-reth)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from H6671 צָהַר ; splendor\nStrong's: Tsereth, an Israelite\nKJV: Zereth.\n\nCognate Group: H6872 (bag), H6864 (flint), H6862 (adversary), H6890 (Zareth-shahar), H6889 (Zereth), H6888 (Zererath), H6891 (Zarthan)",
    6890: "צֶרֶת הַשַּׁחַר (Tsereth hash-Shachar | tseh'-reth hash-shakh'-ar)\n\nDerivation: from the same as H6889 צֶרֶת and H7837 שַׁחַר with the article interposed; splendor of the dawn\nStrong's: Tsereth-hash-Shachar, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zareth-shahar.\n\nCognate Group: H6872 (bag), H6864 (flint), H6862 (adversary), H6890 (Zareth-shahar), H6889 (Zereth), H6888 (Zererath), H6891 (Zarthan)",
    6891: "צָרְתָן (Tsârᵉthân | tsaw-reth-awn')\n\nDerivation: perhaps for H6868 צְרֵדָה\nStrong's: Tsarethan, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Zarthan. q\n\nCognate Group: H6872 (bag), H6864 (flint), H6862 (adversary), H6890 (Zareth-shahar), H6889 (Zereth), H6888 (Zererath), H6891 (Zarthan)",
    6892: "קֵא (qêʼ | kay)\n\nDerivation: or קִיא; from H6958 קֹוא\nStrong's: vomit\nKJV: vomit.\n\nCognate Group: H6958 (spue), H6892 (vomit)\nGreek Equivalents: G1692 ἐμέω",
    6893: "קָאַת (qâʼath | kaw-ath')\n\nDerivation: from H6958 קֹוא\nStrong's: probably the pelican (from vomiting)\nKJV: cormorant.\n\nCognate Group: H6893 (cormorant)\nGreek Equivalents: G3732 ὄρνεον",
    6894: "קַב (qab | kab)\n\nDerivation: from H6895 קָבַב\nStrong's: a hollow, i.e. vessel used as a (dry) measure\nKJV: cab.\n\nCognate Group: H6894 (cab), H6898 (tent)",
    6895: "קָבַב (qâbab | kaw-bab')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to scoop out, i.e. (figuratively) to malign or execrate (i.e. stab with words)\nKJV: [idiom] at all, curse.\n\nCognate Group: H6895 ([idiom] at all)\nGreek Equivalents: G2671 κατάρα, G2672 καταράομαι",
    6896: "קֵבָה (qêbâh | kay-baw')\n\nDerivation: from H6895 קָבַב\nStrong's: the paunch (as a cavity) or first stomach of ruminants\nKJV: maw.\n\nCognate Group: H6897 (belly), H6896 (maw)",
    6897: "קֹבָה (qôbâh | ko'-baw)\n\nDerivation: from H6895 קָבַב\nStrong's: the abdomen (as a cavity)\nKJV: belly.\n\nCognate Group: H6897 (belly), H6896 (maw)\nGreek Equivalents: G3388 μήτρα",
    6898: "קֻבָּה (qubbâh | koob-baw')\n\nDerivation: from H6895 קָבַב\nStrong's: a pavilion (as a domed cavity)\nKJV: tent.\n\nCognate Group: H6894 (cab), H6898 (tent)\nGreek Equivalents: G2575 κάμινος",
    6899: "קִבּוּץ (qibbûwts | kib-boots')\n\nDerivation: from H6908 קָבַץ\nStrong's: a throng\nKJV: company.\n\nCognate Group: H6911 (Kibzaim), H3343 (Jekabzeel), H6910 ([idiom] gather), H6908 (assemble), H6899 (company), H6909 (Kabzeel)",
    6900: "קְבוּרָה (qᵉbûwrâh | keb-oo-raw')\n\nDerivation: or קְבֻרָה; feminine passive participle of H6912 קָבַר\nStrong's: sepulture; (concretely) a sepulchre\nKJV: burial, burying place, grave, sepulchre.\n\nCognate Group: H6900 (burial), H6913 (burying place), H6914 (Kibroth-hattaavah), H6912 ([idiom] in any wise)\nGreek Equivalents: G3418 μνῆμα, G3419 μνημεῖον",
    6901: "קָבַל (qâbal | kaw-bal')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to admit, i.e. take (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: choose, (take) hold, receive, (under-) take.\n\nCognate Group: H6905 (before), H6904 (war), H6901 (choose)\nGreek Equivalents: G496 ἀντιπίπτω, G1209 δέχομαι, G1586 ἐκλέγομαι, G3982 πείθω, G4327 προσδέχομαι, G5297 ὑποφέρω",
    6902: "קְבַל (qᵉbal | keb-al')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6901 קָבַל\nStrong's: to acquire\nKJV: receive, take.\n\nCognate Group: H6933 (first), H6903 (according to), H6902 (receive), H6925 (before), H6928 (afore)\nGreek Equivalents: G218 ἀλείφω, G2983 λαμβάνω, G3880 παραλαμβάνω",
    6903: "קְבֵל (qᵉbêl | keb-ale')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) or קֳבֵל; (Aramaic), (corresponding to H6905 קָבָל\nStrong's: (adverbially) in front of; usually (with other particles) on account of, so as, since, hence\nKJV: [phrase] according to, [phrase] as, [phrase] because, before, [phrase] for this cause, [phrase] forasmuch as, [phrase] by this means, over against, by reason of, [phrase] that, [phrase] therefore, [phrase] though, [phrase] wherefore.\n\nCognate Group: H6933 (first), H6903 (according to), H6902 (receive), H6925 (before), H6928 (afore)",
    6904: "קֹבֶל (qôbel | ko'-bel)\n\nDerivation: from H6901 קָבַל in the sense of confronting (as standing opposite in order to receive)\nStrong's: a battering-ram\nKJV: war.\n\nCognate Group: H6905 (before), H6904 (war), H6901 (choose)",
    6905: "קָבָל (qâbâl | kaw-bawl')\n\nDerivation: from H6901 קָבַל in the sense of opposite (see H6904 קֹבֶל)\nStrong's: the presence, i.e. (adverbially) in front of\nKJV: before.\n\nCognate Group: H6905 (before), H6904 (war), H6901 (choose)",
    6906: "קָבַע (qâbaʻ | kaw-bah')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to cover, i.e. (figuratively) defraud\nKJV: rob, spoil.\n\nCognate Group: H6906 (rob), H6907 (dregs)",
    6907: "קֻבַּעַת (qubbaʻath | koob-bah'-ath)\n\nDerivation: from H6906 קָבַע\nStrong's: a goblet (as deep like a cover)\nKJV: dregs.\n\nCognate Group: H6906 (rob), H6907 (dregs)\nGreek Equivalents: G4221 ποτήριον",
    6908: "קָבַץ (qâbats | kaw-bats')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to grasp, i.e. collect\nKJV: assemble (selves), gather (bring) (together, selves together, up), heap, resort, [idiom] surely, take up.\n\nCognate Group: H6911 (Kibzaim), H3343 (Jekabzeel), H6910 ([idiom] gather), H6908 (assemble), H6899 (company), H6909 (Kabzeel)\nGreek Equivalents: G654 ἀποστρέφω, G1523 εἰσδέχομαι, G1551 ἐκδέχομαι, G1586 ἐκλέγομαι, G1653 ἐλεέω, G1951 ἐπιλέγομαι, G2064 ἔρχομαι, G4863 συνάγω, G4864 συναγωγή, G4867 συναθροίζω, G4867 συναθροίζω, G4876 συναντάω, G4905 συνέρχομαι, G4962 συστρέφω",
    6909: "קַבְצְאֵל (Qabtsᵉʼêl | kab-tseh-ale')\n\nDerivation: from H6908 קָבַץ and H410 אֵל ; God has gathered\nStrong's: Kabtseel, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Kabzeel. Compare H3343 יְקַבְצְאֵל .\n\nCognate Group: H6911 (Kibzaim), H3343 (Jekabzeel), H6910 ([idiom] gather), H6908 (assemble), H6899 (company), H6909 (Kabzeel)",
    6910: "קְבֻצָה (qᵉbutsâh | keb-oo-tsaw')\n\nDerivation: feminine passive participle of H6908 קָבַץ\nStrong's: a hoard\nKJV: [idiom] gather.\n\nCognate Group: H6911 (Kibzaim), H3343 (Jekabzeel), H6910 ([idiom] gather), H6908 (assemble), H6899 (company), H6909 (Kabzeel)",
    6911: "קִבְצַיִם (Qibtsayim | kib-tsah'-yim)\n\nDerivation: dual from H6908 קָבַץ ; a double heap\nStrong's: Kibtsajim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: Kibzaim.\n\nCognate Group: H6911 (Kibzaim), H3343 (Jekabzeel), H6910 ([idiom] gather), H6908 (assemble), H6899 (company), H6909 (Kabzeel)",
    6912: "קָבַר (qâbar | kaw-bar')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to inter\nKJV: [idiom] in any wise, bury(-ier).\n\nCognate Group: H6900 (burial), H6913 (burying place), H6914 (Kibroth-hattaavah), H6912 ([idiom] in any wise)\nGreek Equivalents: G2290 θάπτω, G2875 κόπτω, G5027 ταφή",
    6913: "קֶבֶר (qeber | keh'-ber)\n\nDerivation: or (feminine) קִבְרָה; from H6912 קָבַר\nStrong's: a sepulchre\nKJV: burying place, grave, sepulchre.\n\nCognate Group: H6900 (burial), H6913 (burying place), H6914 (Kibroth-hattaavah), H6912 ([idiom] in any wise)\nGreek Equivalents: G2288 θάνατος, G3418 μνῆμα, G3419 μνημεῖον, G5027 ταφή, G5028 τάφος",
    6914: "קִבְרֹות הַתַּאֲוָה (Qibrôwth hat-Taʼă-vâh | kib-roth' hat-tahav-aw')\n\nDerivation: from the feminine plural of H6913 קֶבֶר and H8378 תַּאֲוָה with the article interposed; graves of the longing\nStrong's: Kibroth-hat-Taavh, a place in the Desert\nKJV: Kibroth-hattaavah.\n\nCognate Group: H6900 (burial), H6913 (burying place), H6914 (Kibroth-hattaavah), H6912 ([idiom] in any wise)",
    6915: "קָדַד (qâdad | kaw-dad')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to shrivel up, i.e. contract or bend the body (or neck) in deference\nKJV: bow (down) (the) head, stoop.\n\nCognate Group: H6915 (bow head)\nGreek Equivalents: G1119 γόνυ, G2106 εὐδοκέω, G2578 κάμπτω, G2955 κύπτω, G4098 πίπτω",
    6916: "קִדָּה (qiddâh | kid-daw')\n\nDerivation: from H6915 קָדַד\nStrong's: cassia bark (as in shrivelled rolls)\nKJV: cassia.\n\nCognate Group: H6936 (crown), H6916 (cassia)\nGreek Equivalents: G2463 ἶρις",
    6917: "קָדוּם (qâdûwm | kaw-doom')\n\nDerivation: passive participle of H6923 קָדַם\nStrong's: a pristine hero\nKJV: ancient.\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)",
    6918: "קָדֹושׁ (qâdôwsh | kaw-doshe')\n\nDerivation: or קָדֹשׁ; from H6942 קָדַשׁ\nStrong's: sacred (ceremonially or morally); (as noun) God (by eminence), an angel, a saint, a sanctuary\nKJV: holy (One), saint.\n\nCognate Group: H6947 (Kadeshbarnea), H4720 (chapel), H6945 (sodomite), H6918 (holy), H6944 (consecrated), H6946 (Kadesh), H6948 (harlot), H6942 (appoint), H6943 (Kedesh)\nGreek Equivalents: G37 ἁγιάζω, G39 ἅγιον, G2513 καθαρός",
    6919: "קָדַח (qâdach | kaw-dakh')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root to inflame\nStrong's: to inflame\nKJV: burn, kindle.\n\nCognate Group: H6919 (burn), H688 (carbuncle), H6920 (burning ague)\nGreek Equivalents: G1572 ἐκκαίω, G2545 καίω",
    6920: "קַדַּחַת (qaddachath | kad-dakh'-ath)\n\nDerivation: from H6919 קָדַח\nStrong's: inflammation, i.e. febrile disease\nKJV: burning ague, fever.\n\nCognate Group: H6919 (burn), H688 (carbuncle), H6920 (burning ague)\nGreek Equivalents: G4446 πυρετός",
    6921: "קָדִים (qâdîym | kaw-deem')\n\nDerivation: or קָדִם; from H6923 קָדַם\nStrong's: the fore or front part; hence (by orientation) the East (often adverbially, eastward, for brevity the east wind)\nKJV: east(-ward, wind).\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)\nGreek Equivalents: G395 ἀνατολή, G972 βίαιος, G1726 ἐναντίον, G1727 ἐναντίος, G2742 καύσων, G3558 νότος, G4151 πνεῦμα",
    6922: "קַדִּישׁ (qaddîysh | kad-deesh')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6918 קָדֹושׁ .\nStrong's: sacred (ceremonially or morally); (as noun) God (by eminence), an angel, a saint, a sanctuary\nKJV: holy (One), saint.\n\nCognate Group: H6922 (holy)\nGreek Equivalents: G39 ἅγιον",
    6923: "קָדַם (qâdam | kaw-dam')\n\nDerivation: a primitive root\nStrong's: to project (one self), i.e. precede; hence, to anticipate, hasten, meet (usually for help)\nKJV: come (go, (flee)) before, [phrase] disappoint, meet, prevent.\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)\nGreek Equivalents: G436 ἀνθίστημι, G906 βάλλω, G1096 γίνομαι, G1911 ἐπιβάλλω, G2638 καταλαμβάνω, G4198 πορεύομαι, G4313 προπορεύομαι, G4399 προφθάνω, G4876 συναντάω",
    6924: "קֶדֶם (qedem | keh'-dem)\n\nDerivation: or קֵדְמָה; from H6923 קָדַם\nStrong's: the front, of place (absolutely, the fore part, relatively the East) or time (antiquity); often used adverbially (before, anciently, eastward)\nKJV: aforetime, ancient (time), before, east (end, part, side, -ward), eternal, [idiom] ever(-lasting), forward, old, past. Compare H6926 קִדְמָה .\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)\nGreek Equivalents: G165 αἰών, G395 ἀνατολή, G744 ἀρχαῖος, G746 ἀρχή, G1715 ἔμπροσθεν, G2246 ἥλιος",
    6925: "קֳדָם (qŏdâm | kod-awm')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) or קְדָם; (Aramaic) (Daniel 7:13), corresponding to H6924 קֶדֶם\nStrong's: before\nKJV: before, [idiom] from, [idiom] I (thought), [idiom] me, [phrase] of, [idiom] it pleased, presence.\n\nCognate Group: H6933 (first), H6903 (according to), H6902 (receive), H6925 (before), H6928 (afore)\nGreek Equivalents: G3936 παρίστημι",
    6926: "קִדְמָה (qidmâh | kid-maw')\n\nDerivation: feminine of H6924 קֶדֶם\nStrong's: the forward part (or relatively) East (often adverbially, on the east or in front)\nKJV: east(-ward).\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)",
    6927: "קַדְמָה (qadmâh | kad-maw')\n\nDerivation: from H6923 קָדַם\nStrong's: priority (in time); also used adverbially (before)\nKJV: afore, antiquity, former (old) estate.\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)",
    6928: "קַדְמָה (qadmâh | kad-maw')\n\nDerivation: (Aramaic) corresponding to H6927 קַדְמָה\nStrong's: former time\nKJV: afore(-time), ago.\n\nCognate Group: H6933 (first), H6903 (according to), H6902 (receive), H6925 (before), H6928 (afore)",
    6929: "קֵדְמָה (Qêdᵉmâh | kayd'-maw)\n\nTypo: lemma קדְמָה missing vowel, corrected to קֵדְמָה\n\nDerivation: from H6923 קָדַם ; precedence\nStrong's: Kedemah, a son of Ishmael\nKJV: Kedemah.\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)",
    6930: "קַדְמֹון (qadmôwn | kad-mone')\n\nDerivation: from H6923 קָדַם\nStrong's: eastern\nKJV: east.\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)",
    6931: "קַדְמֹונִי (qadmôwnîy | kad-mo-nee')\n\nDerivation: or קַדְמֹנִי; from H6930 קַדְמֹון\nStrong's: (of time) anterior or (of place) oriental\nKJV: ancient, they that went before, east, (thing of) old.\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)",
    6932: "קְדֵמֹות (Qᵉdêmôwth | ked-ay-mothe')\n\nDerivation: from H6923 קָדַם ; beginnings\nStrong's: Kedemoth, a place in eastern Palestine\nKJV: Kedemoth.\n\nCognate Group: H6924 (aforetime), H6926 (east), H6917 (ancient), H6935 (Kadmonites), H6921 (east), H6923 (come before), H6931 (ancient), H6927 (afore), H6929 (Kedemah), H6934 (Kadmiel), H6932 (Kedemoth), H6930 (east)",
