    8810: "Imperative \n\nThis verb class indicates an order or a command. Go up to the city. Wash yourself.",
    8811: "Imperfect \n\nThe imperfect expresses an action, process or condition which is incomplete, and it has a wide range of meaning: \n\n1a) It is used to describe a single (as opposed to a repeated) action in the past; it differs from the perfect in being more vivid and pictorial. The perfect expresses the \"fact\", the imperfect adds colour and movement by suggesting the \"process\" preliminary to its completion. He put forth his hand to the door it came to a halt I began to hear \n\n1b) A phrase such as \"What seekest thou?\", refers not only to the present, but assumes that the search has continued for some time. \n\nWhy do you weep? Why refuse to eat? Why are you distressed? \n\nThese relate not so much as to one occasion, as to a continued condition. \n\n2) The kind of progression or imperfection and unfinished condition of the action may consist in its frequent repetition. \n\n2a) In the present: \n\nit is \"said\" today a wise son \"maketh glad\" his father \n\n2b) In the past: \n\n\"and so he did\" - regularly, year by year a mist \"used to go up\" the fish which \"we used to eat\" the manna \"came down\" -regularly He \"spoke\" -repeatedly \n\n3) The imperfect is used to express the \"future\", referring not only to an action which is about to be accomplished but one which has not yet begun: \n\n3a) This may be a future from the point of view of the real present; as: \n\nNow \"shalt thou See what I will do\" \"We will burn\" thy house \n\n3b) It may be a future from any other point of view assumed; as: He took his son that \"was to reign\" she stayed to See what \"should be done\" \n\n4) The usage of 3b may be taken as the transitive to a common use of the imperfect in which it serves for an expression of those shades of relation among acts and thoughts for which English prefers the conditional moods. Such actions are strictly \"future\" in reference to the assumed point of relation, and the simple imperfect sufficiently expresses them; e.g. \n\nof every tree thou \"mayest eat\" \"could we know\" He \"would\" say \n\n5a) The imperfect follows particles expressing \"transition\", \"purpose\", \"result\" and so forth as, \"in order that\", \"lest\"; e.g. \n\nsay thou art my sister, \"that it may be well with thee\" let us deal wisely with the nation, \"lest it multiplies\" \n\n5b) When however there is a strong feeling of \"purpose\", or when it is meant to be strongly marked, then of course the moods are employed; e.g. \n\nraise me up \"that I may requite them\" who will entice Ahab \"that he may go up\" what shall we do \"that the sea may be calm\" \n\nThe moods are also employed to express that class of future actions which we express in the \"optative\" \n\n\"may I die\" \"may\" the LORD \"establish\" his word \"may\" the child \"live\".",
    8812: "Infinitive \n\nThere are two forms of the infinitive: \n\n1a) Infinitive Construct is used as a verbal noun corresponding to the English verbal noun ending in \"- ing\" \n\n1a1) as subject to keep the judgments to seek thy heart \n\n1a2) as object in his \"writing\" He spoke, \"saying\" \n\n1b) The Infinitive Absolute does not allow prefixes or suffixes \n\n1b1) Used with a verb to emphasize the verbal idea. This is often rendered by an English adverb, such as, \"surely\", \"utterly\". He will surely visit you He utterly destroyed the people \n\n1b2) It may be used by itself with the value of a finite form of the verb, especially an imperative. \n\nremember the sabbath day.",
    8813: "Participle \n\n1) The participle represents an action or condition in its unbroken continuity, and corresponds to the English verb, \"to be\" with the present participle. It may be used of present, past or future time. \n\n1a) present time what are you doing 1b) past time \"he was still speaking\" when another came 1c) future time we are destroying-e.g. are about to destroy.",
    8814: "Participle Active \n\nActive form of the participle. See H8813 for description of participle.",
    8815: "Participle Passive \n\nPassive form of the participle. See H8813 for description of participle.",
    8816: "Perfect \n\nThe Perfect expresses a completed action. \n\n1) In reference to time, such an action may be: \n\n1a) one just completed from the standpoint of the present \"I have come\" to tell you the news \n\n1b) one completed in the more or less distant past in the beginning God \"created\" \"I was (once) young\" and \"I have (now) grown old\" but \"I have not seen\" a righteous man forsaken \n\n1c) one already completed from the point of view of another past act God saw everything that \"he had made\" \n\n1d) one completed from the point of view of another action yet future I will draw for thy camels also until \"they have done\" drinking \n\n2) The perfect is often used where the present is employed in English. \n\n2a) in the case of general truths or actions of frequent occurrence--truths or actions which have been often experienced or observed the grass \"withereth\" the sparrow \"findeth\" a house \n\n2b) an action or attitude of the past may be continued into the present \"I stretch out\" my hands to thee \"thou never forsakest\" those who seek thee \n\n2c) the perfect of intransitive verbs is used where English uses the present; The perfect in Hebrew in such a case emphasises a condition which has come into \"complete existence\" and realisation \"I know\" thou wilt be king \"I hate\" all workers of iniquity \n\n2d) Sometimes in Hebrew, future events are conceived so vividly and so realistically that they are regarded as Having virtually taken place and are described by the perfect. \n\n2d1) in promises, threats and language of contracts the field \"give I\" thee and if not, \"I will take it\" \n\n2d2) prophetic language my people \"is gone into captivity\" (i.e. shall assuredly go).",
    8817: "Aphel \n\nIn Biblical Aramaic (Chaldean), the Aphel is a causative conjugation like the Hebrew Hiphil, but with the letter Aleph substituted for the initial He. Other changes in spelling also occur, but otherwise the verb functions similarly to the Hebrew Hiphil, expressing causative action. \n\n See Hiphil H8818",
    8818: "Hiphil \n\na) Hiphil usually expresses the \"causative\" action of Qal-See H8851 \n\nQal Hiphil He ate he caused to eat, he fed He came he caused to come, he brought He reigned he made king, he crowned \n\nb) Hiphil is often used to form verbs from nouns and adjectives. \n\nNoun or Adjective Hiphil \n\near to listen (lend an ear) far to remove oneself, put far away \n\nc) Some \"simple\" verbs are found in Hiphil. \n\nto cast, to destroy, to get up early, to explain, to tell \n\nThe form accounts for 13.3% of the verbs parsed.",
    8819: "Hithpael \n\na) This form primarily expresses a \"reflexive\" action of Qal or Piel See for Qal H8851\nSee for Piel H8840 \n\nQal Hithpael He wore he dressed himself He washed he washed himself He fell he flung himself, he fell upon, he attacked He sold he sold himself, he devoted himself \n\nb) It expresses a reciprocal action. \n\nthey saw they looked upon one another they whispered they whispered one to another \n\nc) Some verbs in Hithpael are translated as a simple action. The reflexive action is understood. He prayed, he mourned, he became angry \n\nThis form accounts for 1.4% of the verbs parsed.",
    8820: "Hithpalel \n\nThis Hebrew form is equivalent in use to the Hithpael, and is causative/reflexive in meaning. The separate term occurs because certain verb forms reduplicate their final consonant and change into a quadriliteral (4-letter) root rather than the normal triliteral (3-letter) root form. \n\n See Hithpael H8819",
    8821: "Hithpalpel \n\nThis Hebrew form is equivalent in use to the Hithpael, and is causative/reflexive in meaning. The separate term occurs because certain verb forms reduplicate their final syllable (final two root letters) and change the normal triliteral (3-letter) root form into a reduplicated two-letter root. \n\n See Hithpael H8819",
    8822: "Hithpeil \n\nIn Biblical Aramaic (Chaldean), this form is a passive causative/ reflexive conjugation like the Hebrew Hophal. The verb functions similarly to the Hebrew Hophal, expressing passive causative action, but with the effect of the action upon oneself, being reflexive. \n\n See Hophal H8825\nSee Hithpael H8819",
    8823: "Hithpoel \n\nA Hebrew intensive reflexive stem, caused by the lengthening of the final vowel in the second syllable of the triliteral (3-letter) root. This form functions similarly to the Hithpael. \n\n See Hithpael H8819",
    8824: "Hithpolel \n\nThis Hebrew form is equivalent in use to the Hithpael, and is causative/reflexive in meaning. The separate term occurs because certain verb forms reduplicate their final consonant and change into a quadriliteral (4-letter) root rather than the normal triliteral (3-letter) root form. This form additionally lengthens the initial vowel in the first consonant. \n\n See Hithpael H8819",
    8825: "Hophal Hophal is the \"passive\" of Hiphil-See H8818 Hiphil Hophal He told it was told He threw he was thrown \n\nThis form is accounts for 0.6% of the verbs parsed.",
    8826: "Hothpael \n\nThis is a passive form of the Hithpael, indicating a passive intensive reflexive receiving of action upon the subject. It thus combines the features of both the Hithpael and the Hophal. \n\n See Hithpael H8819\nSee Hophal H8825",
    8827: "Ishtaphel \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this form is similar to the Hebrew Hithpael, with its form altered due to an initial Aleph and a metathesized (reversed) Tau and Shin. This form occurs only rarely with verbs meeting particular spelling qualities. \n\n See Hithpael H8819",
    8828: "Ithpael \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this form is similar to the Hebrew Hithpael, with its form altered due to an initial Aleph. This form reflects the intensive causative, and occurs only rarely with verbs meeting particular spelling qualities. \n\n See Hithpael H8819",
    8829: "Ithpeal \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this form is similar to the Hebrew Hithpael, with its form altered due to an initial Aleph. However, this form reflects only the intensive reflexive of the common stem (Peal = Hebrew Qal). This form occurs only rarely with verbs meeting particular spelling qualities. \n\n See Hithpael H8819\nSee Qal H8851",
    8830: "Ithpeel \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this form is similar to the Hebrew Hithpael, with its form altered due to an initial Aleph. However, this form reflects only the reflexive of the intensive stem, and functions like a Hebrew Piel and Hithpael combined. \n\n See Hithpael H8819\nSee Piel H8840",
    8831: "Ithpeil \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this form is similar to the Hebrew Hithpael, with its form altered due to an initial Aleph. However, this form reflects only the passive intensive reflexive, and functions like the Hithpael and Hophal combined. \n\n See Hithpael H8819\nSee Hophal H8825",
    8832: "Ithpolel \n\nThis Aramaic (Chaldean) form is equivalent in use to the Hebrew Hithpolel, and is causative/reflexive in meaning like the Hithpael. The separate term occurs because certain verb forms reduplicate their final consonant and change into a quadriliteral (4-letter) root rather than the normal triliteral (3-letter) root form. This form additionally lengthens the initial vowel in the first consonant. \n\n See Hithpael H8819\nSee Hithpolel H8824",
    8833: "Niphal \n\na) Niphal is the \"passive\" of Qal-See H8851 \n\nQal Niphal He saw he was seen, he appeared He saw the angel the angel was seen He sent he was sent He created it was created \n\nb) Niphal sometimes expresses a \"reflexive\" action. He guarded he was guarded, also He guarded himself \n\nc) Several verbs use Niphal, although they express simple action and are active in English. Common examples are: He fought, he remained, he swore, he entered \n\nThis form accounts for 6.0% of the verbs parsed.",
    8834: "Nithpael \n\nThis form is a passive intensive reflexive, combining both the qualities of the Niphal and Hithpael. Example: Eze 23:48, \"that they may be taught\" (= caused to learn). \n\n See Niphal H8833\nSee Hithpael H8819",
    8835: "Pael \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), the intensive form of the verb, equivalent of the Hebrew Piel. \n\n See Piel H8840",
    8836: "Palpal (NOT USED) \n\nA form of a triliteral verb such as \"palal\" which reduplicates the first syllable for euphony. This form otherwise functions like the normal Qal stem. \n\n See Qal H8851",
    8837: "Peal \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this form represents the base stem of the verb, and is equivalent to the Hebrew Qal stem. \n\n See Qal H8851",
    8838: "Peel Or Peil \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this stem reflects the Passive Intensive form, and is equivalent to the Hebrew Pual. \n\n See Pual H8849",
    8839: "Peil \n\nIn Aramaic (Chaldean), this stem reflects the Intensive form, and is equivalent to the Hebrew Piel. \n\n See Piel H8840",
    8840: "Piel \n\na) Piel usually expresses an \"intensive\" or \"intentional\" action. \n\nQal Piel He broke he broke to pieces, he smashed He sent he sent away, he expelled \n\nb) Sometimes the Piel introduces a new meaning to the Qal form. He counted he recounted, he told He completed he paid, he compensated He learned he taught \n\nc) Piel expresses a \"repeated\" or \"extended\" action. He jumped he skipped, he hopped \n\nd) Some intransitive verbs in Qal become transitive in Piel. \n\nto be strong to strengthen, to fortify to become great to make great.",
    8841: "Pilel \n\nThis form is equivalent to the Piel intensive form, and occurs due to reduplication of the final root letter. \n\n See Piel H8840",
    8842: "Pilpel \n\nThis form is equivalent to the Piel intensive form, and occurs due to reduplication of the final root syllable. \n\n See Piel H8840",
    8843: "Poal \n\nThis form is the passive of the Poel, and functions much like the normal Pual. \n\n See Pual H8849\nSee Poel H8845",
    8844: "Poalal \n\nThis form is identical to the Poal, but reduplicates the final root syllable. It functions much like the normal Pual. \n\n See Poal H8843\nSee Pual H8849",
    8845: "Poel \n\nThis form functions much like the normal Piel, and arises only due to certain internal vowel changes. \n\n See Piel H8840",
    8846: "Polal \n\nThis form is identical to the Poal, but reduplicates the final root letter. It functions much like the normal Pual. \n\n See Poal H8843\nSee Pual H8849",
    8847: "Polel \n\nThis form reduplicates the final root letter, but functions like the normal Piel. \n\n See Piel H8840",
    8848: "Polpal \n\nThis form reduplicates the initial root syllable, and functions like the normal Pual. \n\n See Pual H8849",
    8849: "Pual \n\nPual is the \"passive\" of Piel-See H8840 \n\nPiel Pual He smashed it was smashed He told it was told \n\nThis form accounts for 0.7% of the verbs parsed.",
    8850: "Pulal \n\nThis form reduplicates the final root syllable and is otherwise equivalent to the normal Pual. \n\n See Pual H8849",
    8851: "Qal \n\nQal is the most frequently used verb pattern. It expresses the \"simple\" or \"causal\" action of the root in the active voice. \n\nExamples: He sat, he ate, he went, he said, he rose, he bought \n\nThis form accounts for 66.7% of the verbs parsed.",
    8852: "Shaphel \n\nA rare Aramaic (Chaldean) form, this reflects a causative like the Hebrew Hiphil, but with a Shin prefixed rather than the usual He. It otherwise functions like the Hebrew Hiphil. \n\n See Hiphil H8818",
    8853: "Tiphel \n\nA rare Hebrew form, this reflects a causative like the Hebrew Hiphil, but with a Tau prefixed rather than the usual He. It otherwise functions like the Hebrew Hiphil. \n\n See Hiphil H8818",
  },
  "G": {
    1: "Α (A | al'-fah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin\nStrong's: the first letter of the alphabet; figuratively, only (from its use as a numeral) the first\nKJV: —Alpha. Often used (usually ἄν, before a vowel) also in composition (as a contraction from G427) in the sense of privation; so, in many words, beginning with this letter; occasionally in the sense of union (as a contraction of G260).\n\nSee: G427 \nSee: G260 \n\nCognate Group: H8652 (porter), H8674 (Tatnai), H8651 (gate mouth), H8627 (establish)\nVariants: Α, α, ἄλφα, Ἄλφα",
    2: "Ἀαρών (Aarṓn | ah-ar-ohn')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H175)\nStrong's: Aaron, the brother of Moses\nKJV: —Aaron.\nSee: H175\n\nCognate Group: G2 (Aaron)\nVariants: Ἀαρών \nHebrew Equivalents: H175 אַהֲרֹון",
    3: "Ἀβαδδών (Abaddṓn | ab-ad-dohn')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H11)\nStrong's: a destroying angel\nKJV: —Abaddon.\nSee: H11\n\nCognate Group: G3 (Abaddon)\nVariants: Ἀβαδδών \nHebrew Equivalents: H11 אֲבַדֹּון",
    4: "ἀβαρής (abarḗs | ab-ar-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G922\nStrong's: weightless, i.e. (figuratively) not burdensome\nKJV: —from being burdensome.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G922 \n\nCognate Group: G3 (Abaddon)\nVariants: ἀβαρής",
    5: "Ἀββᾶ (Abbâ | ab-bah')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (H2)\nStrong's: father as a vocative\nKJV: —Abba.\nSee: H2\n\nCognate Group: G5 (Abba)\nVariants: Ἀββᾶ, ἀββά, αββα, ἀββα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2 אַב",
    6: "Ἄβελ (Ábel | ab'-el)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H1893)\nStrong's: Abel, the son of Adam\nKJV: —Abel.\nSee: H1893\n\nCognate Group: G6 (Abel)\nVariants: Ἄβελ, Ἅβελ, Ἇβελ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1892 הֶבֶל, H1893 הֶבֶל",
    7: "Ἀβιά (Abiá | ab-ee-ah')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H29)\nStrong's: Abijah, the name of two Israelites\nKJV: —Abia.\nSee: H29\n\nCognate Group: G6 (Abel)\nVariants: Ἀβιά \nHebrew Equivalents: H29 אֲבִיָּה",
    8: "Ἀβιαθάρ (Abiathár | ab-ee-ath'-ar)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H54)\nStrong's: Abiathar, an Israelite\nKJV: —Abiathar.\nSee: H54\n\nCognate Group: G6 (Abel)\nVariants: Ἀβιαθάρ, Ἀβιάθαρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H54 אֶבְיָתָר",
    9: "Ἀβιληνή (Abilēnḗ | ab-ee-lay-nay')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (compare H58)\nStrong's: Abilene, a region of Syria\nKJV: —Abilene.\nSee: H58\n\nCognate Group: G6 (Abel)\nVariants: Ἀβιληνή \nHebrew Equivalents: H58 אָבֵל",
    10: "Ἀβιούδ (Abioúd | ab-ee-ood')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H31)\nStrong's: Abihud, an Israelite\nKJV: —Abiud.\nSee: H31\n\nCognate Group: G6 (Abel)\nVariants: Ἀβιούδ \nHebrew Equivalents: H31 אֲבִיהוּד",
    11: "Ἀβραάμ (Abraám | ab-rah-am')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H85)\nStrong's: Abraham, the Hebrew patriarch\nKJV: —Abraham.\n\nSee: H85\n\nCognate Group: G11 (Abraham)\nVariants: Ἀβραάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H85 אַבְרָהָם",
    12: "ἄβυσσος (ábyssos | ab'-us-sos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a variation of G1037\nStrong's: depthless, i.e. (specially) (infernal) \"abyss\"\nKJV: —deep, (bottomless) pit.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1037 \n\nCognate Group: G12 (deep)\nVariants: ἄβυσσος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4688 מְצֹולָה, H6683 צוּלָה, H7338 רַחַב, H8415 תְּהֹום",
    13: "Ἄγαβος (Ágabos | ag'-ab-os)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (compare H2285)\nStrong's: Agabus, an Israelite\nKJV: —Agabus.\nSee: H2285\n\nCognate Group: G12 (deep)\nVariants: Ἄγαβος, Ἅγαβος, Ἀγαβος, Ἇγαβος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2285 חָגָב",
    14: "ἀγαθοεργέω (agathoergéō | ag-ath-er-gheh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G18 and G2041\nStrong's: to work good\nKJV: —do good.\n\nSee: G18 \nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G15 (do good), G18 (benefit), G16 (well-doing), G14 (do good)\nVariants: ἀγαθοεργέω, ἀγαθουργέω",
    15: "ἀγαθοποιέω (agathopoiéō | ag-ath-op-oy-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G17\nStrong's: to be a well-doer (as a favor or a duty)\nKJV: —(when) do good (well).\n\nSee: G17 \n\nCognate Group: G15 (do good), G18 (benefit), G16 (well-doing), G14 (do good)\nVariants: ἀγαθοποιέω",
    16: "ἀγαθοποιΐα (agathopoiḯa | ag-ath-op-oy-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G17\nStrong's: well-doing, i.e. virtue\nKJV: —well-doing.\n\nSee: G17 \n\nCognate Group: G15 (do good), G18 (benefit), G16 (well-doing), G14 (do good)\nVariants: ἀγαθοποιΐα, ἀγαθοποιί̈α, ἀγαθοποιΐα",
    17: "ἀγαθοποιός (agathopoiós | ag-ath-op-oy-os')\n\nDerivation: from G18 and G4160\nStrong's: a well-doer, i.e. virtuous\nKJV: —them that do well.\n\nSee: G18 \nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G19 (goodness), G865 (despiser of those that are good), G17 (them that do well), G5358 (love of good men)\nVariants: ἀγαθοποιός",
    18: "ἀγαθός (agathós | ag-ath-os')\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: \"good\" (in any sense, often as noun)\nKJV: —benefit, good(-s, things), well. Compare G2570 .\n\nSee: G2570 \n\nCognate Group: G15 (do good), G18 (benefit), G16 (well-doing), G14 (do good)\nVariants: ἀγαθός, ἀγαθοεργός \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H1116 בָּמָה, H1880 דֶּשֶׁן, H2869 טָב, H2896 טֹוב, H3303 יָפֶה, H3477 יָשָׁר, H3559 כּוּן, H5228 נָכֹחַ, H6143 עִקְּשׁוּת, H7060 קָמַל, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8389 תֹּאַר, H8615 תִּקְוָה",
    19: "ἀγαθωσύνη (agathōsýnē | ag-ath-o-soo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from G18\nStrong's: goodness, i.e. virtue or beneficence\nKJV: —goodness.\n\nSee: G18 \n\nCognate Group: G19 (goodness), G865 (despiser of those that are good), G17 (them that do well), G5358 (love of good men)\nVariants: ἀγαθωσύνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2896 טֹוב, H2896 טֹוב, H2898 טוּב",
    20: "ἀγαλλίασις (agallíasis | ag-al-lee'-as-is)\n\nDerivation: from G21\nStrong's: exultation; specially, welcome\nKJV: —gladness, (exceeding) joy.\n\nSee: G21 \n\nCognate Group: G21 (be glad), G20 (gladness)\nVariants: ἀγαλλίασις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1524 גִּיל, H7440 רִנָּה, H8342 שָׂשֹׂון, H8643 תְּרוּעָה",
    21: "ἀγαλλιάω (agalliáō | ag-al-lee-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from agan (much) and G242\nStrong's: properly, to jump for joy, i.e. exult\nKJV: —be (exceeding) glad, with exceeding joy, rejoice (greatly).\n\nSee: G242 \n\nCognate Group: G21 (be glad), G20 (gladness)\nVariants: ἀγαλλιάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1523 גִּיל, H1984 הָלַל, H2142 זָכַר, H5937 עָלַז, H6670 צָהַל, H7442 רָנַן, H7797 שׂוּשׂ, H8055 שָׂמַח",
    22: "ἄγαμος (ágamos | ag'-am-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G1062\nStrong's: unmarried\nKJV: —unmarried.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1062 \n\nCognate Group: G21 (be glad), G20 (gladness)\nVariants: ἄγαμος",
    23: "ἀγανακτέω (aganaktéō | ag-an-ak-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from ἄγαν (much) and ἄχθος (grief)\nStrong's: akin to the base of G43); to be greatly afflicted, i.e. (figuratively) indignant\nKJV: —be much (sore) displeased, have (be moved with, with) indignation.\n\nSee: G43 \n\nCognate Group: G21 (be glad), G20 (gladness)\nVariants: ἀγανακτέω",
    24: "ἀγανάκτησις (aganáktēsis | ag-an-ak'-tay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G23\nStrong's: indignation\nKJV: —indignation.\n\nSee: G23 \n\nCognate Group: G21 (be glad), G20 (gladness)\nVariants: ἀγανάκτησις",
    25: "ἀγαπάω (agapáō | ag-ap-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from ἄγαν (much) (or compare G5689)\nStrong's: to love (in a social or moral sense)\nKJV: —(be-)love(-ed). Compare G5368 .\n\nSee: G5689 \nSee: G5368 \n\nCognate Group: G27 (beloved), G26 (charity), G25 (love)\nVariants: ἀγαπάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H157 אָהַב, H157 אָהַב, H935 בֹּוא, H2142 זָכַר, H2398 חָטָא, H2654 חָפֵץ, H3039 יְדִיד, H3040 יְדִידָה, H3173 יָחִיד, H4191 מוּת, H4885 מָשֹׂושׂ, H5689 עֲגַב, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6601 פָּתָה, H7069 קָנָה, H7355 רָחַם, H7521 רָצָה, H8104 שָׁמַר, H8173 שָׁעַע, H8191 שַׁעְשֻׁעַ",
    26: "ἀγάπη (agápē | ag-ah'-pay)\n\nDerivation: from G25\nStrong's: love, i.e. affection or benevolence; specially (plural) a love-feast\nKJV: —(feast of) charity(-ably), dear, love.\n\nSee: G25 \n\nCognate Group: G27 (beloved), G26 (charity), G25 (love)\nVariants: ἀγάπη \nHebrew Equivalents: H160 אַהֲבָה",
    27: "ἀγαπητός (agapētós | ag-ap-ay-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G25\nStrong's: beloved\nKJV: —(dearly, well) beloved, dear.\n\nSee: G25 \n\nCognate Group: G27 (beloved), G26 (charity), G25 (love)\nVariants: ἀγαπητός \nHebrew Equivalents: H157 אָהַב, H1730 דֹּוד, H3039 יְדִיד, H3173 יָחִיד, H3358 יַקִּיר",
    28: "Ἄγαρ (Ágar | ag'-ar)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H1904)\nStrong's: Hagar, the concubine of Abraham\nKJV: —Hagar.\nSee: H1904\n\nCognate Group: G28 (Hagar)\nVariants: Ἄγαρ, Ἁγάρ, Ἅγαρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1904 הָגָר",
    29: "ἀγγαρεύω (angareúō | ang-ar-yew'-o)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (compare H104)\nStrong's: properly, to be a courier, i.e. (by implication) to press into public service\nKJV: —compel (to go).\nSee: H104\n\nCognate Group: G28 (Hagar)\nVariants: ἀγγαρεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H104 אִגְּרָא",
    30: "ἀγγεῖον (angeîon | ang-eye'-on)\n\nDerivation: from ἄγγος (a pail, perhaps as bent\nStrong's: compare the base of G43); a receptacle\nKJV: —vessel.\n\nSee: G43 \n\nCognate Group: G28 (Hagar)\nVariants: ἀγγεῖον, ἄγγος \nHebrew Equivalents: H953 בֹּור, H3603 כִּכָּר, H3619 כְּלוּב, H3627 כְּלִי, H3627 כְּלִי, H5035 נֶבֶל",
    31: "ἀγγελία (angelía | ang-el-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G32\nStrong's: an announcement, i.e. (by implication) precept\nKJV: —message.\n\nSee: G32 \n\nCognate Group: G1229 (declare), G31 (message), G1804 (shew forth), G518 (bring word), G2604 (setter forth), G4293 (foretell), G312 (declare), G2605 (declare)\nVariants: ἀγγελία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5046 נָגַד",
    32: "ἄγγελος (ángelos | ang'-el-os)\n\nDerivation: from ἀγγέλλω (probably derived from G71; compare G34)\nStrong's: (to bring tidings); a messenger; especially an \"angel\"; by implication, a pastor\nKJV: —angel, messenger.\n\nSee: G71 \nSee: G34 \n\nCognate Group: G743 (archangel), G2465 (equal unto the angels), G32 (angel)\nVariants: ἄγγελος \nHebrew Equivalents: H46 אָבִיר, H410 אֵל, H426 אֱלָהּ, H582 אֱנֹושׁ, H1121 בֵּן, H2719 חֶרֶב, H3027 יָד, H3619 כְּלוּב, H3627 כְּלִי, H4397 מֲלְאָךְ, H4397 מֲלְאָךְ, H4400 מַלְאֲכוּת, H5650 עֶבֶד, H5678 עֶבְרָה, H5892 עִיר, H6735 צִיר, H8269 שַׂר",
    33: "ἄγε (áge | ag'-eh)\n\nDerivation: imperative of G71\nStrong's: properly, lead, i.e. come on\nKJV: —go to.\n\nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G743 (archangel), G2465 (equal unto the angels), G32 (angel)\nVariants: ἄγε, ἄγω G72",
    34: "ἀγέλη (agélē | ag-el'-ay)\n\nDerivation: from G71 (compare G32)\nStrong's: a drove\nKJV: —herd.\n\nSee: G71 \nSee: G32 \n\nCognate Group: G743 (archangel), G2465 (equal unto the angels), G32 (angel)\nVariants: ἀγέλη \nHebrew Equivalents: H1448 גְּדֵרָה, H5739 עֵדֶר, H8229 שִׁפְעָה",
    35: "ἀγενεαλόγητος (agenealógētos | ag-en-eh-al-og'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as negative particle) and G1075\nStrong's: unregistered as to birth\nKJV: —without descent.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1075 \n\nCognate Group: G1074 (age), G35 (without descent), G1075 (count by descent), G1076 (genealogy)\nVariants: ἀγενεαλόγητος",
    36: "ἀγενής (agenḗs | ag-en-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as negative particle) and G1085\nStrong's: properly, without kin, i.e. (of unknown descent, and by implication) ignoble\nKJV: —base things.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1085 \n\nCognate Group: G1074 (age), G35 (without descent), G1075 (count by descent), G1076 (genealogy)\nVariants: ἀγενής",
    37: "ἁγιάζω (hagiázō | hag-ee-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G40\nStrong's: to make holy, i.e. (ceremonially) purify or consecrate; (mentally) to venerate\nKJV: —hallow, be holy, sanctify.\n\nSee: G40 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἁγιάζω, ἀνασῴζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1305 בָּרַר, H3722 כָּפַר, H4720 מִקְדָּשׁ, H5139 נָזִיר, H5144 נָזַר, H6918 קָדֹושׁ, H6942 קָדַשׁ, H6944 קֹדֶשׁ",
    38: "ἁγιασμός (hagiasmós | hag-ee-as-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G37\nStrong's: properly, purification, i.e. (the state) purity; concretely (by Hebraism) a purifier\nKJV: —holiness, sanctification.\n\nSee: G37 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἁγιασμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4720 מִקְדָּשׁ, H4771 מַרְגֹּועַ, H5139 נָזִיר, H6942 קָדַשׁ, H6944 קֹדֶשׁ",
    39: "ἅγιον (hágion | hag'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G40\nStrong's: a sacred thing (i.e. spot)\nKJV: —holiest (of all), holy place, sanctuary.\n\nSee: G40 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἅγιον, ἅγιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H433 אֱלֹוהַּ, H727 אָרֹון, H906 בַּד, H1305 בָּרַר, H1419 גָּדֹול, H2073 זְבוּל, H2889 טָהֹור, H2896 טֹוב, H3493 יַתִּיר, H4720 מִקְדָּשׁ, H4725 מָקֹום, H5139 נָזִיר, H5145 נֶזֶר, H6635 צָבָא, H6697 צוּר, H6845 צָפַן, H6918 קָדֹושׁ, H6922 קַדִּישׁ, H6942 קָדַשׁ, H6944 קֹדֶשׁ, H7682 שָׂגַב",
    40: "ἅγιος (hágios | hag'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from ἅγος (an awful thing) (compare G53, G2282)\nStrong's: sacred (physically, pure, morally blameless or religious, ceremonially, consecrated)\nKJV: —(most) holy (one, thing), saint.\n\nSee: G53 \nSee: G2282 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἅγιος, ἀγνοέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6944 קֹדֶשׁ",
    41: "ἁγιότης (hagiótēs | hag-ee-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G40\nStrong's: sanctity (i.e. properly, the state)\nKJV: —holiness.\n\nSee: G40 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἁγιότης",
    42: "ἁγιωσύνη (hagiōsýnē | hag-ee-o-soo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from G40\nStrong's: sacredness (i.e. properly, the quality)\nKJV: —holiness.\n\nSee: G40 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἁγιωσύνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H1935 הֹוד, H5797 עֹז, H6944 קֹדֶשׁ",
    43: "ἀγκάλη (ankálē | ang-kal'-ay)\n\nDerivation: from ἄγκος (a bend, \"ache\")\nStrong's: an arm (as curved)\nKJV: —arm.\n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἀγκάλη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2436 חֵיק, H6102 עָצֵל",
    44: "ἄγκιστρον (ánkistron | ang'-kis-tron)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G43\nStrong's: a hook (as bent)\nKJV: —hook.\n\nSee: G43 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἄγκιστρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2397 חָח, H2442 חָכָה, H2764 חֵרֶם",
    45: "ἄγκυρα (ánkyra | ang'-koo-rah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G43\nStrong's: an \"anchor\" (as crooked)\nKJV: —anchor.\n\nSee: G43 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἄγκυρα",
    46: "ἄγναφος (ágnaphos | ag'-naf-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and the same as G1102\nStrong's: properly, unfulled, i.e. (by implication) new (cloth)\nKJV: —new.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1102 \n\nCognate Group: G42 (holiness), G41 (holiness), G37 (hallow), G38 (holiness), G40 (holy)\nVariants: ἄγναφος",
    47: "ἁγνεία (hagneía | hag-ni'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G53\nStrong's: cleanliness (the quality), i.e. (specially) chastity\nKJV: —purity.\n\nSee: G53 \n\nCognate Group: G53 (chaste), G47 (purity), G48 (purify), G49 (purification), G54 (pureness)\nVariants: ἁγνεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2893 טׇהֳרָה, H5139 נָזִיר, H5145 נֶזֶר",
    48: "ἁγνίζω (hagnízō | hag-nid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G53\nStrong's: to make clean, i.e. (figuratively) sanctify (ceremonially or morally)\nKJV: —purify (self).\n\nSee: G53 \n\nCognate Group: G53 (chaste), G47 (purity), G48 (purify), G49 (purification), G54 (pureness)\nVariants: ἁγνίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2398 חָטָא, H2891 טָהֵר, H5144 נָזַר, H6942 קָדַשׁ, H6944 קֹדֶשׁ",
    49: "ἁγνισμός (hagnismós | hag-nis-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G48\nStrong's: a cleansing (the act), i.e. (ceremonially) lustration\nKJV: —purification.\n\nSee: G48 \n\nCognate Group: G53 (chaste), G47 (purity), G48 (purify), G49 (purification), G54 (pureness)\nVariants: ἁγνισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2403 חַטָּאָה, H2891 טָהֵר, H4771 מַרְגֹּועַ, H5079 נִדָּה, H5145 נֶזֶר",
    50: "ἀγνοέω (agnoéō | ag-no-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3539\nStrong's: not to know (through lack of information or intelligence); by implication, to ignore (through disinclination)\nKJV: —(be) ignorant(-ly), not know, not understand, unknown.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3539 \n\nCognate Group: G50 (ignorant), G57 (unknown), G51 (error), G56 (ignorance), G52 (ignorance)\nVariants: ἀγνοέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H816 אָשַׁם, H2973 יָאַל, H7561 רָשַׁע, H7683 שָׁגַג, H7686 שָׁגָה, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    51: "ἀγνόημα (agnóēma | ag-no'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G50\nStrong's: a thing ignored, i.e. shortcoming\nKJV: —error.\n\nSee: G50 \n\nCognate Group: G50 (ignorant), G57 (unknown), G51 (error), G56 (ignorance), G52 (ignorance)\nVariants: ἀγνόημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4870 מִשְׁגֶּה",
    52: "ἄγνοια (ágnoia | ag'-noy-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G50\nStrong's: ignorance (properly, the quality)\nKJV: —ignorance.\n\nSee: G50 \n\nCognate Group: G50 (ignorant), G57 (unknown), G51 (error), G56 (ignorance), G52 (ignorance)\nVariants: ἄγνοια \nHebrew Equivalents: H817 אָשָׁם, H819 אַשְׁמָה, H5771 עָוֺן, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H7684 שְׁגָגָה",
    53: "ἁγνός (hagnós | hag-nos')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G40\nStrong's: properly, clean, i.e. (figuratively) innocent, modest, perfect\nKJV: —chaste, clean, pure.\n\nSee: G40 \n\nCognate Group: G53 (chaste), G47 (purity), G48 (purify), G49 (purification), G54 (pureness)\nVariants: ἁγνός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2134 זַךְ, H2135 זָכָה, H2889 טָהֹור",
    54: "ἁγνότης (hagnótēs | hag-not'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G53\nStrong's: cleanness (the state), i.e. (figuratively) blamelessness\nKJV: —pureness.\n\nSee: G53 \n\nCognate Group: G53 (chaste), G47 (purity), G48 (purify), G49 (purification), G54 (pureness)\nVariants: ἁγνότης",
    55: "ἁγνῶς (hagnōs | hag-noce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G53\nStrong's: purely, i.e. honestly\nKJV: —sincerely.\n\nSee: G53 \n\nCognate Group: G53 (chaste), G47 (purity), G48 (purify), G49 (purification), G54 (pureness)\nVariants: ἁγνῶς",
    56: "ἀγνωσία (agnōsía | ag-no-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as negative particle) and G1108\nStrong's: ignorance (properly, the state)\nKJV: —ignorance, not the knowledge.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1108 \n\nCognate Group: G50 (ignorant), G57 (unknown), G51 (error), G56 (ignorance), G52 (ignorance)\nVariants: ἀγνωσία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1097 בְּלִי",
    57: "ἄγνωστος (ágnōstos | ag'-noce-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as negative particle) and G1110\nStrong's: unknown\nKJV: —unknown.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1110 \n\nCognate Group: G50 (ignorant), G57 (unknown), G51 (error), G56 (ignorance), G52 (ignorance)\nVariants: ἄγνωστος",
    58: "ἀγορά (agorá | ag-or-ah')\n\nDerivation: from ἀγείρω (to gather\nStrong's: probably akin to G1453); properly, the town-square (as a place of public resort); by implication, a market or thoroughfare\nKJV: —market(-place), street.\n\nSee: G1453 \n\nCognate Group: G50 (ignorant), G57 (unknown), G51 (error), G56 (ignorance), G52 (ignorance)\nVariants: ἀγορά \nHebrew Equivalents: H5801 עִזָּבֹון, H7784 שׁוּק",
    59: "ἀγοράζω (agorázō | ag-or-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G58\nStrong's: properly, to go to market, i.e. (by implication) to purchase; specially, to redeem\nKJV: —buy, redeem.\n\nSee: G58 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγοράζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2505 חָלַק, H3947 לָקַח, H3947 לָקַח, H7069 קָנָה, H7666 שָׁבַר",
    60: "ἀγοραῖος (agoraîos | ag-or-ah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G58\nStrong's: relating to the market-place, i.e. forensic (times); by implication, vulgar\nKJV: —baser sort, low.\n\nSee: G58 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγοραῖος",
    61: "ἄγρα (ágra | ag'-rah)\n\nDerivation: from G71\nStrong's: (abstractly) a catching (of fish); also (concretely) a haul (of fish)\nKJV: —draught.\n\nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἄγρα",
    62: "ἀγράμματος (agrámmatos | ag-ram-mat-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as negative particle) and G1121\nStrong's: unlettered, i.e. illiterate\nKJV: —unlearned.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1121 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγράμματος",
    63: "ἀγραυλέω (agrauléō | ag-row-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G68 and G832 (in the sense of G833)\nStrong's: to camp out\nKJV: —abide in the field.\n\nSee: G68 \nSee: G832 \nSee: G833 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγραυλέω",
    64: "ἀγρεύω (agreúō | ag-rew'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G61\nStrong's: to hunt, i.e. (figuratively) to entrap\nKJV: —catch.\n\nSee: G61 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγρεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1342 גָּאָה, H3920 לָכַד, H3947 לָקַח, H6696 צוּר, H7843 שָׁחַת",
    65: "ἀγριέλαιος (agriélaios | ag-ree-el'-ah-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G66 and G1636\nStrong's: an oleaster\nKJV: —olive tree (which is) wild.\n\nSee: G66 \nSee: G1636 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγριέλαιος",
    66: "ἄγριος (ágrios | ag'-ree-os)\n\nDerivation: from G68\nStrong's: wild (as pertaining to the country), literally (natural) or figuratively (fierce)\nKJV: —wild, raging.\n\nSee: G68 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἄγριος \nHebrew Equivalents: H618 אָסָם, H776 אֶרֶץ, H1250 בָּר, H2738 חָרוּל, H6501 פֶּרֶא, H7704 שָׂדֶה",
    67: "Ἀγρίππας (Agríppas | ag-rip'-pas)\n\nDerivation: apparently from G66 and G2462\nStrong's: wild-horse tamer; Agrippas, one of the Herods\nKJV: —Agrippa.\n\nSee: G66 \nSee: G2462 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: Ἀγρίππας",
    68: "ἀγρός (agrós | ag-ros')\n\nDerivation: from G71\nStrong's: a field (as a drive for cattle); genitive case, the country; specially, a farm, i.e. hamlet\nKJV: —country, farm, piece of ground, land.\n\nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H776 אֶרֶץ, H1250 בָּר, H3293 יַעַר, H4057 מִדְבָּר, H4264 מַחֲנֶה, H7105 קָצִיר, H7704 שָׂדֶה",
    69: "ἀγρυπνέω (agrypnéō | ag-roop-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: ultimately from G1 (as negative particle) and G5258\nStrong's: to be sleepless, i.e. keep awake\nKJV: —watch.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G5258 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγρυπνέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5782 עוּר, H8245 שָׁקַד",
    70: "ἀγρυπνία (agrypnía | ag-roop-nee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G69\nStrong's: sleeplessness, i.e. a keeping awake\nKJV: —watch.\n\nSee: G69 \n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἀγρυπνία",
    71: "ἄγω (ágō | ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: properly, to lead; by implication, to bring, drive, (reflexively) go, (specially) pass (time), or (figuratively) induce\nKJV: —be, bring (forth), carry, (let) go, keep, lead away, be open.\n\nCognate Group: G1805 (redeem), G59 (buy)\nVariants: ἄγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H935 בֹּוא, H1540 גָּלָה, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2266 חָבַר, H2790 חָרַשׁ, H2974 יָאַל, H2986 יָבַל, H3013 יָגָה, H3318 יָצָא, H3381 יָרַד, H3947 לָקַח, H4491 מִנְהָג, H4900 מָשַׁךְ, H5090 נָהַג, H5095 נָהַל, H5148 נָחָה, H5414 נָתַן, H5844 עָטָה, H5927 עָלָה, H5975 עָמַד, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6231 עָשַׁק, H6589 פָּשַׂק, H7121 קָרָא, H8388 תָּאַר",
    72: "ἀγωγή (agōgḗ | ag-o-gay')\n\nDerivation: reduplicated from G71\nStrong's: a bringing up, i.e. mode of living\nKJV: —manner of life.\n\nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: ἀγωγή",
    73: "ἀγών (agṓn | ag-one')\n\nDerivation: from G71\nStrong's: properly, a place of assembly (as if led), i.e. (by implication) a contest (held there); figuratively, an effort or anxiety\nKJV: —conflict, contention, fight, race.\n\nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G73 (conflict), G464 (strive against), G74 (agony), G1864 (earnestly contend for), G75 (fight), G2610 (subdue)\nVariants: ἀγών \nHebrew Equivalents: H3811 לָאָה",
    74: "ἀγωνία (agōnía | ag-o-nee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G73\nStrong's: a struggle (properly, the state), i.e. (figuratively) anguish\nKJV: —agony.\n\nSee: G73 \n\nCognate Group: G73 (conflict), G464 (strive against), G74 (agony), G1864 (earnestly contend for), G75 (fight), G2610 (subdue)\nVariants: ἀγωνία",
    75: "ἀγωνίζομαι (agōnízomai | ag-o-nid'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G73\nStrong's: to struggle, literally (to compete for a prize), figuratively (to contend with an adversary), or genitive case (to endeavor to accomplish something)\nKJV: —fight, labor fervently, strive.\n\nSee: G73 \n\nCognate Group: G73 (conflict), G464 (strive against), G74 (agony), G1864 (earnestly contend for), G75 (fight), G2610 (subdue)\nVariants: ἀγωνίζομαι, ἄθεσμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7712 שְׁדַר, H7760 שׂוּם",
    76: "Ἀδάμ (Adám | ad-am')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H121)\nStrong's: Adam, the first man; typically (of Jesus) man (as his representative)\nKJV: —Adam.\nSee: H121\n\nCognate Group: G76 (Adam)\nVariants: Ἀδάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H121 אָדָם",
    77: "ἀδάπανος (adápanos | ad-ap'-an-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as negative particle)\nStrong's: and G1160 ; costless, i.e. gratuitous\nKJV: —without expense.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1160 \n\nCognate Group: G76 (Adam)\nVariants: ἀδάπανος",
    78: "Ἀδδί (Addí | ad-dee')\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin (compare H5716)\nStrong's: Addi, an Israelite\nKJV: —Addi.\nSee: H5716\n\nCognate Group: G76 (Adam)\nVariants: Ἀδδί \nHebrew Equivalents: H5716 עֲדִי",
    79: "ἀδελφή (adelphḗ | ad-el-fay')\n\nDerivation: feminine of G80\nStrong's: a sister (naturally or ecclesiastically)\nKJV: —sister.\n\nSee: G80 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ἀδελφή \nHebrew Equivalents: H269 אָחֹות, H2860 חָתָן",
    80: "ἀδελφός (adelphós | ad-el-fos')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a connective particle) and δελφύς (the womb)\nStrong's: a brother (literally or figuratively) near or remote (much like G1)\nKJV: —brother.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ἀδελφός \nHebrew Equivalents: H251 אָח, H376 אִישׁ, H1730 דֹּוד, H1733 דֹּודָה, H2992 יָבַם, H2993 יָבָם, H2994 יְבֵמֶת, H3547 כָּהַן, H7453 רֵעַ",
    81: "ἀδελφότης (adelphótēs | ad-el-fot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G80\nStrong's: brotherhood (properly, the feeling of brotherliness), i.e. the (Christian) fraternity\nKJV: —brethren, brotherhood.\n\nSee: G80 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ἀδελφότης",
    82: "ἄδηλος (ádēlos | ad'-ay-los)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G1212\nStrong's: hidden, figuratively, indistinct\nKJV: —appear not, uncertain.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1212 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ἄδηλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H987 בַּטֻּחֹות",
    83: "ἀδηλότης (adēlótēs | ad-ay-lot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G82\nStrong's: uncertainty\nKJV: —X uncertain.\n\nSee: G82 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ἀδηλότης",
    84: "ἀδήλως (adḗlōs | ad-ay'-loce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G82\nStrong's: uncertainly\nKJV: —uncertainly.\n\nSee: G82 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ἀδήλως",
    85: "ἀδημονέω (adēmonéō | ad-ay-mon-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of ἀδέω (to be sated to loathing)\nStrong's: to be in distress (of mind)\nKJV: —be full of heaviness, be very heavy.\n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ἀδημονέω, ἀκηδεμονέω",
    86: "ᾅδης (háidēs | hah'-dace)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as negative particle) and G1492\nStrong's: properly, unseen, i.e. \"Hades\" or the place (state) of departed souls\nKJV: —grave, hell.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1492 \n\nCognate Group: G86 (grave)\nVariants: ᾅδης, ᾍδης \nHebrew Equivalents: H1745 דּוּמָה, H4191 מוּת, H4194 מָוֶת, H6757 צַלְמָוֶת, H7585 שְׁאֹול",
    87: "ἀδιάκριτος (adiákritos | ad-ee-ak'-ree-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G1252\nStrong's: properly, undistinguished, i.e. (actively) impartial\nKJV: —without partiality.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1252 \n\nCognate Group: G612 (answer), G2631 (condemnation), G470 (answer again), G351 (examination), G2923 (judge), G610 (sentence), G2924 (discerner), G1469 (make of the number), G350 (ask), G1253 (discern), G843 (condemned of self), G2632 (condemn), G2919 (avenge), G2917 (avenge), G1252 (contend), G87 (without partiality), G611 (answer), G178 (uncondemned), G2633 (condemn), G2920 (accusation), G2922 (to judge)\nVariants: ἀδιάκριτος",
    88: "ἀδιάλειπτος (adiáleiptos | ad-ee-al'-ipe-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of a compound of G1223 and G3007\nStrong's: unintermitted, i.e. permanent\nKJV: —without ceasing, continual.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1223 \nSee: G3007 \n\nCognate Group: G612 (answer), G2631 (condemnation), G470 (answer again), G351 (examination), G2923 (judge), G610 (sentence), G2924 (discerner), G1469 (make of the number), G350 (ask), G1253 (discern), G843 (condemned of self), G2632 (condemn), G2919 (avenge), G2917 (avenge), G1252 (contend), G87 (without partiality), G611 (answer), G178 (uncondemned), G2633 (condemn), G2920 (accusation), G2922 (to judge)\nVariants: ἀδιάλειπτος",
    89: "ἀδιαλείπτως (adialeíptōs | ad-ee-al-ipe'-toce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G88\nStrong's: uninterruptedly, i.e. without omission (on an appropriate occasion)\nKJV: —without ceasing.\n\nSee: G88 \n\nCognate Group: G612 (answer), G2631 (condemnation), G470 (answer again), G351 (examination), G2923 (judge), G610 (sentence), G2924 (discerner), G1469 (make of the number), G350 (ask), G1253 (discern), G843 (condemned of self), G2632 (condemn), G2919 (avenge), G2917 (avenge), G1252 (contend), G87 (without partiality), G611 (answer), G178 (uncondemned), G2633 (condemn), G2920 (accusation), G2922 (to judge)\nVariants: ἀδιαλείπτως",
    90: "ἀδιαφθορία (adiaphthoría | ad-ee-af-thor-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G1311\nStrong's: incorruptibleness, i.e. (figuratively) purity (of doctrine)\nKJV: —uncorruptness.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1311 \n\nCognate Group: G5351 (corrupt), G1311 (corrupt), G862 (not corruptible), G5256 (minister), G5349 (corruptible), G90 (uncorruptness), G1312 (corruption), G861 (immortality), G2704 (corrupt)\nVariants: ἀδιαφθορία, ἀφθονία, ἀφθορία \nHebrew Equivalents: H7711 שְׁדֵפָה",
    91: "ἀδικέω (adikéō | ad-ee-keh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G94\nStrong's: to be unjust, i.e. (actively) do wrong (morally, socially or physically)\nKJV: —hurt, injure, be an offender, be unjust, (do, suffer, take) wrong.\n\nSee: G94 \n\nCognate Group: G93 (iniquity), G94 (unjust), G92 (evil doing), G91 (hurt)\nVariants: ἀδικέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1214 בָּצַע, H1792 דָּכָא, H2398 חָטָא, H2554 חָמַס, H2556 חָמֵץ, H3013 יָגָה, H3027 יָד, H3401 יָרִיב, H4603 מָעַל, H4775 מָרַד, H4878 מְשׁוּבָה, H5221 נָכָה, H5753 עָוָה, H5791 עָוַת, H5927 עָלָה, H6184 עָרִיץ, H6229 עָשַׂק, H6231 עָשַׁק, H6467 פֹּעַל, H6586 פָּשַׁע, H7489 רָעַע, H7561 רָשַׁע, H7561 רָשַׁע, H8266 שָׁקַר",
    92: "ἀδίκημα (adíkēma | ad-eek'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G91\nStrong's: a wrong done\nKJV: —evil doing, iniquity, matter of wrong.\n\nSee: G91 \n\nCognate Group: G93 (iniquity), G94 (unjust), G92 (evil doing), G91 (hurt)\nVariants: ἀδίκημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2552 חָמַם, H4941 מִשְׁפָּט, H5766 עֶוֶל, H5771 עָוֺן, H6233 עֹשֶׁק, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H7451 רַע",
    93: "ἀδικία (adikía | ad-ee-kee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G94\nStrong's: (legal) injustice (properly, the quality, by implication, the act); morally, wrongfulness (of character, life or act)\nKJV: —iniquity, unjust, unrighteousness, wrong.\n\nSee: G94 \n\nCognate Group: G93 (iniquity), G94 (unjust), G92 (evil doing), G91 (hurt)\nVariants: ἀδικία \nHebrew Equivalents: H57 אָבֵל, H57 אָבֵל, H185 אַוָּה, H817 אָשָׁם, H1004 בַּיִת, H1215 בֶּצַע, H1347 גָּאֹון, H1942 הַוָּה, H2162 זָמָם, H2195 זַעַם, H2403 חַטָּאָה, H2555 חָמָס, H4297 מֻטֶּה, H4604 מַעַל, H4639 מַעֲשֶׂה, H4642 מַעֲשַׁקָּה, H4820 מִרְמָה, H5753 עָוָה, H5753 עָוָה, H5766 עֶוֶל, H5766 עֶוֶל, H5771 עָוֺן, H5930 עֹלָה, H6041 עָנִי, H6230 עֵשֶׂק, H6233 עֹשֶׁק, H6275 עָתַק, H6563 פֶּרֶק, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H7195 קֶשֶׁר, H7200 רָאָה, H7379 רִיב, H7451 רַע, H7562 רֶשַׁע, H7563 רָשָׁע, H8267 שֶׁקֶר",
    94: "ἄδικος (ádikos | ad'-ee-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G1349\nStrong's: unjust; by extension wicked; by implication, treacherous; specially, heathen\nKJV: —unjust, unrighteous.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1349 \n\nCognate Group: G93 (iniquity), G94 (unjust), G92 (evil doing), G91 (hurt)\nVariants: ἄδικος, ἀδικοκρίτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H57 אָבֵל, H205 אָוֶן, H1697 דָּבָר, H2555 חָמָס, H3891 לְזוּת, H4820 מִרְמָה, H5039 נְבָלָה, H5759 עֲוִיל, H5766 עֶוֶל, H5766 עֶוֶל, H6233 עֹשֶׁק, H7423 רְמִיָּה, H7451 רַע, H7561 רָשַׁע, H7563 רָשָׁע, H7701 שֹׁד, H8267 שֶׁקֶר, H8414 תֹּהוּ, H8419 תַּהְפֻּכָה",
    95: "ἀδίκως (adíkōs | ad-ee'-koce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G94\nStrong's: unjustly\nKJV: —wrongfully.\n\nSee: G94 \n\nCognate Group: G93 (iniquity), G94 (unjust), G92 (evil doing), G91 (hurt)\nVariants: ἀδίκως \nHebrew Equivalents: H1097 בְּלִי, H2600 חִנָּם, H3577 כָּזָב, H6662 צַדִּיק, H7451 רַע, H7563 רָשָׁע, H8267 שֶׁקֶר",
    96: "ἀδόκιμος (adókimos | ad-ok'-ee-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G1384\nStrong's: unapproved, i.e. rejected; by implication, worthless (literally or morally)\nKJV: —castaway, rejected, reprobate.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1384 \n\nCognate Group: G1381 (allow), G1383 (trial), G1382 (experience), G1384 (approved), G96 (castaway)\nVariants: ἀδόκιμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5509 סִיג",
    97: "ἄδολος (ádolos | ad'-ol-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle)\nStrong's: and G1388 ; undeceitful, i.e. (figuratively) unadulterated\nKJV: —sincere.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1388 \n\nCognate Group: G1381 (allow), G1383 (trial), G1382 (experience), G1384 (approved), G96 (castaway)\nVariants: ἄδολος",
    98: "Ἀδραμυττηνός (Adramyttēnós | ad-ram-oot-tay-nos')\n\nDerivation: from Ἀδραμύττειον (a place in Asia Minor)\nStrong's: Adramyttene or belonging to Adramyttium\nKJV: —of Adramyttium.\n\nCognate Group: G1381 (allow), G1383 (trial), G1382 (experience), G1384 (approved), G96 (castaway)\nVariants: Ἀδραμυττηνός",
    99: "Ἀδρίας (Adrías | ad-ree'-as)\n\nDerivation: from Ἀδρία (a place near its shore)\nStrong's: the Adriatic sea (including the Ionian)\nKJV: —Adria.\n\nCognate Group: G1381 (allow), G1383 (trial), G1382 (experience), G1384 (approved), G96 (castaway)\nVariants: Ἀδρίας",
    100: "ἁδρότης (hadrótēs | had-rot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from ἁδρός (stout)\nStrong's: plumpness, i.e. (figuratively) liberality\nKJV: —abundance.\n\nCognate Group: G1381 (allow), G1383 (trial), G1382 (experience), G1384 (approved), G96 (castaway)\nVariants: ἁδρότης",
    101: "ἀδυνατέω (adynatéō | ad-oo-nat-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G102\nStrong's: to be unable, i.e. (passively) impossible\nKJV: —be impossible.\n\nSee: G102 \n\nCognate Group: G1411 (ability), G1743 (enable), G1414 (be mighty), G1410 (be able), G1412 (strengthen), G1413 (of great authority), G101 (be impossible), G1415 (able), G102 (could not do)\nVariants: ἀδυνατέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H597 אָנַס, H1219 בָּצַר, H3766 כָּרַע, H3782 כָּשַׁל, H4191 מוּת, H6381 פָּלָא",
    102: "ἀδύνατος (adýnatos | ad-oo'-nat-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G1415\nStrong's: unable, i.e. weak (literally or figuratively); passively, impossible\nKJV: —could not do, impossible, impotent, not possible, weak.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1415 \n\nCognate Group: G1411 (ability), G1743 (enable), G1414 (be mighty), G1410 (be able), G1412 (strengthen), G1413 (of great authority), G101 (be impossible), G1415 (able), G102 (could not do)\nVariants: ἀδύνατος \nHebrew Equivalents: H34 אֶבְיֹון, H47 אַבִּיר, H1800 דַּל, H2522 חָלַשׁ, H6041 עָנִי, H6381 פָּלָא",
    103: "ᾄδω (áidō | ad'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to sing\nKJV: —sing.\n\nCognate Group: G5603 (song), G103 (sing)\nVariants: ᾄδω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2171 זַמָּר, H6476 פָּצַח, H7891 שִׁיר, H7891 שִׁיר, H8334 שָׁרַת, H8426 תֹּודָה",
    104: "ἀεί (aeí | ah-eye')\n\nDerivation: from an obsolete primary noun (apparently meaning continued duration)\nStrong's: \"ever,\" by qualification regularly; by implication, earnestly\nKJV: —always, ever.\n\nCognate Group: G5603 (song), G103 (sing)\nVariants: ἀεί \nHebrew Equivalents: H5769 עֹולָם, H8548 תָּמִיד",
    105: "ἀετός (aetós | ah-et-os')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G109\nStrong's: an eagle (from its wind-like flight)\nKJV: —eagle.\n\nSee: G109 \n\nCognate Group: G5603 (song), G103 (sing)\nVariants: ἀετός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5403 נְשַׁר, H5404 נֶשֶׁר",
    106: "ἄζυμος (ázymos | ad'-zoo-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2219\nStrong's: unleavened, i.e. (figuratively) uncorrupted; (in the neutral plural) specially (by implication) the Passover week\nKJV: —unleavened (bread).\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2219 \n\nCognate Group: G2220 (leaven), G2219 (leaven), G106 (unleavened)\nVariants: ἄζυμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4680 מָצָה",
    107: "Ἀζώρ (Azṓr | ad-zore')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (compare H5809)\nStrong's: Azor, an Israelite\nKJV: —Azorigin\nSee: H5809\n\nCognate Group: G2220 (leaven), G2219 (leaven), G106 (unleavened)\nVariants: Ἀζώρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5809 עַזּוּר",
    108: "Ἄζωτος (Ázōtos | ad'-zo-tos)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H795)\nStrong's: Azotus (i.e. Ashdod), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Azotus.\nSee: H795\n\nCognate Group: G2220 (leaven), G2219 (leaven), G106 (unleavened)\nVariants: Ἄζωτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H795 אַשְׁדֹּוד",
    109: "ἀήρ (aḗr | ah-ayr')\n\nDerivation: from ἄημι (to breathe unconsciously, i.e. respire\nStrong's: by analogy, to blow); \"air\" (as naturally circumambient)\nKJV: —air. Compare G5594 .\n\nSee: G5594 \n\nCognate Group: G109 (air)\nVariants: ἀήρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7834 שַׁחַק",
    110: "ἀθανασία (athanasía | ath-an-as-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and G2288\nStrong's: deathlessness\nKJV: —immortality.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2288 \n\nCognate Group: G4880 (be dead with), G2289 (become dead), G2348 (be dead), G2288 (X deadly), G599 (be dead), G2349 (mortal), G110 (immortality)\nVariants: ἀθανασία",
    111: "ἀθέμιτος (athémitos | ath-em'-ee-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of θέμις (statute\nStrong's: from the base of G5087); illegal; by implication, flagitious\nKJV: —abominable, unlawful thing.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G111 (abominable)\nVariants: ἀθέμιτος",
    112: "ἄθεος (átheos | ath'-eh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2316\nStrong's: godless\nKJV: —without God.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2316 \n\nCognate Group: G2312 (taught of God), G2320 (godhead), G2304 (divine), G2316 (X exceeding), G2305 (godhead), G112 (without God)\nVariants: ἄθεος",
    113: "ἄθεσμος (áthesmos | ath'-es-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G5087 (in the sense of enacting)\nStrong's: lawless, i.e. (by implication) criminal\nKJV: —wicked.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G113 (wicked)\nVariants: ἄθεσμος",
    114: "ἀθετέω (athetéō | ath-et-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G5087\nStrong's: to set aside, i.e. (by implication) to disesteem, neutralize or violate\nKJV: —cast off, despise, disannul, frustrate, bring to nought, reject.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: ἀθετέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H898 בָּגַד, H1245 בָּקַשׁ, H2554 חָמַס, H4171 מוּר, H4603 מָעַל, H4672 מָצָא, H4775 מָרַד, H5006 נָאַץ, H5106 נוּא, H5493 סוּר, H6586 פָּשַׁע, H7725 שׁוּב, H8085 שָׁמַע, H8133 שְׁנָא, H8138 שָׁנָה, H8266 שָׁקַר",
    115: "ἀθέτησις (athétēsis | ath-et'-ay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G114\nStrong's: cancellation (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —disannulling, put away.\n\nSee: G114 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: ἀθέτησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H899 בֶּגֶד, H4604 מַעַל, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H7200 רָאָה",
    116: "Ἀθῆναι (Athēnai | ath-ay-nahee)\n\nDerivation: plural of Ἀθήνη (the goddess of wisdom, who was reputed to have founded the city)\nStrong's: Athenæ, the capitol of Greece\nKJV: — Athens.\n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: Ἀθῆναι",
    117: "Ἀθηναῖος (Athēnaîos | ath-ay-nah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G116\nStrong's: an Athenæan or inhabitant of Athenæ\nKJV: —Athenian.\n\nSee: G116 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: Ἀθηναῖος",
    118: "ἀθλέω (athléō | ath-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from ἄθλος (a contest in the public lists)\nStrong's: to contend in the competitive games\nKJV: —strive.\n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: ἀθλέω",
    119: "ἄθλησις (áthlēsis | ath'-lay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G118\nStrong's: a struggle (figuratively)\nKJV: —fight.\n\nSee: G118 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: ἄθλησις",
    120: "ἀθυμέω (athyméō | ath-oo-meh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and G2372\nStrong's: to be spiritless, i.e. disheartened\nKJV: —be dismayed.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2372 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: ἀθυμέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H34 אֶבְיֹון, H2734 חָרָה, H3707 כַּעַס, H7268 רַגָּז, H7481 רָעַם",
    121: "ἄθωος (áthōos | ath'-o-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and probably a derivative of G5087 (meaning a penalty)\nStrong's: not guilty\nKJV: —innocent.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: ἄθωος, ἀθῷος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4421 מִלְחָמָה, H5352 נָקָה, H5352 נָקָה, H5355 נָקִי, H5356 נִקָּיֹון, H8549 תָּמִים, H8552 תָּמַם",
    122: "αἴγειος (aígeios | ah'-ee-ghi-os)\n\nDerivation: from αἴξ (a goat)\nStrong's: belonging to a goat\nKJV: —goat.\n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: αἴγειος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5795 עֵז",
    123: "αἰγιαλός (aigialós | ahee-ghee-al-os')\n\nDerivation: from ἀΐσσω (to rush) and G251 (in the sense of the sea\nStrong's: a beach (on which the waves dash)\nKJV: —shore.\n\nSee: G251 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: αἰγιαλός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2348 חֹוף",
    124: "Αἰγύπτιος (Aigýptios | ahee-goop'-tee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G125\nStrong's: an Ægyptian or inhabitant of Ægyptus\nKJV: —Egyptian.\n\nSee: G125 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: Αἰγύπτιος",
    125: "Αἴγυπτος (Aígyptos | ah'-ee-goop-tos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Ægyptus, the land of the Nile\nKJV: —Egypt.\n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: Αἴγυπτος",
    126: "ἀΐδιος (aḯdios | ah-id'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G104\nStrong's: everduring (forward and backward, or forward only)\nKJV: —eternal, everlasting.\n\nSee: G104 \n\nCognate Group: G126 (eternal)\nVariants: ἀΐδιος, ἀί̈διος, ἀΐδιος",
    127: "αἰδώς (aidṓs | ahee-doce')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G1 (as a negative particle) and G1492 (through the idea of downcast eyes)\nStrong's: bashfulness, i.e. (towards men), modesty or (towards God) awe\nKJV: —reverence, shamefacedness.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1492 \n\nCognate Group: G127 (reverence)\nVariants: αἰδώς, δέος",
    128: "Αἰθίοψ (Aithíops | ahee-thee'-ops)\n\nDerivation: from αἴθω (to scorch) and ὤψ (the face, from G3700)\nStrong's: an Æthiopian (as a blackamoor)\nKJV: —Ethiopian.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G127 (reverence)\nVariants: Αἰθίοψ",
    129: "αἷμα (haîma | hah'-ee-mah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: blood, literally (of men or animals), figuratively (the juice of grapes) or specially (the atoning blood of Christ); by implication, bloodshed, also kindred\nKJV: —blood.\n\nCognate Group: G130 (shedding of blood), G129 (blood)\nVariants: αἷμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1818 דָּם, H5331 נֶצַח, H7307 רוּחַ",
    130: "αἱματεκχυσία (haimatekchysía | hahee-mat-ek-khoo-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G129 and a derivative of G1632\nStrong's: an effusion of blood\nKJV: —shedding of blood.\n\nSee: G129 \nSee: G1632 \n\nCognate Group: G130 (shedding of blood), G129 (blood)\nVariants: αἱματεκχυσία",
    131: "αἱμοῤῥέω (haimorrhéō | hahee-mor-hreh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G129 and G4482\nStrong's: to flow blood, i.e. have a hæmorrhage\nKJV: —diseased with an issue of blood.\n\nSee: G129 \nSee: G4482 \n\nCognate Group: G130 (shedding of blood), G129 (blood)\nVariants: αἱμοῤῥέω, αἱμορροέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1739 דָּוֶה",
    132: "Αἰνέας (Ainéas | ahee-neh'-as)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Ænĕas, an Israelite\nKJV: —Æneas.\n\nCognate Group: G130 (shedding of blood), G129 (blood)\nVariants: Αἰνέας",
    133: "αἴνεσις (aínesis | ah'-ee-nes-is)\n\nDerivation: from G134\nStrong's: a praising (the act), i.e. (specially) a thank(-offering)\nKJV: —praise.\n\nSee: G134 \n\nCognate Group: G130 (shedding of blood), G129 (blood)\nVariants: αἴνεσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1576 גְּמוּל, H1984 הָלַל, H2172 זִמְרָה, H3034 יָדָה, H4399 מְלָאכָה, H7440 רִנָּה, H8057 שִׂמְחָה, H8342 שָׂשֹׂון, H8416 תְּהִלָּה, H8426 תֹּודָה",
    134: "αἰνέω (ainéō | ahee-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G136\nStrong's: to praise (God)\nKJV: —praise.\n\nSee: G136 \n\nCognate Group: G134 (praise), G136 (praise)\nVariants: αἰνέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H369 אַיִן, H1288 בָרַךְ, H1927 הֲדָרָה, H1984 הָלַל, H3034 יָדָה, H7321 רוּעַ, H7623 שָׁבַח, H7891 שִׁיר",
    135: "αἴνιγμα (aínigma | ah'-ee-nig-ma)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G136 (in its primary sense)\nStrong's: an obscure saying (\"enigma\"), i.e. (abstractly) obscureness\nKJV: —X darkly.\n\nSee: G136 \n\nCognate Group: G135 (X darkly)\nVariants: αἴνιγμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2420 חִידָה, H8047 שַׁמָּה",
    136: "αἶνος (aînos | ah'-ee-nos)\n\nDerivation: apparently a prime word\nStrong's: properly, a story, but used in the sense of G1868 ; praise (of God)\nKJV: —praise.\n\nSee: G1868 \n\nCognate Group: G134 (praise), G136 (praise)\nVariants: αἶνος, αὶνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל, H5797 עֹז",
    137: "Αἰνών (Ainṓn | ahee-nohn')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (a derivative of G5869, place of springs)\nStrong's: Ænon, a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Ænon.\n\nSee: G5869 \n\nCognate Group: G134 (praise), G136 (praise)\nVariants: Αἰνών \nHebrew Equivalents: H5869 עַיִן",
    138: "αἱρέομαι (hairéomai | hahee-reh'-om-ahee)\n\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G142\nStrong's: to take for oneself, i.e. to prefer\nKJV: —choose. Some of the forms are borrowed from a cognate ἕλλομαι (héllomai | hel'-lom-ahee)\n; which is otherwise obsolete.\n\nSee: G142 \n\nCognate Group: G139 (heresy), G1243 (difference), G1244 (divide), G141 (heretic), G140 (choose), G138 (choose)\nVariants: αἱρέομαι, αἱρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H977 בָּחַר, H2654 חָפֵץ, H2836 חָשַׁק, H5186 נָטָה, H5493 סוּר",
    139: "αἵρεσις (haíresis | hah'-ee-res-is)\n\nDerivation: from G138\nStrong's: properly, a choice, i.e. (specially) a party or (abstractly) disunion\nKJV: —heresy (which is the Greek word itself), sect.\n\nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G139 (heresy), G1243 (difference), G1244 (divide), G141 (heretic), G140 (choose), G138 (choose)\nVariants: αἵρεσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H5071 נְדָבָה",
    140: "αἱρετίζω (hairetízō | hahee-ret-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G138\nStrong's: to make a choice\nKJV: —choose.\n\nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G139 (heresy), G1243 (difference), G1244 (divide), G141 (heretic), G140 (choose), G138 (choose)\nVariants: αἱρετίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H977 בָּחַר, H2082 זָבַל, H2550 חָמַל, H2654 חָפֵץ, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5493 סוּר, H5753 עָוָה",
    141: "αἱρετικός (hairetikós | hahee-ret-ee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G140\nStrong's: a schismatic\nKJV: —heretic (the Greek word itself).\n\nSee: G140 \n\nCognate Group: G139 (heresy), G1243 (difference), G1244 (divide), G141 (heretic), G140 (choose), G138 (choose)\nVariants: αἱρετικός",
    142: "αἴρω (aírō | ah'-ee-ro)\n\nDerivation: a primary root\nStrong's: to lift up; by implication, to take up or away; figuratively, to raise (the voice), keep in suspense (the mind), specially, to sail away (i.e. weigh anchor); by Hebraism (compare H5375) to expiate sin\nKJV: —away with, bear (up), carry, lift up, loose, make to doubt, put away, remove, take (away, up).\nSee: H5375\n\nCognate Group: G142 (away with), G1869 (exalt self)\nVariants: αἴρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H656 אָפֵס, H935 בֹּוא, H1504 גָּזַר, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2846 חָתָה, H2904 טוּל, H3426 יֵשׁ, H3447 יָשַׁט, H3947 לָקַח, H3988 מָאַס, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H5130 נוּף, H5186 נָטָה, H5190 נָטַל, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5493 סוּר, H5647 עָבַד, H5710 עָדָה, H5737 עֲדַר, H6004 עָמַם, H6006 עָמַס, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6565 פָּרַר, H6566 פָּרַשׂ, H7311 רוּם, H7673 שָׁבַת, H7843 שָׁחַת",
    143: "αἰσθάνομαι (aisthánomai | ahee-sthan'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: to apprehend (properly, by the senses)\nKJV: —perceive.\n\nCognate Group: G144 (judgment), G143 (perceive), G145 (senses)\nVariants: αἰσθάνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2648 חָפַז, H3045 יָדַע",
    144: "αἴσθησις (aísthēsis | ah'-ee-sthay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G143\nStrong's: perception, i.e. (figuratively) discernment\nKJV: —judgment.\n\nSee: G143 \n\nCognate Group: G144 (judgment), G143 (perceive), G145 (senses)\nVariants: αἴσθησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1116 בָּמָה, H1847 דַּעַת, H3045 יָדַע",
    145: "αἰσθητήριον (aisthētḗrion | ahee-sthay-tay'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G143\nStrong's: properly, an organ of perception, i.e. (figuratively) judgment\nKJV: —senses.\n\nSee: G143 \n\nCognate Group: G144 (judgment), G143 (perceive), G145 (senses)\nVariants: αἰσθητήριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H7023 קִיר",
    146: "αἰσχροκερδής (aischrokerdḗs | ahee-skhrok-er-dace')\n\nDerivation: from G150 and κέρδος (gain)\nStrong's: sordid\nKJV: —given to (greedy of) filthy lucre.\n\nSee: G150 \n\nCognate Group: G144 (judgment), G143 (perceive), G145 (senses)\nVariants: αἰσχροκερδής",
    147: "αἰσχροκερδῶς (aischrokerdōs | ahee-skhrok-er-doce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G146\nStrong's: sordidly\nKJV: —for filthy lucre's sake.\n\nSee: G146 \n\nCognate Group: G144 (judgment), G143 (perceive), G145 (senses)\nVariants: αἰσχροκερδῶς",
    148: "αἰσχρολογία (aischrología | ahee-skhrol-og-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G150 and G3056\nStrong's: vile conversation\nKJV: —filthy communication.\n\nSee: G150 \nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G144 (judgment), G143 (perceive), G145 (senses)\nVariants: αἰσχρολογία",
    149: "αἰσχρόν (aischrón | ahee-skhron')\n\nDerivation: neuter of G150\nStrong's: a shameful thing, i.e. indecorum\nKJV: —shame.\n\nSee: G150 \n\nCognate Group: G144 (judgment), G143 (perceive), G145 (senses)\nVariants: αἰσχρόν, αἰσχρός G156",
    150: "αἰσχρός (aischrós | ahee-skhros')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G153\nStrong's: shameful, i.e. base (specially, venal)\nKJV: —filthy.\n\nSee: G153 \n\nCognate Group: G2617 (confound), G151 (filthiness), G1870 (be ashamed), G150 (filthy), G152 (dishonesty), G153 (be ashamed)\nVariants: αἰσχρός",
    151: "αἰσχρότης (aischrótēs | ahee-skhrot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G150\nStrong's: shamefulness, i.e. obscenity\nKJV: —filthiness.\n\nSee: G150 \n\nCognate Group: G2617 (confound), G151 (filthiness), G1870 (be ashamed), G150 (filthy), G152 (dishonesty), G153 (be ashamed)\nVariants: αἰσχρότης",
    152: "αἰσχύνη (aischýnē | ahee-skhoo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from G153\nStrong's: shame or disgrace (abstractly or concretely)\nKJV: —dishonesty, shame.\n\nSee: G153 \n\nCognate Group: G2617 (confound), G151 (filthiness), G1870 (be ashamed), G150 (filthy), G152 (dishonesty), G153 (be ashamed)\nVariants: αἰσχύνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H954 בּוּשׁ, H1167 בַּעַל, H1322 בֹּשֶׁת, H1860 דְּרָאֹון, H1942 הַוָּה, H1971 הַכָּרָה, H2781 חֶרְפָּה, H3639 כְּלִמָּה, H4626 מַעַר, H6172 עֶרְוָה",
    153: "αἰσχύνομαι (aischýnomai | ahee-skhoo'-nom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from αἶσχος (disfigurement, i.e. disgrace)\nStrong's: to feel shame (for oneself)\nKJV: —be ashamed.\n\nCognate Group: G2617 (confound), G151 (filthiness), G1870 (be ashamed), G150 (filthy), G152 (dishonesty), G153 (be ashamed)\nVariants: αἰσχύνομαι, αἰσχύνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H887 בָּאַשׁ, H954 בּוּשׁ, H2659 חָפֵר, H2731 חֲרָדָה, H2790 חָרַשׁ, H3637 כָּלַם, H3665 כָּנַע, H5234 נָכַר, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6181 עֶרְיָה",
    154: "αἰτέω (aitéō | ahee-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: to ask (in genitive case)\nKJV: —ask, beg, call for, crave, desire, require. Compare G4441 .\n\nSee: G4441 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: αἰτέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H1156 בְּעָא, H5414 נָתַן, H7592 שָׁאַל",
    155: "αἴτημα (aítēma | ah'-ee-tay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G154\nStrong's: a thing asked or (abstractly) an asking\nKJV: —petition, request, required.\n\nSee: G154 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: αἴτημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1159 בָּעוּ, H4100 מָה, H7596 שְׁאֵלָה",
    156: "αἰτία (aitía | ahee-tee'-a)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G154\nStrong's: a cause (as if asked for), i.e. (logical) reason (motive, matter), (legal) crime (alleged or proved)\nKJV: —accusation, case, cause, crime, fault, (wh-)ere(-fore).\n\nSee: G154 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: αἰτία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5771 עָוֺן, H6231 עָשַׁק",
    157: "αἰτίαμα (aitíama | ahee-tee'-am-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G156\nStrong's: a thing charged\nKJV: —complaint.\n\nSee: G156 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: αἰτίαμα, αἰτίωμα",
    158: "αἴτιον (aítion | ah'-ee-tee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G159\nStrong's: a reason or crime (like G156)\nKJV: —cause, fault.\n\nSee: G159 \nSee: G156 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: αἴτιον, αἴτιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5437 סָבַב",
    159: "αἴτιος (aítios | ah'-ee-tee-os)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G154\nStrong's: causative, i.e. (concretely) a causer\nKJV: —author.\n\nSee: G154 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: αἴτιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5437 סָבַב",
    160: "αἰφνίδιος (aiphnídios | aheef-nid'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and G5316 (compare G1810) (meaning non-apparent)\nStrong's: unexpected, i.e. (adverbially) suddenly\nKJV: —sudden, unawares.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G5316 \nSee: G1810 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: αἰφνίδιος",
    161: "αἰχμαλωσία (aichmalōsía | aheekh-mal-o-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G164\nStrong's: captivity\nKJV: —captivity.\n\nSee: G164 \n\nCognate Group: G4869 (fellowprisoner), G161 (captivity), G162 (lead captive), G163 (lead away captive), G164 (captive)\nVariants: αἰχμαλωσία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1473 גֹּולָה, H4908 מִשְׁכָּן, H7622 שְׁבוּת, H7628 שְׁבִי, H7655 שִׁבְעָה, H7667 שֶׁבֶר",
    162: "αἰχμαλωτεύω (aichmalōteúō | aheekh-mal-o-tew'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G164\nStrong's: to capture (like G163)\nKJV: —lead captive.\n\nSee: G164 \nSee: G163 \n\nCognate Group: G4869 (fellowprisoner), G161 (captivity), G162 (lead captive), G163 (lead away captive), G164 (captive)\nVariants: αἰχμαλωτεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1540 גָּלָה, H3947 לָקַח, H5090 נָהַג, H7617 שָׁבָה",
    163: "αἰχμαλωτίζω (aichmalōtízō | aheekh-mal-o-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G164\nStrong's: to make captive\nKJV: —lead away captive, bring into captivity.\n\nSee: G164 \n\nCognate Group: G4869 (fellowprisoner), G161 (captivity), G162 (lead captive), G163 (lead away captive), G164 (captive)\nVariants: αἰχμαλωτίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1540 גָּלָה, H7650 שָׁבַע",
    164: "αἰχμάλωτος (aichmálōtos | aheekh-mal-o-tos')\n\nDerivation: from αἰχμή (a spear) and a derivative of the same as G259\nStrong's: properly, a prisoner of war, i.e. (genitive case) a captive\nKJV: —captive.\n\nSee: G259 \n\nCognate Group: G4869 (fellowprisoner), G161 (captivity), G162 (lead captive), G163 (lead away captive), G164 (captive)\nVariants: αἰχμάλωτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1473 גֹּולָה, H7617 שָׁבָה, H7758 שֹׁולָל",
    165: "αἰών (aiṓn | ahee-ohn')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G104\nStrong's: properly, an age; by extension, perpetuity (also past); by implication, the world; specially (Jewish) a Messianic period (present or future)\nKJV: —age, course, eternal, (for) ever(-more), (n-)ever, (beginning of the, while the) world (began, without end). Compare G5550 .\n\nSee: G104 \nSee: G5550 \n\nCognate Group: G166 (eternal), G165 (age)\nVariants: αἰών \nHebrew Equivalents: H314 אַחֲרֹון, H1973 הָלְאָה, H1988 הֲלֹם, H5331 נֶצַח, H5703 עַד, H5750 עֹוד, H5769 עֹולָם, H6924 קֶדֶם, H8548 תָּמִיד",
    166: "αἰώνιος (aiṓnios | ahee-o'-nee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G165\nStrong's: perpetual (also used of past time, or past and future as well)\nKJV: —eternal, for ever, everlasting, world (began).\n\nSee: G165 \n\nCognate Group: G166 (eternal), G165 (age)\nVariants: αἰώνιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5769 עֹולָם",
    167: "ἀκαθαρσία (akatharsía | ak-ath-ar-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G169\nStrong's: impurity (the quality), physically or morally\nKJV: —uncleanness.\n\nSee: G169 \n\nCognate Group: G2514 (purification), G169 (foul), G167 (uncleanness), G4027 (filth), G2508 (purge), G1571 (purge), G2512 (cleansing), G2511 (clean), G168 (filthiness), G2513 (clean)\nVariants: ἀκαθαρσία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2931 טָמֵא, H2932 טֻמְאָה, H5034 נָבֵל, H5079 נִדָּה, H6190 עׇרְלָה, H8441 תֹּועֵבַה",
    168: "ἀκαθάρτης (akathártēs | ak-ath-ar'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G169\nStrong's: impurity (the state), morally\nKJV: —filthiness.\n\nSee: G169 \n\nCognate Group: G2514 (purification), G169 (foul), G167 (uncleanness), G4027 (filth), G2508 (purge), G1571 (purge), G2512 (cleansing), G2511 (clean), G168 (filthiness), G2513 (clean)\nVariants: ἀκαθάρτης G175",
    169: "ἀκάθαρτος (akáthartos | ak-ath'-ar-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a presumed derivative of G2508 (meaning cleansed)\nStrong's: impure (ceremonially, morally (lewd) or specially, (demonic))\nKJV: —foul, unclean.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2508 \n\nCognate Group: G2514 (purification), G169 (foul), G167 (uncleanness), G4027 (filth), G2508 (purge), G1571 (purge), G2512 (cleansing), G2511 (clean), G168 (filthiness), G2513 (clean)\nVariants: ἀκάθαρτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H444 אָלַח, H2891 טָהֵר, H2931 טָמֵא, H8441 תֹּועֵבַה",
    170: "ἀκαιρέομαι (akairéomai | ak-ahee-reh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and G2540 (meaning unseasonable)\nStrong's: to be inopportune (for oneself), i.e. to fail of a proper occasion\nKJV: —lack opportunity.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2540 \n\nCognate Group: G2120 (opportunity), G2121 (convenient), G170 (lack opportunity), G4340 (dur- for awhile), G171 (out of season), G2540 (X always), G2122 (conveniently)\nVariants: ἀκαιρέομαι",
    171: "ἀκαίρως (akaírōs | ak-ah'-ee-roce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from the same as G170\nStrong's: inopportunely\nKJV: —out of season.\n\nSee: G170 \n\nCognate Group: G2120 (opportunity), G2121 (convenient), G170 (lack opportunity), G4340 (dur- for awhile), G171 (out of season), G2540 (X always), G2122 (conveniently)\nVariants: ἀκαίρως",
    172: "ἄκακος (ákakos | ak'-ak-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2556\nStrong's: not bad, i.e. (objectively) innocent or (subjectively) unsuspecting\nKJV: —harmless, simple.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2556 \n\nCognate Group: G172 (harmless), G420 (patient), G2549 (evil), G2554 (do evil), G2555 (evil-doer), G2556 (bad), G1573 (faint), G2557 (evil-doer), G2550 (malignity)\nVariants: ἄκακος \nHebrew Equivalents: H441 אַלּוּף, H6612 פְּתִי, H8535 תָּם",
    173: "ἄκανθα (ákantha | ak'-an-thah)\n\nDerivation: probably from the same as G188\nStrong's: a thorn\nKJV: —thorn.\n\nSee: G188 \n\nCognate Group: G172 (harmless), G420 (patient), G2549 (evil), G2554 (do evil), G2555 (evil-doer), G2556 (bad), G1573 (faint), G2557 (evil-doer), G2550 (malignity)\nVariants: ἄκανθα \nHebrew Equivalents: H891 בְּאֻשִׁים, H2312 חֵדֶק, H2336 חֹוחַ, H5518 סִיר, H6975 קֹוץ, H7898 שַׁיִת",
    174: "ἀκάνθινος (akánthinos | ak-an'-thee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G173\nStrong's: thorny\nKJV: —of thorns.\n\nSee: G173 \n\nCognate Group: G172 (harmless), G420 (patient), G2549 (evil), G2554 (do evil), G2555 (evil-doer), G2556 (bad), G1573 (faint), G2557 (evil-doer), G2550 (malignity)\nVariants: ἀκάνθινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5518 סִיר",
    175: "ἄκαρπος (ákarpos | ak'-ar-pos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2590\nStrong's: barren (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —without fruit, unfruitful.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2590 \n\nCognate Group: G2591 (Carpus), G2590 (fruit), G2592 (be fruitful), G2593 (fruitful), G175 (without fruit)\nVariants: ἄκαρπος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6757 צַלְמָוֶת",
    176: "ἀκατάγνωστος (akatágnōstos | ak-at-ag'-noce-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2607\nStrong's: unblamable\nKJV: —that cannot be condemned.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2607 \n\nCognate Group: G1110 (acquaintance), G1097 (allow), G1106 (advice), G4268 (foreknowledge), G1922 (acknowledging), G4774 (permission), G4267 (foreknow), G1107 (certify), G1921 (acknowledge), G1108 (knowledge), G176 (that cannot be condemned), G2607 (blame)\nVariants: ἀκατάγνωστος",
    177: "ἀκατακάλυπτος (akatakályptos | ak-at-ak-al'-oop-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of a compound of G2596 and G2572\nStrong's: unveiled\nKJV: —uncovered.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2596 \nSee: G2572 \n\nCognate Group: G1110 (acquaintance), G1097 (allow), G1106 (advice), G4268 (foreknowledge), G1922 (acknowledging), G4774 (permission), G4267 (foreknow), G1107 (certify), G1921 (acknowledge), G1108 (knowledge), G176 (that cannot be condemned), G2607 (blame)\nVariants: ἀκατακάλυπτος, ἀμαθής \nHebrew Equivalents: H6544 פָּרַע",
    178: "ἀκατάκριτος (akatákritos | ak-at-ak'-ree-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2632\nStrong's: without (legal) trial\nKJV: —uncondemned.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2632 \n\nCognate Group: G612 (answer), G2631 (condemnation), G470 (answer again), G351 (examination), G2923 (judge), G610 (sentence), G2924 (discerner), G1469 (make of the number), G350 (ask), G1253 (discern), G843 (condemned of self), G2632 (condemn), G2919 (avenge), G2917 (avenge), G1252 (contend), G87 (without partiality), G611 (answer), G178 (uncondemned), G2633 (condemn), G2920 (accusation), G2922 (to judge)\nVariants: ἀκατάκριτος",
    179: "ἀκατάλυτος (akatálytos | ak-at-al'-oo-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2647\nStrong's: indissoluble, i.e. (figuratively) permanent\nKJV: —endless.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2647 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: ἀκατάλυτος",
    180: "ἀκατάπαυστος (akatápaustos | ak-at-ap'-ow-stos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2664\nStrong's: unrefraining\nKJV: —that cannot cease.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2664 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: ἀκατάπαυστος, ἀκατάπαστος",
    181: "ἀκαταστασία (akatastasía | ak-at-as-tah-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G182\nStrong's: instability, i.e. disorder\nKJV: —commotion, confusion, tumult.\n\nSee: G182 \n\nCognate Group: G182 (unstable), G181 (commotion), G2525 (appoint)\nVariants: ἀκαταστασία, ἁμάρτημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4072 מִדְחֶה",
    182: "ἀκατάστατος (akatástatos | ak-at-as'-tat-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2525\nStrong's: inconstant\nKJV: —unstable.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2525 \n\nCognate Group: G182 (unstable), G181 (commotion), G2525 (appoint)\nVariants: ἀκατάστατος, ἁμαρτία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5191 נְטַל",
    183: "ἀκατάσχετος (akatáschetos | ak-at-as'-khet-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2722\nStrong's: unrestrainable\nKJV: —unruly.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2722 \n\nCognate Group: G182 (unstable), G181 (commotion), G2525 (appoint)\nVariants: ἀκατάσχετος G191",
    184: "Ἀκελδαμά (Akeldamá | ak-el-dam-ah')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (meaning field of blood\nStrong's: corresponding to H2506 and H1818); Akeldama, a place near Jerusalem\nKJV: —Aceldama.\nSee: H2506\nSee: H1818\n\nCognate Group: G182 (unstable), G181 (commotion), G2525 (appoint)\nVariants: Ἀκελδαμά, Ἁκελδαμάχ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1818 דָּם, H2506 חֵלֶק",
    185: "ἀκέραιος (akéraios | ak-er'-ah-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a presumed derivative of G2767\nStrong's: unmixed, i.e. (figuratively) innocent\nKJV: —harmless, simple.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2767 \n\nCognate Group: G185 (harmless)\nVariants: ἀκέραιος",
    186: "ἀκλινής (aklinḗs | ak-lee-nace')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2827\nStrong's: not leaning, i.e. (figuratively) firm\nKJV: —without wavering.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2827 \n\nCognate Group: G185 (harmless)\nVariants: ἀκλινής",
    187: "ἀκμάζω (akmázō | ak-mad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G188\nStrong's: to make a point, i.e. (figuratively) mature\nKJV: —be fully ripe.\n\nSee: G188 \n\nCognate Group: G185 (harmless)\nVariants: ἀκμάζω",
    188: "ἀκμήν (akmḗn | ak-mane')\n\nDerivation: accusative case of a noun (\"acme\") akin to ἀκή (a point) and meaning the same\nStrong's: adverbially, just now, i.e. still\nKJV: —yet.\n\nCognate Group: G185 (harmless)\nVariants: ἀκμήν",
    189: "ἀκοή (akoḗ | ak-o-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G191\nStrong's: hearing (the act, the sense or the thing heard)\nKJV: —audience, ear, fame, which ye heard, hearing, preached, report, rumor.\n\nSee: G191 \n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: ἀκοή \nHebrew Equivalents: H8085 שָׁמַע, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    190: "ἀκολουθέω (akolouthéō | ak-ol-oo-theh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a particle of union) and κέλευθος (a road)\nStrong's: properly, to be in the same way with, i.e. to accompany (specially, as a disciple)\nKJV: —follow, reach.\n\nSee: G1 \n\nCognate Group: G1811 (follow), G3877 (attain), G1872 (follow), G4870 (follow), G190 (follow)\nVariants: ἀκολουθέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H309 אָחַר, H1692 דָּבַק, H1980 הָלַךְ, H6437 פָּנָה",
    191: "ἀκούω (akoúō | ak-oo'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to hear (in various senses)\nKJV: —give (in the) audience (of), come (to the ears), (shall) hear(-er, -ken), be noised, be reported, understand.\n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: ἀκούω, ἀκουστός \nHebrew Equivalents: H238 אָזַן, H3045 יָדַע, H3176 יָחַל, H3318 יָצָא, H3513 כָּבַד, H3948 לֶקַח, H5753 עָוָה, H6030 עָנָה, H6605 פָּתַח, H7181 קָשַׁב, H7200 רָאָה, H8085 שָׁמַע, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    192: "ἀκρασία (akrasía | ak-ras-ee'-a)\n\nDerivation: from G193\nStrong's: want of self-restraint\nKJV: —excess, incontinency.\n\nSee: G193 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκρασία",
    193: "ἀκρατής (akratḗs | ak-rat'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2904\nStrong's: powerless, i.e. without self-control\nKJV: —incontinent.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2904 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκρατής",
    194: "ἄκρατος (ákratos | ak'-rat-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a presumed derivative of G2767\nStrong's: undiluted\nKJV: —without mixture.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2767 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἄκρατος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2534 חֵמָה, H2562 חֲמַר",
    195: "ἀκρίβεια (akríbeia | ak-ree'-bi-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G196\nStrong's: exactness\nKJV: —perfect manner.\n\nSee: G196 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκρίβεια \nHebrew Equivalents: H3330 יַצִּיב",
    196: "ἀκριβέστατος (akribéstatos | ak-ree-bes'-ta-tos)\n\nDerivation: superlative of ἀκρίβης (a derivative of the same as G206)\nStrong's: most exact\nKJV: —most straitest.\n\nSee: G206 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκριβέστατος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3330 יַצִּיב",
    197: "ἀκριβέστερον (akribésteron | ak-ree-bes'-ter-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of the comparative of the same as G196\nStrong's: (adverbially) more exactly\nKJV: —more perfect(-ly).\n\nSee: G196 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκριβέστερον, ἀκριβῶς G209 \nHebrew Equivalents: H3330 יַצִּיב",
    198: "ἀκριβόω (akribóō | ak-ree-bo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G196\nStrong's: to be exact, i.e. ascertain\nKJV: —enquire diligently.\n\nSee: G196 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκριβόω",
    199: "ἀκριβῶς (akribōs | ak-ree-boce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from the same as G196\nStrong's: exactly\nKJV: —circumspectly, diligently, perfect(-ly).\n\nSee: G196 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκριβῶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H3190 יָטַב, H3321 יְצַב",
    200: "ἀκρίς (akrís | ak-rece')\n\nDerivation: apparently from the same as G206\nStrong's: a locust (as pointed, or as lighting on the top of vegetation)\nKJV: —locust.\n\nSee: G206 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκρίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H697 אַרְבֶּה, H1460 גֵּו, H2284 חָגָב, H3218 יֶלֶק",
    201: "ἀκροατήριον (akroatḗrion | ak-ro-at-ay'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: from G202\nStrong's: an audience-room\nKJV: —place of hearing.\n\nSee: G202 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκροατήριον",
    202: "ἀκροατής (akroatḗs | ak-ro-at-ace')\n\nDerivation: from ἀκροάομαι (to listen\nStrong's: apparently an intensive of G191); a hearer (merely)\nKJV: —hearer.\n\nSee: G191 \n\nCognate Group: G1467 (can contain), G192 (excess), G1466 (temperance), G193 (incontinent), G1468 (temperate)\nVariants: ἀκροατής \nHebrew Equivalents: H3908 לַחַשׁ",
    203: "ἀκροβυστία (akrobystía | ak-rob-oos-tee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G206 and probably a modified form of πόσθη (the penis or male sexual organ)\nStrong's: the prepuce; by implication, an uncircumcised (i.e. gentile, figuratively, unregenerate) state or person\nKJV: —not circumcised, uncircumcised (with G2192), uncircumcision.\n\nSee: G206 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G203 (not circumcised)\nVariants: ἀκροβυστία \nHebrew Equivalents: H6190 עׇרְלָה",
    204: "ἀκρογωνιαῖος (akrogōniaîos | ak-rog-o-nee-ah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G206 and G1137\nStrong's: belonging to the extreme corner\nKJV: —chief corner.\n\nSee: G206 \nSee: G1137 \n\nCognate Group: G204 (chief corner), G1137 (corner)\nVariants: ἀκρογωνιαῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6438 פִּנָּה",
    205: "ἀκροθίνιον (akrothínion | ak-roth-in'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: from G206 and θίς (a heap)\nStrong's: properly (in the plural) the top of the heap, i.e. (by implication) best of the booty\nKJV: —spoils.\n\nSee: G206 \n\nCognate Group: G204 (chief corner), G1137 (corner)\nVariants: ἀκροθίνιον",
    206: "ἄκρον (ákron | ak'-ron)\n\nDerivation: neuter of an adjective probably akin to the base of G188\nStrong's: the extremity\nKJV: —one end… other, tip, top, uttermost participle\n\nSee: G188 \n\nCognate Group: G204 (chief corner), G1137 (corner)\nVariants: ἄκρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H657 אֶפֶס, H931 בֹּהֶן, H3411 יְרֵכָה, H3671 כָּנָף, H5490 סֹוף, H7079 קְנָת, H7218 רֹאשׁ",
    207: "Ἀκύλας (Akýlas | ak-oo'-las)\n\nDerivation: probably for Latin aquila (an eagle)\nStrong's: Akulas, an Israelite\nKJV: —Aquila.\n\nCognate Group: G204 (chief corner), G1137 (corner)\nVariants: Ἀκύλας",
    208: "ἀκυρόω (akyróō | ak-oo-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2964\nStrong's: to invalidate\nKJV: —disannul, make of none effect.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2964 \n\nCognate Group: G208 (disannul), G2964 (confirm)\nVariants: ἀκυρόω",
    209: "ἀκωλύτως (akōlýtōs | ak-o-loo'-toce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2967\nStrong's: in an unhindered manner, i.e. freely\nKJV: —no man forbidding him.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2967 \n\nCognate Group: G208 (disannul), G2964 (confirm)\nVariants: ἀκωλύτως",
    210: "ἄκων (ákōn | ak'-ohn)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G1635\nStrong's: unwilling\nKJV: —against the will.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G1635 \n\nCognate Group: G210 (against the will), G1595 (willingly)\nVariants: ἄκων",
    211: "ἀλάβαστρον (alábastron | al-ab'-as-tron)\n\nDerivation: neuter of ἀλάβαστρος (of uncertain derivation), the name of a stone\nStrong's: properly, an \"alabaster\" box, i.e. (by extension) a perfume vase (of any material)\nKJV: —(alabaster) box.\n\nCognate Group: G210 (against the will), G1595 (willingly)\nVariants: ἀλάβαστρον, ἀλάβαστρος",
    212: "ἀλαζονεία (alazoneía | al-ad-zon-i'-a)\n\nDerivation: from G213\nStrong's: braggadocio, i.e. (by implication) self-confidence\nKJV: —boasting, pride.\n\nSee: G213 \n\nCognate Group: G213 (boaster), G212 (boasting)\nVariants: ἀλαζονεία",
    213: "ἀλαζών (alazṓn | al-ad-zone')\n\nDerivation: from ἄλη (vagrancy)\nStrong's: braggart\nKJV: —boaster.\n\nCognate Group: G213 (boaster), G212 (boasting)\nVariants: ἀλαζών \nHebrew Equivalents: H3093 יָהִיר, H3887 לוּץ, H7830 שַׁחַץ",
    214: "ἀλαλάζω (alalázō | al-al-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from ἀλαλή ( a shout, \"halloo\")\nStrong's: to vociferate, i.e. (by implication) to wail; figuratively, to clang\nKJV: —tinkle, wail.\n\nCognate Group: G214 (tinkle)\nVariants: ἀλαλάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3213 יָלַל, H7321 רוּעַ, H8085 שָׁמַע",
    215: "ἀλάλητος (alálētos | al-al'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G2980\nStrong's: unspeakable\nKJV: —unutterable, which cannot be uttered.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2980 \n\nCognate Group: G214 (tinkle)\nVariants: ἀλάλητος",
    216: "ἄλαλος (álalos | al'-al-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2980\nStrong's: mute\nKJV: —dumb.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2980 \n\nCognate Group: G214 (tinkle)\nVariants: ἄλαλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H483 אִלֵּם, H5956 עָלַם",
    217: "ἅλας (hálas | hal'-as)\n\nDerivation: from G251\nStrong's: salt; figuratively, prudence\nKJV: —salt.\n\nSee: G251 \n\nCognate Group: G217 (salt), G237 (some other way)\nVariants: ἅλας, ἅλα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4414 מָלַח, H4416 מְלַח",
    218: "ἀλείφω (aleíphō | al-i'-fo)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as particle of union) and the base of G3045\nStrong's: to oil (with perfume)\nKJV: —anoint.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3045 \n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: ἀλείφω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4886 מָשַׁח, H5480 סוּךְ, H6902 קְבַל",
    219: "ἀλεκτοροφωνία (alektorophōnía | al-ek-tor-of-o-nee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G220 and G5456\nStrong's: cock-crow, i.e. the third night-watch\nKJV: —cockcrowing.\n\nSee: G220 \nSee: G5456 \n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: ἀλεκτοροφωνία",
    220: "ἀλέκτωρ (aléktōr | al-ek'-tore)\n\nDerivation: from ἀλέκω (to ward off)\nStrong's: a cock or male fowl\nKJV: —cock.\n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: ἀλέκτωρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H2223 זַרְזִיר",
    221: "Ἀλεξανδρεύς (Alexandreús | al-ex-and-reuce')\n\nDerivation: from Ἀλεξάνδρεια (the city so called)\nStrong's: an Alexandreian or inhabitant of Alexandria\nKJV: —of Alexandria, Alexandrian.\n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: Ἀλεξανδρεύς",
    222: "Ἀλεξανδρῖνος (Alexandrînos | al-ex-an-dree'-nos)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G221\nStrong's: Alexandrine, or belonging to Alexandria\nKJV: —of Alexandria.\n\nSee: G221 \n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: Ἀλεξανδρῖνος",
    223: "Ἀλέξανδρος (Aléxandros | al-ex'-an-dros)\n\nDerivation: from the same as (the first part of) G220 and G435\nStrong's: man-defender; Alexander, the name of three Israelites and one other man\nKJV: —Alexander.\n\nSee: G220 \nSee: G435 \n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: Ἀλέξανδρος",
    224: "ἄλευρον (áleuron | al'-yoo-ron)\n\nDerivation: from ἀλέω (to grind)\nStrong's: flour\nKJV: —meal.\n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: ἄλευρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H7058 קֶמַח",
    225: "ἀλήθεια (alḗtheia | al-ay'-thi-a)\n\nDerivation: from G227\nStrong's: truth\nKJV: —true, X truly, truth, verity.\n\nSee: G227 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀλήθεια \nHebrew Equivalents: H217 אוּר, H529 אֵמוּן, H530 אֱמוּנָה, H571 אֶמֶת, H3330 יַצִּיב, H3477 יָשָׁר, H3559 כּוּן, H5228 נָכֹחַ, H7187 קְשֹׁוט, H8537 תֹּם",
    226: "ἀληθεύω (alētheúō | al-ayth-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G227\nStrong's: to be true (in doctrine and profession)\nKJV: —speak (tell) the truth.\n\nSee: G227 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀληθεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H571 אֶמֶת, H3198 יָכַח, H4941 מִשְׁפָּט, H7999 שָׁלַם",
    227: "ἀληθής (alēthḗs | al-ay-thace')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G2990\nStrong's: true (as not concealing)\nKJV: —true, truly, truth.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G2990 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀληθής \nHebrew Equivalents: H571 אֶמֶת, H2450 חָכָם, H2896 טֹוב, H3559 כּוּן, H6662 צַדִּיק, H7189 קֹשֶׁט, H7227 רַב, H8454 תּוּשִׁיָּה",
    228: "ἀληθινός (alēthinós | al-ay-thee-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G227\nStrong's: truthful\nKJV: —true.\n\nSee: G227 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀληθινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H530 אֱמוּנָה, H571 אֶמֶת, H2896 טֹוב, H3330 יַצִּיב, H3477 יָשָׁר, H5355 נָקִי, H7187 קְשֹׁוט, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8365 שָׁתַם, H8535 תָּם",
    229: "ἀλήθω (alḗthō | al-ay'-tho)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G224\nStrong's: to grind\nKJV: —grind.\n\nSee: G224 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀλήθω \nHebrew Equivalents: H911 בְּדַד",
    230: "ἀληθῶς (alēthōs | al-ay-thoce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G227\nStrong's: truly\nKJV: —indeed, surely, of a surety, truly, of a (in) truth, verily, very.\n\nSee: G227 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀληθῶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H375 אֵיפֹה, H543 אָמֵן, H1571 גַּם, H3330 יַצִּיב, H6656 צְדָא",
    231: "ἁλιεύς (halieús | hal-ee-yoos')\n\nDerivation: from G251\nStrong's: a sailor (as engaged on the salt water), i.e. (by implication) a fisher\nKJV: —fisher(-man).\n\nSee: G251 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἁλιεύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H1709 דָּג, H1728 דַּוָּג",
    232: "ἁλιεύω (halieúō | hal-ee-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G231\nStrong's: to be a fisher, i.e. (by implication) to fish\nKJV: —go a-fishing.\n\nSee: G231 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἁλιεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1770 דִּיג",
    233: "ἁλίζω (halízō | hal-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G251\nStrong's: to salt\nKJV: —salt.\n\nSee: G251 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἁλίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4414 מָלַח",
    234: "ἀλίσγεμα (alísgema | al-is'-ghem-ah)\n\nDerivation: from ἀλισγέω (to soil)\nStrong's: (ceremonially) defilement\nKJV: —pollution.\n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀλίσγεμα, ἀλίσγημα",
    235: "ἀλλά (allá | al-lah')\n\nDerivation: neuter plural of G243\nStrong's: properly, other things, i.e. (adverbially) contrariwise (in many relations)\nKJV: —and, but (even), howbeit, indeed, nay, nevertheless, no, notwithstanding, save, therefore, yea, yet.\n\nSee: G243 \n\nCognate Group: G226 (speak the truth), G228 (true), G225 (true), G227 (true)\nVariants: ἀλλά",
    236: "ἀλλάσσω (allássō | al-las'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G243\nStrong's: to make different\nKJV: —change.\n\nSee: G243 \n\nCognate Group: G604 (reconcile), G1259 (reconcile), G2644 (reconcile), G3337 (change), G2643 (atonement), G525 (deliver), G465 (in exchange), G236 (change)\nVariants: ἀλλάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2015 הָפַךְ, H2498 חָלַף, H4171 מוּר, H4487 מָנָה, H6299 פָּדָה, H8133 שְׁנָא",
    237: "ἀλλαχόθεν (allachóthen | al-lakh-oth'-en)\n\nDerivation: from G243\nStrong's: from elsewhere\nKJV: —some other way.\n\nSee: G243 \n\nCognate Group: G217 (salt), G237 (some other way)\nVariants: ἀλλαχόθεν",
    238: "ἀλληγορέω (allēgoréō | al-lay-gor-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G243 and ἀγορέω (to harangue (compare G58))\nStrong's: to allegorize\nKJV: —be an allegory (the Greek word itself).\n\nSee: G243 \nSee: G58 \n\nCognate Group: G238 (be an allegory)\nVariants: ἀλληγορέω",
    239: "ἀλληλουϊα (allēlouïa | al-lay-loo'-ee-ah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (imperative of H1984 and H3050)\nStrong's: praise ye Jah!, an adoring exclamation\nKJV: —alleluiah.\nSee: H1984\nSee: H3050\n\nCognate Group: G239 (alleluiah)\nVariants: ἀλληλουϊα, ἁλληλουϊά, Ἁλληλούϊα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל, H3050 יָהּ",
    240: "ἀλλήλων (allḗlōn | al-lay'-lone)\n\nDerivation: Genitive plural from G243 reduplicated\nStrong's: one another\nKJV: —each other, mutual, one another, (the other), (them-, your-)selves, (selves) together (sometimes with G3326 or G4314).\n\nSee: G243 \nSee: G3326 \nSee: G4314 \n\nCognate Group: G239 (alleluiah)\nVariants: ἀλλήλων",
    241: "ἀλλογενής (allogenḗs | al-log-en-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G243 and G1085\nStrong's: foreign, i.e. not a Jew\nKJV: —stranger.\n\nSee: G243 \nSee: G1085 \n\nCognate Group: G245 (alien), G246 (one of another nation), G526 (alienate), G243 (more), G241 (stranger)\nVariants: ἀλλογενής \nHebrew Equivalents: H2114 זוּר, H4464 מַמְזֵר, H5236 נֵכָר",
    242: "ἅλλομαι (hállomai | hal'-lom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to jump; figuratively, to gush\nKJV: —leap, spring up.\n\nCognate Group: G245 (alien), G246 (one of another nation), G526 (alienate), G243 (more), G241 (stranger)\nVariants: ἅλλομαι, ἀνάλλομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1801 דָּלַג, H6743 צָלַח",
    243: "ἄλλος (állos | al'-los)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: \"else,\" i.e. different (in many applications)\nKJV: —more, one (another), (an-, some an-)other(-s, -wise).\n\nCognate Group: G245 (alien), G246 (one of another nation), G526 (alienate), G243 (more), G241 (stranger)\nVariants: ἄλλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H259 אֶחָד, H312 אַחֵר, H317 אׇחֳרִי, H428 אֵלֶּה, H2088 זֶה, H2114 זוּר, H2373 חָזֶה, H3605 כֹּל, H5750 עֹוד, H8478 תַּחַת",
    244: "ἀλλοτριεπίσκοπος (allotriepískopos | al-lot-ree-ep-is'-kop-os)\n\nDerivation: from G245 and G1985\nStrong's: overseeing others' affairs, i.e. a meddler (specially, in Gentile customs)\nKJV: —busybody in other men's matters.\n\nSee: G245 \nSee: G1985 \n\nCognate Group: G1983 (look diligently), G244 (busybody in other men's matters), G1984 (the office of a), G1980 (look out)\nVariants: ἀλλοτριεπίσκοπος, ἀλλοτριοεπίσκοπος",
    245: "ἀλλότριος (allótrios | al-lot'-ree-os)\n\nDerivation: from G243\nStrong's: another's, i.e. not one's own; by extension foreign, not akin, hostile\nKJV: —alien, (an-)other (man's, men's), strange(-r).\n\nSee: G243 \n\nCognate Group: G245 (alien), G246 (one of another nation), G526 (alienate), G243 (more), G241 (stranger)\nVariants: ἀλλότριος \nHebrew Equivalents: H312 אַחֵר, H2114 זוּר, H5236 נֵכָר",
    246: "ἀλλόφυλος (allóphylos | al-lof'-oo-los)\n\nDerivation: from G243 and G5443\nStrong's: foreign, i.e. (specially) Gentile\nKJV: —one of another nation.\n\nSee: G243 \nSee: G5443 \n\nCognate Group: G245 (alien), G246 (one of another nation), G526 (alienate), G243 (more), G241 (stranger)\nVariants: ἀλλόφυλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1121 בֵּן, H1121 בֵּן, H6191 עָרַם, H6429 פְּלֶשֶׁת",
    247: "ἄλλως (állōs | al'-loce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G243\nStrong's: differently\nKJV: —otherwise.\n\nSee: G243 \n\nCognate Group: G245 (alien), G246 (one of another nation), G526 (alienate), G243 (more), G241 (stranger)\nVariants: ἄλλως",
    248: "ἀλοάω (aloáō | al-o-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G257\nStrong's: to tread out grain\nKJV: —thresh, tread out the corn.\n\nSee: G257 \n\nCognate Group: G245 (alien), G246 (one of another nation), G526 (alienate), G243 (more), G241 (stranger)\nVariants: ἀλοάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1758 דּוּשׁ, H1869 דָּרַךְ, H2742 חֲרוּץ, H7567 רָשַׁשׁ",
    249: "ἄλογος (álogos | al'-og-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3056\nStrong's: irrational\nKJV: —brute, unreasonable.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: ἄλογος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5307 נָפַל",
    250: "ἀλόη (alóē | al-o-ay')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (compare G174)\nStrong's: aloes (the gum)\nKJV: —aloes.\n\nSee: G174 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: ἀλόη \nHebrew Equivalents: H174 אֲהָלִים",
    251: "ἅλς (háls | halce)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: \"salt\"\nKJV: —salt.\n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: ἅλς G265",
    252: "ἁλυκός (halykós | hal-oo-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G251\nStrong's: briny\nKJV: —salt.\n\nSee: G251 \n\nCognate Group: G252 (salt), G218 (anoint)\nVariants: ἁλυκός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4416 מְלַח, H7708 שִׂדִּים",
    253: "ἀλυπότερος (alypóteros | al-oo-pot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of a compound of G1 (as a negative particle) and G3077\nStrong's: more without grief\nKJV: —less sorrowful.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3077 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἀλυπότερος, ἄλυπος",
    254: "ἅλυσις (hálysis | hal'-oo-sis)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a fetter or manacle\nKJV: —bonds, chain.\n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἅλυσις",
    255: "ἀλυσιτελής (alysitelḗs | al-oo-sit-el-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and the base of G3081\nStrong's: gainless, i.e. (by implication) pernicious\nKJV: —unprofitable.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3081 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἀλυσιτελής",
    256: "Ἀλφαῖος (Alphaîos | al-fah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (compare H2501)\nStrong's: Alphæus, an Israelite\nKJV: —Alpheus.\nSee: H2501\n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: Ἀλφαῖος, Ἁλφαῖος, Ἀλφαίος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2501 חֶלֶף",
    257: "ἅλων (hálōn | hal'-ohn)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G1507\nStrong's: a threshing-floor (as rolled hard), i.e. (figuratively) the grain (and chaff, as just threshed)\nKJV: —floor.\n\nSee: G1507 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἅλων",
    258: "ἀλώπηξ (alṓpēx | al-o'-pakes)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a fox, i.e. (figuratively) a cunning person\nKJV: —fox.\n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἀλώπηξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7776 שׁוּעָל",
    259: "ἅλωσις (hálōsis | hal'-o-sis)\n\nDerivation: from a collateral form of G138\nStrong's: capture, be taken.\n\nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἅλωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H8610 תָּפַשׂ",
    260: "ἅμα (háma | ham'-ah)\n\nDerivation: a primary particle\nStrong's: properly, at the \"same\" time, but freely used as a preposition or adverb denoting close association\nKJV: —also, and, together, with(-al).\n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἅμα",
    261: "ἀμαθής (amathḗs | am-ath-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3129\nStrong's: ignorant\nKJV: —unlearned.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3129 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἀμαθής",
    262: "ἀμαράντινος (amarántinos | am-ar-an'-tee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G263\nStrong's: \"amaranthine\", i.e. (by implication) fadeless\nKJV: —that fadeth not away.\n\nSee: G263 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἀμαράντινος",
    263: "ἀμάραντος (amárantos | am-ar'-an-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a presumed derivative of G3133\nStrong's: unfading, i.e. (by implication) perpetual\nKJV: —that fadeth not away.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3133 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: ἀμάραντος",
    264: "ἁμαρτάνω (hamartánō | ham-ar-tan'-o)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G1 (as a negative particle) and the base of G3313\nStrong's: properly, to miss the mark (and so not share in the prize), i.e. (figuratively) to err, especially (morally) to sin\nKJV: —for your faults, offend, sin, trespass.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3313 \n\nCognate Group: G268 (sinful), G266 (offence), G361 (that is without sin), G265 (sin), G264 (for your faults)\nVariants: ἁμαρτάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H816 אָשַׁם, H2398 חָטָא, H4603 מָעַל, H5771 עָוֺן, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6586 פָּשַׁע, H7561 רָשַׁע, H7843 שָׁחַת",
    265: "ἁμάρτημα (hamártēma | ham-ar'-tay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G264\nStrong's: a sin (properly concrete)\nKJV: —sin.\n\nSee: G264 \n\nCognate Group: G268 (sinful), G266 (offence), G361 (that is without sin), G265 (sin), G264 (for your faults)\nVariants: ἁμάρτημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H2399 חֵטְא, H2403 חַטָּאָה, H5771 עָוֺן, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H7110 קֶצֶף, H7562 רֶשַׁע",
    266: "ἁμαρτία (hamartía | ham-ar-tee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G264\nStrong's: a sin (properly abstract)\nKJV: —offence, sin(-ful).\n\nSee: G264 \n\nCognate Group: G268 (sinful), G266 (offence), G361 (that is without sin), G265 (sin), G264 (for your faults)\nVariants: ἁμαρτία \nHebrew Equivalents: H817 אָשָׁם, H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H2248 חֲבוּלָה, H2398 חָטָא, H2403 חַטָּאָה, H2483 חֳלִי, H2932 טֻמְאָה, H4284 מַחֲשָׁבָה, H4878 מְשׁוּבָה, H5771 עָוֺן, H5931 עִלָּה, H5949 עֲלִילָה, H6586 פָּשַׁע, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H7200 רָאָה, H7561 רָשַׁע, H8441 תֹּועֵבַה",
    267: "ἀμάρτυρος (amártyros | am-ar'-too-ros)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a form of G3144\nStrong's: unattested\nKJV: —without witness.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3144 \n\nCognate Group: G268 (sinful), G266 (offence), G361 (that is without sin), G265 (sin), G264 (for your faults)\nVariants: ἀμάρτυρος, ἁμάρτυρος",
    268: "ἁμαρτωλός (hamartōlós | ham-ar-to-los')\n\nDerivation: from G264\nStrong's: sinful, i.e. a sinner\nKJV: —sinful, sinner.\n\nSee: G264 \n\nCognate Group: G268 (sinful), G266 (offence), G361 (that is without sin), G265 (sin), G264 (for your faults)\nVariants: ἁμαρτωλός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2398 חָטָא, H2611 חָנֵף, H2796 חָרָשׁ, H7451 רַע, H7563 רָשָׁע",
    269: "ἄμαχος (ámachos | am'-akh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3163\nStrong's: peaceable\nKJV: —not a brawler.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3163 \n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: ἄμαχος",
    270: "ἀμάω (amáō | am-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G260\nStrong's: properly, to collect, i.e. (by implication) reap\nKJV: —reap down.\n\nSee: G260 \n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: ἀμάω",
    271: "ἀμέθυστος (améthystos | am-eth'-oos-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G3184\nStrong's: the \"amethyst\" (supposed to prevent intoxication)\nKJV: —amethyst.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3184 \n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: ἀμέθυστος \nHebrew Equivalents: H306 אַחְלָמָה",
    272: "ἀμελέω (ameléō | am-el-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3199\nStrong's: to be careless of\nKJV: —make light of, neglect, be negligent, no regard.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3199 \n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: ἀμελέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1167 בַּעַל, H4784 מָרָה",
    273: "ἄμεμπτος (ámemptos | am'-emp-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G3201\nStrong's: irreproachable\nKJV: —blameless, faultless, unblamable.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3201 \n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: ἄμεμπτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1249 בַּר, H2135 זָכָה, H2643 חַף, H2891 טָהֵר, H3477 יָשָׁר, H5355 נָקִי, H6663 צָדַק, H8535 תָּם, H8549 תָּמִים",
    274: "ἀμέμπτως (amémptōs | am-emp'-toce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G273\nStrong's: faultlessly\nKJV: —blameless, unblamably.\n\nSee: G273 \n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: ἀμέμπτως",
    275: "ἀμέριμνος (amérimnos | am-er'-im-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3308\nStrong's: not anxious\nKJV: —without care(-fulness), secure.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3308 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: ἀμέριμνος",
    276: "ἀμετάθετος (ametáthetos | am-et-ath'-et-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G3346\nStrong's: unchangeable, or (neuter as abstract) unchangeability\nKJV: —immutable(-ility).\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3346 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: ἀμετάθετος",
    277: "ἀμετακίνητος (ametakínētos | am-et-ak-in'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G3334\nStrong's: immovable\nKJV: —unmovable.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3334 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: ἀμετακίνητος",
    278: "ἀμεταμέλητος (ametamélētos | am-et-am-el'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a presumed derivative of G3338\nStrong's: irrevocable\nKJV: —without repentance, not to be repented of.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3338 \n\nCognate Group: G3338 (repent), G278 (without repentance)\nVariants: ἀμεταμέλητος",
    279: "ἀμετανόητος (ametanóētos | am-et-an-o'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a presumed derivative of G3340\nStrong's: unrepentant\nKJV: —impenitent.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3340 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: ἀμετανόητος",
    280: "ἄμετρος (ámetros | am'-et-ros)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3358\nStrong's: immoderate\nKJV: —(thing) without measure.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3358 \n\nCognate Group: G280 (without measure), G3354 (figuratively), G3358 (measure), G3357 (a little)\nVariants: ἄμετρος",
    281: "ἀμήν (amḗn | am-ane')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H543)\nStrong's: properly, firm, i.e. (figuratively) trustworthy; adverbially, surely (often as interjection, so be it)\nKJV: —amen, verily.\nSee: H543\n\nCognate Group: G281 (amen)\nVariants: ἀμήν \nHebrew Equivalents: H543 אָמֵן, H6004 עָמַם",
    282: "ἀμήτωρ (amḗtōr | am-ay'-tore)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3384\nStrong's: motherless, i.e. of unknown maternity\nKJV: —without mother.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3384 \n\nCognate Group: G281 (amen)\nVariants: ἀμήτωρ",
    283: "ἀμίαντος (amíantos | am-ee'-an-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G3392\nStrong's: unsoiled, i.e. (figuratively) pure\nKJV: —undefiled.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3392 \n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: ἀμίαντος",
    284: "Ἀμιναδάβ (Aminadáb | am-ee-nad-ab')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5992)\nStrong's: Aminadab, an Israelite\nKJV: —Aminadab.\nSee: H5992\n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: Ἀμιναδάβ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5992 עַמִּינָדָב",
    285: "ἄμμος (ámmos | am'-mos)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G260\nStrong's: sand (as heaped on the beach)\nKJV: —sand.\n\nSee: G260 \n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: ἄμμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2344 חֹול, H6083 עָפָר",
    286: "ἀμνός (amnós | am-nos')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a lamb\nKJV: —lamb.\n\nCognate Group: G704 (lamb), G286 (lamb), G721 (lamb)\nVariants: ἀμνός \nHebrew Equivalents: H352 אַיִל, H3532 כֶּבֶשׂ, H6260 עַתּוּד, H6629 צֹאן, H7192 קְשִׂיטָה, H7353 רָחֵל, H7716 שֶׂה",
    287: "ἀμοιβή (amoibḗ | am-oy-bay')\n\nDerivation: from ἀμείβω (to exchange)\nStrong's: requital\nKJV: —requite.\n\nCognate Group: G704 (lamb), G286 (lamb), G721 (lamb)\nVariants: ἀμοιβή",
    288: "ἄμπελος (ámpelos | am'-pel-os)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G297 and that of G257\nStrong's: a vine (as coiling about a support)\nKJV: —vine.\n\nSee: G297 \nSee: G257 \n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἄμπελος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1588 גַּן, H1612 גֶּפֶן, H3754 כֶּרֶם, H8321 שֹׂרֵק",
    289: "ἀμπελουργός (ampelourgós | am-pel-oor-gos')\n\nDerivation: from G288 and G2041\nStrong's: a vine-worker, i.e. pruner\nKJV: —vine-dresser.\n\nSee: G288 \nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἀμπελουργός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3755 כֹּרֵם",
    290: "ἀμπελών (ampelṓn | am-pel-ohn')\n\nDerivation: from G288\nStrong's: a vineyard\nKJV: —vineyard.\n\nSee: G288 \n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἀμπελών \nHebrew Equivalents: H1612 גֶּפֶן, H3754 כֶּרֶם, H3759 כַּרְמֶל, H7704 שָׂדֶה",
    291: "Ἀμπλίας (Amplías | am-plee'-as)\n\nDerivation: contracted for Latin ampliatus (enlarged)\nStrong's: Amplias, a Roman Christian\nKJV: —Amplias.\n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: Ἀμπλίας, Ἀμπλιᾶτος",
    292: "ἀμύνομαι (amýnomai | am-oo'-nom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of a primary verb\nStrong's: to ward off (for oneself), i.e. protect\nKJV: —defend.\n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἀμύνομαι, ἀμύνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3467 יָשַׁע, H4135 מוּל, H5358 נָקַם",
    293: "ἀμφίβληστρον (amphíblēstron | am-fib'-lace-tron)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of the base of G297 and G906\nStrong's: a (fishing) net (as thrown about the fish)\nKJV: —net.\n\nSee: G297 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἀμφίβληστρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2764 חֵרֶם, H4364 מַכְמָר, H4685 מָצֹוד",
    294: "ἀμφιέννυμι (amphiénnymi | am-fee-en'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G297 and ἕννυμι (to invest)\nStrong's: to enrobe\nKJV: —clothe.\n\nSee: G297 \n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἀμφιέννυμι, ἀμφιέζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2644 חָפָא, H3682 כְּסוּת, H3847 לָבַשׁ",
    295: "Ἀμφίπολις (Amphípolis | am-fip'-ol-is)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G297 and G4172\nStrong's: a city surrounded by a river; Amphipolis, a place in Macedonia\nKJV: —Amphipolis.\n\nSee: G297 \nSee: G4172 \n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: Ἀμφίπολις",
    296: "ἄμφοδον (ámphodon | am'-fod-on)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G297 and G3598\nStrong's: a fork in the road\nKJV: —where two ways meet.\n\nSee: G297 \nSee: G3598 \n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἄμφοδον \nHebrew Equivalents: H563 אִמַּר, H759 אַרְמֹון",
    297: "ἀμφότερος (amphóteros | am-fot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of ἀμφί (around)\nStrong's: (in plural) both\nKJV: —both.\n\nCognate Group: G288 (vine)\nVariants: ἀμφότερος, ἀμφότεροι",
    298: "ἀμώμητος (amṓmētos | am-o'-may-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and a derivative of G3469\nStrong's: unblamable\nKJV: —blameless.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3469 \n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: ἀμώμητος",
    299: "ἄμωμος (ámōmos | am'-o-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G3470\nStrong's: unblemished (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —without blame (blemish, fault, spot), faultless, unblamable.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G3470 \n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: ἄμωμος",
    300: "Ἀμών (Amṓn | am-one')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H526)\nStrong's: Amon, an Israelite\nKJV: —Amon.\nSee: H526\n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: Ἀμών \nHebrew Equivalents: H526 אָמֹון, H527 אָמֹון",
    301: "Ἀμώς (Amṓs | am-oce')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H531)\nStrong's: Amos, an Israelite\nKJV: —Amos.\nSee: H531\n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: Ἀμώς \nHebrew Equivalents: H527 אָמֹון, H531 אָמֹוץ",
    302: "ἄν (án | an)\n\nDerivation: a primary particle, denoting a supposition, wish, possibility or uncertainty\nKJV: —(what-, where-, wither-, who-)soever. Usually unexpressed except by the subjunctive or potential mood. Also contracted for G1437 .\n\nSee: G1437 \n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: ἄν",
    303: "ἀνά (aná | an-ah')\n\nDerivation: a primary preposition and adverb\nStrong's: properly, up; but (by extension) used (distributively) severally, or (locally) at (etc.)\nKJV: —and, apiece, by, each, every (man), in, through. In compounds (as a prefix) it often means (by implication) repetition, intensity, reversal, etc.\n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: ἀνά \nHebrew Equivalents: H8496 תֹּךְ",
    304: "ἀναβαθμός (anabathmós | an-ab-ath-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G305 (compare G898)\nStrong's: a stairway\nKJV: —stairs.\n\nSee: G305 \nSee: G898 \n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: ἀναβαθμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4609 מַעֲלָה",
    305: "ἀναβαίνω (anabaínō | an-ab-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and the base of G939\nStrong's: to go up (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —arise, ascend (up), climb (go, grow, rise, spring) up, come (up).\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise)\nVariants: ἀναβαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1570 גָּלַשׁ, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2199 זָעַק, H2490 חָלַל, H3381 יָרַד, H5050 נָגַהּ, H5559 סְלִק, H5559 סְלִק, H5599 סָפִיחַ, H5641 סָתַר, H5674 עָבַר, H5927 עָלָה, H5975 עָמַד, H6509 פָּרָה, H6817 צָעַק, H6965 קוּם, H7159 קָרַם, H7392 רָכַב, H7725 שׁוּב, H8610 תָּפַשׂ",
    306: "ἀναβάλλομαι (anabállomai | an-ab-al'-lom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G303 and G906\nStrong's: to put off (for oneself)\nKJV: —defer.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise)\nVariants: ἀναβάλλομαι, ἀναβάλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5414 נָתַן, H5674 עָבַר, H5844 עָטָה",
    307: "ἀναβιβάζω (anabibázō | an-ab-ee-bad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and a derivative of the base of G939\nStrong's: to cause to go up, i.e. haul (a net)\nKJV: —draw.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise)\nVariants: ἀναβιβάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3381 יָרַד, H5674 עָבַר, H5927 עָלָה, H6942 קָדַשׁ, H7392 רָכַב",
    308: "ἀναβλέπω (anablépō | an-ab-lep'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G991\nStrong's: to look up; by implication, to recover sight\nKJV: —look (up), see, receive sight.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G991 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise)\nVariants: ἀναβλέπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H215 אֹור, H707 אָרַג, H5027 נָבַט, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6437 פָּנָה, H7200 רָאָה",
    309: "ἀνάβλεψις (anáblepsis | an-ab'-lep-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G308\nStrong's: restoration of sight\nKJV: —recovery of sight.\n\nSee: G308 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise)\nVariants: ἀνάβλεψις \nHebrew Equivalents: H6495 פְּקַח־קֹוחַ",
    310: "ἀναβοάω (anaboáō | an-ab-o-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G994\nStrong's: to halloo\nKJV: —cry (aloud, out).\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G994 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise)\nVariants: ἀναβοάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2199 זָעַק, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6817 צָעַק, H7121 קָרָא, H7321 רוּעַ",
    311: "ἀναβολή (anabolḗ | an-ab-ol-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G306\nStrong's: a putting off\nKJV: —delay.\n\nSee: G306 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise)\nVariants: ἀναβολή",
    312: "ἀναγγέλλω (anangéllō | an-ang-el'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and the base of G32\nStrong's: to announce (in detail)\nKJV: —declare, rehearse, report, show, speak, tell.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G32 \n\nCognate Group: G1229 (declare), G31 (message), G1804 (shew forth), G518 (bring word), G2604 (setter forth), G4293 (foretell), G312 (declare), G2605 (declare)\nVariants: ἀναγγέλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H262 אַחְוָה, H559 אָמַר, H995 בִּין, H1319 בָּשַׂר, H1696 דָבַר, H2331 חָוָה, H2372 חָזָה, H3045 יָדַע, H3289 יָעַץ, H3318 יָצָא, H3384 יָרָה, H4448 מָלַל, H5042 נָבַע, H5046 נָגַד, H5608 סָפַר, H7121 קָרָא, H7200 רָאָה, H7725 שׁוּב, H8085 שָׁמַע",
    313: "ἀναγεννάω (anagennáō | an-ag-en-nah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G1080\nStrong's: to beget or (by extension) bear (again)\nKJV: —beget, (bear) X (again).\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G1080 \n\nCognate Group: G1084 (they that are born), G1080 (bear), G313 (beget), G738 (new born), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: ἀναγεννάω",
    314: "ἀναγινώσκω (anaginṓskō | an-ag-in-oce'-ko)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G1097\nStrong's: to know again, i.e. (by extension) to read\nKJV: —read.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G1097 \n\nCognate Group: G320 (reading), G314 (read)\nVariants: ἀναγινώσκω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1540 גָּלָה, H7121 קָרָא",
    315: "ἀναγκάζω (anankázō | an-ang-kad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G318\nStrong's: to necessitate\nKJV: —compel, constrain.\n\nSee: G318 \n\nCognate Group: G315 (compel), G318 (distress)\nVariants: ἀναγκάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7860 שֹׁטֵר",
    316: "ἀναγκαῖος (anankaîos | an-ang-kah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G318\nStrong's: necessary; by implication, close (of kin)\nKJV: —near, necessary, necessity, needful.\n\nSee: G318 \n\nCognate Group: G315 (compel), G318 (distress)\nVariants: ἀναγκαῖος",
    317: "ἀναγκαστῶς (anankastōs | an-ang-kas-toce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a derivative of G315\nStrong's: compulsorily\nKJV: —by constraint.\n\nSee: G315 \n\nCognate Group: G315 (compel), G318 (distress)\nVariants: ἀναγκαστῶς",
    318: "ἀνάγκη (anánkē | an-ang-kay')\n\nDerivation: from G303 and the base of G43\nStrong's: constraint (literally or figuratively); by implication, distress\nKJV: —distress, must needs, (of) necessity(-sary), needeth, needful.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G43 \n\nCognate Group: G315 (compel), G318 (distress)\nVariants: ἀνάγκη \nHebrew Equivalents: H34 אֶבְיֹון, H2113 זְוָעָה, H3027 יָד, H3939 לַעֲנָה, H4689 מָצֹוק, H6805 צָעַד, H6862 צַר",
    319: "ἀναγνωρίζομαι (anagnōrízomai | an-ag-no-rid'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G303 and G1107\nStrong's: to make (oneself) known\nKJV: —be made known.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G1107 \n\nCognate Group: G315 (compel), G318 (distress)\nVariants: ἀναγνωρίζομαι, ἀναγνωρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3045 יָדַע",
    320: "ἀνάγνωσις (anágnōsis | an-ag'-no-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G314\nStrong's: (the act of) reading\nKJV: —reading.\n\nSee: G314 \n\nCognate Group: G320 (reading), G314 (read)\nVariants: ἀνάγνωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4744 מִקְרָא",
    321: "ἀνάγω (anágō | an-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G71\nStrong's: to lead up; by extension to bring out; specially, to sail away\nKJV: —bring (again, forth, up again), depart, launch (forth), lead (up), loose, offer, sail, set forth, take up.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G320 (reading), G314 (read)\nVariants: ἀνάγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1641 גָּרַר, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2986 יָבַל, H3318 יָצָא, H5090 נָהַג, H5927 עָלָה, H7725 שׁוּב",
    322: "ἀναδείκνυμι (anadeíknymi | an-ad-ike'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G1166\nStrong's: to exhibit, i.e. (by implication) to indicate, appoint\nKJV: —appoint, shew.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G1166 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: ἀναδείκνυμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3045 יָדַע, H4487 מָנָה, H4672 מָצָא",
    323: "ἀνάδειξις (anádeixis | an-ad'-ike-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G322\nStrong's: (the act of) exhibition\nKJV: —shewing.\n\nSee: G322 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: ἀνάδειξις",
    324: "ἀναδέχομαι (anadéchomai | an-ad-ekh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G1209\nStrong's: to entertain (as a guest)\nKJV: —receive.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G1209 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: ἀναδέχομαι",
    325: "ἀναδίδωμι (anadídōmi | an-ad-eed'-om-ee)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G1325\nStrong's: to hand over\nKJV: —deliver.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G1325 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: ἀναδίδωμι",
    326: "ἀναζάω (anazáō | an-ad-zah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G2198\nStrong's: to recover life (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —(be a-)live again, revive.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G2198 \n\nCognate Group: G2225 (live), G1186 (tree), G2226 (beast), G2222 (life), G326 (live again), G2227 (make alive), G2198 (life)\nVariants: ἀναζάω",
    327: "ἀναζητέω (anazētéō | an-ad-zay-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G2212\nStrong's: to search out\nKJV: —seek.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G2212 \n\nCognate Group: G2225 (live), G1186 (tree), G2226 (beast), G2222 (life), G326 (live again), G2227 (make alive), G2198 (life)\nVariants: ἀναζητέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1245 בָּקַשׁ, H1875 דָּרַשׁ",
    328: "ἀναζώννυμι (anazṓnnymi | an-ad-zone'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G2224\nStrong's: to gird afresh\nKJV: —gird up.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G2224 \n\nCognate Group: G2225 (live), G1186 (tree), G2226 (beast), G2222 (life), G326 (live again), G2227 (make alive), G2198 (life)\nVariants: ἀναζώννυμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2296 חָגַר",
    329: "ἀναζωπυρέω (anazōpyréō | an-ad-zo-poor-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and a compound of the base of G2226 and G4442\nStrong's: to re-enkindle\nKJV: —stir up.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G2226 \nSee: G4442 \n\nCognate Group: G2225 (live), G1186 (tree), G2226 (beast), G2222 (life), G326 (live again), G2227 (make alive), G2198 (life)\nVariants: ἀναζωπυρέω",
    330: "ἀναθάλλω (anathállō | an-ath-al'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and θάλλω (to flourish)\nStrong's: to revive\nKJV: —flourish again.\n\nSee: G303 \n\nCognate Group: G2225 (live), G1186 (tree), G2226 (beast), G2222 (life), G326 (live again), G2227 (make alive), G2198 (life)\nVariants: ἀναθάλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5937 עָלַז, H6524 פָּרַח",
    331: "ἀνάθεμα (anáthema | an-ath'-em-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G394\nStrong's: a (religious) ban or (concretely) excommunicated (thing or person)\nKJV: —accused, anathema, curse, X great.\n\nSee: G394 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀνάθεμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2764 חֵרֶם",
    332: "ἀναθεματίζω (anathematízō | an-ath-em-at-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G331\nStrong's: to declare or vow under penalty of execration\nKJV: —(bind under a) curse, bind with an oath.\n\nSee: G331 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀναθεματίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2763 חָרַם",
    333: "ἀναθεωρέω (anatheōréō | an-ath-eh-o-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and G2334\nStrong's: to look again (i.e. attentively) at (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —behold, consider.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G2334 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀναθεωρέω, διϊστορέω",
    334: "ἀνάθημα (anáthēma | an-ath'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G394 (like G331, but in a good sense)\nStrong's: a votive offering\nKJV: —gift.\n\nSee: G394 \nSee: G331 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀνάθημα",
    335: "ἀναίδεια (anaídeia | an-ah'-ee-die-ah')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G1 (as a negative particle (compare G427)) and G127\nStrong's: impudence, i.e. (by implication) importunity\nKJV: —importunity.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G427 \nSee: G127 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀναίδεια",
    336: "ἀναίρεσις (anaíresis | an-ah'-ee-res-is)\n\nDerivation: from G337\nStrong's: (the act of) killing\nKJV: —death.\n\nSee: G337 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀναίρεσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H2026 הָרַג, H7413 רָמָה",
    337: "ἀναιρέω (anairéō | an-ahee-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G303 and (the active of) G138\nStrong's: to take up, i.e. adopt; by implication, to take away (violently), i.e. abolish, murder\nKJV: —put to death, kill, slay, take away, take up.\n\nSee: G303 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀναιρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1214 בָּצַע, H2026 הָרַג, H2763 חָרַם, H3947 לָקַח, H4191 מוּת, H4871 מָשָׁה, H5221 נָכָה, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6293 פָּגַע, H6992 קְטַל, H7311 רוּם, H7324 רוּק",
    338: "ἀναίτιος (anaítios | an-ah'-ee-tee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G1 (as a negative particle) and G159 (in the sense of G156)\nStrong's: innocent\nKJV: —blameless, guiltless.\n\nSee: G1 \nSee: G159 \nSee: G156 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: ἀναίτιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5355 נָקִי",
