    2937: "κτίσις (ktísis | ktis'-is)\n\nDerivation: from G2936\nStrong's: original formation (properly, the act; by implication, the thing, literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —building, creation, creature, ordinance.\n\nSee: G2936 \n\nCognate Group: G2938 (creature), G2937 (building), G2939 (Creator), G2936 (create)\nVariants: κτίσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1952 הֹון, H7075 קִנְיָן",
    2938: "κτίσμα (ktísma | ktis'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G2936\nStrong's: an original formation (concretely), i.e. product (created thing)\nKJV: —creature.\n\nSee: G2936 \n\nCognate Group: G2938 (creature), G2937 (building), G2939 (Creator), G2936 (create)\nVariants: κτίσμα",
    2939: "κτίστης (ktístēs | ktis-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G2936\nStrong's: a founder, i.e. God (as author of all things)\nKJV: —Creator.\n\nSee: G2936 \n\nCognate Group: G2938 (creature), G2937 (building), G2939 (Creator), G2936 (create)\nVariants: κτίστης",
    2940: "κυβεία (kybeía | koo-bi'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from κύβος (a \"cube\", i.e. die for playing)\nStrong's: gambling, i.e. (figuratively) artifice or fraud\nKJV: —sleight.\n\nCognate Group: G2938 (creature), G2937 (building), G2939 (Creator), G2936 (create)\nVariants: κυβεία",
    2941: "κυβέρνησις (kybérnēsis | koo-ber'-nay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from κυβερνάω (of Latin origin, to steer)\nStrong's: pilotage, i.e. (figuratively) directorship (in the church)\nKJV: —government.\n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κυβέρνησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H8458 תַּחְבֻּלָה",
    2942: "κυβερνήτης (kybernḗtēs | koo-ber-nay'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G2941\nStrong's: helmsman, i.e. (by implication) captain\nKJV: —(ship) master.\n\nSee: G2941 \n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κυβερνήτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H2259 חֹבֵל, H7901 שָׁכַב",
    2943: "κυκλόθεν (kyklóthen | koo-kloth'-en)\n\nDerivation: adverb from the same as G2945\nStrong's: from the circle, i.e. all around\nKJV: —(round) about.\n\nSee: G2945 \n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κυκλόθεν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3605 כֹּל, H5439 סָבִיב, H5439 סָבִיב",
    2944: "κυκλόω (kyklóō | koo-klo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G2945\nStrong's: to encircle, i.e. surround\nKJV: —compass (about), come (stand) round about.\n\nSee: G2945 \n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κυκλόω, κυκλεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2583 חָנָה, H5362 נָקַף, H5437 סָבַב, H5437 סָבַב, H5507 סֹחֵרָה, H6584 פָּשַׁט, H8210 שָׁפַךְ",
    2945: "κύκλῳ (kýklōi | koo'-klo)\n\nDerivation: as if dative case of κύκλος (a ring, \"cycle\"\nStrong's: akin to G2947); i.e. in a circle (by implication, of G1722), i.e. (adverbially) all around\nKJV: —round about.\n\nSee: G2947 \nSee: G1722 \n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κύκλῳ \nHebrew Equivalents: H734 אֹרַח, H4605 מַעַל, H5362 נָקַף, H5439 סָבִיב, H5921 עַל, H8478 תַּחַת",
    2946: "κύλισμα (kýlisma | koo'-lis-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G2947\nStrong's: a wallow (the effect of rolling), i.e. filth\nKJV: —wallowing.\n\nSee: G2947 \n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κύλισμα, κυλισμός",
    2947: "κυλιόω (kylióō | koo-lee-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2949 (through the idea of circularity\nStrong's: compare G2945, G1507); to roll about\nKJV: —wallow.\n\nSee: G2949 \nSee: G2945 \nSee: G1507 \n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κυλιόω, κυλίω, κυλίομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1556 גָּלַל, H2015 הָפַךְ, H5264 נָסַס, H5781 עוּק, H7725 שׁוּב, H7760 שׂוּם, H8058 שָׁמַט",
    2948: "κυλλός (kyllós | kool-los')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G2947\nStrong's: rocking about, i.e. crippled (maimed, in feet or hands)\nKJV: —maimed.\n\nSee: G2947 \n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κυλλός",
    2949: "κῦμα (kŷma | koo'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from κύω (to swell (with young), i.e. bend, curve)\nStrong's: a billow (as bursting or toppling)\nKJV: —wave.\n\nCognate Group: G2941 (government)\nVariants: κῦμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H663 אֲפֵק, H841 אֲשַׂרְאֵלָה, H1530 גַּל, H4310 מִי",
    2950: "κύμβαλον (kýmbalon | koom'-bal-on)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the base of G2949\nStrong's: a \"cymbal\" (as hollow)\nKJV: —cymbal.\n\nSee: G2949 \n\nCognate Group: G2950 (cymbal)\nVariants: κύμβαλον \nHebrew Equivalents: H4517 מְנַעְנַע, H4698 מְצִלָּה, H6767 צְלָצַל, H7991 שָׁלִישׁ",
    2951: "κύμινον (kýminon | koo'-min-on)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (compare H3646)\nStrong's: dill or fennel (\"cummin\")\nKJV: —cummin.\nSee: H3646\n\nCognate Group: G2950 (cymbal)\nVariants: κύμινον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3646 כַּמֹּן",
    2952: "κυνάριον (kynárion | koo-nar'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G2965\nStrong's: a puppy\nKJV: —dog.\n\nSee: G2965 \n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: κυνάριον",
    2953: "Κύπριος (Kýprios | koo'-pree-os)\n\nDerivation: from G2954\nStrong's: a Cyprian (Cypriot), i.e. inhabitant of Cyprus\nKJV: —of Cyprus.\n\nSee: G2954 \n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: Κύπριος",
    2954: "Κύπρος (Kýpros | koo'-pros)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Cyprus, an island in the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Cyprus.\n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: Κύπρος",
    2955: "κύπτω (kýptō | koop'-to)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G2949\nStrong's: to bend forward\nKJV: —stoop (down).\n\nSee: G2949 \n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: κύπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1457 גָּהַר, H5456 סָגַד, H6915 קָדַד, H7812 שָׁחָה, H7817 שָׁחַח",
    2956: "Κυρηναῖος (Kyrēnaîos | koo-ray-nah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G2957\nStrong's: i.e. Cyrenæan, i.e. inhabitant of Cyrene\nKJV: —of Cyrene, Cyrenian.\n\nSee: G2957 \n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: Κυρηναῖος, Κυρεῖνος",
    2957: "Κυρήνη (Kyrḗnē | koo-ray'-nay)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Cyrene, a region of Africa\nKJV: —Cyrene.\n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: Κυρήνη",
    2958: "Κυρήνιος (Kyrḗnios | koo-ray'-nee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: Cyrenius (i.e. Quirinus), a Roman\nKJV: —Cyrenius.\n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: Κυρήνιος, Κυρίνιος, Κυρήνιος/Κυρίνιος",
    2959: "Κυρία (Kyría | koo-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G2962\nStrong's: Cyria, a Christian woman\nKJV: —lady.\n\nSee: G2962 \n\nCognate Group: G2959 (lady), G2960 (Lord's), G2962 (God), G2634 (exercise dominion over), G2963 (dominion), G2961 (have dominion over)\nVariants: Κυρία, κυρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1172 בַּעֲלָה, H1404 גְּבֶרֶת",
    2960: "κυριακός (kyriakós | koo-ree-ak-os')\n\nDerivation: from G2962\nStrong's: belonging to the Lord (Jehovah or Jesus)\nKJV: —Lord's.\n\nSee: G2962 \n\nCognate Group: G2959 (lady), G2960 (Lord's), G2962 (God), G2634 (exercise dominion over), G2963 (dominion), G2961 (have dominion over)\nVariants: κυριακός",
    2961: "κυριεύω (kyrieúō | ko-ree-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G2962\nStrong's: to rule\nKJV: —have dominion over, lord, be lord of, exercise lordship over.\n\nSee: G2962 \n\nCognate Group: G2959 (lady), G2960 (Lord's), G2962 (God), G2634 (exercise dominion over), G2963 (dominion), G2961 (have dominion over)\nVariants: κυριεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2233 זֶרַע, H3423 יָרַשׁ, H3920 לָכַד, H4911 מָשַׁל, H4938 מִשְׁעֵנָה, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H7287 רָדָה, H7300 רוּד, H7981 שְׁלֵט",
    2962: "κύριος (kýrios | koo'-ree-os)\n\nDerivation: from κῦρος (supremacy)\nStrong's: supreme in authority, i.e. (as noun) controller; by implication, Master (as a respectful title)\nKJV: — God, Lord, master, Sir.\n\nCognate Group: G2959 (lady), G2960 (Lord's), G2962 (God), G2634 (exercise dominion over), G2963 (dominion), G2961 (have dominion over)\nVariants: κύριος \nHebrew Equivalents: H113 אָדֹון, H136 אֲדֹנָי, H136 אֲדֹנָי, H410 אֵל, H430 אֱלֹהִים, H433 אֱלֹוהַּ, H1167 בַּעַל, H1376 גְּבִיר, H3068 יְהֹוָה, H4756 מָרֵא, H4911 מָשַׁל, H6697 צוּר, H7706 שַׁדַּי, H7980 שָׁלַט, H7989 שַׁלִּיט",
    2963: "κυριότης (kyriótēs | koo-ree-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G2962\nStrong's: mastery, i.e. (concretely and collectively) rulers\nKJV: —dominion, government.\n\nSee: G2962 \n\nCognate Group: G2959 (lady), G2960 (Lord's), G2962 (God), G2634 (exercise dominion over), G2963 (dominion), G2961 (have dominion over)\nVariants: κυριότης",
    2964: "κυρόω (kyróō | koo-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G2962\nStrong's: to make authoritative, i.e. ratify\nKJV: —confirm.\n\nSee: G2962 \n\nCognate Group: G208 (disannul), G2964 (confirm)\nVariants: κυρόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6965 קוּם",
    2965: "κύων (kýōn | koo'-ohn)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: a dog (\"hound\") (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —dog.\n\nCognate Group: G2965 (dog), G2952 (dog)\nVariants: κύων \nHebrew Equivalents: H3611 כֶּלֶב, H5914 עֶכֶס",
    2966: "κῶλον (kōlon | ko'-lon)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2849\nStrong's: a limb of the body (as if lopped)\nKJV: —carcase.\n\nSee: G2849 \n\nCognate Group: G2966 (carcase)\nVariants: κῶλον \nHebrew Equivalents: H6297 פֶּגֶר",
    2967: "κωλύω (kōlýō | ko-loo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2849\nStrong's: to estop, i.e. prevent (by word or act)\nKJV: —forbid, hinder, keep from, let, not suffer, withstand.\n\nSee: G2849 \n\nCognate Group: G2967 (forbid)\nVariants: κωλύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3607 כָּלָא, H4185 מוּשׁ, H4513 מָנַע, H6113 עָצָר, H7725 שׁוּב",
    2968: "κώμη (kṓmē | ko'-may)\n\nDerivation: from G2749\nStrong's: a hamlet (as if laid down)\nKJV: —town, village.\n\nSee: G2749 \n\nCognate Group: G2969 (town), G2968 (town)\nVariants: κώμη \nHebrew Equivalents: H1323 בַּת, H2247 חָבָה, H2691 חָצֵר, H2918 טִירָה, H3723 כָּפָר, H5892 עִיר, H7149 קִרְיָא",
    2969: "κωμόπολις (kōmópolis | ko-mop'-ol-is)\n\nDerivation: from G2968 and G4172\nStrong's: an unwalled city\nKJV: —town.\n\nSee: G2968 \nSee: G4172 \n\nCognate Group: G2969 (town), G2968 (town)\nVariants: κωμόπολις",
    2970: "κῶμος (kōmos | ko'-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G2749\nStrong's: a carousal (as if letting loose)\nKJV: —revelling, rioting.\n\nSee: G2749 \n\nCognate Group: G2970 (revelling)\nVariants: κῶμος",
    2971: "κώνωψ (kṓnōps | ko'-nopes)\n\nDerivation: apparently a derivative of the base of G2759 and a derivative of G3700\nStrong's: a mosquito (from its stinging proboscis)\nKJV: —gnat.\n\nSee: G2759 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G2971 (gnat)\nVariants: κώνωψ",
    2972: "Κώς (Kṓs | koce)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Cos, an island in the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Cos.\n\nCognate Group: G2972 (Cos)\nVariants: Κώς, Κῶς",
    2973: "Κωσάμ (Kōsám | ko-sam')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (compare H7081)\nStrong's: Cosam (i.e. Kosam) an Israelite\nKJV: —Cosam.\nSee: H7081\n\nCognate Group: G2973 (Cosam)\nVariants: Κωσάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7081 קֶסֶם",
    2974: "κωφός (kōphós | ko-fos')\n\nDerivation: from G2875\nStrong's: blunted, i.e. (figuratively) of hearing (deaf) or speech (dumb)\nKJV: —deaf, dumb, speechless.\n\nSee: G2875 \n\nCognate Group: G2974 (deaf)\nVariants: κωφός \nHebrew Equivalents: H483 אִלֵּם, H2796 חָרָשׁ",
    2975: "λαγχάνω (lanchánō | lang-khan'-o)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary verb, which is only used as an alternate in certain tenses\nStrong's: to lot, i.e. determine (by implication, receive) especially by lot\nKJV: —his lot be, cast lots, obtain.\n\nCognate Group: G2975 (his lot be)\nVariants: λαγχάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3920 לָכַד",
    2976: "Λάζαρος (Lázaros | lad'-zar-os)\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin (H499)\nStrong's: Lazarus (i.e. Elazar), the name of two Israelites (one imaginary)\nKJV: —Lazarus.\nSee: H499\n\nCognate Group: G2975 (his lot be)\nVariants: Λάζαρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H499 אֶלְעָזָר",
    2977: "λάθρα (láthra | lath'-rah)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G2990\nStrong's: privately\nKJV: —privily, secretly.\n\nSee: G2990 \n\nCognate Group: G2975 (his lot be)\nVariants: λάθρα, λάθρᾳ, σιωπῇ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4565 מִסְתָּר, H5643 סֵתֶר",
    2978: "λαῖλαψ (laîlaps | lah'-ee-laps)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a whirlwind (squall)\nKJV: —storm, tempest.\n\nCognate Group: G2975 (his lot be)\nVariants: λαῖλαψ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5191 נְטַל, H5492 סוּפָה, H5591 סַעַר",
    2979: "λακτίζω (laktízō | lak-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from adverb λάξ (heelwise)\nStrong's: to recalcitrate\nKJV: —kick.\n\nCognate Group: G2979 (kick)\nVariants: λακτίζω",
    2980: "λαλέω (laléō | lal-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of an otherwise obsolete verb\nStrong's: to talk, i.e. utter words\nKJV: —preach, say, speak (after), talk, tell, utter. Compare G3004 .\n\nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G2636 (backbiting), G2637 (backbiter), G2980 (preach), G2635 (speak against)\nVariants: λαλέω, ἀπολαλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H1696 דָבַר, H2803 חָשַׁב, H4448 מָלַל, H5012 נָבָא, H5046 נָגַד, H5608 סָפַר, H6293 פָּגַע, H6680 צָוָה, H7121 קָרָא, H7378 רִיב",
    2981: "λαλιά (laliá | lal-ee-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G2980\nStrong's: talk\nKJV: —saying, speech.\n\nSee: G2980 \n\nCognate Group: G2636 (backbiting), G2637 (backbiter), G2980 (preach), G2635 (speak against)\nVariants: λαλιά \nHebrew Equivalents: H562 אֹמֶר, H1697 דָּבָר, H1700 דִּבְרָה, H4926 מִשְׁמָע",
    2982: "λαμά (lamá | lam-ah') or λαμμᾶ (lammâ | lam-mah')\n\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4100 with prepositional prefix)\nStrong's: lama (i.e. why)\nKJV: —lama.\nSee: H4100\n\nCognate Group: G2636 (backbiting), G2637 (backbiter), G2980 (preach), G2635 (speak against)\nVariants: λαμά, λεμά, λιμά \nHebrew Equivalents: H4100 מָה",
    2983: "λαμβάνω (lambánō | lam-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary verb, which is use only as an alternate in certain tenses; to take (in very many applications, literally and figuratively (properly objective or active, to get hold of; whereas G1209 is rather subjective or passive, to have offered to one\nStrong's: while G138 is more violent, to seize or remove))\nKJV: —accept, + be amazed, assay, attain, bring, X when I call, catch, come on (X unto), + forget, have, hold, obtain, receive (X after), take (away, up).\n\nSee: G1209 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: λαμβάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H270 אָחַז, H781 אָרַשׂ, H935 בֹּוא, H954 בּוּשׁ, H1887 הֵא, H2029 הָרָה, H2151 זָלַל, H2342 חוּל, H2388 חָזַק, H2505 חָלַק, H2730 חָרֵד, H3318 יָצָא, H3423 יָרַשׁ, H3427 יָשַׁב, H3920 לָכַד, H3947 לָקַח, H4351 מְכוּרָה, H4390 מָלֵא, H4672 מָצָא, H5221 נָכָה, H5254 נָסָה, H5307 נָפַל, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5381 נָשַׂג, H5493 סוּר, H5710 עָדָה, H5927 עָלָה, H6329 פּוּק, H6696 צוּר, H6902 קְבַל, H6965 קוּם, H7121 קָרָא, H7126 קָרַב, H7737 שָׁוָה, H7760 שׂוּם, H8610 תָּפַשׂ",
    2984: "Λάμεχ (Lámech | lam'-ekh)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H3929)\nStrong's: Lamech (i.e. Lemek), a patriarch\nKJV: —Lamech.\nSee: H3929\n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: Λάμεχ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3929 לֶמֶךְ",
    2985: "λαμπάς (lampás | lam-pas')\n\nDerivation: from G2989\nStrong's: a \"lamp\" or flambeau\nKJV: —lamp, light, torch.\n\nSee: G2989 \n\nCognate Group: G2985 (lamp), G2986 (bright), G2989 (give light)\nVariants: λαμπάς, ὑπολαμπάς \nHebrew Equivalents: H3940 לַפִּיד, H5043 נֶבְרְשָׁא",
    2986: "λαμπρός (lamprós | lam-pros')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G2985\nStrong's: radiant; by analogy, limpid; figuratively, magnificent or sumptuous (in appearance)\nKJV: —bright, clear, gay, goodly, gorgeous, white.\n\nSee: G2985 \n\nCognate Group: G2985 (lamp), G2986 (bright), G2989 (give light)\nVariants: λαμπρός",
    2987: "λαμπρότης (lamprótēs | lam-prot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G2986\nStrong's: brilliancy\nKJV: —brightness.\n\nSee: G2986 \n\nCognate Group: G2985 (lamp), G2986 (bright), G2989 (give light)\nVariants: λαμπρότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H1926 הָדָר, H2096 זֹהַר, H5051 נֹגַהּ, H5278 נֹעַם",
    2988: "λαμπρῶς (lamprōs | lam-proce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G2986\nStrong's: brilliantly, i.e. figuratively, luxuriously\nKJV: —sumptuously.\n\nSee: G2986 \n\nCognate Group: G2985 (lamp), G2986 (bright), G2989 (give light)\nVariants: λαμπρῶς",
    2989: "λάμπω (lámpō | lam'-po)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to beam, i.e. radiate brilliancy (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —give light, shine.\n\nCognate Group: G2985 (lamp), G2986 (bright), G2989 (give light)\nVariants: λάμπω, ἐπιλάμπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2095 זְהַר, H5050 נָגַהּ, H6705 צָחַח",
    2990: "λανθάνω (lanthánō | lan-than'-o)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary verb, which is used only as an alternate in certain tenses\nStrong's: to lie hid (literally or figuratively); often used adverbially, unwittingly\nKJV: —be hid, be ignorant of, unawares.\n\nCognate Group: G2985 (lamp), G2986 (bright), G2989 (give light)\nVariants: λανθάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3582 כָּחַד, H4603 מָעַל, H5737 עֲדַר, H5956 עָלַם",
    2991: "λαξευτός (laxeutós | lax-yoo-tos')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of λᾶς (a stone) and the base of G3584 (in its original sense of scraping)\nStrong's: rock-quarried\nKJV: —hewn in stone.\n\nSee: G3584 \n\nCognate Group: G2985 (lamp), G2986 (bright), G2989 (give light)\nVariants: λαξευτός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6449 פִּסְגָּה",
    2992: "λαός (laós | lah-os')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a people (in general; thus differing from G1218, which denotes one's own populace)\nKJV: —people.\n\nSee: G1218 \n\nCognate Group: G2992 (people)\nVariants: λαός \nHebrew Equivalents: H120 אָדָם, H524 אֻמָּה, H582 אֱנֹושׁ, H1004 בַּיִת, H1121 בֵּן, H1471 גֹּוי, H1995 הָמֹון, H2213 זֵר, H2945 טַף, H3816 לְאֹם, H4264 מַחֲנֶה, H4725 מָקֹום, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה, H5650 עֶבֶד, H5971 עַם, H6629 צֹאן, H6951 קָהָל",
    2993: "Λαοδίκεια (Laodíkeia | lah-od-ik'-i-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G2992 and G1349\nStrong's: Laodicia, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Laodicea.\n\nSee: G2992 \nSee: G1349 \n\nCognate Group: G2993 (Laodicea), G2994 (Laodicean)\nVariants: Λαοδίκεια, Λαοδικεία",
    2994: "Λαοδικεύς (Laodikeús | lah-od-ik-yooce')\n\nDerivation: from G2993\nStrong's: a Laodicean, i.e. inhabitant of Laodicia\nKJV: —Laodicean.\n\nSee: G2993 \n\nCognate Group: G2993 (Laodicea), G2994 (Laodicean)\nVariants: Λαοδικεύς",
    2995: "λάρυγξ (lárynx | lar'-oongks)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: the throat (\"larynx\")\nKJV: —throat.\n\nCognate Group: G2995 (throat)\nVariants: λάρυγξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1627 גָּרֹון, H2441 חֵךְ, H4455 מַלְקֹוחַ",
    2996: "Λασαία (Lasaía | las-ah'-yah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Lasæa, a place in Crete\nKJV: —Lasea.\n\nCognate Group: G2995 (throat)\nVariants: Λασαία, Λασέα, Λασαία/Λασέα",
    2997: "λάσχω (láschō | las'-kho)\n\nDerivation: a strengthened form of a primary verb, which only occurs in this and another prolonged form as alternate in certain tenses\nStrong's: to crack open (from a fall)\nKJV: —burst asunder.\n\nCognate Group: G2997 (burst asunder)\nVariants: λάσχω, λακάω, λάσκω",
    2998: "λατομέω (latoméō | lat-om-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as the first part of G2991 and the base of G5114\nStrong's: to quarry\nKJV: —hew.\n\nSee: G2991 \nSee: G5114 \n\nCognate Group: G2998 (hew)\nVariants: λατομέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2672 חָצַב, H3738 כָּרָה",
    2999: "λατρεία (latreía | lat-ri'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3000\nStrong's: ministration of God, i.e. worship\nKJV: —(divine) service.\n\nSee: G3000 \n\nCognate Group: G2999 (service), G3000 (serve)\nVariants: λατρεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5656 עֲבֹדָה",
    3000: "λατρεύω (latreúō | lat-ryoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from λάτρις (a hired menial)\nStrong's: to minister (to God), i.e. render religious homage\nKJV: —serve, do the service, worship(-per).\n\nCognate Group: G2999 (service), G3000 (serve)\nVariants: λατρεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H309 אָחַר, H5647 עָבַד, H6399 פְּלַח, H8334 שָׁרַת",
    3001: "λάχανον (láchanon | lakh'-an-on)\n\nDerivation: from λαχαίνω (to dig)\nStrong's: a vegetable\nKJV: —herb.\n\nCognate Group: G3001 (herb)\nVariants: λάχανον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3418 יֶרֶק, H3419 יָרָק",
    3002: "Λεββαῖος (Lebbaîos | leb-bah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Lebbæus, a Christian\nKJV: —Lebbæus.\n\nCognate Group: G3001 (herb)\nVariants: Λεββαῖος G3304",
    3003: "λεγεών (legeṓn | leg-eh-ohn')\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a \"legion\", i.e. Roman regiment (figuratively)\nKJV: —legion.\n\nCognate Group: G3003 (legion)\nVariants: λεγεών, λεγιών",
    3004: "λέγω (légō | leg'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: properly, to \"lay\" forth, i.e. (figuratively) relate (in words (usually of systematic or set discourse; whereas G2036 and G5346 generally refer to an individual expression or speech respectively; while G4483 is properly to break silence merely, and G2980 means an extended or random harangue)); by implication, to mean\nKJV: —ask, bid, boast, call, describe, give out, name, put forth, say(-ing, on), shew, speak, tell, utter.\n\nSee: G2036 \nSee: G5346 \nSee: G4483 \nSee: G2980 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λέγω, εἴρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H1326 בָּתָה, H1696 דָבַר, H3576 כָּזַב, H4687 מִצְוָה, H4911 מָשַׁל, H5001 נָאַם, H5002 נְאֻם, H5608 סָפַר",
    3005: "λεῖμμα (leîmma | lime'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3007\nStrong's: a remainder\nKJV: —remnant.\n\nSee: G3007 \n\nCognate Group: G4035 (remain), G2640 (remnant), G2641 (forsake), G3005 (remnant), G1257 (cease)\nVariants: λεῖμμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H7611 שְׁאֵרִית",
    3006: "λεῖος (leîos | li'-os)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: smooth, i.e. \"level\"\nKJV: —smooth.\n\nCognate Group: G3006 (smooth)\nVariants: λεῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1237 בִּקְעָה, H2505 חָלַק, H2512 חַלֻּק",
    3007: "λείπω (leípō | li'-po)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to leave, i.e. (intransitively or passively) to fail or be absent\nKJV: —be destitute (wanting), lack.\n\nCognate Group: G3006 (smooth)\nVariants: λείπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3869 לוּז, H6504 פָּרַד, H6504 פָּרַד",
    3008: "λειτουργέω (leitourgéō | li-toorg-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3011\nStrong's: to be a public servant, i.e. (by analogy) to perform religious or charitable functions (worship, obey, relieve)\nKJV: —minister.\n\nSee: G3011 \n\nCognate Group: G3009 (ministration), G3010 (ministering), G3008 (minister), G3011 (minister)\nVariants: λειτουργέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3547 כָּהַן, H5647 עָבַד, H6633 צָבָא, H8120 שְׁמַשׁ, H8334 שָׁרַת",
    3009: "λειτουργία (leitourgía | li-toorg-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3008\nStrong's: public function (as priest (\"liturgy\") or almsgiver)\nKJV: —ministration(-try), service.\n\nSee: G3008 \n\nCognate Group: G3009 (ministration), G3010 (ministering), G3008 (minister), G3011 (minister)\nVariants: λειτουργία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4399 מְלָאכָה, H5656 עֲבֹדָה, H6402 פׇּלְחָן, H6468 פְּעֻלָּה, H6635 צָבָא",
    3010: "λειτουργικός (leitourgikós | li-toorg-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3008\nStrong's: functional publicly (\"liturgic\"); i.e. beneficient\nKJV: —ministering.\n\nSee: G3008 \n\nCognate Group: G3009 (ministration), G3010 (ministering), G3008 (minister), G3011 (minister)\nVariants: λειτουργικός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5656 עֲבֹדָה, H8334 שָׁרַת",
    3011: "λειτουργός (leitourgós | li-toorg-os')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G2992 and G2041\nStrong's: a public servant, i.e. a functionary in the Temple or Gospel, or (genitive case) a worshipper (of God) or benefactor (of man)\nKJV: —minister(-ed).\n\nSee: G2992 \nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G3009 (ministration), G3010 (ministering), G3008 (minister), G3011 (minister)\nVariants: λειτουργός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6400 פֶּלַח, H8334 שָׁרַת",
    3012: "λέντιον (léntion | len'-tee-on)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a \"linen\" cloth, i.e. apron\nKJV: —towel.\n\nCognate Group: G3012 (towel)\nVariants: λέντιον",
    3013: "λεπίς (lepís | lep-is')\n\nDerivation: from λέπω (to peel)\nStrong's: a flake\nKJV: —scale.\n\nCognate Group: G3013 (scale)\nVariants: λεπίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H6341 פַּח, H7193 קַשְׂקֶשֶׂת",
    3014: "λέπρα (lépra | lep'-rah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3013\nStrong's: scaliness, i.e. \"leprosy\"\nKJV: —leprosy.\n\nSee: G3013 \n\nCognate Group: G3015 (leper), G3014 (leprosy)\nVariants: λέπρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6883 צָרַעַת",
    3015: "λεπρός (leprós | lep-ros')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3014\nStrong's: scaly, i.e. leprous (a leper)\nKJV: —leper.\n\nSee: G3014 \n\nCognate Group: G3015 (leper), G3014 (leprosy)\nVariants: λεπρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6879 צָרַע",
    3016: "λεπτόν (leptón | lep-ton')\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of the same as G3013\nStrong's: something scaled (light), i.e. a small coin\nKJV: —mite.\n\nSee: G3013 \n\nCognate Group: G3016 (mite)\nVariants: λεπτόν, λεπτός",
    3017: "Λευΐ (Leuḯ | lyoo'-ee)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H3878)\nStrong's: Levi, the name of three Israelites\nKJV: —Levi. Compare G3018 .\n\nSee: G3018 \nSee: H3878\n\nCognate Group: G3018 (Levi), G3017 (Levi)\nVariants: Λευΐ, Λευί, Λευΐ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3878 לֵוִי",
    3018: "Λευΐς (Leuḯs | lyoo-is')\n\nDerivation: a form of G3017\nStrong's: Lewis (i.e. Levi), a Christian\nKJV: —Levi.\n\nSee: G3017 \n\nCognate Group: G3018 (Levi), G3017 (Levi)\nVariants: Λευΐς, Λευίς, Λευί, Λευΐς",
    3019: "Λευΐτης (Leuḯtēs | lyoo-ee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G3017\nStrong's: a Levite, i.e. descendant of Levi\nKJV: —Levite.\n\nSee: G3017 \n\nCognate Group: G3019 (Levite), G3020 (Levitical)\nVariants: Λευΐτης, Λευίτης, Λευΐτης",
    3020: "Λευϊτικός (Leuïtikós | lyoo-it'-ee-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G3019\nStrong's: Levitic, i.e. relating to the Levites\nKJV: —Levitical.\n\nSee: G3019 \n\nCognate Group: G3019 (Levite), G3020 (Levitical)\nVariants: Λευϊτικός, Λευιτικός",
    3021: "λευκαίνω (leukaínō | lyoo-kah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3022\nStrong's: to whiten\nKJV: —make white, whiten.\n\nSee: G3022 \n\nCognate Group: G3021 (make white), G3022 (white)\nVariants: λευκαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3835 לָבַן",
    3022: "λευκός (leukós | lyoo-kos')\n\nDerivation: from λύκη (\"light\")\nStrong's: white\nKJV: —white.\n\nCognate Group: G3021 (make white), G3022 (white)\nVariants: λευκός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2345 חוּם, H2358 חִוָּר, H3836 לָבָן, H5343 נְקֵא, H5348 נָקֹד, H6121 עָקֹב, H6703 צַח",
    3023: "λέων (léōn | leh-ohn')\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: a \"lion\"\nKJV: —lion.\n\nCognate Group: G3023 (lion)\nVariants: λέων \nHebrew Equivalents: H738 אֲרִי, H744 אַרְיֵה, H3715 כְּפִיר, H3833 לָבִיא, H7826 שַׁחַל",
    3024: "λήθη (lḗthē | lay'-thay)\n\nDerivation: from G2990\nStrong's: forgetfulness\nKJV: —+ forget.\n\nSee: G2990 \n\nCognate Group: G3023 (lion)\nVariants: λήθη \nHebrew Equivalents: H4604 מַעַל, H5375 נָשָׂא, H7911 שָׁכַח",
    3025: "ληνός (lēnós | lay-nos')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a trough, i.e. wine-vat\nKJV: —winepress.\n\nCognate Group: G5276 (winefat), G3025 (winepress)\nVariants: ληνός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1660 גַּת, H1831 דֶּמַע, H3342 יֶקֶב, H4460 מַמְּגֻרָה, H7298 רַהַט",
    3026: "λῆρος (lēros | lay'-ros)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: twaddle, i.e. an incredible story\nKJV: —idle tale.\n\nCognate Group: G3026 (idle tale)\nVariants: λῆρος",
    3027: "λῃστής (lēistḗs | lace-tace')\n\nDerivation: from ληΐζομαι (to plunder)\nStrong's: a brigand\nKJV: —robber, thief.\n\nCognate Group: G3027 (robber)\nVariants: λῃστής, ἀρχιλῃστής, λήστής \nHebrew Equivalents: H1416 גְּדוּד, H6530 פְּרִיץ, H7703 שָׁדַד",
    3028: "λῆμψις (lēmpsis | lape'-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G2983\nStrong's: receipt (the act)\nKJV: —receiving.\n\nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G3027 (robber)\nVariants: λῆμψις, λῆψις",
    3029: "λίαν (lían | lee'-an)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: much (adverbially)\nKJV: —exceeding, great(-ly), sore, very (+ chiefest).\n\nCognate Group: G3027 (robber)\nVariants: λίαν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3966 מְאֹד, H4100 מָה, H5704 עַד",
    3030: "λίβανος (líbanos | lib'-an-os)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (H3828)\nStrong's: the incense-tree, i.e. (by implication) incense itself\nKJV: —frankincense.\nSee: H3828\n\nCognate Group: G3030 (frankincense), G3031 (censer)\nVariants: λίβανος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3828 לְבֹונָה",
    3031: "λιβανωτός (libanōtós | lib-an-o-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G3030\nStrong's: frankincense, i.e. (by extension) a censer for burning it\nKJV: —censer.\n\nSee: G3030 \n\nCognate Group: G3030 (frankincense), G3031 (censer)\nVariants: λιβανωτός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3828 לְבֹונָה",
    3032: "Λιβερτῖνος (Libertînos | lib-er-tee'-nos)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a Roman freedman\nKJV: —Libertine.\n\nCognate Group: G3032 (Libertine)\nVariants: Λιβερτῖνος, Λιβυστῖνος, λιβερτῖνος",
    3033: "Λιβύη (Libýē | lib-oo'-ay)\n\nDerivation: probably from G3047\nStrong's: Libye, a region of Africa\nKJV: —Libya.\n\nSee: G3047 \n\nCognate Group: G3033 (Libya)\nVariants: Λιβύη",
    3034: "λιθάζω (litházō | lith-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3037\nStrong's: to lapidate\nKJV: —stone.\n\nSee: G3037 \n\nCognate Group: G2642 (stone), G3034 (stone), G3036 (stone)\nVariants: λιθάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5619 סָקַל",
    3035: "λίθινος (líthinos | lith-ee'-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G3037\nStrong's: stony, i.e. made of stone\nKJV: —of stone.\n\nSee: G3037 \n\nCognate Group: G3037 (millstone), G3035 (of stone), G3038 (Pavement)\nVariants: λίθινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H68 אֶבֶן, H8336 שֵׁשׁ",
    3036: "λιθοβολέω (lithoboléō | lith-ob-ol-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3037 and G906\nStrong's: to throw stones, i.e. lapidate\nKJV: —stone, cast stones.\n\nSee: G3037 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G2642 (stone), G3034 (stone), G3036 (stone)\nVariants: λιθοβολέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5619 סָקַל, H7275 רָגַם",
    3037: "λίθος (líthos | lee'-thos)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a stone (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —(mill-, stumbling-)stone.\n\nCognate Group: G3037 (millstone), G3035 (of stone), G3038 (Pavement)\nVariants: λίθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H68 אֶבֶן, H1496 גָּזִית, H3800 כֶּתֶם, H4676 מַצֵּבָה, H5619 סָקַל, H6337 פָּז, H6443 פָּנִין, H6443 פָּנִין, H6872 צְרֹור, H7275 רָגַם",
    3038: "λιθόστρωτος (lithóstrōtos | lith-os'-tro-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G3037 and a derivative of G4766\nStrong's: stone-strewed, i.e. a tessellated mosaic on which the Roman tribunal was placed\nKJV: —Pavement.\n\nSee: G3037 \nSee: G4766 \n\nCognate Group: G3037 (millstone), G3035 (of stone), G3038 (Pavement)\nVariants: λιθόστρωτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7528 רָצַף, H7531 רִצְפָּה",
    3039: "λικμάω (likmáō | lik-mah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from λικμός, the equivalent of λίκνον (a winnowing fan or basket)\nStrong's: to winnow, i.e. (by analogy), to triturate\nKJV: —grind to powder.\n\nCognate Group: G3039 (grind to powder)\nVariants: λικμάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2219 זָרָה, H5128 נוּעַ, H5487 סוּף, H5500 סָחָה, H5590 סָעַר",
    3040: "λιμήν (limḗn | lee-mane')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a harbor\nKJV: —haven. Compare G2568 .\n\nSee: G2568 \n\nCognate Group: G3040 (haven), G3041 (lake)\nVariants: λιμήν, Λιμήν, Καλοὶ~Λιμένες, Καλοὶ λιμένες \nHebrew Equivalents: H4231 מָחֹוז",
    3041: "λίμνη (límnē | lim'-nay)\n\nDerivation: probably from G3040 (through the idea of nearness of shore)\nStrong's: a pond (large or small)\nKJV: —lake.\n\nSee: G3040 \n\nCognate Group: G3040 (haven), G3041 (lake)\nVariants: λίμνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H98 אֲגַם, H1295 בְּרֵכָה",
    3042: "λιμός (limós | lee-mos')\n\nDerivation: probably from G3007 (through the idea of destitution)\nStrong's: a scarcity of food\nKJV: —dearth, famine, hunger.\n\nSee: G3007 \n\nCognate Group: G3042 (dearth)\nVariants: λιμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3720 כָּפָן, H7458 רָעָב",
    3043: "λίνον (línon | lee'-non)\n\nDerivation: probably a primary word\nStrong's: flax, i.e. (by implication) \"linen\"\nKJV: —linen.\n\nCognate Group: G3043 (linen)\nVariants: λίνον \nHebrew Equivalents: H906 בַּד, H6593 פִּשְׁתֶּה, H6594 פִּשְׁתָּה",
    3044: "Λίνος (Línos | lee'-nos)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G3043\nStrong's: Linus, a Christian\nKJV: —Linus.\n\nSee: G3043 \n\nCognate Group: G3044 (Linus)\nVariants: Λίνος, Λῖνος",
    3045: "λιπαρός (liparós | lip-ar-os')\n\nDerivation: from λίπος (grease)\nStrong's: fat, i.e. (figuratively) sumptuous\nKJV: —dainty.\n\nCognate Group: G3045 (dainty)\nVariants: λιπαρός",
    3046: "λίτρα (lítra | lee'-trah)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin (libra)\nStrong's: a pound in weight\nKJV: —pound.\n\nCognate Group: G3046 (pound)\nVariants: λίτρα",
    3047: "λίψ (líps | leeps)\n\nDerivation: probably from λείβω (to pour a \"libation\")\nStrong's: the south(- west) wind (as bringing rain, i.e. (by extension) the south quarter)\nKJV: —southwest.\n\nCognate Group: G3047 (southwest)\nVariants: λίψ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1864 דָּרֹום, H4627 מַעֲרָב, H4628 מַעֲרָב, H5045 נֶגֶב, H8486 תֵּימָן",
    3048: "λογία (logía | log-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3056 (in the commercial sense)\nStrong's: a contribution\nKJV: —collection, gathering.\n\nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G3048 (collection)\nVariants: λογία, λογεία",
    3049: "λογίζομαι (logízomai | log-id'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G3056\nStrong's: to take an inventory, i.e. estimate (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —conclude, (ac-)count (of), + despise, esteem, impute, lay, number, reason, reckon, suppose, think (on).\n\nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G3049 (conclude), G3053 (imagination)\nVariants: λογίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1961 הָיָה, H2803 חָשַׁב, H4487 מָנָה, H7121 קָרָא, H7725 שׁוּב",
    3050: "λογικός (logikós | log-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G3056\nStrong's: rational (\"logical\")\nKJV: —reasonable, of the word.\n\nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λογικός",
    3051: "λόγιον (lógion | log'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G3052\nStrong's: an utterance (of God)\nKJV: —oracle.\n\nSee: G3052 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λόγιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H561 אֵמֶר, H565 אִמְרָה, H1697 דָּבָר, H4858 מַשָּׂאָה",
    3052: "λόγιος (lógios | log'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3056\nStrong's: fluent, i.e. an orator\nKJV: —eloquent.\n\nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λόγιος",
    3053: "λογισμός (logismós | log-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3049\nStrong's: computation, i.e. (figuratively) reasoning (conscience, conceit)\nKJV: —imagination, thought.\n\nSee: G3049 \n\nCognate Group: G3049 (conclude), G3053 (imagination)\nVariants: λογισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4284 מַחֲשָׁבָה",
    3054: "λογομαχέω (logomachéō | log-om-akh-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3056 and G3164\nStrong's: to be disputatious (on trifles)\nKJV: —strive about words.\n\nSee: G3056 \nSee: G3164 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λογομαχέω",
    3055: "λογομαχία (logomachía | log-om-akh-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3054\nStrong's: disputation about trifles (\"logomachy\")\nKJV: —strife of words.\n\nSee: G3054 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λογομαχία",
    3056: "λόγος (lógos | log'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3004\nStrong's: something said (including the thought); by implication, a topic (subject of discourse), also reasoning (the mental faculty) or motive; by extension, a computation; specially, (with the article in John) the Divine Expression (i.e. Christ)\nKJV: —account, cause, communication, X concerning, doctrine, fame, X have to do, intent, matter, mouth, preaching, question, reason, + reckon, remove, say(-ing), shew, X speaker, speech, talk, thing, + none of these things move me, tidings, treatise, utterance, word, work.\n\nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λόγος \nHebrew Equivalents: H561 אֵמֶר, H1672 דָּאַג, H1674 דְּאָגָה, H1697 דָּבָר, H2940 טַעַם, H4405 מִלָּה, H4426 מְלִיצָה, H4687 מִצְוָה, H5017 נְבוּאָה, H5612 סֵפֶר, H6310 פֶּה, H6600 פִּתְגָּם, H6963 קֹול, H7626 שֵׁבֶט, H8193 שָׂפָה, H8394 תָּבוּן, H8452 תֹּורָה",
    3057: "λόγχη (lónchē | long'-khay)\n\nDerivation: perhaps a primary word\nStrong's: a \"lance\"\nKJV: —spear.\n\nCognate Group: G3055 (strife of words), G3052 (eloquent), G3004 (ask), G1588 (chosen), G1586 (make choice), G249 (brute), G3050 (reasonable), G3054 (strive about words), G1589 (chosen), G3056 (account), G2980 (preach), G3051 (oracle)\nVariants: λόγχη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2719 חֶרֶב, H3852 לֶהָבָה, H4239 מְחִי, H7227 רַב, H7420 רֹמַח",
    3058: "λοιδορέω (loidoréō | loy-dor-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3060\nStrong's: to reproach, i.e. vilify\nKJV: —revile.\n\nSee: G3060 \n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: λοιδορέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1605 גָּעַר, H7378 רִיב",
    3059: "λοιδορία (loidoría | loy-dor-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3060\nStrong's: slander or vituperation\nKJV: —railing, reproach(-fully).\n\nSee: G3060 \n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: λοιδορία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1681 דִּבָּה, H4808 מְרִיבָה, H7379 רִיב",
    3060: "λοίδορος (loídoros | loy'-dor-os)\n\nDerivation: from λοιδός (mischief)\nStrong's: abusive, i.e. a blackguard\nKJV: —railer, reviler.\n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: λοίδορος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4066 מָדֹון, H4079 מִדְיָן",
    3061: "λοιμός (loimós | loy'-mos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a plague (literally, the disease, or figuratively, a pest)\nKJV: —pestilence(-t).\n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: λοιμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1100 בְּלִיַּעַל, H3887 לוּץ, H5938 עָלֵז, H6184 עָרִיץ, H6530 פְּרִיץ, H7386 רֵיק, H7451 רַע, H7563 רָשָׁע",
    3062: "λοιποί (loipoí | loy-poy')\n\nDerivation: masculine plural of a derivative of G3007\nStrong's: remaining ones\nKJV: —other, which remain, remnant, residue, rest.\n\nSee: G3007 \n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: λοιποί, λοιπός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3498 יָתַר, H3499 יֶתֶר, H3605 כֹּל, H6285 פֵּאָה, H7605 שְׁאָר",
    3063: "λοιπόν (loipón | loy-pon')\n\nDerivation: neuter singular of the same as G3062\nStrong's: something remaining (adverbially)\nKJV: —besides, finally, furthermore, (from) henceforth, moreover, now, + it remaineth, then.\n\nSee: G3062 \n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: λοιπόν, λοιπός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3498 יָתַר, H3499 יֶתֶר, H3605 כֹּל, H6285 פֵּאָה, H7605 שְׁאָר",
    3064: "λοιποῦ (loipoû | loy-poo')\n\nDerivation: genitive case singular of the same as G3062\nStrong's: remaining time\nKJV: —from henceforth.\n\nSee: G3062 \n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: λοιποῦ, λοιπός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3498 יָתַר, H3499 יֶתֶר, H3605 כֹּל, H6285 פֵּאָה, H7605 שְׁאָר",
    3065: "Λουκᾶς (Loukâs | loo-kas')\n\nDerivation: contracted from Latin Lucanus\nStrong's: Lucas, a Christian\nKJV: —Lucas, Luke.\n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: Λουκᾶς",
    3066: "Λούκιος (Loúkios | loo'-kee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: illuminative; Lucius, a Christian\nKJV: —Lucius.\n\nCognate Group: G3058 (revile), G3059 (railing), G3060 (railer), G486 (revile again)\nVariants: Λούκιος",
    3067: "λουτρόν (loutrón | loo-tron')\n\nDerivation: from G3068\nStrong's: a bath, i.e. (figuratively), baptism\nKJV: —washing.\n\nSee: G3068 \n\nCognate Group: G628 (wash), G3067 (washing), G3068 (wash)\nVariants: λουτρόν \nHebrew Equivalents: H7367 רַחְצָה",
    3068: "λούω (loúō | loo'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to bathe (the whole person; whereas G3538 means to wet a part only, and G4150 to wash, cleanse garments exclusively)\nKJV: —wash.\n\nSee: G3538 \nSee: G4150 \n\nCognate Group: G628 (wash), G3067 (washing), G3068 (wash)\nVariants: λούω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7364 רָחַץ, H7811 שָׂחָה",
    3069: "Λύδδα (Lýdda | lud'-dah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H3850)\nStrong's: Lydda (i.e. Lod), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Lydda.\nSee: H3850\n\nCognate Group: G628 (wash), G3067 (washing), G3068 (wash)\nVariants: Λύδδα \nHebrew Equivalents: H3850 לֹד",
    3070: "Λυδία (Lydía | loo-dee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: properly, feminine of Ludios (of foreign origin) (a Lydian, in Asia Minor)\nStrong's: Lydia, a Christian woman\nKJV: —Lydia.\n\nCognate Group: G628 (wash), G3067 (washing), G3068 (wash)\nVariants: Λυδία",
    3071: "Λυκαονία (Lykaonía | loo-kah-on-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: perhaps remotely from G3074\nStrong's: Lycaonia, a region of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Lycaonia.\n\nSee: G3074 \n\nCognate Group: G628 (wash), G3067 (washing), G3068 (wash)\nVariants: Λυκαονία",
    3072: "Λυκαονιστί (Lykaonistí | loo-kah-on-is-tee')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a derivative of G3071\nStrong's: Lycaonistically, i.e. in the language of the Lycaonians\nKJV: —in the speech of Lycaonia.\n\nSee: G3071 \n\nCognate Group: G628 (wash), G3067 (washing), G3068 (wash)\nVariants: Λυκαονιστί",
    3073: "Λυκία (Lykía | loo-kee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: probably remotely from G3074\nStrong's: Lycia, a province of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Lycia.\n\nSee: G3074 \n\nCognate Group: G628 (wash), G3067 (washing), G3068 (wash)\nVariants: Λυκία",
    3074: "λύκος (lýkos | loo'-kos)\n\nDerivation: perhaps akin to the base of G3022 (from the whitish hair)\nStrong's: a wolf\nKJV: —wolf.\n\nSee: G3022 \n\nCognate Group: G3074 (wolf)\nVariants: λύκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1677 דֹּב, H2061 זְאֵב",
    3075: "λυμαίνομαι (lymaínomai | loo-mah'-ee-nom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from a probably derivative of G3089 (meaning filth)\nStrong's: properly, to soil, i.e. (figuratively) insult (maltreat)\nKJV: —make havock of.\n\nSee: G3089 \n\nCognate Group: G3075 (make havock of)\nVariants: λυμαίνομαι, λυμαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1238 בָּקַק, H2255 חֲבַל, H3765 כִּרְסֵם, H5557 סָלַף, H7533 רָצַץ, H7843 שָׁחַת, H8581 תַּעָב",
    3076: "λυπέω (lypéō | loo-peh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3077\nStrong's: to distress; reflexively or passively, to be sad\nKJV: —cause grief, grieve, be in heaviness, (be) sorrow(-ful), be (make) sorry.\n\nSee: G3077 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: λυπέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H57 אָבֵל, H888 בְּאֵשׁ, H1742 דַּוָּי, H2734 חָרָה, H3511 כְּאֵב, H3811 לָאָה, H5701 עָגַם, H6087 עָצַב, H7107 קָצַף, H7264 רָגַז, H7300 רוּד, H7489 רָעַע",
    3077: "λύπη (lýpē | loo'-pay)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: sadness\nKJV: —grief, grievous, + grudgingly, heaviness, sorrow.\n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: λύπη \nHebrew Equivalents: H6 אָבַד, H1742 דַּוָּי, H3015 יָגֹון, H3511 כְּאֵב, H6089 עֶצֶב, H7200 רָאָה, H8424 תּוּגָה",
    3078: "Λυσανίας (Lysanías | loo-san-ee'-as)\n\nDerivation: from G3080 and ἀνία (trouble)\nStrong's: grief-dispelling; Lysanias, a governor of Abilene\nKJV: —Lysanias.\n\nSee: G3080 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: Λυσανίας",
    3079: "Λυσίας (Lysías | loo-see'-as)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: Lysias, a Roman\nKJV: —Lysias.\n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: Λυσίας",
    3080: "λύσις (lýsis | loo'-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3089\nStrong's: a loosening, i.e. (specially), divorce\nKJV: —to be loosed.\n\nSee: G3089 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: λύσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H6592 פֵּשֶׁר",
    3081: "λυσιτελεῖ (lysiteleî | loo-sit-el-i')\n\nDerivation: third person singular present indicative active of a derivative of a compound of G3080 and G5056\nStrong's: impersonally, it answers the purpose, i.e. is advantageous\nKJV: —it is better.\n\nSee: G3080 \nSee: G5056 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: λυσιτελεῖ, λυσιτελέω",
    3082: "Λύστρα (Lýstra | loos'-trah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Lystra, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Lystra.\n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: Λύστρα",
    3083: "λύτρον (lýtron | loo'-tron)\n\nDerivation: from G3089\nStrong's: something to loosen with, i.e. a redemption price (figuratively, atonement)\nKJV: —ransom.\n\nSee: G3089 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: λύτρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1353 גְּאֻלָּה, H3724 כֹּפֶר, H4242 מְחִיר, H6306 פִּדְיֹום",
    3084: "λυτρόω (lytróō | loo-tro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3083\nStrong's: to ransom (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —redeem.\n\nSee: G3083 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: λυτρόω, λυτρόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1350 גָּאַל, H6202 עָרַף, H6299 פָּדָה, H6403 פָּלַט, H6475 פָּצָה, H6561 פָּרַק, H7069 קָנָה, H7682 שָׂגַב, H7804 שְׁזַב",
    3085: "λύτρωσις (lýtrōsis | loo'-tro-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3084\nStrong's: a ransoming (figuratively)\nKJV: —+ redeemed, redemption.\n\nSee: G3084 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: λύτρωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1353 גְּאֻלָּה, H1353 גְּאֻלָּה, H6299 פָּדָה, H6304 פְּדוּת, H6306 פִּדְיֹום",
    3086: "λυτρωτής (lytrōtḗs | loo-tro-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G3084\nStrong's: a redeemer (figuratively)\nKJV: —deliverer.\n\nSee: G3084 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: λυτρωτής \nHebrew Equivalents: H1350 גָּאַל",
    3087: "λυχνία (lychnía | lookh-nee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3088\nStrong's: a lamp-stand (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —candlestick.\n\nSee: G3088 \n\nCognate Group: G3087 (candlestick), G3088 (candle)\nVariants: λυχνία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4501 מְנֹורָה",
    3088: "λύχνος (lýchnos | lookh'-nos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3022\nStrong's: a portable lamp or other illuminator (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —candle, light.\n\nSee: G3022 \n\nCognate Group: G3087 (candlestick), G3088 (candle)\nVariants: λύχνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5216 נִיר, H5216 נִיר",
    3089: "λύω (lýō | loo'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to \"loosen\" (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —break (up), destroy, dissolve, (un-)loose, melt, put off. Compare G4486 .\n\nSee: G4486 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: λύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5375 נָשָׂא, H5394 נָשַׁל, H5425 נָתַר, H5642 סְתַר, H6605 פָּתַח, H7521 רָצָה, H8271 שְׁרֵא",
    3090: "Λωΐς (Lōḯs | lo-ece')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Lois, a Christian woman\nKJV: —Lois.\n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: Λωΐς, Λωί̈ς, Λωΐς",
    3091: "Λώτ (Lṓt | lote)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H3876)\nStrong's: Lot, a patriarch\nKJV: —Lot.\nSee: H3876\n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: Λώτ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3876 לֹוט",
    3092: "Μαάθ (Maáth | mah-ath')\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin\nStrong's: Maath, an Israelite\nKJV: —Maath.\n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: Μαάθ, Μάαθ",
    3093: "Μαγδαλά (Magdalá | mag-dal-ah')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (compare H4026)\nStrong's: the tower; Magdala (i.e. Migdala), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Magdala.\nSee: H4026\n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: Μαγδαλά, Μαγαδάν \nHebrew Equivalents: H4024 מִגְדֹּול, H4026 מִגְדָּל",
    3094: "Μαγδαληνή (Magdalēnḗ | mag-dal-ay-nay')\n\nDerivation: feminine of a derivative of G3093\nStrong's: a female Magdalene, i.e. inhabitant of Magdala\nKJV: —Magdalene.\n\nSee: G3093 \n\nCognate Group: G2647 (destroy), G3089 (break), G3083 (ransom), G179 (endless), G630 (depart), G1956 (determine), G360 (depart), G3085 (+ redeemed), G2646 (guestchamber), G359 (departure), G3086 (deliverer), G1955 (interpretation), G3084 (redeem), G629 (deliverance), G487 (ransom)\nVariants: Μαγδαληνή",
    3095: "μαγεία (mageía | mag-i'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3096\nStrong's: \"magic\"\nKJV: —sorcery.\n\nSee: G3096 \n\nCognate Group: G3096 (use sorcery), G3095 (sorcery), G3097 (sorcerer)\nVariants: μαγεία, μαγία",
    3096: "μαγεύω (mageúō | mag-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3097\nStrong's: to practice magic\nKJV: —use sorcery.\n\nSee: G3097 \n\nCognate Group: G3096 (use sorcery), G3095 (sorcery), G3097 (sorcerer)\nVariants: μαγεύω",
    3097: "μάγος (mágos | mag'-os)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (H7248)\nStrong's: a Magian, i.e. Oriental scientist; by implication, a magician\nKJV: —sorcerer, wise man.\nSee: H7248\n\nCognate Group: G3096 (use sorcery), G3095 (sorcery), G3097 (sorcerer)\nVariants: μάγος \nHebrew Equivalents: H825 אַשָּׁף, H7248 רַב־מָג",
    3098: "Μαγώγ (Magṓg | mag-ogue')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4031)\nStrong's: Magog, a foreign nation, i.e. (figuratively) an Antichristian party\nKJV: —Magog.\nSee: H4031\n\nCognate Group: G3096 (use sorcery), G3095 (sorcery), G3097 (sorcerer)\nVariants: Μαγώγ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4031 מָגֹוג",
    3099: "Μαδιάν (Madián | mad-ee-on')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4080)\nStrong's: Madian (i.e. Midian), a region of Arabia\nKJV: —Madian.\nSee: H4080\n\nCognate Group: G3096 (use sorcery), G3095 (sorcery), G3097 (sorcerer)\nVariants: Μαδιάν, Μαδιάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4080 מִדְיָן",
    3100: "μαθητεύω (mathēteúō | math-ayt-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3101\nStrong's: intransitively, to become a pupil; transitively, to disciple, i.e. enrol as scholar\nKJV: —be disciple, instruct, teach.\n\nSee: G3101 \n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: μαθητεύω",
    3101: "μαθητής (mathētḗs | math-ay-tes')\n\nDerivation: from G3129\nStrong's: a learner, i.e. pupil\nKJV: —disciple.\n\nSee: G3129 \n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: μαθητής",
    3102: "μαθήτρια (mathḗtria | math-ay'-tree-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine from G3101\nStrong's: a female pupil\nKJV: —disciple.\n\nSee: G3101 \n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: μαθήτρια",
    3103: "Μαθουσάλα (Mathousála | math-oo-sal'-ah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4968)\nStrong's: Mathusala (i.e. Methushelach), an antediluvian\nKJV: —Mathusala.\nSee: H4968\n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: Μαθουσάλα, Μαθουσαλά",
    3104: "Μαϊνάν (Maïnán | mahee-nan')\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin\nStrong's: Mainan, an Israelite\nKJV: —Mainan.\n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: Μαϊνάν, Μενάμ",
    3105: "μαίνομαι (maínomai | mah'-ee-nom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from a primary μάω \nStrong's: (to long for; through the idea of insensate craving); to rave as a \"maniac\"\nKJV: —be beside self (mad).\n\nCognate Group: G3105 (be beside self)\nVariants: μαίνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל, H7696 שָׁגַע",
    3106: "μακαρίζω (makarízō | mak-ar-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3107\nStrong's: to beatify, i.e. pronounce (or esteem) fortunate\nKJV: —call blessed, count happy.\n\nSee: G3107 \n\nCognate Group: G3106 (call blessed), G3107 (blessed), G3108 (blessedness)\nVariants: μακαρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H833 אָשַׁר",
    3107: "μακάριος (makários | mak-ar'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of the poetical μάκαρ (meaning the same)\nStrong's: supremely blest; by extension, fortunate, well off\nKJV: — blessed, happy(X -ier).\n\nCognate Group: G3106 (call blessed), G3107 (blessed), G3108 (blessedness)\nVariants: μακάριος \nHebrew Equivalents: H837 אֹשֶׁר",
    3108: "μακαρισμός (makarismós | mak-ar-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3106\nStrong's: beatification, i.e. attribution of good fortune\nKJV: —blessedness.\n\nSee: G3106 \n\nCognate Group: G3106 (call blessed), G3107 (blessed), G3108 (blessedness)\nVariants: μακαρισμός",
    3109: "Μακεδονία (Makedonía | mak-ed-on-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3110\nStrong's: Macedonia, a region of Greece\nKJV: —Macedonia.\n\nSee: G3110 \n\nCognate Group: G3106 (call blessed), G3107 (blessed), G3108 (blessedness)\nVariants: Μακεδονία",
    3110: "Μακεδών (Makedṓn | mak-ed'-ohn)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a Macedon (Macedonian), i.e. inhabitant of Macedonia\nKJV: —of Macedonia, Macedonian.\n\nCognate Group: G3106 (call blessed), G3107 (blessed), G3108 (blessedness)\nVariants: Μακεδών",
    3111: "μάκελλον (mákellon | mak'-el-lon)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin (macellum)\nStrong's: a butcher's stall, meat market or provision-shop\nKJV: —shambles.\n\nCognate Group: G3111 (shambles)\nVariants: μάκελλον",
    3112: "μακράν (makrán | mak-ran')\n\nDerivation: feminine accusative case singular of G3117 (G3598 being implied)\nStrong's: at a distance (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —(a-)far (off), good (great) way off.\n\nSee: G3117 \nSee: G3598 \n\nCognate Group: G3113 (afar off), G3112 (far)\nVariants: μακράν \nHebrew Equivalents: H752 אָרֹךְ, H5274 נָעַל, H5493 סוּר, H6186 עָרַךְ, H7235 רָבָה, H7350 רָחֹוק, H7350 רָחֹוק, H7368 רָחַק, H7368 רָחַק",
    3113: "μακρόθεν (makróthen | mak-roth'-en)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3117\nStrong's: from a distance or afar\nKJV: —afar off, from far.\n\nSee: G3117 \n\nCognate Group: G3113 (afar off), G3112 (far)\nVariants: μακρόθεν \nHebrew Equivalents: H7350 רָחֹוק, H7350 רָחֹוק, H7368 רָחַק",
    3114: "μακροθυμέω (makrothyméō | mak-roth-oo-meh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3116\nStrong's: to be long-spirited, i.e. (objectively) forbearing or (subjectively) patient\nKJV: —bear (suffer) long, be longsuffering, have (long) patience, be patient, patiently endure.\n\nSee: G3116 \n\nCognate Group: G3114 (bear long), G3115 (longsuffering), G3116 (patiently)\nVariants: μακροθυμέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6186 עָרַךְ",
    3115: "μακροθυμία (makrothymía | mak-roth-oo-mee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3116\nStrong's: longanimity, i.e. (objectively) forbearance or (subjectively) fortitude\nKJV: —longsuffering, patience.\n\nSee: G3116 \n\nCognate Group: G3114 (bear long), G3115 (longsuffering), G3116 (patiently)\nVariants: μακροθυμία \nHebrew Equivalents: H753 אֹרֶךְ, H6187 עֵרֶךְ",
    3116: "μακροθυμώς (makrothymṓs | mak-roth-oo-moce')\n\nDerivation: adverb of a compound of G3117 and G2372\nStrong's: with long (enduring) temper, i.e. leniently\nKJV: —patiently.\n\nSee: G3117 \nSee: G2372 \n\nCognate Group: G3114 (bear long), G3115 (longsuffering), G3116 (patiently)\nVariants: μακροθυμώς, μακροθύμως",
    3117: "μακρός (makrós | mak-ros')\n\nDerivation: from G3372\nStrong's: long (in place (distant) or time (neuter plural))\nKJV: —far, long.\n\nSee: G3372 \n\nCognate Group: G3114 (bear long), G3115 (longsuffering), G3116 (patiently)\nVariants: μακρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H752 אָרֹךְ, H753 אֹרֶךְ, H7235 רָבָה, H7350 רָחֹוק, H7350 רָחֹוק, H7350 רָחֹוק",
    3118: "μακροχρόνιος (makrochrónios | mak-rokh-ron'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3117 and G5550\nStrong's: long-timed, i.e. long-lived\nKJV: —live long.\n\nSee: G3117 \nSee: G5550 \n\nCognate Group: G3114 (bear long), G3115 (longsuffering), G3116 (patiently)\nVariants: μακροχρόνιος",
    3119: "μαλακία (malakía | mal-ak-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3120\nStrong's: softness, i.e. enervation (debility)\nKJV: —disease.\n\nSee: G3120 \n\nCognate Group: G3120 (effeminate), G3119 (disease)\nVariants: μαλακία \nHebrew Equivalents: H611 אָסֹון, H2481 חֲלִי, H4341 מַכְאֹב, H8463 תַּחֲלוּא",
    3120: "μαλακός (malakós | mal-ak-os')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: soft, i.e. fine (clothing); figuratively, a catamite\nKJV: —effeminate, soft.\n\nCognate Group: G3120 (effeminate), G3119 (disease)\nVariants: μαλακός \nHebrew Equivalents: H7390 רַךְ",
    3121: "Μαλελεήλ (Maleleḗl | mal-el-eh-ale')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4111)\nStrong's: Maleleel (i.e. Mahalalel), an antediluvian\nKJV: —Maleleel.\nSee: H4111\n\nCognate Group: G3120 (effeminate), G3119 (disease)\nVariants: Μαλελεήλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4111 מַהֲלַלְאֵל",
    3122: "μάλιστα (málista | mal'-is-tah)\n\nDerivation: neuter plural of the superlative of an apparently primary adverb μάλα (very)\nStrong's: (adverbially) most (in the greatest degree) or particularly\nKJV: —chiefly, most of all, (e-)specially.\n\nCognate Group: G3120 (effeminate), G3119 (disease)\nVariants: μάλιστα",
    3123: "μᾶλλον (mâllon | mal'-lon)\n\nDerivation: neuter of the comparative of the same as G3122\nStrong's: (adverbially) more (in a greater degree)) or rather\nKJV: —+ better, X far, (the) more (and more), (so) much (the more), rather.\n\nSee: G3122 \n\nCognate Group: G3120 (effeminate), G3119 (disease)\nVariants: μᾶλλον",
    3124: "Μάλχος (Málchos | mal'-khos)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4429)\nStrong's: Malchus, an Israelite\nKJV: —Malchus.\nSee: H4429\n\nCognate Group: G3120 (effeminate), G3119 (disease)\nVariants: Μάλχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4428 מֶלֶךְ, H4429 מֶלֶךְ",
    3125: "μάμμη (mámmē | mam'-may)\n\nDerivation: of natural origin (\"mammy\")\nStrong's: a grandmother\nKJV: —grandmother.\n\nCognate Group: G3120 (effeminate), G3119 (disease)\nVariants: μάμμη",
    3126: "μαμμωνᾶς (mammōnâs | mam-mo-nas')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (confidence, i.e. wealth, personified)\nStrong's: mammonas, i.e. avarice (deified)\nKJV: —mammon.\n\nCognate Group: G3126 (mammon)\nVariants: μαμμωνᾶς, μαμωνᾶς",
    3127: "Μαναήν (Manaḗn | man-ah-ane')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Manaën, a Christian\nKJV: —Manaen.\n\nCognate Group: G3126 (mammon)\nVariants: Μαναήν",
    3128: "Μανασσῆς (Manassēs | man-as-sace')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4519)\nStrong's: Mannasses (i.e. Menashsheh), an Israelite\nKJV: —Manasses.\nSee: H4519\n\nCognate Group: G3126 (mammon)\nVariants: Μανασσῆς \nHebrew Equivalents: H4519 מְנַשֶּׁה",
    3129: "μανθάνω (manthánō | man-than'-o)\n\nDerivation: prolongation from a primary verb, another form of which, μαθέω, is used as an alternate in certain tenses\nStrong's: to learn (in any way)\nKJV: —learn, understand.\n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: μανθάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H502 אָלַף, H3045 יָדַע, H3286 יָעַף, H3925 לָמַד",
    3130: "μανία (manía | man-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3105\nStrong's: craziness\nKJV: —(+ make) X mad.\n\nSee: G3105 \n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: μανία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4895 מַשְׂטֵמָה",
    3131: "μάννα (mánna | man'-nah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4478)\nStrong's: manna (i.e. man), an edible gum\nKJV: —manna.\nSee: H4478\n\nCognate Group: G3131 (manna)\nVariants: μάννα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4478 מָן, H4503 מִנְחָה",
    3132: "μαντεύομαι (manteúomai | mant-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3105 (meaning a prophet, as supposed to rave through inspiration)\nStrong's: to divine, i.e. utter spells (under pretense of foretelling\nKJV: —by soothsaying.\n\nSee: G3105 \n\nCognate Group: G3131 (manna)\nVariants: μαντεύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H7080 קָסַם",
    3133: "μαραίνω (maraínō | mar-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: to extinguish (as fire), i.e. (figuratively and passively) to pass away\nKJV: —fade away.\n\nCognate Group: G3131 (manna)\nVariants: μαραίνω, μαραίνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3001 יָבֵשׁ",
    3134: "μαρὰν ἀθά (maràn athá | mar'-an ath'-ah)\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (meaning our Lord has come)\nStrong's: maranatha, i.e. an exclamation of the approaching divine judgment\nKJV: —Maran-atha.\n\nCognate Group: G3134 (Maran-atha)\nVariants: μαρὰν ἀθά, θά, μαράνα θά",
    3135: "μαργαρίτης (margarítēs | mar-gar-ee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from μάργαρος (a pearl-oyster)\nStrong's: a pearl\nKJV: —pearl.\n\nCognate Group: G3135 (pearl)\nVariants: μαργαρίτης",
    3136: "Μάρθα (Mártha | mar'-thah)\n\nDerivation: probably of Chaldee origin (meaning mistress)\nStrong's: Martha, a Christian woman\nKJV: —Martha.\n\nCognate Group: G3135 (pearl)\nVariants: Μάρθα",
    3137: "Μαρία (María | mar-ee'-ah) or Μαριάμ (Mariám | mar-ee-am')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4813)\nStrong's: Maria or Mariam (i.e. Mirjam), the name of six Christian females\nKJV: — Mary.\nSee: H4813\n\nCognate Group: G3135 (pearl)\nVariants: Μαρία, Μαριάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4813 מִרְיָם",
    3138: "Μάρκος (Márkos | mar'-kos)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: Marcus, a Christian\nKJV: —Marcus, Mark.\n\nCognate Group: G3135 (pearl)\nVariants: Μάρκος, Μᾶρκος",
    3139: "μάρμαρος (mármaros | mar'-mar-os)\n\nDerivation: from μαρμαίρω (to glisten)\nStrong's: marble (as sparkling white)\nKJV: —marble.\n\nCognate Group: G3135 (pearl)\nVariants: μάρμαρος",
    3140: "μαρτυρέω (martyréō | mar-too-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3144\nStrong's: to be a witness, i.e. testify (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —charge, give (evidence), bear record, have (obtain, of) good (honest) report, be well reported of, testify, give (have) testimony, (be, bear, give, obtain) witness.\n\nSee: G3144 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: μαρτυρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5707 עֵד, H5749 עוּד, H6030 עָנָה",
    3141: "μαρτυρία (martyría | mar-too-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3144\nStrong's: evidence given (judicially or genitive case)\nKJV: —record, report, testimony, witness.\n\nSee: G3144 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: μαρτυρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4150 מֹועֵד, H5707 עֵד, H5715 עֵדוּת, H7717 שָׂהֵד",
    3142: "μαρτύριον (martýrion | mar-too'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G3144\nStrong's: something evidential, i.e. (genitive case) evidence given or (specially), the Decalogue (in the sacred Tabernacle)\nKJV: —to be testified, testimony, witness.\n\nSee: G3144 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: μαρτύριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H4150 מֹועֵד, H5707 עֵד, H5713 עֵדָה, H5715 עֵדוּת",
    3143: "μαρτύρομαι (martýromai | mar-too'-rom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G3144\nStrong's: to be adduced as a witness, i.e. (figuratively) to obtest (in affirmation or exhortation)\nKJV: —take to record, testify.\n\nSee: G3144 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: μαρτύρομαι",
    3144: "μάρτυς (mártys | mar'-toos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a witness (literally (judicially) or figuratively (genitive case)); by analogy, a \"martyr\"\nKJV: — martyr, record, witness.\n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: μάρτυς, πρωτόμαρτυς \nHebrew Equivalents: H5707 עֵד, H7717 שָׂהֵד",
    3145: "μασσάομαι (massáomai | mas-sah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from a primary μάσσω (to handle or squeeze)\nStrong's: to chew\nKJV: —gnaw.\n\nCognate Group: G3145 (gnaw)\nVariants: μασσάομαι, μασάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3899 לֶחֶם",
    3146: "μαστιγόω (mastigóō | mas-tig-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3148\nStrong's: to flog (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —scourge.\n\nSee: G3148 \n\nCognate Group: G3147 (scourge), G3148 (plague), G3146 (scourge)\nVariants: μαστιγόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3806 כָּתַשׁ, H5060 נָגַע, H5221 נָכָה, H5640 סָתַם",
    3147: "μαστίζω (mastízō | mas-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3149\nStrong's: to whip (literally)\nKJV: —scourge.\n\nSee: G3149 \n\nCognate Group: G3147 (scourge), G3148 (plague), G3146 (scourge)\nVariants: μαστίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5221 נָכָה",
    3148: "μάστιξ (mástix | mas'-tix)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G3145 (through the idea of contact)\nStrong's: a whip (literally, the Roman flagellum for criminals; figuratively, a disease)\nKJV: —plague, scourging.\n\nSee: G3145 \n\nCognate Group: G3147 (scourge), G3148 (plague), G3146 (scourge)\nVariants: μάστιξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4341 מַכְאֹב, H5061 נֶגַע, H5222 נֵכֶה, H6763 צֵלָע, H7626 שֵׁבֶט, H7752 שֹׁוט, H8201 שֶׁפֶט",
    3149: "μαστός (mastós | mas-tos')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3145\nStrong's: a (properly, female) breast (as if kneaded up)\nKJV: —pap.\n\nSee: G3145 \n\nCognate Group: G3147 (scourge), G3148 (plague), G3146 (scourge)\nVariants: μαστός, μασθός, μαζός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1730 דֹּוד, H7699 שַׁד",
    3150: "ματαιολογία (mataiología | mat-ah-yol-og-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3151\nStrong's: random talk, i.e. babble\nKJV: —vain jangling.\n\nSee: G3151 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: ματαιολογία",
    3151: "ματαιολόγος (mataiológos | mat-ah-yol-og'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3152 and G3004\nStrong's: an idle (i.e. senseless or mischievous) talker, i.e. a wrangler\nKJV: —vain talker.\n\nSee: G3152 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: ματαιολόγος",
    3152: "μάταιος (mátaios | mat'-ah-yos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3155\nStrong's: empty, i.e. (literally) profitless, or (specially), an idol\nKJV: —vain, vanity.\n\nSee: G3155 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: μάταιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H205 אָוֶן, H392 אַכְזִיב, H457 אֱלִיל, H1892 הֶבֶל, H2403 חַטָּאָה, H2600 חִנָּם, H3577 כָּזָב, H7386 רֵיק, H7723 שָׁוְא, H8163 שָׂעִיר, H8414 תֹּהוּ",
    3153: "ματαιότης (mataiótēs | mat-ah-yot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3152\nStrong's: inutility; figuratively, transientness; morally, depravity\nKJV: —vanity.\n\nSee: G3152 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: ματαιότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H1892 הֶבֶל, H1942 הַוָּה, H7385 רִיק, H7723 שָׁוְא",
    3154: "ματαιόω (mataióō | mat-ah-yo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3152\nStrong's: to render (passively, become) foolish, i.e. (morally) wicked or (specially), idolatrous\nKJV: —become vain.\n\nSee: G3152 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: ματαιόω, ματαιόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H205 אָוֶן, H1197 בָּעַר, H1891 הָבַל, H1892 הֶבֶל, H2600 חִנָּם, H7723 שָׁוְא, H7723 שָׁוְא, H7919 שָׂכַל, H8267 שֶׁקֶר",
    3155: "μάτην (mátēn | mat'-ane)\n\nDerivation: accusative case of a derivative of the base of G3145 (through the idea of tentative manipulation, i.e. unsuccessful search, or else of punishment)\nStrong's: folly, i.e. (adverbially) to no purpose\nKJV: —in vain.\n\nSee: G3145 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: μάτην",
    3156: "Ματθαῖος (Matthaîos | mat-thah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: a shorter form of G3164\nStrong's: Matthæus (i.e. Matthitjah), an Israelite and a Christian\nKJV: —Matthew.\n\nSee: G3164 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: Ματθαῖος, Μαθθαῖος",
    3157: "Ματθάν (Matthán | mat-than')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4977)\nStrong's: Matthan (i.e. Mattan), an Israelite\nKJV: —Matthan.\nSee: H4977\n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: Ματθάν \nHebrew Equivalents: H4977 מַתָּן",
    3158: "Ματθάτ (Matthát | mat-that')\n\nDerivation: probably a shortened form of G3161\nStrong's: Matthat (i.e. Mattithjah), the name of two Israelites\nKJV: —Mathat.\n\nSee: G3161 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: Ματθάτ, Μαθθάτ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4991 מַתָּת",
    3159: "Ματθίας (Matthías | mat-thee'-as)\n\nDerivation: apparently a shortened form of G3161\nStrong's: Matthias (i.e. Mattithjah), an Israelite\nKJV: —Matthias.\n\nSee: G3161 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: Ματθίας, Μαθθίας",
    3160: "Ματταθά (Mattathá | mat-tath-ah')\n\nDerivation: probably a shortened form of G3161 (compare G4992)\nStrong's: Mattatha (i.e. Mattithjah), an Israelite\nKJV: —Mattatha.\n\nSee: G3161 \nSee: G4992 \n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: Ματταθά \nHebrew Equivalents: H4992 מַתַּתָּה",
    3161: "Ματταθίας (Mattathías | mat-tath-ee'-as)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4993)\nStrong's: Mattathias (i.e. Mattithjah), an Israelite and a Christian\nKJV: —Mattathias.\nSee: H4993\n\nCognate Group: G3154 (become vain), G3152 (vain), G3151 (vain talker), G3155 (in vain), G3150 (vain jangling), G3153 (vanity)\nVariants: Ματταθίας \nHebrew Equivalents: H4993 מַתִּתְיָה",
    3162: "μάχαιρα (máchaira | makh'-ahee-rah)\n\nDerivation: probably feminine of a presumed derivative of G3163\nStrong's: a knife, i.e. dirk; figuratively, war, judicial punishment\nKJV: —sword.\n\nSee: G3163 \n\nCognate Group: G3162 (sword)\nVariants: μάχαιρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1270 בַּרְזֶל, H2595 חֲנִית, H2719 חֶרֶב, H3979 מַאֲכֶלֶת",
    3163: "μάχη (máchē | makh'-ay)\n\nDerivation: from G3164\nStrong's: a battle, i.e. (figuratively) controversy\nKJV: —fighting, strive, striving.\n\nSee: G3164 \n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: μάχη \nHebrew Equivalents: H4066 מָדֹון, H4421 מִלְחָמָה, H4682 מַצָּה, H6635 צָבָא, H7378 רִיב",
    3164: "μάχομαι (máchomai | makh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of an apparently primary verb\nStrong's: to war, i.e. (figuratively) to quarrel, dispute\nKJV: —fight, strive.\n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: μάχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2717 חָרַב, H2734 חָרָה, H3898 לָחַם, H5221 נָכָה, H5327 נָצָה, H7378 רִיב",
    3165: "μέ (mé | meh)\n\nDerivation: a shorter (and probably original) form of G1691\nStrong's: me\nKJV: —I, me, my.\n\nSee: G1691 \n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: μέ, ἐγώ",
    3166: "μεγαλαυχέω (megalauchéō | meg-al-ow-kheh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3173 and αὐχέω (to boast\nStrong's: akin to G837 and G2744); to talk big, i.e. be grandiloquent (arrogant, egotistic)\nKJV: —boast great things.\n\nSee: G3173 \nSee: G837 \nSee: G2744 \n\nCognate Group: G2314 (to fight against God), G3163 (fighting), G2313 (fight against God), G269 (not a brawler), G3164 (fight)\nVariants: μεγαλαυχέω, αὐχέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1361 גָּבַהּ, H6206 עָרַץ",
    3167: "μεγαλεῖος (megaleîos | meg-al-i'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3173\nStrong's: magnificent, i.e. (neuter, plural as noun) a conspicuous favor, or (subjectively) perfection\nKJV: —great things, wonderful works.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεγαλεῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1419 גָּדֹול, H1433 גֹּדֶל, H5949 עֲלִילָה",
    3168: "μεγαλειότης (megaleiótēs | meg-al-i-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3167\nStrong's: superbness, i.e. glory or splendor\nKJV: —magnificence, majesty, mighty power.\n\nSee: G3167 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεγαλειότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H7238 רְבוּ, H8597 תִּפְאָרָה",
    3169: "μεγαλοπρεπής (megaloprepḗs | meg-al-op-rep-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G3173 and G4241\nStrong's: befitting greatness or magnificence (majestic)\nKJV: —excellent.\n\nSee: G3173 \nSee: G4241 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεγαλοπρεπής \nHebrew Equivalents: H1346 גַּאֲוָה",
    3170: "μεγαλύνω (megalýnō | meg-al-oo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3173\nStrong's: to make (or declare) great, i.e. increase or (figuratively) extol\nKJV: —enlarge, magnify, shew great.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεγαλύνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1396 גָּבַר, H1431 גָּדַל, H5637 סָרַר, H6381 פָּלָא, H7232 רָבַב, H7235 רָבָה",
    3171: "μεγάλως (megálōs | meg-al'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3173\nStrong's: much\nKJV: —greatly.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεγάλως \nHebrew Equivalents: H1419 גָּדֹול, H6381 פָּלָא, H7230 רֹב",
    3172: "μεγαλωσύνη (megalōsýnē | meg-al-o-soo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from G3173\nStrong's: greatness, i.e. (figuratively) divinity (often God himself)\nKJV: —majesty.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεγαλωσύνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H155 אַדֶּרֶת, H1369 גְּבוּרָה, H1420 גְּדוּלָה, H1433 גֹּדֶל, H7238 רְבוּ, H8597 תִּפְאָרָה",
    3173: "μέγας (mégas | meg'-as)\n\nDerivation: (including the prolonged forms, feminine μεγάλη, plural μεγάλοι, etc.; compare also G3176, G3187)\nStrong's: big (literally or figuratively, in a very wide application)\nKJV: —(+ fear) exceedingly, great(-est), high, large, loud, mighty, + (be) sore (afraid), strong, X to years.\n\nSee: G3176 \nSee: G3187 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μέγας \nHebrew Equivalents: H117 אַדִּיר, H1361 גָּבַהּ, H1377 גְּבִירָה, H1419 גָּדֹול, H1433 גֹּדֶל, H1995 הָמֹון, H3372 יָרֵא, H3513 כָּבַד, H3882 לִוְיָתָן, H3974 מָאֹור, H4390 מָלֵא, H4766 מַרְבֶה, H6091 עָצָב, H6099 עָצוּם, H6381 פָּלָא, H6697 צוּר, H7218 רֹאשׁ, H7227 רַב, H7230 רֹב, H7235 רָבָה, H7235 רָבָה, H7679 שָׂגָא, H7690 שַׂגִּיא",
    3174: "μέγεθος (mégethos | meg'-eth-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3173\nStrong's: magnitude (figuratively)\nKJV: —greatness.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μέγεθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1363 גֹּבַהּ, H1419 גָּדֹול, H6967 קֹומָה",
    3175: "μεγιστᾶνες (megistânes | meg-is-tan'-es)\n\nDerivation: plural from G3176\nStrong's: grandees\nKJV: —great men, lords.\n\nSee: G3176 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεγιστᾶνες, μεγιστάν \nHebrew Equivalents: H117 אַדִּיר, H1419 גָּדֹול, H1907 הַדָּבָר, H7261 רַבְרְבָן, H8269 שַׂר",
    3176: "μέγιστος (mégistos | meg'-is-tos)\n\nDerivation: superlative of G3173\nStrong's: greatest or very great\nKJV: —exceeding great.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μέγιστος G3492",
    3177: "μεθερμηνεύω (methermēneúō | meth-er-mane-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G2059\nStrong's: to explain over, i.e. translate\nKJV: —(by) interpret(-ation).\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G2059 \n\nCognate Group: G3168 (magnificence), G3171 (greatly), G3167 (great things), G3174 (greatness), G3173 (exceedingly), G3169 (excellent), G3172 (majesty), G3170 (enlarge)\nVariants: μεθερμηνεύω",
    3178: "μέθη (méthē | meth'-ay)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: an intoxicant, i.e. (by implication) intoxication\nKJV: —drunkenness.\n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μέθη \nHebrew Equivalents: H6071 עָסִיס, H7937 שָׁכַר, H7941 שֵׁכָר",
    3179: "μεθίστημι (methístēmi | meth-is'-tay-mee)\n\n, or (1 Cor. 13:2) UP9875: LEXEME NOT FOUND FOR STRONGS 3179 lemma_no 1 \n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G2476\nStrong's: to transfer, i.e. carry away, depose or (figuratively) exchange, seduce\nKJV: —put out, remove, translate, turn away.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μεθίστημι, μεθιστάνω, μεθίστημι/μεθιστάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4185 מוּשׁ, H4191 מוּת, H4529 מָסָה, H5493 סוּר, H5710 עָדָה, H6437 פָּנָה",
    3180: "μεθοδεία (methodeía | meth-od-i'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3326 and G3593 (compare \"method\")\nStrong's: travelling over, i.e. travesty (trickery)\nKJV: —wile, lie in wait.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3593 \n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: μεθοδεία",
    3181: "μεθόριος (methórios | meth-or'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G3725\nStrong's: bounded alongside, i.e. contiguous (neuter plural as noun, frontier)\nKJV: —border.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3725 \n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: μεθόριος, μεθόριον G3498",
    3182: "μεθύσκω (methýskō | meth-oos'-ko)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged (transitive) form of G3184\nStrong's: to intoxicate\nKJV: —be drunk(-en).\n\nSee: G3184 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μεθύσκω, μεθύσκομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1986 הָלַם, H7235 רָבָה, H7647 שָׂבָע, H7783 שׁוּק, H7910 שִׁכֹּור, H7937 שָׁכַר, H7937 שָׁכַר, H8354 שָׁתָה",
    3183: "μέθυσος (méthysos | meth'-oo-sos)\n\nDerivation: from G3184\nStrong's: tipsy, i.e. (as noun) a sot\nKJV: —drunkard.\n\nSee: G3184 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μέθυσος",
    3184: "μεθύω (methýō | meth-oo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from another form of G3178\nStrong's: to drink to intoxication, i.e. get drunk\nKJV: —drink well, make (be) drunk(-en).\n\nSee: G3178 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μεθύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7301 רָוָה, H7937 שָׁכַר",
    3185: "μεῖζον (meîzon | mide'-zon)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G3187\nStrong's: (adverbially) in greater degree\nKJV: —the more.\n\nSee: G3187 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μεῖζον, μείζων",
    3186: "μειζότερος (meizóteros | mide-zot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: continued comparative of G3187\nStrong's: still larger (figuratively)\nKJV: —greater.\n\nSee: G3187 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μειζότερος",
    3187: "μείζων (meízōn | mide'-zone)\n\nDerivation: irregular comparative of G3173\nStrong's: larger (literally or figuratively, specially, in age)\nKJV: —elder, greater(-est), more.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μείζων, μέγας",
    3188: "μέλαν (mélan | mel'-an)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G3189 as noun\nStrong's: ink\nKJV: —ink.\n\nSee: G3189 \n\nCognate Group: G3184 (drink well), G3183 (drunkard), G3182 (be drunk), G3178 (drunkenness)\nVariants: μέλαν, μέλας",
    3189: "μέλας (mélas | mel'-as)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: black\nKJV: —black.\n\nCognate Group: G3189 (black)\nVariants: μέλας \nHebrew Equivalents: H2448 חַכְלִלוּת, H7838 שָׁחֹר",
    3190: "Μελεᾶς (Meleâs | mel-eh-as')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Meleas, an Israelite\nKJV: —Meleas.\n\nCognate Group: G3189 (black)\nVariants: Μελεᾶς, Μελεά \nHebrew Equivalents: H4395 מְלֵאָה",
    3191: "μελετάω (meletáō | mel-et-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G3199\nStrong's: to take care of, i.e. (by implication) revolve in the mind\nKJV: —imagine, (pre-)meditate.\n\nSee: G3199 \n\nCognate Group: G3189 (black)\nVariants: μελετάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H1696 דָבַר, H1898 הָגָה, H7878 שִׂיחַ, H8159 שָׁעָה, H8173 שָׁעַע",
    3192: "μέλι (méli | mel'-ee)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: honey\nKJV: —honey.\n\nCognate Group: G3192 (honey)\nVariants: μέλι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1706 דְּבַשׁ, H5317 נֹפֶת",
    3193: "μελίσσιος (melíssios | mel-is'-see-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3192\nStrong's: relating to honey, i.e. bee (comb)\nKJV: —honeycomb.\n\nSee: G3192 \n\nCognate Group: G3192 (honey)\nVariants: μελίσσιος, μελισσεῖον, μελισσῖον G3511, G3512, G3513",
    3194: "Μελίτη (Melítē | mel-ee'-tay)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Melita, an island in the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Melita.\n\nCognate Group: G3192 (honey)\nVariants: Μελίτη, Μελιτήνη",
    3195: "μέλλω (méllō | mel'-lo)\n\nDerivation: a strengthened form of G3199 (through the idea of expectation)\nStrong's: to intend, i.e. be about to be, do, or suffer something (of persons or things, especially events; in the sense of purpose, duty, necessity, probability, possibility, or hesitation)\nKJV: —about, after that, be (almost), (that which is, things, + which was for) to come, intend, was to (be), mean, mind, be at the point, (be) ready, + return, shall (begin), (which, that) should (after, afterwards, hereafter) tarry, which was for, will, would, be yet.\n\nSee: G3199 \n\nCognate Group: G3192 (honey)\nVariants: μέλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H314 אַחֲרֹון, H2656 חֵפֶץ, H6264 עָתִיד",
    3196: "μέλος (mélos | mel'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a limb or part of the body\nKJV: —member.\n\nCognate Group: G3196 (member)\nVariants: μέλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1899 הֶגֶה, H5409 נֵתַח",
    3197: "Μελχί (Melchí | mel-khee')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4428 with pronominal suffix, my king)\nStrong's: Melchi (i.e. Malki), the name of two Israelites\nKJV: —Melchi.\nSee: H4428\n\nCognate Group: G3196 (member)\nVariants: Μελχί \nHebrew Equivalents: H4428 מֶלֶךְ",
    3198: "Μελχισεδέκ (Melchisedék | mel-khis-ed-ek')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4442)\nStrong's: Melchisedek (i.e. Malkitsedek), a patriarch\nKJV: —Melchisedec.\nSee: H4442\n\nCognate Group: G3198 (Melchisedec)\nVariants: Μελχισεδέκ, Μελχισέδεκ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4442 מַלְכִּי־צֶדֶק",
    3199: "μέλω (mélō | mel'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to be of interest to, i.e. to concern (only third person singular present indicative used impersonally, it matters)\nKJV: —(take) care.\n\nCognate Group: G3198 (Melchisedec)\nVariants: μέλω, μέλει \nHebrew Equivalents: H2656 חֵפֶץ",
    3200: "μεμβράνα (membrána | mem-bran'-ah)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin (\"membrane\")\nStrong's: a (written) sheep-skin\nKJV: —parchment.\n\nCognate Group: G3198 (Melchisedec)\nVariants: μεμβράνα",
    3201: "μέμφομαι (mémphomai | mem'-fom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of an apparently primary verb\nStrong's: to blame\nKJV: —find fault.\n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: μέμφομαι",
    3202: "μεμψίμοιρος (mempsímoiros | mem-psim'-oy-ros)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G3201 and μοῖρα (fate\nStrong's: akin to the base of G3313); blaming fate, i.e. querulous (discontented)\nKJV: —complainer.\n\nSee: G3201 \nSee: G3313 \n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: μεμψίμοιρος",
    3303: "μέν (mén | men)\n\nDerivation: a primary particle\nStrong's: properly, indicative of affirmation or concession (in fact); usually followed by a contrasted clause with G1161 (this one, the former, etc.)\nKJV: —even, indeed, so, some, truly, verily. Often compounded with other particles in an intensive or asseverative sense.\n\nSee: G1161 \n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: μέν",
    3304: "μενοῦνγε (menoûnge | men-oon'-geh)\n\nDerivation: from G3203 and G3767 and G1065\nStrong's: so then at least\nKJV: —nay but, yea doubtless (rather, verily).\n\nSee: G3203 \nSee: G3767 \nSee: G1065 \n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: μενοῦνγε, μενοῦν, μενοῦν/μενοῦνγε",
    3305: "μέντοι (méntoi | men'-toy)\n\nDerivation: from G3203 and G5104\nStrong's: indeed though, i.e. however\nKJV: —also, but, howbeit, nevertheless, yet.\n\nSee: G3203 \nSee: G5104 \n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: μέντοι",
    3306: "μένω (ménō | men'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to stay (in a given place, state, relation or expectancy)\nKJV: —abide, continue, dwell, endure, be present, remain, stand, tarry (for), X thine own.\n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: μένω \nHebrew Equivalents: H309 אָחַר, H1961 הָיָה, H2442 חָכָה, H3176 יָחַל, H3318 יָצָא, H3427 יָשַׁב, H3559 כּוּן, H3803 כָּתַר, H3885 לוּן, H5710 עָדָה, H5975 עָמַד, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6565 פָּרַר, H6960 קָוָה, H6965 קוּם, H7121 קָרָא",
    3307: "μερίζω (merízō | mer-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3313\nStrong's: to part, i.e. (literally) to apportion, bestow, share, or (figuratively) to disunite, differ\nKJV: —deal, be difference between, distribute, divide, give participle\n\nSee: G3313 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: μερίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1366 גְּבוּל, H2505 חָלַק, H2673 חָצָה, H3789 כָּתַב, H5157 נָחַל",
    3308: "μέριμνα (mérimna | mer'-im-nah)\n\nDerivation: from G3307 (through the idea of distraction)\nStrong's: solicitude\nKJV: —care.\n\nSee: G3307 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: μέριμνα \nHebrew Equivalents: H3052 יְהַב",
    3309: "μεριμνάω (merimnáō | mer-im-nah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3308\nStrong's: to be anxious about\nKJV: —(be, have) care(-ful), take thought.\n\nSee: G3308 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: μεριμνάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1672 דָּאַג, H3707 כַּעַס, H6089 עֶצֶב, H6213 עָשָׂה, H7264 רָגַז, H8159 שָׁעָה",
    3310: "μερίς (merís | mer-ece')\n\nDerivation: feminine of G3313\nStrong's: a portion, i.e. province, share or (abstractly) participation\nKJV: —part (X -akers).\n\nSee: G3313 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: μερίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H2465 חֶלֶד, H2513 חֶלְקָה, H4490 מָנָה, H4864 מַשְׂאֵת, H5106 נוּא, H5159 נַחֲלָה, H5409 נֵתַח, H6390 פְּלַגָּה, H7079 קְנָת",
    3311: "μερισμός (merismós | mer-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3307\nStrong's: a separation or distribution\nKJV: —dividing asunder, gift.\n\nSee: G3307 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: μερισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4255 מַחְלְקָה, H4256 מַחֲלֹקֶת",
    3312: "μεριστής (meristḗs | mer-is-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G3307\nStrong's: an apportioner (administrator)\nKJV: —divider.\n\nSee: G3307 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: μεριστής",
    3313: "μέρος (méros | mer'-os)\n\nDerivation: from an obsolete but more primary form of μείρομαι (to get as a section or allotment)\nStrong's: a division or share (literally or figuratively, in a wide application)\nKJV: —behalf, course, coast, craft, particular (+ -ly), part (+ -ly), piece, portion, respect, side, some sort(-what).\n\nCognate Group: G3313 (behalf)\nVariants: μέρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H958 בָּזָא, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2465 חֶלֶד, H2549 חֲמִישִׁי, H3027 יָד, H3411 יְרֵכָה, H4136 מוּל, H4327 מִין, H4740 מַקְצֹועַ, H5159 נַחֲלָה, H5642 סְתַר, H5676 עֵבֶר, H6285 פֵּאָה, H6418 פֶּלֶךְ, H6471 פַּעַם, H6654 צַד, H6763 צֵלָע, H7079 קְנָת, H7255 רֹבַע, H7307 רוּחַ, H8193 שָׂפָה, H8532 תְּלָת",
    3314: "μεσημβρία (mesēmbría | mes-ame-bree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3319 and G2250\nStrong's: midday; by implication, the south\nKJV: —noon, south.\n\nSee: G3319 \nSee: G2250 \n\nCognate Group: G3313 (behalf)\nVariants: μεσημβρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H216 אֹור, H5045 נֶגֶב, H6256 עֵת, H6672 צֹהַר",
    3315: "μεσιτεύω (mesiteúō | mes-it-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3316\nStrong's: to interpose (as arbiter), i.e (by implication) to ratify (as surety)\nKJV: —confirm.\n\nSee: G3316 \n\nCognate Group: G3316 (mediator), G3315 (confirm)\nVariants: μεσιτεύω",
    3316: "μεσίτης (mesítēs | mes-ee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G3319\nStrong's: a go-between, i.e. (simply) an internunciator, or (by implication) a reconciler (intercessor)\nKJV: —mediator.\n\nSee: G3319 \n\nCognate Group: G3316 (mediator), G3315 (confirm)\nVariants: μεσίτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H996 בֵּין",
    3317: "μεσονύκτιον (mesonýktion | mes-on-ook'-tee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of compound of G3319 and G3571\nStrong's: midnight (especially as a watch)\nKJV: —midnight.\n\nSee: G3319 \nSee: G3571 \n\nCognate Group: G3316 (mediator), G3315 (confirm)\nVariants: μεσονύκτιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2676 חָצֹות, H2677 חֵצִי, H5399 נֶשֶׁף",
    3318: "Μεσοποταμία (Mesopotamía | mes-op-ot-am-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3319 and G4215\nStrong's: Mesopotamia (as lying between the Euphrates and the Tigris; compare 0763), a region of Asia\nKJV: —Mesopotamia.\n\nSee: G3319 \nSee: G4215 \nSee: H763\n\nCognate Group: G3316 (mediator), G3315 (confirm)\nVariants: Μεσοποταμία",
    3319: "μέσος (mésos | mes'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3326\nStrong's: middle (as an adjective or (neuter) noun)\nKJV: —among, X before them, between, + forth, mid(-day, -night), midst, way.\n\nSee: G3326 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μέσος \nHebrew Equivalents: H996 בֵּין, H1460 גֵּו, H2677 חֵצִי, H7079 קְנָת, H7130 קֶרֶב, H8432 תָּוֶךְ",
    3320: "μεσότοιχον (mesótoichon | mes-ot'-oy-khon)\n\nDerivation: from G3319 and G5109\nStrong's: a partition (figuratively)\nKJV: —middle wall.\n\nSee: G3319 \nSee: G5109 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεσότοιχον",
    3321: "μεσουράνημα (mesouránēma | mes-oo-ran'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed compound of G3319 and G3772\nStrong's: mid-sky\nKJV: —midst of heaven.\n\nSee: G3319 \nSee: G3772 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεσουράνημα",
    3322: "μεσόω (mesóō | mes-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3319\nStrong's: to form the middle, i.e. (in point of time), to be half-way over\nKJV: —be about the midst.\n\nSee: G3319 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεσόω, μεσάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2677 חֵצִי, H8484 תִּיכֹון, H8622 תְּקוּפָה",
    3323: "Μεσσίας (Messías | mes-see'-as)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4899)\nStrong's: the Messias (i.e. Mashiach), or Christ\nKJV: —Messias.\nSee: H4899\n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: Μεσσίας \nHebrew Equivalents: H4899 מָשִׁיחַ",
    3324: "μεστός (mestós | mes-tos')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: replete (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —full.\n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεστός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4390 מָלֵא",
    3325: "μεστόω (mestóō | mes-to'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3324\nStrong's: to replenish, i.e. (by implication) to intoxicate\nKJV: —fill.\n\nSee: G3324 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεστόω",
    3326: "μετά (metá | met-ah')\n\nDerivation: a primary preposition (often used adverbially)\nStrong's: properly, denoting accompaniment; \"amid\" (local or causal); modified variously according to the case (genitive association, or accusative succession) with which it is joined; occupying an intermediate position between G575 or G1537 and G1519 or G4314 ; less intimate than G1722 and less close than G4862)\nKJV: —after(-ward), X that he again, against, among, X and, + follow, hence, hereafter, in, of, (up-)on, + our, X and setting, since, (un-)to, + together, when, with (+ -out). Often used in composition, in substantially the same relations of participation or proximity, and transfer or sequence.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G575 \nSee: G1537 \nSee: G1519 \nSee: G1722 \nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μετά \nHebrew Equivalents: H309 אָחַר, H5973 עִם, H6256 עֵת",
    3327: "μεταβαίνω (metabaínō | met-ab-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and the base of G939\nStrong's: to change place\nKJV: —depart, go, pass, remove.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεταβαίνω",
    3328: "μεταβάλλω (metabállō | met-ab-al'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G906\nStrong's: to throw over, i.e. (middle voice figuratively) to turn about in opinion\nKJV: —change mind.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεταβάλλω, μεταβάλλομαι G3554 \nHebrew Equivalents: H2015 הָפַךְ, H2266 חָבַר, H2498 חָלַף, H6581 פָּשָׂה, H7725 שׁוּב",
    3329: "μετάγω (metágō | met-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G718\nStrong's: to lead over, i.e. transfer (direct)\nKJV: —turn about.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G718 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μετάγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5493 סוּר, H7617 שָׁבָה",
    3330: "μεταδίδωμι (metadídōmi | met-ad-id'-o-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G1325\nStrong's: to give over, i.e. share\nKJV: —give, impart.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G1325 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: μεταδίδωμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H7665 שָׁבַר",
    3331: "μετάθεσις (metáthesis | met-ath'-es-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3346\nStrong's: transposition, i.e. transferral (to heaven), disestablishment (of a law)\nKJV: —change, removing, translation.\n\nSee: G3346 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: μετάθεσις",
    3332: "μεταίρω (metaírō | met-ah'-ee-ro)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G142\nStrong's: to betake oneself, i.e. remove (locally)\nKJV: —depart.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G142 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: μεταίρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1540 גָּלָה, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5472 סוּג, H5493 סוּר",
    3333: "μετακαλέω (metakaléō | met-ak-al-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G2564\nStrong's: to call elsewhere, i.e. summon\nKJV: —call (for, hither).\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G2564 \n\nCognate Group: G1458 (accuse), G2822 (called), G2564 (bid), G1528 (call in), G4779 (call together), G3333 (call), G2821 (calling), G1462 (crime laid against), G1941 (appeal), G4341 (call), G1577 (assembly), G4292 (provoke), G479 (bid again)\nVariants: μετακαλέω, μετακαλέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H7121 קָרָא",
    3334: "μετακινέω (metakinéō | met-ak-ee-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G2795\nStrong's: to stir to a place elsewhere, i.e. remove (figuratively)\nKJV: —move away.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G2795 \n\nCognate Group: G3334 (move away), G2796 (moving), G2795 (move)\nVariants: μετακινέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4191 מוּת, H5079 נִדָּה, H5127 נוּס, H5128 נוּעַ, H5472 סוּג",
    3335: "μεταλαμβάνω (metalambánō | met-al-am-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G2983\nStrong's: to participate; genitive case, to accept (and use)\nKJV: —eat, have, be partaker, receive, take.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: μεταλαμβάνω",
    3336: "μετάλημψις (metálēmpsis | met-al'-ape-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3335\nStrong's: participation\nKJV: —taking.\n\nSee: G3335 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: μετάλημψις",
    3337: "μεταλλάσσω (metallássō | met-al-las'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G236\nStrong's: to exchange\nKJV: —change.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G236 \n\nCognate Group: G604 (reconcile), G1259 (reconcile), G2644 (reconcile), G3337 (change), G2643 (atonement), G525 (deliver), G465 (in exchange), G236 (change)\nVariants: μεταλλάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4191 מוּת",
    3338: "μεταμέλλομαι (metaméllomai | met-am-el'-lom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and the middle voice of G3199\nStrong's: to care afterwards, i.e. regret\nKJV: —repent (self).\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3199 \n\nCognate Group: G3338 (repent), G278 (without repentance)\nVariants: μεταμέλλομαι, μεταμέλομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H816 אָשַׁם, H5098 נָהַם, H5162 נָחַם",
    3339: "μεταμορφόω (metamorphóō | met-am-or-fo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G3445\nStrong's: to transform (literally or figuratively, \"metamorphose\")\nKJV: —change, transfigure, transform.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3445 \n\nCognate Group: G3339 (change), G3445 (form), G3444 (form), G3446 (form)\nVariants: μεταμορφόω, μεταμορφόομαι",
    3340: "μετανοέω (metanoéō | met-an-o-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G3539\nStrong's: to think differently or afterwards, i.e. reconsider (morally, feel compunction)\nKJV: —repent.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3539 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: μετανοέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5162 נָחַם, H7725 שׁוּב",
    3341: "μετάνοια (metánoia | met-an'-oy-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3340\nStrong's: (subjectively) compunction (for guilt, including reformation); by implication, reversal (of (another's) decision)\nKJV: —repentance.\n\nSee: G3340 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: μετάνοια",
    3342: "μεταξύ (metaxý | met-ax-oo')\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and a form of G4862\nStrong's: betwixt (of place or person); (of time) as adjective, intervening, or (by implication) adjoining\nKJV: —between, mean while, next.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: μεταξύ",
    3343: "μεταπέμπω (metapémpō | met-ap-emp'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G3992\nStrong's: to send from elsewhere, i.e. (middle voice) to summon or invite\nKJV: —call (send) for.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3992 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: μεταπέμπω, μεταπέμπομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3947 לָקַח, H5148 נָחָה",
    3344: "μεταστρέφω (metastréphō | met-as-tref'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G4762\nStrong's: to turn across, i.e. transmute or (figuratively) corrupt\nKJV: —pervert, turn.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G4762 \n\nCognate Group: G1994 (come again), G390 (abide), G1995 (conversion), G2692 (overthrow), G1294 (perverse), G2690 (overthrow), G4762 (convert), G3344 (pervert), G654 (bring again), G391 (conversation)\nVariants: μεταστρέφω, μετατρέπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2015 הָפַךְ, H5437 סָבַב",
    3345: "μετασχηματίζω (metaschēmatízō | met-askh-ay-mat-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and a derivative of G4976\nStrong's: to transfigure or disguise; figuratively, to apply (by accommodation)\nKJV: —transfer, transform (self).\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G4976 \n\nCognate Group: G4976 (fashion), G3345 (transfer), G4964 (conform to)\nVariants: μετασχηματίζω",
    3346: "μετατίθημι (metatíthēmi | met-at-ith'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G5087\nStrong's: to transfer, i.e. (literally) transport, (by implication) exchange, (reflexively) change sides, or (figuratively) pervert\nKJV: —carry over, change, remove, translate, turn.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: μετατίθημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3947 לָקַח, H4191 מוּת, H5472 סוּג, H5496 סוּת, H6381 פָּלָא, H7725 שׁוּב",
    3347: "μετέπειτα (metépeita | met-ep'-i-tah)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G1899\nStrong's: thereafter\nKJV: —afterward.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G1899 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: μετέπειτα",
    3348: "μετέχω (metéchō | met-ekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and G2192\nStrong's: to share or participate; by implication, belong to, eat (or drink)\nKJV: —be partaker, pertain, take part, use.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G1777 (in danger of), G2722 (have), G568 (be), G3353 (fellow), G3348 (be partaker), G472 (hold fast), G2192 (hold)\nVariants: μετέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6256 עֵת",
    3349: "μετεωρίζω (meteōrízō | met-eh-o-rid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3326 and a collateral form of G142 or perhaps rather G109 (compare \"meteor\")\nStrong's: to raise in mid-air, i.e. (figuratively) suspend (passively, fluctuate or be anxious)\nKJV: —be of doubtful mind.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G142 \nSee: G109 \n\nCognate Group: G3349 (be of doubtful mind)\nVariants: μετεωρίζω, μετεωρίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1361 גָּבַהּ, H5375 נָשָׂא, H7311 רוּם",
    3350: "μετοικεσία (metoikesía | met-oy-kes-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of a compound of G3326 and G3624\nStrong's: a change of abode, i.e. (specially), expatriation\nKJV: —X brought, carried(-ying) away (in-)to.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3624 \n\nCognate Group: G3349 (be of doubtful mind)\nVariants: μετοικεσία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1473 גֹּולָה, H4868 מִשְׁבָּת, H8537 תֹּם",
    3351: "μετοικίζω (metoikízō | met-oy-kid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3350\nStrong's: to transfer as a settler or captive, i.e colonize or exile\nKJV: —carry away, remove into.\n\nSee: G3350 \n\nCognate Group: G3349 (be of doubtful mind)\nVariants: μετοικίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1540 גָּלָה, H1644 גָּרַשׁ",
    3352: "μετοχή (metochḗ | met-okh-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G3348\nStrong's: participation, i.e. intercourse\nKJV: —fellowship.\n\nSee: G3348 \n\nCognate Group: G3349 (be of doubtful mind)\nVariants: μετοχή \nHebrew Equivalents: H2266 חָבַר",
    3353: "μέτοχος (métochos | met'-okh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3348\nStrong's: participant, i.e. (as noun) a sharer; by implication, an associate\nKJV: —fellow, partaker, partner.\n\nSee: G3348 \n\nCognate Group: G1777 (in danger of), G2722 (have), G568 (be), G3353 (fellow), G3348 (be partaker), G472 (hold fast), G2192 (hold)\nVariants: μέτοχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H977 בָּחַר, H1818 דָּם, H2270 חָבֵר",
    3354: "μετρέω (metréō | met-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3358\nStrong's: to measure (i.e. ascertain in size by a fixed standard); by implication, to admeasure (i.e. allot by rule)\nKJV: —figuratively, to estimate:—measure, mete.\n\nSee: G3358 \n\nCognate Group: G280 (without measure), G3354 (figuratively), G3358 (measure), G3357 (a little)\nVariants: μετρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4058 מָדַד, H4483 מְנָא",
    3355: "μετρητής (metrētḗs | met-ray-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G3354\nStrong's: a measurer, i.e. (specially), a certain standard measure of capacity for liquids\nKJV: —firkin.\n\nSee: G3354 \n\nCognate Group: G280 (without measure), G3354 (figuratively), G3358 (measure), G3357 (a little)\nVariants: μετρητής \nHebrew Equivalents: H1323 בַּת, H5429 סְאָה, H6288 פְּאֹרָה",
    3356: "μετριοπαθέω (metriopathéō | met-ree-op-ath-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of the base of G3357 and G3806\nStrong's: to be moderate in passion, i.e. gentle (to treat indulgently)\nKJV: —have compassion.\n\nSee: G3357 \nSee: G3806 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: μετριοπαθέω",
    3357: "μετρίως (metríōs | met-ree'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a derivative of G3358\nStrong's: moderately, i.e. slightly\nKJV: —a little.\n\nSee: G3358 \n\nCognate Group: G280 (without measure), G3354 (figuratively), G3358 (measure), G3357 (a little)\nVariants: μετρίως",
    3358: "μέτρον (métron | met'-ron)\n\nDerivation: an apparently primary word\nStrong's: a measure (\"metre\"), literally or figuratively; by implication, a limited portion (degree)\nKJV: — measure.\n\nCognate Group: G280 (without measure), G3354 (figuratively), G3358 (measure), G3357 (a little)\nVariants: μέτρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H374 אֵיפָה, H374 אֵיפָה, H520 אַמָּה, H1323 בַּת, H3027 יָד, H4060 מִדָּה, H4884 מְשׂוּרָה, H5429 סְאָה, H6957 קַו, H7991 שָׁלִישׁ",
    3359: "μέτωπον (métōpon | met'-o-pon)\n\nDerivation: from G3326 and ops (the face)\nStrong's: the forehead (as opposite the countenance)\nKJV: —forehead.\n\nSee: G3326 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μέτωπον \nHebrew Equivalents: H4696 מֵצַח",
    3360: "μέχρι (méchri | mekh'-ree) or μεχρίς (mechrís | mekh-ris')\n\nDerivation: from G3372\nStrong's: as far as, i.e. up to a certain point (as a preposition, of extent (denoting the terminus, whereas G891 refers especially to the space of time or place intervening) or conjunction)\nKJV: —till, (un-)to, until.\n\nSee: G3372 \nSee: G891 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μέχρι, μέχρις, μέχρι(ς), μέχρι/μέχρις",
    3361: "μή (mḗ | may)\n\nDerivation: a primary particle of qualified negation (whereas G3756 expresses an absolute denial)\nStrong's: (adverb) not, (conjunction) lest; also (as an interrogative implying a negative answer (whereas G3756 expects an affirmative one)) whether\nKJV: —any but (that), X forbear, + God forbid, + lack, lest, neither, never, no (X wise in), none, nor, (can-)not, nothing, that not, un(-taken), without. Often used in compounds in substantially the same relations. See also G3362, G3363, G3364, G3372, G3373, G3375, G3378 .\n\nSee: G3756 \nSee: G3362 \nSee: G3363 \nSee: G3364 \nSee: G3372 \nSee: G3373 \nSee: G3375 \nSee: G3378 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μή, μήγε, μήπου",
    3362: "ἐὰν μή (eàn mḗ | eh-an' may)\n\nDerivation: i.e. G1437 and G3361\nStrong's: if not, i.e. unless\nKJV: —X before, but, except, if, no, (if, + whosoever) not.\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G1437 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: ἐὰν μή, ἐάν, μή",
    3363: "ἵνα μή (hína mḗ | hin'-ah may)\n\nDerivation: i.e. G2443 and G3361\nStrong's: in order (or so) that not\nKJV: —albeit not, lest, that, no(-t, (-thing)).\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G2443 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: ἵνα μή, ἵνα, μή",
    3364: "οὐ μή (ou mḗ | oo may)\n\nDerivation: i.e. G3756 and G3361\nStrong's: a double negative strengthening the denial; not at all\nKJV: —any more, at all, by any (no) means, neither, never, no (at all), in no case (wise), nor ever, not (at all, in any wise). Compare G3378 .\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G3378 \nSee: G3756 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: οὐ μή, οὐ, μή G4024",
    3365: "μηδαμῶς (mēdamōs | may-dam-oce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a compound of G3361 and ἀμός (somebody)\nStrong's: by no means\nKJV: —not so.\n\nSee: G3361 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μηδαμῶς, μηθαμο͂ς \nHebrew Equivalents: H162 אֲהָהּ, H5921 עַל, H6030 עָנָה",
    3366: "μηδέ (mēdé | may-deh')\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G1161\nStrong's: but not, not even; in a continued negation, nor\nKJV: —neither, nor (yet), (no) not (once, so much as).\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G1161 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μηδέ",
    3367: "μηδείς (mēdeís | may-dice'), including the irregular feminine μηδεμία (mēdemía | may-dem-ee'-ah), and the neuter μηδέν (mēdén | may-den')\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G1520\nStrong's: not even one (man, woman, thing)\nKJV: —any (man, thing), no (man), none, not (at all, any man, a whit), nothing, + without delay.\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G1520 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μηδείς, μηθείς",
    3368: "μηδέποτε (mēdépote | may-dep'-ot-eh)\n\nDerivation: from G3366 and G4218\nStrong's: not even ever\nKJV: —never.\n\nSee: G3366 \nSee: G4218 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μηδέποτε",
    3369: "μηδέπω (mēdépō | may-dep'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3366 and G4452\nStrong's: not even yet\nKJV: —not yet.\n\nSee: G3366 \nSee: G4452 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μηδέπω",
    3370: "Μῆδος (Mēdos | may'-dos)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (compare H4074)\nStrong's: a Median, or inhabitant of Media\nKJV: —Mede.\nSee: H4074\n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: Μῆδος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4074 מָדַי",
    3371: "μηκέτι (mēkéti | may-ket'-ee)\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G2089\nStrong's: no further\nKJV: —any longer, (not) henceforth, hereafter, no henceforward (longer, more, soon), not any more.\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G2089 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μηκέτι",
    3372: "μῆκος (mēkos | may'-kos)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G3173\nStrong's: length (literally or figuratively) length.\n\nSee: G3173 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μῆκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H753 אֹרֶךְ",
    3373: "μηκύνω (mēkýnō | may-koo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3372\nStrong's: to lengthen, i.e. (middle voice) to enlarge\nKJV: —grow up.\n\nSee: G3372 \n\nCognate Group: G3359 (forehead)\nVariants: μηκύνω, μηκύνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1431 גָּדַל, H4900 מָשַׁךְ",
    3374: "μηλωτή (mēlōtḗ | may-lo-tay')\n\nDerivation: from μῆλον (a sheep)\nStrong's: a sheep-skin\nKJV: —sheepskin.\n\nCognate Group: G3374 (sheepskin)\nVariants: μηλωτή \nHebrew Equivalents: H155 אַדֶּרֶת",
    3375: "μήν (mḗn | mane)\n\nDerivation: a stronger form of G3303\nStrong's: a particle of affirmation (only with G2229); assuredly\nKJV: —+ surely.\n\nSee: G3303 \nSee: G2229 \n\nCognate Group: G3374 (sheepskin)\nVariants: μήν, μήν1, μήν/X",
    3376: "μήν (mḗn | mane)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: a month\nKJV: —month.\n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μήν, μήν2, μήν/N \nHebrew Equivalents: H2320 חֹדֶשׁ, H3391 יֶרַח",
    3377: "μηνύω (mēnýō | may-noo'-o)\n\nDerivation: probably from the same base as G3145 and G3415 (i.e. μάω, to strive)\nStrong's: to disclose (through the idea of mental effort and thus calling to mind), i.e. report, declare, intimate\nKJV: —shew, tell.\n\nSee: G3145 \nSee: G3415 \n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μηνύω",
    3378: "μὴ οὐκ (mḕ ouk | ouk)\n\n may ook\n\nDerivation: i.e. G3361 and G3756\nStrong's: as interrogative and negative, is it not that?\nKJV: —neither (followed by no), + never, not. Compare G3364 .\n\nSee: G3756 \nSee: G3364 \nSee: G3361 \n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μὴ οὐκ, μή, οὐ",
    3379: "μήποτε (mḗpote | may'-pot-eh) or μή ποτε (mḗ pote | may pot'-eh)\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G4218\nStrong's: not ever; also if (or lest) ever (or perhaps)\nKJV: —if peradventure, lest (at any time, haply), not at all, whether or not.\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G4218 \n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μήποτε",
    3380: "μήπω (mḗpō | may'-po)\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G4452\nStrong's: not yet\nKJV: —not yet.\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G4452 \n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μήπω",
    3381: "μήπως (mḗpōs | may'-pos) or μή πως (mḗ pōs | may tis)\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G4458\nStrong's: lest somehow\nKJV: —lest (by any means, by some means, haply, perhaps).\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G4458 \n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μήπως G3590, G3610",
    3382: "μηρός (mērós | may-ros')\n\nDerivation: perhaps a primary word\nStrong's: a thigh\nKJV: —thigh.\n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μηρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1121 בֵּן, H1290 בֶּרֶךְ, H3409 יָרֵךְ, H3689 כֶּסֶל",
    3383: "μήτε (mḗte | may'-teh)\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G5037\nStrong's: not too, i.e. (in continued negation) neither or nor; also, not even\nKJV: —neither, (n-)or, so as much.\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G5037 \n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: μήτε",
    3384: "μήτηρ (mḗtēr | may'-tare)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a \"mother\" (literally or figuratively, immediate or remote)\nKJV: —mother.\n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μήτηρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H517 אֵם, H802 אִשָּׁה, H1121 בֵּן, H3205 יָלַד",
    3385: "μήτι (mḗti | may'-tee)\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and the neuter of G5100\nStrong's: whether at all\nKJV: —not (the particle usually not expressed, except by the form of the question).\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G5100 \n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μήτι",
    3386: "μήτιγε (mḗtige | may'-tig-eh)\n\nDerivation: from G3385 and G1065\nStrong's: not at all then, i.e. not to say (the rather still)\nKJV: —how much more.\n\nSee: G3385 \nSee: G1065 \n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μήτιγε G3615",
    3387: "μήτις (mḗtis | may'-tis) or μή τις (mḗ tis | may tis)\n\nDerivation: from G3361 and G5100\nStrong's: whether any\nKJV: —any (sometimes unexpressed except by the simple interrogative form of the sentence).\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G5100 \n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μήτις, μήτι G3614",
    3388: "μήτρα (mḗtra | may'-trah)\n\nDerivation: from G3384\nStrong's: the matrix\nKJV: —womb.\n\nSee: G3384 \n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μήτρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6897 קֹבָה, H7356 רַחַם, H7358 רֶחֶם, H7698 שֶׁגֶר",
    3389: "μητραλῴας (mētralṓias | may-tral-o'-as)\n\nDerivation: from G3384 and the base of G257\nStrong's: a mother-thresher, i.e. matricide\nKJV: —murderer of mothers.\n\nSee: G3384 \nSee: G257 \n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μητραλῴας, μητρολῴας",
    3390: "μητρόπολις (mētrópolis | may-trop'-ol-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3384 and G4172\nStrong's: a mother city, i.e. \"metropolis\"\nKJV: —chiefest city.\n\nSee: G3384 \nSee: G4172 \n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μητρόπολις G3619 \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב, H517 אֵם, H1002 בִּירָה, H5892 עִיר, H5892 עִיר, H7149 קִרְיָא",
    3391: "μία (mía | mee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: irregular feminine of G1520\nStrong's: one or first\nKJV: —a (certain), + agree, first, one, X other.\n\nSee: G1520 \n\nCognate Group: G3384 (mother)\nVariants: μία, εἷς G1651",
    3392: "μιαίνω (miaínō | me-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: perhaps a primary verb\nStrong's: to sully or taint, i.e. contaminate (ceremonially or morally)\nKJV: —defile.\n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: μιαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2186 זָנַח, H2398 חָטָא, H2490 חָלַל, H2610 חָנֵף, H2930 טָמֵא",
    3393: "μίασμα (míasma | mee'-as-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3392 (\"miasma\")\nStrong's: (morally) foulness (properly, the effect)\nKJV: —pollution.\n\nSee: G3392 \n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: μίασμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1215 בֶּצַע, H6292 פִּגּוּל, H8251 שִׁקּוּץ",
    3394: "μιασμός (miasmós | mee-as-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3392\nStrong's: (morally) contamination (properly, the act)\nKJV: —uncleanness.\n\nSee: G3392 \n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: μιασμός",
    3395: "μίγμα (mígma | mig'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3396\nStrong's: a compound\nKJV: —mixture.\n\nSee: G3396 \n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: μίγμα, ἕλιγμα, σμῆγμα, σμίγμα",
    3396: "μίγνυμι (mígnymi | mig'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to mix\nKJV: —mingle.\n\nCognate Group: G3393 (pollution), G283 (undefiled), G3394 (uncleanness), G3392 (defile)\nVariants: μίγνυμι, μείγνυμι, μειγνύω, μίγνυμι/μιγνύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4414 מָלַח, H6148 עָרַב, H7896 שִׁית",
    3397: "μικρόν (mikrón | mik-ron')\n\nDerivation: masculine or neuter singular of G3398 (as noun)\nStrong's: a small space of time or degree\nKJV: —a (little) (while).\n\nSee: G3398 \n\nCognate Group: G305 (arise), G3397 (a), G2597 (come down)\nVariants: μικρόν, μικρός",
    3398: "μικρός (mikrós | mik-ros'), including the comparative μικρότερος (mikróteros | mik-rot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: small (in size, quantity, number or (figuratively) dignity)\nKJV: —least, less, little, small.\n\nCognate Group: G3398 (least), G3397 (a)\nVariants: μικρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H68 אֶבֶן, H2191 זְעֵיר, H4323 מִיכָל, H4591 מָעַט, H4592 מְעַט, H4592 מְעַט, H4705 מִצְעָר, H6810 צָעִיר, H6995 קֹטֶן, H6996 קָטָן, H6996 קָטָן, H6996 קָטָן, H6996 קָטָן, H7043 קָלַל, H8241 שֶׁצֶף",
    3399: "Μίλητος (Mílētos | mil'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Miletus, a city of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Miletus.\n\nCognate Group: G3398 (least), G3397 (a)\nVariants: Μίλητος",
    3400: "μίλιον (mílion | mil'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a thousand paces, i.e. a \"mile\"\nKJV: —mile.\n\nCognate Group: G3398 (least), G3397 (a)\nVariants: μίλιον",
    3401: "μιμέομαι (miméomai | mim-eh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from μῖμος (a \"mimic\")\nStrong's: to imitate\nKJV: —follow.\n\nCognate Group: G4831 (follower together), G3402 (follower), G3401 (follow)\nVariants: μιμέομαι",
    3402: "μιμητής (mimētḗs | mim-ay-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G3401\nStrong's: an imitator\nKJV: —follower.\n\nSee: G3401 \n\nCognate Group: G4831 (follower together), G3402 (follower), G3401 (follow)\nVariants: μιμητής",
    3403: "μιμνήσκω (mimnḗskō | mim-nace'-ko)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of G3415 (from which some of the tenses are borrowed)\nStrong's: to remind, i.e. (middle voice) to recall to mind\nKJV: —be mindful, remember.\n\nSee: G3415 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μιμνήσκω, μιμνῄσκομαι, μιμνῄσκω, μιμνήσκομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2142 זָכַר, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5545 סָלַח, H5782 עוּר, H6485 פָּקַד",
    3404: "μισέω (miséō | mis-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a primary μῖσος (hatred)\nStrong's: to detest (especially to persecute); by extension, to love less\nKJV: —hate(-ful).\n\nCognate Group: G3404 (hate)\nVariants: μισέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2194 זָעַם, H3988 מָאַס, H6887 צָרַר, H6965 קוּם, H7602 שָׁאַף, H8130 שָׂנֵא",
    3405: "μισθαποδοσία (misthapodosía | mis-thap-od-os-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3406\nStrong's: requital (good or bad)\nKJV: —recompence of reward.\n\nSee: G3406 \n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μισθαποδοσία",
    3406: "μισθαποδότης (misthapodótēs | mis-thap-od-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3409 and G591\nStrong's: a renumerator\nKJV: —rewarder.\n\nSee: G3409 \nSee: G591 \n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μισθαποδότης",
    3407: "μίσθιος (místhios | mis'-thee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3408\nStrong's: a wage-earner\nKJV: —hired servant.\n\nSee: G3408 \n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μίσθιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7916 שָׂכִיר",
    3408: "μισθός (misthós | mis-thos')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: pay for service (literally or figuratively), good or bad\nKJV: —hire, reward, wages.\n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μισθός \nHebrew Equivalents: H814 אֶשְׁכָּר, H868 אֶתְנַן, H4242 מְחִיר, H5801 עִזָּבֹון, H6467 פֹּעַל, H7939 שָׂכָר, H7939 שָׂכָר",
    3409: "μισθόω (misthóō | mis-tho'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3408\nStrong's: to let out for wages, i.e. (middle voice) to hire\nKJV: —hire.\n\nSee: G3408 \n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μισθόω, μισθόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3738 כָּרָה, H7939 שָׂכָר, H7939 שָׂכָר",
    3410: "μίσθωμα (místhōma | mis'-tho-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3409\nStrong's: a rented building\nKJV: —hired house.\n\nSee: G3409 \n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μίσθωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H866 אֶתְנָה, H868 אֶתְנַן, H5078 נֵדֶה, H5083 נָדָן",
    3411: "μισθωτός (misthōtós | mis-tho-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G3409\nStrong's: a wage-worker (good or bad)\nKJV: —hired servant, hireling.\n\nSee: G3409 \n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μισθωτός \nHebrew Equivalents: H7916 שָׂכִיר",
    3412: "Μιτυλήνη (Mitylḗnē | mit-oo-lay'-nay)\n\nDerivation: for μυτιλήνη (abounding in shellfish)\nStrong's: Mitylene (or Mytilene), a town on the island of Lesbos\nKJV: —Mitylene.\n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: Μιτυλήνη",
    3413: "Μιχαήλ (Michaḗl | mikh-ah-ale')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4317)\nStrong's: Michael, an archangel\nKJV: —Michael.\nSee: H4317\n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: Μιχαήλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4317 מִיכָאֵל",
    3414: "μνᾶ (mnâ | mnah)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a mna (i.e. mina), a certain weight\nKJV: — pound.\n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μνᾶ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1871 דַּרְכְּמֹון, H4488 מָנֶה",
    3415: "μνάομαι (mnáomai | mnah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of a derivative of G3306 or perhaps of the base of G3145 (through the idea of fixture in the mind or of mental grasp)\nStrong's: to bear in mind, i.e. recollect; by implication, to reward or punish\nKJV: —be mindful, remember, come (have) in remembrance. Compare G3403 .\n\nSee: G3403 \nSee: G3306 \nSee: G3145 \n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: μνάομαι",
    3416: "Μνάσων (Mnásōn | mnah'-sohn)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Mnason, a Christian\nKJV: —Mnason.\n\nCognate Group: G3408 (hire), G3410 (hired house), G3411 (hired servant), G3405 (recompence of reward), G3407 (hired servant), G489 (recompense), G3406 (rewarder), G3409 (hire)\nVariants: Μνάσων",
    3417: "μνεία (mneía | mni'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3415 or G3403\nStrong's: recollection; by implication, recital\nKJV: —mention, remembrance.\n\nSee: G3415 \nSee: G3403 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μνεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2142 זָכַר, H2145 זָכָר",
    3418: "μνῆμα (mnēma | mnay'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3415\nStrong's: a memorial, i.e. sepulchral monument (burial-place)\nKJV: —grave, sepulchre, tomb.\n\nSee: G3415 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μνῆμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6900 קְבוּרָה, H6913 קֶבֶר",
    3419: "μνημεῖον (mnēmeîon | mnay-mi'-on)\n\nDerivation: from G3420\nStrong's: a remembrance, i.e. cenotaph (place of interment)\nKJV: —grave, sepulchre, tomb.\n\nSee: G3420 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μνημεῖον \nHebrew Equivalents: H6900 קְבוּרָה, H6913 קֶבֶר",
    3420: "μνήμη (mnḗmē | mnay'-may)\n\nDerivation: from G3403\nStrong's: memory\nKJV: —remembrance.\n\nSee: G3403 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μνήμη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2143 זֵכֶר, H2146 זִכְרֹון",
    3421: "μνημονεύω (mnēmoneúō | mnay-mon-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3420\nStrong's: to exercise memory, i.e. recollect; by implication, to punish; also to rehearse\nKJV: —make mention; be mindful, remember.\n\nSee: G3420 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μνημονεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2142 זָכַר",
    3422: "μνημόσυνον (mnēmósynon | mnay-mos'-oo-non)\n\nDerivation: from G3421\nStrong's: a reminder (memorandum), i.e. record\nKJV: —memorial.\n\nSee: G3421 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μνημόσυνον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2142 זָכַר, H2143 זֵכֶר, H2146 זִכְרֹון",
    3423: "μνηστεύω (mnēsteúō | mnace-tyoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3415\nStrong's: to give a souvenir (engagement present), i.e. betroth\nKJV: —espouse.\n\nSee: G3415 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μνηστεύω, μνηστεύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H781 אָרַשׂ",
    3424: "μογιλάλος (mogilálos | mog-il-al'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3425 and G2980\nStrong's: hardly talking, i.e. dumb (tongue-tied)\nKJV: —having an impediment in his speech.\n\nSee: G3425 \nSee: G2980 \n\nCognate Group: G3420 (remembrance), G3422 (memorial), G3403 (be mindful), G3419 (grave), G3421 (make mention; be mindful), G3418 (grave), G3417 (mention)\nVariants: μογιλάλος, μογγιλάλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H483 אִלֵּם",
    3425: "μόγις (mógis | mog'-is)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a primary μόγος (toil)\nStrong's: with difficulty\nKJV: —hardly.\n\nCognate Group: G3433 (hardly), G3425 (hardly)\nVariants: μόγις",
    3426: "μόδιος (módios | mod'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a modius, i.e. certain measure for things dry (the quantity or the utensil)\nKJV: —bushel.\n\nCognate Group: G3433 (hardly), G3425 (hardly)\nVariants: μόδιος",
    3427: "μοί (moí | moy)\n\nDerivation: the simpler form of G1698\nStrong's: to me\nKJV: —I, me, mine, my.\n\nSee: G1698 \n\nCognate Group: G3433 (hardly), G3425 (hardly)\nVariants: μοί, ἐγώ",
    3428: "μοιχαλίς (moichalís | moy-khal-is')\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of the feminine of G3432\nStrong's: an adulteress (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —adulteress(-ous, -y).\n\nSee: G3432 \n\nCognate Group: G3432 (adulterer), G3431 (commit adultery), G3430 (adultery), G3429 (commit adultery), G3428 (adulteress)\nVariants: μοιχαλίς",
    3429: "μοιχάω (moicháō | moy-khah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3432\nStrong's: (middle voice) to commit adultery\nKJV: —commit adultery.\n\nSee: G3432 \n\nCognate Group: G3432 (adulterer), G3431 (commit adultery), G3430 (adultery), G3429 (commit adultery), G3428 (adulteress)\nVariants: μοιχάω, μοιχάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5003 נָאַף",
    3430: "μοιχεία (moicheía | moy-khi'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3431\nStrong's: adultery\nKJV: —adultery.\n\nSee: G3431 \n\nCognate Group: G3432 (adulterer), G3431 (commit adultery), G3430 (adultery), G3429 (commit adultery), G3428 (adulteress)\nVariants: μοιχεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5003 נָאַף, H5004 נִאֻף, H5005 נַאֲפוּף",
    3431: "μοιχεύω (moicheúō | moy-khyoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3432\nStrong's: to commit adultery\nKJV: —commit adultery.\n\nSee: G3432 \n\nCognate Group: G3432 (adulterer), G3431 (commit adultery), G3430 (adultery), G3429 (commit adultery), G3428 (adulteress)\nVariants: μοιχεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5003 נָאַף",
    3432: "μοιχός (moichós | moy-khos')\n\nDerivation: perhaps a primary word\nStrong's: a (male) paramour; figuratively, apostate\nKJV: —adulterer.\n\nCognate Group: G3432 (adulterer), G3431 (commit adultery), G3430 (adultery), G3429 (commit adultery), G3428 (adulteress)\nVariants: μοιχός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5003 נָאַף",
    3433: "μόλις (mólis | mol'-is)\n\nDerivation: probably by variation for G3425\nStrong's: with difficulty\nKJV: —hardly, scarce(-ly), + with much work.\n\nSee: G3425 \n\nCognate Group: G3433 (hardly), G3425 (hardly)\nVariants: μόλις",
    3434: "Μολόχ (Molóch | mol-okh')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H4432)\nStrong's: Moloch (i.e. Molek), an idol\nKJV: —Moloch.\nSee: H4432\n\nCognate Group: G3434 (Moloch)\nVariants: Μολόχ, Μόλοχ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4428 מֶלֶךְ, H4432 מֹלֶךְ",
    3435: "μολύνω (molýnō | mol-oo'-no)\n\nDerivation: probably from G3189\nStrong's: to soil (figuratively)\nKJV: —defile.\n\nSee: G3189 \n\nCognate Group: G3435 (defile), G3436 (filthiness)\nVariants: μολύνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H947 בּוּס, H1351 גָּאַל, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2610 חָנֵף, H2881 טָבַל, H2936 טָנַף, H6292 פִּגּוּל, H7693 שָׁגַל, H7901 שָׁכַב",
    3436: "μολυσμός (molysmós | mol-oos-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3435\nStrong's: a stain; i.e. (figuratively) immorality\nKJV: —filthiness.\n\nSee: G3435 \n\nCognate Group: G3435 (defile), G3436 (filthiness)\nVariants: μολυσμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2613 חֲנֻפָה",
    3437: "μομφή (momphḗ | mom-fay')\n\nDerivation: from G3201\nStrong's: blame, i.e. (by implication), a fault\nKJV: —quarrel.\n\nSee: G3201 \n\nCognate Group: G274 (blameless), G3437 (quarrel), G3201 (find fault), G3202 (complainer), G273 (blameless)\nVariants: μομφή, μέμψις",
    3438: "μονή (monḗ | mon-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G3306\nStrong's: a staying, i.e. residence (the act or the place)\nKJV: —abode, mansion.\n\nSee: G3306 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: μονή",
    3439: "μονογενής (monogenḗs | mon-og-en-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G3441 and G1096\nStrong's: only-born, i.e. sole\nKJV: —only (begotten, child).\n\nSee: G3441 \nSee: G1096 \n\nCognate Group: G3442 (with one eye), G3441 (alone), G3443 (be desolate), G3439 (only), G3440 (alone)\nVariants: μονογενής \nHebrew Equivalents: H3173 יָחִיד",
    3440: "μόνον (mónon | mon'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G3441 as adverb\nStrong's: merely\nKJV: —alone, but, only.\n\nSee: G3441 \n\nCognate Group: G3442 (with one eye), G3441 (alone), G3443 (be desolate), G3439 (only), G3440 (alone)\nVariants: μόνον, μόνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H389 אַךְ, H905 בַּד, H905 בַּד, H1610 גַּף, H7535 רַק",
    3441: "μόνος (mónos | mon'-os)\n\nDerivation: probably from G3306\nStrong's: remaining, i.e. sole or single; by implication, mere\nKJV: —alone, only, by themselves.\n\nSee: G3306 \n\nCognate Group: G3442 (with one eye), G3441 (alone), G3443 (be desolate), G3439 (only), G3440 (alone)\nVariants: μόνος",
    3442: "μονόφθαλμος (monóphthalmos | mon-of'-thal-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G3441 and G3788\nStrong's: one-eyed\nKJV: —with one eye.\n\nSee: G3441 \nSee: G3788 \n\nCognate Group: G3442 (with one eye), G3441 (alone), G3443 (be desolate), G3439 (only), G3440 (alone)\nVariants: μονόφθαλμος",
    3443: "μονόω (monóō | mon-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3441\nStrong's: to isolate, i.e. bereave\nKJV: —be desolate.\n\nSee: G3441 \n\nCognate Group: G3442 (with one eye), G3441 (alone), G3443 (be desolate), G3439 (only), G3440 (alone)\nVariants: μονόω, μονόομαι",
    3444: "μορφή (morphḗ | mor-fay')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the base of G3313 (through the idea of adjustment of parts)\nStrong's: shape; figuratively, nature\nKJV: —form.\n\nSee: G3313 \n\nCognate Group: G3339 (change), G3445 (form), G3444 (form), G3446 (form)\nVariants: μορφή \nHebrew Equivalents: H2122 זִיו, H6754 צֶלֶם, H8389 תֹּאַר, H8403 תַּבְנִית, H8544 תְּמוּנָה",
    3445: "μορφόω (morphóō | mor-fo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3444\nStrong's: to fashion (figuratively)\nKJV: —form.\n\nSee: G3444 \n\nCognate Group: G3339 (change), G3445 (form), G3444 (form), G3446 (form)\nVariants: μορφόω",
    3446: "μόρφωσις (mórphōsis | mor'-fo-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3445\nStrong's: formation, i.e. (by implication), appearance (semblance or (concretely) formula)\nKJV: —form.\n\nSee: G3445 \n\nCognate Group: G3339 (change), G3445 (form), G3444 (form), G3446 (form)\nVariants: μόρφωσις",
    3447: "μοσχοποιέω (moschopoiéō | mos-khop-oy-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3448 and G4160\nStrong's: to fabricate the image of a bullock\nKJV: —make a calf.\n\nSee: G3448 \nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G3448 (calf), G3447 (make a calf)\nVariants: μοσχοποιέω",
    3448: "μόσχος (móschos | mos'-khos)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened for όσχος (a shoot)\nStrong's: a young bullock\nKJV: —calf.\n\nCognate Group: G3448 (calf), G3447 (make a calf)\nVariants: μόσχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1241 בָּקָר, H5695 עֵגֶל, H6499 פַּר, H7794 שֹׁור",
    3449: "μόχθος (móchthos | mokh'-thos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3425\nStrong's: toil, i.e. (by implication) sadness\nKJV: —painfulness, travail.\n\nSee: G3425 \n\nCognate Group: G3448 (calf), G3447 (make a calf)\nVariants: μόχθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H205 אָוֶן, H3015 יָגֹון, H3018 יְגִיעַ, H5999 עָמָל, H6468 פְּעֻלָּה, H6531 פֶּרֶךְ, H8513 תְּלָאָה",
    3450: "μοῦ (moû | moo)\n\nDerivation: the simpler form of G1700\nStrong's: of me\nKJV: —I, me, mine (own), my.\n\nSee: G1700 \n\nCognate Group: G3448 (calf), G3447 (make a calf)\nVariants: μοῦ, ἐγώ",
    3451: "μουσικός (mousikós | moo-sik-os')\n\nDerivation: from Μοῦσα (a Muse)\nStrong's: \"musical\", i.e. (as noun) a minstrel\nKJV: —musician.\n\nCognate Group: G3448 (calf), G3447 (make a calf)\nVariants: μουσικός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2170 זְמָר, H7892 שִׁיר",
    3452: "μυελός (myelós | moo-el-os')\n\nDerivation: perhaps a primary word\nStrong's: the marrow\nKJV: —marrow.\n\nCognate Group: G3448 (calf), G3447 (make a calf)\nVariants: μυελός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2459 חֶלֶב, H4221 מֹחַ",
    3453: "μυέω (myéō | moo-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3466\nStrong's: to initiate, i.e. (by implication) to teach\nKJV: —instruct.\n\nSee: G3466 \n\nCognate Group: G3466 (mystery), G3453 (instruct)\nVariants: μυέω, μυέομαι",
    3454: "μῦθος (mŷthos | moo'-thos)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the same as G3453 (through the idea of tuition)\nStrong's: a tale, i.e. fiction (\"myth\")\nKJV: —fable.\n\nSee: G3453 \n\nCognate Group: G3454 (fable)\nVariants: μῦθος",
    3455: "μυκάομαι (mykáomai | moo-kah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of μύζω ( to \"moo\")\nStrong's: to bellow (roar)\nKJV: —roar.\n\nCognate Group: G3455 (roar)\nVariants: μυκάομαι",
    3456: "μυκτηρίζω (myktērízō | mook-tay-rid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the base of G3455 (meaning snout, as that whence lowing proceeds)\nStrong's: to make mouths at, i.e. ridicule\nKJV: —mock.\n\nSee: G3455 \n\nCognate Group: G3456 (mock), G1592 (deride)\nVariants: μυκτηρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H639 אַף, H936 בּוּז, H959 בָּזָה, H2048 הָתַל, H3931 לָעַב, H3932 לָעַג, H5006 נָאַץ",
    3457: "μυλικός (mylikós | moo-lee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G3458\nStrong's: belonging to a mill\nKJV: —mill(-stone).\n\nSee: G3458 \n\nCognate Group: G3459 (mill), G3457 (mill), G3458 (millstone)\nVariants: μυλικός",
    3458: "μύλος (mýlos | moo'-los)\n\nDerivation: probably ultimately from the base of G3433 (through the idea of hardship)\nStrong's: a \"mill\", i.e. (by implication), a grinder (millstone)\nKJV: —millstone.\n\nSee: G3433 \n\nCognate Group: G3459 (mill), G3457 (mill), G3458 (millstone)\nVariants: μύλος, μύλινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6400 פֶּלַח, H7347 רֵחֶה",
    3459: "μύλων (mýlōn | moo'-lone)\n\nDerivation: from G3458\nStrong's: a mill-house\nKJV: —mill.\n\nSee: G3458 \n\nCognate Group: G3459 (mill), G3457 (mill), G3458 (millstone)\nVariants: μύλων, μυλών G3686",
    3460: "Μύρα (Mýra | moo'-rah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Myra, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Myra.\n\nCognate Group: G3459 (mill), G3457 (mill), G3458 (millstone)\nVariants: Μύρα, Μύρρα",
    3461: "μυριάς (myriás | moo-ree'-as)\n\nDerivation: from G3463\nStrong's: a ten-thousand; by extension, a \"myriad\" or indefinite number\nKJV: —ten thousand.\n\nSee: G3463 \n\nCognate Group: G3459 (mill), G3457 (mill), G3458 (millstone)\nVariants: μυριάς \nHebrew Equivalents: H7233 רְבָבָה, H8336 שֵׁשׁ",
    3462: "μυρίζω (myrízō | moo-rid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3464\nStrong's: to apply (perfumed) unguent to\nKJV: —anoint.\n\nSee: G3464 \n\nCognate Group: G3464 (ointment), G3462 (anoint)\nVariants: μυρίζω",
    3463: "μύριοι (mýrioi | moo'-ree-oi)\n\nDerivation: plural of an apparently primary word (properly, meaning very many)\nStrong's: ten thousand; by extension, innumerably many\nKJV: —ten thousand.\n\nCognate Group: G3464 (ointment), G3462 (anoint)\nVariants: μύριοι, μυρίος, μυρίοι",
    3464: "μύρον (mýron | moo'-ron)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin (compare H4753, G4666)\nStrong's: \"myrrh\", i.e. (by implication) perfumed oil\nKJV: —ointment.\nSee: H4753\n\nSee: G4666 \n\nCognate Group: G3464 (ointment), G3462 (anoint)\nVariants: μύρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1314 בֶּשֶׂם, H4753 מֹר, H7545 רֹקַח, H8081 שֶׁמֶן",
    3465: "Μυσία (Mysía | moo-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Mysia, a region of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Mysia.\n\nCognate Group: G3464 (ointment), G3462 (anoint)\nVariants: Μυσία",
    3466: "μυστήριον (mystḗrion | moos-tay'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of μύω (to shut the mouth)\nStrong's: a secret or \"mystery\" (through the idea of silence imposed by initiation into religious rites)\nKJV: —mystery.\n\nCognate Group: G3466 (mystery), G3453 (instruct)\nVariants: μυστήριον, μυστῆριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H7328 רָז",
    3467: "μυωπάζω (myōpázō | moo-ope-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of the base of G3466 and ὤψ (the face\nStrong's: from G3700); to shut the eyes, i.e. blink (see indistinctly)\nKJV: — cannot see far off.\n\nSee: G3466 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3466 (mystery), G3453 (instruct)\nVariants: μυωπάζω",
    3468: "μώλωψ (mṓlōps | mo'-lopes)\n\nDerivation: from μῶλος (\"moil\"\nStrong's: probably akin to the base of G3433) and probably ὤψ (the face; from G3700); a mole (\"black eye\") or blow-mark\nKJV: —stripe.\n\nSee: G3433 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3468 (stripe)\nVariants: μώλωψ \nHebrew Equivalents: H2250 חַבּוּרָה",
    3469: "μωμάομαι (mōmáomai | mo-mah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3470\nStrong's: to carp at, i.e. censure (discredit)\nKJV: —blame.\n\nSee: G3470 \n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: μωμάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3971 מאוּם",
    3470: "μῶμος (mōmos | mo'-mos)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G3201\nStrong's: a flaw or blot, i.e. (figuratively) disgraceful person\nKJV: —blemish.\n\nSee: G3201 \n\nCognate Group: G3470 (blemish), G299 (without blame), G298 (blameless), G3469 (blame)\nVariants: μῶμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3971 מאוּם",
    3471: "μωραίνω (mōraínō | mo-rah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3474\nStrong's: to become insipid; figuratively, to make (passively, act) as a simpleton\nKJV: —become fool, make foolish, lose savour.\n\nSee: G3474 \n\nCognate Group: G3473 (foolish talking), G3471 (become fool), G3474 (foolish), G3472 (foolishness)\nVariants: μωραίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1197 בָּעַר, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    3472: "μωρία (mōría | mo-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3474\nStrong's: silliness, i.e. absurdity\nKJV: —foolishness.\n\nSee: G3474 \n\nCognate Group: G3473 (foolish talking), G3471 (become fool), G3474 (foolish), G3472 (foolishness)\nVariants: μωρία",
    3473: "μωρολογία (mōrología | mo-rol-og-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3474 and G3004\nStrong's: silly talk, i.e. buffoonery\nKJV: —foolish talking.\n\nSee: G3474 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G3473 (foolish talking), G3471 (become fool), G3474 (foolish), G3472 (foolishness)\nVariants: μωρολογία",
    3474: "μωρός (mōrós | mo-ros')\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G3466\nStrong's: dull or stupid (as if shut up), i.e. heedless, (morally) blockhead, (apparently) absurd\nKJV: —fool(-ish, X -ishness).\n\nSee: G3466 \n\nCognate Group: G3473 (foolish talking), G3471 (become fool), G3474 (foolish), G3472 (foolishness)\nVariants: μωρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H191 אֱוִיל, H5036 נָבָל, H7919 שָׂכַל, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    3475: "Μωσεύς (Mōseús | moce-yoos') or Μωσῆς (Mōsēs | mo-sace'), or Μωϋσῆς (Mōÿsēs | mo-oo-sace')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin; (H4872)\nStrong's: Moseus, Moses, or Mouses (i.e. Mosheh), the Hebrew lawgiver\nKJV: —Moses.\nSee: H4872\n\nCognate Group: G3475 (Moses)\nVariants: Μωσεύς, Μωσῆς, Μωϋσῆς \nHebrew Equivalents: H4872 מֹשֶׁה",
    3476: "Ναασσών (Naassṓn | nah-as-sone')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5177)\nStrong's: Naasson (i.e. Nachshon), an Israelite\nKJV: —Naasson.\nSee: H5177\n\nCognate Group: G3476 (Naasson)\nVariants: Ναασσών \nHebrew Equivalents: H5177 נַחְשֹׁון",
    3477: "Ναγγαί (Nangaí | nang-gah'-ee)\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin (compare H5052)\nStrong's: Nangæ (i.e. perhaps Nogach), an Israelite\nKJV: —Nagge.\nSee: H5052\n\nCognate Group: G3477 (Nagge)\nVariants: Ναγγαί \nHebrew Equivalents: H5052 נֹגַהּ",
    3478: "Ναζαρέθ (Nazaréth | nad-zar-eth') or Ναζαρέτ (Nazarét | nad-zar-et')\n\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Nazareth or Nazaret, a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Nazareth.\n\nCognate Group: G3478 (Nazareth), G3480 (Nazarene), G3479 (of Nazareth)\nVariants: Ναζαρέθ, Ναζαρά, Ναζαράθ, Ναζαράτ, Ναζαρέτ, Ναζαρά/Ναζαρέθ/Ναζαρέτ",
    3479: "Ναζαρηνός (Nazarēnós | nad-zar-ay-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G3478\nStrong's: a Nazarene, i.e. inhabitant of Nazareth\nKJV: —of Nazareth.\n\nSee: G3478 \n\nCognate Group: G3478 (Nazareth), G3480 (Nazarene), G3479 (of Nazareth)\nVariants: Ναζαρηνός",
    3480: "Ναζωραῖος (Nazōraîos | nad-zo-rah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G3478\nStrong's: a Nazoræan, i.e. inhabitant of Nazareth; by extension, a Christian\nKJV: —Nazarene, of Nazareth.\n\nSee: G3478 \n\nCognate Group: G3478 (Nazareth), G3480 (Nazarene), G3479 (of Nazareth)\nVariants: Ναζωραῖος",
    3481: "Ναθάν (Nathán | nath-an')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5416)\nStrong's: Nathan, an Israelite\nKJV: —Nathan.\nSee: H5416\n\nCognate Group: G3478 (Nazareth), G3480 (Nazarene), G3479 (of Nazareth)\nVariants: Ναθάν, Ναθάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5414 נָתַן, H5416 נָתָן",
    3482: "Ναθαναήλ (Nathanaḗl | nath-an-ah-ale')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5417)\nStrong's: Nathanaël (i.e. Nathanel), an Israelite and Christian\nKJV: —Nathanael.\nSee: H5417\n\nCognate Group: G3482 (Nathanael)\nVariants: Ναθαναήλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5417 נְתַנְאֵל",
    3483: "ναί (naí | nahee)\n\nDerivation: a primary particle of strong affirmation\nStrong's: yes\nKJV: —even so, surely, truth, verily, yea, yes.\n\nCognate Group: G3483 (even so)\nVariants: ναί",
    3484: "Ναΐν (Naḯn | nah-in')\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin (compare H4999)\nStrong's: Nain, a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Nain.\nSee: H4999\n\nCognate Group: G3484 (Nain)\nVariants: Ναΐν, Ναί̈ν, Ναΐν, Ναῖν \nHebrew Equivalents: H4999 נָאָה",
    3485: "ναός (naós | nah-os')\n\nDerivation: from a primary ναίω (to dwell)\nStrong's: a fane, shrine, temple \nKJV: —shrine, temple. Compare G2411 .\n\nSee: G2411 \n\nCognate Group: G3485 (shrine)\nVariants: ναός \nHebrew Equivalents: H197 אוּלָם, H1004 בַּיִת, H1687 דְּבִיר, H1964 הֵיכָל",
    3486: "Ναούμ (Naoúm | nah-oom')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5151)\nStrong's: Naum (i.e. Nachum), an Israelite\nKJV: —Naum.\nSee: H5151\n\nCognate Group: G3486 (Naum)\nVariants: Ναούμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5151 נַחוּם",
    3487: "νάρδος (nárdos | nar'dos)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (compare H5373)\nStrong's: \"nard\"\nKJV: —(spike-)nard.\nSee: H5373\n\nCognate Group: G3487 (nard)\nVariants: νάρδος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5373 נֵרְדְּ",
    3488: "Νάρκισσος (Nárkissos | nar'-kis-sos)\n\nDerivation: a flower of the same name, from νάρκη (stupefaction, as a \"narcotic\")\nStrong's: Narcissus, a Roman\nKJV: —Narcissus.\n\nCognate Group: G3488 (Narcissus)\nVariants: Νάρκισσος",
    3489: "ναυαγέω (nauagéō | now-ag-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3491 and G71\nStrong's: to be shipwrecked (stranded, \"navigate\"), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —make (suffer) shipwreck.\n\nSee: G3491 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G3489 (make shipwreck)\nVariants: ναυαγέω",
    3490: "ναύκληρος (naúklēros | now'-klay-ros)\n\nDerivation: from G3491 and G2819 (\"clerk\")\nStrong's: a captain\nKJV: —owner of a ship.\n\nSee: G3491 \nSee: G2819 \n\nCognate Group: G3490 (owner of a ship), G3492 (sailor), G3491 (ship)\nVariants: ναύκληρος",
    3491: "ναῦς (naûs | nowce)\n\nDerivation: from νάω or νέω (to float)\nStrong's: a boat (of any size)\nKJV: —ship.\n\nCognate Group: G3490 (owner of a ship), G3492 (sailor), G3491 (ship)\nVariants: ναῦς \nHebrew Equivalents: H592 אֲנִיָּה, H652 אֹפֶל, H2914 טְחֹר, H6041 עָנִי",
    3492: "ναύτης (naútēs | now'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G3491\nStrong's: a boatman, i.e. seaman\nKJV: —sailor, shipman.\n\nSee: G3491 \n\nCognate Group: G3490 (owner of a ship), G3492 (sailor), G3491 (ship)\nVariants: ναύτης",
    3493: "Ναχώρ (Nachṓr | nakh-ore')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5152)\nStrong's: Nachor, the grandfather of Abraham\nKJV: —Nachor.\nSee: H5152\n\nCognate Group: G3493 (Nachor)\nVariants: Ναχώρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5152 נָחֹור",
    3494: "νεανίας (neanías | neh-an-ee'-as)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3501\nStrong's: a youth (up to about forty years)\nKJV: —young man.\n\nSee: G3501 \n\nCognate Group: G3512 (youthful), G3495 (young man), G3494 (young man)\nVariants: νεανίας \nHebrew Equivalents: H970 בָּחוּר, H3206 יֶלֶד, H5288 נַעַר",
    3495: "νεανίσκος (neanískos | neh-an-is'-kos)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3494\nStrong's: a youth (under forty)\nKJV: —young man.\n\nSee: G3494 \n\nCognate Group: G3512 (youthful), G3495 (young man), G3494 (young man)\nVariants: νεανίσκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H582 אֱנֹושׁ, H970 בָּחוּר, H3206 יֶלֶד, H5288 נַעַר, H5958 עֶלֶם",
    3496: "Νεάπολις (Neápolis | neh-ap'-ol-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3501 and G4172\nStrong's: new town; Neapolis, a place in Macedonia\nKJV: —Neapolis.\n\nSee: G3501 \nSee: G4172 \n\nCognate Group: G3512 (youthful), G3495 (young man), G3494 (young man)\nVariants: Νεάπολις, Νεά πολις G3735, G3736",
    3497: "Νεεμάν (Neemán | neh-eh-man')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5283)\nStrong's: Neeman (i.e. Naaman), a Syrian\nKJV: —Naaman.\nSee: H5283\n\nCognate Group: G3497 (Naaman)\nVariants: Νεεμάν, Ναιμάν",
    3498: "νεκρός (nekrós | nek-ros')\n\nDerivation: from an apparently primary νέκυς (a corpse)\nStrong's: dead (literally or figuratively; also as noun)\nKJV: —dead.\n\nCognate Group: G3500 (deadness), G3498 (dead), G3499 (be dead)\nVariants: νεκρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2026 הָרַג, H2490 חָלַל, H4191 מוּת, H5038 נְבֵלָה, H6297 פֶּגֶר",
    3499: "νεκρόω (nekróō | nek-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3498\nStrong's: to deaden, i.e. (figuratively) to subdue\nKJV: —be dead, mortify.\n\nSee: G3498 \n\nCognate Group: G3500 (deadness), G3498 (dead), G3499 (be dead)\nVariants: νεκρόω",
    3500: "νέκρωσις (nékrōsis | nek'-ro-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3499\nStrong's: decease; figuratively, impotency\nKJV: —deadness, dying.\n\nSee: G3499 \n\nCognate Group: G3500 (deadness), G3498 (dead), G3499 (be dead)\nVariants: νέκρωσις",
    3501: "νέος (néos | neh'-os)\n\n\nDerivation: including the comparative νεότερος (neóteros | neh-o'-ter-os)\n; a primary word\nStrong's: \"new\", i.e. (of persons) youthful, or (of things) fresh; figuratively, regenerate\nKJV: —new, young.\n\nCognate Group: G3503 (youth), G3504 (novice), G3502 (young), G3556 (chicken), G3555 (brood), G3501 (new)\nVariants: νέος \nHebrew Equivalents: H24 אָבִיב, H1061 בִּכּוּר, H2319 חָדָשׁ, H3206 יֶלֶד, H5288 נַעַר, H5288 נַעַר, H6071 עָסִיס, H6810 צָעִיר, H6996 קָטָן",
    3502: "νεοσσός (neossós | neh-os-sos')\n\nDerivation: from G3501\nStrong's: a youngling (nestling)\nKJV: —young.\n\nSee: G3501 \n\nCognate Group: G3503 (youth), G3504 (novice), G3502 (young), G3556 (chicken), G3555 (brood), G3501 (new)\nVariants: νεοσσός, νοσσός \nHebrew Equivalents: H667 אֶפְרֹחַ, H1121 בֵּן, H1469 גֹּוזָל, H3206 יֶלֶד, H7064 קֵן",
    3503: "νεότης (neótēs | neh-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3501\nStrong's: newness, i.e. youthfulness\nKJV: —youth.\n\nSee: G3501 \n\nCognate Group: G3503 (youth), G3504 (novice), G3502 (young), G3556 (chicken), G3555 (brood), G3501 (new)\nVariants: νεότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H979 בְּחֻרֹות, H3208 יַלְדוּת, H5271 נָעוּר, H5290 נֹעַר, H5750 עֹוד, H5934 עָלוּם, H6812 צְעִירָה",
    3504: "νεόφυτος (neóphytos | neh-of'-oo-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G3501 and a derivative of G5453\nStrong's: newly planted, i.e. (figuratively) a young convert (\"neophyte\")\nKJV: —novice.\n\nSee: G3501 \nSee: G5453 \n\nCognate Group: G3503 (youth), G3504 (novice), G3502 (young), G3556 (chicken), G3555 (brood), G3501 (new)\nVariants: νεόφυτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5194 נֶטַע, H8363 שְׁתִל",
    3505: "Νέρων (Nérōn | ner'-ohn)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: Neron (i.e. Nero), a Roman emperor\nKJV: —Nero.\n\nCognate Group: G3503 (youth), G3504 (novice), G3502 (young), G3556 (chicken), G3555 (brood), G3501 (new)\nVariants: Νέρων G3746",
    3506: "νεύω (neúō | nyoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to \"nod\", i.e. (by analogy), signal\nKJV: —beckon.\n\nCognate Group: G3506 (beckon)\nVariants: νεύω",
    3507: "νεφέλη (nephélē | nef-el'-ay)\n\nDerivation: from G3509\nStrong's: properly, cloudiness, i.e. (concretely) a cloud\nKJV: —cloud.\n\nSee: G3509 \n\nCognate Group: G3509 (cloud), G3507 (cloud)\nVariants: νεφέλη \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב, H343 אֵיד, H3990 מַאֲפֵל, H5387 נָשִׂיא, H5688 עֲבֹת, H6051 עָנָן, H7834 שַׁחַק",
    3508: "Νεφθαλείμ (Nephthaleím | nef-thal-ime')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5321)\nStrong's: Nephthaleim (i.e. Naphthali), a tribe in Palestine\nKJV: —Nephthalim.\nSee: H5321\n\nCognate Group: G3509 (cloud), G3507 (cloud)\nVariants: Νεφθαλείμ, Νεφθαλίμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5321 נַפְתָּלִי",
    3509: "νέφος (néphos | nef'-os)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a cloud\nKJV: —cloud.\n\nCognate Group: G3509 (cloud), G3507 (cloud)\nVariants: νέφος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב, H374 אֵיפָה, H2091 זָהָב, H2822 חֹשֶׁךְ, H3713 כְּפֹור, H5387 נָשִׂיא, H6049 עָנַן, H6205 עֲרָפֶל, H7834 שַׁחַק",
    3510: "νεφρός (nephrós | nef-ros')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a kidney (plural), i.e. (figuratively) the inmost mind\nKJV: —reins.\n\nCognate Group: G3510 (reins)\nVariants: νεφρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3629 כִּלְיָה",
    3511: "νεωκόρος (neōkóros | neh-o-kor'-os)\n\nDerivation: from a form of G3485 and κορέω (to sweep)\nStrong's: a temple-servant, i.e. (by implication) a votary\nKJV: —worshipper.\n\nSee: G3485 \n\nCognate Group: G3511 (worshipper)\nVariants: νεωκόρος",
    3512: "νεωτερικός (neōterikós | neh-o-ter'-ik-os)\n\nDerivation: from the comparative of G3501\nStrong's: appertaining to younger persons, i.e. juvenile\nKJV: —youthful.\n\nSee: G3501 \n\nCognate Group: G3512 (youthful), G3495 (young man), G3494 (young man)\nVariants: νεωτερικός",
    3513: "νή (nḗ | nay)\n\nDerivation: probably an intensive form of G3483\nStrong's: a particle of attestation (accompanied by the object invoked or appealed to in confirmation); as sure as\nKJV: —I protest by.\n\nSee: G3483 \n\nCognate Group: G3512 (youthful), G3495 (young man), G3494 (young man)\nVariants: νή",
    3514: "νήθω (nḗthō | nay'-tho)\n\nDerivation: from νέω (of like meaning)\nStrong's: to spin\nKJV: —spin.\n\nCognate Group: G3514 (spin)\nVariants: νήθω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2901 טָוָה, H7806 שָׁזַר",
    3515: "νηπιάζω (nēpiázō | nay-pee-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3516\nStrong's: to act as a babe, i.e. (figuratively) innocently\nKJV: —be a child.\n\nSee: G3516 \n\nCognate Group: G1025 (babe), G3516 (babe), G3515 (be a child)\nVariants: νηπιάζω",
    3516: "νήπιος (nḗpios | nay'-pee-os)\n\nDerivation: from an obsolete particle νή- (implying negation) and G2031\nStrong's: not speaking, i.e. an infant (minor); figuratively, a simple-minded person, an immature Christian\nKJV: —babe, child (+ -ish).\n\nSee: G2031 \n\nCognate Group: G1025 (babe), G3516 (babe), G3515 (be a child)\nVariants: νήπιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2945 טַף, H3243 יָנַק, H5290 נֹעַר, H5768 עֹולֵל, H6612 פְּתִי",
    3517: "Νηρεύς (Nēreús | nare-yoos')\n\nDerivation: apparently from a derivative of the base of G3491 (meaning wet)\nStrong's: Nereus, a Christian\nKJV: —Nereus.\n\nSee: G3491 \n\nCognate Group: G1025 (babe), G3516 (babe), G3515 (be a child)\nVariants: Νηρεύς",
    3518: "Νηρί (Nērí | nay-ree')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5374)\nStrong's: Neri (i.e. Nerijah), an Israelite\nKJV: —Neri.\nSee: H5374\n\nCognate Group: G1025 (babe), G3516 (babe), G3515 (be a child)\nVariants: Νηρί \nHebrew Equivalents: H5374 נֵרִיָּה",
    3519: "νησίον (nēsíon | nay-see'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G3520\nStrong's: an islet\nKJV: —island.\n\nSee: G3520 \n\nCognate Group: G3519 (island), G3520 (island)\nVariants: νησίον",
    3520: "νῆσος (nēsos | nay'-sos)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G3491\nStrong's: an island\nKJV: —island, isle.\n\nSee: G3491 \n\nCognate Group: G3519 (island), G3520 (island)\nVariants: νῆσος \nHebrew Equivalents: H339 אִי",
    3521: "νηστεία (nēsteía | nace-ti'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3522\nStrong's: abstinence (from lack of food, or voluntary and religious); specially, the fast of the Day of Atonement\nKJV: — fast(-ing).\n\nSee: G3522 \n\nCognate Group: G3522 (fast), G3523 (fasting), G3521 (fast)\nVariants: νηστεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H6685 צֹום",
    3522: "νηστεύω (nēsteúō | nace-tyoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3523\nStrong's: to abstain from food (religiously)\nKJV: —fast.\n\nSee: G3523 \n\nCognate Group: G3522 (fast), G3523 (fasting), G3521 (fast)\nVariants: νηστεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6684 צוּם, H7121 קָרָא",
    3523: "νῆστις (nēstis | nace'-tis)\n\nDerivation: from the inseparable negative particle νή- (not) and G2068\nStrong's: not eating, i.e. abstinent from food (religiously)\nKJV: —fasting.\n\nSee: G2068 \n\nCognate Group: G3522 (fast), G3523 (fasting), G3521 (fast)\nVariants: νῆστις \nHebrew Equivalents: H2908 טְוָת",
    3524: "νηφάλεος (nēpháleos | nay-fal'-eh-os), or νηφάλιος (nēphálios | nay-fal'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3525\nStrong's: sober, i.e. (figuratively) circumspect\nKJV: —sober.\n\nSee: G3525 \n\nCognate Group: G3524 (sober), G3525 (be sober), G1594 (awake)\nVariants: νηφάλεος, νηφαλέος, νηφάλιος",
    3525: "νήφω (nḗphō | nay'-fo)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: to abstain from wine (keep sober), i.e. (figuratively) be discreet\nKJV: —be sober, watch.\n\nCognate Group: G3524 (sober), G3525 (be sober), G1594 (awake)\nVariants: νήφω",
    3526: "Νίγερ (Níger | neeg'-er)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: black; Niger, a Christian\nKJV: —Niger.\n\nCognate Group: G3524 (sober), G3525 (be sober), G1594 (awake)\nVariants: Νίγερ",
    3527: "Νικάνωρ (Nikánōr | nik-an'-ore)\n\nDerivation: probably from G3528\nStrong's: victorious; Nicanor, a Christian\nKJV: —Nicanor.\n\nSee: G3528 \n\nCognate Group: G3527 (Nicanor), G3534 (victory), G3528 (conquer), G3529 (victory), G5245 (more than conquer)\nVariants: Νικάνωρ",
    3528: "νικάω (nikáō | nik-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3529\nStrong's: to subdue (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —conquer, overcome, prevail, get the victory.\n\nSee: G3529 \n\nCognate Group: G3527 (Nicanor), G3534 (victory), G3528 (conquer), G3529 (victory), G5245 (more than conquer)\nVariants: νικάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2135 זָכָה, H2530 חָמַד, H5329 נָצַח",
    3529: "νίκη (níkē | nee'-kay)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: conquest (abstractly), i.e. (figuratively) the means of success\nKJV: —victory.\n\nCognate Group: G3527 (Nicanor), G3534 (victory), G3528 (conquer), G3529 (victory), G5245 (more than conquer)\nVariants: νίκη \nHebrew Equivalents: H5331 נֶצַח",
    3530: "Νικόδημος (Nikódēmos | nik-od'-ay-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G3534 and G1218\nStrong's: victorious among his people; Nicodemus, an Israelite\nKJV: —Nicodemus.\n\nSee: G3534 \nSee: G1218 \n\nCognate Group: G3530 (Nicodemus)\nVariants: Νικόδημος",
    3531: "Νικολαΐτης (Nikolaḯtēs | nik-ol-ah-ee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G3532\nStrong's: a Nicolaite, i.e. adherent of Nicolaus\nKJV: —Nicolaitane.\n\nSee: G3532 \n\nCognate Group: G3532 (Nicolaus), G3531 (Nicolaitane)\nVariants: Νικολαΐτης, Νικολαί̈της, Νικολαΐτης",
    3532: "Νικόλαος (Nikólaos | nik-ol'-ah-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3534 and G2994\nStrong's: victorious over the people; Nicolaus, a heretic\nKJV: —Nicolaus.\n\nSee: G3534 \nSee: G2994 \n\nCognate Group: G3532 (Nicolaus), G3531 (Nicolaitane)\nVariants: Νικόλαος",
    3533: "Νικόπολις (Nikópolis | nik-op'-ol-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3534 and G4172\nStrong's: victorious city; Nicopolis, a place in Macedonia\nKJV: —Nicopolis.\n\nSee: G3534 \nSee: G4172 \n\nCognate Group: G3532 (Nicolaus), G3531 (Nicolaitane)\nVariants: Νικόπολις",
    3534: "νῖκος (nîkos | nee'-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G3529\nStrong's: a conquest (concretely), i.e. (by implication) triumph\nKJV: —victory.\n\nSee: G3529 \n\nCognate Group: G3527 (Nicanor), G3534 (victory), G3528 (conquer), G3529 (victory), G5245 (more than conquer)\nVariants: νῖκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5331 נֶצַח",
    3535: "Νινευΐ (Nineuḯ | nin-yoo-ee')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5210)\nStrong's: Ninevi (i.e. Nineveh), the capital of Assyria\nKJV: —Nineve.\nSee: H5210\n\nCognate Group: G3535 (Nineve), G3536 (of Nineve)\nVariants: Νινευΐ, Νινευή, Νινευί̈ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5210 נִינְוֵה",
    3536: "Νινευΐτης (Nineuḯtēs | nin-yoo-ee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G3535\nStrong's: a Ninevite, i.e. inhabitant of Nineveh\nKJV: —of Nineve, Ninevite.\n\nSee: G3535 \n\nCognate Group: G3535 (Nineve), G3536 (of Nineve)\nVariants: Νινευΐτης, Νινευίτης",
    3537: "νιπτήρ (niptḗr | nip-tare')\n\nDerivation: from G3538\nStrong's: a ewer\nKJV: —bason.\n\nSee: G3538 \n\nCognate Group: G3537 (bason), G449 (unwashen), G3538 (wash)\nVariants: νιπτήρ, ποδονιπτήρ",
    3538: "νίπτω (níptō | nip'-to)\n\n\nStrong's: to cleanse (especially the hands or the feet or the face); ceremonially, to perform ablution\nKJV: —wash. Compare G3068 .\n\nSee: G3068 \n\nCognate Group: G3537 (bason), G449 (unwashen), G3538 (wash)\nVariants: νίπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4305 מָטַר, H7364 רָחַץ, H7857 שָׁטַף",
    3539: "νοιέω (noiéō | noy-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3563\nStrong's: to exercise the mind (observe), i.e. (figuratively) to comprehend, heed\nKJV: —consider, perceive, think, understand.\n\nSee: G3563 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νοιέω, νοέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6381 פָּלָא, H7760 שׂוּם, H7843 שָׁחַת, H7896 שִׁית, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    3540: "νόημα (nóēma | no'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3539\nStrong's: a perception, i.e. purpose, or (by implication) the intellect, disposition, itself\nKJV: —device, mind, thought.\n\nSee: G3539 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νόημα",
    3541: "νόθος (nóthos | noth'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a spurious or illegitimate son\nKJV: —bastard.\n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νόθος",
    3542: "νομή (nomḗ | nom-ay')\n\nDerivation: feminine from the same as G3551\nStrong's: pasture, i.e. (the act) feeding (figuratively, spreading of a gangrene), or (the food) pasturage\nKJV: —X eat, pasture.\n\nSee: G3551 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νομή \nHebrew Equivalents: H4769 מַרְבֵּץ, H4829 מִרְעֶה, H4999 נָאָה, H5116 נָוֶה",
    3543: "νομίζω (nomízō | nom-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3551\nStrong's: properly, to do by law (usage), i.e. to accustom (passively, be usual); by extension, to deem or regard\nKJV: — suppose, thing, be wont.\n\nSee: G3551 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νομίζω",
    3544: "νομικός (nomikós | nom-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G3551\nStrong's: according (or pertaining) to law, i.e. legal (ceremonially); as noun, an expert in the (Mosaic) law\nKJV: —about the law, lawyer.\n\nSee: G3551 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: νομικός",
    3545: "νομίμως (nomímōs | nom-im'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a derivative of G3551\nStrong's: legitimately (specially, agreeably to the rules of the lists)\nKJV: —lawfully.\n\nSee: G3551 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: νομίμως",
    3546: "νόμισμα (nómisma | nom'-is-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3543\nStrong's: what is reckoned as of value (after the Latin numisma), i.e. current coin\nKJV: —money.\n\nSee: G3543 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: νόμισμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1871 דַּרְכְּמֹון, H1881 דָּת",
    3547: "νομοδιδάσκαλος (nomodidáskalos | nom-od-id-as'-kal-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3551 and G1320\nStrong's: an expounder of the (Jewish) law, i.e. a Rabbi\nKJV: —doctor (teacher) of the law.\n\nSee: G3551 \nSee: G1320 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G2085 (teach other doctrine), G1322 (doctrine), G3547 (doctor of the law), G1317 (apt to teach), G1320 (doctor), G2567 (teacher of good things), G1321 (teach), G1319 (doctrine), G1318 (taught)\nVariants: νομοδιδάσκαλος",
    3548: "νομοθεσία (nomothesía | nom-oth-es-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3550\nStrong's: legislation (specially, the institution of the Mosaic code)\nKJV: —giving of the law.\n\nSee: G3550 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: νομοθεσία",
    3549: "νομοθετέω (nomothetéō | nom-oth-et-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3550\nStrong's: to legislate, i.e. (passively) to have (the Mosaic) enactments injoined, be sanctioned (by them)\nKJV: —establish, receive the law.\n\nSee: G3550 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: νομοθετέω, νομοθετέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3384 יָרָה",
    3550: "νομοθέτης (nomothétēs | nom-oth-et'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3551 and a derivative of G5087\nStrong's: a legislator\nKJV: —lawgiver.\n\nSee: G3551 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: νομοθέτης",
    3551: "νόμος (nómos | nom'-os)\n\nDerivation: from a primary νέμω (to parcel out, especially food or grazing to animals)\nStrong's: law (through the idea of prescriptive usage), genitive case (regulation), specially, (of Moses (including the volume); also of the Gospel), or figuratively (a principle)\nKJV: —law.\n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: νόμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1697 דָּבָר, H1881 דָּת, H2703 חֲצַר עֵינֹון, H2706 חֹק, H4687 מִצְוָה, H4941 מִשְׁפָּט, H6600 פִּתְגָּם, H8452 תֹּורָה",
    3552: "νοσέω (noséō | nos-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3554\nStrong's: to be sick, i.e. (by implication, of a diseased appetite) to hanker after (figuratively, to harp upon)\nKJV: —dote.\n\nSee: G3554 \n\nCognate Group: G3553 (disease), G3552 (dote), G3554 (disease)\nVariants: νοσέω",
    3553: "νόσημα (nósēma | nos'-ay-ma)\n\nDerivation: from G3552\nStrong's: an ailment\nKJV: —disease.\n\nSee: G3552 \n\nCognate Group: G3553 (disease), G3552 (dote), G3554 (disease)\nVariants: νόσημα G3797",
    3554: "νόσος (nósos | nos'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a malady (rarely figuratively, of moral disability)\nKJV: —disease, infirmity, sickness.\n\nCognate Group: G3553 (disease), G3552 (dote), G3554 (disease)\nVariants: νόσος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2481 חֲלִי, H4064 מַדְוֶה, H4347 מַכָּה, H8463 תַּחֲלוּא",
    3555: "νοσσιά (nossiá | nos-see-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G3502\nStrong's: a brood (of chickens)\nKJV: —brood.\n\nSee: G3502 \n\nCognate Group: G3503 (youth), G3504 (novice), G3502 (young), G3556 (chicken), G3555 (brood), G3501 (new)\nVariants: νοσσιά \nHebrew Equivalents: H2356 חֹור, H7064 קֵן",
    3556: "νοσσίον (nossíon | nos-see'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G3502\nStrong's: a birdling\nKJV: —chicken.\n\nSee: G3502 \n\nCognate Group: G3503 (youth), G3504 (novice), G3502 (young), G3556 (chicken), G3555 (brood), G3501 (new)\nVariants: νοσσίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H667 אֶפְרֹחַ",
    3557: "νοσφίζομαι (nosphízomai | nos-fid'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from νοσφί (apart or clandestinely)\nStrong's: to sequestrate for oneself, i.e. embezzle\nKJV: —keep back, purloin.\n\nCognate Group: G3557 (keep back)\nVariants: νοσφίζομαι, νοσφίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3947 לָקַח",
    3558: "νότος (nótos | not'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: the south(-west) wind; by extension, the southern quarter itself\nKJV: —south (wind).\n\nCognate Group: G3558 (south)\nVariants: νότος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1864 דָּרֹום, H3802 כָּתֵף, H5045 נֶגֶב, H6921 קָדִים, H8486 תֵּימָן",
    3559: "νουθεσία (nouthesía | noo-thes-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3563 and a derivative of G5087\nStrong's: calling attention to, i.e. (by implication) mild rebuke or warning\nKJV: —admonition.\n\nSee: G3563 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νουθεσία",
    3560: "νουθετέω (nouthetéō | noo-thet-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3559\nStrong's: to put in mind, i.e. (by implication) to caution or reprove gently\nKJV: —admonish, warn.\n\nSee: G3559 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νουθετέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H3256 יָסַר, H3543 כָּהָה",
    3561: "νουμηνία (noumēnía | noo-may-nee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a compound of G3501 and G3376 (as noun by implication, of G2250)\nStrong's: the festival of new moon\nKJV: —new moon.\n\nSee: G3501 \nSee: G3376 \nSee: G2250 \n\nCognate Group: G3376 (month), G3561 (new moon)\nVariants: νουμηνία, νεομηνία \nHebrew Equivalents: H259 אֶחָד, H2320 חֹדֶשׁ, H7218 רֹאשׁ",
    3562: "νουνεχῶς (nounechōs | noon-ekh-oce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a comparative of the accusative case of G3563 and G2192\nStrong's: in a mind-having way, i.e. prudently\nKJV: —discreetly.\n\nSee: G3563 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G3562 (discreetly), G3563 (mind)\nVariants: νουνεχῶς",
    3563: "νοῦς (noûs | nooce)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G1097\nStrong's: the intellect, i.e. mind (divine or human; in thought, feeling, or will); by implication, meaning\nKJV: —mind, understanding. Compare G5590 .\n\nSee: G1097 \nSee: G5590 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: νοῦς \nHebrew Equivalents: H241 אֹזֶן, H1847 דַּעַת, H3820 לֵב, H3824 לֵבָב, H7307 רוּחַ",
    3564: "Νυμφᾶς (Nymphâs | noom-fas')\n\nDerivation: probably contracted for a compound of G3565 and G1435\nStrong's: nymph-given (i.e. -born); Nymphas, a Christian\nKJV: —Nymphas.\n\nSee: G3565 \nSee: G1435 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: Νυμφᾶς, Νύμφαν, Νύμφα",
    3565: "νύμφη (nýmphē | noom-fay')\n\nDerivation: from a primary but obsolete verb νύπτω (to veil as a bride\nStrong's: compare Latin \"nupto,\" to marry); a young married woman (as veiled), including a betrothed girl; by implication, a son's wife\nKJV: —bride, daughter in law.\n\nCognate Group: G3565 (bride), G3566 (bridegroom), G3567 (bridechamber)\nVariants: νύμφη \nHebrew Equivalents: H1330 בְּתוּלָה, H3618 כַּלָּה",
    3566: "νυμφίος (nymphíos | noom-fee'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3565\nStrong's: a bride-groom (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —bridegroom.\n\nSee: G3565 \n\nCognate Group: G3565 (bride), G3566 (bridegroom), G3567 (bridechamber)\nVariants: νυμφίος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2860 חָתָן",
    3567: "νυμφών (nymphṓn | noom-fohn')\n\nDerivation: from G3565\nStrong's: the bridal room\nKJV: —bridechamber.\n\nSee: G3565 \n\nCognate Group: G3565 (bride), G3566 (bridegroom), G3567 (bridechamber)\nVariants: νυμφών",
    3568: "νῦν (nŷn | noon)\n\nDerivation: a primary particle of present time\nStrong's: \"now\" (as adverb of date, a transition or emphasis); also as noun or adjective present or immediate\nKJV: —henceforth, + hereafter, of late, soon, present, this (time). See also G3569, G3570 .\n\nSee: G3569 \nSee: G3570 \n\nCognate Group: G3568 (henceforth), G3569 (now), G3570 (now)\nVariants: νῦν \nHebrew Equivalents: H375 אֵיפֹה, H389 אַךְ, H639 אַף, H1972 הָלָא, H2008 הֵנָּה, H2009 הִנֵּה, H3117 יֹום, H3861 לָהֵן, H4994 נָא, H5728 עֲדֶן, H5844 עָטָה, H5844 עָטָה, H6285 פֵּאָה, H6471 פַּעַם",
    3569: "τανῦν (tanŷn | tan-oon') or τὰ νῦν (tà nŷn | tah noon)\n\nDerivation: from neuter plural of G3588 and G3568\nStrong's: the things now, i.e. (adverbially) at present\nKJV: —(but) now.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G3568 \n\nCognate Group: G3568 (henceforth), G3569 (now), G3570 (now)\nVariants: τανῦν G5422",
    3570: "νυνί (nyní | noo-nee')\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of G3568 for emphasis\nStrong's: just now\nKJV: —now.\n\nSee: G3568 \n\nCognate Group: G3568 (henceforth), G3569 (now), G3570 (now)\nVariants: νυνί",
    3571: "νύξ (nýx | noox)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: \"night\" (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: — (mid-)night.\n\nCognate Group: G3571 (night), G3574 (night and day)\nVariants: νύξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3915 לַיִל, H3916 לֵילְיָא, H5399 נֶשֶׁף",
    3572: "νύσσω (nýssō | noos'-so)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: to prick (\"nudge\")\nKJV: —pierce.\n\nCognate Group: G3571 (night), G3574 (night and day)\nVariants: νύσσω",
    3573: "νυστάζω (nystázō | noos-tad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G3506\nStrong's: to nod, i.e. (by implication) to fall asleep; figuratively, to delay\nKJV: —slumber.\n\nSee: G3506 \n\nCognate Group: G3573 (slumber)\nVariants: νυστάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1811 דָּלַף, H5123 נוּם, H7290 רָדַם, H8142 שֵׁנָה",
    3574: "νυχθήμερον (nychthḗmeron | nookh-thay'-mer-on)\n\nDerivation: from G3571 and G2250\nStrong's: a day-and-night, i.e. full day of twenty-four hours\nKJV: —night and day.\n\nSee: G3571 \nSee: G2250 \n\nCognate Group: G3571 (night), G3574 (night and day)\nVariants: νυχθήμερον",
    3575: "Νῶε (Nōe | no'-eh)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5146)\nStrong's: Noë, (i.e. Noäch), a patriarch\nKJV: —Noe.\nSee: H5146\n\nCognate Group: G3575 (Noe)\nVariants: Νῶε \nHebrew Equivalents: H5146 נֹחַ",
    3576: "νωθρός (nōthrós | no-thros')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3541\nStrong's: sluggish, i.e. (literally) lazy, or (figuratively) stupid\nKJV: —dull, slothful.\n\nSee: G3541 \n\nCognate Group: G3576 (dull)\nVariants: νωθρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2823 חָשֹׁךְ",
    3577: "νῶτος (nōtos | no'-tos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: the back\nKJV: —back.\n\nCognate Group: G3577 (back)\nVariants: νῶτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1354 גַב, H1460 גֵּו, H1975 הַלָּז, H3802 כָּתֵף, H4665 מִפְרֶקֶת, H6203 עֹרֶף, H7926 שְׁכֶם",
    3578: "ξενία (xenía | xen-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3581\nStrong's: hospitality, i.e. (by implication) a place of entertainment\nKJV: —lodging.\n\nSee: G3581 \n\nCognate Group: G3579 (entertain), G5382 (given to hospitality), G5381 (entertain stranger), G3578 (lodging), G3580 (lodge strangers), G3581 (host)\nVariants: ξενία",
    3579: "ξενίζω (xenízō | xen-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3581\nStrong's: to be a host (passively, a guest); by implication, be (make, appear) strange\nKJV: —entertain, lodge, (think it) strange.\n\nSee: G3581 \n\nCognate Group: G3579 (entertain), G5382 (given to hospitality), G5381 (entertain stranger), G3578 (lodging), G3580 (lodge strangers), G3581 (host)\nVariants: ξενίζω",
    3580: "ξενοδοχέω (xenodochéō | xen-od-okh-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3581 and G1209\nStrong's: to be hospitable\nKJV: —lodge strangers.\n\nSee: G3581 \nSee: G1209 \n\nCognate Group: G3579 (entertain), G5382 (given to hospitality), G5381 (entertain stranger), G3578 (lodging), G3580 (lodge strangers), G3581 (host)\nVariants: ξενοδοχέω",
    3581: "ξένος (xénos | xen'-os)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: foreign (literally, alien, or figuratively, novel); by implication, a guest or (vice-versa) entertainer\nKJV: —host, strange(-r).\n\nCognate Group: G3579 (entertain), G5382 (given to hospitality), G5381 (entertain stranger), G3578 (lodging), G3580 (lodge strangers), G3581 (host)\nVariants: ξένος \nHebrew Equivalents: H732 אָרַח, H1616 גֵּר, H5237 נׇכְרִי, H7121 קָרָא",
    3582: "ξέστης (xéstēs | xes'-tace)\n\nDerivation: as if from ξέω (properly, to smooth\nStrong's: by implication, (of friction) to boil or heat); a vessel (as fashioned or for cooking) (or perhaps by corruption from the Latin sextarius, the sixth of a modius, i.e. about a pint), i.e. (specially), a measure for liquids or solids, (by analogy, a pitcher)\nKJV: —pot.\n\nCognate Group: G3579 (entertain), G5382 (given to hospitality), G5381 (entertain stranger), G3578 (lodging), G3580 (lodge strangers), G3581 (host)\nVariants: ξέστης",
    3583: "ξηραίνω (xēraínō | xay-rah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3584\nStrong's: to desiccate; by implication, to shrivel, to mature\nKJV: —dry up, pine away, be ripe, wither (away).\n\nSee: G3584 \n\nCognate Group: G3584 (dry land), G3583 (dry up)\nVariants: ξηραίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2717 חָרַב, H3001 יָבֵשׁ, H5405 נָשַׁת, H7602 שָׁאַף, H8045 שָׁמַד",
    3584: "ξηρός (xērós | xay-ros')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3582 (through the idea of scorching)\nStrong's: arid; by implication, shrunken, earth (as opposed to water)\nKJV: —dry land, withered.\n\nSee: G3582 \n\nCognate Group: G3584 (dry land), G3583 (dry up)\nVariants: ξηρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2724 חָרָבָה, H3002 יָבֵשׁ, H3002 יָבֵשׁ, H3004 יַבָּשָׁה, H6723 צִיָּה, H6784 צָמַק",
    3585: "ξύλινος (xýlinos | xoo'-lin-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3586\nStrong's: wooden\nKJV: —of wood.\n\nSee: G3586 \n\nCognate Group: G3585 (of wood), G3586 (staff)\nVariants: ξύλινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2553 חַמָּן, H6086 עֵץ",
    3586: "ξύλον (xýlon | xoo'-lon)\n\nDerivation: from another form of the base of G3582\nStrong's: timber (as fuel or material); by implication, a stick, club or tree or other wooden article or substance\nKJV: —staff, stocks, tree, wood.\n\nSee: G3582 \n\nCognate Group: G3585 (of wood), G3586 (staff)\nVariants: ξύλον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5097 נַהֲלֹל, H5465 סַד, H6086 עֵץ, H6763 צֵלָע",
    3587: "ξυράω (xyráō | xoo-rah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the same as G3586 (meaning a razor)\nStrong's: to shave or \"shear\" the hair\nKJV: —shave.\n\nSee: G3586 \n\nCognate Group: G3587 (shave)\nVariants: ξυράω, ξυράομαι, ξυράω/ξυρέω/ξύρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1548 גָּלַח, H1639 גָּרַע, H7139 קָרַח",
    3588: "ὁ (ho | ho), including the feminine ἡ (hē | hay), and the neuter τό (tó | to)\n\n in all their inflections\n\nDerivation: the definite article\nStrong's: the (sometimes to be supplied, at others omitted, in English idiom)\nKJV: —the, this, that, one, he, she, it, etc.\n\nCognate Group: G3587 (shave)\nVariants: ὁ",
    3589: "ὀγδοήκοντα (ogdoḗkonta | og-do-ay'-kon-tah)\n\nDerivation: from G3590\nStrong's: ten times eight\nKJV: —fourscore.\n\nSee: G3590 \n\nCognate Group: G3589 (fourscore), G3590 (eighth)\nVariants: ὀγδοήκοντα",
    3590: "ὄγδοος (ógdoos | og'-do-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3638\nStrong's: the eighth\nKJV: —eighth.\n\nSee: G3638 \n\nCognate Group: G3589 (fourscore), G3590 (eighth)\nVariants: ὄγδοος",
    3591: "ὄγκος (ónkos | ong'-kos)\n\nDerivation: probably from the same as G43\nStrong's: a mass (as bending or bulging by its load), i.e. burden (hindrance)\nKJV: —weight.\n\nSee: G43 \n\nCognate Group: G3591 (weight)\nVariants: ὄγκος",
    3592: "ὅδε (hóde | hod'-eh), including the feminine ἥδε (hḗde | hay'-deh), and the neuter τόδε (tóde | tod'-e)\n\nDerivation: from G3588 and G1161\nStrong's: the same, i.e. this or that one (plural these or those); often used as a personal pronoun\nKJV: —he, she, such, these, thus.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G1161 \n\nCognate Group: G3591 (weight)\nVariants: ὅδε",
    3593: "ὁδεύω (hodeúō | hod-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3598\nStrong's: to travel\nKJV: —journey.\n\nSee: G3598 \n\nCognate Group: G3593 (journey)\nVariants: ὁδεύω",
    3594: "ὁδηγέω (hodēgéō | hod-ayg-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3595\nStrong's: to show the way (literally or figuratively (teach))\nKJV: —guide, lead.\n\nSee: G3595 \n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: ὁδηγέω",
    3595: "ὁδηγός (hodēgós | hod-ayg-os')\n\nDerivation: from G3598 and G2233\nStrong's: a conductor (literally or figuratively (teacher))\nKJV: —guide, leader.\n\nSee: G3598 \nSee: G2233 \n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: ὁδηγός",
    3596: "ὁδοιπορέω (hodoiporéō | hod-oy-por-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3598 and G4198\nStrong's: to be a wayfarer, i.e. travel\nKJV: —go on a journey.\n\nSee: G3598 \nSee: G4198 \n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: ὁδοιπορέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5549 סָלַל, H6424 פָּלַס, H6437 פָּנָה",
    3597: "ὁδοιπορία (hodoiporía | hod-oy-por-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3596\nStrong's: travel\nKJV: —journey(-ing).\n\nSee: G3596 \n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: ὁδοιπορία",
    3598: "ὁδός (hodós | hod-os')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a road; by implication, a progress (the route, act or distance); figuratively, a mode or means\nKJV: —journey, (high-)way.\n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: ὁδός, ὁδοποιέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H734 אֹרַח, H776 אֶרֶץ, H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H1978 הָלִיךְ, H2351 חוּץ, H3117 יֹום, H3996 מָבֹוא, H4156 מֹועֵצָה, H4174 מֹורָד, H4546 מְסִלָּה, H4547 מַסְלוּל, H4570 מַעְגָּל, H4687 מִצְוָה, H4725 מָקֹום, H5410 נָתִיב, H7200 רָאָה, H7339 רְחֹב, H7704 שָׂדֶה",
    3599: "ὀδούς (odoús | od-ooce)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the base of G2068\nStrong's: a \"tooth\"\nKJV: —tooth.\n\nSee: G2068 \n\nCognate Group: G3595 (guide), G3594 (guide), G3598 (journey), G1327 (highway), G1841 (decease), G1529 (coming), G3180 (wile)\nVariants: ὀδούς \nHebrew Equivalents: H8127 שֵׁן",
    3600: "ὀδυνάω (odynáō | od-oo-nah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3601\nStrong's: to grieve\nKJV: —sorrow, torment.\n\nSee: G3601 \n\nCognate Group: G3600 (sorrow), G3601 (sorrow)\nVariants: ὀδυνάω, ὀδυνάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1739 דָּוֶה, H2342 חוּל, H4843 מָרַר",
    3601: "ὀδύνη (odýnē | od-oo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from G1416\nStrong's: grief (as dejecting)\nKJV: —sorrow.\n\nSee: G1416 \n\nCognate Group: G3600 (sorrow), G3601 (sorrow)\nVariants: ὀδύνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H60 אֵבֶל, H205 אָוֶן, H1091 בַּלָּהָה, H1739 דָּוֶה, H1942 הַוָּה, H2256 חֶבֶל, H2427 חִיל, H3015 יָגֹון, H3939 לַעֲנָה, H4064 מַדְוֶה, H4205 מָזֹור, H4273 מַחַץ, H4341 מַכְאֹב, H4751 מַר, H4751 מַר, H4806 מְרִיא, H5061 נֶגַע, H5076 נָדֻד, H5492 סוּפָה, H5588 סֵעֵף, H5999 עָמָל, H6040 עֳנִי, H6089 עֶצֶב, H6427 פַּלָּצוּת, H7264 רָגַז, H7494 רַעַשׁ, H8424 תּוּגָה",
    3602: "ὀδυρμός (odyrmós | od-oor-mos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the base of G1416\nStrong's: moaning, i.e. lamentation\nKJV: —mourning.\n\nSee: G1416 \n\nCognate Group: G3602 (mourning)\nVariants: ὀδυρμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H8564 תַּמְרוּר",
    3603: "ὅ ἐστι (hó esti | ho es-tee')\n\nDerivation: from the neuter of G3739 and the third person singular present indicative of G1510\nStrong's: which is\nKJV: —called, which is (make), that is (to say).\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G1510 \n\nCognate Group: G3603 (called)\nVariants: ὅ ἐστι, ὅς, εἰμί G4005",
    3604: "Ὀζίας (Ozías | od-zee'-as)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5818)\nStrong's: Ozias (i.e. Uzzijah), an Israelite\nKJV: —Ozias.\nSee: H5818\n\nCognate Group: G3603 (called)\nVariants: Ὀζίας \nHebrew Equivalents: H5818 עֻזִּיָּה",
    3605: "ὄζω (ózō | od'-zo)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (in a strengthened form)\nStrong's: to scent (usually an ill \"odor\")\nKJV: —stink.\n\nCognate Group: G3603 (called)\nVariants: ὄζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H887 בָּאַשׁ",
    3606: "ὅθεν (hóthen | hoth'-en)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 with the directive enclitic of source\nStrong's: from which place or source or cause (adverb or conjunction)\nKJV: —from thence, (from) whence, where(-by, -fore, -upon).\n\nSee: G3739 \n\nCognate Group: G3603 (called)\nVariants: ὅθεν",
    3607: "ὀθόνη (othónē | oth-on'-ay)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a linen cloth, i.e. (especially) a sail\nKJV: —sheet.\n\nCognate Group: G3607 (sheet), G3608 (linen clothes)\nVariants: ὀθόνη",
    3608: "ὀθόνιον (othónion | oth-on'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G3607\nStrong's: a linen bandage\nKJV: —linen clothes.\n\nSee: G3607 \n\nCognate Group: G3607 (sheet), G3608 (linen clothes)\nVariants: ὀθόνιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5466 סָדִין, H6593 פִּשְׁתֶּה",
    3609: "οἰκεῖος (oikeîos | oy-ki'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3624\nStrong's: domestic, i.e. (as noun), a relative, adherent\nKJV: —(those) of the (his own) house(-hold).\n\nSee: G3624 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκεῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H441 אַלּוּף, H582 אֱנֹושׁ, H1004 בַּיִת, H1730 דֹּוד, H7607 שְׁאֵר",
    3610: "οἰκέτης (oikétēs | oy-ket'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3611\nStrong's: a fellow resident, i.e. menial domestic\nKJV: —(household) servant.\n\nSee: G3611 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκέτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H5650 עֶבֶד",
    3611: "οἰκέω (oikéō | oy-keh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3624\nStrong's: to occupy a house, i.e. reside (figuratively, inhabit, remain, inhere); by implication, to cohabit\nKJV: —dwell. See also G3625 .\n\nSee: G3624 \nSee: G3625 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H582 אֱנֹושׁ, H776 אֶרֶץ, H1481 גּוּר, H1752 דּוּר, H3427 יָשַׁב, H5414 נָתַן, H7931 שָׁכַן",
    3612: "οἴκημα (oíkēma | oy'-kay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3611\nStrong's: a tenement, i.e. (specially), a jail\nKJV: —prison.\n\nSee: G3611 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἴκημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1354 גַב",
    3613: "οἰκητήριον (oikētḗrion | oy-kay-tay'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G3611 (equivalent to G3612)\nStrong's: a residence (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —habitation, house.\n\nSee: G3611 \nSee: G3612 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκητήριον",
    3614: "οἰκία (oikía | oy-kee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3624\nStrong's: properly, residence (abstractly), but usually (concretely) an abode (literally or figuratively); by implication, a family (especially domestics)\nKJV: —home, house(-hold).\n\nSee: G3624 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1004 בַּיִת",
    3615: "οἰκιακός (oikiakós | oy-kee-ak-os')\n\nDerivation: from G3614\nStrong's: familiar, i.e. (as noun) relatives\nKJV: —they (them) of (his own) household.\n\nSee: G3614 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκιακός, οἰκειακός",
    3616: "οἰκοδεσποτέω (oikodespotéō | oy-kod-es-pot-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3617\nStrong's: to be the head of (i.e. rule) a family\nKJV: —guide the house.\n\nSee: G3617 \n\nCognate Group: G1203 (Lord), G3617 (goodman), G3616 (guide the house)\nVariants: οἰκοδεσποτέω",
    3617: "οἰκοδεσπότης (oikodespótēs | oy-kod-es-pot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3624 and G1203\nStrong's: the head of a family\nKJV: —goodman (of the house), householder, master of the house.\n\nSee: G3624 \nSee: G1203 \n\nCognate Group: G1203 (Lord), G3617 (goodman), G3616 (guide the house)\nVariants: οἰκοδεσπότης",
    3618: "οἰκοδομέω (oikodoméō | oy-kod-om-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3619\nStrong's: to be a house-builder, i.e. construct or (figuratively) confirm\nKJV: —(be in) build(-er, -ing, up), edify, embolden.\n\nSee: G3619 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκοδομέω, οἰκοδόμος",
    3619: "οἰκοδομή (oikodomḗ | oy-kod-om-ay')\n\nDerivation: feminine (abstract) of a compound of G3624 and the base of G1430\nStrong's: architecture, i.e. (concretely) a structure; figuratively, confirmation\nKJV: —building, edify(-ication, -ing).\n\nSee: G3624 \nSee: G1430 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκοδομή \nHebrew Equivalents: H1002 בִּירָה, H1129 בָּנָה, H4011 מִבְנֶה",
    3620: "οἰκοδομία (oikodomía | oy-kod-om-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3619\nStrong's: confirmation\nKJV: —edifying.\n\nSee: G3619 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκοδομία G3870, G3871",
    3621: "οἰκονομέω (oikonoméō | oy-kon-om-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3623\nStrong's: to manage (a house, i.e. an estate)\nKJV: —be steward.\n\nSee: G3623 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκονομέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3557 כּוּל",
    3622: "οἰκονομία (oikonomía | oy-kon-om-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3623\nStrong's: administration (of a household or estate); specially, a (religious) \"economy\"\nKJV: —dispensation, stewardship.\n\nSee: G3623 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκονομία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4475 מֶמְשָׁלָה, H4673 מַצָּב",
    3623: "οἰκονόμος (oikonómos | oy-kon-om'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3624 and the base of G3551\nStrong's: a house-distributor (i.e. manager), or overseer, i.e. an employee in that capacity; by extension, a fiscal agent (treasurer); figuratively, a preacher (of the Gospel)\nKJV: —chamberlain, governor, steward.\n\nSee: G3624 \nSee: G3551 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκονόμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5921 עַל, H6347 פֶּחָה, H7227 רַב",
    3624: "οἶκος (oîkos | oy'-kos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a dwelling (more or less extensive, literal or figurative); by implication, a family (more or less related, literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —home, house(-hold), temple.\n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἶκος, οὶκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H168 אֹהֶל, H759 אַרְמֹון, H776 אֶרֶץ, H1002 בִּירָה, H1004 בַּיִת, H1055 בִּיתָן, H1121 בֵּן, H1964 הֵיכָל, H2073 זְבוּל, H3957 לִשְׁכָּה, H4264 מַחֲנֶה, H4583 מָעֹון, H4725 מָקֹום, H4908 מִשְׁכָּן, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה, H4999 נָאָה, H5159 נַחֲלָה, H5650 עֶבֶד, H6944 קֹדֶשׁ, H6951 קָהָל, H8478 תַּחַת",
    3625: "οἰκουμένη (oikouménē | oy-kou-men'-ay)\n\nDerivation: feminine participle present passive of G3611 (as noun, by implication, of G1093)\nStrong's: land, i.e. the (terrene part of the) globe; specially, the Roman empire\nKJV: —earth, world.\n\nSee: G3611 \nSee: G1093 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: οἰκουμένη \nHebrew Equivalents: H776 אֶרֶץ, H8398 תֵּבֵל",
    3626: "οἰκουρός (oikourós | oy-koo-ros')\n\nDerivation: from G3624 and οὖρος \nStrong's: (a guard; be \"ware\"); a stayer at home, i.e. domestically inclined (a \"good housekeeper\")\nKJV: —keeper at home.\n\nSee: G3624 \n\nCognate Group: G3626 (keeper at home)\nVariants: οἰκουρός, οἰκουργός, οὐκουρός",
    3627: "οἰκτείρω (oikteírō | oyk-ti'-ro) also (in certain tenses) prolonged οἰκτερέω (oikteréō | oyk-ter-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from οἶκτος (pity)\nStrong's: to exercise pity\nKJV: —have compassion on.\n\nCognate Group: G3628 (mercy), G3627 (have compassion on), G3629 (merciful)\nVariants: οἰκτείρω, οἰκτίρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2603 חָנַן, H3045 יָדַע, H5414 נָתַן, H7355 רָחַם, H7725 שׁוּב",
    3628: "οἰκτιρμός (oiktirmós | oyk-tir-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3627\nStrong's: pity\nKJV: —mercy.\n\nSee: G3627 \n\nCognate Group: G3628 (mercy), G3627 (have compassion on), G3629 (merciful)\nVariants: οἰκτιρμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2603 חָנַן, H7356 רַחַם",
    3629: "οἰκτίρμων (oiktírmōn | oyk-tir'-mone)\n\nDerivation: from G3627\nStrong's: compassionate\nKJV: —merciful, of tender mercy.\n\nSee: G3627 \n\nCognate Group: G3628 (mercy), G3627 (have compassion on), G3629 (merciful)\nVariants: οἰκτίρμων \nHebrew Equivalents: H2603 חָנַן, H7349 רַחוּם",
    3630: "οἰνοπότης (oinopótēs | oy-nop-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3631 and a derivative of the alternate of G4095\nStrong's: a tippler\nKJV: —winebibber.\n\nSee: G3631 \nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G3630 (winebibber), G3631 (wine)\nVariants: οἰνοπότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H5435 סֹבֶא",
    3631: "οἶνος (oînos | oy'-nos)\n\nDerivation: a primary word (or perhaps of Hebrew origin (H3196))\nStrong's: \"wine\" (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —wine.\nSee: H3196\n\nCognate Group: G3630 (winebibber), G3631 (wine)\nVariants: οἶνος, οὶνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2561 חֶמֶר, H3196 יַיִן, H3342 יֶקֶב, H5435 סֹבֶא, H6071 עָסִיס, H7941 שֵׁכָר, H8105 שֶׁמֶר, H8492 תִּירֹושׁ",
    3632: "οἰνοφλυγία (oinophlygía | oy-nof-loog-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3631 and a form of the base of G5397\nStrong's: an overflow (or surplus) of wine, i.e. vinolency (drunkenness)\nKJV: —excess of wine.\n\nSee: G3631 \nSee: G5397 \n\nCognate Group: G3630 (winebibber), G3631 (wine)\nVariants: οἰνοφλυγία",
    3633: "οἴομαι (oíomai | oy'-om-ahee), or (shorter) οἶμαι (oîmai | oy'-mahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice apparently from G3634\nStrong's: to make like (oneself), i.e. imagine (be of the opinion)\nKJV: —suppose, think.\n\nSee: G3634 \n\nCognate Group: G3630 (winebibber), G3631 (wine)\nVariants: οἴομαι, οἶμαι, οἴομαι contracted as οἶμαι, οἴμαι, οὶμαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2009 הִנֵּה",
    3634: "οἷος (hoîos | hoy'-os)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G3588, G3739, and G3745\nStrong's: such or what sort of (as a correlation or exclamation); especially the neuter (adverbially) with negative, not so\nKJV: —so (as), such as, what (manner of), which.\n\nSee: G3745 \nSee: G3588 \nSee: G3739 \n\nCognate Group: G3630 (winebibber), G3631 (wine)\nVariants: οἷος, οἵος",
    3635: "ὀκνέω (oknéō | ok-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from ὄκνος (hesitation)\nStrong's: to be slow (figuratively, loath)\nKJV: —delay.\n\nCognate Group: G3630 (winebibber), G3631 (wine)\nVariants: ὀκνέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H680 אָצַל, H4513 מָנַע",
    3636: "ὀκνηρός (oknērós | ok-nay-ros')\n\nDerivation: from G3635\nStrong's: tardy, i.e. indolent; (figuratively) irksome\nKJV: —grievous, slothful.\n\nSee: G3635 \n\nCognate Group: G3636 (grievous)\nVariants: ὀκνηρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6102 עָצֵל",
    3637: "ὀκταήμερος (oktaḗmeros | ok-tah-ay'-mer-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3638 and G2250\nStrong's: an eight-day old person or act\nKJV: —the eighth day.\n\nSee: G3638 \nSee: G2250 \n\nCognate Group: G3636 (grievous)\nVariants: ὀκταήμερος",
    3638: "ὀκτώ (oktṓ | ok-to')\n\nDerivation: a primary numeral\nStrong's: \"eight\"\nKJV: —eight.\n\nCognate Group: G3636 (grievous)\nVariants: ὀκτώ",
    3639: "ὄλεθρος (ólethros | ol'-eth-ros)\n\nDerivation: from a primary ὄλλυμι \nStrong's: (to destroy; a prolonged form); ruin, i.e. death, punishment\nKJV: —destruction.\n\nCognate Group: G3644 (destroyer), G3639 (destruction), G3645 (destroy), G1842 (destroy)\nVariants: ὄλεθρος, ὀλέθριος \nHebrew Equivalents: H343 אֵיד, H2764 חֵרֶם, H3582 כָּחַד, H4923 מְשַׁמָּה, H6343 פַּחַד, H7057 קִמֹּושׁ, H7701 שֹׁד, H7703 שָׁדַד",
    3640: "ὀλιγόπιστος (oligópistos | ol-ig-op'-is-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G3641 and G4102\nStrong's: incredulous, i.e. lacking confidence (in Christ)\nKJV: —of little faith.\n\nSee: G3641 \nSee: G4102 \n\nCognate Group: G3641 (+ almost), G3642 (feebleminded), G3643 (despise), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: ὀλιγόπιστος",
    3641: "ὀλίγος (olígos | ol-ee'-gos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: puny (in extent, degree, number, duration or value); especially neuter (adverbially) somewhat\nKJV: —+ almost, brief(-ly), few, (a) little, + long, a season, short, small, a while.\n\nCognate Group: G3641 (+ almost), G3642 (feebleminded), G3643 (despise), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: ὀλίγος \nHebrew Equivalents: H259 אֶחָד, H369 אַיִן, H657 אֶפֶס, H4193 מֹות, H4213 מִזְעָר, H4591 מָעַט, H4591 מָעַט, H4592 מְעַט, H4592 מְעַט, H4705 מִצְעָר, H6819 צָעַר",
    3642: "ὀλιγόψυχος (oligópsychos | ol-ig-op'-soo-khos)\n\nDerivation: from G3641 and G6590\nStrong's: little-spirited, i.e. faint-hearted\nKJV: —feebleminded.\n\nSee: G6590 \nSee: G3641 \n\nCognate Group: G3641 (+ almost), G3642 (feebleminded), G3643 (despise), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: ὀλιγόψυχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1792 דָּכָא, H4116 מָהַר, H7306 רוּחַ",
    3643: "ὀλιγωρέω (oligōréō | ol-ig-o-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3641 and ὤρα (\"care\")\nStrong's: to have little regard for, i.e. to disesteem\nKJV: —despise.\n\nSee: G3641 \n\nCognate Group: G3641 (+ almost), G3642 (feebleminded), G3643 (despise), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: ὀλιγωρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3988 מָאַס",
    3644: "ὀλοθρευτής (olothreutḗs | ol-oth-ryoo-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G3645\nStrong's: a ruiner, i.e. (specially), a venomous serpent\nKJV: —destroyer.\n\nSee: G3645 \n\nCognate Group: G3644 (destroyer), G3639 (destruction), G3645 (destroy), G1842 (destroy)\nVariants: ὀλοθρευτής, ὀλεθρευτής",
    3645: "ὀλοθρεύω (olothreúō | ol-oth-ryoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3639\nStrong's: to spoil, i.e. slay\nKJV: —destroy.\n\nSee: G3639 \n\nCognate Group: G3644 (destroyer), G3639 (destruction), G3645 (destroy), G1842 (destroy)\nVariants: ὀλοθρεύω, ὀλεθρεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2763 חָרַם, H3423 יָרַשׁ, H3772 כָּרַת, H5916 עָכַר, H7703 שָׁדַד, H7843 שָׁחַת, H8045 שָׁמַד",
    3646: "ὁλοκαύτωμα (holokaútōma | hol-ok-ow'-to-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of a compound of G3650 and a derivative of G2545\nStrong's: a wholly-consumed sacrifice (\"holocaust\")\nKJV: —(whole) burnt offering.\n\nSee: G3650 \nSee: G2545 \n\nCognate Group: G3644 (destroyer), G3639 (destruction), G3645 (destroy), G1842 (destroy)\nVariants: ὁλοκαύτωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H801 אִשָּׁה, H2113 זְוָעָה, H3632 כָּלִיל, H4503 מִנְחָה, H5930 עֹלָה",
    3647: "ὁλοκληρία (holoklēría | hol-ok-lay-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3648\nStrong's: integrity, i.e. physical wholeness\nKJV: —perfect soundness.\n\nSee: G3648 \n\nCognate Group: G3647 (perfect soundness), G2819 (heritage), G2817 (inheritance), G4789 (fellow -heir), G2820 (obtain an inheritance), G3648 (entire), G2624 (divide by lot), G2816 (be heir), G4345 (consort with), G2818 (heir)\nVariants: ὁλοκληρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4974 מְתֹם",
    3648: "ὁλόκληρος (holóklēros | hol'-ok'-lay-ros)\n\nDerivation: from G3650 and G2819\nStrong's: complete in every part, i.e. perfectly sound (in body)\nKJV: —entire, whole.\n\nSee: G3650 \nSee: G2819 \n\nCognate Group: G3647 (perfect soundness), G2819 (heritage), G2817 (inheritance), G4789 (fellow -heir), G2820 (obtain an inheritance), G3648 (entire), G2624 (divide by lot), G2816 (be heir), G4345 (consort with), G2818 (heir)\nVariants: ὁλόκληρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5324 נָצַב, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8549 תָּמִים",
    3649: "ὀλολύζω (ololýzō | ol-ol-odd'-zo)\n\nDerivation: a reduplicated primary verb\nStrong's: to \"howl\" or \"halloo\", i.e. shriek\nKJV: —howl.\n\nCognate Group: G3649 (howl)\nVariants: ὀλολύζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3213 יָלַל, H6682 צְוָחָה",
    3650: "ὅλος (hólos | hol'-os)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: \"whole\" or \"all\", i.e. complete (in extent, amount, time or degree), especially (neuter) as noun or adverb\nKJV: —all, altogether, every whit, + throughout, whole.\n\nCognate Group: G3650 (all), G3651 (wholly)\nVariants: ὅλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3605 כֹּל, H8548 תָּמִיד, H8549 תָּמִים",
    3651: "ὁλοτελής (holotelḗs | hol-ot-el-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G3650 and G5056\nStrong's: complete to the end, i.e. absolutely perfect\nKJV: —wholly.\n\nSee: G3650 \nSee: G5056 \n\nCognate Group: G3650 (all), G3651 (wholly)\nVariants: ὁλοτελής",
    3652: "Ὀλυμπᾶς (Olympâs | ol-oom-pas')\n\nDerivation: probably a contraction from Ὀλυμπιόδωρος (Olympian-bestowed, i.e. heaven-descended)\nStrong's: Olympas, a Christian\nKJV: —Olympas.\n\nCognate Group: G3650 (all), G3651 (wholly)\nVariants: Ὀλυμπᾶς",
    3653: "ὄλυνθος (ólynthos | ol'-oon-thos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: an unripe (because out of season) fig\nKJV: —untimely fig.\n\nCognate Group: G3650 (all), G3651 (wholly)\nVariants: ὄλυνθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6291 פַּג",
    3654: "ὅλως (hólōs | hol'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3650\nStrong's: completely, i.e. altogether; (by analogy), everywhere; (negatively) not by any means\nKJV: —at all, commonly, utterly.\n\nSee: G3650 \n\nCognate Group: G3654 (at all)\nVariants: ὅλως",
    3655: "ὄμβρος (ómbros | om'-bros)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a thunder storm\nKJV: —shower.\n\nCognate Group: G3655 (shower)\nVariants: ὄμβρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8163 שָׂעִיר",
    3656: "ὁμιλέω (homiléō | hom-il-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3658\nStrong's: to be in company with, i.e. (by implication) to converse\nKJV: —commune, talk.\n\nSee: G3658 \n\nCognate Group: G3657 (communication), G3658 (company), G3656 (commune)\nVariants: ὁμιλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1696 דָבַר, H7235 רָבָה",
    3657: "ὁμιλία (homilía | hom-il-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3658\nStrong's: companionship (\"homily\"), i.e. (by implication) intercourse\nKJV: —communication.\n\nSee: G3658 \n\nCognate Group: G3657 (communication), G3658 (company), G3656 (commune)\nVariants: ὁμιλία \nHebrew Equivalents: H3948 לֶקַח, H5772 עֹונָה",
    3658: "ὅμιλος (hómilos | hom'-il-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3674 and a derivative of the alternate of G138 (meaning a crowd)\nStrong's: association together, i.e. a multitude\nKJV: —company.\n\nSee: G3674 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G3657 (communication), G3658 (company), G3656 (commune)\nVariants: ὅμιλος G3919, G3920",
    3659: "ὄμμα (ómma | om'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3700\nStrong's: a sight, i.e. (by implication) the eye\nKJV: —eye.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3657 (communication), G3658 (company), G3656 (commune)\nVariants: ὄμμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H5869 עַיִן",
    3660: "ὀμνύω (omnýō | om-noo'-o)\n\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary, but obsolete ὄμω, for which another prolonged form ὀμόω (omóō | om-o'-o)\n is used in certain tenses\nStrong's: to swear, i.e. take (or declare on) oath\nKJV: —swear.\n\nCognate Group: G3660 (swear)\nVariants: ὀμνύω, ὄμνυμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H7650 שָׁבַע, H8610 תָּפַשׂ",
    3661: "ὁμοθυμαδόν (homothymadón | hom-oth-oo-mad-on')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a compound of the base of G3674 and G2372\nStrong's: unanimously\nKJV: —with one accord (mind).\n\nSee: G3674 \nSee: G2372 \n\nCognate Group: G3661 (with one accord), G3674 (together)\nVariants: ὁμοθυμαδόν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3162 יַחַד, H3162 יַחַד",
    3662: "ὁμοιάζω (homoiázō | hom-oy-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3664\nStrong's: to resemble\nKJV: —agree.\n\nSee: G3664 \n\nCognate Group: G3661 (with one accord), G3674 (together)\nVariants: ὁμοιάζω G3925",
    3663: "ὁμοιοπαθής (homoiopathḗs | hom-oy-op-ath-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G3664 and the alternate of G3958\nStrong's: similarly affected\nKJV: —of (subject to) like passions.\n\nSee: G3664 \nSee: G3958 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: ὁμοιοπαθής",
    3664: "ὅμοιος (hómoios | hom'-oy-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3674\nStrong's: similar (in appearance or character)\nKJV: —like, + manner.\n\nSee: G3674 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: ὅμοιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1819 דָּמָה, H1823 דְּמוּת, H3238 יָנָה, H3238 יָנָה, H3644 כְּמֹו, H3653 כֵּן, H4915 מֹשֶׁל, H5048 נֶגֶד, H7775 שַׁוְעָה",
    3665: "ὁμοιότης (homoiótēs | hom-oy-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3664\nStrong's: resemblance\nKJV: —like as, similitude.\n\nSee: G3664 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: ὁμοιότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H4327 מִין",
    3666: "ὁμοιόω (homoióō | hom-oy-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3664\nStrong's: to assimilate, i.e. compare; passively, to become similar\nKJV: —be (make) like, (in the) liken(-ess), resemble.\n\nSee: G3664 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: ὁμοιόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H225 אוּת, H1819 דָּמָה, H4911 מָשַׁל, H6186 עָרַךְ",
    3667: "ὁμοίωμα (homoíōma | hom-oy'-o-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3666\nStrong's: a form; abstractly, resemblance\nKJV: —made like to, likeness, shape, similitude.\n\nSee: G3666 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: ὁμοίωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1823 דְּמוּת, H4758 מַרְאֶה, H5869 עַיִן, H6754 צֶלֶם, H8389 תֹּאַר, H8403 תַּבְנִית, H8544 תְּמוּנָה",
    3668: "ὁμοίως (homoíōs | hom-oy'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3664\nStrong's: similarly\nKJV: —likewise, so.\n\nSee: G3664 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: ὁμοίως",
    3669: "ὁμοίωσις (homoíōsis | hom-oy'-o-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3666\nStrong's: assimilation, i.e. resemblance\nKJV: —similitude.\n\nSee: G3666 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: ὁμοίωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1823 דְּמוּת, H8403 תַּבְנִית, H8508 תׇּכְנִית",
    3670: "ὁμολογέω (homologéō | hom-ol-og-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of the base of G3674 and G3056\nStrong's: to assent, i.e. covenant, acknowledge\nKJV: —con- (pro-)fess, confession is made, give thanks, promise.\n\nSee: G3674 \nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: ὁμολογέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3034 יָדָה, H5087 נָדַר, H7650 שָׁבַע",
    3671: "ὁμολογία (homología | hom-ol-og-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3670\nStrong's: acknowledgment\nKJV: —con- (pro-)fession, professed.\n\nSee: G3670 \n\nCognate Group: G437 (give thanks), G1843 (confess), G3672 (without controversy), G3671 (con- fession), G3670 (con- fess)\nVariants: ὁμολογία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5071 נְדָבָה, H5088 נֶדֶר",
    3672: "ὁμολογουμένως (homologouménōs | hom-ol-og-ow-men'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb of present passive participle of G3670\nStrong's: confessedly\nKJV: —without controversy.\n\nSee: G3670 \n\nCognate Group: G437 (give thanks), G1843 (confess), G3672 (without controversy), G3671 (con- fession), G3670 (con- fess)\nVariants: ὁμολογουμένως",
    3673: "ὁμότεχνος (homótechnos | hom-ot'-ekh-nos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3674 and G5078\nStrong's: a fellow-artificer\nKJV: —of the same craft.\n\nSee: G3674 \nSee: G5078 \n\nCognate Group: G437 (give thanks), G1843 (confess), G3672 (without controversy), G3671 (con- fession), G3670 (con- fess)\nVariants: ὁμότεχνος",
    3674: "ὁμοῦ (homoû | hom-oo')\n\nDerivation: genitive case of ὁμός (the same\nStrong's: akin to G260) as adverb; at the same place or time\nKJV: —together.\n\nSee: G260 \n\nCognate Group: G3661 (with one accord), G3674 (together)\nVariants: ὁμοῦ",
    3675: "ὁμόφρων (homóphrōn | hom-of'-rone)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3674 and G5424\nStrong's: like-minded, i.e. harmonious\nKJV: —of one mind.\n\nSee: G5424 \nSee: G3674 \n\nCognate Group: G3661 (with one accord), G3674 (together)\nVariants: ὁμόφρων",
    3676: "ὅμως (hómōs | hom'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from the base of G3674\nStrong's: at the same time, i.e. (conjunctionally) notwithstanding, yet still\nKJV: —and even, nevertheless, though but.\n\nSee: G3674 \n\nCognate Group: G3661 (with one accord), G3674 (together)\nVariants: ὅμως",
    3677: "ὄναρ (ónar | on'-ar)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a dream\nKJV: —dream.\n\nCognate Group: G3677 (dream)\nVariants: ὄναρ",
    3678: "ὀνάριον (onárion | on-ar'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G3688\nStrong's: a little ass\nKJV: —young ass.\n\nSee: G3688 \n\nCognate Group: G3678 (young ass), G3688 (an ass)\nVariants: ὀνάριον",
    3679: "ὀνειδίζω (oneidízō | on-i-did'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3681\nStrong's: to defame, i.e. rail at, chide, taunt\nKJV: —cast in teeth, (suffer) reproach, revile, upbraid.\n\nSee: G3681 \n\nCognate Group: G3680 (reproach), G3679 (cast in teeth), G3681 (reproach)\nVariants: ὀνειδίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2659 חָפֵר, H2778 חָרַף, H3198 יָכַח, H3637 כָּלַם, H5086 נָדַף",
    3680: "ὀνειδισμός (oneidismós | on-i-dis-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3679\nStrong's: contumely\nKJV: —reproach.\n\nSee: G3679 \n\nCognate Group: G3680 (reproach), G3679 (cast in teeth), G3681 (reproach)\nVariants: ὀνειδισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H961 בִּזָּה, H1421 גִּדּוּף, H2781 חֶרְפָּה, H3639 כְּלִמָּה, H8433 תֹּוכֵחָה",
    3681: "ὄνειδος (óneidos | on'-i-dos)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to the base of G3686\nStrong's: notoriety, i.e. a taunt (disgrace)\nKJV: —reproach.\n\nSee: G3686 \n\nCognate Group: G3680 (reproach), G3679 (cast in teeth), G3681 (reproach)\nVariants: ὄνειδος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2555 חָמָס, H2617 חֵסֵד, H2778 חָרַף, H2781 חֶרְפָּה, H3639 כְּלִמָּה, H3933 לַעַג",
    3682: "Ὀνήσιμος (Onḗsimos | on-ay'-sim-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3685\nStrong's: profitable; Onesimus, a Christian\nKJV: —Onesimus.\n\nSee: G3685 \n\nCognate Group: G3680 (reproach), G3679 (cast in teeth), G3681 (reproach)\nVariants: Ὀνήσιμος",
    3683: "Ὀνησίφορος (Onēsíphoros | on-ay-sif'-or-os)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3685 and G5411\nStrong's: profit-bearer; Onesiphorus, a Christian\nKJV: —Onespiphorus.\n\nSee: G3685 \nSee: G5411 \n\nCognate Group: G3680 (reproach), G3679 (cast in teeth), G3681 (reproach)\nVariants: Ὀνησίφορος",
    3684: "ὀνικός (onikós | on-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G3688\nStrong's: belonging to a ass, i.e. large (so as to be turned by a ass)\nKJV: —millstone.\n\nSee: G3688 \n\nCognate Group: G3680 (reproach), G3679 (cast in teeth), G3681 (reproach)\nVariants: ὀνικός",
    3685: "ὀνίνημι (onínēmi | on-in'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of an apparently primary verb ( ὄνομαι, to slur)\nStrong's: for which another prolonged form ( ὀνάω) is used as an alternate in some tenses (unless indeed it be identical with the base of G3686 through the idea of notoriety); to gratify, i.e. (middle voice) to derive pleasure or advantage from\nKJV: —have joy.\n\nSee: G3686 \n\nCognate Group: G3680 (reproach), G3679 (cast in teeth), G3681 (reproach)\nVariants: ὀνίνημι",
    3686: "ὄνομα (ónoma | on'-om-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of the base of G1097 (compare G3685)\nStrong's: a \"name\" (literally or figuratively) (authority, character)\nKJV: —called, (+ sur-)name(-d).\n\nSee: G1097 \nSee: G3685 \n\nCognate Group: G5581 (falsely so called), G3686 (called), G3687 (call), G2028 (call)\nVariants: ὄνομα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2143 זֵכֶר, H5220 נֶכֶד, H8034 שֵׁם, H8034 שֵׁם, H8088 שֵׁמַע",
    3687: "ὀνομάζω (onomázō | on-om-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3686\nStrong's: to name, i.e. assign an appellation; by extension, to utter, mention, profess\nKJV: —call, name.\n\nSee: G3686 \n\nCognate Group: G5581 (falsely so called), G3686 (called), G3687 (call), G2028 (call)\nVariants: ὀνομάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2142 זָכַר, H5344 נָקַב, H7121 קָרָא",
    3688: "ὄνος (ónos | on'-os)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a donkey\nKJV: —an ass.\n\nCognate Group: G3678 (young ass), G3688 (an ass)\nVariants: ὄνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H860 אָתֹון, H2543 חֲמֹור, H5679 עֲבָרָה, H5895 עַיִר, H6501 פֶּרֶא",
    3689: "ὄντως (óntōs | on'-toce)\n\nDerivation: adverb of the oblique cases of G5607\nStrong's: really\nKJV: —certainly, clean, indeed, of a truth, verily.\n\nSee: G5607 \n\nCognate Group: G3678 (young ass), G3688 (an ass)\nVariants: ὄντως, ὀλίγως \nHebrew Equivalents: H389 אַךְ, H403 אָכֵן, H551 אׇמְנָם",
    3690: "ὄξος (óxos | oz-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3691\nStrong's: vinegar, i.e. sour wine\nKJV: —vinegar.\n\nSee: G3691 \n\nCognate Group: G3690 (vinegar)\nVariants: ὄξος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2558 חֹמֶץ",
    3691: "ὀξύς (oxýs | oz-oos')\n\nDerivation: probably akin to the base of G188 (\"acid\")\nStrong's: keen; by analogy, rapid\nKJV: —sharp, swift.\n\nSee: G188 \n\nCognate Group: G3690 (vinegar)\nVariants: ὀξύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H2299 חַד, H2789 חֶרֶשׂ, H4106 מָהִיר, H7031 קַל, H7858 שֶׁטֶף, H8150 שָׁנַן",
    3692: "ὀπή (opḗ | op-ay')\n\nDerivation: probably from G3700\nStrong's: a hole (as if for light), i.e. cavern; by analogy, a spring (of water)\nKJV: —cave, place.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3690 (vinegar)\nVariants: ὀπή \nHebrew Equivalents: H699 אֲרֻבָּה, H2288 חֲגָו, H2356 חֹור, H5366 נְקָרָה, H5595 סָפָה",
    3693: "ὄπισθεν (ópisthen | op'-is-then)\n\nDerivation: from ὄπις (regard\nStrong's: from G3700) with enclitic of source; from the rear (as a secure aspect), i.e. at the back (adverb and preposition of place or time)\nKJV: —after, backside, behind.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3693 (after), G3694 (after)\nVariants: ὄπισθεν \nHebrew Equivalents: H268 אָחֹור, H310 אַחַר, H310 אַחַר, H810 אֶשֶׁךְ",
    3694: "ὀπίσω (opísō | op-is'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3693 with enclitic of direction\nStrong's: to the back, i.e. aback (as adverb or preposition of time or place; or as noun)\nKJV: —after, back(-ward), (+ get) behind, + follow.\n\nSee: G3693 \n\nCognate Group: G3693 (after), G3694 (after)\nVariants: ὀπίσω, τοὐπισω \nHebrew Equivalents: H268 אָחֹור, H309 אָחַר, H310 אַחַר, H310 אַחַר, H410 אֵל, H854 אֵת, H1334 בָּתַר, H5973 עִם, H6040 עֳנִי, H8478 תַּחַת",
    3695: "ὁπλίζω (hoplízō | hop-lid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3696\nStrong's: to equip (with weapons (middle voice and figuratively))\nKJV: —arm self.\n\nSee: G3696 \n\nCognate Group: G3695 (arm self), G3696 (armour), G2528 (arm), G3833 (all armour)\nVariants: ὁπλίζω, ὁπλίζομαι",
    3696: "ὅπλον (hóplon | hop'-lon)\n\nDerivation: probably from a primary ἕπω (to be busy about)\nStrong's: an implement or utensil or tool (literally or figuratively, especially, offensive for war)\nKJV: —armour, instrument, weapon.\n\nCognate Group: G3695 (arm self), G3696 (armour), G2528 (arm), G3833 (all armour)\nVariants: ὅπλον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2595 חֲנִית, H3627 כְּלִי, H4043 מָגֵן, H5402 נֶשֶׁק, H5630 סִרְיֹן, H6793 צִנָּה, H7973 שֶׁלַח, H7982 שֶׁלֶט",
    3697: "ὁποῖος (hopoîos | hop-oy'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 and G4169\nStrong's: of what kind that, i.e. how (as) great (excellent) (specially, as an indefinite correlative to the definite antecedent G5108 of quality)\nKJV: —what manner (sort) of, such as whatsoever.\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G4169 \nSee: G5108 \n\nCognate Group: G3695 (arm self), G3696 (armour), G2528 (arm), G3833 (all armour)\nVariants: ὁποῖος",
    3698: "ὁπότε (hopóte | hop-ot'-eh)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 and G4218\nStrong's: what(-ever) then, i.e. (of time) as soon as\nKJV: —when.\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G4218 \n\nCognate Group: G3695 (arm self), G3696 (armour), G2528 (arm), G3833 (all armour)\nVariants: ὁπότε G3962",
    3699: "ὅπου (hópou | hop'-oo)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 and G4225\nStrong's: what(-ever) where, i.e. at whichever spot\nKJV: —in what place, where(-as, -soever), whither (+ soever).\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G4225 \n\nCognate Group: G3695 (arm self), G3696 (armour), G2528 (arm), G3833 (all armour)\nVariants: ὅπου",
    3700: "ὀπτάνομαι (optánomai | op-tan'-om-ahee)\n\n\nDerivation: a (middle voice) prolonged form of the primary (middle voice) ὄπτομαι (óptomai | op'-tom-ahee)\n; which is used for it in certain tenses; and both as alternate of G3708\nStrong's: to gaze (i.e. with wide-open eyes, as at something remarkable; and thus differing from G991, which denotes simply voluntary observation; and from G1492, which expresses merely mechanical, passive or casual vision; while G2300, and still more emphatically its intensive G2334, signifies an earnest but more continued inspection; and G4648 a watching from a distance)\nKJV: —appear, look, see, shew self.\n\nSee: G3708 \nSee: G991 \nSee: G1492 \nSee: G2300 \nSee: G2334 \nSee: G4648 \n\nCognate Group: G845 (eye-witness), G2030 (eye-witness), G991 (behold), G2029 (behold), G3701 (vision), G3700 (appear)\nVariants: ὀπτάνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3700 כָּסַף",
    3701: "ὀπτασία (optasía | op-tas-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G3700\nStrong's: visuality, i.e. (concretely) an apparition\nKJV: —vision.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G845 (eye-witness), G2030 (eye-witness), G991 (behold), G2029 (behold), G3701 (vision), G3700 (appear)\nVariants: ὀπτασία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4758 מַרְאֶה, H4759 מַרְאָה, H7200 רָאָה",
    3702: "ὀπτός (optós | op-tos')\n\nDerivation: from an obsolete verb akin to ἕπσω (to \"steep\")\nStrong's: cooked, i.e. roasted\nKJV: —broiled.\n\nCognate Group: G845 (eye-witness), G2030 (eye-witness), G991 (behold), G2029 (behold), G3701 (vision), G3700 (appear)\nVariants: ὀπτός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6748 צָלִי",
    3703: "ὀπώρα (opṓra | op-o'-rah)\n\nDerivation: apparently from the base of G3796 and G5610\nStrong's: properly, even-tide of the (summer) season (dog-days), i.e. (by implication) ripe fruit\nKJV: —fruit.\n\nSee: G3796 \nSee: G5610 \n\nCognate Group: G845 (eye-witness), G2030 (eye-witness), G991 (behold), G2029 (behold), G3701 (vision), G3700 (appear)\nVariants: ὀπώρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H7019 קַיִץ",
    3704: "ὅπως (hópōs | hop'-oce)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 and G4459\nStrong's: what(-ever) how, i.e. in the manner that (as adverb or conjunction of coincidence, intentional or actual)\nKJV: —because, how, (so) that, to, when.\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G4459 \n\nCognate Group: G845 (eye-witness), G2030 (eye-witness), G991 (behold), G2029 (behold), G3701 (vision), G3700 (appear)\nVariants: ὅπως",
    3705: "ὅραμα (hórama | hor'-am-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3708\nStrong's: something gazed at, i.e. a spectacle (especially supernatural)\nKJV: —sight, vision.\n\nSee: G3708 \n\nCognate Group: G2300 (behold), G3705 (sight), G517 (invisible), G3707 (visible), G2334 (behold), G3708 (behold), G2529 (clearly see), G4308 (foresee), G3706 (sight)\nVariants: ὅραμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2377 חָזֹון, H2377 חָזֹון, H2472 חֲלֹום, H4172 מֹורָא, H4758 מַרְאֶה, H4853 מַשָּׂא",
    3706: "ὅρασις (hórasis | hor'-as-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3708\nStrong's: the act of gazing, i.e. (externally) an aspect or (internally) an inspired appearance\nKJV: —sight, vision.\n\nSee: G3708 \n\nCognate Group: G2300 (behold), G3705 (sight), G517 (invisible), G3707 (visible), G2334 (behold), G3708 (behold), G2529 (clearly see), G4308 (foresee), G3706 (sight)\nVariants: ὅρασις \nHebrew Equivalents: H2122 זִיו, H2377 חָזֹון, H4707 מִצְפֶּה, H4758 מַרְאֶה, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H5869 עַיִן, H7299 רֵו, H8447 תֹּור",
    3707: "ὁρατός (horatós | hor-at-os')\n\nDerivation: from G3708\nStrong's: gazed at, i.e. (by implication) capable of being seen\nKJV: —visible.\n\nSee: G3708 \n\nCognate Group: G2300 (behold), G3705 (sight), G517 (invisible), G3707 (visible), G2334 (behold), G3708 (behold), G2529 (clearly see), G4308 (foresee), G3706 (sight)\nVariants: ὁρατός",
    3708: "ὁράω (horáō | hor-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: properly, to stare at (compare G3700), i.e. (by implication) to discern clearly (physically or mentally)\nStrong's: by extension, to attend to; by Hebraism, to experience; passively, to appear\nKJV: —behold, perceive, see, take heed.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G2300 (behold), G3705 (sight), G517 (invisible), G3707 (visible), G2334 (behold), G3708 (behold), G2529 (clearly see), G4308 (foresee), G3706 (sight)\nVariants: ὁράω, εἶδον \nHebrew Equivalents: H389 אַךְ, H935 בֹּוא, H2009 הִנֵּה, H2372 חָזָה, H2492 חָלַם, H5239 נָלָה, H6437 פָּנָה, H7200 רָאָה, H7789 שׁוּר, H7931 שָׁכַן",
    3709: "ὀργή (orgḗ | or-gay')\n\nDerivation: from G3713\nStrong's: properly, desire (as a reaching forth or excitement of the mind), i.e. (by analogy), violent passion (ire, or (justifiable) abhorrence); by implication punishment\nKJV: —anger, indignation, vengeance, wrath.\n\nSee: G3713 \n\nCognate Group: G3949 (anger), G3710 (be angry), G3950 (wrath), G3709 (anger), G3711 (soon angry)\nVariants: ὀργή \nHebrew Equivalents: H639 אַף, H2195 זַעַם, H2197 זַעַף, H2534 חֵמָה, H2740 חָרֹון, H3708 כַּעַס, H3708 כַּעַס, H5007 נְאָצָה, H5191 נְטַל, H5492 סוּפָה, H5591 סַעַר, H5678 עֶבְרָה, H7107 קָצַף, H7107 קָצַף, H7110 קֶצֶף, H7267 רֹגֶז, H7267 רֹגֶז",
    3710: "ὀργίζω (orgízō | or-gid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3709\nStrong's: to provoke or enrage, i.e. (passively) become exasperated\nKJV: —be angry (wroth).\n\nSee: G3709 \n\nCognate Group: G3949 (anger), G3710 (be angry), G3950 (wrath), G3709 (anger), G3711 (soon angry)\nVariants: ὀργίζω, ὀργίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H639 אַף, H2194 זָעַם, H2196 זָעַף, H2534 חֵמָה, H2734 חָרָה, H4843 מָרַר, H6058 עָנֵף, H6226 עָשֵׁן, H7107 קָצַף, H7264 רָגַז, H7378 רִיב",
    3711: "ὀργίλος (orgílos | org-ee'-los)\n\nDerivation: from G3709\nStrong's: irascible\nKJV: —soon angry.\n\nSee: G3709 \n\nCognate Group: G3949 (anger), G3710 (be angry), G3950 (wrath), G3709 (anger), G3711 (soon angry)\nVariants: ὀργίλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H639 אַף, H2534 חֵמָה, H3708 כַּעַס",
    3712: "ὀργυιά (orguiá | org-wee-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G3713\nStrong's: a stretch of the arms, i.e. a fathom\nKJV: —fathom.\n\nSee: G3713 \n\nCognate Group: G3949 (anger), G3710 (be angry), G3950 (wrath), G3709 (anger), G3711 (soon angry)\nVariants: ὀργυιά, ὀργυιά (ὄργυια)",
    3713: "ὀρέγομαι (orégomai | or-eg'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of apparently a prolonged form of an obsolete primary (compare G3735)\nStrong's: to stretch oneself, i.e. reach out after (long for)\nKJV: —covet after, desire.\n\nSee: G3735 \n\nCognate Group: G3713 (covet after), G3715 (lust)\nVariants: ὀρέγομαι, ὀρέγω",
    3714: "ὀρεινός (oreinós | or-i-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G3735\nStrong's: mountainous, i.e. (feminine by implication, of G5561) the Highlands (of Judæa)\nKJV: —hill country.\n\nSee: G3735 \nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G3713 (covet after), G3715 (lust)\nVariants: ὀρεινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2022 הַר, H2022 הַר",
    3715: "ὄρεξις (órexis | or'-ex-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3713\nStrong's: excitement of the mind, i.e. longing after\nKJV: —lust.\n\nSee: G3713 \n\nCognate Group: G3713 (covet after), G3715 (lust)\nVariants: ὄρεξις",
    3716: "ὀρθοποδέω (orthopodéō | or-thop-od-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3717 and G4228\nStrong's: to be straight-footed, i.e. (figuratively) to go directly forward\nKJV: —walk uprightly.\n\nSee: G3717 \nSee: G4228 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὀρθοποδέω",
    3717: "ὀρθός (orthós | or-thos')\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G3735\nStrong's: right (as rising), i.e. (perpendicularly) erect (figuratively, honest), or (horizontally) level or direct\nKJV: —straight, upright.\n\nSee: G3735 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὀρθός \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H3476 יֹשֶׁר, H3477 יָשָׁר, H5227 נֹכַח, H6424 פָּלַס, H7054 קָמָה, H7317 רֹומָה",
    3718: "ὀρθοτομέω (orthotoméō | or-thot-om-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3717 and the base of G5114, to make a straight cut, i.e. (figuratively) to dissect (expound) correctly (the divine message)\nKJV: —rightly divide.\n\nSee: G3717 \nSee: G5114 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὀρθοτομέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3474 יָשַׁר",
    3719: "ὀρθρίζω (orthrízō | or-thrid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3722\nStrong's: to use the dawn, i.e. (by implication) to repair betimes\nKJV: —come early in the morning.\n\nSee: G3722 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὀρθρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7836 שָׁחַר, H7925 שָׁכַם",
    3720: "ὀρθρινός (orthrinós | or-thrin-os')\n\nDerivation: from G3722\nStrong's: relating to the dawn, i.e. matutinal (as an epithet of Venus, especially brilliant in the early day)\nKJV: —morning.\n\nSee: G3722 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὀρθρινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H7925 שָׁכַם",
    3721: "ὄρθριος (órthrios | or'-three-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3722\nStrong's: in the dawn, i.e. up at day-break\nKJV: —early.\n\nSee: G3722 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὄρθριος G3985",
    3722: "ὄρθρος (órthros | or'-thros)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3735\nStrong's: dawn (as sun-rise, rising of light); by extension, morn\nKJV: —early in the morning.\n\nSee: G3735 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὄρθρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H821 אַשְׁמֻרָה, H1242 בֹּקֶר, H3364 יָקַץ, H7837 שַׁחַר",
    3723: "ὀρθῶς (orthōs | or-thoce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3717\nStrong's: in a straight manner, i.e. (figuratively) correctly (also morally)\nKJV: —plain, right(-ly).\n\nSee: G3717 \n\nCognate Group: G3718 (rightly divide), G3723 (plain), G3717 (straight), G3716 (walk uprightly), G3722 (early in the morning), G1882 (correction), G1357 (reformation), G3721 (early), G3720 (morning), G3719 (come early in the morning)\nVariants: ὀρθῶς",
    3724: "ὁρίζω (horízō | hor-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3725\nStrong's: to mark out or bound (\"horizon\"), i.e. (figuratively) to appoint, decree, specify\nKJV: —declare, determine, limit, ordain.\n\nSee: G3725 \n\nCognate Group: G3724 (declare), G4309 (determine before), G592 (separate), G873 (divide)\nVariants: ὁρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H631 אָסַר, H1362 גָּבָהּ, H1366 גְּבוּל, H6504 פָּרַד, H7169 קָרַץ, H7560 רְשַׁם",
    3725: "ὅριον (hórion | hor'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of an apparently primary ὅρος (a bound or limit)\nStrong's: a boundary-line, i.e. (by implication) a frontier (region)\nKJV: —border, coast.\n\nCognate Group: G3724 (declare), G4309 (determine before), G592 (separate), G873 (divide)\nVariants: ὅριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H776 אֶרֶץ, H1323 בַּת, H1366 גְּבוּל, H1486 גֹּורָל, H1552 גְלִילָה, H2706 חֹק, H3027 יָד, H4135 מוּל, H6285 פֵּאָה, H7093 קֵץ, H7704 שָׂדֶה",
    3726: "ὁρκίζω (horkízō | hor-kid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3727\nStrong's: to put on oath, i.e. make swear; by analogy, to solemnly enjoin\nKJV: —adjure, charge.\n\nSee: G3727 \n\nCognate Group: G1964 (forswear self), G1844 (adjure), G3728 (oath), G3726 (adjure), G1845 (exorcist), G1965 (perjured person), G3727 (oath)\nVariants: ὁρκίζω, ἐνορκίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7650 שָׁבַע, H7650 שָׁבַע",
    3727: "ὅρκος (hórkos | hor'-kos)\n\nDerivation: from ἕρκος (a fence\nStrong's: perhaps akin to G3725); a limit, i.e. (sacred) restraint (specially, an oath)\nKJV: —oath.\n\nSee: G3725 \n\nCognate Group: G1964 (forswear self), G1844 (adjure), G3728 (oath), G3726 (adjure), G1845 (exorcist), G1965 (perjured person), G3727 (oath)\nVariants: ὅρκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5927 עָלָה, H7655 שִׁבְעָה, H7773 שֶׁוַע",
    3728: "ὁρκωμοσία (horkōmosía | hor-ko-mos-ee'ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3727 and a derivative of G3660\nStrong's: asseveration on oath\nKJV: —oath.\n\nSee: G3727 \nSee: G3660 \n\nCognate Group: G1964 (forswear self), G1844 (adjure), G3728 (oath), G3726 (adjure), G1845 (exorcist), G1965 (perjured person), G3727 (oath)\nVariants: ὁρκωμοσία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5927 עָלָה",
    3729: "ὁρμάω (hormáō | hor-mah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3730\nStrong's: to start, spur or urge on, i.e. (reflexively) to dash or plunge\nKJV: —run (violently), rush.\n\nSee: G3730 \n\nCognate Group: G3729 (run), G874 (occasion), G3730 (assault), G3731 (violence)\nVariants: ὁρμάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2363 חוּשׁ, H5860 עִיט, H6437 פָּנָה, H6584 פָּשַׁט, H7725 שׁוּב, H7760 שׂוּם",
    3730: "ὁρμή (hormḗ | hor-may')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a violent impulse, i.e. onset\nKJV: —assault.\n\nCognate Group: G3729 (run), G874 (occasion), G3730 (assault), G3731 (violence)\nVariants: ὁρμή \nHebrew Equivalents: H2534 חֵמָה, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H6388 פֶּלֶג, H7110 קֶצֶף, H7722 שֹׁוא, H8161 שַׁעֲטָה",
    3731: "ὅρμημα (hórmēma | hor'-may-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3730\nStrong's: an attack, i.e. (abstractly) precipitancy\nKJV: —violence.\n\nSee: G3730 \n\nCognate Group: G3729 (run), G874 (occasion), G3730 (assault), G3731 (violence)\nVariants: ὅρμημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1676 דָּאָה, H5678 עֶבְרָה, H6388 פֶּלֶג",
    3732: "ὄρνεον (órneon | or'-neh-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G3733\nStrong's: a birdling\nKJV: —bird, fowl.\n\nSee: G3733 \n\nCognate Group: G3729 (run), G874 (occasion), G3730 (assault), G3731 (violence)\nVariants: ὄρνεον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5775 עֹוף, H5861 עַיִט, H6833 צִפֹּור, H6893 קָאַת",
    3733: "ὄρνις (órnis | or'-nis)\n\nDerivation: probably from a prolonged form of the base of G3735\nStrong's: a bird (as rising in the air), i.e. (specially), a hen (or female domestic fowl)\nKJV: —hen.\n\nSee: G3735 \n\nCognate Group: G3729 (run), G874 (occasion), G3730 (assault), G3731 (violence)\nVariants: ὄρνις, ὄρινξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1257 בַּרְבֻּר",
    3734: "ὁροθεσία (horothesía | hor-oth-es-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of the base of G3725 and a derivative of G5087\nStrong's: a limit-placing, i.e. (concretely) boundary-line\nKJV: —bound.\n\nSee: G3725 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G3729 (run), G874 (occasion), G3730 (assault), G3731 (violence)\nVariants: ὁροθεσία",
    3735: "ὄρος (óros | or'-os)\n\nDerivation: probably from an obsolete ὄρω (to rise or \"rear\"\nStrong's: perhaps akin to G142 ; compare G3733); a mountain (as lifting itself above the plain)\nKJV: —hill, mount(-ain).\n\nSee: G142 \nSee: G3733 \n\nCognate Group: G3735 (hill)\nVariants: ὄρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1389 גִּבְעָה, H1926 הָדָר, H2022 הַר, H2022 הַר, H2905 טוּר, H4791 מָרֹום, H6697 צוּר, H8205 שְׁפִי",
    3736: "ὀρύσσω (orýssō | or-oos'-so)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to \"burrow\" in the ground, i.e. dig\nKJV: —dig.\n\nCognate Group: G3735 (hill)\nVariants: ὀρύσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1624 גָּרָה, H2658 חָפַר, H2672 חָצַב, H2864 חָתַר, H3738 כָּרָה, H5365 נָקַר, H6605 פָּתַח",
    3737: "ὀρφανός (orphanós | or-fan-os')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: bereaved (\"orphan\"), i.e. parentless\nKJV: —comfortless, fatherless.\n\nCognate Group: G3737 (comfortless)\nVariants: ὀρφανός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3490 יָתֹום",
    3738: "ὀρχέομαι (orchéomai | or-kheh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from ὄρχος (a row or ring)\nStrong's: to dance (from the ranklike or regular motion)\nKJV: —dance.\n\nCognate Group: G3737 (comfortless)\nVariants: ὀρχέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3769 כָּרַר, H6339 פָּזַז, H7540 רָקַד, H7832 שָׂחַק",
    3739: "ὅς (hós | hos), including feminine ἥ (hḗ | hay), and neuter ὅ (hó | ho)\n\nDerivation: probably a primary word (or perhaps a form of the article G3588)\nStrong's: the relatively (sometimes demonstrative) pronoun, who, which, what, that\nKJV: —one, (an-, the) other, some, that, what, which, who(-m, -se), etc. See also G3757 .\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G3757 \n\nCognate Group: G3737 (comfortless)\nVariants: ὅς, ὅσγε",
    3740: "ὁσάκις (hosákis | hos-ak'-is)\n\nDerivation: multiple adverb from G3739\nStrong's: how (i.e. with G302, so) many times as\nKJV: —as oft(-en) as.\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G302 \n\nCognate Group: G3737 (comfortless)\nVariants: ὁσάκις",
    3741: "ὅσιος (hósios | hos'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity; properly, right (by intrinsic or divine character; thus distinguished from 1342, which refers rather to human statutes and relations; from G2413, which denotes formal consecration\nStrong's: and from G40, which relates to purity from defilement), i.e. hallowed (pious, sacred, sure)\nKJV: —holy, mercy, shalt be. 342\n\nSee: G2413 \nSee: G40 \n\nCognate Group: G3741 (holy), G462 (unholy), G3743 (holily), G3742 (holiness)\nVariants: ὅσιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2134 זַךְ, H2616 חָסַד, H2617 חֵסֵד, H2623 חָסִיד, H2889 טָהֹור, H3477 יָשָׁר, H4720 מִקְדָּשׁ, H7965 שָׁלֹום, H8537 תֹּם, H8549 תָּמִים",
    3742: "ὁσιότης (hosiótēs | hos-ee-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3741\nStrong's: piety\nKJV: —holiness.\n\nSee: G3741 \n\nCognate Group: G3741 (holy), G462 (unholy), G3743 (holily), G3742 (holiness)\nVariants: ὁσιότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H3476 יֹשֶׁר, H8537 תֹּם, H8549 תָּמִים",
    3743: "ὁσίως (hosíōs | hos-ee-oce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3741\nStrong's: piously\nKJV: —holily.\n\nSee: G3741 \n\nCognate Group: G3741 (holy), G462 (unholy), G3743 (holily), G3742 (holiness)\nVariants: ὁσίως",
    3744: "ὀσμή (osmḗ | os-may')\n\nDerivation: from G3605\nStrong's: fragrance (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —odour, savour.\n\nSee: G3605 \n\nCognate Group: G3744 (odour)\nVariants: ὀσμή \nHebrew Equivalents: H887 בָּאַשׁ, H1314 בֶּשֶׂם, H7381 רֵיחַ",
    3745: "ὅσος (hósos | hos'-os)\n\nDerivation: by reduplication from G3739\nStrong's: as (much, great, long, etc.) as\nKJV: —all (that), as (long, many, much) (as), how great (many, much), (in-)asmuch as, so many as, that (ever), the more, those things, what (great, -soever), wheresoever, wherewithsoever, which, X while, who(-soever).\n\nSee: G3739 \n\nCognate Group: G3744 (odour)\nVariants: ὅσος",
    3746: "ὅσπερ (hósper | hos'-per)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 and G4007\nStrong's: who especially\nKJV: —whomsoever.\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G4007 \n\nCognate Group: G3744 (odour)\nVariants: ὅσπερ G4013",
    3747: "ὀστέον (ostéon | os-teh'-on), or contracted ὀστοῦν (ostoûn | os-toon')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a bone\nKJV: —bone.\n\nCognate Group: G3744 (odour)\nVariants: ὀστέον, ὀστοῦν \nHebrew Equivalents: H1634 גֶּרֶם, H6106 עֶצֶם",
    3748: "ὅστις (hóstis | hos'-tis), including the feminine ἥτις (hḗtis | hay'-tis), and the neuter ὅτι (hóti | hot'-ee)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 and G5100\nStrong's: which some, i.e. any that; also (definite) which same\nKJV: —X and (they), (such) as, (they) that, in that they, what(-soever), whereas ye, (they) which, who(-soever). Compare G3754 .\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G5100 \nSee: G3754 \n\nCognate Group: G3744 (odour)\nVariants: ὅστις",
    3749: "ὀστράκινος (ostrákinos | os-tra'-kin-os)\n\nDerivation: from ὄστρακον (\"oyster\") (a tile, i.e. terra cotta)\nStrong's: earthen-ware, i.e. clayey; by implication, frail\nKJV: —of earth, earthen.\n\nCognate Group: G3744 (odour)\nVariants: ὀστράκινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2635 חֲסַף, H2789 חֶרֶשׂ",
    3750: "ὄσφρησις (ósphrēsis | os'-fray-sis)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3605\nStrong's: smell (the sense)\nKJV: —smelling.\n\nSee: G3605 \n\nCognate Group: G3744 (odour)\nVariants: ὄσφρησις",
    3751: "ὀσφῦς (osphŷs | os-foos')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: the loin (externally), i.e. the hip; internally (by extension) procreative power\nKJV: —loin.\n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: ὀσφῦς, ὀσφύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H451 אַלְיָה, H1975 הַלָּז, H2502 חָלַץ, H2783 חֲרַץ",
    3752: "ὅταν (hótan | hot'-an)\n\nDerivation: from G3753 and G302\nStrong's: whenever (implying hypothesis or more or less uncertainty); also causatively (conjunctionally) inasmuch as\nKJV: —as long (soon) as, that, + till, when(-soever), while.\n\nSee: G3753 \nSee: G302 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: ὅταν",
    3753: "ὅτε (hóte | hot'-eh)\n\nDerivation: from G3739 and G5037\nStrong's: at which (thing) too, i.e. when\nKJV: —after (that), as soon as, that, when, while.\n\nSee: G3739 \nSee: G5037 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: ὅτε",
    3754: "ὅτι (hóti | hot'-ee)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G3748 as conjunction\nStrong's: demonstrative, that (sometimes redundant); causative, because\nKJV: —as concerning that, as though, because (that), for (that), how (that), (in) that, though, why.\n\nSee: G3748 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: ὅτι",
    3755: "ὅτου (hótou | hot'-oo)\n\nDerivation: for the genitive case of G3748 (as adverb)\nStrong's: during which same time, i.e. whilst\nKJV: —whiles.\n\nSee: G3748 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: ὅτου, ὅστις",
    3756: "οὐ (ou | oo), also (before a vowel) οὐκ (ouk | ook), and (before an aspirate) οὐχ (ouch | ookh)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: the absolute negative (compare G3361) adverb; no or not\nKJV: —+ long, nay, neither, never, no (X man), none, (can-)not, + nothing, + special, un(-worthy), when, + without, + yet but. See also G3364, G3372 .\n\nSee: G3361 \nSee: G3364 \nSee: G3372 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐ, οὐ/οὐκ/οὐχ, οὔ",
    3757: "οὗ (hoû | hoo)\n\nDerivation: genitive case of G3739 as adverb\nStrong's: at which place, i.e. where\nKJV: —where(-in), whither(-soever).\n\nSee: G3739 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὗ",
    3758: "οὐά (ouá | oo-ah')\n\nDerivation: a primary exclamation of surprise\nStrong's: \"ah\"\nKJV: —ah.\n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐά",
    3759: "οὐαί (ouaí | oo-ah'-ee)\n\nDerivation: a primary exclamation of grief\nStrong's: \"woe\"\nKJV: —alas, woe.\n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐαί \nHebrew Equivalents: H330 אֵטוּן, H1930 הֹו, H1945 הֹוי",
    3760: "οὐδαμῶς (oudamōs | oo-dam-oce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from (the feminine) of G3762\nStrong's: by no means\nKJV: —not.\n\nSee: G3762 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐδαμῶς",
    3761: "οὐδέ (oudé | oo-deh')\n\nDerivation: from G3756 and G1161\nStrong's: not however, i.e. neither, nor, not even\nKJV: —neither (indeed), never, no (more, nor, not), nor (yet), (also, even, then) not (even, so much as), + nothing, so much as.\n\nSee: G3756 \nSee: G1161 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐδέ",
    3762: "οὐδείς (oudeís | oo-dice'), including feminine οὐδεμία (oudemía | oo-dem-ee'-ah), and neuter οὐδέν (oudén | oo-den')\n\nDerivation: from G3761 and G1520\nStrong's: not even one (man, woman or thing), i.e. none, nobody, nothing\nKJV: —any (man), aught, man, neither any (thing), never (man), no (man), none (+ of these things), not (any, at all, -thing), nought.\n\nSee: G3761 \nSee: G1520 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐδείς, οὐθείς, οὐδείς/οὐθενός",
    3763: "οὐδέποτε (oudépote | oo-dep'-ot-eh)\n\nDerivation: from G3761 and G4218\nStrong's: not even at any time, i.e. never at all\nKJV: —neither at any time, never, nothing at any time.\n\nSee: G3761 \nSee: G4218 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐδέποτε \nHebrew Equivalents: H3808 לֹא",
    3764: "οὐδέπω (oudépō | oo-dep'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3761 and G4452\nStrong's: not even yet\nKJV: —as yet not, never before (yet), (not) yet.\n\nSee: G3761 \nSee: G4452 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐδέπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2962 טֶרֶם",
    3765: "οὐκέτι (oukéti | ook-et'-ee), also (separately) οὐκ ἔτι (ouk éti | ook et'-ee)\n\nDerivation: from G3756 and G2089\nStrong's: not yet, no longer\nKJV: —after that (not), (not) any more, henceforth (hereafter) not, no longer (more), not as yet (now), now no more (not), yet (not).\n\nSee: G3756 \nSee: G2089 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐκέτι \nHebrew Equivalents: H657 אֶפֶס, H3254 יָסַף, H3807 כָּתַת, H3808 לֹא, H5750 עֹוד, H5869 עַיִן",
    3766: "οὐκοῦν (oukoûn | ook-oon')\n\nDerivation: from G3756 and G3767\nStrong's: is it not therefore that, i.e. (affirmatively) hence or so\nKJV: —then.\n\nSee: G3756 \nSee: G3767 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐκοῦν",
    3767: "οὖν (oûn | oon)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: (adverbially) certainly, or (conjunctionally) accordingly\nKJV: —and (so, truly), but, now (then), so (likewise then), then, therefore, verily, wherefore.\n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὖν",
    3768: "οὔπω (oúpō | oo'-po)\n\nDerivation: from G3756 and G4452\nStrong's: not yet\nKJV: —hitherto not, (no…) as yet, not yet.\n\nSee: G3756 \nSee: G4452 \n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὔπω",
    3769: "οὐρά (ourá | oo-rah')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a tail\nKJV: —tail.\n\nCognate Group: G3751 (loin)\nVariants: οὐρά \nHebrew Equivalents: H2180 זָנָב",
    3770: "οὐράνιος (ouránios | oo-ran'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3772\nStrong's: celestial, i.e. belonging to or coming from the sky\nKJV: —heavenly.\n\nSee: G3772 \n\nCognate Group: G2032 (celestial), G3771 (from heaven), G3772 (air), G3770 (heavenly)\nVariants: οὐράνιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8064 שָׁמַיִם",
    3771: "οὐρανόθεν (ouranóthen | oo-ran-oth'-en)\n\nDerivation: from G3772 and the enclitic of source\nStrong's: from the sky\nKJV: —from heaven.\n\nSee: G3772 \n\nCognate Group: G2032 (celestial), G3771 (from heaven), G3772 (air), G3770 (heavenly)\nVariants: οὐρανόθεν",
    3772: "οὐρανός (ouranós | oo-ran-os')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the same as G3735 (through the idea of elevation)\nStrong's: the sky; by extension, heaven (as the abode of God); by implication, happiness, power, eternity; specially, the Gospel (Christianity)\nKJV: —air, heaven(-ly), sky.\n\nSee: G3735 \n\nCognate Group: G2032 (celestial), G3771 (from heaven), G3772 (air), G3770 (heavenly)\nVariants: οὐρανός \nHebrew Equivalents: H410 אֵל, H776 אֶרֶץ, H4791 מָרֹום, H7549 רָקִיעַ, H7834 שַׁחַק, H8064 שָׁמַיִם",
    3773: "Οὐρβανός (Ourbanós | oor-ban-os')\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: Urbanus (of the city, \"urbane\"), a Christian\nKJV: —Urbanus.\n\nCognate Group: G2032 (celestial), G3771 (from heaven), G3772 (air), G3770 (heavenly)\nVariants: Οὐρβανός",
    3774: "Οὐρίας (Ourías | oo-ree'-as)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H223)\nStrong's: Urias (i.e. Urijah), a Hittite\nKJV: —Urias.\nSee: H223\n\nCognate Group: G3774 (Urias)\nVariants: Οὐρίας \nHebrew Equivalents: H223 אוּרִיָּה",
    3775: "οὖς (oûs | ooce)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: the ear (physically or mentally)\nKJV: —ear.\n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: οὖς, οὺς \nHebrew Equivalents: H241 אֹזֶן, H410 אֵל, H5048 נֶגֶד",
    3776: "οὐσία (ousía | oo-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the feminine of G5607\nStrong's: substance, i.e. property (possessions)\nKJV: —goods, substance.\n\nSee: G5607 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: οὐσία",
    3777: "οὔτε (oúte | oo'-teh)\n\nDerivation: from G3756 and G5037\nStrong's: not too, i.e. neither or nor; by analogy, not even\nKJV: —neither, none, nor (yet), (no, yet) not, nothing.\n\nSee: G3756 \nSee: G5037 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: οὔτε",
    3778: "οὗτος (hoûtos | hoo'-tos), including nominative masculine plural οὗτοι (hoûtoi | hoo'-toy), nominative feminine singular αὕτη (haútē | how'-tay), and nominative feminine plural αὕται (haútai | how'-tahee)\n\nDerivation: from the article G3588 and G846\nStrong's: the he (she or it), i.e. this or that (often with article repeated)\nKJV: —he (it was that), hereof, it, she, such as, the same, these, they, this (man, same, woman), which, who.\n\nSee: G846 \nSee: G3588 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: οὗτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H389 אַךְ, H403 אָכֵן, H403 אָכֵן, H834 אֲשֶׁר, H2063 זֹאת, H2088 זֶה, H3541 כֹּה, H3588 כִּי, H3602 כָּכָה, H3653 כֵּן, H3660 כְּנֵמָא, H5794 עַז",
    3779: "οὕτω (hoútō | hoo'-to), or (before a vowel οὕτως (hoútōs | hoo'-toce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3778\nStrong's: in this way (referring to what precedes or follows)\nKJV: —after that, after (in) this manner, as, even (so), for all that, like(-wise), no more, on this fashion(-wise), so (in like manner), thus, what.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: οὕτω, οὕτως, οὕτω(ς), οὕτω/οὕτως \nHebrew Equivalents: H3653 כֵּן",
    3780: "οὐχί (ouchí | oo-khee')\n\nDerivation: intensive of G3756\nStrong's: not indeed\nKJV: —nay, not.\n\nSee: G3756 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: οὐχί",
    3781: "ὀφειλέτης (opheilétēs | of-i-let'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3784\nStrong's: an ower, i.e. person indebted; figuratively, a delinquent; morally, a transgressor (against God)\nKJV: —debtor, which owed, sinner.\n\nSee: G3784 \n\nCognate Group: G3782 (debt), G3781 (debtor), G3783 (debt), G3784 (behove)\nVariants: ὀφειλέτης",
    3782: "ὀφειλή (opheilḗ | of-i-lay')\n\nDerivation: from G3784\nStrong's: indebtedness, i.e. (concretely) a sum owed; figuratively, obligation, i.e. (conjugal) duty\nKJV: —debt, due.\n\nSee: G3784 \n\nCognate Group: G3782 (debt), G3781 (debtor), G3783 (debt), G3784 (behove)\nVariants: ὀφειλή",
    3783: "ὀφείλημα (opheílēma | of-i'-lay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from (the alternate of) G3784\nStrong's: something owed, i.e. (figuratively) a due; morally, a fault\nKJV: —debt.\n\nSee: G3784 \n\nCognate Group: G3782 (debt), G3781 (debtor), G3783 (debt), G3784 (behove)\nVariants: ὀφείλημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4859 מַשָּׁאָה, H5382 נָשָׁה",
    3784: "ὀφείλω (opheílō | of-i'-lo), or (in certain tenses), its prolonged form ὀφειλέω (opheiléō | of-i-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G3786 (through the idea of accruing)\nStrong's: to owe (pecuniarily); figuratively, to be under obligation (ought, must, should); morally, to fail in duty\nKJV: —behove, be bound, (be) debt(-or), (be) due(-ty), be guilty (indebted), (must) need(-s), ought, owe, should. See also G3785 .\n\nSee: G3786 \nSee: G3785 \n\nCognate Group: G3782 (debt), G3781 (debtor), G3783 (debt), G3784 (behove)\nVariants: ὀφείλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H305 אַחֲלַי, H954 בּוּשׁ, H2243 חֹב, H3863 לוּא, H4310 מִי, H4310 מִי, H5377 נָשָׁא, H5382 נָשָׁה",
    3785: "ὄφελον (óphelon | of'-el-on)\n\nDerivation: first person singular of a past tense of G3784\nStrong's: I ought (wish), i.e. (interjection) oh that!\nKJV: —would (to God).\n\nSee: G3784 \n\nCognate Group: G3782 (debt), G3781 (debtor), G3783 (debt), G3784 (behove)\nVariants: ὄφελον",
    3786: "ὄφελος (óphelos | of'-el-os)\n\nDerivation: from ὀφέλλω (to heap up, i.e. accumulate or benefit)\nStrong's: gain\nKJV: —advantageth, profit.\n\nCognate Group: G3782 (debt), G3781 (debtor), G3783 (debt), G3784 (behove)\nVariants: ὄφελος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2974 יָאַל",
    3787: "ὀφθαλμοδουλεία (ophthalmodouleía | of-thal-mod-oo-li'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3788 and G1397\nStrong's: sight-labor, i.e. that needs watching (remissness)\nKJV: —eye-service.\n\nSee: G3788 \nSee: G1397 \n\nCognate Group: G1399 (handmaid), G4889 (fellowservant), G1402 (bring into bondage), G1397 (bondage), G1396 (bring into subjection), G1401 (bond), G2615 (bring into bondage), G3787 (eye-service), G1398 (be in bondage)\nVariants: ὀφθαλμοδουλεία, ὀφθαλμοδουλία",
    3788: "ὀφθαλμός (ophthalmós | of-thal-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3700\nStrong's: the eye (literally or figuratively); by implication, vision; figuratively, envy (from the jealous side-glance)\nKJV: —eye, sight.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3787 (eye-service), G3788 (eye)\nVariants: ὀφθαλμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H308 אֲחַסְבַּי, H4758 מַרְאֶה, H5869 עַיִן, H6079 עַפְעַף, H6440 פָּנִים",
    3789: "ὄφις (óphis | of'-is)\n\nDerivation: probably from G3700 (through the idea of sharpness of vision)\nStrong's: a snake, figuratively, (as a type of sly cunning) an artful malicious person, especially Satan\nKJV: —serpent.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3789 (serpent)\nVariants: ὄφις \nHebrew Equivalents: H660 אֶפְעֶה, H5175 נָחָשׁ, H8314 שָׂרָף",
    3790: "ὀφρῦς (ophrŷs | of-roos')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G3700 (through the idea of the shading or proximity to the organ of vision)\nStrong's: the eye-\"brow\" or forehead, i.e. (figuratively) the brink of a precipice\nKJV: —brow.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3789 (serpent)\nVariants: ὀφρῦς \nHebrew Equivalents: H1354 גַב",
    3791: "ὀχλέω (ochléō | okh-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3793\nStrong's: to mob, i.e. (by implication) to harass\nKJV: —vex.\n\nSee: G3793 \n\nCognate Group: G3789 (serpent)\nVariants: ὀχλέω, ὀχλέομαι",
    3792: "ὀχλοποιέω (ochlopoiéō | okh-lop-oy-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3793 and G4160\nStrong's: to make a crowd, i.e. raise a public disturbance\nKJV: —gather a company.\n\nSee: G3793 \nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G3792 (gather a company), G3793 (company)\nVariants: ὀχλοποιέω",
    3793: "ὄχλος (óchlos | okh'los)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G2192 (meaning a vehicle)\nStrong's: a throng (as borne along); by implication, the rabble; by extension, a class of people; figuratively, a riot\nKJV: —company, multitude, number (of people), people, press.\n\nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G3792 (gather a company), G3793 (company)\nVariants: ὄχλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H1995 הָמֹון, H2428 חַיִל, H2945 טַף, H5971 עַם, H6951 קָהָל, H7235 רָבָה",
    3794: "ὀχύρωμα (ochýrōma | okh-oo'-ro-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a remote derivative of G2192 (meaning to fortify, through the idea of holding safely)\nStrong's: a castle (figuratively, argument)\nKJV: —stronghold.\n\nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G3794 (stronghold)\nVariants: ὀχύρωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H953 בֹּור, H1004 בַּיִת, H1219 בָּצַר, H1964 הֵיכָל, H4013 מִבְצָר, H4525 מַסְגֵּר, H4639 מַעֲשֶׂה, H4679 מְצַד, H4692 מָצֹור, H5797 עֹז, H6877 צְרִיחַ",
    3795: "ὀψάριον (opsárion | op-sar'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of the base of G3702\nStrong's: a relish to other food (as if cooked sauce), i.e. (specially), fish (presumably salted and dried as a condiment)\nKJV: —fish.\n\nSee: G3702 \n\nCognate Group: G3794 (stronghold)\nVariants: ὀψάριον",
    3796: "ὀψέ (opsé | op-seh')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3694 (through the idea of backwardness)\nStrong's: (adverbially) late in the day; by extension, after the close of the day\nKJV: —(at) even, in the end.\n\nSee: G3694 \n\nCognate Group: G3794 (stronghold)\nVariants: ὀψέ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1121 בֵּן, H5399 נֶשֶׁף, H6153 עֶרֶב",
    3797: "ὄψιμος (ópsimos | op'-sim-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3796\nStrong's: later, i.e. vernal (showering)\nKJV: —latter.\n\nSee: G3796 \n\nCognate Group: G3794 (stronghold)\nVariants: ὄψιμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H648 אָפִיל, H4456 מַלְקֹושׁ",
    3798: "ὄψιος (ópsios | op'-see-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3796\nStrong's: late; feminine (as noun) afternoon (early eve) or nightfall (later eve)\nKJV: —even(-ing, (-tide)).\n\nSee: G3796 \n\nCognate Group: G3794 (stronghold)\nVariants: ὄψιος, ὀψία",
    3799: "ὄψις (ópsis | op'-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3700\nStrong's: properly, sight (the act), i.e. (by implication) the visage, an external show\nKJV: —appearance, countenance, face.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G3794 (stronghold)\nVariants: ὄψις \nHebrew Equivalents: H2122 זִיו, H4696 מֵצַח, H4758 מַרְאֶה, H5869 עַיִן, H6285 פֵּאָה, H6754 צֶלֶם, H7613 שְׂאֵת, H8389 תֹּאַר",
    3800: "ὀψώνιον (opsṓnion | op-so'-nee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of the same as G3795\nStrong's: rations for a soldier, i.e. (by extension) his stipend or pay\nKJV: — wages.\n\nSee: G3795 \n\nCognate Group: G3800 (wages)\nVariants: ὀψώνιον",
    3801: "ὁ ὢν καί ὁ ἦν καί ὁ ἐρχόμενος (ho ṑn kaí ho ēn kaí ho erchómenos | ho own kahee ho ane kahee ho er-khom'-en-os)\n\nDerivation: a phrase combining G3588 with the present participle and imperfect of G1510 and the present participle of G2064 by means of G2532\nStrong's: the one being and the one that was and the one coming, i.e. the Eternal, as a divine epithet of Christ\nKJV: —which art (is, was), and (which) wast (is, was), and art (is) to come (shalt be).\n\nSee: G1510 \nSee: G2532 \nSee: G3588 \nSee: G2064 \n\nCognate Group: G3800 (wages)\nVariants: ὁ ὢν καί ὁ ἦν καί ὁ ἐρχόμενος, ὁ, εἰμί, καί, ἔρχομαι",
    3802: "παγιδεύω (pagideúō | pag-id-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3803\nStrong's: to ensnare (figuratively)\nKJV: —entangle.\n\nSee: G3803 \n\nCognate Group: G3803 (snare), G3802 (entangle)\nVariants: παγιδεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3369 יָקֹשׁ, H5367 נָקַשׁ",
    3803: "παγίς (pagís | pag-ece')\n\nDerivation: from G4078\nStrong's: a trap (as fastened by a noose or notch); figuratively, a trick or statagem (temptation)\nKJV: —snare.\n\nSee: G4078 \n\nCognate Group: G3803 (snare), G3802 (entangle)\nVariants: παγίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H3369 יָקֹשׁ, H3619 כְּלוּב, H4170 מֹוקֵשׁ, H4170 מֹוקֵשׁ, H4686 מָצוּד, H6341 פַּח, H7568 רֶשֶׁת, H7722 שֹׁוא",
    3804: "πάθημα (páthēma | path'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G3806\nStrong's: something undergone, i.e. hardship or pain; subjectively, an emotion or influence\nKJV: — affection, affliction, motion, suffering.\n\nSee: G3806 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: πάθημα",
    3805: "παθητός (pathētós | path-ay-tos')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3804\nStrong's: liable (i.e. doomed) to experience pain\nKJV: —suffer.\n\nSee: G3804 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: παθητός",
    3806: "πάθος (páthos | path'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G3958\nStrong's: properly, suffering (\"pathos\"), i.e. (subjectively) a passion (especially concupiscence)\nKJV: — (inordinate) affection, lust.\n\nSee: G3958 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: πάθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H60 אֵבֶל",
    3807: "παιδαγωγός (paidagōgós | pahee-dag-o-gos')\n\nDerivation: from G3816 and a reduplicated form of G71\nStrong's: a boy-leader, i.e. a servant whose office it was to take the children to school; (by implication, (figuratively) a tutor (\"pædagogue\"))\nKJV: — instructor, schoolmaster.\n\nSee: G3816 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G3811 (chasten), G3809 (chastening), G521 (unlearned), G3807 (instructor), G3810 (which corrected)\nVariants: παιδαγωγός",
    3808: "παιδάριον (paidárion | pahee-dar'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G3816\nStrong's: a little boy\nKJV: —child, lad.\n\nSee: G3816 \n\nCognate Group: G3814 (bondmaid), G3813 (child), G3808 (child), G3816 (child), G3812 (of a child)\nVariants: παιδάριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3206 יֶלֶד, H5288 נַעַר, H5650 עֶבֶד",
    3809: "παιδεία (paideía | pahee-di'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3811\nStrong's: tutorage, i.e. education or training; by implication, disciplinary correction\nKJV: —chastening, chastisement, instruction, nurture.\n\nSee: G3811 \n\nCognate Group: G3811 (chasten), G3809 (chastening), G521 (unlearned), G3807 (instructor), G3810 (which corrected)\nVariants: παιδεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H999 בִּינָה, H1248 בַּר, H1847 דַּעַת, H2940 טַעַם, H3928 לִמּוּד, H4148 מוּסָר, H6038 עֲנָוָה, H7626 שֵׁבֶט, H8332 שְׁרֹשׁוּ, H8433 תֹּוכֵחָה",
    3810: "παιδευτής (paideutḗs | pahee-dyoo-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G3811\nStrong's: a trainer, i.e. teacher or (by implication) discipliner\nKJV: —which corrected, instructor.\n\nSee: G3811 \n\nCognate Group: G3811 (chasten), G3809 (chastening), G521 (unlearned), G3807 (instructor), G3810 (which corrected)\nVariants: παιδευτής \nHebrew Equivalents: H4148 מוּסָר",
    3811: "παιδεύω (paideúō | pahee-dyoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3816\nStrong's: to train up a child, i.e. educate, or (by implication), discipline (by punishment)\nKJV: —chasten(-ise), instruct, learn, teach.\n\nSee: G3816 \n\nCognate Group: G3811 (chasten), G3809 (chastening), G521 (unlearned), G3807 (instructor), G3810 (which corrected)\nVariants: παιδεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H631 אָסַר, H995 בִּין, H1986 הָלַם, H3198 יָכַח, H3256 יָסַר, H3381 יָרַד, H3384 יָרָה, H3947 לָקַח, H5062 נָגַף, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6006 עָמַס",
    3812: "παιδιόθεν (paidióthen | pahee-dee-oth'-en)\n\nDerivation: adverb (of source) from G3813\nStrong's: from infancy\nKJV: —of a child.\n\nSee: G3813 \n\nCognate Group: G3814 (bondmaid), G3813 (child), G3808 (child), G3816 (child), G3812 (of a child)\nVariants: παιδιόθεν, παιδόθεν",
    3813: "παιδίον (paidíon | pahee-dee'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter diminutive of G3816\nStrong's: a childling (of either sex), i.e. (properly), an infant, or (by extension) a half-grown boy or girl; figuratively, an immature Christian\nKJV: —(little, young) child, damsel.\n\nSee: G3816 \n\nCognate Group: G3814 (bondmaid), G3813 (child), G3808 (child), G3816 (child), G3812 (of a child)\nVariants: παιδίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1060 בְּכֹור, H1121 בֵּן, H2945 טַף, H3126 יֹונֵק, H3126 יֹונֵק, H3206 יֶלֶד, H5288 נַעַר, H5650 עֶבֶד, H5764 עוּל, H7356 רַחַם",
    3814: "παιδίσκη (paidískē | pahee-dis'-kay)\n\nDerivation: feminine diminutive of G3816\nStrong's: a girl, i.e. (specially), a female slave or servant\nKJV: —bondmaid(-woman), damsel, maid(-en).\n\nSee: G3816 \n\nCognate Group: G3814 (bondmaid), G3813 (child), G3808 (child), G3816 (child), G3812 (of a child)\nVariants: παιδίσκη \nHebrew Equivalents: H519 אָמָה, H3207 יַלְדָּה, H5291 נַעֲרָה, H8198 שִׁפְחָה",
    3815: "παίζω (paízō | paheed'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3816\nStrong's: to sport (as a boy)\nKJV: —play.\n\nSee: G3816 \n\nCognate Group: G3814 (bondmaid), G3813 (child), G3808 (child), G3816 (child), G3812 (of a child)\nVariants: παίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5913 עָכַס, H6711 צָחַק, H7832 שָׂחַק",
    3816: "παῖς (paîs | paheece)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G3817\nStrong's: a boy (as often beaten with impunity), or (by analogy), a girl, and (genitive case) a child; specially, a slave or servant (especially a minister to a king; and by eminence to God)\nKJV: —child, maid(-en), (man) servant, son, young man.\n\nSee: G3817 \n\nCognate Group: G1703 (mocker), G1701 (mocking), G1702 (mock), G3816 (child)\nVariants: παῖς \nHebrew Equivalents: H376 אִישׁ, H582 אֱנֹושׁ, H1121 בֵּן, H2428 חַיִל, H3206 יֶלֶד, H4397 מֲלְאָךְ, H5288 נַעַר, H5291 נַעֲרָה, H5650 עֶבֶד, H5971 עַם",
    3817: "παίω (paíō | pah'-yo)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to hit (as if by a single blow and less violently than G5180); specially, to sting (as a scorpion)\nKJV: —smite, strike.\n\nSee: G5180 \n\nCognate Group: G1703 (mocker), G1701 (mocking), G1702 (mock), G3816 (child)\nVariants: παίω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1104 בָּלַע, H1792 דָּכָא, H4272 מָחַץ, H5062 נָגַף, H5221 נָכָה",
    3818: "Πακατιανή (Pakatianḗ | pak-at-ee-an-ay')\n\nDerivation: feminine of an adjective of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Pacatianian, a section of Phrygia\nKJV: —Pacatiana.\n\nCognate Group: G1703 (mocker), G1701 (mocking), G1702 (mock), G3816 (child)\nVariants: Πακατιανή, Πακατιανός G4092",
    3819: "πάλαι (pálai | pal'-ahee)\n\nDerivation: probably another form for G3825 (through the idea of retrocession)\nStrong's: (adverbially) formerly, or (by relatively) sometime since; (elliptically as adjective) ancient\nKJV: —any while, a great while ago, (of) old, in time past.\n\nSee: G3825 \n\nCognate Group: G3821 (oldness), G3819 (any while), G3822 (decay), G3820 (old)\nVariants: πάλαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H227 אָז, H7350 רָחֹוק",
    3820: "παλαιός (palaiós | pal-ah-yos')\n\nDerivation: from G3819\nStrong's: antique, i.e. not recent, worn out\nKJV: —old.\n\nSee: G3819 \n\nCognate Group: G3821 (oldness), G3819 (any while), G3822 (decay), G3820 (old)\nVariants: παλαιός \nHebrew Equivalents: H227 אָז, H1087 בָּלֶה, H3453 יָשִׁישׁ, H3462 יָשֵׁן, H3462 יָשֵׁן, H6268 עַתִּיק",
    3821: "παλαιότης (palaiótēs | pal-ah-yot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3820\nStrong's: antiquatedness\nKJV: —oldness.\n\nSee: G3820 \n\nCognate Group: G3821 (oldness), G3819 (any while), G3822 (decay), G3820 (old)\nVariants: παλαιότης",
    3822: "παλαιόω (palaióō | pal-ah-yo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3820\nStrong's: to make (passively, become) worn out, or declare obsolete\nKJV: —decay, make (wax) old.\n\nSee: G3820 \n\nCognate Group: G3821 (oldness), G3819 (any while), G3822 (decay), G3820 (old)\nVariants: παλαιόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1086 בָּלָה, H3462 יָשֵׁן, H5034 נָבֵל, H6275 עָתַק",
    3823: "πάλη (pálē | pal'-ay)\n\nDerivation: from πάλλω (to vibrate\nStrong's: another form for G906); wrestling\nKJV: —+ wrestle.\n\nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G3823 (+ wrestle)\nVariants: πάλη",
    3824: "παλιγγενεσία (palingenesía | pal-ing-ghen-es-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3825 and G1078\nStrong's: (spiritual) rebirth (the state or the act), i.e. (figuratively) spiritual renovation; specially, Messianic restoration\nKJV: —regeneration.\n\nSee: G3825 \nSee: G1078 \n\nCognate Group: G1085 (born), G1078 (generation), G3824 (regeneration), G1096 (arise), G581 (being dead), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: παλιγγενεσία, παλινγενεσία",
    3825: "πάλιν (pálin | pal'-in)\n\nDerivation: probably from the same as G3823 (through the idea of oscillatory repetition)\nStrong's: (adverbially) anew, i.e. (of place) back, (of time) once more, or (conjunctionally) furthermore or on the other hand\nKJV: —again.\n\nSee: G3823 \n\nCognate Group: G1085 (born), G1078 (generation), G3824 (regeneration), G1096 (arise), G581 (being dead), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: πάλιν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3254 יָסַף, H5750 עֹוד, H7725 שׁוּב, H7725 שׁוּב",
    3826: "παμπληθεί (pamplētheí | pam-play-thi')\n\nDerivation: dative case (adverb) of a compound of G3956 and G4128\nStrong's: in full multitude, i.e. concertedly or simultaneously\nKJV: —all at once.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G4128 \n\nCognate Group: G1085 (born), G1078 (generation), G3824 (regeneration), G1096 (arise), G581 (being dead), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: παμπληθεί, πανπληθεί",
    3827: "πάμπολυς (pámpolys | pam-pol-ooce)\n\nDerivation: from G3956 and G4183\nStrong's: full many, i.e. immense\nKJV: —very great.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G4183 \n\nCognate Group: G1085 (born), G1078 (generation), G3824 (regeneration), G1096 (arise), G581 (being dead), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: πάμπολυς G4102",
    3828: "Παμφυλία (Pamphylía | pam-fool-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3956 and G4443\nStrong's: every-tribal, i.e. heterogeneous (G5561 being implied); Pamphylia, a region of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Pamphylia.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G4443 \nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G1085 (born), G1078 (generation), G3824 (regeneration), G1096 (arise), G581 (being dead), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: Παμφυλία",
    3829: "πανδοχεῖον (pandocheîon | pan-dokk-i'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed compound of G3956 and a derivative of G1209\nStrong's: all-receptive, i.e. a public lodging-place (caravanserai or khan)\nKJV: —inn.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G1209 \n\nCognate Group: G1085 (born), G1078 (generation), G3824 (regeneration), G1096 (arise), G581 (being dead), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: πανδοχεῖον, πανδοκεῖον",
    3830: "πανδοχεύς (pandocheús | pan-dokh-yoos')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3829\nStrong's: an innkeeper (warden of a caravanserai)\nKJV: —host.\n\nSee: G3829 \n\nCognate Group: G1085 (born), G1078 (generation), G3824 (regeneration), G1096 (arise), G581 (being dead), G1081 (fruit)\nVariants: πανδοχεύς, πανδοκεύς",
    3831: "πανήγυρις (panḗgyris | pan-ay'-goo-ris)\n\nDerivation: from G3956 and a derivative of G58\nStrong's: a mass-meeting, i.e. (figuratively) universal companionship\nKJV: —general assembly.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G58 \n\nCognate Group: G3831 (general assembly)\nVariants: πανήγυρις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4150 מֹועֵד, H6116 עֲצָרָה",
    3832: "πανοικί (panoikí | pan-oy-kee')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3956 and G3624\nStrong's: with the whole family\nKJV: —with all his house.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G3624 \n\nCognate Group: G3831 (general assembly)\nVariants: πανοικί, πανοικεί, πανοικεί/πανοικί \nHebrew Equivalents: H1004 בַּיִת",
    3833: "πανοπλία (panoplía | pan-op-lee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3956 and G3696\nStrong's: full armor (\"panoply\")\nKJV: —all (whole) armour.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G3696 \n\nCognate Group: G3695 (arm self), G3696 (armour), G2528 (arm), G3833 (all armour)\nVariants: πανοπλία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2488 חֲלִיצָה",
    3834: "πανουργία (panourgía | pan-oorg-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3835\nStrong's: adroitness, i.e. (in a bad sense) trickery or sophistry\nKJV: —(cunning) craftiness, subtilty.\n\nSee: G3835 \n\nCognate Group: G3835 (crafty), G3834 (craftiness)\nVariants: πανουργία \nHebrew Equivalents: H6195 עׇרְמָה",
    3835: "πανοῦργος (panoûrgos | pan-oor'-gos)\n\nDerivation: from G3956 and G2041\nStrong's: all-working, i.e. adroit (shrewd)\nKJV: —crafty.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G3835 (crafty), G3834 (craftiness)\nVariants: πανοῦργος \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2450 חָכָם, H3045 יָדַע, H6175 עָרוּם, H6191 עָרַם",
    3836: "πανταχόθεν (pantachóthen | pan-takh-oth'-en)\n\nDerivation: adverb (of source) from G3837\nStrong's: from all directions\nKJV: —from every quarter.\n\nSee: G3837 \n\nCognate Group: G3835 (crafty), G3834 (craftiness)\nVariants: πανταχόθεν G4115",
    3837: "πανταχοῦ (pantachoû | pan-takh-oo')\n\nDerivation: genitive case (as adverb of place) of a presumed derivative of G3956\nStrong's: universally\nKJV: —in all places, everywhere.\n\nSee: G3956 \n\nCognate Group: G3835 (crafty), G3834 (craftiness)\nVariants: πανταχοῦ, πανταχῇ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3637 כָּלַם",
    3838: "παντελής (pantelḗs | pan-tel-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G3956 and G5056\nStrong's: full-ended, i.e. entire (neuter as noun, completion)\nKJV: —+ in (no) wise, uttermost.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G5056 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: παντελής",
    3839: "πάντη (pántē | pan'-tay)\n\nDerivation: adverb (of manner) from G3956\nStrong's: wholly\nKJV: —always.\n\nSee: G3956 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: πάντη, πάντῃ, πάντή",
    3840: "πάντοθεν (pántothen | pan-toth'-en)\n\nDerivation: adverb (of source) from G3956\nStrong's: from (i.e. on) all sides\nKJV: —on every side, round about.\n\nSee: G3956 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: πάντοθεν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3615 כָּלָה",
    3841: "παντοκράτωρ (pantokrátōr | pan-tok-rat'-ore)\n\nDerivation: from G3956 and G2904\nStrong's: the all-ruling, i.e. God (as absolute and universal sovereign)\nKJV: —Almighty, Omnipotent.\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G2904 \n\nCognate Group: G2888 (ruler), G2901 (be strengthened), G3841 (Almighty), G2900 (mighty), G2903 (most excellent), G2902 (hold), G2904 (dominion)\nVariants: παντοκράτωρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7706 שַׁדַּי",
    3842: "πάντοτε (pántote | pan'-tot-eh)\n\nDerivation: from G3956 and G3753\nStrong's: every when, i.e. at all times\nKJV: —alway(-s), ever(-more).\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G3753 \n\nCognate Group: G2888 (ruler), G2901 (be strengthened), G3841 (Almighty), G2900 (mighty), G2903 (most excellent), G2902 (hold), G2904 (dominion)\nVariants: πάντοτε",
    3843: "πάντως (pántōs | pan'-toce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G3956\nStrong's: entirely; specially, at all events, (with negative, following) in no event\nKJV: —by all means, altogether, at all, needs, no doubt, in (no) wise, surely.\n\nSee: G3956 \n\nCognate Group: G2888 (ruler), G2901 (be strengthened), G3841 (Almighty), G2900 (mighty), G2903 (most excellent), G2902 (hold), G2904 (dominion)\nVariants: πάντως",
    3844: "παρά (pará | par-ah')\n\nDerivation: a primary preposition\nStrong's: properly, near; i.e. (with genitive case) from beside (literally or figuratively), (with dative case) at (or in) the vicinity of (objectively or subjectively), (with accusative case) to the proximity with (local (especially beyond or opposed to) or causal (on account of)\nKJV: —above, against, among, at, before, by, contrary to, X friend, from, + give (such things as they), + that (she) had, X his, in, more than, nigh unto, (out) of, past, save, side…by, in the sight of, than, (there-)fore, with. In compounds it retains the same variety of application.\n\nCognate Group: G3844 (above)\nVariants: παρά",
    3845: "παραβαίνω (parabaínō | par-ab-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the base of G939\nStrong's: to go contrary to, i.e. violate a command\nKJV: —(by) transgress(-ion).\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G3845 (transgress), G5233 (go beyond), G531 (unchangeable), G3847 (breaking), G3848 (breaker)\nVariants: παραβαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4603 מָעַל, H4784 מָרָה, H5493 סוּר, H5674 עָבַר, H6565 פָּרַר, H6586 פָּשַׁע, H7847 שָׂטָה",
    3846: "παραβάλλω (parabállō | par-ab-al'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G906\nStrong's: to throw alongside, i.e. (reflexively) to reach a place, or (figuratively) to liken\nKJV: —arrive, compare.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G3845 (transgress), G5233 (go beyond), G531 (unchangeable), G3847 (breaking), G3848 (breaker)\nVariants: παραβάλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5186 נָטָה, H7997 שָׁלַל",
    3847: "παράβασις (parábasis | par-ab'-as-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3845\nStrong's: violation\nKJV: —breaking, transgression.\n\nSee: G3845 \n\nCognate Group: G3845 (transgress), G5233 (go beyond), G531 (unchangeable), G3847 (breaking), G3848 (breaker)\nVariants: παράβασις \nHebrew Equivalents: H7846 שֵׂט",
    3848: "παραβάτης (parabátēs | par-ab-at'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3845\nStrong's: a violator\nKJV: —breaker, transgress(-or).\n\nSee: G3845 \n\nCognate Group: G3845 (transgress), G5233 (go beyond), G531 (unchangeable), G3847 (breaking), G3848 (breaker)\nVariants: παραβάτης, ἀποστάτης",
    3849: "παραβιάζομαι (parabiázomai | par-ab-ee-ad'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the middle voice of G971\nStrong's: to force contrary to (nature), i.e. compel (by entreaty)\nKJV: —constrain.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G971 \n\nCognate Group: G3845 (transgress), G5233 (go beyond), G531 (unchangeable), G3847 (breaking), G3848 (breaker)\nVariants: παραβιάζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2102 זוּד, H2864 חָתַר",
    3850: "παραβολή (parabolḗ | par-ab-ol-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G3846\nStrong's: a similitude (\"parable\"), i.e. (symbolic) fictitious narrative (of common life conveying a moral), apothegm or adage\nKJV: —comparison, figure, parable, proverb.\n\nSee: G3846 \n\nCognate Group: G3850 (comparison)\nVariants: παραβολή \nHebrew Equivalents: H4911 מָשַׁל",
    3851: "παραβουλεύομαι (parabouleúomai | par-ab-ool-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the middle voice of G1011\nStrong's: to misconsult, i.e. disregard\nKJV: —not (to) regard(-ing).\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1011 \n\nCognate Group: G3850 (comparison)\nVariants: παραβουλεύομαι, παραβολεύομαι",
    3852: "παραγγελία (parangelía | par-ang-gel-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3853\nStrong's: a mandate\nKJV: —charge, command.\n\nSee: G3853 \n\nCognate Group: G3852 (charge), G3853 (charge)\nVariants: παραγγελία",
    3853: "παραγγέλλω (parangéllō | par-ang-gel'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the base of G32\nStrong's: to transmit a message, i.e. (by implication) to enjoin\nKJV: —(give in) charge, (give) command(-ment), declare.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G32 \n\nCognate Group: G3852 (charge), G3853 (charge)\nVariants: παραγγέλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H2199 זָעַק, H3289 יָעַץ, H5674 עָבַר, H6817 צָעַק, H8085 שָׁמַע",
    3854: "παραγίνομαι (paragínomai | par-ag-in'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1096\nStrong's: to become near, i.e. approach (have arrived); by implication, to appear publicly\nKJV: —come, go, be present.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1096 \n\nCognate Group: G3852 (charge), G3853 (charge)\nVariants: παραγίνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1961 הָיָה, H1980 הָלַךְ, H5060 נָגַע, H7725 שׁוּב",
    3855: "παράγω (parágō | par-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G71\nStrong's: to lead near, i.e. (reflexively or intransitively) to go along or away\nKJV: —depart, pass (away, by, forth).\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: παράγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5423 נָתַק, H5674 עָבַר, H6148 עָרַב",
    3856: "παραδειγματίζω (paradeigmatízō | par-ad-igue-mat-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1165\nStrong's: to show alongside (the public), i.e. expose to infamy\nKJV: —make a public example, put to an open shame.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1165 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: παραδειγματίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2554 חָמַס, H3363 יָקַע, H7207 רַאֲוָה",
    3857: "παράδεισος (parádeisos | par-ad'-i-sos)\n\nDerivation: of Oriental origin (compare H6508)\nStrong's: a park, i.e. (specially), an Eden (place of future happiness, \"paradise\")\nKJV: —paradise.\nSee: H6508\n\nCognate Group: G3857 (paradise)\nVariants: παράδεισος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1588 גַּן, H5731 עֵדֶן, H6508 פַּרְדֵּס",
    3858: "παραδέχομαι (paradéchomai | par-ad-ekh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1209\nStrong's: to accept near, i.e. admit or (by implication) delight in\nKJV: —receive.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1209 \n\nCognate Group: G3857 (paradise)\nVariants: παραδέχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5375 נָשָׂא, H7521 רָצָה",
    3859: "παραδιατριβή (paradiatribḗ | par-ad-ee-at-ree-bay')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3844 and G1304\nStrong's: misemployment, i.e. meddlesomeness\nKJV: —perverse disputing.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1304 \n\nCognate Group: G3857 (paradise)\nVariants: παραδιατριβή, διαπαρατριβή",
    3860: "παραδίδωμι (paradídōmi | par-ad-id'-o-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1325\nStrong's: to surrender, i.e yield up, intrust, transmit\nKJV: —betray, bring forth, cast, commit, deliver (up), give (over, up), hazard, put in prison, recommend.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1325 \n\nCognate Group: G1432 (without a cause), G3860 (betray), G467 (recompense), G3862 (ordinance), G1325 (adventure), G468 (recompense), G1431 (gift), G591 (deliver), G1434 (gift), G469 (reward), G1433 (give), G1435 (gift)\nVariants: παραδίδωμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H2505 חָלַק, H2865 חָתַת, H2904 טוּל, H3051 יָהַב, H3381 יָרַד, H3607 כָּלָא, H3960 לָשַׁן, H4042 מָגַן, H4376 מָכַר, H4487 מָנָה, H4672 מָצָא, H5064 נָגַר, H5117 נוּחַ, H5221 נָכָה, H5414 נָתַן, H5462 סָגַר, H5534 סָכַר, H6030 עָנָה, H6168 עָרָה, H6293 פָּגַע, H7136 קָרָה, H7411 רָמָה, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8610 תָּפַשׂ, H8628 תָּקַע",
    3861: "παράδοξος (parádoxos | par-ad'-ox-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1391 (in the sense of seeming)\nStrong's: contrary to expectation, i.e. extraordinary (\"paradox\")\nKJV: —strange.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1391 \n\nCognate Group: G1391 (dignity), G3861 (strange), G1741 (glorious), G1740 (glorify), G4888 (glorify together), G1392 (glorify), G1380 (be accounted)\nVariants: παράδοξος",
    3862: "παράδοσις (parádosis | par-ad'-os-is)\n\nDerivation: from G3860\nStrong's: transmission, i.e. (concretely) a precept; specially, the Jewish traditionary law\nKJV: —ordinance, tradition.\n\nSee: G3860 \n\nCognate Group: G1432 (without a cause), G3860 (betray), G467 (recompense), G3862 (ordinance), G1325 (adventure), G468 (recompense), G1431 (gift), G591 (deliver), G1434 (gift), G469 (reward), G1433 (give), G1435 (gift)\nVariants: παράδοσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H612 אֵסוּר, H5414 נָתַן",
    3863: "παραζηλόω (parazēlóō | par-ad-zay-lo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2206\nStrong's: to stimulate alongside, i.e. excite to rivalry\nKJV: —provoke to emulation (jealousy).\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2206 \n\nCognate Group: G3863 (provoke to emulation), G2205 (emulation), G2207 (zealous), G2206 (affect)\nVariants: παραζηλόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2734 חָרָה, H7065 קָנָא",
    3864: "παραθαλάσσιος (parathalássios | par-ath-al-as'-see-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2281\nStrong's: along the sea, i.e. maritime (lacustrine)\nKJV: —upon the sea coast.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2281 \n\nCognate Group: G3863 (provoke to emulation), G2205 (emulation), G2207 (zealous), G2206 (affect)\nVariants: παραθαλάσσιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2348 חֹוף, H5921 עַל",
    3865: "παραθεωρέω (paratheōréō | par-ath-eh-o-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2334\nStrong's: to overlook or disregard\nKJV: —neglect.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2334 \n\nCognate Group: G3863 (provoke to emulation), G2205 (emulation), G2207 (zealous), G2206 (affect)\nVariants: παραθεωρέω",
    3866: "παραθήκη (parathḗkē | par-ath-ay'-kay)\n\nDerivation: from G3908\nStrong's: a deposit, i.e. (figuratively) trust\nKJV: —committed unto.\n\nSee: G3908 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραθήκη \nHebrew Equivalents: H6487 פִּקָּדֹון",
    3867: "παραινέω (parainéō | par-ahee-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G134\nStrong's: to mispraise, i.e. recommend or advise (a different course)\nKJV: —admonish, exhort.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G134 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραινέω",
    3868: "παραιτέομαι (paraitéomai | par-ahee-teh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the middle voice of G154\nStrong's: to beg off, i.e. deprecate, decline, shun\nKJV: —avoid, (make) excuse, intreat, refuse, reject.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G154 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: παραιτέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1245 בָּקַשׁ, H2603 חָנַן, H7592 שָׁאַל",
    3869: "παρακαθίζω (parakathízō | par-ak-ath-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2523\nStrong's: to sit down near\nKJV: —sit.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2523 \n\nCognate Group: G3868 (avoid), G155 (petition), G523 (ask again), G1809 (desire), G154 (ask)\nVariants: παρακαθίζω, παρακαθέζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3427 יָשַׁב",
    3870: "παρακαλέω (parakaléō | par-ak-al-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2564\nStrong's: to call near, i.e. invite, invoke (by imploration, hortation or consolation)\nKJV: —beseech, call for, (be of good) comfort, desire, (give) exhort(-ation), intreat, pray.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2564 \n\nCognate Group: G3875 (advocate), G3870 (beseech), G3874 (comfort), G870 (without fear)\nVariants: παρακαλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H553 אָמַץ, H559 אָמַר, H2388 חָזַק, H2492 חָלַם, H4427 מָלַךְ, H5090 נָהַג, H5095 נָהַל, H5130 נוּף, H5148 נָחָה, H5162 נָחַם, H5496 סוּת, H7121 קָרָא, H7355 רָחַם, H7673 שָׁבַת, H8173 שָׁעַע",
    3871: "παρακαλύπτω (parakalýptō | par-ak-al-oop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2572\nStrong's: to cover alongside, i.e. veil (figuratively)\nKJV: —hide.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2572 \n\nCognate Group: G3875 (advocate), G3870 (beseech), G3874 (comfort), G870 (without fear)\nVariants: παρακαλύπτω, παρακαλύπτομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5956 עָלַם, H6342 פָּחַד",
    3872: "παρακαταθήκη (parakatathḗkē | par-ak-at-ath-ay'-kay)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3844 and G2698\nStrong's: something put down alongside, i.e. a deposit (sacred trust)\nKJV: —that (thing) which is committed (un-)to (trust).\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2698 \n\nCognate Group: G3875 (advocate), G3870 (beseech), G3874 (comfort), G870 (without fear)\nVariants: παρακαταθήκη G4153 \nHebrew Equivalents: H4399 מְלָאכָה",
    3873: "παράκειμαι (parákeimai | par-ak'-i-mahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2749\nStrong's: to lie near, i.e. be at hand (figuratively, be prompt or easy)\nKJV: —be present.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2749 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: παράκειμαι",
    3874: "παράκλησις (paráklēsis | par-ak'-lay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3870\nStrong's: imploration, hortation, solace\nKJV: —comfort, consolation, exhortation, intreaty.\n\nSee: G3870 \n\nCognate Group: G3875 (advocate), G3870 (beseech), G3874 (comfort), G870 (without fear)\nVariants: παράκλησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H5162 נָחַם, H8469 תַּחֲנוּן",
    3875: "παράκλητος (paráklētos | par-ak'-lay-tos)\n\n\nStrong's: an intercessor, consoler\nKJV: —advocate, comforter.\n\nCognate Group: G3875 (advocate), G3870 (beseech), G3874 (comfort), G870 (without fear)\nVariants: παράκλητος",
    3876: "παρακοή (parakoḗ | par-ak-o-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G3878\nStrong's: inattention, i.e. (by implication) disobedience\nKJV: —disobedience.\n\nSee: G3878 \n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: παρακοή",
    3877: "παρακολουθέω (parakolouthéō | par-ak-ol-oo-theh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G190\nStrong's: to follow near, i.e. (figuratively) attend (as a result), trace out, conform to\nKJV: —attain, follow, fully know, have understanding.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G190 \n\nCognate Group: G1811 (follow), G3877 (attain), G1872 (follow), G4870 (follow), G190 (follow)\nVariants: παρακολουθέω",
    3878: "παρακούω (parakoúō | par-ak-oo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G191\nStrong's: to mishear, i.e. (by implication) to disobey\nKJV: —neglect to hear.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G191 \n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: παρακούω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2790 חָרַשׁ, H5674 עָבַר, H6213 עָשָׂה, H8085 שָׁמַע",
    3879: "παρακύπτω (parakýptō | par-ak-oop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2955\nStrong's: to bend beside, i.e. lean over (so as to peer within)\nKJV: —look (into), stoop down.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2955 \n\nCognate Group: G3879 (look)\nVariants: παρακύπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7688 שָׁגַח, H8259 שָׁקַף",
    3880: "παραλαμβάνω (paralambánō | par-al-am-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2983\nStrong's: to receive near, i.e. associate with oneself (in any familiar or intimate act or relation); by analogy, to assume an office; figuratively, to learn\nKJV: —receive, take (unto, with).\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: παραλαμβάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3423 יָרַשׁ, H3947 לָקַח, H5090 נָהַג, H6902 קְבַל",
    3881: "παραλέγομαι (paralégomai | par-al-eg'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the middle voice of G3004 (in its original sense)\nStrong's: (specially), to lay one's course near, i.e. sail past\nKJV: —pass, sail by.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: παραλέγομαι, παραλέγω",
    3882: "παράλιος (parálios | par-al'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G251\nStrong's: beside the salt (sea), i.e. maritime\nKJV: —sea coast.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G251 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: παράλιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H2344 חֹול, H2348 חֹוף, H2348 חֹוף, H3220 יָם",
    3883: "παραλλαγή (parallagḗ | par-al-lag-ay')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3844 and G236\nStrong's: transmutation (of phase or orbit), i.e. (figuratively) fickleness\nKJV: —variableness.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G236 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: παραλλαγή \nHebrew Equivalents: H7697 שִׁגָּעֹון",
    3884: "παραλογίζομαι (paralogízomai | par-al-og-id'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3049\nStrong's: to misreckon, i.e. delude\nKJV: —beguile, deceive.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3049 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: παραλογίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2498 חָלַף, H2926 טָלַל, H7411 רָמָה",
    3885: "παραλυτικός (paralytikós | par-al-oo-tee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G3886\nStrong's: as if dissolved, i.e. \"paralytic\"\nKJV: —that had (sick of) the palsy.\n\nSee: G3886 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: παραλυτικός, παράλυτος",
    3886: "παραλύω (paralýō | par-al-oo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3089\nStrong's: to loosen beside, i.e. relax (perfect passive participle, paralyzed or enfeebled)\nKJV: —feeble, sick of the (taken with) palsy.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3089 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: παραλύω, παραλύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H926 בָּהַל, H1920 הָדַף, H2490 חָלַל, H2865 חָתַת, H3782 כָּשַׁל, H3811 לָאָה, H5358 נָקַם, H5414 נָתַן, H6131 עָקַר, H6533 פָּרַם, H6605 פָּתַח, H7503 רָפָה",
    3887: "παραμένω (paraménō | par-am-en'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3306\nStrong's: to stay near, i.e. remain (literally, tarry; or figuratively, be permanent, persevere)\nKJV: —abide, continue.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3306 \nSee: G3306 \nSee: G3844 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: παραμένω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3427 יָשַׁב, H5975 עָמַד",
    3888: "παραμυθέομαι (paramythéomai | par-am-oo-theh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the middle voice of a derivative of G3454\nStrong's: to relate near, i.e. (by implication) encourage, console\nKJV: —comfort.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3454 \n\nCognate Group: G3889 (comfort), G3890 (comfort), G3888 (comfort)\nVariants: παραμυθέομαι",
    3889: "παραμυθία (paramythía | par-am-oo-thee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3888\nStrong's: consolation (properly, abstract)\nKJV: —comfort.\n\nSee: G3888 \n\nCognate Group: G3889 (comfort), G3890 (comfort), G3888 (comfort)\nVariants: παραμυθία",
    3890: "παραμύθιον (paramýthion | par-am-oo'-thee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G3889\nStrong's: consolation (properly, concretely)\nKJV: —comfort.\n\nSee: G3889 \n\nCognate Group: G3889 (comfort), G3890 (comfort), G3888 (comfort)\nVariants: παραμύθιον",
    3891: "παρανομέω (paranoméō | par-an-om-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3844 and G3551\nStrong's: to be opposed to law, i.e. to transgress\nKJV: —contrary to law.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3551 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: παρανομέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל, H3887 לוּץ, H5765 עֲוַל, H5956 עָלַם",
    3892: "παρανομία (paranomía | par-an-om-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3891\nStrong's: transgression\nKJV: —iniquity.\n\nSee: G3891 \n\nCognate Group: G459 (without law), G3892 (iniquity), G3891 (contrary to law), G3545 (lawfully), G460 (without law), G3551 (law), G3547 (doctor of the law), G3550 (lawgiver), G458 (iniquity), G1772 (lawful), G3549 (establish), G3548 (giving of the law), G3544 (about the law)\nVariants: παρανομία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4209 מְזִמָּה, H5771 עָוֺן, H6102 עָצֵל",
    3893: "παραπικραίνω (parapikraínō | par-ap-ik-rah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G4087\nStrong's: to embitter alongside, i.e. (figuratively) to exasperate\nKJV: —provoke.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4087 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: παραπικραίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3707 כַּעַס, H4775 מָרַד, H4784 מָרָה, H4806 מְרִיא, H5637 סָרַר",
    3894: "παραπικρασμός (parapikrasmós | par-ap-ik-ras-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3893\nStrong's: irritation\nKJV: —provocation.\n\nSee: G3893 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: παραπικρασμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4808 מְרִיבָה",
    3895: "παραπίπτω (parapíptō | par-ap-ip'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G4098\nStrong's: to fall aside, i.e. (figuratively) to apostatize\nKJV: —fall away.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4098 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παραπίπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H816 אָשַׁם, H4603 מָעַל, H5307 נָפַל",
    3896: "παραπλέω (parapléō | par-ap-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G4126\nStrong's: to sail near\nKJV: —sail by.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4126 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παραπλέω",
    3897: "παραπλήσιον (paraplḗsion | par-ap-lay'-see-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G3844 and the base of G4139 (as adverb)\nStrong's: close by, i.e. (figuratively) almost\nKJV: —nigh unto.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4139 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παραπλήσιον, παραπλήσιος",
    3898: "παραπλησίως (paraplēsíōs | par-ap-lay-see'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from the same as G3897\nStrong's: in a manner near by, i.e. (figuratively) similarly\nKJV: —likewise.\n\nSee: G3897 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παραπλησίως",
    3899: "παραπορεύομαι (paraporeúomai | par-ap-or-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G4198\nStrong's: to travel near\nKJV: —go, pass (by).\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4198 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παραπορεύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1980 הָלַךְ, H5674 עָבַר, H5975 עָמַד",
    3900: "παράπτωμα (paráptōma | par-ap'-to-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3895\nStrong's: a side-slip (lapse or deviation), i.e. (unintentional) error or (wilful) transgression\nKJV: —fall, fault, offence, sin, trespass.\n\nSee: G3895 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παράπτωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H57 אָבֵל, H2248 חֲבוּלָה, H4604 מַעַל, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H7691 שְׁגִיאָה, H7960 שָׁלוּ",
    3901: "παραῤῥυέω (pararrhyéō | par-ar-hroo-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the alternate of G4482\nStrong's: to flow by, i.e. (figuratively) carelessly pass (miss)\nKJV: —let slip.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4482 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παραῤῥυέω, παραρρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2986 יָבַל, H3869 לוּז",
    3902: "παράσημος (parásēmos | par-as'-ay-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the base of G4591\nStrong's: side-marked, i.e. labelled (with a badge (figure-head) of a ship)\nKJV: —sign.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4591 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παράσημος",
    3903: "παρασκευάζω (paraskeuázō | par-ask-yoo-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and a derivative of G4632\nStrong's: to furnish aside, i.e. get ready\nKJV: —prepare self, be (make) ready.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G4632 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παρασκευάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1305 בָּרַר, H1624 גָּרָה, H2505 חָלַק, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6257 עָתַד, H6424 פָּלַס, H6942 קָדַשׁ",
    3904: "παρασκευή (paraskeuḗ | par-ask-yoo-ay')\n\nDerivation: as if from G3903\nStrong's: readiness\nKJV: —preparation.\n\nSee: G3903 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παρασκευή \nHebrew Equivalents: H5656 עֲבֹדָה",
    3905: "παρατείνω (parateínō | par-at-i'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and teino (to stretch)\nStrong's: to extend along, i.e. prolong (in point of time)\nKJV: —continue.\n\nSee: G3844 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: παρατείνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1028 בֵּית הָרָן, H3411 יְרֵכָה, H4900 מָשַׁךְ, H4901 מֶשֶׁךְ, H8259 שָׁקַף",
    3906: "παρατηρέω (paratēréō | par-at-ay-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G5083\nStrong's: to inspect alongside, i.e. note insidiously or scrupulously\nKJV: —observe, watch.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G5083 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: παρατηρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2161 זָמַם, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    3907: "παρατήρησις (paratḗrēsis | par-at-ay'-ray-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G3906\nStrong's: inspection, i.e. ocular evidence\nKJV: —obervation.\n\nSee: G3906 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: παρατήρησις",
    3908: "παρατίθημι (paratíthēmi | par-at-ith'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G5087\nStrong's: to place alongside, i.e. present (food, truth); by implication, to deposit (as a trust or for protection)\nKJV: —allege, commend, commit (the keeping of), put forth, set before.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παρατίθημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3322 יָצַג, H3455 יָשַׂם, H3738 כָּרָה, H5414 נָתַן, H6040 עֳנִי, H6485 פָּקַד, H7760 שׂוּם",
    3909: "παρατυγχάνω (paratynchánō | par-at-oong-khan'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G5177\nStrong's: to chance near, i.e. fall in with\nKJV: —meet with.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G5177 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παρατυγχάνω",
    3910: "παραυτίκα (parautíka | par-ow-tee'-kah)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and a derivative of G846\nStrong's: at the very instant, i.e. momentary\nKJV: —but for a moment.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G846 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραυτίκα",
    3911: "παραφέρω (paraphérō | par-af-er'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G5342 (including its alternate forms)\nStrong's: to bear along or aside, i.e. carry off (literally or figuratively); by implication, to avert\nKJV: —remove, take away.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραφέρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל, H5674 עָבַר",
    3912: "παραφρονέω (paraphronéō | par-af-ron-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G5426\nStrong's: to misthink, i.e. be insane (silly)\nKJV: —as a fool.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G5426 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραφρονέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5637 סָרַר",
    3913: "παραφρονία (paraphronía | par-af-ron-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3912\nStrong's: insanity, i.e. foolhardiness\nKJV: —madness.\n\nSee: G3912 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραφρονία, παράνοια, παραφροσύνη",
    3914: "παραχειμάζω (paracheimázō | par-akh-i-mad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G5492\nStrong's: to winter near, i.e. stay with over the rainy season\nKJV: —winter.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G5492 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραχειμάζω",
    3915: "παραχειμασία (paracheimasía | par-akh-i-mas-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3914\nStrong's: a wintering over\nKJV: —winter in.\n\nSee: G3914 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραχειμασία",
    3916: "παραχρῆμα (parachrēma | par-akh-ray'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G5536 (in its original sense)\nStrong's: at the thing itself, i.e. instantly\nKJV: —forthwith, immediately, presently, straightway, soon.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G5536 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: παραχρῆμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6597 פִּתְאֹום, H6621 פֶּתַע",
    3917: "πάρδαλις (párdalis | par'-dal-is)\n\nDerivation: feminine of πάρδος (a panther)\nStrong's: a leopard\nKJV: —leopard.\n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: πάρδαλις \nHebrew Equivalents: H5246 נָמֵר",
    3918: "πάρειμι (páreimi | par'-i-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1510 (including its various forms)\nStrong's: to be near, i.e. at hand; neuter present participle (singular) time being, or (plural) property\nKJV: —come, X have, be here, + lack, (be here) present.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1510 \n\nCognate Group: G3952 (coming), G3918 (come)\nVariants: πάρειμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H2009 הִנֵּה, H2363 חוּשׁ, H4291 מְטָא, H5060 נָגַע, H5844 עָטָה, H7130 קֶרֶב",
    3919: "παρεισάγω (pareiságō | par-ice-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1521\nStrong's: to lead in aside, i.e. introduce surreptitiously\nKJV: —privily bring in.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1521 \n\nCognate Group: G3919 (privily bring in), G3920 (unawares brought in)\nVariants: παρεισάγω",
    3920: "παρείσακτος (pareísaktos | par-ice'-ak-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G3919\nStrong's: smuggled in\nKJV: —unawares brought in.\n\nSee: G3919 \n\nCognate Group: G3919 (privily bring in), G3920 (unawares brought in)\nVariants: παρείσακτος",
    3921: "παρεισδύνω (pareisdýnō | par-ice-doo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and a compound of G1519 and G1416\nStrong's: to settle in alongside, i.e. lodge stealthily\nKJV: —creep in unawares.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1519 \nSee: G1416 \n\nCognate Group: G3919 (privily bring in), G3920 (unawares brought in)\nVariants: παρεισδύνω, παρεισδύω, παρεισδύ(ν)ω",
    3922: "παρεισέρχομαι (pareisérchomai | par-ice-er'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1525\nStrong's: to come in alongside, i.e. supervene additionally or steathily\nKJV: —come in privily, enter.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1525 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: παρεισέρχομαι",
    3923: "παρεισφέρω (pareisphérō | par-ice-fer'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1533\nStrong's: to bear in alongside, i.e. introduce simultaneously\nKJV: —give.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1533 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: παρεισφέρω",
    3924: "παρεκτός (parektós | par-ek-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1622\nStrong's: near outside, i.e. besides\nKJV: —except, saving, without.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1622 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: παρεκτός",
    3925: "παρεμβολή (parembolḗ | par-em-bol-ay')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3844 and G1685\nStrong's: a throwing in beside (juxtaposition), i.e. (specially), battle-array, encampment or barracks (tower Antonia)\nKJV: —army, camp, castle.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1685 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: παρεμβολή \nHebrew Equivalents: H1995 הָמֹון, H4264 מַחֲנֶה, H4411 מָלֹון, H4634 מַעֲרָכָה, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H4931 מִשְׁמֶרֶת, H5712 עֵדָה",
    3926: "παρενοχλέω (parenochléō | par-en-okh-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G1776\nStrong's: to harass further, i.e. annoy\nKJV: —trouble.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1776 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: παρενοχλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H509 אָלַץ, H2730 חָרֵד, H3811 לָאָה, H4834 מָרַץ, H6687 צוּף, H7264 רָגַז",
    3927: "παρεπίδημος (parepídēmos | par-ep-id'-ay-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the base of G1927\nStrong's: an alien alongside, i.e. a resident foreigner\nKJV: —pilgrim, stranger.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G1927 \n\nCognate Group: G3927 (pilgrim), G1218 (people), G1553 (be absent), G1736 (be at home)\nVariants: παρεπίδημος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8453 תֹּושָׁב",
    3928: "παρέρχομαι (parérchomai | par-er'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2064\nStrong's: to come near or aside, i.e. to approach (arrive), go by (or away), (figuratively) perish or neglect, (causative) avert\nKJV: —come (forth), go, pass (away, by, over), past, transgress.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2064 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: παρέρχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2308 חָדַל, H2498 חָלַף, H2559 חָמַק, H3318 יָצָא, H3426 יֵשׁ, H3615 כָּלָה, H5437 סָבַב, H5493 סוּר, H5674 עָבַר, H5710 עָדָה, H5927 עָלָה, H5956 עָלַם, H5975 עָמַד, H6452 פָּסַח, H8133 שְׁנָא",
    3929: "πάρεσις (páresis | par'-es-is)\n\nDerivation: from G2935\nStrong's: praetermission, i.e. toleration\nKJV: —remission.\n\nSee: G2935 \n\nCognate Group: G3935 (hang down), G3929 (remission), G859 (deliverance), G863 (cry)\nVariants: πάρεσις",
    3930: "παρέχω (paréchō | par-ekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2192\nStrong's: to hold near, i.e. present, afford, exhibit, furnish occasion\nKJV: —bring, do, give, keep, minister, offer, shew, + trouble.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G3935 (hang down), G3929 (remission), G859 (deliverance), G863 (cry)\nVariants: παρέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3811 לָאָה, H5674 עָבַר, H5975 עָמַד, H8252 שָׁקַט",
    3931: "παρηγορία (parēgoría | par-ay-gor-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3844 and a derivative of G58 (meaning to harangue an assembly)\nStrong's: an address alongside, i.e. (specially), consolation\nKJV: —comfort.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G58 \n\nCognate Group: G3935 (hang down), G3929 (remission), G859 (deliverance), G863 (cry)\nVariants: παρηγορία",
    3932: "παρθενία (parthenía | par-then-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3933\nStrong's: maidenhood\nKJV: —virginity.\n\nSee: G3933 \n\nCognate Group: G3935 (hang down), G3929 (remission), G859 (deliverance), G863 (cry)\nVariants: παρθενία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5271 נָעוּר",
    3933: "παρθένος (parthénos | par-then'-os)\n\nDerivation: of unknown origin\nStrong's: a maiden; by implication, an unmarried daughter\nKJV: —virgin.\n\nCognate Group: G3933 (virgin)\nVariants: παρθένος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1330 בְּתוּלָה, H5288 נַעַר, H5291 נַעֲרָה, H5959 עַלְמָה",
    3934: "Πάρθος (Párthos | par'-thos)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: a Parthian, i.e. inhabitant of Parthia\nKJV: —Parthian.\n\nCognate Group: G3933 (virgin)\nVariants: Πάρθος, Πάρθοι",
    3935: "παρίημι (paríēmi | par-ee'-ay-mi)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and hiemi (to send)\nStrong's: to let by, i.e. relax\nKJV: —hang down.\n\nSee: G3844 \n\nCognate Group: G3935 (hang down), G3929 (remission), G859 (deliverance), G863 (cry)\nVariants: παρίημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H926 בָּהַל, H2308 חָדַל, H2310 חָדֵל, H3811 לָאָה, H5800 עָזַב, H6566 פָּרַשׂ, H7330 רָזֶה, H7503 רָפָה",
    3936: "παρίστημι (parístēmi | par-is'-tay-mee), or prolonged παριστάνω (paristánō | par-is-tan'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G2476\nStrong's: to stand beside, i.e. (transitively) to exhibit, proffer, (specially), recommend, (figuratively) substantiate; or (intransitively) to be at hand (or ready), aid\nKJV: —assist, bring before, command, commend, give presently, present, prove, provide, shew, stand (before, by, here, up, with), yield.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G3936 (assist)\nVariants: παρίστημι, παριστάνω, παρίστημι/παριστάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H24 אָבִיב, H1310 בָּשַׁל, H3320 יָצַב, H3322 יָצַג, H5324 נָצַב, H5975 עָמַד, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6633 צָבָא, H6925 קֳדָם, H7284 רְגַשׁ, H8254 שָׁקַל, H8334 שָׁרַת",
    3937: "Παρμενᾶς (Parmenâs | par-men-as')\n\nDerivation: probably by contraction for Παρμενίδης (a derivative of a compound of G3844 and G3306)\nStrong's: constant; Parmenas, a Christian\nKJV: —Parmenas.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3306 \n\nCognate Group: G3936 (assist)\nVariants: Παρμενᾶς",
    3938: "πάροδος (párodos | par'-od-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3598\nStrong's: a by-road, i.e. (actively) a route\nKJV: —way.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3598 \n\nCognate Group: G3936 (assist)\nVariants: πάροδος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H1982 הֵלֶךְ, H5674 עָבַר",
    3939: "παροικέω (paroikéō | par-oy-keh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3611\nStrong's: to dwell near, i.e. reside as a foreigner\nKJV: —sojourn in, be a stranger.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3611 \n\nCognate Group: G3939 (sojourn in), G3940 (sojourning), G3941 (foreigner)\nVariants: παροικέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1481 גּוּר, H3427 יָשַׁב, H7931 שָׁכַן",
    3940: "παροικία (paroikía | par-oy-kee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3941\nStrong's: foreign residence\nKJV: —sojourning, X as strangers.\n\nSee: G3941 \n\nCognate Group: G3939 (sojourn in), G3940 (sojourning), G3941 (foreigner)\nVariants: παροικία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1473 גֹּולָה, H4032 מָגֹור",
    3941: "πάροικος (pároikos | par'-oy-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3624\nStrong's: having a home near, i.e. (as noun) a by-dweller (alien resident)\nKJV: —foreigner, sojourn, stranger.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3624 \n\nCognate Group: G3939 (sojourn in), G3940 (sojourning), G3941 (foreigner)\nVariants: πάροικος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1471 גֹּוי, H1616 גֵּר, H7934 שָׁכֵן, H8453 תֹּושָׁב",
    3942: "παροιμία (paroimía | par-oy-mee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3844 and perhaps a derivative of G3633\nStrong's: apparently a state alongside of supposition, i.e. (concretely) an adage; specially, an enigmatical or fictitious illustration\nKJV: —parable, proverb.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3633 \n\nCognate Group: G3942 (parable)\nVariants: παροιμία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4911 מָשַׁל",
    3943: "πάροινος (pároinos | par'-oy-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3631\nStrong's: staying near wine, i.e. tippling (a toper)\nKJV: —given to wine.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3631 \n\nCognate Group: G3942 (parable)\nVariants: πάροινος",
    3944: "παροίχομαι (paroíchomai | par-oy'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and οἴχομαι (to depart)\nStrong's: to escape along, i.e. be gone\nKJV: —past.\n\nSee: G3844 \n\nCognate Group: G3942 (parable)\nVariants: παροίχομαι",
    3945: "παρομοιάζω (paromoiázō | par-om-oy-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3946\nStrong's: to resemble\nKJV: —be like unto.\n\nSee: G3946 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: παρομοιάζω",
    3946: "παρόμοιος (parómoios | par-om'-oy-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3664\nStrong's: alike nearly, i.e. similar\nKJV: —like.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3664 \n\nCognate Group: G3664 (like), G3946 (like), G3666 (be like), G3668 (likewise), G3667 (made like to), G871 (make like), G3669 (similitude), G3665 (like as), G3670 (con- fess), G3945 (be like unto)\nVariants: παρόμοιος",
    3947: "παροξύνω (paroxýnō | par-ox-oo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and a derivative of G3691\nStrong's: to sharpen alongside, i.e. (figuratively) to exasperate\nKJV: —easily provoke, stir.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3691 \n\nCognate Group: G3948 (contention), G3947 (easily provoke)\nVariants: παροξύνω, παροξύνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2300 חָדַד, H2734 חָרָה, H2778 חָרַף, H3286 יָעַף, H3707 כַּעַס, H4784 מָרָה, H5006 נָאַץ, H5086 נָדַף, H5674 עָבַר, H6030 עָנָה, H6058 עָנֵף, H6087 עָצַב, H7065 קָנָא, H7107 קָצַף, H7264 רָגַז, H8150 שָׁנַן, H8427 תָּוָה",
    3948: "παροξυσμός (paroxysmós | par-ox-oos-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3947 (\"paroxysm\")\nStrong's: incitement (to good), or dispute (in anger)\nKJV: —contention, provoke unto.\n\nSee: G3947 \n\nCognate Group: G3948 (contention), G3947 (easily provoke)\nVariants: παροξυσμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H7110 קֶצֶף",
    3949: "παροργίζω (parorgízō | par-org-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and G3710\nStrong's: to anger alongside, i.e. enrage\nKJV: —anger, provoke to wrath.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3710 \n\nCognate Group: G3949 (anger), G3710 (be angry), G3950 (wrath), G3709 (anger), G3711 (soon angry)\nVariants: παροργίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1442 גָּדַף, H3707 כַּעַס, H5006 נָאַץ, H6087 עָצַב, H7065 קָנָא, H7107 קָצַף, H7114 קָצַר, H7264 רָגַז, H7311 רוּם, H8564 תַּמְרוּר",
    3950: "παροργισμός (parorgismós | par-org-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3949\nStrong's: rage\nKJV: —wrath.\n\nSee: G3949 \n\nCognate Group: G3949 (anger), G3710 (be angry), G3950 (wrath), G3709 (anger), G3711 (soon angry)\nVariants: παροργισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3708 כַּעַס, H5007 נְאָצָה, H7110 קֶצֶף",
    3951: "παροτρύνω (parotrýnō | par-ot-roo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and ὀτρύνω (to spur)\nStrong's: to urge along, i.e. stimulate (to hostility)\nKJV: —stir up.\n\nSee: G3844 \n\nCognate Group: G3949 (anger), G3710 (be angry), G3950 (wrath), G3709 (anger), G3711 (soon angry)\nVariants: παροτρύνω",
    3952: "παρουσία (parousía | par-oo-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the present participle of G3918\nStrong's: a being near, i.e. advent (often, return; specially, of Christ to punish Jerusalem, or finally the wicked); (by implication) physically, aspect\nKJV: —coming, presence.\n\nSee: G3918 \n\nCognate Group: G3952 (coming), G3918 (come)\nVariants: παρουσία",
    3953: "παροψίς (paropsís | par-op-sis')\n\nDerivation: from G3844 and the base of G3795\nStrong's: a side-dish (the receptacle)\nKJV: —platter.\n\nSee: G3844 \nSee: G3795 \n\nCognate Group: G3952 (coming), G3918 (come)\nVariants: παροψίς",
    3954: "παῤῥησία (parrhēsía | par-rhay-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G3956 and a derivative of G4483\nStrong's: all out-spokenness, i.e. frankness, bluntness, publicity; by implication, assurance\nKJV: —bold (X -ly, -ness, -ness of speech), confidence, X freely, X openly, X plainly(-ness).\n\nSee: G3956 \nSee: G4483 \n\nCognate Group: G3955 (be bold), G3954 (bold)\nVariants: παῤῥησία, παρρησία \nHebrew Equivalents: H6998 קָטַף",
    3955: "παῤῥησιάζομαι (parrhēsiázomai | par-hray-see-ad'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G3954\nStrong's: to be frank in utterance, or confident in spirit and demeanor\nKJV: —be (wax) bold, (preach, speak) boldly.\n\nSee: G3954 \n\nCognate Group: G3955 (be bold), G3954 (bold)\nVariants: παῤῥησιάζομαι, παρρησιάζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3313 יָפַע, H6026 עָנַג",
    3956: "πᾶς (pâs | pas)\n\nDerivation: including all the forms of declension; apparently a primary word\nStrong's: all, any, every, the whole\nKJV: —all (manner of, means), alway(-s), any (one), X daily, + ever, every (one, way), as many as, + no(-thing), X thoroughly, whatsoever, whole, whosoever.\n\nCognate Group: G3955 (be bold), G3954 (bold)\nVariants: πᾶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H3605 כֹּל, H3617 כָּלָה, H3632 כָּלִיל, H3632 כָּלִיל, H7706 שַׁדַּי, H8548 תָּמִיד, H8548 תָּמִיד",
    3957: "πάσχα (páscha | pas'-khah)\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (compare H6453)\nStrong's: the Passover (the meal, the day, the festival or the special sacrifices connected with it)\nKJV: —Easter, Passover.\nSee: H6453\n\nCognate Group: G3957 (Easter)\nVariants: πάσχα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6453 פֶּסַח",
    3958: "πάσχω (páschō | pas'-kho), including the forms πάθω (páthō | path'-o), and πένθω (pénthō | pen'-tho)\n\n, used only in certain tenses for it\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to experience a sensation or impression (usually painful)\nKJV: —feel, passion, suffer, vex.\n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: πάσχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2470 חָלָה",
    3959: "Πάταρα (Pátara | pat'-ar-ah)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: Patara, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Patara.\n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: Πάταρα",
    3960: "πατάσσω (patássō | pat-as'-so)\n\nDerivation: probably prolongation from G3817\nStrong's: to knock (gently or with a weapon or fatally)\nKJV: —smite, strike. Compare G5180 .\n\nSee: G3817 \nSee: G5180 \n\nCognate Group: G3960 (smite)\nVariants: πατάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1696 דָבַר, H1855 דְּקַק, H3898 לָחַם, H3920 לָכַד, H4191 מוּת, H4223 מְחָא, H4272 מָחַץ, H5062 נָגַף, H5221 נָכָה, H6887 צָרַר",
    3961: "πατέω (patéō | pat-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative probably of G3817 (meaning a \"path\")\nStrong's: to trample (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —tread (down, under foot).\n\nSee: G3817 \n\nCognate Group: G3961 (tread), G1704 (walk in), G2662 (trample), G4043 (go)\nVariants: πατέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H947 בּוּס, H1758 דּוּשׁ, H1869 דָּרַךְ, H1869 דָּרַךְ, H7287 רָדָה, H7429 רָמַס",
    3962: "πατήρ (patḗr | pat-ayr')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a \"father\" (literally or figuratively, near or more remote)\nKJV: —father, parent.\n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατήρ, προπάτωρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב, H1730 דֹּוד, H1733 דֹּודָה, H7223 רִאשֹׁון",
    3963: "Πάτμος (Pátmos | pat'-mos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Patmus, an islet in the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Patmos.\n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: Πάτμος",
    3964: "πατραλῴας (patralṓias | pat-ral-o'-as)\n\nDerivation: from G3962 and the same as the latter part of G3389\nStrong's: a parricide\nKJV: —murderer of fathers.\n\nSee: G3962 \nSee: G3389 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατραλῴας, πατρολῴας",
    3965: "πατριά (patriá | pat-ree-ah')\n\nDerivation: as if feminine of a derivative of G3962\nStrong's: paternal descent, i.e. (concretely) a group of families or a whole race (nation)\nKJV: —family, kindred, lineage.\n\nSee: G3962 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατριά \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה",
    3966: "πατριάρχης (patriárchēs | pat-ree-arkh'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G3965 and G757\nStrong's: a progenitor (\"patriarch\")\nKJV: —patriarch.\n\nSee: G3965 \nSee: G757 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατριάρχης \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב, H7218 רֹאשׁ, H8269 שַׂר, H8269 שַׂר",
    3967: "πατρικός (patrikós | pat-ree-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G3962\nStrong's: paternal, i.e. ancestral\nKJV: —of fathers.\n\nSee: G3962 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατρικός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב",
    3968: "πατρίς (patrís | pat-rece')\n\nDerivation: from G3902\nStrong's: a father-land, i.e. native town; (figuratively) heavenly home\nKJV: —(own) country.\n\nSee: G3902 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατρίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H4138 מֹולֶדֶת, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה",
    3969: "Πατροβᾶς (Patrobâs | pat-rob'-as)\n\nDerivation: perhaps a contraction for Πατρόβιος (a compound of G3962 and G979)\nStrong's: father's life; Patrobas, a Christian\nKJV: —Patrobas.\n\nSee: G3962 \nSee: G979 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: Πατροβᾶς, Πατρόβας",
    3970: "πατροπαράδοτος (patroparádotos | pat-rop-ar-ad'-ot-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3962 and a derivative of G3860 (in the sense of handing over or down)\nStrong's: traditionary\nKJV: —received by tradition from fathers.\n\nSee: G3962 \nSee: G3860 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατροπαράδοτος",
    3971: "πατρῷος (patrōios | pat-ro'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G3962\nStrong's: paternal, i.e. hereditary\nKJV: —of fathers.\n\nSee: G3962 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: πατρῷος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1 אָב",
    3972: "Παῦλος (Paûlos | pow'-los)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: (little; but remotely from a derivative of G3973, meaning the same); Paulus, the name of a Roman and of an apostle\nKJV: —Paul, Paulus.\n\nSee: G3973 \n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: Παῦλος",
    3973: "παύω (paúō | pow'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (\"pause\")\nStrong's: to stop (transitively or intransitively), i.e. restrain, quit, desist, come to an end\nKJV: —cease, leave, refrain.\n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: παύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1826 דָּמַם, H2308 חָדַל, H3615 כָּלָה, H4185 מוּשׁ, H5162 נָחַם, H5314 נָפַשׁ, H5341 נָצַר, H6113 עָצָר, H6148 עָרַב, H7503 רָפָה, H7599 שָׁאַן, H7673 שָׁבַת, H7725 שׁוּב, H8552 תָּמַם",
    3974: "Πάφος (Páphos | paf'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Paphus, a place in Cyprus\nKJV: —Paphos.\n\nCognate Group: G3968 (country), G3965 (family), G540 (without father), G3971 (of fathers), G3966 (patriarch), G3967 (of fathers), G3962 (father)\nVariants: Πάφος",
    3975: "παχύνω (pachýnō | pakh-oo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4078 (meaning thick)\nStrong's: to thicken, i.e. (by implication) to fatten (figuratively, stupefy or render callous)\nKJV: —wax gross.\n\nSee: G4078 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: παχύνω, παχύνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1878 דָּשֵׁן, H5445 סָבַל, H5753 עָוָה, H8082 שָׁמֵן",
    3976: "πέδη (pédē | ped'-ay)\n\nDerivation: ultimately from G4228\nStrong's: a shackle for the feet\nKJV: —fetter.\n\nSee: G4228 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: πέδη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2131 זִיקָה, H3525 כֶּבֶל, H5178 נְחֹשֶׁת",
    3977: "πεδινός (pedinós | ped-ee-nos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4228 (meaning the ground)\nStrong's: level (as easy for the feet)\nKJV: —plain.\n\nSee: G4228 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: πεδινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1237 בִּקְעָה, H4334 מִישֹׁור, H8192 שָׁפָה, H8219 שְׁפֵלָה, H8219 שְׁפֵלָה, H8219 שְׁפֵלָה",
    3978: "πεζεύω (pezeúō | ped-zyoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3979\nStrong's: to foot a journey, i.e. travel by land\nKJV: —go afoot.\n\nSee: G3979 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: πεζεύω",
    3979: "πεζῇ (pezēi | ped-zay')\n\nDerivation: dative case feminine of a derivative of G4228 (as adverb)\nStrong's: foot-wise, i.e. by walking\nKJV: —a- (on) foot.\n\nSee: G4228 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: πεζῇ, πεζός \nHebrew Equivalents: H376 אִישׁ, H7273 רַגְלִי",
    3980: "πειθαρχέω (peitharchéō | pi-tharkh-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3982 and G757\nStrong's: to be persuaded by a ruler, i.e. (genitive case) to submit to authority; by analogy, to conform to advice\nKJV: —hearken, obey (magistrates).\n\nSee: G3982 \nSee: G757 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πειθαρχέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H8086 שְׁמַע",
    3981: "πειθός (peithós | pi-thos')\n\nDerivation: from G3982\nStrong's: persuasive\nKJV: —enticing.\n\nSee: G3982 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πειθός, πειθώ, πιθός",
    3982: "πείθω (peíthō | pi'-tho)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to convince (by argument, true or false); by analogy, to pacify or conciliate (by other fair means); reflexively or passively, to assent (to evidence or authority), to rely (by inward certainty)\nKJV: —agree, assure, believe, have confidence, be (wax) conflent, make friend, obey, persuade, trust, yield.\n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πείθω, ἐπισείω \nHebrew Equivalents: H982 בָּטַח, H982 בָּטַח, H982 בָּטַח, H983 בֶּטַח, H2620 חָסָה, H5377 נָשָׁא, H6004 עָמַם, H6901 קָבַל, H6960 קָוָה, H7365 רְחַץ, H8156 שָׁסַע, H8172 שָׁעַן, H8252 שָׁקַט",
    3983: "πεινάω (peináō | pi-nah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G3993 \nStrong's: (through the idea of pinching toil; \"pine\"); to famish (absolutely or comparatively); figuratively, to crave\nKJV: —be an hungered.\n\nSee: G3993 \n\nCognate Group: G3983 (be an hungered)\nVariants: πεινάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3287 יָעֵף, H5098 נָהַם, H5888 עָיֵף, H7456 רָעֵב",
    3984: "πεῖρα (peîra | pi'-rah)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4008 (through the idea of piercing)\nStrong's: a test, i.e. attempt, experience\nKJV: —assaying, trial.\n\nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G3985 (assay), G3986 (temptation), G551 (not to be tempted), G1598 (tempt), G3984 (assaying), G3987 (assay)\nVariants: πεῖρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4531 מַסָּה, H5254 נָסָה",
    3985: "πειράζω (peirázō | pi-rad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G3984\nStrong's: to test (objectively), i.e. endeavor, scrutinize, entice, discipline\nKJV: —assay, examine, go about, prove, tempt(-er), try.\n\nSee: G3984 \n\nCognate Group: G3985 (assay), G3986 (temptation), G551 (not to be tempted), G1598 (tempt), G3984 (assaying), G3987 (assay)\nVariants: πειράζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5254 נָסָה",
    3986: "πειρασμός (peirasmós | pi-ras-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G3985\nStrong's: a putting to proof (by experiment (of good), experience (of evil), solicitation, discipline or provocation); by implication, adversity\nKJV: —temptation, X try.\n\nSee: G3985 \n\nCognate Group: G3985 (assay), G3986 (temptation), G551 (not to be tempted), G1598 (tempt), G3984 (assaying), G3987 (assay)\nVariants: πειρασμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4531 מַסָּה, H6045 עִנְיָן",
    3987: "πειράω (peiráō | pi-rah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3984\nStrong's: to test (subjectively), i.e. (reflexively) to attempt\nKJV: —assay.\n\nSee: G3984 \n\nCognate Group: G3985 (assay), G3986 (temptation), G551 (not to be tempted), G1598 (tempt), G3984 (assaying), G3987 (assay)\nVariants: πειράω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5254 נָסָה",
    3988: "πεισμονή (peismonḗ | pice-mon-ay')\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G3982\nStrong's: persuadableness, i.e. credulity\nKJV: —persuasion.\n\nSee: G3982 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πεισμονή",
    3989: "πέλαγος (pélagos | pel'-ag-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: deep or open sea, i.e. the main\nKJV: —depth, sea.\n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πέλαγος",
    3990: "πελεκίζω (pelekízō | pel-ek-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4141 (meaning an axe)\nStrong's: to chop off (the head), i.e. truncate\nKJV: —behead.\n\nSee: G4141 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πελεκίζω",
    3991: "πέμπτος (pémptos | pemp'-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G4002\nStrong's: fifth\nKJV: —fifth.\n\nSee: G4002 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πέμπτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2549 חֲמִישִׁי, H2549 חֲמִישִׁי, H2571 חָמֻשׁ",
    3992: "πέμπω (pémpō | pem'-po)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to dispatch (from the subjective view or point of departure, whereas ἵημι (as a stronger form of εἶμι) refers rather to the objective point or terminus ad quem, and G4724 denotes properly, the orderly motion involved), especially on a temporary errand; also to transmit, bestow, or wield\nKJV: —send, thrust in.\n\nSee: G4724 \n\nCognate Group: G651 (apostleship), G649 (put in), G5570 (false teacher), G1821 (send), G652 (apostle), G3992 (send)\nVariants: πέμπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7971 שָׁלַח",
    3993: "πένης (pénēs | pen'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from a primary πένω (to toil for daily subsistence)\nStrong's: starving, i.e. indigent\nKJV: —poor. Compare G4434 .\n\nSee: G4434 \n\nCognate Group: G3993 (poor)\nVariants: πένης \nHebrew Equivalents: H34 אֶבְיֹון, H1790 דַּךְ, H1800 דַּל, H2489 חֵלְכָא, H4542 מִסְכֵּן, H6035 עָנָו, H6041 עָנִי, H7218 רֹאשׁ",
    3994: "πενθερά (pentherá | pen-ther-ah')\n\nDerivation: feminine of G3995\nStrong's: a wife's mother\nKJV: —mother in law, wife's mother.\n\nSee: G3995 \n\nCognate Group: G3993 (poor)\nVariants: πενθερά \nHebrew Equivalents: H2545 חֲמֹות, H2859 חָתַן",
    3995: "πενθερός (pentherós | pen-ther-os')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a wife's father\nKJV: —father in law.\n\nCognate Group: G3993 (poor)\nVariants: πενθερός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2524 חָם, H2859 חָתַן",
    3996: "πενθέω (penthéō | pen-theh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G3997\nStrong's: to grieve (the feeling or the act)\nKJV: —mourn, (be-)wail.\n\nSee: G3997 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πενθέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H56 אָבַל, H1058 בָּכָה, H1984 הָלַל, H5110 נוּד, H5999 עָמָל, H6937 קָדַר",
    3997: "πένθος (pénthos | pen'-thos)\n\nDerivation: strengthened from the alternate of G3958\nStrong's: grief\nKJV: —mourning, sorrow.\n\nSee: G3958 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πένθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H60 אֵבֶל, H205 אָוֶן, H3015 יָגֹון, H4553 מִסְפֵּד, H8424 תּוּגָה",
    3998: "πενιχρός (penichrós | pen-tikh-ros')\n\nDerivation: prolongation from the base of G3993\nStrong's: necessitous\nKJV: —poor.\n\nSee: G3993 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πενιχρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1800 דַּל, H6041 עָנִי",
    3999: "πεντάκις (pentákis | pen-tak-ece')\n\nDerivation: multiplicative adverb from G4002\nStrong's: five times\nKJV: —five times.\n\nSee: G4002 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πεντάκις",
    4000: "πεντακισχίλιοι (pentakischílioi | pen-tak-is-khil'-ee-oy)\n\nDerivation: from G3999 and G5507\nStrong's: five times a thousand\nKJV: —five thousand.\n\nSee: G3999 \nSee: G5507 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πεντακισχίλιοι",
    4001: "πεντακόσιοι (pentakósioi | pen-tak-os'-ee-oy)\n\nDerivation: from G4002 and G1540\nStrong's: five hundred\nKJV: —five hundred.\n\nSee: G4002 \nSee: G1540 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πεντακόσιοι",
    4002: "πέντε (pénte | pen'-teh)\n\nDerivation: a primary number\nStrong's: \"five\"\nKJV: —five.\n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πέντε",
    4003: "πεντεκαιδέκατος (pentekaidékatos | pen-tek-ahee-dek'-at-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4002 and G2532 and G1182\nStrong's: five and tenth\nKJV: —fifteenth.\n\nSee: G4002 \nSee: G2532 \nSee: G1182 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πεντεκαιδέκατος",
    4004: "πεντήκοντα (pentḗkonta | pen-tay'-kon-tah)\n\nDerivation: multiplicative of G4002\nStrong's: fifty\nKJV: —fifty.\n\nSee: G4002 \n\nCognate Group: G3997 (mourning), G3996 (mourn)\nVariants: πεντήκοντα",
    4005: "πεντηκοστή (pentēkostḗ | pen-tay-kos-tay')\n\nDerivation: feminine of the ordinal of G4004\nStrong's: fiftieth (G2250 being implied) from Passover, i.e. the festival of \"Pentecost\"\nKJV: —Pentecost.\n\nSee: G4004 \nSee: G2250 \n\nCognate Group: G4005 (Pentecost)\nVariants: πεντηκοστή \nHebrew Equivalents: H2549 חֲמִישִׁי",
    4006: "πεποίθησις (pepoíthēsis | pep-oy'-thay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from the perfect of the alternate of G3958\nStrong's: reliance\nKJV: —confidence, trust.\n\nSee: G3958 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πεποίθησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H986 בִּטָּחֹון",
    4007: "περ (per | per)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4008\nStrong's: an enclitic particle significant of abundance (thoroughness), i.e. emphasis; much, very or ever\nKJV: —(whom-)soever.\n\nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: περ G4302",
    4008: "πέραν (péran | per'-an)\n\nDerivation: apparently accusative case of an obsolete derivative of πείρω (to \"pierce\")\nStrong's: through (as adverb or preposition), i.e. across\nKJV: —beyond, farther (other) side, over.\n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πέραν \nHebrew Equivalents: H5676 עֵבֶר",
    4009: "πέρας (péras | per'-as)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4008\nStrong's: an extremity\nKJV: —end, ut-(ter-)most participle\n\nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: πέρας \nHebrew Equivalents: H657 אֶפֶס, H2714 חֵקֶר, H5490 סֹוף, H6256 עֵת, H6763 צֵלָע, H7079 קְנָת, H7093 קֵץ, H8503 תַּכְלִית",
    4010: "Πέργαμος (Pérgamos | per'-gam-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4444\nStrong's: fortified; Pergamus, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Pergamos.\n\nSee: G4444 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: Πέργαμος",
    4011: "Πέργη (Pérgē | perg'-ay)\n\nDerivation: probably from the same as G4010\nStrong's: a tower; Perga, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Perga.\n\nSee: G4010 \n\nCognate Group: G545 (disobedient), G3981 (enticing), G4006 (confidence), G3980 (hearken), G544 (not believe), G543 (disobedience), G3982 (agree), G3988 (persuasion)\nVariants: Πέργη",
    4012: "περί (perí | per-ee')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4008\nStrong's: properly, through (all over), i.e. around; figuratively with respect to; used in various applications, of place, cause or time (with the genitive case denoting the subject or occasion or superlative point; with the accusative case the locality, circuit, matter, circumstance or general period)\nKJV: —(there-)about, above, against, at, on behalf of, X and his company, which concern, (as) concerning, for, X how it will go with, ((there-, where-)) of, on, over, pertaining (to), for sake, X (e-)state, (as) touching, (where-)by (in), with. In comparative, it retains substantially the same meaning of circuit (around), excess (beyond), or completeness (through).\n\nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περί, περαιτέρω",
    4013: "περιάγω (periágō | per-ee-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G71\nStrong's: to take around (as a companion); reflexively, to walk around\nKJV: —compass, go (round) about, lead about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιάγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1980 הָלַךְ, H5437 סָבַב, H5674 עָבַר",
    4014: "περιαιρέω (periairéō | per-ee-ahee-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G138 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to remove all around, i.e. unveil, cast off (anchor); figuratively, to expiate\nKJV: —take away (up).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιαιρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1556 גָּלַל, H5337 נָצַל, H5437 סָבַב, H5493 סוּר, H5674 עָבַר, H6561 פָּרַק, H6565 פָּרַר, H7311 רוּם, H7918 שָׁכַךְ",
    4015: "περιαστράπτω (periastráptō | per-ee-as-trap'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G797\nStrong's: to flash all around, i.e. envelop in light\nKJV: —shine round (about).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G797 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιαστράπτω",
    4016: "περιβάλλω (peribállō | per-ee-bal'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G906\nStrong's: to throw all around, i.e. invest (with a palisade or with clothing)\nKJV: —array, cast about, clothe(-d me), put on.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιβάλλω, παρεμβάλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2263 חָבַק, H2583 חָנָה, H3680 כָּסָה, H3847 לָבַשׁ, H3885 לוּן, H5307 נָפַל, H5362 נָקַף, H5844 עָטָה, H5848 עָטַף, H6279 עָתַר, H6566 פָּרַשׂ, H8210 שָׁפַךְ",
    4017: "περιβλέπω (periblépō | per-ee-blep'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G991\nStrong's: to look all around\nKJV: —look (round) about (on).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G991 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιβλέπω, περιβλέπομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5027 נָבַט, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6437 פָּנָה, H7789 שׁוּר",
    4018: "περιβόλαιον (peribólaion | per-ib-ol'-ah-yon)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G4016\nStrong's: something thrown around one, i.e. a mantle, veil\nKJV: —covering, vesture.\n\nSee: G4016 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιβόλαιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H899 בֶּגֶד, H3682 כְּסוּת, H3830 לְבוּשׁ, H4555 מִסְפָּחָה",
    4019: "περιδέω (peridéō | per-ee-deh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G1210\nStrong's: to bind around one, i.e. enwrap\nKJV: —bind about.\n\nSee: G1210 \nSee: G4012 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιδέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H631 אָסַר",
    4020: "περιεργάζομαι (periergázomai | per-ee-er-gad'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2038\nStrong's: to work all around, i.e. bustle about (meddle)\nKJV: —be a busybody.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2038 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περιεργάζομαι",
    4021: "περίεργος (períergos | per-ee'-er-gos)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2041\nStrong's: working all around, i.e. officious (meddlesome, neuter plural magic)\nKJV: —busybody, curious arts.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G4012 (about)\nVariants: περίεργος",
    4022: "περιέρχομαι (periérchomai | per-ee-er'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2064 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to come all around, i.e. stroll, vacillate, veer\nKJV: —fetch a compass, vagabond, wandering about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2064 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: περιέρχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5437 סָבַב, H5674 עָבַר, H7751 שׁוּט",
    4023: "περιέχω (periéchō | per-ee-ekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2192\nStrong's: to hold all around, i.e. include, clasp (figuratively)\nKJV: —+ astonished, contain, after (this manner).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: περιέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H247 אָזַר, H661 אָפַף, H3803 כָּתַר, H5341 נָצַר, H5362 נָקַף, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6822 צָפָה, H7751 שׁוּט",
    4024: "περιζώννυμι (perizṓnnymi | per-id-zone'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2224\nStrong's: to gird all around, i.e. (middle voice or passive) to fasten on one's belt (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —gird (about, self).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2224 \n\nCognate Group: G2224 (gird), G2223 (girdle), G1241 (gird), G4024 (gird)\nVariants: περιζώννυμι, περιζωννύω, περιζώννυμι/περιζωννύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H247 אָזַר, H631 אָסַר, H2296 חָגַר, H3736 כַּרְבֵּל",
    4025: "περίθεσις (períthesis | per-ith'-es-is)\n\nDerivation: from G4060\nStrong's: a putting all around, i.e. decorating oneself with\nKJV: —wearing.\n\nSee: G4060 \n\nCognate Group: G2224 (gird), G2223 (girdle), G1241 (gird), G4024 (gird)\nVariants: περίθεσις",
    4026: "περιΐστημι (periḯstēmi | per-ee-is'-tay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2476\nStrong's: to stand all around, i.e. (near) to be a bystander, or (aloof) to keep away from\nKJV: —avoid, shun, stand by (round about).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G2224 (gird), G2223 (girdle), G1241 (gird), G4024 (gird)\nVariants: περιΐστημι, περιί̈στημι, περιΐστημι, περιίστημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5324 נָצַב, H5437 סָבַב",
    4027: "περικάθαρμα (perikátharma | per-ee-kath'-ar-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4012 and G2508\nStrong's: something cleaned off all around, i.e. refuse (figuratively)\nKJV: —filth.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2508 \n\nCognate Group: G2514 (purification), G169 (foul), G167 (uncleanness), G4027 (filth), G2508 (purge), G1571 (purge), G2512 (cleansing), G2511 (clean), G168 (filthiness), G2513 (clean)\nVariants: περικάθαρμα, κάθαρμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H3724 כֹּפֶר",
    4028: "περικαλύπτω (perikalýptō | per-ee-kal-oop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2572\nStrong's: to cover all around, i.e. entirely (the face, a surface)\nKJV: —blindfold, cover, overlay.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2572 \n\nCognate Group: G2514 (purification), G169 (foul), G167 (uncleanness), G4027 (filth), G2508 (purge), G1571 (purge), G2512 (cleansing), G2511 (clean), G168 (filthiness), G2513 (clean)\nVariants: περικαλύπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2664 חָפַשׂ, H3680 כָּסָה, H5526 סָכַךְ, H7660 שָׁבַץ",
    4029: "περίκειμαι (períkeimai | per-ik'-i-mahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2749\nStrong's: to lie all around, i.e. inclose, encircle, hamper (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —be bound (compassed) with, hang about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2749 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: περίκειμαι",
    4030: "περικεφαλαία (perikephalaía | per-ee-kef-al-ah'-yah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a compound of G4012 and G2776\nStrong's: encirclement of the head, i.e. a helmet\nKJV: —helmet.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2776 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: περικεφαλαία \nHebrew Equivalents: H3553 כֹּובַע, H6959 קֹובַע",
    4031: "περικρατής (perikratḗs | per-ee-krat-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2904\nStrong's: strong all around, i.e. a master (manager)\nKJV: —+ come by.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2904 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: περικρατής",
    4032: "περικρύπτω (perikrýptō | per-ee-kroop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2928\nStrong's: to conceal all around, i.e. entirely\nKJV: —hide.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2928 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: περικρύπτω, περικρύβω",
    4033: "περικυκλόω (perikyklóō | per-ee-koo-klo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2944\nStrong's: to encircle all around, i.e. blockade completely\nKJV: —compass round.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2944 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: περικυκλόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5362 נָקַף, H5437 סָבַב, H5582 סָעַד",
    4034: "περιλάμπω (perilámpō | per-ee-lam'-po)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G2989\nStrong's: to illuminate all around, i.e. invest with a halo\nKJV: —shine round about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2989 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: περιλάμπω",
    4035: "περιλείπω (perileípō | per-ee-li'-po)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G3007\nStrong's: to leave all around, i.e. (passively) survive\nKJV: —remain.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G3007 \n\nCognate Group: G4035 (remain), G2640 (remnant), G2641 (forsake), G3005 (remnant), G1257 (cease)\nVariants: περιλείπω, περιλείπομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H7604 שָׁאַר",
    4036: "περίλυπος (perílypos | per-il'-oo-pos)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G3077\nStrong's: grieved all around, i.e. intensely sad\nKJV: —exceeding (very) sorry(-owful).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G3077 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: περίλυπος",
    4037: "περιμένω (periménō | per-ee-men'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G3306\nStrong's: to stay around, i.e. await\nKJV: —wait for.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G3306 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: περιμένω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6960 קָוָה",
    4038: "πέριξ (périx | per'-ix)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4012\nStrong's: all around, i.e. (as an adjective) circumjacent\nKJV: —round about.\n\nSee: G4012 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: πέριξ",
    4039: "περιοικέω (perioikéō | per-ee-oy-keh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G3611\nStrong's: to reside around, i.e. be a neighbor\nKJV: —dwell round about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G3611 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: περιοικέω",
    4040: "περίοικος (períoikos | per-ee'-oy-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G3624\nStrong's: housed around, i.e. neighboring (used elliptically as a noun)\nKJV: —neighbour.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G3624 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: περίοικος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1323 בַּת, H3603 כִּכָּר, H5439 סָבִיב, H7934 שָׁכֵן",
    4041: "περιούσιος (perioúsios | per-ee-oo'-see-os)\n\nDerivation: from the present participle feminine of a compound of 4012 and G1510\nStrong's: being beyond usual, i.e. special (one's own)\nKJV: —peculiar.\n\nSee: G1510 \n\nCognate Group: G4041 (peculiar)\nVariants: περιούσιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5459 סְגֻלָּה",
    4042: "περιοχή (periochḗ | per-ee-okh-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4023\nStrong's: a being held around, i.e. (concretely) a passage (of Scripture, as circumscribed)\nKJV: —place.\n\nSee: G4023 \n\nCognate Group: G4041 (peculiar)\nVariants: περιοχή \nHebrew Equivalents: H4013 מִבְצָר, H4679 מְצַד, H4686 מָצוּד, H4692 מָצֹור",
    4043: "περιπατέω (peripatéō | per-ee-pat-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G3961\nStrong's: to tread all around, i.e. walk at large (especially as proof of ability); figuratively, to live, deport oneself, follow (as a companion or votary)\nKJV: —go, be occupied with, walk (about).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G3961 \n\nCognate Group: G3961 (tread), G1704 (walk in), G2662 (trample), G4043 (go)\nVariants: περιπατέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1869 דָּרַךְ, H1980 הָלַךְ",
    4044: "περιπείρω (peripeírō | per-ee-pi'-ro)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and the base of G4008\nStrong's: to penetrate entirely, i.e. transfix (figuratively)\nKJV: —pierce through.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G3961 (tread), G1704 (walk in), G2662 (trample), G4043 (go)\nVariants: περιπείρω",
    4045: "περιπίπτω (peripíptō | per-ee-pip'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G4098\nStrong's: to fall into something that is all around, i.e. light among or upon, be surrounded with\nKJV: —fall among (into).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G4098 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: περιπίπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5307 נָפַל, H7136 קָרָה",
    4046: "περιποιέομαι (peripoiéomai | per-ee-poy-eh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4012 and G4160\nStrong's: to make around oneself, i.e. acquire (buy)\nKJV: —purchase.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: περιποιέομαι, περιποιέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2421 חָיָה, H2550 חָמַל, H2820 חָשַׂךְ, H3498 יָתַר, H3502 יִתְרָה, H5459 סְגֻלָּה, H6452 פָּסַח, H7121 קָרָא, H7408 רָכַשׁ, H8210 שָׁפַךְ",
    4047: "περιποίησις (peripoíēsis | per-ee-poy'-ay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4046\nStrong's: acquisition (the act or the thing); by extension, preservation\nKJV: —obtain(-ing), peculiar, purchased, possession, saving.\n\nSee: G4046 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: περιποίησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4241 מִחְיָה, H5459 סְגֻלָּה",
    4048: "περιῤῥήγνυμι (perirrhḗgnymi | per-ir-hrayg'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G4486\nStrong's: to tear all around, i.e. completely away\nKJV: —rend off.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G4486 \n\nCognate Group: G4048 (rend off)\nVariants: περιῤῥήγνυμι, περιρήγνυμι, περι(ρ)ρήγνυμι, περιρρήγνυμι",
    4049: "περισπάω (perispáō | per-ee-spah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G4685\nStrong's: to drag all around, i.e. (figuratively) to distract (with care)\nKJV: —cumber.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G4685 \n\nCognate Group: G4048 (rend off)\nVariants: περισπάω, περισπάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H6030 עָנָה, H8058 שָׁמַט",
    4050: "περισσεία (perisseía | per-is-si'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4052\nStrong's: surplusage, i.e. superabundance\nKJV: —abundance(-ant, (-ly)), superfluity.\n\nSee: G4052 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περισσεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H3504 יִתְרֹון, H7468 רְעוּת",
    4051: "περίσσευμα (perísseuma | per-is'-syoo-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4052\nStrong's: a surplus, or superabundance\nKJV: —abundance, that was left, over and above.\n\nSee: G4052 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περίσσευμα",
    4052: "περισσεύω (perisseúō | per-is-syoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4053\nStrong's: to superabound (in quantity or quality), be in excess, be superfluous; also (transitively) to cause to superabound or excel\nKJV: —(make, more) abound, (have, have more) abundance (be more) abundant, be the better, enough and to spare, exceed, excel, increase, be left, redound, remain (over and above).\n\nSee: G4053 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περισσεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3498 יָתַר, H4768 מַרְבִּית",
    4053: "περισσός (perissós | per-is-sos')\n\nDerivation: from G4012 (in the sense of beyond)\nStrong's: superabundant (in quantity) or superior (in quality); by implication, excessive; adverbially (with G1537) violently; neuter (as noun) preeminence\nKJV: —exceeding abundantly above, more abundantly, advantage, exceedingly, very highly, beyond measure, more, superfluous, vehement(-ly).\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G1537 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περισσός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3499 יֶתֶר",
    4054: "περισσότερον (perissóteron | per-is-sot'-er-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G4055 (as adverb)\nStrong's: in a more superabundant way\nKJV: —more abundantly, a great deal, far more.\n\nSee: G4055 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περισσότερον",
    4055: "περισσότερος (perissóteros | per-is-sot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of G4053\nStrong's: more superabundant (in number, degree or character)\nKJV: —more abundant, greater (much) more, overmuch.\n\nSee: G4053 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περισσότερος G4358",
    4056: "περισσοτέρως (perissotérōs | per-is-sot-er'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4055\nStrong's: more superabundantly\nKJV: —more abundant(-ly), X the more earnest, (more) exceedingly, more frequent, much more, the rather.\n\nSee: G4055 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περισσοτέρως",
    4057: "περισσῶς (perissōs | per-is-soce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4053\nStrong's: superabundantly\nKJV: —exceedingly, out of measure, the more.\n\nSee: G4053 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: περισσῶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H3499 יֶתֶר, H5921 עַל",
    4058: "περιστερά (peristerá | per-is-ter-ah')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a pigeon\nKJV: —dove, pigeon.\n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: περιστερά \nHebrew Equivalents: H1469 גֹּוזָל, H3123 יֹונָה",
    4059: "περιτέμνω (peritémnō | per-ee-tem'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and the base of G5114\nStrong's: to cut around, i.e. (specially) to circumcise\nKJV: —circumcise.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G5114 \n\nCognate Group: G4059 (circumcise), G4061 (X circumcised), G564 (uncircumcised)\nVariants: περιτέμνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3054 יָהַד, H3772 כָּרַת, H4135 מוּל, H5493 סוּר",
    4060: "περιτίθημι (peritíthēmi | per-ee-tith'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G5087\nStrong's: to place around; by implication, to present\nKJV: —bestow upon, hedge round about, put about (on, upon), set about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G4059 (circumcise), G4061 (X circumcised), G564 (uncircumcised)\nVariants: περιτίθημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H553 אָמַץ, H1431 גָּדַל, H1961 הָיָה, H2280 חָבַשׁ, H3423 יָרַשׁ, H3513 כָּבַד, H3547 כָּהַן, H3632 כָּלִיל, H3847 לָבַשׁ, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5414 נָתַן, H5647 עָבַד, H5710 עָדָה, H5823 עָזַק, H6801 צָנַף, H7194 קָשַׁר, H7725 שׁוּב, H7760 שׂוּם",
    4061: "περιτομή (peritomḗ | per-it-om-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4059\nStrong's: circumcision (the rite, the condition or the people, literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —X circumcised, circumcision.\n\nSee: G4059 \n\nCognate Group: G4059 (circumcise), G4061 (X circumcised), G564 (uncircumcised)\nVariants: περιτομή \nHebrew Equivalents: H4135 מוּל, H4139 מוּלָה",
    4062: "περιτρέπω (peritrépō | per-ee-trep'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and the base of G5157\nStrong's: to turn around, i.e. (mentally) to craze\nKJV: —+ make mad.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G5157 \n\nCognate Group: G4059 (circumcise), G4061 (X circumcised), G564 (uncircumcised)\nVariants: περιτρέπω",
    4063: "περιτρέχω (peritréchō | per-ee-trekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G5143 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to run around, i.e. traverse\nKJV: —run through.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G5143 \n\nCognate Group: G4059 (circumcise), G4061 (X circumcised), G564 (uncircumcised)\nVariants: περιτρέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7751 שׁוּט",
    4064: "περιφέρω (periphérō | per-ee-fer'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G5342\nStrong's: to convey around, i.e. transport hither and thither\nKJV: —bear (carry) about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G4059 (circumcise), G4061 (X circumcised), G564 (uncircumcised)\nVariants: περιφέρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל",
    4065: "περιφρονέω (periphronéō | per-ee-fron-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G5426\nStrong's: to think beyond, i.e. depreciate (contemn)\nKJV: —despise.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G5426 \n\nCognate Group: G2706 (despise), G2707 (despiser), G4065 (despise)\nVariants: περιφρονέω",
    4066: "περίχωρος (períchōros | per-ikh'-o-ros)\n\nDerivation: from G4012 and G5561\nStrong's: around the region, i.e. circumjacent (as noun, with G1093 implied vicinity)\nKJV: —country (round) about, region (that lieth) round about.\n\nSee: G4012 \nSee: G5561 \nSee: G1093 \n\nCognate Group: G2706 (despise), G2707 (despiser), G4065 (despise)\nVariants: περίχωρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2256 חֶבֶל, H3603 כִּכָּר, H4054 מִגְרָשׁ, H4543 מִסְכְּנָה, H6418 פֶּלֶךְ",
    4067: "περίψωμα (perípsōma | per-ip'-so-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a comparative of G4012 and ψάω (to rub)\nStrong's: something brushed all around, i.e. off-scrapings (figuratively, scum)\nKJV: —offscouring.\n\nSee: G4012 \n\nCognate Group: G4067 (offscouring)\nVariants: περίψωμα, περίψημα",
    4068: "περπερεύομαι (perpereúomai | per-per-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from πέρπερος (braggart\nStrong's: perhaps by reduplication of the base of G4008); to boast\nKJV: —vaunt itself.\n\nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G4067 (offscouring)\nVariants: περπερεύομαι",
    4069: "Περσίς (Persís | per-sece')\n\n\nStrong's: a Persian woman; Persis, a Christian female\nKJV: —Persis.\n\nCognate Group: G4067 (offscouring)\nVariants: Περσίς",
    4070: "πέρυσι (pérysi | per'-oo-si)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4009\nStrong's: the by-gone, i.e. (as noun) last year\nKJV: —+ a year ago.\n\nSee: G4009 \n\nCognate Group: G4067 (offscouring)\nVariants: πέρυσι, πέρυσι(ν)",
    4071: "πετεινόν (peteinón | pet-i-non')\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of G4072\nStrong's: a flying animal, i.e. bird\nKJV: —bird, fowl.\n\nSee: G4072 \n\nCognate Group: G4067 (offscouring)\nVariants: πετεινόν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3671 כָּנָף, H5775 עֹוף, H5861 עַיִט, H6833 צִפֹּור",
    4072: "πέτομαι (pétomai | pet'-om-ahee), or prolongation πετάομαι (petáomai | pet-ah'-om-ahee), or contracted πτάομαι (ptáomai | ptah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of a primary verb\nStrong's: to fly\nKJV: —fly(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: G4067 (offscouring)\nVariants: πέτομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2907 טוּשׂ, H5074 נָדַד, H5774 עוּף",
    4073: "πέτρα (pétra | pet'-ra)\n\nDerivation: feminine of the same as G4074\nStrong's: a (mass of) rock (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —rock.\n\nSee: G4074 \n\nCognate Group: G2786 (Cephas), G4073 (rock), G4074 (Peter)\nVariants: πέτρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2496 חַלָּמִישׁ, H3710 כֵּף, H5553 סֶלַע, H6697 צוּר",
    4074: "Πέτρος (Pétros | pet'-ros)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a (piece of) rock (larger than G3037); as a name, Petrus, an apostle\nKJV: —Peter, rock. Compare G2786 .\n\nSee: G3037 \nSee: G2786 \n\nCognate Group: G2786 (Cephas), G4073 (rock), G4074 (Peter)\nVariants: Πέτρος",
    4075: "πετρώδης (petrṓdēs | pet-ro'-dace)\n\nDerivation: from G4073 and G1491\nStrong's: rock-like, i.e. rocky\nKJV: —stony.\n\nSee: G4073 \nSee: G1491 \n\nCognate Group: G2786 (Cephas), G4073 (rock), G4074 (Peter)\nVariants: πετρώδης, πετρῶδες",
    4076: "πήγανον (pḗganon | pay'-gan-on)\n\nDerivation: from G4078\nStrong's: rue (from its thick or fleshy leaves)\nKJV: —rue.\n\nSee: G4078 \n\nCognate Group: G2786 (Cephas), G4073 (rock), G4074 (Peter)\nVariants: πήγανον",
    4077: "πηγή (pēgḗ | pay-gay')\n\nDerivation: probably from G4078 (through the idea of gushing plumply)\nStrong's: a fount (literally or figuratively), i.e. source or supply (of water, blood, enjoyment) (not necessarily the original spring)\nKJV: —fountain, well.\n\nSee: G4078 \n\nCognate Group: G4077 (fountain)\nVariants: πηγή \nHebrew Equivalents: H343 אֵיד, H663 אֲפֵק, H4002 מַבּוּעַ, H4161 מֹוצָא, H4726 מָקֹור, H5033 נֵבֶךְ, H5869 עַיִן",
    4078: "πήγνυμι (pḗgnymi | payg'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary verb (which in its simpler form occurs only as an alternate in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to fix (\"peg\"), i.e. (specially) to set up (a tent)\nKJV: —pitch.\n\nCognate Group: G4077 (fountain)\nVariants: πήγνυμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2342 חוּל, H2883 טָבַע, H3332 יָצַק, H4223 מְחָא, H5186 נָטָה, H5193 נָטַע, H5324 נָצַב, H6821 צָפַד, H7087 קָפָא, H7931 שָׁכַן, H8628 תָּקַע",
    4079: "πηδάλιον (pēdálion | pay-dal'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a (presumed) derivative of πηδόν (the blade of an oar\nStrong's: from the same as G3976); a \"pedal\", i.e. helm\nKJV: —rudder.\n\nSee: G3976 \n\nCognate Group: G4077 (fountain)\nVariants: πηδάλιον",
    4080: "πηλίκος (pēlíkos | pay-lee'-kos)\n\nDerivation: a quantitative form (the feminine) of the base of G4225\nStrong's: how much (as an indefinite), i.e. in size or (figuratively) dignity\nKJV: —how great (large).\n\nSee: G4225 \n\nCognate Group: G4077 (fountain)\nVariants: πηλίκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3642 כָּמַהּ",
    4081: "πηλός (pēlós | pay-los')\n\nDerivation: perhaps a primary word\nStrong's: clay\nKJV: —clay.\n\nCognate Group: G4081 (clay)\nVariants: πηλός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2563 חֹמֶר, H2916 טִיט, H6083 עָפָר",
    4082: "πήρα (pḗra | pay'-rah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a wallet or leather pouch for food\nKJV: —scrip.\n\nCognate Group: G4082 (scrip)\nVariants: πήρα",
    4083: "πῆχυς (pēchys | pay'-khoos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: the fore-arm, i.e. (as a measure) a cubit\nKJV: —cubit.\n\nCognate Group: G4082 (scrip)\nVariants: πῆχυς \nHebrew Equivalents: H520 אַמָּה, H3027 יָד, H3709 כַּף, H7070 קָנֶה",
    4084: "πιάζω (piázō | pee-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: probably another form of G971\nStrong's: to squeeze, i.e. seize (gently by the hand (press), or officially (arrest), or in hunting (capture))\nKJV: —apprehend, catch, lay hand on, take. Compare G4085 .\n\nSee: G971 \nSee: G4085 \n\nCognate Group: G4082 (scrip)\nVariants: πιάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H270 אָחַז",
    4085: "πιέζω (piézō | pee-ed'-zo)\n\nDerivation: another form for G4084\nStrong's: to pack\nKJV: —press down.\n\nSee: G4084 \n\nCognate Group: G4082 (scrip)\nVariants: πιέζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1869 דָּרַךְ",
    4086: "πιθανολογία (pithanología | pith-an-ol-og-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of a derivative of G3982 and G3056\nStrong's: persuasive language\nKJV: —enticing words.\n\nSee: G3982 \nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G4082 (scrip)\nVariants: πιθανολογία, πιθαναλογία",
    4087: "πικραίνω (pikraínō | pik-rah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4089\nStrong's: to embitter (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —be (make) bitter.\n\nSee: G4089 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: πικραίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3707 כַּעַס, H4775 מָרַד, H7107 קָצַף, H7264 רָגַז",
    4088: "πικρία (pikría | pik-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4089\nStrong's: acridity (especially poison), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —bitterness.\n\nSee: G4089 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: πικρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2195 זַעַם, H3939 לַעֲנָה, H3992 מָאַר, H4751 מַר, H4754 מָרָא, H4759 מַרְאָה, H4820 מִרְמָה, H6001 עָמֵל, H7600 שַׁאֲנָן",
    4089: "πικρός (pikrós | pik-ros')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G4078 (through the idea of piercing)\nStrong's: sharp (pungent), i.e. acrid (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —bitter.\n\nSee: G4078 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: πικρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4751 מַר, H4843 מָרַר, H7218 רֹאשׁ",
    4090: "πικρῶς (pikrōs | pik-roce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4089\nStrong's: bitterly, i.e. (figuratively) violently\nKJV: —bitterly.\n\nSee: G4089 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: πικρῶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H4751 מַר, H4843 מָרַר",
    4091: "Πιλᾶτος (Pilâtos | pil-at'-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: close-pressed, i.e. firm; Pilatus, a Roman\nKJV: —Pilate.\n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: Πιλᾶτος, Πειλᾶτος",
    4092: "πίμπρημι (pímprēmi | pim'-pray-mee)\n\n\nDerivation: a reduplicated and prolonged form of a primary πρέω (préō | preh'-o)\n; which occurs only as an alternate in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to fire, i.e. burn (figuratively and passively, become inflamed with fever)\nKJV: —be (X should have) swollen.\n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: πίμπρημι",
    4093: "πινακίδιον (pinakídion | pin-ak-id'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G4094\nStrong's: a tablet (for writing on)\nKJV: —writing table.\n\nSee: G4094 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: πινακίδιον, πινακίς",
    4094: "πίναξ (pínax | pin'-ax)\n\nDerivation: apparently a form of G4109\nStrong's: a plate\nKJV: —charger, platter.\n\nSee: G4109 \n\nCognate Group: G4087 (be bitter), G3893 (provoke), G4089 (bitter), G4088 (bitterness), G4090 (bitterly), G3894 (provocation)\nVariants: πίναξ",
    4095: "πίνω (pínō | pee'-no)\n\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of πίω (píō | pee'-o); which (together with another form) πόω (póō | po'-o)\n; occurs only as an alternate in certain tenses\nStrong's: to imbibe (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —drink.\n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: πίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5405 נָשַׁת, H8248 שָׁקָה, H8354 שָׁתָה",
    4096: "πιότης (piótēs | pee-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from πίων (fat\nStrong's: perhaps akin to the alternate of G4095 through the idea of repletion); plumpness, i.e. (by implication) richness (oiliness)\nKJV: —fatness.\n\nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: πιότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H1880 דֶּשֶׁן, H2461 חָלָב, H2896 טֹוב, H3323 יִצְהָר, H8080 שָׁמַן",
    4097: "πιπράσκω (pipráskō | pip-ras'-ko), a reduplicated and prolonged form of πράω (práō | prah'-o)\n\n; (which occurs only as an alternate in certain tenses)\n\nDerivation: contracted from περάω (to traverse\nStrong's: from the base of G4008); to traffic (by travelling), i.e. dispose of as merchandise or into slavery (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —sell.\n\nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G4097 (sell)\nVariants: πιπράσκω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4376 מָכַר, H5234 נָכַר",
    4098: "πίπτω (píptō | pip'-to), a reduplicated and contracted form of πέτω (pétō | pet'-o)\n\n; (which occurs only as an alternate in certain tenses)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G4072 through the idea of alighting\nStrong's: to fall (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —fail, fall (down), light on.\n\nSee: G4072 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: πίπτω, συμπίπτω",
    4099: "Πισιδία (Pisidía | pis-id-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: Pisidia, a region of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Pisidia.\n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: Πισιδία, Πισίδιος",
    4100: "πιστεύω (pisteúō | pist-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4102\nStrong's: to have faith (in, upon, or with respect to, a person or thing), i.e. credit; by implication, to entrust (especially one's spiritual well-being to Christ)\nKJV: —believe(-r), commit (to trust), put in trust with.\n\nSee: G4102 \n\nCognate Group: G570 (unbelief), G571 (that believeth not), G4104 (assure of), G4103 (believe), G4100 (believe), G569 (believe not), G4102 (assurance), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: πιστεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6004 עָמַם, H8085 שָׁמַע",
    4101: "πιστικός (pistikós | pis-tik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4102\nStrong's: trustworthy, i.e. genuine (unadulterated)\nKJV: —spike-(nard).\n\nSee: G4102 \n\nCognate Group: G570 (unbelief), G571 (that believeth not), G4104 (assure of), G4103 (believe), G4100 (believe), G569 (believe not), G4102 (assurance), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: πιστικός",
    4102: "πίστις (pístis | pis'-tis)\n\nDerivation: from G3982\nStrong's: persuasion, i.e. credence; moral conviction (of religious truth, or the truthfulness of God or a religious teacher), especially reliance upon Christ for salvation; abstractly, constancy in such profession; by extension, the system of religious (Gospel) truth itself\nKJV: —assurance, belief, believe, faith, fidelity.\n\nSee: G3982 \n\nCognate Group: G570 (unbelief), G571 (that believeth not), G4104 (assure of), G4103 (believe), G4100 (believe), G569 (believe not), G4102 (assurance), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: πίστις \nHebrew Equivalents: H530 אֱמוּנָה",
    4103: "πιστός (pistós | pis-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G3982\nStrong's: objectively, trustworthy; subjectively, trustful\nKJV: —believe(-ing, -r), faithful(-ly), sure, true.\n\nSee: G3982 \n\nCognate Group: G570 (unbelief), G571 (that believeth not), G4104 (assure of), G4103 (believe), G4100 (believe), G569 (believe not), G4102 (assurance), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: πιστός \nHebrew Equivalents: H539 אָמַן, H3499 יֶתֶר, H6662 צַדִּיק, H8419 תַּהְפֻּכָה",
    4104: "πιστόω (pistóō | pis-to'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4103\nStrong's: to assure\nKJV: —assure of.\n\nSee: G4103 \n\nCognate Group: G570 (unbelief), G571 (that believeth not), G4104 (assure of), G4103 (believe), G4100 (believe), G569 (believe not), G4102 (assurance), G3640 (of little faith)\nVariants: πιστόω, πιστόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H5975 עָמַד, H6004 עָמַם, H6965 קוּם",
    4105: "πλανάω (planáō | plan-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4106\nStrong's: to (properly, cause to) roam (from safety, truth, or virtue)\nKJV: —go astray, deceive, err, seduce, wander, be out of the way.\n\nSee: G4106 \n\nCognate Group: G4105 (go astray), G4107 (wandering), G4108 (deceiver), G4106 (deceit), G635 (err)\nVariants: πλανάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H833 אָשַׁר, H943 בּוּךְ, H2926 טָלַל, H3782 כָּשַׁל, H4603 מָעַל, H5080 נָדַח, H5186 נָטָה, H5493 סוּר, H6340 פָּזַר, H6437 פָּנָה, H6586 פָּשַׁע, H6601 פָּתָה, H7686 שָׁגָה, H7953 שָׁלָה, H7971 שָׁלַח, H8582 תָּעָה, H8582 תָּעָה",
    4106: "πλάνη (plánē | plan'-ay)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4108 (as abstractly)\nStrong's: objectively, fraudulence; subjectively, a straying from orthodoxy or piety\nKJV: —deceit, to deceive, delusion, error.\n\nSee: G4108 \n\nCognate Group: G4105 (go astray), G4107 (wandering), G4108 (deceiver), G4106 (deceit), G635 (err)\nVariants: πλάνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H4820 מִרְמָה, H6588 פֶּשַׁע, H8307 שְׁרִירוּת",
    4107: "πλανήτης (planḗtēs | plan-ay'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4108\nStrong's: a rover (\"planet\"), i.e. (figuratively) an erratic teacher\nKJV: —wandering.\n\nSee: G4108 \n\nCognate Group: G4105 (go astray), G4107 (wandering), G4108 (deceiver), G4106 (deceit), G635 (err)\nVariants: πλανήτης, πλάνης \nHebrew Equivalents: H5074 נָדַד",
    4108: "πλάνος (plános | plan'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: roving (as a tramp), i.e. (by implication) an impostor or misleader; \nKJV: —deceiver, seducing.\n\nCognate Group: G4105 (go astray), G4107 (wandering), G4108 (deceiver), G4106 (deceit), G635 (err)\nVariants: πλάνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4879 מְשׁוּגָה",
    4109: "πλάξ (pláx | plax)\n\nDerivation: from G4111\nStrong's: a moulding-board, i.e. flat surface (\"plate\", or tablet, literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —table.\n\nSee: G4111 \n\nCognate Group: G4105 (go astray), G4107 (wandering), G4108 (deceiver), G4106 (deceit), G635 (err)\nVariants: πλάξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3871 לוּחַ",
    4110: "πλάσμα (plásma | plas'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4111\nStrong's: something moulded\nKJV: —thing formed.\n\nSee: G4111 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλάσμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H3336 יֵצֶר",
    4111: "πλάσσω (plássō | plas'-so)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to mould, i.e. shape or fabricate\nKJV: —form.\n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H908 בָּדָא, H2342 חוּל, H3335 יָצַר, H3559 כּוּן, H5341 נָצַר, H6087 עָצַב, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6696 צוּר",
    4112: "πλαστός (plastós | plas-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G4111\nStrong's: moulded, i.e. (by implication) artificial or (figuratively) fictitious (false)\nKJV: —feigned.\n\nSee: G4111 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλαστός",
    4113: "πλατεῖα (plateîa | plat-i'-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4116\nStrong's: a wide \"plat\" or \"place\", i.e. open square\nKJV: —street.\n\nSee: G4116 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλατεῖα",
    4114: "πλάτος (plátos | plat'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4116\nStrong's: width\nKJV: —breadth.\n\nSee: G4116 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλάτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3709 כַּף, H3871 לוּחַ, H5672 עֲבִי, H6613 פְּתַי, H7341 רֹחַב",
    4115: "πλατύνω (platýnō | plat-oo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4116\nStrong's: to widen (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —make broad, enlarge.\n\nSee: G4116 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλατύνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3680 כָּסָה, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6555 פָּרַץ, H6601 פָּתָה, H7337 רָחַב",
    4116: "πλατύς (platýs | plat-oos')\n\nDerivation: from G4111\nStrong's: spread out \"flat\" (\"plot\"), i.e. broad\nKJV: —wide.\n\nSee: G4111 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλατύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H7342 רָחָב",
    4117: "πλέγμα (plégma | pleg'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4120\nStrong's: a plait (of hair)\nKJV: —broidered hair.\n\nSee: G4120 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλέγμα",
    4118: "πλεῖστος (pleîstos | plice'-tos)\n\nDerivation: irregular superlative of G4183\nStrong's: the largest number or very large\nKJV: —very great, most.\n\nSee: G4183 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλεῖστος, πολύς",
    4119: "πλείων (pleíōn | pli-own), or neuter πλεῖον (pleîon | pli'-on), or πλέον (pléon | pleh'-on)\n\nDerivation: comparative of G4183\nStrong's: more in quantity, number, or quality; also (in plural) the major portion\nKJV: —X above, + exceed, more excellent, further, (very) great(-er), long(-er), (very) many, greater (more) part, + yet but.\n\nSee: G4183 \n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλείων, πολύς",
    4120: "πλέκω (plékō | plek'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: to twine or braid\nKJV: —plait.\n\nCognate Group: G4112 (feigned), G4111 (form), G4110 (thing formed)\nVariants: πλέκω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5688 עֲבֹת, H6843 צְפִירָה",
    4121: "πλεονάζω (pleonázō | pleh-on-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4119\nStrong's: to do, make or be more, i.e. increase (transitively or intransitively); by extension, to superabound\nKJV: —abound, abundant, make to increase, have over.\n\nSee: G4119 \n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G5250 (be exceeding abundant)\nVariants: πλεονάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3513 כָּבַד, H4767 מִרְבָּה, H5736 עֲדַף, H6186 עָרַךְ, H7235 רָבָה, H7971 שָׁלַח",
    4122: "πλεονεκτέω (pleonektéō | pleh-on-cek-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4123\nStrong's: to be covetous, i.e. (by implication) to over-reach\nKJV: —get an advantage, defraud, make a gain.\n\nSee: G4123 \n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G4123 (covetous), G4122 (get an advantage)\nVariants: πλεονεκτέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1214 בָּצַע",
    4123: "πλεονέκτης (pleonéktēs | pleh-on-ek'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4119 and G2192\nStrong's: holding (desiring) more, i.e. eager for gain (avaricious, hence a defrauder)\nKJV: —covetous.\n\nSee: G4119 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G4123 (covetous), G4122 (get an advantage)\nVariants: πλεονέκτης",
    4124: "πλεονεξία (pleonexía | pleh-on-ex-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4123\nStrong's: avarice, i.e. (by implication) fraudulency, extortion\nKJV: —covetous(-ness) practices, greediness.\n\nSee: G4123 \n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G4123 (covetous), G4122 (get an advantage)\nVariants: πλεονεξία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1215 בֶּצַע",
    4125: "πλευρά (pleurá | plyoo-rah')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a rib, i.e. (by extension) side\nKJV: —side.\n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G4123 (covetous), G4122 (get an advantage)\nVariants: πλευρά \nHebrew Equivalents: H663 אֲפֵק, H2502 חָלַץ, H3326 יָצוּעַ, H3326 יָצוּעַ, H5966 עָלַע, H6106 עֶצֶם, H6654 צַד, H6763 צֵלָע",
    4126: "πλέω (pléō | pleh'-o), another form for πλεύω (pleúō | plyoo'-o)\n\n; which is used as an alternate in certain tenses\n\nDerivation: probably a form of G4150 (through the idea of plunging through the water)\nStrong's: to pass in a vessel\nKJV: —sail. See also G4130 .\n\nSee: G4150 \nSee: G4130 \n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G4123 (covetous), G4122 (get an advantage)\nVariants: πλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא",
    4127: "πληγή (plēgḗ | play-gay')\n\nDerivation: from G4141\nStrong's: a stroke; by implication, a wound; figuratively, a calamity\nKJV: —plague, stripe, wound(-ed).\n\nSee: G4141 \n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G4123 (covetous), G4122 (get an advantage)\nVariants: πληγή \nHebrew Equivalents: H3511 כְּאֵב, H4046 מַגֵּפָה, H4294 מַטֶּה, H4347 מַכָּה, H5061 נֶגַע, H5063 נֶגֶף, H7626 שֵׁבֶט",
    4128: "πλῆθος (plēthos | play'-thos)\n\nDerivation: from G4130\nStrong's: a fulness, i.e. a large number, throng, populace\nKJV: —bundle, company, multitude.\n\nSee: G4130 \n\nCognate Group: G4128 (bundle), G4129 (abound)\nVariants: πλῆθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1215 בֶּצַע, H1363 גֹּבַהּ, H1995 הָמֹון, H3515 כָּבֵד, H3524 כַּבִּיר, H4393 מְלֹא, H4557 מִסְפָּר, H5971 עַם, H6099 עָצוּם, H6635 צָבָא, H6951 קָהָל, H7230 רֹב, H7285 רֶגֶשׁ, H8229 שִׁפְעָה",
    4129: "πληθύνω (plēthýnō | play-thoo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from another form of G4128\nStrong's: to increase (transitively or intransitively)\nKJV: —abound, multiply.\n\nSee: G4128 \n\nCognate Group: G4128 (bundle), G4129 (abound)\nVariants: πληθύνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1711 דָּגָה, H3498 יָתַר, H4390 מָלֵא, H5107 נוּב, H5848 עָטַף, H6105 עָצַם, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6555 פָּרַץ, H7232 רָבַב, H7235 רָבָה, H7372 רָטַב, H7646 שָׂבַע, H7685 שָׂגָה, H8317 שָׁרַץ",
    4130: "πλήθω (plḗthō | play'-tho)\n\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary πλέω (pléō | pleh'-o)\n (which appears only as an alternate in certain tenses and in the reduplicated form pimplemi)\nStrong's: to \"fill\" (literally or figuratively (imbue, influence, supply)); specially, to fulfil (time)\nKJV: —accomplish, full (…come), furnish.\n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλήθω, πίμπλημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H4390 מָלֵא, H7491 רָעַף, H7646 שָׂבַע",
    4131: "πλήκτης (plḗktēs | plake'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4141\nStrong's: a smiter, i.e. pugnacious (quarrelsome)\nKJV: —striker.\n\nSee: G4141 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλήκτης",
    4132: "πλήμμυρα (plḗmmyra | plame-moo'-rah)\n\nDerivation: prolonged from G4130\nStrong's: flood-tide, i.e. (by analogy) a freshet\nKJV: —flood.\n\nSee: G4130 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλήμμυρα, πλημμύρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6231 עָשַׁק",
    4133: "πλήν (plḗn | plane)\n\nDerivation: from G4119\nStrong's: moreover (besides), i.e. albeit, save that, rather, yet\nKJV: —but (rather), except, nevertheless, notwithstanding, save, than.\n\nSee: G4119 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλήν \nHebrew Equivalents: H56 אָבַל, H197 אוּלָם, H389 אַךְ, H403 אָכֵן, H639 אַף, H657 אֶפֶס, H905 בַּד, H1115 בִּלְתִּי, H1297 בְּרַם, H2009 הִנֵּה, H2108 זוּלָה, H3588 כִּי, H3861 לָהֵן, H4327 מִין, H5921 עַל, H5973 עִם, H6118 עֵקֶב, H7535 רַק",
    4134: "πλήρης (plḗrēs | play'-race)\n\nDerivation: from G4130\nStrong's: replete, or covered over; by analogy, complete\nKJV: —full.\n\nSee: G4130 \n\nCognate Group: G4137 (accomplish), G4136 (assurance), G1604 (accomplishment), G4845 (come), G466 (fill up), G378 (fill up), G4134 (full), G4135 (most surely believe), G1603 (fulfill), G4138 (which is put in to fill up)\nVariants: πλήρης \nHebrew Equivalents: H3515 כָּבֵד, H4390 מָלֵא, H4390 מָלֵא, H5674 עָבַר, H7647 שָׂבָע, H7649 שָׂבֵעַ, H8003 שָׁלֵם",
    4135: "πληροφορέω (plērophoréō | play-rof-or-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4134 and G5409\nStrong's: to carry out fully (in evidence), i.e. completely assure (or convince), entirely accomplish\nKJV: —most surely believe, fully know (persuade), make full proof of.\n\nSee: G4134 \nSee: G5409 \n\nCognate Group: G4137 (accomplish), G4136 (assurance), G1604 (accomplishment), G4845 (come), G466 (fill up), G378 (fill up), G4134 (full), G4135 (most surely believe), G1603 (fulfill), G4138 (which is put in to fill up)\nVariants: πληροφορέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4390 מָלֵא",
    4136: "πληροφορία (plērophoría | play-rof-or-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4135\nStrong's: entire confidence\nKJV: —(full) assurance.\n\nSee: G4135 \n\nCognate Group: G4137 (accomplish), G4136 (assurance), G1604 (accomplishment), G4845 (come), G466 (fill up), G378 (fill up), G4134 (full), G4135 (most surely believe), G1603 (fulfill), G4138 (which is put in to fill up)\nVariants: πληροφορία",
    4137: "πληρόω (plēróō | play-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4134\nStrong's: to make replete, i.e. (literally) to cram (a net), level up (a hollow), or (figuratively) to furnish (or imbue, diffuse, influence), satisfy, execute (an office), finish (a period or task), verify (or coincide with a prediction), etc.\nKJV: —accomplish, X after, (be) complete, end, expire, fill (up), fulfil, (be, make) full (come), fully preach, perfect, supply.\n\nSee: G4134 \n\nCognate Group: G4137 (accomplish), G4136 (assurance), G1604 (accomplishment), G4845 (come), G466 (fill up), G378 (fill up), G4134 (full), G4135 (most surely believe), G1603 (fulfill), G4138 (which is put in to fill up)\nVariants: πληρόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3615 כָּלָה, H4390 מָלֵא, H5375 נָשָׂא, H7093 קֵץ, H7646 שָׂבַע, H8002 שֶׁלֶם, H8552 תָּמַם",
    4138: "πλήρωμα (plḗrōma | play'-ro-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4137\nStrong's: repletion or completion, i.e. (subjectively) what fills (as contents, supplement, copiousness, multitude), or (objectively) what is filled (as container, performance, period)\nKJV: —which is put in to fill up, piece that filled up, fulfilling, full, fulness.\n\nSee: G4137 \n\nCognate Group: G4137 (accomplish), G4136 (assurance), G1604 (accomplishment), G4845 (come), G466 (fill up), G378 (fill up), G4134 (full), G4135 (most surely believe), G1603 (fulfill), G4138 (which is put in to fill up)\nVariants: πλήρωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H663 אֲפֵק, H4393 מְלֹא",
    4139: "πλησίον (plēsíon | play-see'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of πέλας (near)\nStrong's: (adverbially) close by; as noun, a neighbor, i.e. fellow (as man, countryman, Christian or friend)\nKJV: —near, neighbour.\n\nCognate Group: G4139 (near)\nVariants: πλησίον",
    4140: "πλησμονή (plēsmonḗ | place-mon-ay')\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G4130\nStrong's: a filling up, i.e. (figuratively) gratification\nKJV: —satisfying.\n\nSee: G4130 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλησμονή \nHebrew Equivalents: H1879 דָּשֵׁן, H2960 טֹרַח, H7241 רָבִיב, H7647 שָׂבָע, H7649 שָׂבֵעַ",
    4141: "πλήσσω (plḗssō | place'-so)\n\nDerivation: apparently another form of G4111 (through the idea of flattening out)\nStrong's: to pound, i.e. (figuratively) to inflict with (calamity)\nKJV: —smite. Compare G5180 .\n\nSee: G5180 \nSee: G4111 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλήσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3027 יָד, H3772 כָּרַת, H5062 נָגַף, H5221 נָכָה, H5307 נָפַל, H5391 נָשַׁךְ, H6398 פָּלַח",
    4142: "πλοιάριον (ploiárion | ploy-ar'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G4143\nStrong's: a boat\nKJV: —boat, little (small) ship.\n\nSee: G4143 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλοιάριον",
    4143: "πλοῖον (ploîon | ploy'-on)\n\nDerivation: from G4126\nStrong's: a sailer, i.e. vessel\nKJV: —ship(-ing).\n\nSee: G4126 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλοῖον \nHebrew Equivalents: H591 אֳנִיָּה, H5600 סְפִינָה, H6040 עֳנִי, H6716 צִי",
    4144: "πλόος (plóos | plo'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4126\nStrong's: a sail, i.e. navigation\nKJV: —course, sailing, voyage.\n\nSee: G4126 \n\nCognate Group: G1705 (fill), G4130 (accomplish), G4140 (satisfying)\nVariants: πλόος, πλοῦς",
    4145: "πλούσιος (ploúsios | ploo'-see-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4149\nStrong's: wealthy; figuratively, abounding with\nKJV: —rich.\n\nSee: G4149 \n\nCognate Group: G4147 (be increased with goods), G4145 (rich), G4149 (riches), G4148 (en- rich), G4146 (abundantly)\nVariants: πλούσιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3515 כָּבֵד, H3715 כְּפִיר, H5947 עַלִּיז, H6223 עָשִׁיר, H7600 שַׁאֲנָן",
    4146: "πλουσίως (plousíōs | ploo-see'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4145\nStrong's: copiously\nKJV: —abundantly, richly.\n\nSee: G4145 \n\nCognate Group: G4147 (be increased with goods), G4145 (rich), G4149 (riches), G4148 (en- rich), G4146 (abundantly)\nVariants: πλουσίως",
    4147: "πλουτέω (ploutéō | ploo-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4148\nStrong's: to be (or become) wealthy (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —be increased with goods, (be made, wax) rich.\n\nSee: G4148 \n\nCognate Group: G4147 (be increased with goods), G4145 (rich), G4149 (riches), G4148 (en- rich), G4146 (abundantly)\nVariants: πλουτέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1952 הֹון, H6238 עָשַׁר, H6555 פָּרַץ",
    4148: "πλουτίζω (ploutízō | ploo-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4149\nStrong's: to make wealthy (figuratively)\nKJV: —en- (make) rich.\n\nSee: G4149 \n\nCognate Group: G4147 (be increased with goods), G4145 (rich), G4149 (riches), G4148 (en- rich), G4146 (abundantly)\nVariants: πλουτίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6238 עָשַׁר",
    4149: "πλοῦτος (ploûtos | ploo'-tos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4130\nStrong's: wealth (as fulness), i.e. (literally) money, possessions, or (figuratively) abundance, richness, (specially), valuable bestowment\nKJV: —riches.\n\nSee: G4130 \n\nCognate Group: G4147 (be increased with goods), G4145 (rich), G4149 (riches), G4148 (en- rich), G4146 (abundantly)\nVariants: πλοῦτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H214 אֹוצָר, H1420 גְּדוּלָה, H1952 הֹון, H1995 הָמֹון, H2428 חַיִל, H3519 כָּבֹוד, H6239 עֹשֶׁר, H7404 רְכֻלָּה, H8228 שֶׁפַע",
    4150: "πλύνω (plýnō | ploo'-no)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of an obsolete πλύω (to \"flow\")\nStrong's: to \"plunge\", i.e. launder clothing\nKJV: —wash. Compare G3068, G3538 .\n\nSee: G3068 \nSee: G3538 \n\nCognate Group: G4147 (be increased with goods), G4145 (rich), G4149 (riches), G4148 (en- rich), G4146 (abundantly)\nVariants: πλύνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1740 דּוּחַ, H3526 כָּבַס, H7364 רָחַץ",
    4151: "πνεῦμα (pneûma | pnyoo'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4154\nStrong's: a current of air, i.e. breath (blast) or a breeze; by analogy or figuratively, a spirit, i.e. (human) the rational soul, (by implication) vital principle, mental disposition, etc., or (superhuman) an angel, demon, or (divine) God, Christ's spirit, the Holy Spirit\nKJV: —ghost, life, spirit(-ual, -ually), mind. Compare G5590 .\n\nSee: G4154 \nSee: G5590 \n\nCognate Group: G4151 (ghost), G2315 (given by inspiration of God), G4154 (blow), G4153 (spiritually), G1606 (give up the ghost), G4152 (spiritual)\nVariants: πνεῦμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H5397 נְשָׁמָה, H6921 קָדִים, H7307 רוּחַ, H7602 שָׁאַף",
    4152: "πνευματικός (pneumatikós | pnyoo-mat-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4151\nStrong's: non-carnal, i.e. (humanly) ethereal (as opposed to gross), or (dæmoniacally) a spirit (concretely), or (divinely) supernatural, regenerate, religious\nKJV: —spiritual. Compare G5591 .\n\nSee: G4151 \nSee: G5591 \n\nCognate Group: G4151 (ghost), G2315 (given by inspiration of God), G4154 (blow), G4153 (spiritually), G1606 (give up the ghost), G4152 (spiritual)\nVariants: πνευματικός",
    4153: "πνευματικῶς (pneumatikōs | pnyoo-mat-ik-oce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4152\nStrong's: non-physically, i.e. divinely, figuratively\nKJV: —spiritually.\n\nSee: G4152 \n\nCognate Group: G4151 (ghost), G2315 (given by inspiration of God), G4154 (blow), G4153 (spiritually), G1606 (give up the ghost), G4152 (spiritual)\nVariants: πνευματικῶς",
    4154: "πνέω (pnéō | pneh'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: to breathe hard, i.e. breeze\nKJV: —blow. Compare G5594 .\n\nSee: G5594 \n\nCognate Group: G4151 (ghost), G2315 (given by inspiration of God), G4154 (blow), G4153 (spiritually), G1606 (give up the ghost), G4152 (spiritual)\nVariants: πνέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5380 נָשַׁב, H5398 נָשַׁף",
    4155: "πνίγω (pnígō | pnee'-go)\n\nDerivation: strengthened from G4154\nStrong's: to wheeze, i.e. (causative, by implication) to throttle or strangle (drown)\nKJV: —choke, take by the throat.\n\nSee: G4154 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: πνίγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1204 בָּעַת",
    4156: "πνικτός (pniktós | pnik-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G4155\nStrong's: throttled, i.e. (neuter concretely) an animal choked to death (not bled)\nKJV: —strangled.\n\nSee: G4155 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: πνικτός",
    4157: "πνοή (pnoḗ | pno-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4154\nStrong's: respiration, a breeze\nKJV: —breath, wind.\n\nSee: G4154 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: πνοή \nHebrew Equivalents: H5315 נֶפֶשׁ, H5397 נְשָׁמָה, H7307 רוּחַ",
    4158: "ποδήρης (podḗrēs | pod-ay'-race)\n\nDerivation: from G4228 and another element of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a dress (G2066 implied) reaching the ankles\nKJV: —garment down to the foot.\n\nSee: G4228 \nSee: G2066 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: ποδήρης \nHebrew Equivalents: H646 אֵפֹוד, H906 בַּד, H2833 חֹשֶׁן, H4254 מַחֲלָצָה, H4598 מְעִיל",
    4159: "πόθεν (póthen | poth'-en)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4213 with enclitic adverb of origin\nStrong's: from which (as interrogative) or what (as relative) place, state, source or cause\nKJV: —whence.\n\nSee: G4213 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: πόθεν",
    4160: "ποιέω (poiéō | poy-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: apparently a prolonged form of an obsolete primary\nStrong's: to make or do (in a very wide application, more or less direct)\nKJV: —abide, + agree, appoint, X avenge, + band together, be, bear, + bewray, bring (forth), cast out, cause, commit, + content, continue, deal, + without any delay, (would) do(-ing), execute, exercise, fulfil, gain, give, have, hold, X journeying, keep, + lay wait, + lighten the ship, make, X mean, + none of these things move me, observe, ordain, perform, provide, + have purged, purpose, put, + raising up, X secure, shew, X shoot out, spend, take, tarry, + transgress the law, work, yield. Compare G4238 .\n\nSee: G4238 \n\nCognate Group: G4160 (abide), G4163 (doer), G4161 (thing that is made), G4162 (deed)\nVariants: ποιέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H398 אָכַל, H644 אָפָה, H962 בָּזַז, H1129 בָּנָה, H1254 בָּרָא, H1961 הָיָה, H2164 זְמַן, H2398 חָטָא, H2672 חָצַב, H2803 חָשַׁב, H2974 יָאַל, H3190 יָטַב, H3201 יָכֹל, H3313 יָפַע, H3318 יָצָא, H3335 יָצַר, H3467 יָשַׁע, H3772 כָּרַת, H3898 לָחַם, H4376 מָכַר, H5186 נָטָה, H5258 נָסַךְ, H5324 נָצַב, H5341 נָצַר, H5414 נָתַן, H5437 סָבַב, H5647 עָבַד, H5765 עֲוַל, H5927 עָלָה, H5975 עָמַד, H5999 עָמָל, H6087 עָצַב, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6466 פָּעַל, H6586 פָּשַׁע, H6965 קוּם, H7235 רָבָה, H7543 רָקַח, H7760 שׂוּם, H7812 שָׁחָה, H7997 שָׁלַל, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8085 שָׁמַע, H8104 שָׁמַר, H8210 שָׁפַךְ",
    4161: "ποίημα (poíēma | poy'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4160\nStrong's: a product, i.e. fabric (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —thing that is made, workmanship.\n\nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G4160 (abide), G4163 (doer), G4161 (thing that is made), G4162 (deed)\nVariants: ποίημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4639 מַעֲשֶׂה, H6467 פֹּעַל",
    4162: "ποίησις (poíēsis | poy'-ay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4160\nStrong's: action, i.e. performance (of the law)\nKJV: —deed.\n\nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G4160 (abide), G4163 (doer), G4161 (thing that is made), G4162 (deed)\nVariants: ποίησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4639 מַעֲשֶׂה",
    4163: "ποιητής (poiētḗs | poy-ay-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G4160\nStrong's: a performer; specially, a \"poet\"; \nKJV: —doer, poet.\n\nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G4160 (abide), G4163 (doer), G4161 (thing that is made), G4162 (deed)\nVariants: ποιητής",
    4164: "ποικίλος (poikílos | poy-kee'-los)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: motley, i.e. various in character\nKJV: —divers, manifold.\n\nCognate Group: G4164 (divers), G4182 (manifold)\nVariants: ποικίλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1261 בָּרֹד, H2345 חוּם, H5348 נָקֹד, H6447 פַּס, H7553 רִקְמָה, H8220 שִׁפְלוּת",
    4165: "ποιμαίνω (poimaínō | poy-mah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4166\nStrong's: to tend as a shepherd of (figuratively, superviser)\nKJV: —feed (cattle), rule.\n\nSee: G4166 \n\nCognate Group: G4167 (flock), G4165 (feed), G750 (chief shepherd), G4168 (flock), G4166 (shepherd)\nVariants: ποιμαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5090 נָהַג, H7462 רָעָה",
    4166: "ποιμήν (poimḗn | poy-mane')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a shepherd (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —shepherd, pastor.\n\nCognate Group: G4167 (flock), G4165 (feed), G750 (chief shepherd), G4168 (flock), G4166 (shepherd)\nVariants: ποιμήν \nHebrew Equivalents: H7200 רָאָה",
    4167: "ποίμνη (poímnē | poym'-nay)\n\nDerivation: contraction from G4165\nStrong's: a flock (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —flock, fold.\n\nSee: G4165 \n\nCognate Group: G4167 (flock), G4165 (feed), G750 (chief shepherd), G4168 (flock), G4166 (shepherd)\nVariants: ποίμνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H5739 עֵדֶר",
    4168: "ποίμνιον (poímnion | poym'-nee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G4167\nStrong's: a flock, i.e. (figuratively) group (of believers)\nKJV: —flock.\n\nSee: G4167 \n\nCognate Group: G4167 (flock), G4165 (feed), G750 (chief shepherd), G4168 (flock), G4166 (shepherd)\nVariants: ποίμνιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2835 חָשִׂף, H4356 מִכְלָאָה, H4830 מִרְעִית, H5739 עֵדֶר, H6253 עַשְׁתֹּרֶת, H6629 צֹאן, H7716 שֶׂה",
    4169: "ποῖος (poîos | poy'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4226 and G3634\nStrong's: individualizing interrogative (of character) what sort of, or (of number) which one\nKJV: —what (manner of), which.\n\nSee: G4226 \nSee: G3634 \n\nCognate Group: G4167 (flock), G4165 (feed), G750 (chief shepherd), G4168 (flock), G4166 (shepherd)\nVariants: ποῖος, ποία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1287 בֹּרִית, H1877 דֶּשֶׁא, H2088 זֶה, H4100 מָה, H4478 מָן",
    4170: "πολεμέω (poleméō | pol-em-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4171\nStrong's: to be (engaged) in warfare, i.e. to battle (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —fight, (make) war.\n\nSee: G4171 \n\nCognate Group: G4171 (battle), G4170 (fight)\nVariants: πολεμέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3898 לָחַם, H6887 צָרַר",
    4171: "πόλεμος (pólemos | pol'-em-os)\n\nDerivation: from πέλομαι (to bustle)\nStrong's: warfare (literally or figuratively; a single encounter or a series)\nKJV: —battle, fight, war.\n\nCognate Group: G4171 (battle), G4170 (fight)\nVariants: πόλεμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1624 גָּרָה, H2428 חַיִל, H2719 חֶרֶב, H4264 מַחֲנֶה, H4421 מִלְחָמָה, H5402 נֶשֶׁק, H6635 צָבָא, H7128 קְרָב, H7973 שֶׁלַח",
    4172: "πόλις (pólis | pol'-is)\n\nDerivation: probably from the same as G4171, or perhaps from G4183\nStrong's: a town (properly, with walls, of greater or less size)\nKJV: —city.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G4171 \n\nCognate Group: G4172 (city), G4173 (ruler of the city), G4175 (conversation), G4177 (citizen), G4174 (commonwealth), G4176 (let conversation be)\nVariants: πόλις \nHebrew Equivalents: H202 אֹון, H759 אַרְמֹון, H1002 בִּירָה, H4725 מָקֹום, H5892 עִיר, H7149 קִרְיָא, H8179 שַׁעַר",
    4173: "πολιτάρχης (politárchēs | pol-it-ar'-khace)\n\nDerivation: from G4172 and G757\nStrong's: a town-officer, i.e. magistrate\nKJV: —ruler of the city.\n\nSee: G4172 \nSee: G757 \n\nCognate Group: G4172 (city), G4173 (ruler of the city), G4175 (conversation), G4177 (citizen), G4174 (commonwealth), G4176 (let conversation be)\nVariants: πολιτάρχης",
    4174: "πολιτεία (politeía | pol-ee-ti'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4177 (\"polity\")\nStrong's: citizenship; concretely, a community\nKJV: —commonwealth, freedom.\n\nSee: G4177 \n\nCognate Group: G4172 (city), G4173 (ruler of the city), G4175 (conversation), G4177 (citizen), G4174 (commonwealth), G4176 (let conversation be)\nVariants: πολιτεία",
    4175: "πολίτευμα (políteuma | pol-it'-yoo-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4176\nStrong's: a community, i.e. (abstractly) citizenship (figuratively)\nKJV: —conversation.\n\nSee: G4176 \n\nCognate Group: G4172 (city), G4173 (ruler of the city), G4175 (conversation), G4177 (citizen), G4174 (commonwealth), G4176 (let conversation be)\nVariants: πολίτευμα",
    4176: "πολιτεύομαι (politeúomai | pol-it-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of a derivative of G4177\nStrong's: to behave as a citizen (figuratively)\nKJV: —let conversation be, live.\n\nSee: G4177 \n\nCognate Group: G4172 (city), G4173 (ruler of the city), G4175 (conversation), G4177 (citizen), G4174 (commonwealth), G4176 (let conversation be)\nVariants: πολιτεύομαι, πολιτεύω",
    4177: "πολίτης (polítēs | pol-ee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4172\nStrong's: a townsman\nKJV: —citizen.\n\nSee: G4172 \n\nCognate Group: G4172 (city), G4173 (ruler of the city), G4175 (conversation), G4177 (citizen), G4174 (commonwealth), G4176 (let conversation be)\nVariants: πολίτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H1121 בֵּן, H5997 עָמִית, H7453 רֵעַ",
    4178: "πολλάκις (pollákis | pol-lak'-is)\n\nDerivation: multiplicative adverb from G4183\nStrong's: many times, i.e. frequently\nKJV: —oft(-en, -entimes, -times).\n\nSee: G4183 \n\nCognate Group: G4181 (at sundry times), G4178 (often), G4182 (manifold), G4183 (abundant), G4180 (much speaking)\nVariants: πολλάκις, ἐνίοτε",
    4179: "πολλαπλασίων (pollaplasíōn | pol-lap-las-ee'-ohn)\n\nDerivation: from G4183 and probably a derivative of G4120\nStrong's: manifold, i.e. (neuter as noun) very much more\nKJV: —manifold more.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G4120 \n\nCognate Group: G4181 (at sundry times), G4178 (often), G4182 (manifold), G4183 (abundant), G4180 (much speaking)\nVariants: πολλαπλασίων, ἑπταπλασίων",
    4180: "πολυλογία (polylogía | pol-oo-log-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4183 and G3056\nStrong's: loquacity, i.e. prolixity\nKJV: —much speaking.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G4181 (at sundry times), G4178 (often), G4182 (manifold), G4183 (abundant), G4180 (much speaking)\nVariants: πολυλογία",
    4181: "πολυμερῶς (polymerōs | pol-oo-mer'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a compound of G4183 and G3313\nStrong's: in many portions, i.e. variously as to time and agency (piecemeal)\nKJV: —at sundry times.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G3313 \n\nCognate Group: G4181 (at sundry times), G4178 (often), G4182 (manifold), G4183 (abundant), G4180 (much speaking)\nVariants: πολυμερῶς",
    4182: "πολυποίκιλος (polypoíkilos | pol-oo-poy'-kil-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4183 and G4164\nStrong's: much variegated, i.e. multifarious\nKJV: —manifold.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G4164 \n\nCognate Group: G4164 (divers), G4182 (manifold)\nVariants: πολυποίκιλος",
    4183: "πολύς (polýs | pol-oos')\n\nDerivation: including the forms from the alternate πολλός\nStrong's: (singular) much (in any respect) or (plural) many; neuter (singular) as adverbial, largely; neuter (plural) as adverb or noun often, mostly, largely\nKJV: —abundant, + altogether, common, + far (passed, spent), (+ be of a) great (age, deal, -ly, while), long, many, much, oft(-en (-times)), plenteous, sore, straitly. Compare G4118, G4119 .\n\nSee: G4118 \nSee: G4119 \n\nCognate Group: G4181 (at sundry times), G4178 (often), G4182 (manifold), G4183 (abundant), G4180 (much speaking)\nVariants: πολύς",
    4184: "πολύσπλαγχνος (polýsplanchnos | pol-oo'-splankh-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G4183 and G4698 (figuratively)\nStrong's: extremely compassionate\nKJV: —very pitiful.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G4698 \n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: πολύσπλαγχνος, πολυεύσπλαγχνος",
    4185: "πολυτελής (polytelḗs | pol-oo-tel-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G4183 and G5056\nStrong's: extremely expensive\nKJV: —costly, very precious, of great price.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G5056 \n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: πολυτελής \nHebrew Equivalents: H3368 יָקָר, H3800 כֶּתֶם, H6320 פּוּךְ, H6443 פָּנִין",
    4186: "πολύτιμος (polýtimos | pol-oot'-ee-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G4183 and G5092\nStrong's: extremely valuable\nKJV: —very costly, of great price.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G5092 \n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: πολύτιμος",
    4187: "πολυτρόπως (polytrópōs | pol-oot-rop'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a compound of G4183 and G5158\nStrong's: in many ways, i.e. variously as to method or form\nKJV: —in divers manners.\n\nSee: G4183 \nSee: G5158 \n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: πολυτρόπως",
    4188: "πόμα (póma | pom'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G4095\nStrong's: a beverage\nKJV: —drink.\n\nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: πόμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4960 מִשְׁתֶּה, H8250 שִׁקּוּי",
    4189: "πονηρία (ponēría | pon-ay-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4190\nStrong's: depravity, i.e. (specially), malice; plural (concretely) plots, sins\nKJV: —iniquity, wickedness.\n\nSee: G4190 \n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: πονηρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H205 אָוֶן, H3336 יֵצֶר, H5999 עָמָל, H7200 רָאָה, H7451 רַע, H8441 תֹּועֵבַה",
    4190: "πονηρός (ponērós | pon-ay-ros')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4192\nStrong's: hurtful, i.e. evil (properly, in effect or influence, and thus differing from G2556, which refers rather to essential character, as well as from G4550, which indicates degeneracy from original virtue); figuratively, calamitous; also (passively) ill, i.e. diseased; but especially (morally) culpable, i.e. derelict, vicious, facinorous; neuter (singular) mischief, malice, or (plural) guilt; masculine (singular) the devil, or (plural) sinners\nKJV: —bad, evil, grievous, harm, lewd, malicious, wicked(-ness). See also G4191 .\n\nSee: G4192 \nSee: G2556 \nSee: G4550 \nSee: G4191 \n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: πονηρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H343 אֵיד, H873 בִּאוּשׁ, H1681 דִּבָּה, H1800 דַּל, H7200 רָאָה, H7227 רַב, H7451 רַע, H7489 רָעַע, H7489 רָעַע, H7563 רָשָׁע, H8441 תֹּועֵבַה",
    4191: "πονηρότερος (ponēróteros | pon-ay-rot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of G4190\nStrong's: more evil\nKJV: —more wicked.\n\nSee: G4190 \n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: πονηρότερος, πονηρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H343 אֵיד, H873 בִּאוּשׁ, H1681 דִּבָּה, H1800 דַּל, H7200 רָאָה, H7227 רַב, H7451 רַע, H7489 רָעַע, H7489 רָעַע, H7563 רָשָׁע, H8441 תֹּועֵבַה",
    4192: "πόνος (pónos | pon'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G3993\nStrong's: toil, i.e. (by implication) anguish\nKJV: —pain.\n\nSee: G3993 \n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: πόνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H205 אָוֶן, H343 אֵיד, H2256 חֶבֶל, H2483 חֳלִי, H3015 יָגֹון, H3018 יְגִיעַ, H3510 כָּאַב, H3511 כְּאֵב, H4245 מַחֲלֶה, H5060 נָגַע, H5450 סְבָלָה, H5999 עָמָל, H6001 עָמֵל, H6089 עֶצֶב, H6468 פְּעֻלָּה, H8463 תַּחֲלוּא",
    4193: "Ποντικός (Pontikós | pon-tik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4195\nStrong's: a Pontican, i.e. native of Pontus\nKJV: —born in Pontus.\n\nSee: G4195 \n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: Ποντικός",
    4194: "Πόντιος (Póntios | pon'-tee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: apparently bridged; Pontius, a Roman\nKJV: —Pontius.\n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: Πόντιος",
    4195: "Πόντος (Póntos | pon'-tos)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a sea; Pontus, a region of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Pontus.\n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: Πόντος",
    4196: "Πόπλιος (Póplios | pop'-lee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: apparently \"popular\"; Poplius (i.e. Publius), a Roman\nKJV: —Publius.\n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: Πόπλιος",
    4197: "πορεία (poreía | por-i'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4198\nStrong's: travel (by land); figuratively (plural) proceedings, i.e. career\nKJV: —journey(-ing), ways.\n\nSee: G4198 \n\nCognate Group: G4189 (iniquity), G4191 (more wicked), G4190 (bad)\nVariants: πορεία, πορία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1979 הֲלִיכָה, H4161 מֹוצָא, H6806 צַעַד",
    4198: "πορεύομαι (poreúomai | por-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from a derivative of the same as G3984\nStrong's: to traverse, i.e. travel (literally or figuratively; especially to remove (figuratively, die), live, etc.); \nKJV: —depart, go (away, forth, one's way, up), (make a, take a) journey, walk.\n\nSee: G3984 \n\nCognate Group: G4198 (depart), G1607 (come), G1531 (come in)\nVariants: πορεύομαι, πορεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H235 אָזַל, H935 בֹּוא, H1980 הָלַךְ, H1980 הָלַךְ, H3318 יָצָא, H3381 יָרַד, H4390 מָלֵא, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H5503 סָחַר, H5674 עָבַר, H5927 עָלָה, H6805 צָעַד, H6923 קָדַם, H7126 קָרַב, H7725 שׁוּב",
    4199: "πορθέω (porthéō | por-theh'-o)\n\nDerivation: prolongation from πέρθω (to sack)\nStrong's: to ravage (figuratively)\nKJV: —destroy, waste.\n\nCognate Group: G4198 (depart), G1607 (come), G1531 (come in)\nVariants: πορθέω",
    4200: "πορισμός (porismós | por-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of πόρος (a way, i.e. means)\nStrong's: furnishing (procuring), i.e. (by implication) money-getting (acquisition)\nKJV: —gain.\n\nCognate Group: G4198 (depart), G1607 (come), G1531 (come in)\nVariants: πορισμός",
    4201: "Πόρκιος (Pórkios | por'-kee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: apparently swinish; Porcius, a Roman\nKJV: —Porcius.\n\nCognate Group: G4198 (depart), G1607 (come), G1531 (come in)\nVariants: Πόρκιος",
    4202: "πορνεία (porneía | por-ni'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4203\nStrong's: harlotry (including adultery and incest); figuratively, idolatry\nKJV: —fornication.\n\nSee: G4203 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πορνεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2183 זָנוּן, H8457 תַּזְנוּת",
    4203: "πορνεύω (porneúō | porn-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4204\nStrong's: to act the harlot, i.e. (literally) indulge unlawful lust (of either sex), or (figuratively) practise idolatry\nKJV: —commit (fornication).\n\nSee: G4204 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πορνεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2181 זָנָה, H6945 קָדֵשׁ",
    4204: "πόρνη (pórnē | por'-nay)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4205\nStrong's: a strumpet; figuratively, an idolater\nKJV: —harlot, whore.\n\nSee: G4205 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πόρνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2181 זָנָה, H2219 זָרָה, H4464 מַמְזֵר, H6945 קָדֵשׁ",
    4205: "πόρνος (pórnos | por'-nos)\n\nDerivation: from πέρνημι (to sell\nStrong's: akin to the base of G4097); a (male) prostitute (as venal), i.e. (by analogy) a debauchee (libertine)\nKJV: —fornicator, whoremonger.\n\nSee: G4097 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πόρνος",
    4206: "πόῤῥω (pórrhō | por'-rho)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4253\nStrong's: forwards, i.e. at a distance\nKJV: —far, a great way off. See also G4207 .\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G4207 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πόῤῥω, πόρρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5704 עַד, H7350 רָחֹוק, H7368 רָחַק, H7368 רָחַק, H7368 רָחַק",
    4207: "πόῤῥωθεν (pórrhōthen | por'-rho-then)\n\nDerivation: from G4206 with adverbial enclitic of source\nStrong's: from far, or (by implication) at a distance, i.e. distantly\nKJV: —afar off.\n\nSee: G4206 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πόῤῥωθεν, πόρρωθεν \nHebrew Equivalents: H7350 רָחֹוק, H7350 רָחֹוק",
    4208: "ποῤῥωτέρω (porrhōtérō | por-rho-ter'-o)\n\nDerivation: adverb comparative of G4206\nStrong's: further, i.e. a greater distance\nKJV: —farther.\n\nSee: G4206 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: ποῤῥωτέρω, πορρωτέρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5704 עַד, H7350 רָחֹוק, H7368 רָחַק, H7368 רָחַק, H7368 רָחַק",
    4209: "πορφύρα (porphýra | por-foo'-rah)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: the \"purple\" mussel, i.e. (by implication) the red-blue color itself, and finally a garment dyed with it\nKJV: —purple.\n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πορφύρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H710 אַרְגְּוָן, H713 אַרְגָּמָן",
    4210: "πορφυροῦς (porphyroûs | por-foo-rooce')\n\nDerivation: from G4209\nStrong's: purpureal, i.e. bluish red\nKJV: —purple.\n\nSee: G4209 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πορφυροῦς, πορφύρεος \nHebrew Equivalents: H713 אַרְגָּמָן",
    4211: "πορφυρόπωλις (porphyrópōlis | por-foo-rop'-o-lis)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a compound of G4209 and G4453\nStrong's: a female trader in purple cloth\nKJV: —seller of purple.\n\nSee: G4209 \nSee: G4453 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: πορφυρόπωλις",
    4212: "ποσάκις (posákis | pos-ak'-is)\n\nDerivation: multiplicative from G4214\nStrong's: how many times\nKJV: —how oft(-en).\n\nSee: G4214 \n\nCognate Group: G4203 (commit), G4202 (fornication), G1608 (give self over to fornication), G4204 (harlot), G4205 (fornicator)\nVariants: ποσάκις",
    4213: "πόσις (pósis | pos'-is)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G4095\nStrong's: a drinking (the act), i.e. (concretely) a draught\nKJV: —drink.\n\nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: πόσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4960 מִשְׁתֶּה",
    4214: "πόσος (pósos | pos'-os)\n\nDerivation: from an absolute πός (who, what) and G3739\nStrong's: interrogative pronoun (of amount) how much (large, long or (plural) many)\nKJV: —how great (long, many), what.\n\nSee: G3739 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: πόσος",
    4215: "ποταμός (potamós | pot-am-os')\n\nDerivation: probably from a derivative of the alternate of G4095 (compare G4224)\nStrong's: a current, brook or freshet (as drinkable), i.e. running water\nKJV: —flood, river, stream, water.\n\nSee: G4095 \nSee: G4224 \n\nCognate Group: G4215 (flood), G4216 (carried away of the flood)\nVariants: ποταμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2975 יְאֹר, H5104 נָהָר, H5158 נַחַל, H6388 פֶּלֶג",
    4216: "ποταμοφόρητος (potamophórētos | pot-am-of-or'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G4215 and a derivative of G5409\nStrong's: river-borne, i.e. overwhelmed by a stream\nKJV: —carried away of the flood.\n\nSee: G4215 \nSee: G5409 \n\nCognate Group: G4215 (flood), G4216 (carried away of the flood)\nVariants: ποταμοφόρητος",
    4217: "ποταπός (potapós | pot-ap-os')\n\nDerivation: apparently from G4219 and the base of G4226\nStrong's: interrogatively, whatever, i.e. of what possible sort\nKJV: —what (manner of).\n\nSee: G4219 \nSee: G4226 \n\nCognate Group: G4215 (flood), G4216 (carried away of the flood)\nVariants: ποταπός, ποδαπός",
    4218: "ποτέ (poté | pot-eh')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4225 and G5037\nStrong's: indefinite adverb, at some time, ever\nKJV: —afore-(any, some-)time(-s), at length (the last), (+ n-)ever, in the old time, in time past, once, when.\n\nSee: G4225 \nSee: G5037 \n\nCognate Group: G4215 (flood), G4216 (carried away of the flood)\nVariants: ποτέ",
    4219: "πότε (póte | pot'-eh)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4226 and G5037\nStrong's: interrogative adverb, at what time\nKJV: —+ how long, when.\n\nSee: G4226 \nSee: G5037 \n\nCognate Group: G4215 (flood), G4216 (carried away of the flood)\nVariants: πότε",
    4220: "πότερον (póteron | pot'-er-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a comparative of the base of G4226\nStrong's: interrogative as adverb, which (of two), i.e. is it this or that\nKJV: —whether.\n\nSee: G4226 \n\nCognate Group: G4215 (flood), G4216 (carried away of the flood)\nVariants: πότερον",
    4221: "ποτήριον (potḗrion | pot-ay'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of the alternate of G4095\nStrong's: a drinking-vessel; by extension, the contents thereof, i.e. a cupful (draught); figuratively, a lot or fate\nKJV: —cup.\n\nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: ποτήριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3563 כֹּוס, H3627 כְּלִי, H6907 קֻבַּעַת",
    4222: "ποτίζω (potízō | pot-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the alternate of G4095\nStrong's: to furnish drink, irrigate\nKJV: —give (make) to drink, feed, water.\n\nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: ποτίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1572 גָּמָא, H5258 נָסַךְ, H8248 שָׁקָה",
    4223: "Ποτίολοι (Potíoloi | pot-ee'-ol-oy)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: little wells, i.e. mineral springs; Potioli (i.e. Puteoli), a place in Italy\nKJV: —Puteoli.\n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: Ποτίολοι",
    4224: "πότος (pótos | pot'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G4095\nStrong's: a drinking-bout or carousal\nKJV: —banqueting.\n\nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: πότος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4960 מִשְׁתֶּה",
    4225: "πού (poú | poo)\n\nDerivation: genitive case of an indefinite pronoun πός (some) otherwise obsolete (compare G4214)\nStrong's: as adverb of place, somewhere, i.e. nearly\nKJV: —about, a certain place.\n\nSee: G4214 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: πού",
    4226: "ποῦ (poû | poo)\n\nDerivation: genitive case of an interrogative pronoun πός (what) otherwise obsolete (perhaps the same as G4225 used with the rising slide of inquiry)\nStrong's: as adverb of place; at (by implication, to) what locality\nKJV: —where, whither.\n\nSee: G4225 \n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: ποῦ \nHebrew Equivalents: H346 אַיֵּה, H6030 עָנָה",
    4227: "Πούδης (Poúdēs | poo'-dace)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: modest; Pudes (i.e. Pudens), a Christian\nKJV: —Pudens.\n\nCognate Group: G2666 (devour), G4188 (drink), G4221 (cup), G4095 (drink), G4224 (banqueting), G4213 (drink), G4222 (give to drink)\nVariants: Πούδης",
    4228: "πούς (poús | pooce)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: a \"foot\" (figuratively or literally)\nKJV: —foot(-stool).\n\nCognate Group: G4228 (foot)\nVariants: πούς \nHebrew Equivalents: H838 אָשֻׁר, H3767 כָּרָע, H4772 מַרְגְלָה, H6119 עָקֵב, H6471 פַּעַם, H6541 פַּרְסָה, H7272 רֶגֶל",
    4229: "πρᾶγμα (prâgma | prag'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4238\nStrong's: a deed; by implication, an affair; by extension, an object (material)\nKJV: —business, matter, thing, work.\n\nSee: G4238 \n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πρᾶγμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1697 דָּבָר, H2656 חֵפֶץ, H4405 מִלָּה, H4611 מַעֲלָל, H4639 מַעֲשֶׂה, H5673 עֲבִידָה, H6640 צְבוּ",
    4230: "πραγματεία (pragmateía | prag-mat-i'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4231\nStrong's: a transaction, i.e. negotiation\nKJV: —affair.\n\nSee: G4231 \n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πραγματεία",
    4231: "πραγματεύομαι (pragmateúomai | prag-mat-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4229\nStrong's: to busy oneself with, i.e. to trade\nKJV: —occupy.\n\nSee: G4229 \n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πραγματεύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2836 חָשַׁק, H6213 עָשָׂה",
    4232: "πραιτώριον (praitṓrion | prahee-to'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: the prætorium or governor's courtroom (sometimes including the whole edifice and camp)\nKJV: —(common, judgment) hall (of judgment), palace, prætorium.\n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πραιτώριον",
    4233: "πράκτωρ (práktōr | prak'-tor)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4238\nStrong's: a practiser, i.e. (specially), an official collector\nKJV: —officer.\n\nSee: G4238 \n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πράκτωρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5066 נָגַשׁ",
    4234: "πρᾶξις (prâxis | prax'-is)\n\nDerivation: from G4238\nStrong's: practice, i.e. (concretely) an act; by extension, a function\nKJV: —deed, office, work.\n\nSee: G4238 \n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πρᾶξις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H6467 פֹּעַל",
    4235: "πρᾷος (prâios | prah'-os)\n\nDerivation: a form of G4239, used in certain parts\nStrong's: gentle, i.e. humble\nKJV: —meek.\n\nSee: G4239 \n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πρᾷος, πρᾶος G4553 \nHebrew Equivalents: H6035 עָנָו, H6041 עָנִי",
    4236: "πρᾳότης (praiótēs | prah-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G4235\nStrong's: gentleness, by implication, humility\nKJV: —meekness.\n\nSee: G4235 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: πρᾳότης, πραότης, πραϋπαθία \nHebrew Equivalents: H6037 עַנְוָה",
    4237: "πρασιά (prasiá | pras-ee-ah')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from πράσον (a leek, and so an onion-patch)\nStrong's: a garden plot, i.e. (by implication, of regular beds) a row (repeated in plural by Hebraism, to indicate an arrangement)\nKJV: —in ranks.\n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: πρασιά",
    4238: "πράσσω (prássō | pras'-so)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to \"practise\", i.e. perform repeatedly or habitually (thus differing from G4160, which properly refers to a single act); by implication, to execute, accomplish, etc.; specially, to collect (dues), fare (personally)\nKJV: —commit, deeds, do, exact, keep, require, use arts.\n\nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G4230 (affair), G1281 (gain by trading), G4238 (commit), G4234 (deed), G4229 (business), G4231 (occupy)\nVariants: πράσσω, ἀναπράσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1980 הָלַךְ, H5065 נָגַשׂ, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6466 פָּעַל",
    4239: "πραΰς (praÿs | prah-ooce')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: mild, i.e. (by implication) humble\nKJV: —meek. See also G4235 .\n\nSee: G4235 \n\nCognate Group: G4240 (meekness), G4236 (meekness), G4239 (meek)\nVariants: πραΰς, πραΰς \nHebrew Equivalents: H6035 עָנָו, H6041 עָנִי",
    4240: "πραΰτης (praÿtēs | prah-oo'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4239\nStrong's: mildness, i.e. (by implication) humility\nKJV: —meekness.\n\nSee: G4239 \n\nCognate Group: G4240 (meekness), G4236 (meekness), G4239 (meek)\nVariants: πραΰτης, πραΰτης, πρᾳΰτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H6037 עַנְוָה",
    4241: "πρέπω (prépō | prep'-o)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to tower up (be conspicuous), i.e. (by implication) to be suitable or proper (third person singular present indicative, often used impersonally, it is fit or right)\nKJV: —become, comely.\n\nCognate Group: G4240 (meekness), G4236 (meekness), G4239 (meek)\nVariants: πρέπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1747 דּוּמִיָּה, H4998 נָאָה",
    4242: "πρεσβεία (presbeía | pres-bi'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4243\nStrong's: seniority (eldership), i.e. (by implication) an embassy (concretely, ambassadors)\nKJV: —ambassage, message.\n\nSee: G4243 \n\nCognate Group: G4240 (meekness), G4236 (meekness), G4239 (meek)\nVariants: πρεσβεία",
    4243: "πρεσβεύω (presbeúō | pres-byoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4245\nStrong's: to be a senior, i.e. (by implication) act as a representative (figuratively, preacher)\nKJV: —be an ambassador.\n\nSee: G4245 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρεσβεύω",
    4244: "πρεσβυτέριον (presbytérion | pres-boo-ter'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G4245\nStrong's: the order of elders, i.e. (specially), Israelite Sanhedrin or Christian \"presbytery\"\nKJV: —(estate of) elder(-s), presbytery.\n\nSee: G4245 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρεσβυτέριον",
    4245: "πρεσβύτερος (presbýteros | pres-boo'-ter-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of πρέσβυς (elderly)\nStrong's: older; as noun, a senior; specially, an Israelite Sanhedrist (also figuratively, member of the celestial council) or Christian \"presbyter\"\nKJV: — elder(-est), old.\n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρεσβύτερος",
    4246: "πρεσβύτης (presbýtēs | pres-boo'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4245\nStrong's: an old man\nKJV: —aged (man), old man.\n\nSee: G4245 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρεσβύτης, πρεσβευτής \nHebrew Equivalents: H376 אִישׁ, H2205 זָקֵן, H3453 יָשִׁישׁ, H3887 לוּץ, H7868 שִׂיב",
    4247: "πρεσβῦτις (presbŷtis | pres-boo'-tis)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4246\nStrong's: an old woman\nKJV: —aged woman.\n\nSee: G4246 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρεσβῦτις",
    4248: "πρηνής (prēnḗs | pray-nace')\n\nDerivation: from G4253\nStrong's: leaning (falling) forward (\"prone\"), i.e. head foremost\nKJV: —headlong.\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρηνής",
    4249: "πρίζω (prízō | prid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: a strengthened form of a primary prio (to saw)\nStrong's: to saw in two\nKJV: —saw asunder.\n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρίζω, πρίω, πρίζω/πρίω",
    4250: "πρίν (prín | prin)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4253\nStrong's: prior, sooner\nKJV: —before (that), ere.\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: πρίν",
    4251: "Πρίσκα (Príska | pris'-kah)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: feminine of Priscus, ancient; Priska, a Christian woman\nKJV: —Prisca. See also G4252 .\n\nSee: G4252 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: Πρίσκα, Πρίσκα/Πρίσκιλλα (dim.)",
    4252: "Πρίσκιλλα (Prískilla | pris'-cil-lah)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G4251\nStrong's: Priscilla (i.e. little Prisca), a Christian woman\nKJV: —Priscilla.\n\nSee: G4251 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: Πρίσκιλλα, Πρίσκα, Πρίσκα/Πρίσκιλλα (dim.)",
    4253: "πρό (pró | pro)\n\nDerivation: a primary preposition\nStrong's: \"fore\", i.e. in front of, prior (figuratively, superior) to\nKJV: —above, ago, before, or ever. In the comparative, it retains the same significations.\n\nCognate Group: G4253 (above)\nVariants: πρό \nHebrew Equivalents: H6040 עֳנִי",
    4254: "προάγω (proágō | pro-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G71\nStrong's: to lead forward (magisterially); intransitively, to precede (in place or time (participle, previous))\nKJV: —bring (forth, out), go before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προάγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5066 נָגַשׁ",
    4255: "προαιρέομαι (proairéomai | pro-ahee-reh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G138\nStrong's: to choose for oneself before another thing (prefer), i.e. (by implication) to propose (intend)\nKJV: —purpose.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προαιρέομαι, προαιρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1334 בָּתַר, H2836 חָשַׁק, H3985 מָאֵן, H3988 מָאַס",
    4256: "προαιτιάομαι (proaitiáomai | pro-ahee-tee-ah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and a derivative of G156\nStrong's: to accuse already, i.e. previously charge\nKJV: —prove before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G156 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προαιτιάομαι, αἰτιάομαι",
    4257: "προακούω (proakoúō | pro-ak-oo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G191\nStrong's: to hear already,i.e. anticipate\nKJV: —hear before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G191 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προακούω",
    4258: "προαμαρτάνω (proamartánō | pro-am-ar-tan'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G264\nStrong's: to sin previously (to conversion)\nKJV: —sin already, heretofore sin.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G264 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προαμαρτάνω",
    4259: "προαύλιον (proaúlion | pro-ow'-lee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed compound of G4253 and G833\nStrong's: a forecourt, i.e. vestibule (alley-way)\nKJV: —porch.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G833 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προαύλιον",
    4260: "προβαίνω (probaínō | prob-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and the base of G939\nStrong's: to walk forward, i.e. advance (literally, or in years)\nKJV: —+ be of a great age, go farther (on), be well stricken.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προβαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1980 הָלַךְ, H3381 יָרַד",
    4261: "προβάλλω (probállō | prob-al'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G906\nStrong's: to throw forward, i.e. push to the front, germinate\nKJV: —put forward, shoot forth.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προβάλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2330 חוּד, H3384 יָרָה, H4838 מָרַק, H7971 שָׁלַח",
    4262: "προβατικός (probatikós | prob-at-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4263\nStrong's: relating to sheep, i.e. (a gate) through which they were led into Jerusalem\nKJV: —sheep (market).\n\nSee: G4263 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προβατικός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6629 צֹאן",
    4263: "πρόβατον (próbaton | prob'-at-on)\n\nDerivation: probably neuter of a presumed derivative of G4260\nStrong's: something that walks forward (a quadruped), i.e. (specially), a sheep (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —sheep(-fold).\n\nSee: G4260 \n\nCognate Group: G4263 (sheep)\nVariants: πρόβατον, προβάτιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3532 כֶּבֶשׂ, H3775 כֶּשֶׂב, H6629 צֹאן, H7353 רָחֵל, H7716 שֶׂה",
    4264: "προβιβάζω (probibázō | prob-ib-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and a reduplicated form of G971\nStrong's: to force forward, i.e. bring to the front, instigate\nKJV: —draw, before instruct.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G971 \n\nCognate Group: G4263 (sheep)\nVariants: προβιβάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3384 יָרָה, H8150 שָׁנַן",
    4265: "προβλέπω (problépō | prob-lep'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G991\nStrong's: to look out beforehand, i.e. furnish in advance\nKJV: —provide.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G991 \n\nCognate Group: G4263 (sheep)\nVariants: προβλέπω, προβλέπομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H7200 רָאָה",
    4266: "προγίνομαι (progínomai | prog-in'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1096\nStrong's: to be already, i.e. have previousy transpired\nKJV: —be past.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1096 \n\nCognate Group: G4263 (sheep)\nVariants: προγίνομαι",
    4267: "προγινώσκω (proginṓskō | prog-in-oce'-ko)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1097\nStrong's: to know beforehand, i.e. foresee\nKJV: —foreknow (ordain), know (before).\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1097 \n\nCognate Group: G1110 (acquaintance), G1097 (allow), G1106 (advice), G4268 (foreknowledge), G1922 (acknowledging), G4774 (permission), G4267 (foreknow), G1107 (certify), G1921 (acknowledge), G1108 (knowledge), G176 (that cannot be condemned), G2607 (blame)\nVariants: προγινώσκω",
    4268: "πρόγνωσις (prógnōsis | prog'-no-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4267\nStrong's: forethought\nKJV: —foreknowledge.\n\nSee: G4267 \n\nCognate Group: G1110 (acquaintance), G1097 (allow), G1106 (advice), G4268 (foreknowledge), G1922 (acknowledging), G4774 (permission), G4267 (foreknow), G1107 (certify), G1921 (acknowledge), G1108 (knowledge), G176 (that cannot be condemned), G2607 (blame)\nVariants: πρόγνωσις",
    4269: "πρόγονος (prógonos | prog'-on-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4266\nStrong's: an ancestor, (grand-)parent\nKJV: —forefather, parent.\n\nSee: G4266 \n\nCognate Group: G1110 (acquaintance), G1097 (allow), G1106 (advice), G4268 (foreknowledge), G1922 (acknowledging), G4774 (permission), G4267 (foreknow), G1107 (certify), G1921 (acknowledge), G1108 (knowledge), G176 (that cannot be condemned), G2607 (blame)\nVariants: πρόγονος",
    4270: "προγράφω (prográphō | prog-raf'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1125\nStrong's: to write previously; figuratively, to announce, prescribe\nKJV: —before ordain, evidently set forth, write (afore, aforetime).\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1125 \n\nCognate Group: G4270 (before ordain), G1121 (bill), G1124 (scripture), G1123 (written), G5261 (example), G1125 (describe)\nVariants: προγράφω",
    4271: "πρόδηλος (pródēlos | prod'-ay-los)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1212\nStrong's: plain before all men, i.e. obvious\nKJV: —evident, manifest (open) beforehand.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1212 \n\nCognate Group: G4270 (before ordain), G1121 (bill), G1124 (scripture), G1123 (written), G5261 (example), G1125 (describe)\nVariants: πρόδηλος",
    4272: "προδίδωμι (prodídōmi | prod-id'-o-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1325\nStrong's: to give before the other party has given\nKJV: —first give.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1325 \n\nCognate Group: G4270 (before ordain), G1121 (bill), G1124 (scripture), G1123 (written), G5261 (example), G1125 (describe)\nVariants: προδίδωμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5414 נָתַן",
    4273: "προδότης (prodótēs | prod-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G4272 (in the sense of giving forward into another's (the enemy's) hands)\nStrong's: a surrender\nKJV: —betrayer, traitor.\n\nSee: G4272 \n\nCognate Group: G4270 (before ordain), G1121 (bill), G1124 (scripture), G1123 (written), G5261 (example), G1125 (describe)\nVariants: προδότης",
    4274: "πρόδρομος (pródromos | prod'-rom-os)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G4390\nStrong's: a runner ahead, i.e. scout (figuratively, precursor)\nKJV: —forerunner.\n\nSee: G4390 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: πρόδρομος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1061 בִּכּוּר, H1063 בִּכּוּרָה",
    4275: "προείδω (proeídō | pro-i'-do)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1492\nStrong's: foresee\nKJV: —foresee, saw before.\n\nSee: G1492 \nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: προείδω, προοράω",
    4276: "προελπίζω (proelpízō | pro-el-pid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1679\nStrong's: to hope in advance of other confirmation\nKJV: —first trust.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1679 \n\nCognate Group: G1680 (faith), G1679 (hope), G4276 (first trust), G560 (hope for again)\nVariants: προελπίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5532 סָכַן, H7200 רָאָה",
    4277: "προέπω (proépō | pro-ep'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2036\nStrong's: to say already, to predict\nKJV: —forewarn, say (speak, tell) before. Compare G4280 .\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2036 \nSee: G4280 \n\nCognate Group: G1680 (faith), G1679 (hope), G4276 (first trust), G560 (hope for again)\nVariants: προέπω, προλέγω, προεῖπον",
    4278: "προενάρχομαι (proenárchomai | pro-en-ar'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G1728\nStrong's: to commence already\nKJV: —begin (before).\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1728 \n\nCognate Group: G1680 (faith), G1679 (hope), G4276 (first trust), G560 (hope for again)\nVariants: προενάρχομαι",
    4279: "προεπαγγέλλομαι (proepangéllomai | pro-ep-ang-ghel'-lom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4253 and G1861\nStrong's: to promise of old\nKJV: —promise before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G1861 \n\nCognate Group: G1861 (profess), G1860 (message), G4279 (promise before), G1862 (promise)\nVariants: προεπαγγέλλομαι, προεπαγγέλλω",
    4280: "προερέω (proeréō | pro-er-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2046\nStrong's: used as alternate of G4277 ; to say already, predict\nKJV: —foretell, say (speak, tell) before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2046 \nSee: G4277 \n\nCognate Group: G1861 (profess), G1860 (message), G4279 (promise before), G1862 (promise)\nVariants: προερέω, προλέγω, προεῖπον",
    4281: "προέρχομαι (proérchomai | pro-er'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2064 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to go onward, precede (in place or time)\nKJV: —go before (farther, forward), outgo, pass on.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2064 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: προέρχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5674 עָבַר",
    4282: "προετοιμάζω (proetoimázō | pro-et-oy-mad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2090\nStrong's: to fit up in advance (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —ordain before, prepare afore.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2090 \n\nCognate Group: G2092 (prepared), G2091 (preparation), G2090 (prepare), G4282 (ordain before)\nVariants: προετοιμάζω",
    4283: "προευαγγελίζομαι (proeuangelízomai | pro-yoo-ang-ghel-id'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4253 and G2097\nStrong's: to announce glad news in advance\nKJV: —preach before the gospel.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2097 \n\nCognate Group: G2099 (evangelist), G2097 (declare), G4283 (preach before the gospel), G2098 (gospel)\nVariants: προευαγγελίζομαι",
    4284: "προέχομαι (proéchomai | pro-ekh-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4253 and G2192\nStrong's: to hold oneself before others, i.e. (figuratively) to excel\nKJV: —be better.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G2099 (evangelist), G2097 (declare), G4283 (preach before the gospel), G2098 (gospel)\nVariants: προέχομαι, προέχω, προκατέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2388 חָזַק",
    4285: "προηγέομαι (proēgéomai | pro-ay-geh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2233\nStrong's: to lead the way for others, i.e. show deference\nKJV: —prefer.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2233 \n\nCognate Group: G4285 (prefer), G2233 (account), G1834 (declare), G1335 (declaration)\nVariants: προηγέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1265 בְּרֹושׁ, H6040 עֳנִי",
    4286: "πρόθεσις (próthesis | proth'-es-is)\n\nDerivation: from G4388\nStrong's: a setting forth, i.e. (figuratively) proposal (intention); specially, the show-bread (in the Temple) as exposed before God\nKJV: —purpose, shew(-bread).\n\nSee: G4388 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: πρόθεσις, πρόσθεσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4635 מַעֲרֶכֶת, H6187 עֵרֶךְ, H6440 פָּנִים",
    4287: "προθέσμιος (prothésmios | proth-es'-mee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and a derivative of G5087\nStrong's: fixed beforehand, i.e. (feminine with G2250 implied) a designated day\nKJV: —time appointed.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5087 \nSee: G2250 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προθέσμιος, προθεσμία",
    4288: "προθυμία (prothymía | proth-oo-mee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4289\nStrong's: predisposition, i.e. alacrity\nKJV: —forwardness of mind, readiness (of mind), ready (willing) mind.\n\nSee: G4289 \n\nCognate Group: G4289 (ready), G4290 (willingly), G4288 (forwardness of mind)\nVariants: προθυμία",
    4289: "πρόθυμος (próthymos | proth'-oo-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2372\nStrong's: forward in spirit, i.e. predisposed; neuter (as noun) alacrity\nKJV: —ready, willing.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2372 \n\nCognate Group: G4289 (ready), G4290 (willingly), G4288 (forwardness of mind)\nVariants: πρόθυμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2363 חוּשׁ, H5081 נָדִיב",
    4290: "προθύμως (prothýmōs | proth-oo'-moce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4289\nStrong's: with alacrity\nKJV: —willingly.\n\nSee: G4289 \n\nCognate Group: G4289 (ready), G4290 (willingly), G4288 (forwardness of mind)\nVariants: προθύμως",
    4291: "προΐστημι (proḯstēmi | pro-is'-tay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2476\nStrong's: to stand before, i.e. (in rank) to preside, or (by implication) to practise\nKJV: —maintain, be over, rule.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G547 (X straitly), G4291 (maintain)\nVariants: προΐστημι, προί̈στημι, προΐστημι",
    4292: "προκαλέομαι (prokaléomai | prok-al-eh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4253 and G2564\nStrong's: to call forth to oneself (challenge), i.e. (by implication) to irritate\nKJV: —provoke.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2564 \n\nCognate Group: G1458 (accuse), G2822 (called), G2564 (bid), G1528 (call in), G4779 (call together), G3333 (call), G2821 (calling), G1462 (crime laid against), G1941 (appeal), G4341 (call), G1577 (assembly), G4292 (provoke), G479 (bid again)\nVariants: προκαλέομαι, προκαλέω",
    4293: "προκαταγγέλλω (prokatangéllō | prok-at-ang-ghel'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2605\nStrong's: to anounce beforehand, i.e. predict, promise\nKJV: —foretell, have notice, (shew) before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2605 \n\nCognate Group: G1229 (declare), G31 (message), G1804 (shew forth), G518 (bring word), G2604 (setter forth), G4293 (foretell), G312 (declare), G2605 (declare)\nVariants: προκαταγγέλλω",
    4294: "προκαταρτίζω (prokatartízō | prok-at-ar-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2675\nStrong's: to prepare in advance\nKJV: —make up beforehand.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2675 \n\nCognate Group: G1229 (declare), G31 (message), G1804 (shew forth), G518 (bring word), G2604 (setter forth), G4293 (foretell), G312 (declare), G2605 (declare)\nVariants: προκαταρτίζω",
    4295: "πρόκειμαι (prókeimai | prok'-i-mahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2749\nStrong's: to lie before the view, i.e. (figuratively) to be present (to the mind), to stand forth (as an example or reward)\nKJV: —be first, set before (forth).\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2749 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: πρόκειμαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H6440 פָּנִים",
    4296: "προκηρύσσω (prokērýssō | prok-ay-rooce'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2784\nStrong's: to herald (i.e. proclaim) in advance\nKJV: —before (first) preach.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2784 \n\nCognate Group: G2784 (preacher), G4296 (before preach), G2783 (preacher), G2782 (preaching)\nVariants: προκηρύσσω",
    4297: "προκοπή (prokopḗ | prok-op-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4298\nStrong's: progress, i.e. advancement (subjectively or objectively)\nKJV: —furtherance, profit.\n\nSee: G4298 \n\nCognate Group: G4298 (increase), G4297 (furtherance)\nVariants: προκοπή",
    4298: "προκόπτω (prokóptō | prok-op'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2875\nStrong's: to drive forward (as if by beating), i.e. (figuratively and intransitively) to advance (in amount, to grow; in time, to be well along)\nKJV: —increase, proceed, profit, be far spent, wax.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2875 \n\nCognate Group: G4298 (increase), G4297 (furtherance)\nVariants: προκόπτω",
    4299: "πρόκριμα (prókrima | prok'-ree-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4253 and G2919\nStrong's: a prejudgment (prejudice), i.e. prepossession\nKJV: —prefer one before another.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2919 \n\nCognate Group: G4298 (increase), G4297 (furtherance)\nVariants: πρόκριμα",
    4300: "προκυρόω (prokyróō | prok-oo-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2964\nStrong's: to ratify previously\nKJV: —confirm before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2964 \n\nCognate Group: G4298 (increase), G4297 (furtherance)\nVariants: προκυρόω",
    4301: "προλαμβάνω (prolambánō | prol-am-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G2983\nStrong's: to take in advance, i.e. (literally) eat before others have an opportunity; (figuratively) to anticipate, surprise\nKJV: —come aforehand, overtake, take before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προλαμβάνω",
    4302: "προλέγω (prolégō | prol-eg'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3004\nStrong's: to say beforehand, i.e. predict, forewarn\nKJV: —foretell, tell before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προλέγω, προεῖπον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5046 נָגַד",
    4303: "προμαρτύρομαι (promartýromai | prom-ar-too'-rom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3143\nStrong's: to be a witness in advance i.e. predict\nKJV: —testify beforehand.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3143 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: προμαρτύρομαι",
    4304: "προμελετάω (promeletáō | prom-el-et-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3191\nStrong's: to premeditate\nKJV: —meditate before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3191 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: προμελετάω",
    4305: "προμεριμνάω (promerimnáō | prom-er-im-nah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3309\nStrong's: to care (anxiously) in advance\nKJV: —take thought beforehand.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3309 \n\nCognate Group: G275 (without care), G3309 (be careful), G3308 (care), G4305 (take thought beforehand)\nVariants: προμεριμνάω",
    4306: "προνοέω (pronoéō | pron-o-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3539\nStrong's: to consider in advance, i.e. look out for beforehand (actively, by way of maintenance for others; middle voice by way of circumspection for oneself)\nKJV: —provide (for).\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3539 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: προνοέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H7789 שׁוּר",
    4307: "πρόνοια (prónoia | pron'-oy-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4306\nStrong's: forethought, i.e. provident care or supply\nKJV: —providence, provision.\n\nSee: G4306 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: πρόνοια",
    4308: "προοράω (prooráō | pro-or-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3708\nStrong's: to behold in advance, i.e. (actively) to notice (another) previously, or (middle voice) to keep in (one's own) view\nKJV: —foresee, see before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3708 \n\nCognate Group: G2300 (behold), G3705 (sight), G517 (invisible), G3707 (visible), G2334 (behold), G3708 (behold), G2529 (clearly see), G4308 (foresee), G3706 (sight)\nVariants: προοράω, πρόοιδα \nHebrew Equivalents: H7737 שָׁוָה",
    4309: "προορίζω (proorízō | pro-or-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3724\nStrong's: to limit in advance, i.e. (figuratively) predetermine\nKJV: —determine before, ordain, predestinate.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3724 \n\nCognate Group: G3724 (declare), G4309 (determine before), G592 (separate), G873 (divide)\nVariants: προορίζω",
    4310: "προπάσχω (propáschō | prop-as'-kho)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3958\nStrong's: to undergo hardship previously\nKJV: —suffer before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3958 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: προπάσχω",
    4311: "προπέμπω (propémpō | prop-em'-po)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G3992\nStrong's: to send forward, i.e. escort or aid in travel\nKJV: —accompany, bring (forward) on journey (way), conduct forth.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3992 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: προπέμπω",
    4312: "προπετής (propetḗs | prop-et-ace')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4253 and G4098\nStrong's: falling forward, i.e. headlong (figuratively, precipitate)\nKJV: —heady, rash(-ly).\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G4098 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: προπετής \nHebrew Equivalents: H191 אֱוִיל, H6589 פָּשַׂק",
    4313: "προπορεύομαι (proporeúomai | prop-or-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G4198\nStrong's: to precede (as guide or herald)\nKJV: —go before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G4198 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: προπορεύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H213 אוּץ, H1980 הָלַךְ, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5674 עָבַר, H6040 עֳנִי, H6923 קָדַם, H7126 קָרַב",
    4314: "πρός (prós | pros)\n\nDerivation: a strengthened form of G4253\nStrong's: a preposition of direction; forward to, i.e. toward (with the genitive case, the side of, i.e. pertaining to; with the dative case, by the side of, i.e. near to; usually with the accusative case, the place, time, occasion, or respect, which is the destination of the relation, i.e. whither or for which it is predicated)\nKJV: —about, according to, against, among, at, because of, before, between, (where-)by, for, X at thy house, in, for intent, nigh unto, of, which pertain to, that, to (the end that), X together, to (you) -ward, unto, with(-in). In the comparative case, it denotes essentially the same applications, namely, motion towards, accession to, or nearness at.\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G4314 (about)\nVariants: πρός",
    4315: "προσάββατον (prosábbaton | pros-ab'-bat-on)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G4521\nStrong's: a fore-sabbath, i.e. the Sabbath-eve\nKJV: —day before the sabbath. Compare G3904 .\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G4521 \nSee: G3904 \n\nCognate Group: G4314 (about)\nVariants: προσάββατον \nHebrew Equivalents: H7676 שַׁבָּת",
    4316: "προσαγορεύω (prosagoreúō | pros-ag-or-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and a derivative of G58 (mean to harangue)\nStrong's: to address, i.e. salute by name\nKJV: —call.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G58 \n\nCognate Group: G4314 (about)\nVariants: προσαγορεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1875 דָּרַשׁ, H3034 יָדָה",
    4317: "προσάγω (proságō | pros-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G71\nStrong's: to lead towards, i.e. (transitively) to conduct near (summon, present), or (intransitively) to approach\nKJV: —bring, draw near.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προσάγω, προσανέχω, προσαχέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H935 בֹּוא, H3947 לָקַח, H5060 נָגַע, H5065 נָגַשׂ, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H5437 סָבַב, H5493 סוּר, H5564 סָמַךְ, H5674 עָבַר, H7126 קָרַב, H7725 שׁוּב",
    4318: "προσαγωγή (prosagōgḗ | pros-ag-ogue-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4317 (compare G72)\nStrong's: admission\nKJV: —access.\n\nSee: G4317 \nSee: G72 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προσαγωγή",
    4319: "προσαιτέω (prosaitéō | pros-ahee-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G154\nStrong's: to ask repeatedly (importune), i.e. solicit\nKJV: —beg.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G154 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προσαιτέω, προσαίτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H5927 עָלָה",
    4320: "προσαναβαίνω (prosanabaínō | pros-an-ab-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G305\nStrong's: to ascend farther, i.e. be promoted (take an upper (more honorable) seat)\nKJV: —go up.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G305 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προσαναβαίνω",
    4321: "προσαναλίσκω (prosanalískō | pros-an-al-is'-ko)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G355\nStrong's: to expend further\nKJV: —spend.\n\nSee: G355 \nSee: G4314 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προσαναλίσκω, προσαναλόω, προσαναλίσκω/προσαναλόω",
    4322: "προσαναπληρόω (prosanaplēróō | pros-an-ap-lay-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G378\nStrong's: to fill up further, i.e. furnish fully\nKJV: —supply.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G378 \n\nCognate Group: G4318 (access), G4317 (bring), G4254 (bring), G3855 (depart), G72 (manner of life)\nVariants: προσαναπληρόω",
    4323: "προσανατίθημι (prosanatíthēmi | pros-an-at-ith'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G394\nStrong's: to lay up in addition, i.e. (middle voice and figuratively) to impart or (by implication) to consult\nKJV: —in conference add, confer.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G394 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: προσανατίθημι",
    4324: "προσαπειλέω (prosapeiléō | pros-ap-i-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G546\nStrong's: to menace additionally\nKJV: —i.e. threaten further.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G546 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: προσαπειλέω, προσαπειλέομαι",
    4325: "προσδαπανάω (prosdapanáō | pros-dap-an-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G1159\nStrong's: to expend additionally\nKJV: —spend more.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G1159 \n\nCognate Group: G334 (gift), G2652 (curse), G4323 (in conference add), G2653 (curse), G332 (curse), G394 (communicate), G331 (accused)\nVariants: προσδαπανάω",
    4326: "προσδέομαι (prosdéomai | pros-deh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G1189\nStrong's: to require additionally, i.e. want further\nKJV: —need.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G1189 \n\nCognate Group: G1189 (beseech), G1162 (prayer), G4326 (need)\nVariants: προσδέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2637 חָסֵר",
    4327: "προσδέχομαι (prosdéchomai | pros-dekh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G1209\nStrong's: to admit (to intercourse, hospitality, credence, or (figuratively) endurance); by implication, to await (with confidence or patience)\nKJV: —accept, allow, look (wait) for, take.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G1209 \n\nCognate Group: G1403 (feast), G1209 (accept), G1184 (accepted), G553 (look for), G1523 (receive), G587 (acceptable), G588 (accept), G2144 (acceptable), G4327 (accept), G594 (acceptation), G1561 (looking for)\nVariants: προσδέχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H3176 יָחַל, H3947 לָקַח, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5445 סָבַל, H5452 סְבַר, H6901 קָבַל, H7519 רָצָא, H7521 רָצָה",
    4328: "προσδοκάω (prosdokáō | pros-dok-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and dokeuo (to watch)\nStrong's: to anticipate (in thought, hope or fear); by implication, to await\nKJV: —(be in) expect(-ation), look (for), when looked, tarry, wait for.\n\nSee: G4314 \n\nCognate Group: G4328 (expect), G4329 (expectation)\nVariants: προσδοκάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5445 סָבַל, H6960 קָוָה",
    4329: "προσδοκία (prosdokía | pros-dok-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4328\nStrong's: apprehension (of evil); by implication, infliction anticipated\nKJV: —expectation, looking after.\n\nSee: G4328 \n\nCognate Group: G4328 (expect), G4329 (expectation)\nVariants: προσδοκία \nHebrew Equivalents: H3349 יִקָּהָה, H7664 שֵׂבֶר",
    4330: "προσεάω (proseáō | pros-eh-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G1439\nStrong's: to permit further progress\nKJV: —suffer.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G1439 \n\nCognate Group: G4328 (expect), G4329 (expectation)\nVariants: προσεάω",
    4331: "προσεγγίζω (prosengízō | pros-eng-ghid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G1448\nStrong's: to approach near\nKJV: —come nigh.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G1448 \n\nCognate Group: G1448 (approach), G4331 (come nigh), G1451 (from)\nVariants: προσεγγίζω G4662 \nHebrew Equivalents: H5060 נָגַע, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H7126 קָרַב",
    4332: "προσεδρεύω (prosedreúō | pros-ed-ryoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4314 and the base of G1476\nStrong's: to sit near, i.e. attend as a servant\nKJV: —wait at.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G1476 \n\nCognate Group: G1448 (approach), G4331 (come nigh), G1451 (from)\nVariants: προσεδρεύω, παρεδρεύω",
    4333: "προσεργάζομαι (prosergázomai | pros-er-gad'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2038\nStrong's: to work additionally, i.e. (by implication) acquire besides\nKJV: —gain.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2038 \n\nCognate Group: G1448 (approach), G4331 (come nigh), G1451 (from)\nVariants: προσεργάζομαι",
    4334: "προσέρχομαι (prosérchomai | pros-er'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2064 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to approach, i.e. (literally) come near, visit, or (figuratively) worship, assent to\nKJV: —(as soon as he) come (unto), come thereunto, consent, draw near, go (near, to, unto).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2064 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: προσέρχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1481 גּוּר, H1980 הָלַךְ, H3259 יָעַד, H5027 נָבַט, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H5674 עָבַר, H6030 עָנָה, H6293 פָּגַע, H7126 קָרַב, H7284 רְגַשׁ, H7725 שׁוּב",
    4335: "προσευχή (proseuchḗ | pros-yoo-khay')\n\nDerivation: from G4336\nStrong's: prayer (worship); by implication, an oratory (chapel)\nKJV: —X pray earnestly, prayer.\n\nSee: G4336 \n\nCognate Group: G2172 (pray), G4335 (X pray earnestly), G4336 (pray), G2171 (prayer)\nVariants: προσευχή \nHebrew Equivalents: H6963 קֹול, H8467 תְּחִנָּה, H8605 תְּפִלָּה",
    4336: "προσεύχομαι (proseúchomai | pros-yoo'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2172\nStrong's: to pray to God, i.e. supplicate, worship\nKJV: —pray (X earnestly, for), make prayer.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2172 \n\nCognate Group: G2172 (pray), G4335 (X pray earnestly), G4336 (pray), G2171 (prayer)\nVariants: προσεύχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5307 נָפַל, H6279 עָתַר, H6419 פָּלַל, H6739 צְלָא",
    4337: "προσέχω (proséchō | pros-ekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2192\nStrong's: (figuratively) to hold the mind (3563 implied) towards, i.e. pay attention to, be cautious about, apply oneself to, adhere to\nKJV: —(give) attend(-ance, -ance at, -ance to, unto), beware, be given to, give (take) heed (to unto); have regard.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G2172 (pray), G4335 (X pray earnestly), G4336 (pray), G2171 (prayer)\nVariants: προσέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H238 אָזַן, H995 בִּין, H1692 דָּבַק, H2095 זְהַר, H2308 חָדַל, H2363 חוּשׁ, H2388 חָזַק, H2388 חָזַק, H3045 יָדַע, H3313 יָפַע, H3364 יָקַץ, H5144 נָזַר, H5186 נָטָה, H5753 עָוָה, H6437 פָּנָה, H7126 קָרַב, H7181 קָשַׁב, H7760 שׂוּם, H7896 שִׁית, H8104 שָׁמַר, H8159 שָׁעָה",
    4338: "προσηλόω (prosēlóō | pros-ay-lo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and a derivative of G2247\nStrong's: to peg to, i.e. spike fast\nKJV: —nail to.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2247 \n\nCognate Group: G2172 (pray), G4335 (X pray earnestly), G4336 (pray), G2171 (prayer)\nVariants: προσηλόω",
    4339: "προσήλυτος (prosḗlytos | pros-ay'-loo-tos)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G4334\nStrong's: an arriver from a foreign region, i.e. (specially), an acceder (convert) to Judaism (\"proselyte\")\nKJV: —proselyte.\n\nSee: G4334 \n\nCognate Group: G4339 (proselyte)\nVariants: προσήλυτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1616 גֵּר",
    4340: "πρόσκαιρος (próskairos | pros'-kahee-ros)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2540\nStrong's: for the occasion only, i.e. temporary\nKJV: —dur-(eth) for awhile, endure for a time, for a season, temporal.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2540 \n\nCognate Group: G2120 (opportunity), G2121 (convenient), G170 (lack opportunity), G4340 (dur- for awhile), G171 (out of season), G2540 (X always), G2122 (conveniently)\nVariants: πρόσκαιρος",
    4341: "προσκαλέομαι (proskaléomai | pros-kal-eh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4314 and G2564\nStrong's: to call toward oneself, i.e. summon, invite\nKJV: —call (for, to, unto).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2564 \n\nCognate Group: G1458 (accuse), G2822 (called), G2564 (bid), G1528 (call in), G4779 (call together), G3333 (call), G2821 (calling), G1462 (crime laid against), G1941 (appeal), G4341 (call), G1577 (assembly), G4292 (provoke), G479 (bid again)\nVariants: προσκαλέομαι, προσκαλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H7121 קָרָא, H7136 קָרָה",
    4342: "προσκαρτερέω (proskarteréō | pros-kar-ter-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2594\nStrong's: to be earnest towards, i.e. (to a thing) to persevere, be constantly diligent, or (in a place) to attend assiduously all the exercises, or (to a person) to adhere closely to (as a servitor)\nKJV: —attend (give self) continually (upon), continue (in, instant in, with), wait on (continually).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2594 \n\nCognate Group: G4343 (perseverance), G4342 (attend continually), G2594 (endure)\nVariants: προσκαρτερέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2388 חָזַק",
    4343: "προσκαρτέρησις (proskartérēsis | pros-kar-ter'-ay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4342\nStrong's: persistancy\nKJV: —perseverance.\n\nSee: G4342 \n\nCognate Group: G4343 (perseverance), G4342 (attend continually), G2594 (endure)\nVariants: προσκαρτέρησις",
    4344: "προσκεφάλαιον (proskephálaion | pros-kef-al'-ahee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed compound of G4314 and G2776\nStrong's: something for the head, i.e. a cushion\nKJV: —pillow.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2776 \n\nCognate Group: G4343 (perseverance), G4342 (attend continually), G2594 (endure)\nVariants: προσκεφάλαιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3704 כֶּסֶת",
    4345: "προσκληρόω (prosklēróō | pros-klay-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2820\nStrong's: to give a common lot to, i.e. (figuratively) to associate with\nKJV: —consort with.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2820 \n\nCognate Group: G3647 (perfect soundness), G2819 (heritage), G2817 (inheritance), G4789 (fellow -heir), G2820 (obtain an inheritance), G3648 (entire), G2624 (divide by lot), G2816 (be heir), G4345 (consort with), G2818 (heir)\nVariants: προσκληρόω, προσκληρόομαι",
    4346: "πρόσκλισις (prósklisis | pros'-klis-is)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4314 and G2827\nStrong's: a leaning towards, i.e. (figuratively) proclivity (favoritism)\nKJV: —partiality.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2827 \n\nCognate Group: G3647 (perfect soundness), G2819 (heritage), G2817 (inheritance), G4789 (fellow -heir), G2820 (obtain an inheritance), G3648 (entire), G2624 (divide by lot), G2816 (be heir), G4345 (consort with), G2818 (heir)\nVariants: πρόσκλισις, πρόσκλησις",
    4347: "προσκολλάω (proskolláō | pros-kol-lah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2853\nStrong's: to glue to, i.e. (figuratively) to adhere\nKJV: —cleave, join (self).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2853 \n\nCognate Group: G4347 (cleave), G2853 (cleave)\nVariants: προσκολλάω, προσκλίνω, προσκολλάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1245 בָּקַשׁ, H1692 דָּבַק, H7132 קְרָבָה",
    4348: "πρόσκομμα (próskomma | pros'-kom-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4350\nStrong's: a stub, i.e. (figuratively) occasion of apostasy\nKJV: —offence, stumbling(-block, (-stone)).\n\nSee: G4350 \n\nCognate Group: G4350 (beat upon), G677 (none), G4348 (offence), G4349 (offence)\nVariants: πρόσκομμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4170 מֹוקֵשׁ, H5063 נֶגֶף, H6983 קֹושׁ",
    4349: "προσκοπή (proskopḗ | pros-kop-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4350\nStrong's: a stumbling, i.e. (figuratively and concretely) occasion of sin\nKJV: —offence.\n\nSee: G4350 \n\nCognate Group: G4350 (beat upon), G677 (none), G4348 (offence), G4349 (offence)\nVariants: προσκοπή",
    4350: "προσκόπτω (proskóptō | pros-kop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2875\nStrong's: to strike at, i.e. surge against (as water); specially, to stub on, i.e. trip up (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —beat upon, dash, stumble (at).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2875 \n\nCognate Group: G4350 (beat upon), G677 (none), G4348 (offence), G4349 (offence)\nVariants: προσκόπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3782 כָּשַׁל, H5062 נָגַף, H7293 רַהַב",
    4351: "προσκυλίω (proskylíō | pros-koo-lee'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2947\nStrong's: to roll towards, i.e. block against\nKJV: —roll (to).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2947 \n\nCognate Group: G4350 (beat upon), G677 (none), G4348 (offence), G4349 (offence)\nVariants: προσκυλίω",
    4352: "προσκυνέω (proskynéō | pros-koo-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and a probable derivative of G2965 (meaning to kiss, like a dog licking his master's hand)\nStrong's: to fawn or crouch to, i.e. (literally or figuratively) prostrate oneself in homage (do reverence to, adore)\nKJV: —worship.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2965 \n\nCognate Group: G4352 (worship), G4353 (worshipper)\nVariants: προσκυνέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2111 זוּעַ, H3766 כָּרַע, H5401 נָשַׁק, H5456 סָגַד, H5647 עָבַד, H7812 שָׁחָה",
    4353: "προσκυνητής (proskynētḗs | pros-koo-nay-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G4352\nStrong's: an adorer\nKJV: —worshipper.\n\nSee: G4352 \n\nCognate Group: G4352 (worship), G4353 (worshipper)\nVariants: προσκυνητής",
    4354: "προσλαλέω (proslaléō | pros-lal-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2980\nStrong's: to talk to, i.e. converse with\nKJV: —speak to (with).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2980 \n\nCognate Group: G4352 (worship), G4353 (worshipper)\nVariants: προσλαλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1696 דָבַר",
    4355: "προσλαμβάνω (proslambánō | pros-lam-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G2983\nStrong's: to take to oneself, i.e. use (food), lead (aside), admit (to friendship or hospitality)\nKJV: —receive, take (unto).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσλαμβάνω, προσαναλαμβάνω, προσλαμβάνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H3947 לָקַח, H4871 מָשָׁה, H6213 עָשָׂה, H7126 קָרַב",
    4356: "πρόσληψις (próslēpsis | pros'-lape-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4355\nStrong's: admission\nKJV: —receiving.\n\nSee: G4355 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: πρόσληψις, πρόσλημψις, πρόσλημψις/πρόσληψις",
    4357: "προσμένω (prosménō | pros-men'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G3306\nStrong's: to stay further, i.e. remain in a place, with a person; figuratively, to adhere to, persevere in\nKJV: —abide still, be with, cleave unto, continue in (with).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G3306 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσμένω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2342 חוּל",
    4358: "προσορμίζω (prosormízō | pros-or-mid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and a derivative of the same as G3730 (meaning to tie (anchor) or lull)\nStrong's: to moor to, i.e. (by implication) land at\nKJV: —draw to the shore.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G3730 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσορμίζω, προσορμίζομαι",
    4359: "προσοφείλω (prosopheílō | pros-of-i'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G3784\nStrong's: to be indebted additionally\nKJV: —over besides.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G3784 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσοφείλω",
    4360: "προσοχθίζω (prosochthízō | pros-okh-thid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and a form of ὀχθέω (to be vexed with something irksome)\nStrong's: to feel indignant at\nKJV: —be grieved at.\n\nSee: G4314 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσοχθίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1350 גָּאַל, H3988 מָאַס, H6958 קֹוא, H6962 קוּט, H6973 קוּץ, H8262 שָׁקַץ, H8581 תַּעָב",
    4361: "πρόσπεινος (próspeinos | pros'-pi-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and the same as G3983\nStrong's: hungering further, i.e. intensely hungry\nKJV: —very hungry.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G3983 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: πρόσπεινος",
    4362: "προσπήγνυμι (prospḗgnymi | pros-payg'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G4078\nStrong's: to fasten to, i.e. (specially), to impale (on a cross)\nKJV: —crucify.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G4078 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσπήγνυμι",
    4363: "προσπίπτω (prospíptō | pros-pip'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G4098\nStrong's: to fall towards, i.e. (gently) prostrate oneself (in supplication or homage), or (violently) to rush upon (in storm)\nKJV: —beat upon, fall (down) at (before).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G4098 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσπίπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3318 יָצָא, H3766 כָּרַע, H5060 נָגַע, H5307 נָפַל",
    4364: "προσποιέομαι (prospoiéomai | pros-poy-eh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4314 and G4160\nStrong's: to do forward for oneself, i.e. pretend (as if about to do a thing)\nKJV: —make as though.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσποιέομαι, προσποιέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל",
    4365: "προσπορεύομαι (prosporeúomai | pros-por-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G4198\nStrong's: to journey towards, i.e. approach (not the same as G4313)\nKJV: —go before.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G4198 \nSee: G4313 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσπορεύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1481 גּוּר, H1980 הָלַךְ, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H7126 קָרַב",
    4366: "προσρήγνυμι (prosrḗgnymi | pros-rayg'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G4486\nStrong's: to tear towards, i.e. burst upon (as a tempest or flood)\nKJV: —beat vehemently against (upon).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G4486 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: προσρήγνυμι, προσρήσσω, προσρήγνυμι/προσρήσσω",
    4367: "προστάσσω (prostássō | pros-tas'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G5021\nStrong's: to arrange towards, i.e. (figuratively) enjoin\nKJV: —bid, command.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G5021 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: προστάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H1696 דָבַר, H3318 יָצָא, H3982 מַאֲמַר, H4487 מָנָה, H4662 מִפְקָד, H6680 צָוָה, H7760 שׂוּם",
    4368: "προστάτις (prostátis | pros-tat'-is)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a derivative of G4291\nStrong's: a patroness, i.e. assistant\nKJV: —succourer.\n\nSee: G4291 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: προστάτις, παραστάτις",
    4369: "προστίθημι (prostíthēmi | pros-tith'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G5087\nStrong's: to place additionally, i.e. lay beside, annex, repeat\nKJV: —add, again, give more, increase, lay unto, proceed further, speak to any more.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προστίθημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H1692 דָּבַק, H2270 חָבֵר, H3254 יָסַף, H3867 לָוָה, H5307 נָפַל, H5414 נָתַן, H5595 סָפָה, H5596 סָפַח, H6186 עָרַךְ, H7235 רָבָה, H7725 שׁוּב, H7760 שׂוּם, H7896 שִׁית, H8138 שָׁנָה",
    4370: "προστρέχω (prostréchō | pros-trekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G5143 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to run towards, i.e. hasten to meet or join\nKJV: —run (thither to, to).\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G5143 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προστρέχω, προσχαίρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7323 רוּץ",
    4371: "προσφάγιον (prosphágion | pros-fag'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of a compound of G4314 and G5315\nStrong's: something eaten in addition to bread, i.e. a relish (specially, fish; compare G3795)\nKJV: —meat.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G5315 \nSee: G3795 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προσφάγιον",
    4372: "πρόσφατος (prósphatos | pros'-fat-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and a derivative of G4969\nStrong's: previously (recently) slain (fresh), i.e. (figuratively) lately made\nKJV: —new.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G4969 \n\nCognate Group: G4373 (lately), G4372 (new)\nVariants: πρόσφατος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2114 זוּר, H2319 חָדָשׁ, H3892 לַח",
    4373: "προσφάτως (prosphátōs | pros-fat'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4372\nStrong's: recently\nKJV: —lately.\n\nSee: G4372 \n\nCognate Group: G4373 (lately), G4372 (new)\nVariants: προσφάτως \nHebrew Equivalents: H2319 חָדָשׁ",
    4374: "προσφέρω (prosphérō | pros-fer'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G5342 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to bear towards, i.e. lead to, tender (especially to God), treat\nKJV: —bring (to, unto), deal with, do, offer (unto, up), present unto, put to.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: προσφέρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H2076 זָבַח, H4672 מָצָא, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5927 עָלָה, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6999 קָטַר, H7126 קָרַב, H7311 רוּם, H7725 שׁוּב",
    4375: "προσφιλής (prosphilḗs | pros-fee-lace')\n\nDerivation: from a presumed compound of G4314 and G5368\nStrong's: friendly towards, i.e. acceptable\nKJV: —lovely.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G5368 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: προσφιλής",
    4376: "προσφορά (prosphorá | pros-for-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G4374\nStrong's: presentation; concretely, an oblation (bloodless) or sacrifice\nKJV: —offering (up).\n\nSee: G4374 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: προσφορά \nHebrew Equivalents: H4503 מִנְחָה, H6440 פָּנִים",
    4377: "προσφωνέω (prosphōnéō | pros-fo-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and G5455\nStrong's: to sound towards, i.e. address, exclaim, summon\nKJV: —call unto, speak (un-)to.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G5455 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: προσφωνέω",
    4378: "πρόσχυσις (próschysis | pros'-khoo-sis)\n\nDerivation: from a comparative of G4314 and χέω (to pour)\nStrong's: a shedding forth, i.e. affusion\nKJV: —sprinkling.\n\nSee: G4314 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: πρόσχυσις",
    4379: "προσψαύω (prospsaúō | pros-psow'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and ψαύω (to touch)\nStrong's: to impinge, i.e. lay a finger on (in order to relieve)\nKJV: —touch.\n\nSee: G4314 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: προσψαύω",
    4380: "προσωποληπτέω (prosōpolēptéō | pros-o-pol-ape-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4381\nStrong's: to favor an individual, i.e. show partiality\nKJV: —have respect to persons.\n\nSee: G4381 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: προσωποληπτέω, προσωπολημπτέω",
    4381: "προσωπολήπτης (prosōpolḗptēs | pros-o-pol-ape'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4383 and G2983\nStrong's: an accepter of a face (individual), i.e. (specially), one exhibiting partiality\nKJV: —respecter of persons.\n\nSee: G4383 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: προσωπολήπτης, προσωπολήμπτης",
    4382: "προσωποληψία (prosōpolēpsía | pros-o-pol-ape-see'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4381\nStrong's: partiality, i.e. favoritism\nKJV: —respect of persons.\n\nSee: G4381 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: προσωποληψία, προσωπολημψία",
    4383: "πρόσωπον (prósōpon | pros'-o-pon)\n\nDerivation: from G4314 and ὤψ (the visage, from G3700)\nStrong's: the front (as being towards view), i.e. the countenance, aspect, appearance, surface; by implication, presence, person\nKJV: —(outward) appearance, X before, countenance, face, fashion, (men's) person, presence.\n\nSee: G4314 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: πρόσωπον \nHebrew Equivalents: H639 אַף, H649 אַפַּיִם, H4758 מַרְאֶה, H5869 עַיִן, H5921 עַל, H6057 עָנָף, H6285 פֵּאָה, H6310 פֶּה, H6440 פָּנִים, H6440 פָּנִים, H6440 פָּנִים, H6440 פָּנִים, H6440 פָּנִים, H6441 פְּנִימָה",
    4384: "προτάσσω (protássō | prot-as'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G5021\nStrong's: to pre-arrange, i.e. prescribe\nKJV: —before appoint.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5021 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: προτάσσω G4726",
    4385: "προτείνω (proteínō | prot-i'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and τείνω (to stretch)\nStrong's: to protend, i.e. tie prostrate (for scourging)\nKJV: —bind.\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: προτείνω",
    4386: "πρότερον (próteron | prot'-er-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G4387 as adverb (with or without the article)\nStrong's: previously\nKJV: —before, (at the) first, former.\n\nSee: G4387 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: πρότερον, πρότερος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2470 חָלָה, H4327 מִין, H6440 פָּנִים, H7223 רִאשֹׁון",
    4387: "πρότερος (próteros | prot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of G4253\nStrong's: prior or previous\nKJV: —former.\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G2146 (make a fair show), G4380 (have respect to persons), G4382 (respect of persons), G678 (without respect of persons), G4383 (appearance), G4381 (respecter of persons)\nVariants: πρότερος \nHebrew Equivalents: H259 אֶחָד, H7223 רִאשֹׁון",
    4388: "προτίθεμαι (protíthemai | prot-ith'-em-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4253 and G5087\nStrong's: to place before, i.e. (for oneself) to exhibit; (to oneself) to propose (determine)\nKJV: —purpose, set forth.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προτίθεμαι, προτίθημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3254 יָסַף, H3867 לָוָה, H5414 נָתַן, H6186 עָרַךְ, H7760 שׂוּם, H7896 שִׁית",
    4389: "προτρέπομαι (protrépomai | prot-rep'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4253 and the base of G5157\nStrong's: to turn forward for oneself, i.e. encourage\nKJV: —exhort.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5157 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προτρέπομαι, προτρέπω",
    4390: "προτρέχω (protréchō | prot-rekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G5143 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to run forward, i.e. outstrip, precede\nKJV: —outrun, run before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5143 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προτρέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1750 דּוּץ, H7323 רוּץ",
    4391: "προϋπάρχω (proÿpárchō | pro-oop-ar'-kho)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G5225\nStrong's: to exist before, i.e. (adverbially) to be or do something previously\nKJV: —+ be before(-time).\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5225 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προϋπάρχω",
    4392: "πρόφασις (próphasis | prof'-as-is)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4253 and G5316\nStrong's: an outward showing, i.e. pretext\nKJV: —cloke, colour, pretence, show.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: πρόφασις \nHebrew Equivalents: H5931 עִלָּה, H5949 עֲלִילָה",
    4393: "προφέρω (prophérō | prof-er'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G5342\nStrong's: to bear forward, i.e. produce\nKJV: —bring forth.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: προφέρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4672 מָצָא",
    4394: "προφητεία (prophēteía | prof-ay-ti'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4396 (\"prophecy\")\nStrong's: prediction (scriptural or other)\nKJV: —prophecy, prophesying.\n\nSee: G4396 \n\nCognate Group: G4394 (prophecy), G4395 (prophesy), G4397 (of prophecy), G4396 (prophet), G5578 (false prophet), G4398 (prophetess)\nVariants: προφητεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2377 חָזֹון, H5016 נְבוּאָה",
    4395: "προφητεύω (prophēteúō | prof-ate-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4396\nStrong's: to foretell events, divine, speak under inspiration, exercise the prophetic office\nKJV: —prophesy.\n\nSee: G4396 \n\nCognate Group: G4394 (prophecy), G4395 (prophesy), G4397 (of prophecy), G4396 (prophet), G5578 (false prophet), G4398 (prophetess)\nVariants: προφητεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H5012 נָבָא",
    4396: "προφήτης (prophḗtēs | prof-ay'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4253 and G5346\nStrong's: a foreteller (\"prophet\"); by analogy, an inspired speaker; by extension, a poet\nKJV: —prophet.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5346 \n\nCognate Group: G4394 (prophecy), G4395 (prophesy), G4397 (of prophecy), G4396 (prophet), G5578 (false prophet), G4398 (prophetess)\nVariants: προφήτης",
    4397: "προφητικός (prophētikós | prof-ay-tik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4396\nStrong's: pertaining to a foreteller (\"prophetic\")\nKJV: —of prophecy, of the prophets.\n\nSee: G4396 \n\nCognate Group: G4394 (prophecy), G4395 (prophesy), G4397 (of prophecy), G4396 (prophet), G5578 (false prophet), G4398 (prophetess)\nVariants: προφητικός",
    4398: "προφῆτις (prophētis | prof-ay'-tis)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4396\nStrong's: a female foreteller or an inspired woman\nKJV: —prophetess.\n\nSee: G4396 \n\nCognate Group: G4394 (prophecy), G4395 (prophesy), G4397 (of prophecy), G4396 (prophet), G5578 (false prophet), G4398 (prophetess)\nVariants: προφῆτις \nHebrew Equivalents: H5031 נְבִיאָה",
    4399: "προφθάνω (prophthánō | prof-than'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G5348\nStrong's: to get an earlier start of, i.e. anticipate\nKJV: —prevent.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5348 \n\nCognate Group: G5348 (attain), G4399 (prevent)\nVariants: προφθάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6923 קָדַם, H7323 רוּץ",
    4400: "προχειρίζομαι (procheirízomai | prokh-i-rid'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4253 and a derivative of G5495\nStrong's: to handle for oneself in advance, i.e. (figuratively) to purpose\nKJV: —choose, make.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5495 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: προχειρίζομαι, προχειρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3947 לָקַח, H7971 שָׁלַח",
    4401: "προχειροτονέω (procheirotonéō | prokh-i-rot-on-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G5500\nStrong's: to elect in advance\nKJV: —choose before.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5500 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: προχειροτονέω",
    4402: "Πρόχορος (Próchoros | prokh'-or-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4253 and G5525\nStrong's: before the dance; Prochorus, a Christian\nKJV: —Prochorus.\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G5525 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: Πρόχορος",
    4403: "πρύμνα (prýmna | proom'-nah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of πρυμνύς (hindmost)\nStrong's: the stern of a ship\nKJV: —hinder part, stern.\n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: πρύμνα",
    4404: "πρωΐ (prōḯ | pro-ee')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4253\nStrong's: at dawn; by implication, the day-break watch\nKJV: —early (in the morning), (in the) morning.\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: πρωΐ, πρωί̈, πρωΐ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1242 בֹּקֶר, H5060 נָגַע, H5399 נֶשֶׁף, H5704 עַד, H7837 שַׁחַר",
    4405: "πρωΐα (prōḯa | pro-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a derivative of G4404 as noun\nStrong's: day-dawn\nKJV: —early, morning.\n\nSee: G4404 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: πρωΐα, πρωί̈α, πρωΐα",
    4406: "πρώϊμος (prṓïmos | pro'-ee-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G4404\nStrong's: dawning, i.e. (by analogy) autumnal (showering, the first of the rainy season)\nKJV: —early.\n\nSee: G4404 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: πρώϊμος, πρόϊμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1063 בִּכּוּרָה, H3137 יֹוקִים, H3384 יָרָה, H7525 רִצְיָא, H7836 שָׁחַר",
    4407: "πρωϊνός (prōïnós | pro-ee-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G4404\nStrong's: pertaining to the dawn, i.e. matutinal\nKJV: —morning.\n\nSee: G4404 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: πρωϊνός, προϊνός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1242 בֹּקֶר",
    4408: "πρῶρα (prōra | pro'-ra)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a presumed derivative of G4253 as noun\nStrong's: the prow, i.e. forward part of a vessel\nKJV: —forepart(-ship).\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G4400 (choose)\nVariants: πρῶρα, πρῷρα, πρῴρα",
    4409: "πρωτεύω (prōteúō | prote-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4413\nStrong's: to be first (in rank or influence)\nKJV: —have the preeminence.\n\nSee: G4413 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρωτεύω",
    4410: "πρωτοκαθεδρία (prōtokathedría | pro-tok-ath-ed-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4413 and G2515\nStrong's: a sitting first (in the front row), i.e. preeminence in council\nKJV: —chief (highest, uppermost) seat.\n\nSee: G4413 \nSee: G2515 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρωτοκαθεδρία",
    4411: "πρωτοκλισία (prōtoklisía | pro-tok-lis-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4413 and G2828\nStrong's: a reclining first (in the place of honor) at the dinner-bed, i.e. preeminence at meals\nKJV: —chief (highest, uppermost) room.\n\nSee: G4413 \nSee: G2828 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρωτοκλισία",
    4412: "πρῶτον (prōton | pro'-ton)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G4413 as adverb (with or without G3588)\nStrong's: firstly (in time, place, order, or importance)\nKJV: —before, at the beginning, chiefly (at, at the) first (of all).\n\nSee: G4413 \nSee: G3588 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρῶτον, πρώτως, πρῶτος",
    4413: "πρῶτος (prōtos | pro'-tos)\n\nDerivation: contracted superlative of G4253\nStrong's: foremost (in time, place, order or importance)\nKJV: —before, beginning, best, chief(-est), first (of all), former.\n\nSee: G4253 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρῶτος",
    4414: "πρωτοστάτης (prōtostátēs | pro-tos-tat'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G4413 and G2476\nStrong's: one standing first in the ranks, i.e. a captain (champion)\nKJV: —ringleader.\n\nSee: G4413 \nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρωτοστάτης",
    4415: "πρωτοτόκια (prōtotókia | pro-tot-ok'-ee-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4416\nStrong's: primogeniture (as a privilege)\nKJV: —birthright.\n\nSee: G4416 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρωτοτόκια \nHebrew Equivalents: H1062 בְּכֹורָה",
    4416: "πρωτότοκος (prōtótokos | pro-tot-ok'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4413 and the alternate of G5088\nStrong's: first-born (usually as noun, literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —firstbegotten(-born).\n\nSee: G4413 \nSee: G5088 \n\nCognate Group: G4416 (firstbegotten), G4409 (have the preeminence), G4414 (ringleader), G4412 (before), G4410 (chief), G4415 (birthright), G4411 (chief), G4413 (before)\nVariants: πρωτότοκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1060 בְּכֹור, H6363 פֶּטֶר, H7218 רֹאשׁ, H7223 רִאשֹׁון",
    4417: "πταίω (ptaíō | ptah'-yo)\n\nDerivation: a form of G4098\nStrong's: to trip, i.e. (figuratively) to err, sin, fail (of salvation)\nKJV: —fall, offend, stumble.\n\nSee: G4098 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πταίω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3369 יָקֹשׁ, H5062 נָגַף",
    4418: "πτέρνα (ptérna | pter'-nah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: the heel (figuratively)\nKJV: —heel.\n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτέρνα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6119 עָקֵב",
    4419: "πτερύγιον (pterýgion | pter-oog'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G4420\nStrong's: a winglet, i.e. (figuratively) extremity (top corner)\nKJV: —pinnacle.\n\nSee: G4420 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτερύγιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3671 כָּנָף, H5579 סְנַפִּיר, H7098 קָצָה",
    4420: "πτέρυξ (ptéryx | pter'-oox)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4072 (meaning a feather)\nStrong's: a wing\nKJV: —wing.\n\nSee: G4072 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτέρυξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3671 כָּנָף, H5676 עֵבֶר",
    4421: "πτηνόν (ptēnón | ptay-non')\n\nDerivation: contraction for G4071\nStrong's: a bird\nKJV: —bird.\n\nSee: G4071 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτηνόν, πτηνός",
    4422: "πτοέω (ptoéō | pto-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to the alternate of G4098 (through the idea of causing to fall) or to G4072 (through that of causing to fly away)\nStrong's: to scare\nKJV: —frighten.\n\nSee: G4098 \nSee: G4072 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτοέω, πτοέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2730 חָרֵד, H2865 חָתַת, H3372 יָרֵא, H4549 מָסַס, H5074 נָדַד, H6206 עָרַץ, H6342 פָּחַד, H7264 רָגַז",
    4423: "πτόησις (ptóēsis | pto'-ay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4422\nStrong's: alarm\nKJV: —amazement.\n\nSee: G4422 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτόησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H6343 פַּחַד",
    4424: "Πτολεμαΐς (Ptolemaḯs | ptol-em-ah-is')\n\nDerivation: from Πτολεμαῖος (Ptolemy, after whom it was named)\nStrong's: Ptolemais, a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Ptolemais.\n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: Πτολεμαΐς, Πτολεμαί̈ς, Πτολεμαΐς",
    4425: "πτύον (ptýon | ptoo'-on)\n\nDerivation: from G4429\nStrong's: a winnowing-fork (as scattering like spittle)\nKJV: —fan.\n\nSee: G4429 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτύον",
    4426: "πτύρω (ptýrō | ptoo'-ro)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G4429 (and thus akin to G4422)\nStrong's: to frighten\nKJV: —terrify.\n\nSee: G4429 \nSee: G4422 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτύρω, πτύρομαι",
    4427: "πτύσμα (ptýsma | ptoos'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4429\nStrong's: saliva\nKJV: —spittle.\n\nSee: G4429 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτύσμα",
    4428: "πτύσσω (ptýssō | ptoos'-so)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to πετάννυμι (to spread\nStrong's: and thus apparently allied to G4072 through the idea of expansion, and to 4429 through that of flattening; compare G3961); to fold, i.e. furl a scroll\nKJV: —close.\n\nSee: G4072 \nSee: G3961 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτύσσω",
    4429: "πτύω (ptýō | ptoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (compare G4428)\nStrong's: to spit\nKJV: —spit.\n\nSee: G4428 \n\nCognate Group: G4417 (fall)\nVariants: πτύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3417 יָרָק",
    4430: "πτῶμα (ptōma | pto'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G4098\nStrong's: a ruin, i.e. (specially), lifeless body (corpse, carrion)\nKJV: —dead body, carcase, corpse.\n\nSee: G4098 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: πτῶμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H343 אֵיד, H1472 גְּוִיָּה, H3783 כִּשָּׁלֹון, H4658 מַפֶּלֶת, H6297 פֶּגֶר, H6365 פִּיד, H6556 פֶּרֶץ, H7667 שֶׁבֶר, H7701 שֹׁד",
    4431: "πτῶσις (ptōsis | pto'-sis)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G4098\nStrong's: a crash, i.e. downfall (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —fall.\n\nSee: G4098 \n\nCognate Group: G3895 (fall away), G4098 (fail), G4045 (fall among), G1601 (be cast), G3900 (fall), G2667 (fall), G4431 (fall), G4430 (dead body)\nVariants: πτῶσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4654 מַפָּלָה, H4658 מַפֶּלֶת, H5063 נֶגֶף, H6297 פֶּגֶר, H8653 תַּרְעֵלָה",
    4432: "πτωχεία (ptōcheía | pto-khi'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4433\nStrong's: beggary, i.e. indigence (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —poverty.\n\nSee: G4433 \n\nCognate Group: G4433 (become poor), G4434 (beggar), G4432 (poverty), G4431 (fall)\nVariants: πτωχεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4544 מִסְכֵּנֻת, H6040 עֳנִי",
    4433: "πτωχεύω (ptōcheúō | pto-khyoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4434\nStrong's: to be a beggar, i.e. (by implication) to become indigent (figuratively)\nKJV: —become poor.\n\nSee: G4434 \n\nCognate Group: G4433 (become poor), G4434 (beggar), G4432 (poverty), G4431 (fall)\nVariants: πτωχεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1809 דָּלַל, H3423 יָרַשׁ, H7326 רוּשׁ",
    4434: "πτωχός (ptōchós | pto-khos')\n\nDerivation: from πτώσσω (to crouch)\nStrong's: akin to G4422 and the alternate of G4098); a beggar (as cringing), i.e. pauper (strictly denoting absolute or public mendicancy, although also used in a qualified or relative sense; whereas G3993 properly means only straitened circumstances in private), literally (often as noun) or figuratively (distressed)\nKJV: —beggar(-ly), poor.\n\nSee: G4422 \nSee: G4098 \nSee: G3993 \n\nCognate Group: G4433 (become poor), G4434 (beggar), G4432 (poverty), G4431 (fall)\nVariants: πτωχός \nHebrew Equivalents: H34 אֶבְיֹון, H779 אָרַר, H1800 דַּל, H2489 חֵלְכָא, H6041 עָנִי, H6996 קָטָן, H7326 רוּשׁ",
    4435: "πυγμή (pygmḗ | poog-may')\n\nDerivation: from a primary πύξ (the fist as a weapon)\nStrong's: the clenched hand, i.e. (only in dative case as adverb) with the fist (hard scrubbing)\nKJV: —oft.\n\nCognate Group: G4438 (fight), G4435 (oft)\nVariants: πυγμή \nHebrew Equivalents: H106 אֶגְרֹף",
    4436: "Πύθων (Pýthōn | poo'-thone)\n\nDerivation: from Πυθώ (the name of the region where Delphi, the seat of the famous oracle, was located)\nStrong's: a Python, i.e. (by analogy, with the supposed diviner there) inspiration (soothsaying)\nKJV: — divination.\n\nCognate Group: G4436 (divination)\nVariants: Πύθων, πύθων",
    4437: "πυκνός (pyknós | pook-nos')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4635\nStrong's: clasped (thick), i.e. (figuratively) frequent; neuter plural (as adverb) frequently\nKJV: —often(-er).\n\nSee: G4635 \n\nCognate Group: G4436 (divination)\nVariants: πυκνός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2793 חֹרֶשׁ",
    4438: "πυκτέω (pyktéō | pook-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the same as G4435\nStrong's: to box (with the fist), i.e. contend (as a boxer) at the games (figuratively)\nKJV: —fight.\n\nSee: G4435 \n\nCognate Group: G4438 (fight), G4435 (oft)\nVariants: πυκτέω, πυκτεύω",
    4439: "πύλη (pýlē | poo'-lay)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a gate, i.e. the leaf or wing of a folding entrance (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —gate.\n\nCognate Group: G4440 (gate), G4439 (gate)\nVariants: πύλη \nHebrew Equivalents: H1817 דֶּלֶת, H5592 סַף, H6607 פֶּתַח, H8179 שַׁעַר",
    4440: "πυλών (pylṓn | poo-lone')\n\nDerivation: from G4439\nStrong's: a gate-way, door-way of a building or city; by implication, a portal or vestibule\nKJV: —gate, porch.\n\nSee: G4439 \n\nCognate Group: G4440 (gate), G4439 (gate)\nVariants: πυλών \nHebrew Equivalents: H5592 סַף, H6607 פֶּתַח, H8179 שַׁעַר",
    4441: "πυνθάνομαι (pynthánomai | poon-than'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice prolonged from a primary πύθω (which occurs only as an alternate in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to question, i.e. ascertain by inquiry (as a matter of information merely; and thus differing from G2065, which properly means a request as a favor; and from G154, which is strictly a demand for something due; as well as from G2212, which implies a search for something hidden; and from G1189, which involves the idea of urgent need); by implication, to learn (by casual intelligence)\nKJV: —ask, demand, enquire, understand.\n\nSee: G2065 \nSee: G154 \nSee: G2212 \nSee: G1189 \n\nCognate Group: G4440 (gate), G4439 (gate)\nVariants: πυνθάνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1875 דָּרַשׁ",
    4442: "πῦρ (pŷr | poor)\n\nDerivation: a primary word\nStrong's: \"fire\" (literally or figuratively, specially, lightning)\nKJV: —fiery, fire.\n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πῦρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H217 אוּר, H784 אֵשׁ, H1200 בְּעֵרָה, H3827 לַבָּה, H3852 לֶהָבָה, H5135 נוּר, H7565 רֶשֶׁף, H8316 שְׂרֵפָה",
    4443: "πυρά (pyrá | poo-rah')\n\nDerivation: from G4442\nStrong's: a fire (concretely)\nKJV: —fire.\n\nSee: G4442 \n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πυρά",
    4444: "πύργος (pýrgos | poor'-gos)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word (\"burgh\")\nStrong's: a tower or castle\nKJV: —tower.\n\nCognate Group: G4444 (tower)\nVariants: πύργος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4026 מִגְדָּל, H4694 מְצוּרָה, H4864 מַשְׂאֵת",
    4445: "πυρέσσω (pyréssō | poo-res'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G4443\nStrong's: to be on fire, i.e. (specially), to have a fever\nKJV: —be sick of a fever.\n\nSee: G4443 \n\nCognate Group: G4445 (be sick of a fever), G546 (threaten), G4446 (fever)\nVariants: πυρέσσω",
    4446: "πυρετός (pyretós | poo-ret-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4445\nStrong's: inflamed, i.e. (by implication) feverish (as noun, fever)\nKJV: —fever.\n\nSee: G4445 \n\nCognate Group: G4445 (be sick of a fever), G546 (threaten), G4446 (fever)\nVariants: πυρετός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6920 קַדַּחַת",
    4447: "πύρινος (pýrinos | poo'-ree-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G4443\nStrong's: fiery, i.e. (by implication) flaming\nKJV: —of fire.\n\nSee: G4443 \n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πύρινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H784 אֵשׁ",
    4448: "πυρόω (pyróō | poo-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4442\nStrong's: to kindle, i.e. (passively) to be ignited, glow (literally), be refined (by implication), or (figuratively) to be inflamed (with anger, grief, lust)\nKJV: —burn, fiery, be on fire, try.\n\nSee: G4442 \n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πυρόω, πυρόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H119 אָדַם, H977 בָּחַר, H6884 צָרַף, H8443 תֹּועָפָה",
    4449: "πυῤῥάζω (pyrrházō | poor-hrad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4450\nStrong's: to redden (intransitively)\nKJV: —be red.\n\nSee: G4450 \n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πυῤῥάζω, πυρράζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H125 אֲדַמְדָּם",
    4450: "πυῤῥός (pyrrhós | poor-hros')\n\nDerivation: from G4442\nStrong's: fire-like, i.e. (specially), flame- colored\nKJV: —red.\n\nSee: G4442 \n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πυῤῥός, πυρρός, πυρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H123 אֱדֹם",
    4451: "πύρωσις (pýrōsis | poo'-ro-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4448\nStrong's: ignition, i.e. (specially), smelting (figuratively, conflagration, calamity as a test)\nKJV: —burning, trial.\n\nSee: G4448 \n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πύρωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H3564 כּוּר, H7711 שְׁדֵפָה",
    4452: "-πω (-pō | po)\n\nDerivation: another form of the base of G4458\nStrong's: an enclitic particle of indefiniteness; yet, even; used only in the comparative. See G3369, G3380, G3764, G3768, G4455 .\n\nSee: G4458 \nSee: G3369 \nSee: G3380 \nSee: G3764 \nSee: G3768 \nSee: G4455 \n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: -πω, πώς G4803",
    4453: "πωλέω (pōléō | po-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: probably ultimately from πέλομαι (to be busy, to trade)\nStrong's: to barter (as a pedlar), i.e. to sell\nKJV: —sell, whatever is sold.\n\nCognate Group: G4449 (be red), G4451 (burning), G4448 (burn), G4443 (fire), G4447 (of fire), G4450 (red), G4442 (fiery)\nVariants: πωλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4376 מָכַר",
    4454: "πῶλος (pōlos | po'-los)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a \"foal\" or \"filly\", i.e. (specially), a young ass\nKJV: —colt.\n\nCognate Group: G4454 (colt)\nVariants: πῶλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1121 בֵּן, H3280 יַעֲלָה, H5895 עַיִר",
    4455: "πώποτε (pṓpote | po'-pot-e)\n\nDerivation: from G4452 and G4218\nStrong's: at any time, i.e. (with negative particle) at no time\nKJV: —at any time, + never (…to any man), + yet, never man.\n\nSee: G4452 \nSee: G4218 \n\nCognate Group: G4454 (colt)\nVariants: πώποτε",
    4456: "πωρόω (pōróō | po-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: apparently from πῶρος (a kind of stone)\nStrong's: to petrify, i.e. (figuratively) to indurate (render stupid or callous)\nKJV: — blind, harden.\n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: πωρόω, πηρόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3543 כָּהָה",
    4457: "πώρωσις (pṓrōsis | po'-ro-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4456\nStrong's: stupidity or callousness\nKJV: —blindness, hardness.\n\nSee: G4456 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: πώρωσις, πήρωσις",
    4458: "-πώς (-pṓs | poce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from the base of G4225\nStrong's: an enclitic particle of indefiniteness of manner; somehow or anyhow; used only in composition\nKJV: —haply, by any (some) means, perhaps. See G1513, G3381 . Compare G4459 .\n\nSee: G4225 \nSee: G4459 \nSee: G1513 \nSee: G3381 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: -πώς, πώς, ποταπῶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H7665 שָׁבַר",
    4459: "πῶς (pōs | poce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from the base of G4226\nStrong's: an interrogative particle of manner; in what way? (sometimes the question is indirect, how?); also as exclamation, how much!\nKJV: —how, after (by) what manner (means), that. (Occasionally unexpressed in English).\n\nSee: G4226 \n\nCognate Group: G4457 (blindness), G3975 (wax gross), G4456 (blind)\nVariants: πῶς",
    4460: "Ῥαάβ (Rhaáb | hrah-ab')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (G7343)\nStrong's: Raab (i.e. Rachab), a Canaanitess\nKJV: —Rahab. See also H4477 .\n\nSee: G7343 \nSee: H4477\n\nCognate Group: G4460 (Rahab)\nVariants: Ῥαάβ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7342 רָחָב, H7343 רָחָב",
    4461: "ῥαββί (rhabbí | hrab-bee')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7227 with pronominal suffix)\nStrong's: my master, i.e Rabbi, as an official title of honor\nKJV: —Master, Rabbi.\nSee: H7227\n\nCognate Group: G4462 (Lord), G4461 (Master)\nVariants: ῥαββί \nHebrew Equivalents: H7227 רַב",
    4462: "ῥαββονί (rhabboní | hrab-bon-ee'), or ῥαββουνί (rhabbouní | hrab-boo-nee')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin\nStrong's: corresponding to G4461 \nKJV: —Lord, Rabboni.\n\nSee: G4461 \n\nCognate Group: G4462 (Lord), G4461 (Master)\nVariants: ῥαββονί, ῥαββουνί, ῥαββωνί, ραββουνι",
    4463: "ῥαβδίζω (rhabdízō | hrab-did'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4464\nStrong's: to strike with a stick, i.e. bastinado\nKJV: —beat (with rods).\n\nSee: G4464 \n\nCognate Group: G4463 (beat), G4464 (rod), G4465 (serjeant)\nVariants: ῥαβδίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2251 חָבַט",
    4464: "ῥάβδος (rhábdos | hrab'-dos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4474\nStrong's: a stick or wand (as a cudgel, a cane or a baton of royalty)\nKJV: —rod, sceptre, staff.\n\nSee: G4474 \n\nCognate Group: G4463 (beat), G4464 (rod), G4465 (serjeant)\nVariants: ῥάβδος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2415 חֹטֵר, H4294 מַטֶּה, H4731 מַקֵּל, H4938 מִשְׁעֵנָה, H6086 עֵץ, H7626 שֵׁבֶט, H8275 שַׁרְבִיט",
    4465: "ῥαβδοῦχος (rhabdoûchos | hrab-doo'-khos)\n\nDerivation: from G4464 and G2192\nStrong's: a rod- (the Latin fasces) holder, i.e. a Roman lictor (constable or executioner)\nKJV: —serjeant.\n\nSee: G4464 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G4463 (beat), G4464 (rod), G4465 (serjeant)\nVariants: ῥαβδοῦχος",
    4466: "Ῥαγαῦ (Rhagaû | hrag-ow')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7466)\nStrong's: Ragau (i.e. Reu), a patriarch\nKJV: —Ragau.\nSee: H7466\n\nCognate Group: G4463 (beat), G4464 (rod), G4465 (serjeant)\nVariants: Ῥαγαῦ, Ῥαγαύ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7466 רְעוּ",
    4467: "ῥᾳδιούργημα (rhaidioúrgēma | hrad-ee-oorg'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a comparative of ῥᾴδιος (easy, i.e. reckless) and G2041\nStrong's: easy-going behavior, i.e. (by extension) a crime\nKJV: —lewdness.\n\nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G4468 (mischief), G4467 (lewdness)\nVariants: ῥᾳδιούργημα, ῥάδιούργημα",
    4468: "ῥᾳδιουργία (rhaidiourgía | hrad-ee-oorg-ee'-a)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4467\nStrong's: recklessness, i.e. (by extension) malignity\nKJV: —mischief.\n\nSee: G4467 \n\nCognate Group: G4468 (mischief), G4467 (lewdness)\nVariants: ῥᾳδιουργία, ῥάδιουργία",
    4469: "ῥακά (rhaká | rhak-ah')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (compare H7386)\nStrong's: O empty one, i.e. thou worthless (as a term of utter vilification)\nKJV: —Raca.\nSee: H7386\n\nCognate Group: G4469 (Raca)\nVariants: ῥακά, ῥαχά \nHebrew Equivalents: H7386 רֵיק",
    4470: "ῥάκος (rhákos | hrak'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4486\nStrong's: a \"rag,\" i.e. piece of cloth\nKJV: —cloth.\n\nSee: G4486 \n\nCognate Group: G4469 (Raca)\nVariants: ῥάκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H899 בֶּגֶד, H5499 סְחָבָה",
    4471: "Ῥαμᾶ (Rhamâ | hram-ah')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7414)\nStrong's: Rama (i.e. Ramah), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Rama.\nSee: H7414\n\nCognate Group: G4469 (Raca)\nVariants: Ῥαμᾶ, Ῥαμά \nHebrew Equivalents: H7413 רָמָה, H7414 רָמָה",
    4472: "ῥαντίζω (rhantízō | hran-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of ῥαίνω (to sprinkle)\nStrong's: to render besprinkled, i.e. asperse (ceremonially or figuratively)\nKJV: — sprinkle.\n\nCognate Group: G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)\nVariants: ῥαντίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2398 חָטָא, H5137 נָזָה",
    4473: "ῥαντισμός (rhantismós | hran-tis-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G4472\nStrong's: aspersion (ceremonially or figuratively)\nKJV: —sprinkling.\n\nSee: G4472 \n\nCognate Group: G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)\nVariants: ῥαντισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5079 נִדָּה",
    4474: "ῥαπίζω (rhapízō | hrap-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of a primary ῥέπω (to let fall, \"rap\")\nStrong's: to slap\nKJV: —smite (with the palm of the hand). Compare G5180 .\n\nSee: G5180 \n\nCognate Group: G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)\nVariants: ῥαπίζω",
    4475: "ῥάπισμα (rhápisma | hrap'-is-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4474\nStrong's: a slap\nKJV: —(+ strike with the) palm of the hand, smite with the hand.\n\nSee: G4474 \n\nCognate Group: G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)\nVariants: ῥάπισμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4803 מָרַט",
    4476: "ῥαφίς (rhaphís | hraf-ece')\n\nDerivation: from a primary ῥάπτω (to sew\nStrong's: perhaps rather akin to the base of G4474 through the idea of puncturing); a needle\nKJV: — needle.\n\nSee: G4474 \n\nCognate Group: G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)\nVariants: ῥαφίς, βελόνη",
    4477: "Ῥαχάβ (Rhacháb | hrakh-ab')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4460\nStrong's: Rachab, a Canaanitess\nKJV: —Rachab.\n\nSee: G4460 \n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: Ῥαχάβ",
    4478: "Ῥαχήλ (Rhachḗl | hrakh-ale')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7354)\nStrong's: Rachel, the wife of Jacob\nKJV: —Rachel.\nSee: H7354\n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: Ῥαχήλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7354 רָחֵל",
    4479: "Ῥεβέκκα (Rhebékka | hreb-bek'-kah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7259)\nStrong's: Rebecca (i.e. Ribkah), the wife of Isaac\nKJV: —Rebecca.\nSee: H7259\n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: Ῥεβέκκα \nHebrew Equivalents: H7259 רִבְקָה",
    4480: "ῥέδα (rhéda | hred'-ah)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a rheda, i.e. four-wheeled carriage (wagon for riding)\nKJV: —chariot.\n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: ῥέδα, ῥέδη",
    4481: "Ῥεμφάν (Rhemphán | hrem-fan')\n\nDerivation: by incorrect transliteration for a word of Hebrew origin (H3594)\nStrong's: Remphan (i.e. Kijun), an Egyptian idol\nKJV: —Remphan.\nSee: H3594\n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: Ῥεμφάν, Ῥαιφάν, Ῥεφάν, Ῥομφά \nHebrew Equivalents: H3594 כִּיּוּן",
    4482: "ῥέω (rhéō | hreh'-o)\n\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: for some tenses of which a prolonged form ῥεύω (rheúō | hryoo'-o)\n is used to flow (\"run\"; as water)\nKJV: —flow.\n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: ῥέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1980 הָלַךְ, H2100 זוּב, H4743 מָקַק, H5107 נוּב, H5140 נָזַל, H7491 רָעַף",
    4483: "ῥέω (rhéō | hreh'-o), for certain tenses of which a prolonged form ἐρέω (eréō | er-eh'-o)\n\n is used; and both as alternate for G2036 \n\nDerivation: perhaps akin (or identical) with G4482 (through the idea of pouring forth)\nStrong's: to utter, i.e. speak or say\nKJV: —command, make, say, speak (of). Compare G3004 .\n\nSee: G2036 \nSee: G4482 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: ῥέω, λέγω",
    4484: "Ῥήγιον (Rhḗgion | hrayg'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: Rhegium, a place in Italy\nKJV: —Rhegium.\n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: Ῥήγιον",
    4485: "ῥῆγμα (rhēgma | hrayg'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4486\nStrong's: something torn, i.e. a fragment (by implication and abstractly, a fall)\nKJV: —ruin.\n\nSee: G4486 \n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: ῥῆγμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1233 בְּקִיעַ, H7168 קֶרַע",
    4486: "ῥήγνυμι (rhḗgnymi | hrayg'-noo-mee) or ῥήσσω (rhḗssō | hrace'-so)\n\nDerivation: both prolonged forms of ῥήκω (which appears only in certain forms, and is itself probably a strengthened form of ἄγνυμι (see in G2608)) to \"break,\" \"wreck\" or \"crack\", i.e. (especially) to sunder (by separation of the parts\nStrong's: G2608 being its intensive (with the preposition in composition), and G2352 a shattering to minute fragments; but not a reduction to the constituent particles, like G3089) or disrupt, lacerate; by implication, to convulse (with spasms); figuratively, to give vent to joyful emotions\nKJV: —break (forth), burst, rend, tear.\n\nSee: G2608 \nSee: G2608 \nSee: G2352 \nSee: G3089 \n\nCognate Group: G4477 (Rachab)\nVariants: ῥήγνυμι, ῥάσσω, ῥήσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1234 בָּקַע, H2040 הָרַס, H4998 נָאָה, H5423 נָתַק, H6476 פָּצַח, H7121 קָרָא",
    4487: "ῥῆμα (rhēma | hray'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4483\nStrong's: an utterance (individually, collectively or specially),; by implication, a matter or topic (especially of narration, command or dispute); with a negative naught whatever\nKJV: —+ evil, + nothing, saying, word.\n\nSee: G4483 \n\nCognate Group: G4487 (+ evil)\nVariants: ῥῆμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H561 אֵמֶר, H1681 דִּבָּה, H1697 דָּבָר, H4405 מִלָּה, H4687 מִצְוָה, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H6310 פֶּה, H6600 פִּתְגָּם",
    4488: "Ῥησά (Rhēsá | hray-sah')\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin (apparently for H7509)\nStrong's: Resa (i.e. Rephajah), an Israelite\nKJV: —Rhesa.\nSee: H7509\n\nCognate Group: G4487 (+ evil)\nVariants: Ῥησά \nHebrew Equivalents: H7509 רְפָיָה",
    4489: "ῥήτωρ (rhḗtōr | hray'-tore)\n\nDerivation: from G4483\nStrong's: a speaker, i.e. (by implication) a forensic advocate\nKJV: —orator.\n\nSee: G4483 \n\nCognate Group: G4487 (+ evil)\nVariants: ῥήτωρ",
    4490: "ῥητῶς (rhētōs | hray-toce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a derivative of G4483\nStrong's: out-spokenly, i.e. distinctly\nKJV: —expressly.\n\nSee: G4483 \n\nCognate Group: G4487 (+ evil)\nVariants: ῥητῶς",
    4491: "ῥίζα (rhíza | hrid'-zah)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a \"root\" (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —root.\n\nCognate Group: G4492 (root), G4491 (root)\nVariants: ῥίζα \nHebrew Equivalents: H3 אֵב, H1503 גֶּזַע, H3653 כֵּן, H4351 מְכוּרָה, H8327 שָׁרַשׁ, H8327 שָׁרַשׁ, H8330 שֹׁרֶשׁ",
    4492: "ῥιζόω (rhizóō | hrid-zo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4491\nStrong's: to root (figuratively, become stable)\nKJV: —root.\n\nSee: G4491 \n\nCognate Group: G4492 (root), G4491 (root)\nVariants: ῥιζόω, ῥιζόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H8327 שָׁרַשׁ",
    4493: "ῥιπή (rhipḗ | hree-pay')\n\nDerivation: from G4496\nStrong's: a jerk (of the eye, i.e. (by analogy) an instant)\nKJV: —twinkling.\n\nSee: G4496 \n\nCognate Group: G4492 (root), G4491 (root)\nVariants: ῥιπή, ῥοπή \nHebrew Equivalents: H6425 פֶּלֶס, H7834 שַׁחַק",
    4494: "ῥιπίζω (rhipízō | hrip-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4496 (meaning a fan or bellows)\nStrong's: to breeze up, i.e. (by analogy) to agitate (into waves)\nKJV: —toss.\n\nSee: G4496 \n\nCognate Group: G4492 (root), G4491 (root)\nVariants: ῥιπίζω, ῥιπίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5376 נְשָׂא",
    4495: "ῥιπτέω (rhiptéō | hrip-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4496\nStrong's: to toss up\nKJV: —cast off.\n\nSee: G4496 \n\nCognate Group: G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)\nVariants: ῥιπτέω",
    4496: "ῥίπτω (rhíptō | hrip'-to)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (perhaps rather akin to the base of G4474, through the idea of sudden motion)\nStrong's: to fling (properly, with a quick toss, thus differing from G906, which denotes a deliberate hurl; and from τείνω (see in G1614), which indicates an extended projection); by qualification, to deposit (as if a load); by extension, to disperse\nKJV: —cast (down, out), scatter abroad, throw.\n\nSee: G4474 \nSee: G906 \nSee: G1614 \n\nCognate Group: G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)\nVariants: ῥίπτω, ῥίπτω/ῥιπτέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7993 שָׁלַךְ",
    4497: "Ῥοβοάμ (Rhoboám | hrob-o-am')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7346)\nStrong's: Roboam (i.e. Rechobam), an Israelite\nKJV: —Roboam.\nSee: H7346\n\nCognate Group: G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)\nVariants: Ῥοβοάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7346 רְחַבְעָם",
    4498: "Ῥόδη (Rhódē | hrod'-ay)\n\nDerivation: probably for rhode (a rose)\nStrong's: Rode, a servant girl\nKJV: —Rhoda.\n\nCognate Group: G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)\nVariants: Ῥόδη",
    4499: "Ῥόδος (Rhódos | hrod'-os)\n\nDerivation: probably from ῥόδον (a rose)\nStrong's: Rhodus, an island of the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Rhodes.\n\nCognate Group: G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)\nVariants: Ῥόδος",
    4500: "ῥοιζηδόν (rhoizēdón | hroyd-zay-don')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a derivative of ῥοῖζος (a whir)\nStrong's: whizzingly, i.e. with a crash\nKJV: —with a great noise.\n\nCognate Group: G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)\nVariants: ῥοιζηδόν",
    4501: "ῥομφαία (rhomphaía | hrom-fah'-yah)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: a sabre, i.e. a long and broad cutlass (any weapon of the kind, literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —sword.\n\nCognate Group: G4501 (sword)\nVariants: ῥομφαία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2595 חֲנִית, H2719 חֶרֶב, H3979 מַאֲכֶלֶת",
    4502: "Ῥουβήν (Rhoubḗn | hroo-bane')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7205)\nStrong's: Ruben (i.e. Reuben), an Israelite\nKJV: —Reuben.\nSee: H7205\n\nCognate Group: G4501 (sword)\nVariants: Ῥουβήν, Ῥουβίμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7205 רְאוּבֵן",
    4503: "Ῥούθ (Rhoúth | hrooth)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7327)\nStrong's: Ruth, a Moabitess\nKJV: —Ruth.\nSee: H7327\n\nCognate Group: G4503 (Ruth)\nVariants: Ῥούθ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7327 רוּת",
    4504: "Ῥοῦφος (Rhoûphos | hroo'-fos)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: red; Rufus, a Christian\nKJV: —Rufus.\n\nCognate Group: G4503 (Ruth)\nVariants: Ῥοῦφος",
    4505: "ῥύμη (rhýmē | hroo'-may)\n\nDerivation: prolongation from G4506 in its original sense\nStrong's: an alley or avenue (as crowded)\nKJV: —lane, street.\n\nSee: G4506 \n\nCognate Group: G4503 (Ruth)\nVariants: ῥύμη \nHebrew Equivalents: H7339 רְחֹב",
    4506: "ῥύομαι (rhýomai | rhoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of an obsolete verb, akin to G4482 (through the idea of a current\nStrong's: compare G4511); to rush or draw (for oneself), i.e. rescue\nKJV: —deliver(-er).\n\nSee: G4482 \nSee: G4511 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1350 גָּאַל, H2502 חָלַץ, H3467 יָשַׁע, H4422 מָלַט, H5337 נָצַל, H5341 נָצַר, H6299 פָּדָה, H6403 פָּלַט, H6475 פָּצָה, H7804 שְׁזַב",
    4507: "ῥυπαρία (rhyparía | hroo-par-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4508\nStrong's: dirtiness (morally)\nKJV: —turpitude.\n\nSee: G4508 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥυπαρία",
    4508: "ῥυπαρός (rhyparós | rhoo-par-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4509\nStrong's: dirty, i.e. (relatively) cheap or shabby; morally, wicked\nKJV: —vile.\n\nSee: G4509 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥυπαρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6674 צֹוא",
    4509: "ῥύπος (rhýpos | hroo'-pos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: dirt, i.e. (morally) depravity\nKJV: —filth.\n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥύπος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2931 טָמֵא, H6675 צֹואָה, H7845 שַׁחַת",
    4510: "ῥυπόω (rhypóō | rhoo-po'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4509\nStrong's: to soil, i.e. (intransitively) to become dirty (morally)\nKJV: —be filthy.\n\nSee: G4509 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥυπόω, ῥυπαίνω, ῥυπαρεύω, ῥυπαρεύομαι",
    4511: "ῥύσις (rhýsis | hroo'-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4506 in the sense of its congener G4482\nStrong's: a flux (of blood)\nKJV: —issue.\n\nSee: G4506 \nSee: G4482 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥύσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H2100 זוּב, H2101 זֹוב, H4726 מָקֹור, H7137 קָרֶה, H8585 תְּעָלָה",
    4512: "ῥυτίς (rhytís | hroo-tece')\n\nDerivation: from G4506\nStrong's: a fold (as drawing together), i.e. a wrinkle (especially on the face)\nKJV: —wrinkle.\n\nSee: G4506 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥυτίς",
    4513: "Ῥωμαϊκός (Rhōmaïkós | rho-mah-ee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G4514\nStrong's: Romaic, i.e. Latin\nKJV: —Latin.\n\nSee: G4514 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: Ῥωμαϊκός G4869, G4870",
    4514: "Ῥωμαῖος (Rhōmaîos | hro-mah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G4516\nStrong's: Romæan, i.e. Roman (as noun)\nKJV: —Roman, of Rome.\n\nSee: G4516 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: Ῥωμαῖος",
    4515: "Ῥωμαϊστί (Rhōmaïstí | hro-mah-is-tee')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a presumed derivative of G4516\nStrong's: Romaistically, i.e. in the Latin language\nKJV: —Latin.\n\nSee: G4516 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: Ῥωμαϊστί",
    4516: "Ῥώμη (Rhṓmē | hro'-may)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4517\nStrong's: strength; Roma, the capital of Italy\nKJV: —Rome.\n\nSee: G4517 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: Ῥώμη",
    4517: "ῥώννυμι (rhṓnnymi | hrone'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: prolongation from ῥώομαι (to dart\nStrong's: probably akin to G4506); to strengthen, i.e. (impersonal passive) have health (as a parting exclamation, good-bye)\nKJV: —farewell.\n\nSee: G4506 \n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: ῥώννυμι, ῥώννυμαι",
    4518: "σαβαχθάνι (sabachtháni | sab-akh-than-ee')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee or (H7662 with pronominal suffix)\nStrong's: thou hast left me; sabachthani (i.e. shebakthani), a cry of distress\nKJV: —sabachthani.\nSee: H7662\n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: σαβαχθάνι, ζαφθάνι, σαβαχθανι, σαβαχθανί \nHebrew Equivalents: H7662 שְׁבַק",
    4519: "σαβαώθ (sabaṓth | sab-ah-owth')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6635 in feminine plural)\nStrong's: armies; sabaoth (i.e. tsebaoth), a military epithet of God\nKJV: —sabaoth.\nSee: H6635\n\nCognate Group: G4506 (deliver)\nVariants: σαβαώθ, Σαβαώθ \nHebrew Equivalents: H6635 צָבָא",
    4520: "σαββατισμός (sabbatismós | sab-bat-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4521\nStrong's: a \"sabbatism\", i.e. (figuratively) the repose of Christianity (as a type of heaven)\nKJV: —rest.\n\nSee: G4521 \n\nCognate Group: G4521 (sabbath), G4520 (rest)\nVariants: σαββατισμός",
    4521: "σάββατον (sábbaton | sab'-bat-on)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7676)\nStrong's: the Sabbath (i.e. Shabbath), or day of weekly repose from secular avocations (also the observance or institution itself); by extension, a se'nnight, i.e. the interval between two Sabbaths; likewise the plural in all the above applications\nKJV: —sabbath (day), week.\nSee: H7676\n\nCognate Group: G4521 (sabbath), G4520 (rest)\nVariants: σάββατον \nHebrew Equivalents: H7676 שַׁבָּת, H7677 שַׁבָּתֹון",
    4522: "σαγήνη (sagḗnē | sag-ay'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of σάττω (to equip) meaning furniture, especially a pack-saddle (which in the East is merely a bag of netted rope)\nStrong's: a \"seine\" for fishing\nKJV: —net.\n\nCognate Group: G4521 (sabbath), G4520 (rest)\nVariants: σαγήνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2764 חֵרֶם, H4365 מִכְמֶרֶת",
    4523: "Σαδδουκαῖος (Saddoukaîos | sad-doo-kah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: probably from G4524\nStrong's: a Sadducæan (i.e. Tsadokian), or follower of a certain heretical Israelite\nKJV: —Sadducee.\n\nSee: G4524 \n\nCognate Group: G4523 (Sadducee)\nVariants: Σαδδουκαῖος",
    4524: "Σαδώκ (Sadṓk | sad-oke')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6659)\nStrong's: Sadoc (i.e. Tsadok), an Israelite\nKJV: —Sadoc.\nSee: H6659\n\nCognate Group: G4523 (Sadducee)\nVariants: Σαδώκ \nHebrew Equivalents: H6659 צָדֹוק",
    4525: "σαίνω (saínō | sah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: akin to G4579\nStrong's: to wag (as a dog its tail fawningly), i.e. (generally) to shake (figuratively, disturb)\nKJV: —move.\n\nSee: G4579 \n\nCognate Group: G4525 (move)\nVariants: σαίνω, σιαίνομαι, σαίνομαι",
    4526: "σάκκος (sákkos | sak'-kos)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8242)\nStrong's: \"sack\"-cloth, i.e. mohair (the material or garments made of it, worn as a sign of grief)\nKJV: — sackcloth.\nSee: H8242\n\nCognate Group: G4526 (sackcloth)\nVariants: σάκκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8242 שַׂק",
    4527: "Σαλά (Salá | sal-ah')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7974)\nStrong's: Sala (i.e. Shelach), a patriarch \nKJV: —Sala.\nSee: H7974\n\nCognate Group: G4526 (sackcloth)\nVariants: Σαλά \nHebrew Equivalents: H7971 שָׁלַח, H7974 שֶׁלַח",
    4528: "Σαλαθιήλ (Salathiḗl | sal-ath-ee-ale')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7597)\nStrong's: Salathiel (i.e. Shealtiel), an Israelite\nKJV: —Salathiel.\nSee: H7597\n\nCognate Group: G4526 (sackcloth)\nVariants: Σαλαθιήλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7597 שְׁאַלְתִּיאֵל",
    4529: "Σαλαμίς (Salamís | sal-am-ece')\n\nDerivation: probably from G4535 (from the surge on the shore)\nStrong's: Salamis, a place in Cyprus\nKJV: —Salamis.\n\nSee: G4535 \n\nCognate Group: G4526 (sackcloth)\nVariants: Σαλαμίς",
    4530: "Σαλείμ (Saleím | sal-ime')\n\nDerivation: probably from the same as G4531\nStrong's: Salim, a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Salim.\n\nSee: G4531 \n\nCognate Group: G4526 (sackcloth)\nVariants: Σαλείμ, Σαλίμ",
    4531: "σαλεύω (saleúō | sal-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4535\nStrong's: to waver, i.e. agitate, rock, topple or (by implication) destroy; figuratively, to disturb, incite\nKJV: —move, shake (together), which can(-not) be shaken, stir up.\n\nSee: G4535 \n\nCognate Group: G4535 (wave), G4531 (move)\nVariants: σαλεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1607 גָּעַשׁ, H2111 זוּעַ, H2151 זָלַל, H2342 חוּל, H2648 חָפַז, H3921 לֶכֶד, H4127 מוּג, H4128 מוּד, H4191 מוּת, H4549 מָסַס, H4571 מָעַד, H5110 נוּד, H5120 נוּט, H5128 נוּעַ, H5186 נָטָה, H5493 סוּר, H6030 עָנָה, H6426 פָּלַץ, H6813 צָעַן, H7264 רָגַז, H7369 רָחֵק, H7477 רָעַל, H7481 רָעַם",
    4532: "Σαλήμ (Salḗm | sal-ame')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8004)\nStrong's: Salem (i.e. Shalem), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Salem.\nSee: H8004\n\nCognate Group: G4535 (wave), G4531 (move)\nVariants: Σαλήμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8004 שָׁלֵם",
    4533: "Σαλμών (Salmṓn | sal-mone')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8012)\nStrong's: Salmon, an Israelite\nKJV: —Salmon.\nSee: H8012\n\nCognate Group: G4535 (wave), G4531 (move)\nVariants: Σαλμών \nHebrew Equivalents: H8012 שַׂלְמֹון",
    4534: "Σαλμώνη (Salmṓnē | sal-mo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: perhaps of similar origin to G4529\nStrong's: Salmone, a place in Crete\nKJV: —Salmone.\n\nSee: G4529 \n\nCognate Group: G4535 (wave), G4531 (move)\nVariants: Σαλμώνη",
    4535: "σάλος (sálos | sal'-os)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G4525\nStrong's: a vibration, i.e. (specially), billow\nKJV: —wave.\n\nSee: G4525 \n\nCognate Group: G4535 (wave), G4531 (move)\nVariants: σάλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2197 זַעַף, H4132 מֹוט, H5206 נִידָה, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5591 סַעַר",
    4536: "σάλπιγξ (sálpinx | sal'-pinx)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G4535 (through the idea of quavering or reverberation)\nStrong's: a trumpet\nKJV: —trump(-et).\n\nSee: G4535 \n\nCognate Group: G4537 (sound), G4536 (trump), G4538 (trumpeter)\nVariants: σάλπιγξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H2689 חֲצֹצְרָה, H3104 יֹובֵל, H7161 קֶרֶן, H7782 שֹׁופָר, H8619 תָּקֹועַ, H8643 תְּרוּעָה",
    4537: "σαλπίζω (salpízō | sal-pid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4536\nStrong's: to trumpet, i.e. sound a blast (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —(which are yet to) sound (a trumpet).\n\nSee: G4536 \n\nCognate Group: G4537 (sound), G4536 (trump), G4538 (trumpeter)\nVariants: σαλπίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2690 חָצַר, H7321 רוּעַ, H8628 תָּקַע",
    4538: "σαλπιστής (salpistḗs | sal-pis-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G4537\nStrong's: a trumpeter\nKJV: —trumpeter.\n\nSee: G4537 \n\nCognate Group: G4537 (sound), G4536 (trump), G4538 (trumpeter)\nVariants: σαλπιστής",
    4539: "Σαλώμη (Salṓmē | sal-o'-may)\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin (feminine from H7965)\nStrong's: Salome (i.e. Shelomah), an Israelitess\nKJV: —Salome.\nSee: H7965\n\nCognate Group: G4537 (sound), G4536 (trump), G4538 (trumpeter)\nVariants: Σαλώμη \nHebrew Equivalents: H7965 שָׁלֹום",
    4540: "Σαμάρεια (Samáreia | sam-ar'-i-ah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8111)\nStrong's: Samaria (i.e. Shomeron), a city and region of Palestine\nKJV: —Samaria.\nSee: H8111\n\nCognate Group: G4542 (of Samaria), G4541 (Samaritan), G4540 (Samaria)\nVariants: Σαμάρεια, Σαμαρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H8111 שֹׁמְרֹון",
    4541: "Σαμαρείτης (Samareítēs | sam-ar-i'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4540\nStrong's: a Samarite, i.e. inhabitant of Samaria\nKJV: —Samaritan.\n\nSee: G4540 \n\nCognate Group: G4542 (of Samaria), G4541 (Samaritan), G4540 (Samaria)\nVariants: Σαμαρείτης, Σαμαρίτης",
    4542: "Σαμαρεῖτις (Samareîtis | sam-ar-i'-tis)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4541\nStrong's: a Samaritess, i.e. woman of Samaria\nKJV: —of Samaria.\n\nSee: G4541 \n\nCognate Group: G4542 (of Samaria), G4541 (Samaritan), G4540 (Samaria)\nVariants: Σαμαρεῖτις, Σαμαρῖτις",
    4543: "Σαμοθρᾴκη (Samothráikē | sam-oth-rak'-ay)\n\nDerivation: from G4544 and Θτρᾴκη (Thrace)\nStrong's: Samo-thrace (Samos of Thrace), an island in the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Samothracia.\n\nSee: G4544 \n\nCognate Group: G4542 (of Samaria), G4541 (Samaritan), G4540 (Samaria)\nVariants: Σαμοθρᾴκη",
    4544: "Σάμος (Sámos | sam'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: Samus, an island of the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Samos.\n\nCognate Group: G4542 (of Samaria), G4541 (Samaritan), G4540 (Samaria)\nVariants: Σάμος",
    4545: "Σαμουήλ (Samouḗl | sam-oo-ale')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8050)\nStrong's: Samuel (i.e. Shemuel), an Israelite\nKJV: —Samuel.\nSee: H8050\n\nCognate Group: G4542 (of Samaria), G4541 (Samaritan), G4540 (Samaria)\nVariants: Σαμουήλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H8050 שְׁמוּאֵל",
    4546: "Σαμψών (Sampsṓn | samp-sone')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8123)\nStrong's: Sampson (i.e. Shimshon), an Israelite\nKJV: —Samson.\nSee: H8123\n\nCognate Group: G4542 (of Samaria), G4541 (Samaritan), G4540 (Samaria)\nVariants: Σαμψών \nHebrew Equivalents: H8123 שִׁמְשֹׁון",
    4547: "σανδάλιον (sandálion | san-dal'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of σάνδαλον \nStrong's: (a \"sandal\"; of uncertain origin); a slipper or sole-pad\nKJV: —sandal.\n\nCognate Group: G4547 (sandal)\nVariants: σανδάλιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5274 נָעַל",
    4548: "σανίς (sanís | san-ece')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a plank\nKJV: —board.\n\nCognate Group: G4547 (sandal)\nVariants: σανίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H1817 דֶּלֶת, H3871 לוּחַ",
    4549: "Σαούλ (Saoúl | sah-ool')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7586)\nStrong's: Saul (i.e. Shaul), the Jewish name of Paul\nKJV: —Saul. Compare G4569 .\n\nSee: G4569 \nSee: H7586\n\nCognate Group: G4547 (sandal)\nVariants: Σαούλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7586 שָׁאוּל",
    4550: "σαπρός (saprós | sap-ros')\n\nDerivation: from G4595\nStrong's: rotten, i.e. worthless (literally or morally)\nKJV: —bad, corrupt. Compare G4190 .\n\nSee: G4595 \nSee: G4190 \n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σαπρός",
    4551: "Σαπφείρη (Sappheírē | sap-fi'-ray)\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4552\nStrong's: Sapphire, an Israelitess\nKJV: —Sapphira.\n\nSee: G4552 \n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: Σαπφείρη, Σάπφιρα",
    4552: "σάπφειρος (sáppheiros | sap'-fi-ros)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5601)\nStrong's: a \"sapphire\" or lapis-lazuli gem\nKJV: —sapphire.\nSee: H5601\n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σάπφειρος, σάπφιρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5601 סַפִּיר",
    4553: "σαργάνη (sargánē | sar-gan'-ay)\n\nDerivation: apparently of Hebrew origin (H8276)\nStrong's: a basket (as interwoven or wicker-work\nKJV: —basket.\nSee: H8276\n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σαργάνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H8276 שָׂרַג",
    4554: "Σάρδεις (Sárdeis | sar'-dice)\n\nDerivation: plural of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Sardis, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Sardis.\n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: Σάρδεις",
    4555: "σάρδινος (sárdinos | sar'-dee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4556\nStrong's: sardine (G3037 being implied), i.e. a gem, so called\nKJV: —sardine.\n\nSee: G4556 \nSee: G3037 \n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σάρδινος G4916",
    4556: "σάρδιος (sárdios | sar'-dee-os)\n\nDerivation: properly, an adjective from an uncertain base\nStrong's: sardian (3037 being implied), i.e. (as noun) the gem so called\nKJV: —sardius.\n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σάρδιος, σάρδιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H124 אֹדֶם, H7718 שֹׁהַם",
    4557: "σαρδόνυξ (sardónyx | sar-don'-oox)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4556 and ὄνυξ \nStrong's: (the nail of a finger; hence the \"onyx\" stone); a \"sardonyx\", i.e. the gem so called\nKJV: — sardonyx.\n\nSee: G4556 \n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σαρδόνυξ",
    4558: "Σάρεπτα (Sárepta | sar'-ep-tah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6886)\nStrong's: Sarepta (i.e. Tsarephath), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Sarepta.\nSee: H6886\n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: Σάρεπτα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6886 צָרְפַת",
    4559: "σαρκικός (sarkikós | sar-kee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G4561\nStrong's: pertaining to flesh, i.e. (by extension) bodily, temporal, or (by implication) animal, unregenerate\nKJV: —carnal, fleshly.\n\nSee: G4561 \n\nCognate Group: G4560 (fleshly), G4559 (carnal), G4561 (carnal)\nVariants: σαρκικός",
    4560: "σάρκινος (sárkinos | sar'-kee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G4561\nStrong's: similar to flesh, i.e. (by analogy) soft\nKJV: —fleshly.\n\nSee: G4561 \n\nCognate Group: G4560 (fleshly), G4559 (carnal), G4561 (carnal)\nVariants: σάρκινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1320 בָּשָׂר",
    4561: "σάρξ (sárx | sarx)\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G4563\nStrong's: flesh (as stripped of the skin), i.e. (strictly) the meat of an animal (as food), or (by extension) the body (as opposed to the soul (or spirit), or as the symbol of what is external, or as the means of kindred), or (by implication) human nature (with its frailties (physically or morally) and passions), or (specially), a human being (as such)\nKJV: —carnal(-ly, + -ly minded), flesh(-ly).\n\nSee: G4563 \n\nCognate Group: G4560 (fleshly), G4559 (carnal), G4561 (carnal)\nVariants: σάρξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1320 בָּשָׂר, H3894 לָחוּם, H7607 שְׁאֵר",
    4562: "Σαρούχ (Saroúch | sar-ooch')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8286)\nStrong's: Saruch (i.e. Serug), a patriarch\nKJV: —Saruch.\nSee: H8286\n\nCognate Group: G4560 (fleshly), G4559 (carnal), G4561 (carnal)\nVariants: Σαρούχ, Σερούκ, Σερούχ \nHebrew Equivalents: H8286 שְׂרוּג",
    4563: "σαρόω (saróō | sar-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of σαίρω (to brush off\nStrong's: akin to G4951); meaning a broom; to sweep\nKJV: —sweep.\n\nSee: G4951 \n\nCognate Group: G4560 (fleshly), G4559 (carnal), G4561 (carnal)\nVariants: σαρόω",
    4564: "Σάῤῥα (Sárrha | sar'-hrah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8283)\nStrong's: Sarra (i.e. Sarah), the wife of Abraham\nKJV: —Sara, Sarah.\nSee: H8283\n\nCognate Group: G4560 (fleshly), G4559 (carnal), G4561 (carnal)\nVariants: Σάῤῥα, Σάρρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H8283 שָׂרָה",
    4565: "Σάρων (Sárōn | sar'-one)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8289)\nStrong's: Saron (i.e. Sharon), a district of Palestine\nKJV: —Saron.\nSee: H8289\n\nCognate Group: G4560 (fleshly), G4559 (carnal), G4561 (carnal)\nVariants: Σάρων, Ἀσσάρων, Σαρών \nHebrew Equivalents: H8289 שָׁרֹון",
    4566: "Σατᾶν (Satân | sat-an')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7854)\nStrong's: Satan, i.e. the devil\nKJV: —Satan. Compare G4567 .\n\nSee: G4567 \nSee: H7854\n\nCognate Group: G4567 (Satan), G4566 (Satan)\nVariants: Σατᾶν, Σατάν \nHebrew Equivalents: H7854 שָׂטָן",
    4567: "Σατανᾶς (Satanâs | sat-an-as')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin corresponding to H4566 (with the definite affix)\nStrong's: the accuser, i.e. the devil\nKJV: —Satan.\nSee: H4566\n\nCognate Group: G4567 (Satan), G4566 (Satan)\nVariants: Σατανᾶς, σατανᾶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H7854 שָׂטָן",
    4568: "σάτον (sáton | sat'-on)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5429)\nStrong's: a certain measure for things dry\nKJV: —measure.\nSee: H5429\n\nCognate Group: G4567 (Satan), G4566 (Satan)\nVariants: σάτον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5429 סְאָה",
    4569: "Σαῦλος (Saûlos | sow'-los)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin, the same as G4549\nStrong's: Saulus (i.e. Shaul), the Jewish name of Paul\nKJV: —Saul.\n\nSee: G4549 \n\nCognate Group: G4567 (Satan), G4566 (Satan)\nVariants: Σαῦλος",
    4570: "σβέννυμι (sbénnymi | sben'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of an apparently primary verb\nStrong's: to extinguish (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —go out, quench.\n\nCognate Group: G4570 (go out)\nVariants: σβέννυμι, ζβέννυμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1846 דָּעַךְ, H3554 כָּוָה, H5218 נָכֵא",
    4571: "σέ (sé | seh)\n\nDerivation: accusative case singular of G4771\nStrong's: thee\nKJV: —thee, thou, X thy house.\n\nSee: G4771 \n\nCognate Group: G4570 (go out)\nVariants: σέ, σύ",
    4572: "σεαυτοῦ (seautoû | seh-ow-too')\n\n\nDerivation: genitive case from G4571 \n, respectively\nStrong's: of (with, to) thyself\nKJV: —thee, thine own self, (thou) thy(-self).\n\nSee: G4571 \nSee: G846 \n\nCognate Group: G4570 (go out)",
    4573: "σεβάζομαι (sebázomai | seb-ad'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from a derivative of G4576\nStrong's: to venerate, i.e. adore\nKJV: —worship.\n\nSee: G4576 \n\nCognate Group: G4573 (worship), G763 (ungodly), G2152 (devout), G4574 (devotion), G4576 (devout), G2150 (godliness), G764 (commit ungodly), G765 (ungodly), G2153 (godly), G4575 (Augustus), G2151 (show piety)\nVariants: σεβάζομαι",
    4574: "σέβασμα (sébasma | seb'-as-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4573\nStrong's: something adored, i.e. an object of worship (god, altar, etc)\nKJV: —devotion, that is worshipped.\n\nSee: G4573 \n\nCognate Group: G4573 (worship), G763 (ungodly), G2152 (devout), G4574 (devotion), G4576 (devout), G2150 (godliness), G764 (commit ungodly), G765 (ungodly), G2153 (godly), G4575 (Augustus), G2151 (show piety)\nVariants: σέβασμα",
    4575: "σεβαστός (sebastós | seb-as-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G4573\nStrong's: venerable (august), i.e. (as noun) a title of the Roman Emperor, or (as adjective) imperial\nKJV: —Augustus(-').\n\nSee: G4573 \n\nCognate Group: G4573 (worship), G763 (ungodly), G2152 (devout), G4574 (devotion), G4576 (devout), G2150 (godliness), G764 (commit ungodly), G765 (ungodly), G2153 (godly), G4575 (Augustus), G2151 (show piety)\nVariants: σεβαστός, Σεβαστός",
    4576: "σέβομαι (sébomai | seb'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of an apparently primary verb\nStrong's: to revere, i.e. adore\nKJV: —devout, religious, worship.\n\nCognate Group: G4573 (worship), G763 (ungodly), G2152 (devout), G4574 (devotion), G4576 (devout), G2150 (godliness), G764 (commit ungodly), G765 (ungodly), G2153 (godly), G4575 (Augustus), G2151 (show piety)\nVariants: σέβομαι, σέβω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3372 יָרֵא, H5650 עֶבֶד",
    4577: "σειρά (seirá | si-rah')\n\nDerivation: probably from G4951 through its congener εἴρω (to fasten\nStrong's: akin to G138); a chain (as binding or drawing)\nKJV: —chain.\n\nSee: G4951 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G4573 (worship), G763 (ungodly), G2152 (devout), G4574 (devotion), G4576 (devout), G2150 (godliness), G764 (commit ungodly), G765 (ungodly), G2153 (godly), G4575 (Augustus), G2151 (show piety)\nVariants: σειρά, σιρός, σειρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2256 חֶבֶל, H4253 מַחְלָפָה",
    4578: "σεισμός (seismós | sice-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G4579\nStrong's: a commotion, i.e. (of the air) a gale, (of the ground) an earthquake\nKJV: —earthquake, tempest.\n\nSee: G4579 \n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: σεισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5591 סַעַר, H7494 רַעַשׁ",
    4579: "σείω (seíō | si'-o)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to rock (vibrate, properly, sideways or to and fro), i.e. (generally) to agitate (in any direction; cause to tremble); figuratively, to throw into a tremor (of fear or concern)\nKJV: —move, quake, shake.\n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: σείω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5123 נוּם, H5128 נוּעַ, H5590 סָעַר, H6813 צָעַן, H7264 רָגַז, H7493 רָעַשׁ, H8442 תֹּועָה",
    4580: "Σεκοῦνδος (Sekoûndos | sek-oon'-dos)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: \"second\"; Secundus, a Christian\nKJV: —Secundus.\n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: Σεκοῦνδος",
    4581: "Σελεύκεια (Seleúkeia | sel-yook'-i-ah)\n\nDerivation: from Σέλευκος (Seleucus, a Syrian king)\nStrong's: Seleuceia, a place in Syria\nKJV: —Seleucia.\n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: Σελεύκεια",
    4582: "σελήνη (selḗnē | sel-ay'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from σέλας (brilliancy\nStrong's: probably akin to the alternate of G138, through the idea of attractiveness); the moon\nKJV: —moon.\n\nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: σελήνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H3394 יָרֵחַ, H3842 לְבָנָה",
    4583: "σεληνιάζομαι (selēniázomai | sel-ay-nee-ad'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice or passive from a presumed derivative of G4582\nStrong's: to be moon-struck, i.e. crazy\nKJV: —be a lunatic.\n\nSee: G4582 \n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: σεληνιάζομαι",
    4584: "Σεμεΐ (Semeḯ | sem-eh-ee')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8096)\nStrong's: Semei (i.e. Shimi), an Israelite\nKJV: —Semei.\nSee: H8096\n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: Σεμεΐ, Σεμεί̈, Σεμεί̈ν, Σεμεΐν, Σεμεῖν, Σεμεΐ \nHebrew Equivalents: H8096 שִׁמְעִי",
    4585: "σεμίδαλις (semídalis | sem-id'-al-is)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: fine wheaten flour\nKJV: —fine flour.\n\nCognate Group: G4579 (move), G4578 (earthquake)\nVariants: σεμίδαλις \nHebrew Equivalents: H5560 סֹלֶת, H7058 קֶמַח",
    4586: "σεμνός (semnós | sem-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G4576\nStrong's: venerable, i.e. honorable\nKJV: —grave, honest.\n\nSee: G4576 \n\nCognate Group: G4586 (grave), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σεμνός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5057 נָגִיד, H5278 נֹעַם",
    4587: "σεμνότης (semnótēs | sem-not'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G4586\nStrong's: venerableness, i.e. probity\nKJV: —gravity, honesty.\n\nSee: G4586 \n\nCognate Group: G4586 (grave), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σεμνότης",
    4588: "Σέργιος (Sérgios | serg'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: Sergius, a Roman\nKJV: —Sergius.\n\nCognate Group: G4586 (grave), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: Σέργιος",
    4589: "Σήθ (Sḗth | sayth)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8352)\nStrong's: Seth (i.e. Sheth), a patriarch\nKJV: —Seth.\nSee: H8352\n\nCognate Group: G4586 (grave), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: Σήθ \nHebrew Equivalents: H8352 שֵׁת",
    4590: "Σήμ (Sḗm | same)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8035)\nStrong's: Sem (i.e. Shem), a patriarch\nKJV: — Sem.\nSee: H8035\n\nCognate Group: G4586 (grave), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: Σήμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H8034 שֵׁם, H8035 שֵׁם",
    4591: "σημαίνω (sēmaínō | say-mah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from σῆμα \nStrong's: (a mark; of uncertain derivation); to indicate\nKJV: —signify.\n\nCognate Group: G4593 (note), G4953 (token), G2154 (easy to be understood), G4591 (signify), G4592 (miracle), G1978 (notable), G767 (mean)\nVariants: σημαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2095 זְהַר, H3045 יָדַע, H4448 מָלַל, H5046 נָגַד, H7321 רוּעַ, H8628 תָּקַע",
    4592: "σημεῖον (sēmeîon | say-mi'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of the base of G4591\nStrong's: an indication, especially ceremonially or supernaturally\nKJV: —miracle, sign, token, wonder.\n\nSee: G4591 \n\nCognate Group: G4593 (note), G4953 (token), G2154 (easy to be understood), G4591 (signify), G4592 (miracle), G1978 (notable), G767 (mean)\nVariants: σημεῖον \nHebrew Equivalents: H226 אֹות, H4150 מֹועֵד, H4159 מֹופֵת, H4864 מַשְׂאֵת, H5251 נֵס, H6725 צִיּוּן, H8420 תָּו, H8615 תִּקְוָה",
    4593: "σημειόω (sēmeióō | say-mi-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4592\nStrong's: to distinguish, i.e. mark (for avoidance)\nKJV: —note.\n\nSee: G4592 \n\nCognate Group: G4593 (note), G4953 (token), G2154 (easy to be understood), G4591 (signify), G4592 (miracle), G1978 (notable), G767 (mean)\nVariants: σημειόω, σημειόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5375 נָשָׂא",
    4594: "σήμερον (sḗmeron | say'-mer-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter (as adverb) of a presumed compound of the article G3588 (t changed to s) and G2250\nStrong's: on the (i.e. this) day (or night current or just passed); generally, now (i.e. at present, hitherto)\nKJV: —this (to-)day.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G2250 \n\nCognate Group: G4594 (this day)\nVariants: σήμερον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3117 יֹום, H8117 שִׁמְרֹנִי",
    4595: "σήπω (sḗpō | say'-po)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to putrefy, i.e. (figuratively) perish\nKJV: —be corrupted.\n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σήπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3615 כָּלָה, H4743 מָקַק",
    4596: "σηρικός (sērikós | say-ree-kos')\n\nDerivation: from Σήρ \nStrong's: (an Indian tribe from whom silk was procured; hence the name of the silk-worm); Seric, i.e. silken (neuter as noun, a silky fabric)\nKJV: —silk.\n\nCognate Group: G4550 (bad), G4595 (be corrupted)\nVariants: σηρικός, σιρικός",
    4597: "σής (sḗs | sace)\n\nDerivation: apparently of Hebrew origin (H5580)\nStrong's: a moth\nKJV: —moth.\nSee: H5580\n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σής \nHebrew Equivalents: H5580 סָס, H6211 עָשׁ, H7538 רָקָב",
    4598: "σητόβρωτος (sētóbrōtos | say-tob'-ro-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G4597 and a derivative of G977\nStrong's: moth-eaten\nKJV: —motheaten.\n\nSee: G4597 \nSee: G977 \n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σητόβρωτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H398 אָכַל",
    4599: "σθενόω (sthenóō | sthen-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from σθένος (bodily vigor\nStrong's: probably akin to the base of G2476); to strengthen, i.e. (figuratively) confirm (in spiritual knowledge and power)\nKJV: —strengthen.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σθενόω",
    4600: "σιαγών (siagṓn | see-ag-one')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: the jaw-bone, i.e. (by implication) the cheek or side of the face\nKJV: —cheek.\n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σιαγών \nHebrew Equivalents: H3895 לְחִי",
    4601: "σιγάω (sigáō | see-gah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4602\nStrong's: to keep silent (transitively or intransitively)\nKJV: —keep close (secret, silence), hold peace.\n\nSee: G4602 \n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σιγάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1819 דָּמָה, H2013 הָסָה, H2790 חָרַשׁ, H2814 חָשָׁה",
    4602: "σιγή (sigḗ | see-gay')\n\nDerivation: apparently from σίζω (to hiss, i.e. hist or hush)\nStrong's: silence\nKJV: —silence. Compare G4623 .\n\nSee: G4623 \n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σιγή",
    4603: "σιδήρεος (sidḗreos | sid-ay'-reh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4604\nStrong's: made of iron\nKJV: —(of) iron.\n\nSee: G4604 \n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σιδήρεος, σιδηροῦς \nHebrew Equivalents: H1270 בַּרְזֶל, H6523 פַּרְזֶל",
    4604: "σίδηρος (sídēros | sid'-ay-ros)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: iron\nKJV: —iron.\n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: σίδηρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1270 בַּרְזֶל, H1631 גַּרְזֶן, H2719 חֶרֶב, H2796 חָרָשׁ, H4177 מֹורָה, H6523 פַּרְזֶל",
    4605: "Σιδών (Sidṓn | sid-one')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6721)\nStrong's: Sidon (i.e. Tsidon), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Sidon.\nSee: H6721\n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: Σιδών \nHebrew Equivalents: H6721 צִידֹון",
    4606: "Σιδώνιος (Sidṓnios | sid-o'-nee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4605\nStrong's: a Sidonian, i.e. inhabitant of Sidon\nKJV: —of Sidon.\n\nSee: G4605 \n\nCognate Group: G4598 (motheaten), G4597 (moth)\nVariants: Σιδώνιος",
    4607: "σικάριος (sikários | sik-ar'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a dagger-man or assassin; a freebooter (Jewish fanatic outlawed by the Romans)\nKJV: —murderer. Compare G5406 .\n\nSee: G5406 \n\nCognate Group: G4607 (murderer)\nVariants: σικάριος",
    4608: "σίκερα (síkera | sik'-er-ah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7941)\nStrong's: an intoxicant, i.e. intensely fermented liquor\nKJV: —strong drink.\nSee: H7941\n\nCognate Group: G4607 (murderer)\nVariants: σίκερα \nHebrew Equivalents: H7941 שֵׁכָר",
    4609: "Σίλας (Sílas | see'-las)\n\nDerivation: contraction for G4610\nStrong's: Silas, a Christian\nKJV: —Silas.\n\nSee: G4610 \n\nCognate Group: G4607 (murderer)\nVariants: Σίλας, Σιλᾶς",
    4610: "Σιλουανός (Silouanós | sil-oo-an-os')\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: \"silvan\"; Silvanus, a Christian\nKJV: —Silvanus. Compare G4609 .\n\nSee: G4609 \n\nCognate Group: G4607 (murderer)\nVariants: Σιλουανός",
    4611: "Σιλωάμ (Silōám | sil-o-am')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7975)\nStrong's: Siloam (i.e. Shiloach), a pool of Jerusalem\nKJV: —Siloam.\nSee: H7975\n\nCognate Group: G4607 (murderer)\nVariants: Σιλωάμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7975 שִׁלֹחַ",
    4612: "σιμικίνθιον (simikínthion | sim-ee-kin'-thee-on)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a semicinctium or half-girding, i.e. narrow covering (apron)\nKJV: —apron.\n\nCognate Group: G4607 (murderer)\nVariants: σιμικίνθιον, σημικίνθιον",
    4613: "Σίμων (Símōn | see'-mone)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8095)\nStrong's: Simon (i.e. Shimon), the name of nine Israelites\nKJV: —Simon. Compare G4826 .\n\nSee: G4826 \nSee: H8095\n\nCognate Group: G4607 (murderer)\nVariants: Σίμων \nHebrew Equivalents: H8095 שִׁמְעֹון",
    4614: "Σινᾶ (Sinâ | see-nah')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5514)\nStrong's: Sina (i.e. Sinai), a mountain in Arabia\nKJV: —Sina.\nSee: H5514\n\nCognate Group: G4614 (Sina)\nVariants: Σινᾶ, Σινά \nHebrew Equivalents: H5514 סִינַי",
    4615: "σίναπι (sínapi | sin'-ap-ee)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from σίνομαι (to hurt, i.e. sting)\nStrong's: mustard (the plant)\nKJV: —mustard.\n\nCognate Group: G4615 (mustard)\nVariants: σίναπι",
    4616: "σινδών (sindṓn | sin-done')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain (perhaps foreign) origin\nStrong's: byssos, i.e. bleached linen (the cloth or a garment of it)\nKJV: —(fine) linen (cloth).\n\nCognate Group: G4615 (mustard)\nVariants: σινδών \nHebrew Equivalents: H5466 סָדִין",
    4617: "σινιάζω (siniázō | sin-ee-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from σινίον (a sieve)\nStrong's: to riddle (figuratively)\nKJV: —sift.\n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift)\nVariants: σινιάζω",
    4618: "σιτευτός (siteutós | sit-yoo-ros')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4621\nStrong's: grain-fed, i.e. fattened\nKJV: —fatted.\n\nSee: G4621 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift)\nVariants: σιτευτός \nHebrew Equivalents: H75 אָבַס, H4770 מַרְבֵּק, H7794 שֹׁור, H8145 שֵׁנִי",
    4619: "σιτιστός (sitistós | sit-is-tos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4621\nStrong's: grained, i.e. fatted\nKJV: —fatling.\n\nSee: G4621 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift)\nVariants: σιτιστός",
    4620: "σιτόμετρον (sitómetron | sit-om'-et-ron)\n\nDerivation: from G4621 and G3358\nStrong's: a grain-measure, i.e. (by implication) ration (allowance of food)\nKJV: —portion of meat.\n\nSee: G4621 \nSee: G3358 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift)\nVariants: σιτόμετρον, σιτομέτριον",
    4621: "σῖτος (sîtos | see'-tos), also plural irregular neuter σῖτα (sîta | see'-tah)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: grain, especially wheat\nKJV: —corn, wheat.\n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift)\nVariants: σῖτος, σιτίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H400 אֹכֶל, H1249 בַּר, H1430 גָּדִישׁ, H1715 דָּגָן, H2406 חִטָּה, H3899 לֶחֶם, H3899 לֶחֶם, H5669 עָבוּר, H6182 עֲרִיסָה, H7667 שֶׁבֶר",
    4622: "Σιών (Siṓn | see-own')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6726)\nStrong's: Sion (i.e. Tsijon), a hill of Jerusalem; figuratively, the Church (militant or triumphant)\nKJV: —Sion.\nSee: H6726\n\nCognate Group: G4622 (Sion)\nVariants: Σιών \nHebrew Equivalents: H6726 צִיֹּון",
    4623: "σιωπάω (siōpáō | see-o-pah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from σιωπή (silence, i.e. a hush\nStrong's: properly, muteness, i.e. involuntary stillness, or inability to speak; and thus differing from G4602, which is rather a voluntary refusal or indisposition to speak, although the terms are often used synonymously); to be dumb (but not deaf also, like 2974 properly); figuratively, to be calm (as quiet water)\nKJV: —dumb, (hold) peace.\n\nSee: G4602 \n\nCognate Group: G4622 (Sion)\nVariants: σιωπάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1819 דָּמָה, H1826 דָּמַם, H2013 הָסָה, H2308 חָדַל, H2790 חָרַשׁ, H2814 חָשָׁה, H5535 סָכַת, H5956 עָלַם",
    4624: "σκανδαλίζω (skandalízō | skan-dal-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4625\nStrong's: to entrap, i.e. trip up (figuratively, stumble (transitively) or entice to sin, apostasy or displeasure)\nKJV: —(make to) offend.\n\nSee: G4625 \n\nCognate Group: G4625 (occasion to fall), G4624 (offend)\nVariants: σκανδαλίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3782 כָּשַׁל",
    4625: "σκάνδαλον (skándalon | skan'-dal-on)\n\nDerivation: (\"scandal\"); probably from a derivative of G2578\nStrong's: a trap-stick (bent sapling), i.e. snare (figuratively, cause of displeasure or sin)\nKJV: —occasion to fall (of stumbling), offence, thing that offends, stumblingblock.\n\nSee: G2578 \n\nCognate Group: G4625 (occasion to fall), G4624 (offend)\nVariants: σκάνδαλον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1848 דׇּפִי, H3689 כֶּסֶל, H4170 מֹוקֵשׁ, H4383 מִכְשֹׁול",
    4626: "σκάπτω (skáptō | skap'-to)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to dig\nKJV: —dig.\n\nCognate Group: G4625 (occasion to fall), G4624 (offend)\nVariants: σκάπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5737 עֲדַר",
    4627: "σκάφη (skáphē | skaf'-ay)\n\n\nStrong's: a \"skiff\" (as if dug out), or yawl (carried aboard a large vessel for landing)\nKJV: —boat.\n\nCognate Group: G4625 (occasion to fall), G4624 (offend)\nVariants: σκάφη",
    4628: "σκέλος (skélos | skel'-os)\n\nDerivation: apparently from σκέλλω \nStrong's: (to parch; through the idea of leanness); the leg (as lank)\nKJV: —leg.\n\nCognate Group: G4625 (occasion to fall), G4624 (offend)\nVariants: σκέλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3409 יָרֵךְ, H3767 כָּרָע, H7176 קֶרֶת, H7272 רֶגֶל, H7785 שֹׁוק",
    4629: "σκέπασμα (sképasma | skep'-as-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of σκέπας (a covering\nStrong's: perhaps akin to the base of G4649 through the idea of noticeableness); clothing\nKJV: —raiment.\n\nSee: G4649 \n\nCognate Group: G4625 (occasion to fall), G4624 (offend)\nVariants: σκέπασμα",
    4630: "Σκευᾶς (Skeuâs | skyoo-as')\n\nDerivation: apparently of Latin origin\nStrong's: left-handed; Scevas (i.e. Scævus), an Israelite\nKJV: —Sceva.\n\nCognate Group: G4625 (occasion to fall), G4624 (offend)\nVariants: Σκευᾶς",
    4631: "σκευή (skeuḗ | skyoo-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4632\nStrong's: furniture, i.e. spare tackle\nKJV: —tackling.\n\nSee: G4632 \n\nCognate Group: G4632 (goods), G4631 (tackling)\nVariants: σκευή",
    4632: "σκεῦος (skeûos | skyoo'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a vessel, implement, equipment or apparatus (literally or figuratively [specially, a wife as contributing to the usefulness of the husband])\nKJV: —goods, sail, stuff, vessel.\n\nCognate Group: G4632 (goods), G4631 (tackling)\nVariants: σκεῦος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3627 כְּלִי, H4478 מָן",
    4633: "σκηνή (skēnḗ | skay-nay')\n\nDerivation: apparently akin to G4632 and G4639\nStrong's: a tent or cloth hut (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —habitation, tabernacle.\n\nSee: G4639 \nSee: G4632 \n\nCognate Group: G2681 (lodge), G4638 (tabernacle), G4635 (tent-maker), G4637 (dwell), G4636 (tabernacle), G4633 (habitation), G1981 (rest upon), G4634 (tabernacles)\nVariants: σκηνή \nHebrew Equivalents: H168 אֹהֶל, H2691 חָצֵר, H4908 מִשְׁכָּן, H5520 סֹךְ, H5521 סֻכָּה",
    4634: "σκηνοπηγία (skēnopēgía | skay-nop-ayg-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4636 and G4078\nStrong's: the Festival of Tabernacles (so called from the custom of erecting booths for temporary homes)\nKJV: —tabernacles.\n\nSee: G4636 \nSee: G4078 \n\nCognate Group: G2681 (lodge), G4638 (tabernacle), G4635 (tent-maker), G4637 (dwell), G4636 (tabernacle), G4633 (habitation), G1981 (rest upon), G4634 (tabernacles)\nVariants: σκηνοπηγία \nHebrew Equivalents: H5521 סֻכָּה",
    4635: "σκηνοποιός (skēnopoiós | skay-nop-oy-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4633 and G4160\nStrong's: a manufacturer of tents\nKJV: —tent-maker.\n\nSee: G4633 \nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G2681 (lodge), G4638 (tabernacle), G4635 (tent-maker), G4637 (dwell), G4636 (tabernacle), G4633 (habitation), G1981 (rest upon), G4634 (tabernacles)\nVariants: σκηνοποιός",
    4636: "σκῆνος (skēnos | skay'-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G4633\nStrong's: a hut or temporary residence, i.e. (figuratively) the human body (as the abode of the spirit)\nKJV: —tabernacle.\n\nSee: G4633 \n\nCognate Group: G2681 (lodge), G4638 (tabernacle), G4635 (tent-maker), G4637 (dwell), G4636 (tabernacle), G4633 (habitation), G1981 (rest upon), G4634 (tabernacles)\nVariants: σκῆνος",
    4637: "σκηνόω (skēnóō | skay-no'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4636\nStrong's: to tent or encamp, i.e. (figuratively) to occupy (as a mansion) or (specially), to reside (as God did in the Tabernacle of old, a symbol of protection and communion)\nKJV: — dwell.\n\nSee: G4636 \n\nCognate Group: G2681 (lodge), G4638 (tabernacle), G4635 (tent-maker), G4637 (dwell), G4636 (tabernacle), G4633 (habitation), G1981 (rest upon), G4634 (tabernacles)\nVariants: σκηνόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H167 אָהַל, H7931 שָׁכַן",
    4638: "σκήνωμα (skḗnōma | skay'-no-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4637\nStrong's: an encampment, i.e. (figuratively) the Temple (as God's residence), the body (as a tenement for the soul)\nKJV: — tabernacle.\n\nSee: G4637 \n\nCognate Group: G2681 (lodge), G4638 (tabernacle), G4635 (tent-maker), G4637 (dwell), G4636 (tabernacle), G4633 (habitation), G1981 (rest upon), G4634 (tabernacles)\nVariants: σκήνωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H168 אֹהֶל, H4543 מִסְכְּנָה, H4908 מִשְׁכָּן, H5520 סֹךְ, H5521 סֻכָּה",
    4639: "σκιά (skiá | skee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: \"shade\" or a shadow (literally or figuratively (darkness of error or an adumbration))\nKJV: — shadow.\n\nCognate Group: G644 (shadow), G4639 (shadow), G1982 (overshadow)\nVariants: σκιά \nHebrew Equivalents: H6738 צֵל, H6757 צַלְמָוֶת",
    4640: "σκιρτάω (skirtáō | skeer-tah'-o)\n\nDerivation: akin to σκαίρω (to skip)\nStrong's: to jump, i.e. sympathetically move (as the quickening of a fetus)\nKJV: —leap (for joy).\n\nCognate Group: G4640 (leap)\nVariants: σκιρτάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6335 פּוּשׁ, H7533 רָצַץ, H7540 רָקַד",
    4641: "σκληροκαρδία (sklērokardía | sklay-rok-ar-dee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a compound of G4642 and G2588\nStrong's: hard-heartedness, i.e. (specially), destitution of (spiritual) perception\nKJV: —hardness of heart.\n\nSee: G4642 \nSee: G2588 \n\nCognate Group: G4641 (hardness of heart), G2589 (which knowest the hearts), G2588 (heart)\nVariants: σκληροκαρδία",
    4642: "σκληρός (sklērós | sklay-ros')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4628\nStrong's: dry, i.e. hard or tough (figuratively, harsh, severe)\nKJV: —fierce, hard.\n\nSee: G4628 \n\nCognate Group: G4641 (hardness of heart), G4643 (hardness), G4644 (stiffnecked), G4642 (fierce), G4645 (harden)\nVariants: σκληρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H533 אַמִּיץ, H2734 חָרָה, H5794 עַז, H6530 פְּרִיץ, H7185 קָשָׁה, H7185 קָשָׁה, H7185 קָשָׁה, H7563 רָשָׁע",
    4643: "σκληρότης (sklērótēs | sklay-rot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G4642\nStrong's: callousness, i.e. (figuratively) stubbornness\nKJV: —hardness.\n\nSee: G4642 \n\nCognate Group: G4641 (hardness of heart), G4643 (hardness), G4644 (stiffnecked), G4642 (fierce), G4645 (harden)\nVariants: σκληρότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H2742 חֲרוּץ, H4170 מֹוקֵשׁ, H7190 קְשִׁי",
    4644: "σκληροτράχηλος (sklērotráchēlos | sklay-rot-rakh'-ay-los)\n\nDerivation: from G4642 and G5137\nStrong's: hardnaped, i.e. (figuratively) obstinate\nKJV: —stiffnecked.\n\nSee: G4642 \nSee: G5137 \n\nCognate Group: G4641 (hardness of heart), G4643 (hardness), G4644 (stiffnecked), G4642 (fierce), G4645 (harden)\nVariants: σκληροτράχηλος",
    4645: "σκληρύνω (sklērýnō | sklay-roo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4642\nStrong's: to indurate, i.e. (figuratively) render stubborn\nKJV: —harden.\n\nSee: G4642 \n\nCognate Group: G4641 (hardness of heart), G4643 (hardness), G4644 (stiffnecked), G4642 (fierce), G4645 (harden)\nVariants: σκληρύνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2388 חָזַק, H3513 כָּבַד, H7185 קָשָׁה, H7188 קָשַׁח",
    4646: "σκολιός (skoliós | skol-ee-os')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4628\nStrong's: warped, i.e. winding; figuratively, perverse\nKJV: —crooked, froward, untoward.\n\nSee: G4628 \n\nCognate Group: G4646 (crooked)\nVariants: σκολιός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2019 הֲפַכְפַּךְ, H5637 סָרַר, H6121 עָקֹב, H6129 עֲקַלָּתֹון, H6140 עָקַשׁ, H6141 עִקֵּשׁ, H6141 עִקֵּשׁ, H6617 פָּתַל, H8417 תׇּהֳלָה, H8419 תַּהְפֻּכָה",
    4647: "σκόλοψ (skólops | skol'-ops)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the base of G4628 and G3700\nStrong's: withered at the front, i.e. a point or prickle (figuratively, a bodily annoyance or disability)\nKJV: —thorn.\n\nSee: G4628 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G4647 (thorn)\nVariants: σκόλοψ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5518 סִיר, H5544 סִלֹּון, H7899 שֵׂךְ",
    4648: "σκοπέω (skopéō | skop-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4649\nStrong's: to take aim at (spy), i.e. (figuratively) regard\nKJV: —consider, take heed, look at (on), mark. Compare G3700 .\n\nSee: G4649 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G4648 (consider), G2685 (spy), G2684 (spy out), G4649 (mark)\nVariants: σκοπέω",
    4649: "σκοπός (skopós | skop-os')\n\nDerivation: from σκέπτομαι (to peer about (\"skeptic\")\nStrong's: perhaps akin to G4626 through the idea of concealment; compare G4629); a watch (sentry or scout), i.e. (by implication) a goal\nKJV: —mark.\n\nSee: G4626 \nSee: G4629 \n\nCognate Group: G4648 (consider), G2685 (spy), G2684 (spy out), G4649 (mark)\nVariants: σκοπός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4307 מַטָּרָא, H4906 מַשְׂכִּית, H6822 צָפָה",
    4650: "σκορπίζω (skorpízō | skor-pid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: apparently from the same as G4651 (through the idea of penetrating)\nStrong's: to dissipate, i.e. (figuratively) put to flight, waste, be liberal\nKJV: —disperse abroad, scatter (abroad).\n\nSee: G4651 \n\nCognate Group: G4650 (disperse abroad), G1287 (disperse)\nVariants: σκορπίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2114 זוּר, H2219 זָרָה, H5287 נָעַר, H6327 פּוּץ, H6340 פָּזַר, H6504 פָּרַד",
    4651: "σκορπίος (skorpíos | skor-pee'-os)\n\nDerivation: probably from an obsolete σκέρπω (perhaps strengthened from the base of G4649 and meaning to pierce)\nStrong's: a \"scorpion\" (from its sting)\nKJV: —scorpion.\n\nSee: G4649 \n\nCognate Group: G4650 (disperse abroad), G1287 (disperse)\nVariants: σκορπίος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6137 עַקְרָב",
    4652: "σκοτεινός (skoteinós | skot-i-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G4655\nStrong's: opaque, i.e. (figuratively) benighted\nKJV: —dark, full of darkness.\n\nSee: G4655 \n\nCognate Group: G4652 (dark), G4653 (dark), G4655 (darkness), G4654 (darken), G4656 (be full of darkness)\nVariants: σκοτεινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H653 אֲפֵלָה, H2822 חֹשֶׁךְ, H4426 מְלִיצָה, H5399 נֶשֶׁף, H5642 סְתַר",
    4653: "σκοτία (skotía | skot-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4655\nStrong's: dimness, obscurity (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —dark(-ness).\n\nSee: G4655 \n\nCognate Group: G4652 (dark), G4653 (dark), G4655 (darkness), G4654 (darken), G4656 (be full of darkness)\nVariants: σκοτία \nHebrew Equivalents: H652 אֹפֶל, H2821 חָשַׁךְ",
    4654: "σκοτίζω (skotízō | skot-id-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4655\nStrong's: to obscure (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —darken.\n\nSee: G4655 \n\nCognate Group: G4652 (dark), G4653 (dark), G4655 (darkness), G4654 (darken), G4656 (be full of darkness)\nVariants: σκοτίζω, σκοτίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2821 חָשַׁךְ",
    4655: "σκότος (skótos | skot'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4639\nStrong's: shadiness, i.e. obscurity (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —darkness.\n\nSee: G4639 \n\nCognate Group: G4652 (dark), G4653 (dark), G4655 (darkness), G4654 (darken), G4656 (be full of darkness)\nVariants: σκότος \nHebrew Equivalents: H374 אֵיפָה, H652 אֹפֶל, H2822 חֹשֶׁךְ, H5399 נֶשֶׁף, H6049 עָנַן, H6205 עֲרָפֶל, H6757 צַלְמָוֶת, H6940 קַדְרוּת, H7661 שָׁבָץ",
    4656: "σκοτόω (skotóō | skot-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4655\nStrong's: to obscure or blind (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —be full of darkness.\n\nSee: G4655 \n\nCognate Group: G4652 (dark), G4653 (dark), G4655 (darkness), G4654 (darken), G4656 (be full of darkness)\nVariants: σκοτόω, σκοτόομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2821 חָשַׁךְ, H6937 קָדַר, H7835 שָׁחַר",
    4657: "σκύβαλον (skýbalon | skoo'-bal-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G1519 and G2965 and G906\nStrong's: what is thrown to the dogs, i.e. refuse (ordure)\nKJV: —dung.\n\nSee: G1519 \nSee: G2965 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G4657 (dung)\nVariants: σκύβαλον",
    4658: "Σκύθης (Skýthēs | skoo'-thace)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: a Scythene or Scythian, i.e. (by implication) a savage\nKJV: —Scythian.\n\nCognate Group: G4658 (Scythian)\nVariants: Σκύθης",
    4659: "σκυθρωπός (skythrōpós | skoo-thro-pos')\n\nDerivation: from σκυθρός (sullen) and a derivative of G3700\nStrong's: angry-visaged, i.e. gloomy or affecting a mournful appearance\nKJV: —of a sad countenance.\n\nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G4659 (of a sad countenance)\nVariants: σκυθρωπός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2197 זַעַף, H7451 רַע",
    4660: "σκύλλω (skýllō | skool'-lo)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to flay, i.e. (figuratively) to harass\nKJV: —trouble(self).\n\nCognate Group: G4659 (of a sad countenance)\nVariants: σκύλλω",
    4661: "σκῦλον (skŷlon | skoo'-lon)\n\nDerivation: neuter from G4660\nStrong's: something stripped (as a hide), i.e. booty\nKJV: —spoil.\n\nSee: G4660 \n\nCognate Group: G4659 (of a sad countenance)\nVariants: σκῦλον \nHebrew Equivalents: H957 בַּז, H4455 מַלְקֹוחַ, H7998 שָׁלָל",
    4662: "σκωληκόβρωτος (skōlēkóbrōtos | sko-lay-kob'-ro-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G4663 and a derivative of G977\nStrong's: worm-eaten, i.e. diseased with maggots\nKJV: —eaten of worms.\n\nSee: G4663 \nSee: G977 \n\nCognate Group: G4663 (worm), G4662 (eaten of worms)\nVariants: σκωληκόβρωτος",
    4663: "σκώληξ (skṓlēx | sko'-lakes)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a grub, maggot or earth-worm\nKJV: —worm.\n\nCognate Group: G4663 (worm), G4662 (eaten of worms)\nVariants: σκώληξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7415 רִמָּה, H8438 תֹּולָע",
    4664: "σμαράγδινος (smarágdinos | smar-ag'-dee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G4665\nStrong's: consisting of emerald\nKJV: —emerald.\n\nSee: G4665 \n\nCognate Group: G4663 (worm), G4662 (eaten of worms)\nVariants: σμαράγδινος",
    4665: "σμάραγδος (smáragdos | smar'-ag-dos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: the emerald or green gem so called\nKJV: —emerald.\n\nCognate Group: G4663 (worm), G4662 (eaten of worms)\nVariants: σμάραγδος \nHebrew Equivalents: H923 בַּהַט, H1304 בָּרֶקֶת, H3095 יַהֲלֹם, H7718 שֹׁהַם",
    4666: "σμύρνα (smýrna | smoor'-nah)\n\nDerivation: apparently strengthened for G3464\nStrong's: myrrh\nKJV: —myrrh.\n\nSee: G3464 \n\nCognate Group: G4666 (myrrh), G4669 (mingle with myrrh)\nVariants: σμύρνα, σμύρνα1 \nHebrew Equivalents: H4753 מֹר",
    4667: "Σμύρνα (Smýrna | smoor'-nah)\n\nDerivation: the same as G4666\nStrong's: Smyrna, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Smyrna.\n\nSee: G4666 \n\nCognate Group: G4668 (in Smyrna), G4667 (Smyrna)\nVariants: Σμύρνα, Ζμύρνα, Σμύρνα2",
    4668: "Σμυρναῖος (Smyrnaîos | smoor-nah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G4667\nStrong's: a Smyrnæan\nKJV: —in Smyrna.\n\nSee: G4667 \n\nCognate Group: G4668 (in Smyrna), G4667 (Smyrna)\nVariants: Σμυρναῖος G5045",
    4669: "σμυρνίζω (smyrnízō | smoor-nid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4667\nStrong's: to tincture with myrrh, i.e. embitter (as a narcotic)\nKJV: —mingle with myrrh.\n\nSee: G4667 \n\nCognate Group: G4666 (myrrh), G4669 (mingle with myrrh)\nVariants: σμυρνίζω",
    4670: "Σόδομα (Sódoma | sod'-om-ah)\n\nDerivation: plural of Hebrew origin (H5467)\nStrong's: Sodoma (i.e. Sedom), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Sodom.\nSee: H5467\n\nCognate Group: G4666 (myrrh), G4669 (mingle with myrrh)\nVariants: Σόδομα \nHebrew Equivalents: H5467 סְדֹם",
    4671: "σοί (soí | soy)\n\nDerivation: dative case of G4771\nStrong's: to thee\nKJV: —thee, thine own, thou, thy.\n\nSee: G4771 \n\nCognate Group: G4666 (myrrh), G4669 (mingle with myrrh)\nVariants: σοί, σύ",
    4672: "Σολομών (Solomṓn | sol-om-one')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8010)\nStrong's: Solomon (i.e. Shelomoh), the son of David\nKJV: —Solomon.\nSee: H8010\n\nCognate Group: G4672 (Solomon)\nVariants: Σολομών, Σαλωμών, Σολομῶν \nHebrew Equivalents: H8010 שְׁלֹמֹה",
    4673: "σορός (sorós | sor-os')\n\nDerivation: probably akin to the base of G4987\nStrong's: a funereal receptacle (urn, coffin), i.e. (by analogy) a bier\nKJV: —bier.\n\nSee: G4987 \n\nCognate Group: G4672 (Solomon)\nVariants: σορός \nHebrew Equivalents: H727 אָרֹון",
    4674: "σός (sós | sos)\n\nDerivation: from G4771\nStrong's: thine\nKJV: —thine (own), thy (friend).\n\nSee: G4771 \n\nCognate Group: G4672 (Solomon)\nVariants: σός",
    4675: "σοῦ (soû | soo)\n\nDerivation: genitive case of G4771\nStrong's: of thee, thy\nKJV: —X home, thee, thine (own), thou, thy.\n\nSee: G4771 \n\nCognate Group: G4672 (Solomon)\nVariants: σοῦ, σύ",
    4676: "σουδάριον (soudárion | soo-dar'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a sudarium (sweat-cloth), i.e. towel (for wiping the perspiration from the face, or binding the face of a corpse)\nKJV: —handkerchief, napkin.\n\nCognate Group: G4672 (Solomon)\nVariants: σουδάριον",
    4677: "Σουσάννα (Sousánna | soo-san'-nah)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7799 feminine)\nStrong's: lily; Susannah (i.e. Shoshannah), an Israelitess\nKJV: —Susanna.\nSee: H7799\n\nCognate Group: G4672 (Solomon)\nVariants: Σουσάννα \nHebrew Equivalents: H7799 שׁוּשַׁן",
    4678: "σοφία (sophía | sof-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4680\nStrong's: wisdom (higher or lower, worldly or spiritual)\nKJV: —wisdom.\n\nSee: G4680 \n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σοφία \nHebrew Equivalents: H999 בִּינָה, H1847 דַּעַת, H2451 חׇכְמָה, H4148 מוּסָר, H4284 מַחֲשָׁבָה, H7922 שֶׂכֶל",
    4679: "σοφίζω (sophízō | sof-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4680\nStrong's: to render wise; in a sinister acceptation, to form \"sophisms\", i.e. continue plausible error\nKJV: —cunningly devised, make wise.\n\nSee: G4680 \n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σοφίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2450 חָכָם",
    4680: "σοφός (sophós | sof-os')\n\nDerivation: akin to σαφής (clear)\nStrong's: wise (in a most general application)\nKJV: —wise. Compare G5429 .\n\nSee: G5429 \n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σοφός \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2450 חָכָם, H2450 חָכָם, H2450 חָכָם, H2748 חַרְטֹם, H5228 נָכֹחַ",
    4681: "Σπανία (Spanía | span-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: Spania, a region of Europe\nKJV: —Spain.\n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: Σπανία",
    4682: "σπαράσσω (sparássō | spar-as'-so)\n\nDerivation: prolongation from σπαίρω (to grasp\nStrong's: apparently strengthened from G4685 through the idea of spasmodic contraction); to mangle, i.e. convluse with epilepsy\nKJV: —rend, tear.\n\nSee: G4685 \n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σπαράσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1607 גָּעַשׁ, H1993 הָמָה, H7993 שָׁלַךְ",
    4683: "σπαργανόω (sparganóō | spar-gan-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from σπάργανον (a strip\nStrong's: from a derivative of the base of G4682 meaning to strap or wrap with strips); to swathe (an infant after the Oriental custom)\nKJV: —wrap in swaddling clothes.\n\nSee: G4682 \n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σπαργανόω",
    4684: "σπαταλάω (spataláō | spat-al-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from σπατάλη (luxury)\nStrong's: to be voluptuous\nKJV: —live in pleasure, be wanton.\n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σπαταλάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H8252 שָׁקַט",
    4685: "σπάω (spáō | spah'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to draw\nKJV: —draw (out).\n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σπάω, σπάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H4803 מָרַט, H5423 נָתַק, H5782 עוּר, H6605 פָּתַח, H8025 שָׁלַף",
    4686: "σπεῖρα (speîra | spi'-rah)\n\nDerivation: of immediate Latin origin, but ultimately a derivative of G138 in the sense of its cognate G1507\nStrong's: a coil (spira, \"spire\"), i.e. (figuratively) a mass of men (a Roman military cohort; also (by analogy) a squad of Levitical janitors)\nKJV: —band.\n\nSee: G1507 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G4679 (cunningly devised), G4678 (wisdom), G4680 (wise)\nVariants: σπεῖρα",
    4687: "σπείρω (speírō | spi'-ro)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from G4685 (through the idea of extending)\nStrong's: to scatter, i.e. sow (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —sow(- er), receive seed.\n\nSee: G4685 \n\nCognate Group: G4586 (grave), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπείρω, ἐπισπείρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2219 זָרָה, H2232 זָרַע, H2236 זָרַק, H5080 נָדַח, H6327 פּוּץ, H6340 פָּזַר, H7760 שׂוּם",
    4688: "σπεκουλάτωρ (spekoulátōr | spek-oo-lat'-ore)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a speculator, i.e. military scout (spy or (by extension) life-guardsman)\nKJV: —executioner.\n\nCognate Group: G4586 (grave), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπεκουλάτωρ",
    4689: "σπένδω (spéndō | spen'-do)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to pour out as a libation, i.e. (figuratively) to devote (one's life or blood, as a sacrifice) (\"spend\")\nKJV: —(be ready to) be offered.\n\nCognate Group: G4689 (be offered)\nVariants: σπένδω, σπένδομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5258 נָסַךְ",
    4690: "σπέρμα (spérma | sper'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4687\nStrong's: something sown, i.e. seed (including the male \"sperm\"); by implication, offspring; specially, a remnant (figuratively, as if kept over for planting)\nKJV: —issue, seed.\n\nSee: G4687 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπέρμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H319 אַחֲרִית, H1121 בֵּן, H1320 בָּשָׂר, H2233 זֶרַע, H5209 נִין, H5220 נֶכֶד",
    4691: "σπερμολόγος (spermológos | sper-mol-og'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4690 and G3004\nStrong's: a seed-picker (as the crow), i.e. (figuratively) a sponger, loafer (specially, a gossip or trifler in talk)\nKJV: —babbler.\n\nSee: G4690 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπερμολόγος",
    4692: "σπεύδω (speúdō | spyoo'-do)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from G4228\nStrong's: to \"speed\" (\"study\"), i.e. urge on (diligently or earnestly); by implication, to await eagerly\nKJV: —(make, with) haste unto.\n\nSee: G4228 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπεύδω \nHebrew Equivalents: H553 אָמַץ, H926 בָּהַל, H1765 דָּחַף, H2363 חוּשׁ, H2648 חָפַז, H4116 מָהַר",
    4693: "σπήλαιον (spḗlaion | spay'-lah-yon)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of σπέος (a grotto)\nStrong's: a cavern; by implication, a hiding-place or resort\nKJV: —cave, den.\n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπήλαιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H4631 מְעָרָה, H5341 נָצַר, H5595 סָפָה",
    4694: "σπιλάς (spilás | spee-las')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a ledge or reef of rock in the sea\nKJV: —spot (by confusion with G4696).\n\nSee: G4696 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπιλάς",
    4695: "σπιλόω (spilóō | spee-lo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4696\nStrong's: to stain or soil (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —defile, spot.\n\nSee: G4696 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπιλόω",
    4696: "σπίλος (spílos | spee'-los)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a stain or blemish, i.e. (figuratively) defect, disgrace\nKJV: —spot.\n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπίλος, σπῖλος",
    4697: "σπλαγχνίζομαι (splanchnízomai | splangkh-nid'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4698\nStrong's: to have the bowels yearn, i.e. (figuratively) feel sympathy, to pity\nKJV: —have (be moved with) compassion.\n\nSee: G4698 \n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: σπλαγχνίζομαι",
    4698: "σπλάγχνον (splánchnon | splangkh'-non)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from σπλήν (the \"spleen\")\nStrong's: an intestine (plural); figuratively, pity or sympathy\nKJV: —bowels, inward affection, + tender mercy.\n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: σπλάγχνον, σπλάγχνα \nHebrew Equivalents: H990 בֶּטֶן, H7356 רַחַם",
    4699: "σπόγγος (spóngos | spong'-gos)\n\nDerivation: perhaps of foreign origin\nStrong's: a \"sponge\"\nKJV: —spunge.\n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: σπόγγος",
    4700: "σποδός (spodós | spod-os')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: ashes\nKJV: —ashes.\n\nCognate Group: G2155 (pitiful), G4697 (have compassion), G4698 (bowels), G4184 (very pitiful)\nVariants: σποδός \nHebrew Equivalents: H665 אֵפֶר, H1880 דֶּשֶׁן",
    4701: "σπορά (sporá | spor-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G4687\nStrong's: a sowing, i.e. (by implication) parentage\nKJV: —seed.\n\nSee: G4687 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπορά \nHebrew Equivalents: H2232 זָרַע",
    4702: "σπόριμος (spórimos | spor'-ee-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G4703\nStrong's: sown, i.e. (neuter plural) a planted field\nKJV: —corn(-field).\n\nSee: G4703 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπόριμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2232 זָרַע",
    4703: "σπόρος (spóros | spro'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4687\nStrong's: a scattering (of seed), i.e. (concretely) seed (as sown)\nKJV: —seed (X sown).\n\nSee: G4687 \n\nCognate Group: G4703 (seed), G4691 (babbler), G4702 (corn), G4690 (issue), G4701 (seed), G4687 (sow)\nVariants: σπόρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2233 זֶרַע, H2758 חָרִישׁ, H2981 יְבוּל",
    4704: "σπουδάζω (spoudázō | spoo-dad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4710\nStrong's: to use speed, i.e. to make effort, be prompt or earnest\nKJV: —do (give) diligence, be diligent (forward), endeavour, labour, study.\n\nSee: G4710 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπουδάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H213 אוּץ, H926 בָּהַל, H2814 חָשָׁה",
    4705: "σπουδαῖος (spoudaîos | spoo-dah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G4710\nStrong's: prompt, energetic, earnest\nKJV: —diligent.\n\nSee: G4710 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπουδαῖος",
    4706: "σπουδαιότερον (spoudaióteron | spoo-dah-yot'-er-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G4707 \nStrong's: (as adverb; more earnestly than others), i.e. very promptly\nKJV: —very diligently.\n\nSee: G4707 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπουδαιότερον, σπουδαῖος",
    4707: "σπουδαιότερος (spoudaióteros | spoo-dah-yot'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of G4705\nStrong's: more prompt, more earnest\nKJV: —more diligent (forward).\n\nSee: G4705 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπουδαιότερος, σπουδαῖος G5080",
    4708: "σπουδαιοτέρως (spoudaiotérōs | spoo-dah-yot-er'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4707\nStrong's: more speedily, i.e. sooner than otherwise\nKJV: —more carefully.\n\nSee: G4707 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπουδαιοτέρως, σπουδαίως",
    4709: "σπουδαίως (spoudaíōs | spoo-dah'-yoce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4705\nStrong's: earnestly, promptly\nKJV: —diligently, instantly.\n\nSee: G4705 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπουδαίως",
    4710: "σπουδή (spoudḗ | spoo-day')\n\nDerivation: from G4692\nStrong's: \"speed\", i.e. (by implication) despatch, eagerness, earnestness\nKJV: —business, (earnest) care(-fulness), diligence, forwardness, haste.\n\nSee: G4692 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπουδή \nHebrew Equivalents: H924 בְּהִילוּ, H926 בָּהַל, H1205 בְּעָתָה, H2649 חִפָּזֹון, H4116 מָהַר, H5169 נָחַץ, H7281 רֶגַע",
    4711: "σπυρίς (spyrís | spoo-rece')\n\nDerivation: from G4687 (as woven)\nStrong's: a hamper or lunch-receptacle\nKJV: —basket.\n\nSee: G4687 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: σπυρίς",
    4712: "στάδιον (stádion | stad'-ee-on), or masculine (in plural) στάδιος (stádios | stad'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2476 (as fixed)\nStrong's: a stade or certain measure of distance; by implication, a stadium or race-course\nKJV: —furlong, race.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: στάδιον",
    4713: "στάμνος (stámnos | stam'-nos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2476 (as stationary)\nStrong's: a jar or earthen tank\nKJV: —pot.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4709 (diligently), G4710 (business), G4706 (very diligently), G4705 (diligent), G4707 (more diligent), G4708 (more carefully), G4704 (do diligence)\nVariants: στάμνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1228 בַּקְבֻּק, H6803 צִנְצֶנֶת",
    4714: "στάσις (stásis | stas'-is)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2476\nStrong's: a standing (properly, the act), i.e. (by analogy) position (existence); by implication, a popular uprising; figuratively, controversy\nKJV: —dissension, insurrection, X standing, uproar.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4955 (make insurrection with), G4714 (dissension)\nVariants: στάσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1916 הֲדֹם, H4494 מָנֹוחַ, H4581 מָעֹוז, H4674 מֻצָּב, H4971 מַתְכֹנֶת, H5975 עָמַד, H5982 עַמּוּד, H7010 קְיָם, H7378 רִיב",
    4715: "στατήρ (statḗr | stat-air')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2746\nStrong's: a stander (standard of value), i.e. (specially), a stater or certain coin\nKJV: —piece of money.\n\nSee: G2746 \n\nCognate Group: G4955 (make insurrection with), G4714 (dissension)\nVariants: στατήρ",
    4716: "σταυρός (staurós | stow-ros')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2476\nStrong's: a stake or post (as set upright), i.e. (specially), a pole or cross (as an instrument of capital punishment); figuratively, exposure to death, i.e. self-denial; by implication, the atonement of Christ\nKJV: —cross.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift), G388 (crucify afresh), G4716 (cross)\nVariants: σταυρός",
    4717: "σταυρόω (stauróō | stow-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4716\nStrong's: to impale on the cross; figuratively, to extinguish (subdue) passion or selfishness\nKJV: —crucify.\n\nSee: G4716 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift), G388 (crucify afresh), G4716 (cross)\nVariants: σταυρόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H8518 תָּלָה",
    4718: "σταφυλή (staphylḗ | staf-oo-lay')\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G4735\nStrong's: a cluster of grapes (as if intertwined)\nKJV: —grapes.\n\nSee: G4735 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift), G388 (crucify afresh), G4716 (cross)\nVariants: σταφυλή \nHebrew Equivalents: H6025 עֵנָב",
    4719: "στάχυς (stáchys | stakh'-oos)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G2476\nStrong's: a head of grain (as standing out from the stalk)\nKJV: —ear (of corn).\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift), G388 (crucify afresh), G4716 (cross)\nVariants: στάχυς, στάχυσ1 \nHebrew Equivalents: H4425 מְלִילָה, H7054 קָמָה, H7641 שִׁבֹּל",
    4720: "Στάχυς (Stáchys | stakh'-oos)\n\nDerivation: the same as G4719\nStrong's: Stachys, a Christian\nKJV: —Stachys.\n\nSee: G4719 \n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift), G388 (crucify afresh), G4716 (cross)\nVariants: Στάχυς, Στάχυσ2",
    4721: "στέγη (stégē | steg'-ay)\n\nDerivation: strengthened from a primary τέγος (a \"thatch\" or \"deck\" of a building)\nStrong's: a roof\nKJV: —roof.\n\nCognate Group: G4617 (sift), G388 (crucify afresh), G4716 (cross)\nVariants: στέγη \nHebrew Equivalents: H4374 מְכַסֶּה, H6738 צֵל",
    4722: "στέγω (stégō | steg'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4721\nStrong's: to roof over, i.e. (figuratively) to cover with silence (endure patiently)\nKJV: —(for-)bear, suffer.\n\nSee: G4721 \n\nCognate Group: G4722 (bear)\nVariants: στέγω",
    4723: "στείρος (steíros | sti'-ros)\n\nDerivation: a contraction from G4731 (as stiff and unnatural)\nStrong's: \"sterile\"\nKJV: —barren.\n\nSee: G4731 \n\nCognate Group: G4722 (bear)\nVariants: στείρος, στεῖρα, στεῖρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6113 עָצָר, H6135 עָקָר",
    4724: "στέλλω (stéllō | stel'-lo)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from the base of G2476\nStrong's: properly, to set fast (\"stall\"), i.e. (figuratively) to repress (reflexively, abstain from associating with)\nKJV: —avoid, withdraw self.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G5288 (draw back), G1291 (charge), G4958 (short), G2689 (apparel), G2687 (appease), G1992 (\"epistle), G1989 (write), G5289 (draw back), G1293 (difference), G4724 (avoid)\nVariants: στέλλω, στέλλομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2865 חָתַת",
    4725: "στέμμα (stémma | stem'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4735\nStrong's: a wreath for show\nKJV: —garland.\n\nSee: G4735 \n\nCognate Group: G5288 (draw back), G1291 (charge), G4958 (short), G2689 (apparel), G2687 (appease), G1992 (\"epistle), G1989 (write), G5289 (draw back), G1293 (difference), G4724 (avoid)\nVariants: στέμμα",
    4726: "στεναγμός (stenagmós | sten-ag-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G4727\nStrong's: a sigh\nKJV: —groaning.\n\nSee: G4727 \n\nCognate Group: G4959 (groan together), G4727 (with grief), G4726 (groaning)\nVariants: στεναγμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H585 אֲנָחָה, H603 אֲנָקָה, H2032 הֵרֹון, H5009 נְאָקָה, H5100 נְהָמָה, H6869 צָרָה",
    4727: "στενάζω (stenázō | sten-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4728\nStrong's: to make (intransitively, be) in straits, i.e. (by implication) to sigh, murmur, pray inaudibly\nKJV: —with grief, groan, grudge, sigh.\n\nSee: G4728 \n\nCognate Group: G4959 (groan together), G4727 (with grief), G4726 (groaning)\nVariants: στενάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H56 אָבַל, H584 אָנַח, H602 אָנַק, H1993 הָמָה, H2199 זָעַק, H5110 נוּד, H5701 עָגַם, H6030 עָנָה, H7650 שָׁבַע",
    4728: "στενός (stenós | sten-os')\n\nDerivation: probably from the base of G2476\nStrong's: narrow (from obstacles standing close about)\nKJV: —strait.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4730 (anguish), G4728 (strait), G4729 (distress)\nVariants: στενός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3906 לַחַץ, H4679 מְצַד, H6862 צַר",
    4729: "στενοχωρέω (stenochōréō | sten-okh-o-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4730\nStrong's: to hem in closely, i.e. (figuratively) cramp\nKJV: —distress, straiten.\n\nSee: G4730 \n\nCognate Group: G4730 (anguish), G4728 (strait), G4729 (distress)\nVariants: στενοχωρέω, στενοχωρέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H213 אוּץ, H509 אָלַץ, H6887 צָרַר, H7114 קָצַר",
    4730: "στενοχωρία (stenochōría | sten-okh-o-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4728 and G5561\nStrong's: narrowness of room, i.e. (figuratively) calamity\nKJV: —anguish, distress.\n\nSee: G4728 \nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G4730 (anguish), G4728 (strait), G4729 (distress)\nVariants: στενοχωρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4692 מָצֹור, H6695 צֹוק",
    4731: "στερεός (stereós | ster-eh-os')\n\nDerivation: from G2476\nStrong's: stiff, i.e. solid, stable (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —stedfast, strong, sure.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4732 (establish), G4731 (stedfast), G4733 (stedfastness)\nVariants: στερεός \nHebrew Equivalents: H117 אַדִּיר, H394 אַכְזָרִי, H2388 חָזַק, H2496 חַלָּמִישׁ, H4749 מִקְשָׁה, H6064 עָנַשׁ, H6697 צוּר, H6862 צַר",
    4732: "στερεόω (stereóō | ster-eh-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4731\nStrong's: to solidify, i.e. confirm (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —establish, receive strength, make strong.\n\nSee: G4731 \n\nCognate Group: G4732 (establish), G4731 (stedfast), G4733 (stedfastness)\nVariants: στερεόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H553 אָמַץ, H2388 חָזַק, H2946 טָפַח, H3559 כּוּן, H4013 מִבְצָר, H5186 נָטָה, H5324 נָצַב, H5970 עָלַץ, H6105 עָצַם, H6213 עָשָׂה, H7554 רָקַע, H8505 תָּכַן",
    4733: "στερέωμα (steréōma | ster-eh'-o-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4732\nStrong's: something established, i.e. (abstractly) confirmation (stability)\nKJV: —stedfastness.\n\nSee: G4732 \n\nCognate Group: G4732 (establish), G4731 (stedfast), G4733 (stedfastness)\nVariants: στερέωμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H5553 סֶלַע, H7549 רָקִיעַ, H7834 שַׁחַק",
    4734: "Στεφανᾶς (Stephanâs | stef-an-as')\n\nDerivation: probably contraction for στεφανωτός (crowned\nStrong's: from G4737); Stephanas, a Christian\nKJV: —Stephanas.\n\nSee: G4737 \n\nCognate Group: G4732 (establish), G4731 (stedfast), G4733 (stedfastness)\nVariants: Στεφανᾶς",
    4735: "στέφανος (stéphanos | stef'-an-os)\n\nDerivation: from an apparently primary στέφω (to twine or wreathe)\nStrong's: a chaplet (as a badge of royalty, a prize in the public games or a symbol of honor generally; but more conspicuous and elaborate than the simple fillet, G1238), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —crown.\n\nSee: G1238 \n\nCognate Group: G4737 (crown), G4735 (crown)\nVariants: στέφανος, στέφανοσ2 \nHebrew Equivalents: H73 אַבְנֵט, H3632 כָּלִיל, H3880 לִוְיָה, H5850 עֲטָרָה",
    4736: "Στέφανος (Stéphanos | stef'-an-os)\n\nDerivation: the same as G4735\nStrong's: Stephanus, a Christian\nKJV: —Stephen.\n\nSee: G4735 \n\nCognate Group: G4737 (crown), G4735 (crown)\nVariants: Στέφανος, Στέφανοσ1",
    4737: "στεφανόω (stephanóō | stef-an-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4735\nStrong's: to adorn with an honorary wreath (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —crown.\n\nSee: G4735 \n\nCognate Group: G4737 (crown), G4735 (crown)\nVariants: στεφανόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6279 עָתַר",
    4738: "στῆθος (stēthos | stay'-thos)\n\nDerivation: from G2476 (as standing prominently)\nStrong's: the (entire external) bosom, i.e. chest\nKJV: —breast.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4737 (crown), G4735 (crown)\nVariants: στῆθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1512 גָּחֹון, H2306 חֲדִי, H3820 לֵב",
    4739: "στήκω (stḗkō | stay'-ko)\n\nDerivation: from the perfect tense of G2476\nStrong's: to be stationary, i.e. (figuratively) to persevere\nKJV: —stand (fast).\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4739 (stand)\nVariants: στήκω",
    4740: "στηριγμός (stērigmós | stay-rig-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G4741\nStrong's: stability (figuratively)\nKJV: —stedfastness.\n\nSee: G4741 \n\nCognate Group: G4740 (stedfastness), G4741 (fix), G793 (unstable), G1991 (confirm)\nVariants: στηριγμός",
    4741: "στηρίζω (stērízō | stay-rid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G2476 (like G4731)\nStrong's: to set fast, i.e. (literally) to turn resolutely in a certain direction, or (figuratively) to confirm\nKJV: —fix, (e-)stablish, stedfastly set, strengthen.\n\nSee: G2476 \nSee: G4731 \n\nCognate Group: G4740 (stedfastness), G4741 (fix), G793 (unstable), G1991 (confirm)\nVariants: στηρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H530 אֱמוּנָה, H5307 נָפַל, H5324 נָצַב, H5414 נָתַן, H5426 נְתַר, H5564 סָמַךְ, H5582 סָעַד, H5596 סָפַח, H7760 שׂוּם, H8551 תָּמַךְ, H8628 תָּקַע",
    4742: "στίγμα (stígma | stig'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a primary στίζω (to \"stick\", i.e. prick)\nStrong's: a mark incised or punched (for recognition of ownership), i.e. (figuratively) scar of service\nKJV: —mark.\n\nCognate Group: G4742 (mark)\nVariants: στίγμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H5351 נְקֻדָּה",
    4743: "στιγμή (stigmḗ | stig-may')\n\nDerivation: feminine of G4742\nStrong's: a point of time, i.e. an instant\nKJV: —moment.\n\nSee: G4742 \n\nCognate Group: G4742 (mark)\nVariants: στιγμή \nHebrew Equivalents: H6621 פֶּתַע",
    4744: "στίλβω (stílbō | stil'-bo)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to gleam, i.e. flash intensely\nKJV: —shining.\n\nCognate Group: G4744 (shining)\nVariants: στίλβω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3851 לַהַב, H6668 צָהַב, H7043 קָלַל",
    4745: "στοά (stoá | sto-ah')\n\nDerivation: probably from G2476\nStrong's: a colonnade or interior piazza\nKJV: —porch.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4744 (shining)\nVariants: στοά, Στοά \nHebrew Equivalents: H4201 מְזוּזָה, H6268 עַתִּיק, H7531 רִצְפָּה",
    4746: "στοιβάς (stoibás | stoy-bas')\n\nDerivation: from a primary στείβω (to \"step\" or \"stamp\")\nStrong's: a spread (as if tramped flat) of loose materials for a couch, i.e. (by implication) a bough of a tree so employed\nKJV: —branch.\n\nCognate Group: G4744 (shining)\nVariants: στοιβάς, στιβάς",
    4747: "στοιχεῖον (stoicheîon | stoy-khi'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of the base of G4748\nStrong's: something orderly in arrangement, i.e. (by implication) a serial (basal, fundamental, initial) constituent (literally), proposition (figuratively)\nKJV: —element, principle, rudiment.\n\nSee: G4748 \n\nCognate Group: G4747 (element), G4748 (walk), G4960 (answer to)\nVariants: στοιχεῖον",
    4748: "στοιχέω (stoichéō | stoy-kheh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of στείχω (to range in regular line)\nStrong's: to march in (military) rank (keep step), i.e. (figuratively) to conform to virtue and piety\nKJV: —walk (orderly).\n\nCognate Group: G4747 (element), G4748 (walk), G4960 (answer to)\nVariants: στοιχέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3787 כָּשֵׁר",
    4749: "στολή (stolḗ | stol-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4724\nStrong's: equipment, i.e. (specially), a \"stole\" or long-fitting gown (as a mark of dignity)\nKJV: —long clothing (garment), (long) robe.\n\nSee: G4724 \n\nCognate Group: G4749 (long clothing)\nVariants: στολή \nHebrew Equivalents: H155 אַדֶּרֶת, H646 אֵפֹוד, H899 בֶּגֶד, H2487 חֲלִיפָה, H2488 חֲלִיצָה, H3801 כְּתֹנֶת, H3830 לְבוּשׁ, H4598 מְעִיל, H5716 עֲדִי, H6187 עֵרֶךְ, H8008 שַׂלְמָה, H8071 שִׂמְלָה",
    4750: "στόμα (stóma | stom'-a)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from a presumed derivative of the base of G5114\nStrong's: the mouth (as if a gash in the face); by implication, language (and its relations); figuratively, an opening (in the earth); specially, the front or edge (of a weapon)\nKJV: —edge, face, mouth.\n\nSee: G5114 \n\nCognate Group: G4750 (edge)\nVariants: στόμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1697 דָּבָר, H6310 פֶּה, H6433 פֻּם, H8193 שָׂפָה",
    4751: "στόμαχος (stómachos | stom'-akh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4750\nStrong's: an orifice (the gullet), i.e. (specially), the \"stomach\"\nKJV: —stomach.\n\nSee: G4750 \n\nCognate Group: G4750 (edge)\nVariants: στόμαχος",
    4752: "στρατεία (strateía | strat-i'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4754\nStrong's: military service, i.e. (figuratively) the apostolic career (as one of hardship and danger)\nKJV: —warfare.\n\nSee: G4754 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2428 חַיִל, H4475 מֶמְשָׁלָה, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H6635 צָבָא",
    4753: "στράτευμα (stráteuma | strat'-yoo-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4754\nStrong's: an armament, i.e. (by implication) a body of troops (more or less extensive or systematic)\nKJV: —army, soldier, man of war.\n\nSee: G4754 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στράτευμα",
    4754: "στρατεύομαι (strateúomai | strat-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from the base of G4756\nStrong's: to serve in a military campaign; figuratively, to execute the apostolate (with its arduous duties and functions), to contend with carnal inclinations\nKJV: —soldier, (go to) war(-fare).\n\nSee: G4756 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατεύομαι, στρατεύω",
    4755: "στρατηγός (stratēgós | strat-ay-gos')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4756 and G71 or G2233\nStrong's: a general, i.e. (by implication or analogy) a (military) governor (prætor), the chief (præfect) of the (Levitical) temple-wardens\nKJV: —captain, magistrate.\n\nSee: G4756 \nSee: G71 \nSee: G2233 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατηγός \nHebrew Equivalents: H323 אֲחַשְׁדַּרְפַּן, H4428 מֶלֶךְ, H5461 סָגָן, H5461 סָגָן, H5633 סֶרֶן, H8269 שַׂר",
    4756: "στρατιά (stratiá | strat-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a derivative of στρατός (an army\nStrong's: from the base of G4766, as encamped); camp-likeness, i.e. an army, i.e. (figuratively) the angels, the celestial luminaries\nKJV: —host.\n\nSee: G4766 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατιά \nHebrew Equivalents: H2428 חַיִל, H4475 מֶמְשָׁלָה, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H6635 צָבָא",
    4757: "στρατιώτης (stratiṓtēs | strat-ee-o'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of the same as G4756\nStrong's: a camper-out, i.e. a (common) warrior (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —soldier.\n\nSee: G4756 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατιώτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H3318 יָצָא, H4102 מָהַהּ, H6633 צָבָא",
    4758: "στρατολογέω (stratologéō | strat-ol-og-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of the base of G4756 and G3004 (in its original sense)\nStrong's: to gather (or select) as a warrior, i.e. enlist in the army\nKJV: —choose to be a soldier.\n\nSee: G4756 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατολογέω",
    4759: "στρατοπεδάρχης (stratopedárchēs | strat-op-ed-ar'-khace)\n\nDerivation: from G4760 and G757\nStrong's: a ruler of an army, i.e. (specially), a Prætorian præfect\nKJV: —captain of the guard.\n\nSee: G4760 \nSee: G757 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατοπεδάρχης, στρατοπέδαρχος G5134, G5135",
    4760: "στρατόπεδον (stratópedon | strat-op'-ed-on)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4756 and the same as G3977\nStrong's: a camping-ground, i.e. (by implication) a body of troops\nKJV: —army.\n\nSee: G4756 \nSee: G3977 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρατόπεδον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2428 חַיִל",
    4761: "στρεβλόω (streblóō | streb-lo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4762\nStrong's: to wrench, i.e. (specially), to torture (by the rack), but only figuratively, to pervert\nKJV: — wrest.\n\nSee: G4762 \n\nCognate Group: G4760 (army), G4752 (warfare), G4759 (captain of the guard), G4753 (army), G4757 (soldier), G4754 (soldier), G4756 (host), G4758 (choose to be a soldier), G4755 (captain)\nVariants: στρεβλόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6617 פָּתַל",
    4762: "στρέφω (stréphō | stref'-o)\n\nDerivation: strengthened from the base of G5157\nStrong's: to twist, i.e. turn quite around or reverse (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —convert, turn (again, back again, self, self about).\n\nSee: G5157 \n\nCognate Group: G1994 (come again), G390 (abide), G1995 (conversion), G2692 (overthrow), G1294 (perverse), G2690 (overthrow), G4762 (convert), G3344 (pervert), G654 (bring again), G391 (conversation)\nVariants: στρέφω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1550 גָּלִיל, H1641 גָּרַר, H2015 הָפַךְ, H5437 סָבַב, H6437 פָּנָה, H7613 שְׂאֵת, H7725 שׁוּב",
    4763: "στρηνιάω (strēniáō | stray-nee-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G4764\nStrong's: to be luxurious\nKJV: —live deliciously.\n\nSee: G4764 \n\nCognate Group: G1994 (come again), G390 (abide), G1995 (conversion), G2692 (overthrow), G1294 (perverse), G2690 (overthrow), G4762 (convert), G3344 (pervert), G654 (bring again), G391 (conversation)\nVariants: στρηνιάω",
    4764: "στρῆνος (strēnos | stray'-nos)\n\nDerivation: akin to G4731\nStrong's: a \"straining\", \"strenuousness\" or \"strength\", i.e. (figuratively) luxury (voluptuousness)\nKJV: —delicacy.\n\nSee: G4731 \n\nCognate Group: G1994 (come again), G390 (abide), G1995 (conversion), G2692 (overthrow), G1294 (perverse), G2690 (overthrow), G4762 (convert), G3344 (pervert), G654 (bring again), G391 (conversation)\nVariants: στρῆνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7600 שַׁאֲנָן",
    4765: "στρουθίον (strouthíon | stroo-thee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of στρουθός (a sparrow)\nStrong's: a little sparrow\nKJV: —sparrow.\n\nCognate Group: G4765 (sparrow)\nVariants: στρουθίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3283 יָעֵן, H5698 עֶגְלָה, H6833 צִפֹּור",
    4766: "στρώννυμι (strṓnnymi | strone'-noo-mee), or simpler στρωννύω (strōnnýō | strone-noo'-o)\n\n\nDerivation: prolongation from a still simpler στρόω (stróō | stro'-o)\n, (used only as an alternate in certain tenses) (probably akin to G4731 through the idea of positing)\nStrong's: to \"strew,\" i.e. spread (as a carpet or couch)\nKJV: —make bed, furnish, spread, strew.\n\nSee: G4731 \n\nCognate Group: G4765 (sparrow)\nVariants: στρώννυμι, στρωννύω, στρωννύω/στρώννυμι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2490 חָלַל, H3318 יָצָא, H3519 כָּבֹוד, H4272 מָחַץ, H4881 מְשׂוּכָה, H6428 פָּלַשׁ, H7502 רָפַד",
    4767: "στυγνητός (stygnētós | stoog-nay-tos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of an obsolete apparently primary στύγω (to hate)\nStrong's: hated, i.e. odious\nKJV: —hateful.\n\nCognate Group: G4765 (sparrow)\nVariants: στυγνητός, στυγητός",
    4768: "στυγνάζω (stygnázō | stoog-nad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4767\nStrong's: to render gloomy, i.e. (by implication) glower (be overcast with clouds, or sombreness of speech)\nKJV: —lower, be sad.\n\nSee: G4767 \n\nCognate Group: G4765 (sparrow)\nVariants: στυγνάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3805 כֹתֶרֶת, H4676 מַצֵּבָה, H5977 עֹמֶד, H5982 עַמּוּד, H7175 קֶרֶשׁ, H8074 שָׁמֵם",
    4769: "στῦλος (stŷlos | stoo'-los)\n\nDerivation: from στύω (to stiffen\nStrong's: properly akin to the base of G2476); a post (\"style\"), i.e. (figuratively) support\nKJV: —pillar.\n\nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4769 (pillar)\nVariants: στῦλος",
    4770: "Στωϊκός (Stōïkós | sto-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G4745\nStrong's: a \"Stoic\" (as occupying a particular porch in Athens), i.e. adherent of a certin philosophy\nKJV: —Stoick.\n\nSee: G4745 \n\nCognate Group: G4769 (pillar)\nVariants: Στωϊκός, Στοϊκός",
    4771: "σύ (sý | soo)\n\nDerivation: the personal pronoun of the second person singular\nStrong's: thou\nKJV: — thou. See also G4571, G4671, G4675 ; and for the plural G5209, G5210, G5213, G5216 .\n\nSee: G4675 \nSee: G4571 \nSee: G4671 \nSee: G5209 \nSee: G5210 \nSee: G5213 \nSee: G5216 \n\nCognate Group: G4769 (pillar)\nVariants: σύ",
    4772: "συγγένεια (syngéneia | soong-ghen'-i-ah)\n\n\nDerivation: from G4773\nStrong's: relationship, i.e. (concretely) relatives\nKJV: —kindred.\n\nSee: G4773 \n\nCognate Group: G4772 (kindred), G4773 (cousin)\nVariants: συγγένεια \nHebrew Equivalents: H1733 דֹּודָה, H2945 טַף, H4138 מֹולֶדֶת, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה, H5209 נִין, H8435 תֹּולְדָה",
    4773: "συγγενής (syngenḗs | soong-ghen-ace')\n\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1085\nStrong's: a relative (by blood); by extension, a fellow countryman\nKJV: —cousin, kin(-sfolk, -sman).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1085 \n\nCognate Group: G4772 (kindred), G4773 (cousin)\nVariants: συγγενής, συγγενίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H1730 דֹּוד, H1733 דֹּודָה, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה",
    4774: "συγγνώμη (syngnṓmē | soong-gno'-may)\n\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4862 and G1097\nStrong's: fellow knowledge, i.e. concession\nKJV: —permission.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1097 \n\nCognate Group: G1110 (acquaintance), G1097 (allow), G1106 (advice), G4268 (foreknowledge), G1922 (acknowledging), G4774 (permission), G4267 (foreknow), G1107 (certify), G1921 (acknowledge), G1108 (knowledge), G176 (that cannot be condemned), G2607 (blame)\nVariants: συγγνώμη",
    4775: "συγκάθημαι (synkáthēmai | soong-kath'-ay-mahee)\n\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2521\nStrong's: to seat oneself in company with\nKJV: —sit with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2521 \n\nCognate Group: G1110 (acquaintance), G1097 (allow), G1106 (advice), G4268 (foreknowledge), G1922 (acknowledging), G4774 (permission), G4267 (foreknow), G1107 (certify), G1921 (acknowledge), G1108 (knowledge), G176 (that cannot be condemned), G2607 (blame)\nVariants: συγκάθημαι",
    4776: "συγκαθίζω (synkathízō | soong-kath-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2523\nStrong's: to give (or take) a seat in company with\nKJV: —(make) sit (down) together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2523 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συγκαθίζω, περικαθίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2583 חָנָה, H3898 לָחַם, H6696 צוּר, H7194 קָשַׁר",
    4777: "συγκακοπαθέω (synkakopathéō | soong-kak-op-ath-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2553\nStrong's: to suffer hardship in company with\nKJV: —be partaker of afflictions.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2553 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συγκακοπαθέω",
    4778: "συγκακουχέω (synkakouchéō | soong-kak-oo-kheh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2558\nStrong's: to maltreat in company with, i.e. (passively) endure persecution together\nKJV: —suffer affliction with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2558 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συγκακουχέω, συγκακουχέομαι",
    4779: "συγκαλέω (synkaléō | soong-kal-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2564\nStrong's: to convoke\nKJV: —call together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2564 \n\nCognate Group: G1458 (accuse), G2822 (called), G2564 (bid), G1528 (call in), G4779 (call together), G3333 (call), G2821 (calling), G1462 (crime laid against), G1941 (appeal), G4341 (call), G1577 (assembly), G4292 (provoke), G479 (bid again)\nVariants: συγκαλέω",
    4780: "συγκαλύπτω (synkalýptō | soong-kal-oop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2572\nStrong's: to conceal altogether\nKJV: —cover.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2572 \n\nCognate Group: G4780 (cover)\nVariants: συγκαλύπτω",
    4781: "συγκάμπτω (synkámptō | soong-kamp'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2578\nStrong's: to bend together, i.e. (figuratively) to afflict\nKJV: —bow down.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2578 \n\nCognate Group: G4780 (cover)\nVariants: συγκάμπτω",
    4782: "συγκαταβαίνω (synkatabaínō | soong-kat-ab-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2597\nStrong's: to descend in company with\nKJV: —go down with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2597 \n\nCognate Group: G4780 (cover)\nVariants: συγκαταβαίνω",
    4783: "συγκατάθεσις (synkatáthesis | soong-kat-ath'-es-is)\n\nDerivation: from G4784\nStrong's: a deposition (of sentiment) in company with, i.e. (figuratively) accord with\nKJV: —agreement.\n\nSee: G4784 \n\nCognate Group: G4780 (cover)\nVariants: συγκατάθεσις",
    4784: "συγκατατίθεμαι (synkatatíthemai | soong-kat-at-ith'-em-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle from G4862 and G2698\nStrong's: to deposit (one's vote or opinion) in company with, i.e. (figuratively) to accord with\nKJV: —consent.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2698 \n\nCognate Group: G4780 (cover)\nVariants: συγκατατίθεμαι, συγκατατίθημι",
    4785: "συγκαταψηφίζω (synkatapsēphízō | soong-kat-aps-ay-fid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a compound of G2596 and G5585\nStrong's: to count down in company with, i.e. enroll among\nKJV: —number with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2596 \nSee: G5585 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: συγκαταψηφίζω, καταψηφίζομαι, συγκαταψηφίζομαι",
    4786: "συγκεράννυμι (synkeránnymi | soong-ker-an'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2767\nStrong's: to commingle, i.e. (figuratively) to combine or assimilate\nKJV: —mix with, temper together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2767 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: συγκεράννυμι",
    4787: "συγκινέω (synkinéō | soong-kin-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4682 and G2795\nStrong's: to move together, i.e. (specially), to excite as a mass (to sedition)\nKJV: —stir up.\n\nSee: G4682 \nSee: G2795 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: συγκινέω",
    4788: "συγκλείω (synkleíō | soong-kli'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2808\nStrong's: to shut together, i.e. include or (figuratively) embrace in a common subjection to\nKJV: —conclude, inclose, shut up.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2808 \n\nCognate Group: G4788 (conclude)\nVariants: συγκλείω",
    4789: "συγκληρονόμος (synklēronómos | soong-klay-ron-om'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2818\nStrong's: a co-heir, i.e. (by analogy) participant in common\nKJV: —fellow (joint)-heir, heir together, heir with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2818 \n\nCognate Group: G3647 (perfect soundness), G2819 (heritage), G2817 (inheritance), G4789 (fellow -heir), G2820 (obtain an inheritance), G3648 (entire), G2624 (divide by lot), G2816 (be heir), G4345 (consort with), G2818 (heir)\nVariants: συγκληρονόμος",
    4790: "συγκοινωνέω (synkoinōnéō | soong-koy-no-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2841\nStrong's: to share in company with, i.e. co-participate in\nKJV: —communicate (have fellowship) with, be partaker of.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2841 \n\nCognate Group: G2842 (communicate), G2844 (companion), G2840 (call common), G4790 (communicate with), G2841 (communicate), G4791 (companion), G2843 (willing to communicate), G2839 (common)\nVariants: συγκοινωνέω",
    4791: "συγκοινωνός (synkoinōnós | soong-koy-no-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2844\nStrong's: a co-participant\nKJV: —companion, partake(-r, -r with).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2844 \n\nCognate Group: G2842 (communicate), G2844 (companion), G2840 (call common), G4790 (communicate with), G2841 (communicate), G4791 (companion), G2843 (willing to communicate), G2839 (common)\nVariants: συγκοινωνός",
    4792: "συγκομίζω (synkomízō | soong-kom-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2865\nStrong's: to convey together, i.e. collect or bear away in company with others\nKJV: —carry.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2865 \n\nCognate Group: G2842 (communicate), G2844 (companion), G2840 (call common), G4790 (communicate with), G2841 (communicate), G4791 (companion), G2843 (willing to communicate), G2839 (common)\nVariants: συγκομίζω",
    4793: "συγκρίνω (synkrínō | soong-kree'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2919\nStrong's: to judge of one thing in connection with another, i.e. combine (spiritual ideas with appropriate expressions) or collate (one person with another by way of contrast or resemblance)\nKJV: —compare among (with).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2919 \n\nCognate Group: G4793 (compare among)\nVariants: συγκρίνω",
    4794: "συγκύπτω (synkýptō | soong-koop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2955\nStrong's: to stoop altogether, i.e. be completely overcome by\nKJV: —bow together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2955 \n\nCognate Group: G4793 (compare among)\nVariants: συγκύπτω",
    4795: "συγκυρία (synkyría | soong-koo-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a comparative of G4862 and κυρέω (to light or happen\nStrong's: from the base of G2962); concurrence, i.e. accident\nKJV: —chance.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2962 \n\nCognate Group: G4793 (compare among)\nVariants: συγκυρία, συντυχία",
    4796: "συγχαίρω (synchaírō | soong-khah'-ee-ro)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5463\nStrong's: to sympathize in gladness, congratulate\nKJV: —rejoice in (with).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5463 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: συγχαίρω",
    4797: "συγχέω (synchéō | soong-kheh'-o) or συγχύνω (synchýnō | soong-khoo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and χέω (to pour) or its alternate\nStrong's: to commingle promiscuously, i.e. (figuratively) to throw (an assembly) into disorder, to perplex (the mind)\nKJV: —confound, confuse, stir up, be in an uproar.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: συγχέω, συγχύνω, συγχέω/συγχύν(ν)ω",
    4798: "συγχράομαι (synchráomai | soong-khrah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5530\nStrong's: to use jointly, i.e. (by implication) to hold intercourse in common\nKJV: —have dealings with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: συγχράομαι",
    4799: "σύγχυσις (sýnchysis | soong'-khoo-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4797\nStrong's: commixture, i.e. (figuratively) riotous disturbance\nKJV: —confusion.\n\nSee: G4797 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: σύγχυσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H894 בָּבֶל, H4103 מְהוּמָה",
    4800: "συζάω (syzáō | sood-zah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2198\nStrong's: to continue to live in common with, i.e. co-survive (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —live with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2198 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συζάω",
    4801: "συζεύγνυμι (syzeúgnymi | sood-zyoog'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and the base of G2201\nStrong's: to yoke together, i.e. (figuratively) conjoin (in marriage)\nKJV: —join together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2201 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συζεύγνυμι, ζεύγνυμι",
    4802: "συζητέω (syzētéō | sood-zay-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2212\nStrong's: to investigate jointly, i.e. discuss, controvert, cavil\nKJV: —dispute (with), enquire, question (with), reason (together).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2212 \n\nCognate Group: G4802 (dispute), G4803 (disputation), G4804 (disputer)\nVariants: συζητέω",
    4803: "συζήτησις (syzḗtēsis | sood-zay'-tay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4802\nStrong's: mutual questioning, i.e. discussion\nKJV: —disputation(-ting), reasoning.\n\nSee: G4802 \n\nCognate Group: G4802 (dispute), G4803 (disputation), G4804 (disputer)\nVariants: συζήτησις G5185",
    4804: "συζητητής (syzētētḗs | sood-zay-tay-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G4802\nStrong's: a disputant, i.e. sophist\nKJV: —disputer.\n\nSee: G4802 \n\nCognate Group: G4802 (dispute), G4803 (disputation), G4804 (disputer)\nVariants: συζητητής",
    4805: "σύζυγος (sýzygos | sood'-zoo-gos)\n\nDerivation: from G4801\nStrong's: co-yoked, i.e. (figuratively) as noun, a colleague; probably rather as a proper name; Syzygus, a Christian\nKJV: —yokefellow.\n\nSee: G4801 \n\nCognate Group: G4805 (yokefellow)\nVariants: σύζυγος",
    4806: "συζωοποιέω (syzōopoiéō | sood-zo-op-oy-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2227\nStrong's: to reanimate conjointly with (figuratively)\nKJV: —quicken together with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2227 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συζωοποιέω",
    4807: "συκάμινος (sykáminos | soo-kam'-ee-nos)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H8256) in imitation of G4809\nStrong's: a sycamore-fig tree\nKJV: —sycamine tree.\n\nSee: G4809 \nSee: H8256\n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: συκάμινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8256 שִׁקְמָה",
    4808: "συκῆ (sykē | soo-kay')\n\nDerivation: from G4810\nStrong's: a fig-tree\nKJV: —fig tree.\n\nSee: G4810 \n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: συκῆ \nHebrew Equivalents: H8384 תְּאֵן",
    4809: "συκομωραία (sykomōraía | soo-kom-o-rah'-yah)\n\nDerivation: from G4810 and μόρον (the mulberry)\nStrong's: the \"sycamore\"-fig tree\nKJV: —sycamore tree. Compare G4807 .\n\nSee: G4810 \nSee: G4807 \n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: συκομωραία, συκομορέα",
    4810: "σῦκον (sŷkon | soo'-kon)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a fig\nKJV: —fig.\n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: σῦκον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1063 בִּכּוּרָה, H8384 תְּאֵן",
    4811: "συκοφαντέω (sykophantéō | soo-kof-an-teh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4810 and a derivative of G5316\nStrong's: to be a fig-informer (reporter of the law forbidding the exportation of figs from Greece), \"sycophant\", i.e. (genitive and by extension) to defraud (exact unlawfully, extort)\nKJV: —accuse falsely, take by false accusation.\n\nSee: G4810 \nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: συκοφαντέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1556 גָּלַל, H6231 עָשַׁק, H8266 שָׁקַר",
    4812: "συλαγωγέω (sylagōgéō | soo-lag-ogue-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4813 and (the reduplicated form of) G71\nStrong's: to lead away as booty, i.e. (figuratively) seduce\nKJV: —spoil.\n\nSee: G4813 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: συλαγωγέω",
    4813: "συλάω (syláō | soo-lah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of σύλλω (to strip\nStrong's: probably akin to G138 ; compare G4661); to despoil\nKJV: —rob.\n\nSee: G138 \nSee: G4661 \n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: συλάω",
    4814: "συλλαλέω (syllaléō | sool-lal-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2980\nStrong's: to talk together, i.e. converse\nKJV: —commune (confer, talk) with, speak among.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2980 \n\nCognate Group: G4807 (sycamine tree), G4810 (fig), G4811 (accuse falsely), G4809 (sycamore tree), G4808 (fig tree)\nVariants: συλλαλέω",
    4815: "συλλαμβάνω (syllambánō | sool-lam-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2983\nStrong's: to clasp, i.e. seize (arrest, capture); specially, to conceive (literally or figuratively); by implication, to aid\nKJV: —catch, conceive, help, take.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G4815 (catch)\nVariants: συλλαμβάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2029 הָרָה, H2029 הָרָה, H2342 חוּל, H3920 לָכַד, H3947 לָקַח, H5367 נָקַשׁ, H7059 קָמַט, H8610 תָּפַשׂ",
    4816: "συλλέγω (syllégō | sool-leg'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3004 in its original sense\nStrong's: to collect\nKJV: —gather (together, up).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G4815 (catch)\nVariants: συλλέγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H3947 לָקַח, H3950 לָקַט, H6014 עָמַר, H6998 קָטַף, H7197 קָשַׁשׁ",
    4817: "συλλογίζομαι (syllogízomai | sool-log-id'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3049\nStrong's: to reckon together (with oneself), i.e. deliberate\nKJV: —reason with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3049 \n\nCognate Group: G4815 (catch)\nVariants: συλλογίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2803 חָשַׁב",
    4818: "συλλυπέω (syllypéō | sool-loop-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3076\nStrong's: to afflict jointly, i.e. (passive) sorrow at (on account of) someone\nKJV: —be grieved.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3076 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: συλλυπέω, συλλυπέομαι",
    4819: "συμβαίνω (symbaínō | soom-bah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and the base of G939\nStrong's: to walk (figuratively, transpire) together, i.e. concur (take place)\nKJV: —be(-fall), happen (unto).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: συμβαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1961 הָיָה, H4672 מָצָא, H6213 עָשָׂה, H7121 קָרָא, H7136 קָרָה",
    4820: "συμβάλλω (symbállō | soom-bal'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G906\nStrong's: to combine, i.e. (in speaking) to converse, consult, dispute, (mentally) to consider, (by implication) to aid, (personally) to join, attack\nKJV: —confer, encounter, help, make, meet with, ponder.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G253 (less sorrowful), G3077 (grief), G4036 (exceeding sorry), G4818 (be grieved), G3076 (cause grief)\nVariants: συμβάλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3289 יָעַץ, H5496 סוּת, H8254 שָׁקַל",
    4821: "συμβασιλεύω (symbasileúō | soom-bas-il-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G936\nStrong's: to be co-regent (figuratively)\nKJV: —reign with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G936 \n\nCognate Group: G934 (royal), G936 (king), G4821 (reign with), G937 (king's), G932 (kingdom), G935 (king), G938 (queen)\nVariants: συμβασιλεύω",
    4822: "συμβιβάζω (symbibázō | soom-bib-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and βιβάζω (to force\nStrong's: causative (by reduplication) of the base of G939); to drive together, i.e. unite (in association or affection), (mentally) to infer, show, teach\nKJV: —compact, assuredly gather, intrust, knit together, prove.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G4822 (compact)\nVariants: συμβιβάζω",
    4823: "συμβουλεύω (symbouleúō | soom-bool-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1011\nStrong's: to give (or take) advice jointly, i.e. recommend, deliberate or determine\nKJV: —consult, (give, take) counsel (together).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1011 \n\nCognate Group: G4822 (compact)\nVariants: συμβουλεύω",
    4824: "συμβούλιον (symboúlion | soom-boo'-lee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G4825\nStrong's: advisement; specially, a deliberative body, i.e. the provincial assessors or lay-court\nKJV: —consultation, counsel, council.\n\nSee: G4825 \n\nCognate Group: G4822 (compact)\nVariants: συμβούλιον",
    4825: "σύμβουλος (sýmboulos | soom'-boo-los)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1012\nStrong's: a consultor, i.e. adviser\nKJV: —counsellor.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1012 \n\nCognate Group: G4822 (compact)\nVariants: σύμβουλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3289 יָעַץ",
    4826: "Συμεών (Symeṓn | soom-eh-one')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4613\nStrong's: Symeon (i.e. Shimon), the name of five Israelites\nKJV: —Simeon, Simon.\n\nSee: G4613 \n\nCognate Group: G4822 (compact)\nVariants: Συμεών \nHebrew Equivalents: H8095 שִׁמְעֹון",
    4827: "συμμαθητής (symmathētḗs | soom-math-ay-tace')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4862 and G3129\nStrong's: a co-learner (of Christianity)\nKJV: —fellow disciple.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3129 \n\nCognate Group: G3102 (disciple), G2648 (consider), G3101 (disciple), G3129 (learn), G4827 (fellow disciple), G3100 (be disciple)\nVariants: συμμαθητής",
    4828: "συμμαρτυρέω (symmartyréō | soom-mar-too-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3140\nStrong's: to testify jointly, i.e. corroborate by (concurrent) evidence\nKJV: —testify unto, (also) bear witness (with).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3140 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: συμμαρτυρέω, συμματυρέω",
    4829: "συμμερίζομαι (symmerízomai | soom-mer-id'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4862 and G3307\nStrong's: to share jointly, i.e. participate in\nKJV: —be partaker with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3307 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: συμμερίζομαι, συμμερίζω",
    4830: "συμμέτοχος (symmétochos | soom-met'-okh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3353\nStrong's: a co-participant\nKJV: —partaker.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3353 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: συμμέτοχος",
    4831: "συμμιμητής (symmimētḗs | soom-mim-ay-tace')\n\nDerivation: from a presumed compound of G4862 and G3401\nStrong's: a co-imitator, i.e. fellow votary\nKJV: —follower together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3401 \n\nCognate Group: G4831 (follower together), G3402 (follower), G3401 (follow)\nVariants: συμμιμητής",
    4832: "σύμμορφος (sýmmorphos | soom-mor-fos')\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3444\nStrong's: jointly formed, i.e. (figuratively) similar\nKJV: —conformed to, fashioned like unto.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3444 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: σύμμορφος",
    4833: "συμμορφόω (symmorphóō | soom-mor-fo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4832\nStrong's: to render like, i.e. (figuratively) to assimilate\nKJV: —make conformable unto.\n\nSee: G4832 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συμμορφόω, συμμορφίζω, συμφορτίζω",
    4834: "συμπαθέω (sympathéō | soom-path-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4835\nStrong's: to feel \"sympathy\" with, i.e. (by implication) to commiserate\nKJV: —have compassion, be touched with a feeling of.\n\nSee: G4835 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπαθέω",
    4835: "συμπαθής (sympathḗs | soom-path-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G4841\nStrong's: having a fellow-feeling (\"sympathetic\"), i.e. (by implication) mutually commiserative\nKJV: —having compassion one of another.\n\nSee: G4841 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπαθής \nHebrew Equivalents: H57 אָבֵל",
    4836: "συμπαραγίνομαι (symparagínomai | soom-par-ag-in'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3854\nStrong's: to be present together, i.e. to convene; by implication, to appear in aid\nKJV: —come together, stand with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3854 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπαραγίνομαι",
    4837: "συμπαρακαλέω (symparakaléō | soom-par-ak-al-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3870\nStrong's: to console jointly\nKJV: —comfort together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3870 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπαρακαλέω, συμπαρακαλέομαι",
    4838: "συμπαραλαμβάνω (symparalambánō | soom-par-al-am-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3880\nStrong's: to take along in company\nKJV: —take with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3880 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπαραλαμβάνω",
    4839: "συμπαραμένω (symparaménō | soom-par-am-en'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3887\nStrong's: to remain in company, i.e. still live\nKJV: —continue with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3887 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπαραμένω G5222",
    4840: "συμπάρειμι (sympáreimi | soom-par'-i-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3918\nStrong's: to be at hand together, i.e. now present\nKJV: —be here present with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3918 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπάρειμι",
    4841: "συμπάσχω (sympáschō | soom-pas'-kho)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3958 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to experience pain jointly or of the same kind (specially, persecution; to \"sympathize\")\nKJV: —suffer with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3958 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπάσχω",
    4842: "συμπέμπω (sympémpō | soom-pem'-po)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3992\nStrong's: to despatch in company\nKJV: —send with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3992 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπέμπω",
    4843: "συμπεριλαμβάνω (symperilambánō | soom-per-ee-lam-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a compound of G4012 and G2983\nStrong's: to take by enclosing altogether, i.e. earnestly throw the arms about one\nKJV: —embrace.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4012 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπεριλαμβάνω",
    4844: "συμπίνω (sympínō | soom-pee'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4095\nStrong's: to partake a beverage in company\nKJV: —drink with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G3663 (of like passions), G4834 (have compassion), G2552 (suffering affliction), G4236 (meekness), G3804 (affection), G3958 (feel), G3356 (have compassion), G4777 (be partaker of afflictions), G2553 (be afflicted), G4310 (suffer before), G4835 (having compassion one of another), G4841 (suffer with), G3805 (suffer), G3806 (affection)\nVariants: συμπίνω",
    4845: "συμπληρόω (symplēróō | soom-play-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4137\nStrong's: to implenish completely, i.e. (of space) to swamp (a boat), or (of time) to accomplish (passive, be complete)\nKJV: —(fully) come, fill up.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4137 \n\nCognate Group: G4137 (accomplish), G4136 (assurance), G1604 (accomplishment), G4845 (come), G466 (fill up), G378 (fill up), G4134 (full), G4135 (most surely believe), G1603 (fulfill), G4138 (which is put in to fill up)\nVariants: συμπληρόω",
    4846: "συμπνίγω (sympnígō | soom-pnee'-go)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4155\nStrong's: to strangle completely, i.e. (literally) to drown, or (figuratively) to crowd\nKJV: —choke, throng.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4155 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: συμπνίγω",
    4847: "συμπολίτης (sympolítēs | soom-pol-ee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4177\nStrong's: a native of the same town, i.e. (figuratively) co-religionist (fellow-Christian)\nKJV: —fellow- citizen.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4177 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: συμπολίτης",
    4848: "συμπορεύομαι (symporeúomai | soom-por-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4198\nStrong's: to journey together; by implication, to assemble\nKJV: —go with, resort.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4198 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: συμπορεύομαι",
    4849: "συμπόσιον (sympósion | soom-pos'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of the alternate of G4844\nStrong's: a drinking-party (\"symposium\"), i.e. (by extension) a room of guests\nKJV: —company.\n\nSee: G4844 \n\nCognate Group: G4156 (strangled), G4846 (choke), G638 (choke), G4155 (choke)\nVariants: συμπόσιον, συμποσία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4960 מִשְׁתֶּה",
    4850: "συμπρεσβύτερος (sympresbýteros | soom-pres-boo'-ter-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4245\nStrong's: a co-presbyter\nKJV: —presbyter, also an elder.\n\nSee: G4245 \nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G4245 (elder), G4850 (presbyter), G4244 (elder), G4246 (aged), G4243 (be an ambassador), G4247 (aged woman)\nVariants: συμπρεσβύτερος",
    4851: "συμφέρω (symphérō | soom-fer'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5342 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to bear together (contribute), i.e. (literally) to collect, or (figuratively) to conduce; especially (neuter participle as a noun) advantage\nKJV: —be better for, bring together, be expedient (for), be good, (be) profit(-able for).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: συμφέρω, σύμφορος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2895 טֹוב, H5116 נָוֶה, H7737 שָׁוָה",
    4852: "σύμφημι (sýmphēmi | soom'-fay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5346\nStrong's: to say jointly, i.e. assent to\nKJV: —consent unto.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5346 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: σύμφημι",
    4853: "συμφυλέτης (symphylétēs | soom-foo-let'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of G5443\nStrong's: a co-tribesman, i.e. native of the same country\nKJV: —countryman.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5443 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: συμφυλέτης",
    4854: "σύμφυτος (sýmphytos | soom'-foo-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of G5453\nStrong's: grown along with (connate), i.e. (figuratively) closely united to\nKJV: —planted together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5453 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: σύμφυτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1219 בָּצַר, H4127 מוּג",
    4855: "συμφύω (symphýō | soom-foo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5453\nStrong's: passive, to grow jointly\nKJV: —spring up with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5453 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συμφύω, συμφύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H225 אוּת, H2266 חָבַר, H5117 נוּחַ",
    4856: "συμφωνέω (symphōnéō | soom-fo-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4859\nStrong's: to be harmonious, i.e. (figuratively) to accord (be suitable, concur) or stipulate (by compact)\nKJV: —agree (together, with).\n\nSee: G4859 \n\nCognate Group: G4859 (consent), G2019 (cry), G4857 (concord), G4858 (music), G4856 (agree together), G5455 (call), G5456 (noise)\nVariants: συμφωνέω",
    4857: "συμφώνησις (symphṓnēsis | soom-fo'-nay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4856\nStrong's: accordance\nKJV: —concord.\n\nSee: G4856 \n\nCognate Group: G4859 (consent), G2019 (cry), G4857 (concord), G4858 (music), G4856 (agree together), G5455 (call), G5456 (noise)\nVariants: συμφώνησις",
    4858: "συμφωνία (symphōnía | soom-fo-nee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4859\nStrong's: unison of sound (\"symphony\"), i.e. a concert of instruments (harmonious note)\nKJV: —music.\n\nSee: G4859 \n\nCognate Group: G4859 (consent), G2019 (cry), G4857 (concord), G4858 (music), G4856 (agree together), G5455 (call), G5456 (noise)\nVariants: συμφωνία",
    4859: "σύμφωνος (sýmphōnos | soom'-fo-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5456\nStrong's: sounding together (alike), i.e. (figuratively) accordant (neuter as noun, agreement)\nKJV: — consent.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5456 \n\nCognate Group: G4859 (consent), G2019 (cry), G4857 (concord), G4858 (music), G4856 (agree together), G5455 (call), G5456 (noise)\nVariants: σύμφωνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4962 מַת",
    4860: "συμψηφίζω (sympsēphízō | soom-psay-fid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5585\nStrong's: to compute jointly\nKJV: —reckon.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5585 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: συμψηφίζω",
    4861: "σύμψυχος (sýmpsychos | soom'-psoo-khos)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5590\nStrong's: co-spirited, i.e. similar in sentiment\nKJV: —like-minded.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5590 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: σύμψυχος",
    4862: "σύν (sýn | soon)\n\nDerivation: a primary preposition denoting union\nStrong's: with or together (but much closer than G3326 or G3844), i.e. by association, companionship, process, resemblance, possession, instrumentality, addition, etc.\nKJV: —beside, with. In composition it has similar applications, including completeness.\n\nSee: G3326 \nSee: G3844 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: σύν, συνεπίσκοπος",
    4863: "συνάγω (synágō | soon-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G71\nStrong's: to lead together, i.e. collect or convene; specially, to entertain (hospitably)\nKJV: —+ accompany, assemble (selves, together), bestow, come together, gather (selves together, up, together), lead into, resort, take in.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συνάγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H857 אָתָה, H935 בֹּוא, H1104 בָּלַע, H1716 דָּגַר, H2363 חוּשׁ, H2404 חָטַב, H2630 חָסַן, H2820 חָשַׂךְ, H3245 יָסַד, H3254 יָסַף, H3259 יָעַד, H3318 יָצָא, H3320 יָצַב, H3647 כָּמַס, H3664 כָּנַס, H3950 לָקַט, H4513 מָנַע, H5060 נָגַע, H5102 נָהַר, H5117 נוּחַ, H5127 נוּס, H5337 נָצַל, H5344 נָקַב, H5414 נָתַן, H5590 סָעַר, H5595 סָפָה, H5608 סָפַר, H5756 עוּז, H5927 עָלָה, H5975 עָמַד, H6113 עָצָר, H6299 פָּדָה, H6651 צָבַר, H6817 צָעַק, H6908 קָבַץ, H6950 קָהַל, H6960 קָוָה, H6973 קוּץ, H6998 קָטַף, H7092 קָפַץ, H7105 קָצִיר, H7126 קָרַב, H7194 קָשַׁר, H7197 קָשַׁשׁ, H7264 רָגַז, H7725 שׁוּב, H7939 שָׂכָר, H7939 שָׂכָר, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    4864: "συναγωγή (synagōgḗ | soon-ag-o-gay')\n\nDerivation: from (the reduplicated form of) G4863\nStrong's: an assemblage of persons; specially, a Jewish \"synagogue\" (the meeting or the place); by analogy, a Christian church\nKJV: —assembly, congregation, synagogue.\n\nSee: G4863 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συναγωγή \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H1004 בַּיִת, H1121 בֵּן, H1530 גַּל, H1995 הָמֹון, H2428 חַיִל, H4234 מָחֹול, H4264 מַחֲנֶה, H4723 מִקְוֶה, H4725 מָקֹום, H4908 מִשְׁכָּן, H5475 סֹוד, H5712 עֵדָה, H5971 עַם, H5971 עַם, H6908 קָבַץ, H6951 קָהָל, H8615 תִּקְוָה",
    4865: "συναγωνίζομαι (synagōnízomai | soon-ag-o-nid'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G75\nStrong's: to struggle in company with, i.e. (figuratively) to be a partner (assistant)\nKJV: —strive together with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G75 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συναγωνίζομαι",
    4866: "συναθλέω (synathléō | soon-ath-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G118\nStrong's: to wrestle in company with, i.e. (figuratively) to seek jointly\nKJV: —labour with, strive together for.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G118 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: συναθλέω",
    4867: "συναθροίζω (synathroízō | soon-ath-royd'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and ἀθροίζω (to hoard)\nStrong's: to convene\nKJV: —call (gather) together.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: συναθροίζω, ἀθροίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1951 הוּן, H3259 יָעַד, H4032 מָגֹור, H5064 נָגַר, H5128 נוּעַ, H5789 עוּשׁ, H6908 קָבַץ, H6908 קָבַץ, H6950 קָהַל, H6950 קָהַל",
    4868: "συναίρω (synaírō | soon-ah'-ee-ro)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G142\nStrong's: to make up together, i.e. (figuratively) to compute (an account)\nKJV: —reckon, take.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G142 \n\nCognate Group: G118 (strive), G119 (fight), G4866 (labour with)\nVariants: συναίρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5800 עָזַב",
    4869: "συναιχμάλωτος (synaichmálōtos | soon-aheekh-mal'-o-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G164\nStrong's: a co-captive\nKJV: —fellowprisoner.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G164 \n\nCognate Group: G4869 (fellowprisoner), G161 (captivity), G162 (lead captive), G163 (lead away captive), G164 (captive)\nVariants: συναιχμάλωτος",
    4870: "συνακολουθέω (synakolouthéō | soon-ak-ol-oo-theh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G190\nStrong's: to accompany\nKJV: —follow.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G190 \n\nCognate Group: G1811 (follow), G3877 (attain), G1872 (follow), G4870 (follow), G190 (follow)\nVariants: συνακολουθέω",
    4871: "συναλίζω (synalízō | soon-al-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and ἁλίζω (to throng)\nStrong's: to accumulate, i.e. convene\nKJV: —assemble together.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G1811 (follow), G3877 (attain), G1872 (follow), G4870 (follow), G190 (follow)\nVariants: συναλίζω, συναλίσκομαι, συναυλίζομαι, συναλίζομαι",
    4872: "συναναβαίνω (synanabaínō | soon-an-ab-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G305\nStrong's: to ascend in company with\nKJV: —come up with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G305 \n\nCognate Group: G1811 (follow), G3877 (attain), G1872 (follow), G4870 (follow), G190 (follow)\nVariants: συναναβαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5927 עָלָה",
    4873: "συνανάκειμαι (synanákeimai | soon-an-ak'-i-mahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G345\nStrong's: to recline in company with (at a meal)\nKJV: —sit (down, at the table, together) with (at meat).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G345 \n\nCognate Group: G4029 (be bound with), G4295 (be first), G1945 (impose), G606 (be appointed), G4873 (sit with), G345 (guest), G480 (adversary), G3873 (be present), G2749 (be appointed), G2621 (keep)\nVariants: συνανάκειμαι, σύγκειμαι",
    4874: "συναναμίγνυμι (synanamígnymi | soon-an-am-ig'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a compound of G303 and G3396\nStrong's: to mix up together, i.e. (figurative) associate with\nKJV: —(have, keep) company (with).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G303 \nSee: G3396 \n\nCognate Group: G4874 (have company with)\nVariants: συναναμίγνυμι, συναναμείγνυμι",
    4875: "συναναπαύομαι (synanapaúomai | soon-an-ap-ow'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle from G4862 and G373\nStrong's: to recruit oneself in company with\nKJV: —refresh with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G373 \n\nCognate Group: G4874 (have company with)\nVariants: συναναπαύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H7257 רָבַץ",
    4876: "συναντάω (synantáō | soon-an-tah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of G473\nStrong's: to meet with; figuratively, to occur\nKJV: —befall, meet.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G473 \n\nCognate Group: G4874 (have company with)\nVariants: συναντάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H4136 מוּל, H4672 מָצָא, H5060 נָגַע, H5381 נָשַׂג, H6293 פָּגַע, H6298 פָּגַשׁ, H6908 קָבַץ, H6923 קָדַם, H7121 קָרָא, H7136 קָרָה",
    4877: "συνάντησις (synántēsis | soon-an'-tay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G4876\nStrong's: a meeting with\nKJV: —meet.\n\nSee: G4876 \n\nCognate Group: G4874 (have company with)\nVariants: συνάντησις G5268",
    4878: "συναντιλαμβάνομαι (synantilambánomai | soon-an-tee-lam-ban'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G482\nStrong's: to take hold of opposite together, i.e. co-operate (assist)\nKJV: —help.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G482 \n\nCognate Group: G4878 (help), G484 (help), G482 (help)\nVariants: συναντιλαμβάνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3559 כּוּן, H5375 נָשָׂא",
    4879: "συναπάγω (synapágō | soon-ap-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G520\nStrong's: to take off together, i.e. transport with (seduce, passively, yield)\nKJV: —carry (lead) away with, condescend.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G520 \n\nCognate Group: G4878 (help), G484 (help), G482 (help)\nVariants: συναπάγω, συναπάγομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3947 לָקַח",
    4880: "συναποθνήσκω (synapothnḗskō | soon-ap-oth-nace'-ko)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G599\nStrong's: to decease (literally) in company with, or (figuratively), similarly to\nKJV: —be dead (die) with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G599 \n\nCognate Group: G4880 (be dead with), G2289 (become dead), G2348 (be dead), G2288 (X deadly), G599 (be dead), G2349 (mortal), G110 (immortality)\nVariants: συναποθνήσκω, συναποθνῄσκω",
    4881: "συναπόλλυμι (synapóllymi | soon-ap-ol'-loo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G622\nStrong's: to destroy (middle voice or passively, be slain) in company with\nKJV: —perish with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G622 \n\nCognate Group: G4880 (be dead with), G2289 (become dead), G2348 (be dead), G2288 (X deadly), G599 (be dead), G2349 (mortal), G110 (immortality)\nVariants: συναπόλλυμι, συναπόλλυμαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H5595 סָפָה, H6992 קְטַל",
    4882: "συναποστέλλω (synapostéllō | soon-ap-os-tel'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G649\nStrong's: to despatch (on an errand) in company with\nKJV: —send with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G649 \n\nCognate Group: G4880 (be dead with), G2289 (become dead), G2348 (be dead), G2288 (X deadly), G599 (be dead), G2349 (mortal), G110 (immortality)\nVariants: συναποστέλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7971 שָׁלַח",
    4883: "συναρμολογέω (synarmologéō | soon-ar-mol-og-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of a compound of G719 and G3004 (in its original sense of laying)\nStrong's: to render close-jointed together, i.e. organize compactly\nKJV: —be fitly framed (joined) together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G719 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G4883 (be fitly framed together)\nVariants: συναρμολογέω, συναρμολογέομαι",
    4884: "συναρπάζω (synarpázō | soon-ar-pad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G726\nStrong's: to snatch together, i.e. seize\nKJV: —catch.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G726 \n\nCognate Group: G4883 (be fitly framed together)\nVariants: συναρπάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3947 לָקַח",
    4885: "συναυξάνω (synauxánō | soon-owx-an'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G837\nStrong's: to increase (grow up) together\nKJV: —grow together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G837 \n\nCognate Group: G4883 (be fitly framed together)\nVariants: συναυξάνω, συναυξάνομαι",
    4886: "σύνδεσμος (sýndesmos | soon'-des-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1199\nStrong's: a joint tie, i.e. ligament, (figuratively) uniting principle, control\nKJV: —band, bond.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1199 \n\nCognate Group: G4886 (band)\nVariants: σύνδεσμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1004 בַּיִת, H2784 חַרְצֻבָּה, H4133 מֹוטָה, H5462 סָגַר, H7001 קְטַר, H7195 קֶשֶׁר",
    4887: "συνδέω (syndéō | soon-deh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1210\nStrong's: to bind with, i.e. (passively) be a fellow-prisoner (figuratively)\nKJV: —be bound with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1210 \n\nCognate Group: G4886 (band)\nVariants: συνδέω, συνδέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1692 דָּבַק, H6437 פָּנָה, H7194 קָשַׁר, H7197 קָשַׁשׁ, H7660 שָׁבַץ, H8628 תָּקַע",
    4888: "συνδοξάζω (syndoxázō | soon-dox-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1392\nStrong's: to exalt to dignity in company (i.e. similarly) with\nKJV: —glorify together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1392 \n\nCognate Group: G1391 (dignity), G3861 (strange), G1741 (glorious), G1740 (glorify), G4888 (glorify together), G1392 (glorify), G1380 (be accounted)\nVariants: συνδοξάζω, συνδοξάζομαι",
    4889: "σύνδουλος (sýndoulos | soon'-doo-los)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1401\nStrong's: a co-slave, i.e. servitor or ministrant of the same master (human or divine)\nKJV: —fellowservant.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1401 \n\nCognate Group: G1399 (handmaid), G4889 (fellowservant), G1402 (bring into bondage), G1397 (bondage), G1396 (bring into subjection), G1401 (bond), G2615 (bring into bondage), G3787 (eye-service), G1398 (be in bondage)\nVariants: σύνδουλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3674 כְּנָת",
    4890: "συνδρομή (syndromḗ | soon-drom-ay')\n\nDerivation: from (the alternate of) G4936\nStrong's: a running together, i.e. (riotous) concourse\nKJV: —run together.\n\nSee: G4936 \n\nCognate Group: G1399 (handmaid), G4889 (fellowservant), G1402 (bring into bondage), G1397 (bondage), G1396 (bring into subjection), G1401 (bond), G2615 (bring into bondage), G3787 (eye-service), G1398 (be in bondage)\nVariants: συνδρομή",
    4891: "συνεγείρω (synegeírō | soon-eg-i'-ro)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1453\nStrong's: to rouse (from death) in company with, i.e. (figuratively) to revivify (spirtually) in resemblance to\nKJV: —raise up together, rise with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1453 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συνεγείρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5782 עוּר, H5800 עָזַב",
    4892: "συνέδριον (synédrion | soon-ed'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of a compound of G4862 and the base of G1476\nStrong's: a joint session, i.e. (specially), the Jewish Sanhedrin; by analogy, a subordinate tribunal\nKJV: —council.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1476 \n\nCognate Group: G4892 (council)\nVariants: συνέδριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1779 דִּין, H4193 מֹות, H5475 סֹוד, H6951 קָהָל",
    4893: "συνείδησις (syneídēsis | soon-i'-day-sis)\n\nDerivation: from a prolonged form of G4894\nStrong's: co-perception, i.e. moral consciousness\nKJV: —conscience.\n\nSee: G4894 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: συνείδησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4093 מַדָּע",
    4894: "συνείδω (syneídō | soon-i'-do)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1492\nStrong's: to see completely; used (like its primary) only in two past tenses, respectively meaning to understand or become aware, and to be conscious or (clandestinely) informed of\nKJV: —consider, know, be privy, be ware of.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1492 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: συνείδω, σύνοιδα, συνοράω",
    4895: "σύνειμι (sýneimi | soon'-i-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1510 (including its various inflections)\nStrong's: to be in company with, i.e. present at the time\nKJV: —be with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1510 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: σύνειμι, σύνειμι1 \nHebrew Equivalents: H7453 רֵעַ",
    4896: "σύνειμι (sýneimi | soon'-i-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and εἶμι (to go)\nStrong's: to assemble\nKJV: —gather together.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: σύνειμι, σύνειμι2",
    4897: "συνεισέρχομαι (syneisérchomai | soon-ice-er'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1525\nStrong's: to enter in company with\nKJV: —go in with, go with into.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1525 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: συνεισέρχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא",
    4898: "συνέκδημος (synékdēmos | soon-ek'-day-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and the base of G1553\nStrong's: a co-absentee from home, i.e. fellow-traveller\nKJV: —companion in travel, travel with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1553 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: συνέκδημος",
    4899: "συνεκλεκτός (syneklektós | soon-ek-lek-tos')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4862 and G1586\nStrong's: chosen in company with, i.e. co-elect (fellow Christian)\nKJV: —elected together with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1586 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: συνεκλεκτός",
    4900: "συνελαύνω (synelaúnō | soon-el-ow'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1643\nStrong's: to drive together, i.e. (figuratively) exhort (to reconciliation)\nKJV: —+ set at one again.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1643 \n\nCognate Group: G4894 (consider), G4893 (conscience)\nVariants: συνελαύνω, συναλλάσσω",
    4901: "συνεπιμαρτυρέω (synepimartyréō | soon-ep-ee-mar-too-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G1957\nStrong's: to testify further jointly, i.e. unite in adding evidence\nKJV: —also bear witness.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G1957 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: συνεπιμαρτυρέω",
    4902: "συνέπομαι (synépomai | soon-ep'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4862 and a primary ἕπω (to follow)\nStrong's: to attend (travel) in company with\nKJV: —accompany.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: συνέπομαι",
    4903: "συνεργέω (synergéō | soon-erg-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4904\nStrong's: to be a fellow-worker, i.e. co-operate\nKJV: —help (work) with, work(-er) together.\n\nSee: G4904 \n\nCognate Group: G4903 (help with), G4904 (companion in labour)\nVariants: συνεργέω",
    4904: "συνεργός (synergós | soon-er-gos')\n\nDerivation: from a presumed compound of G4862 and the base of G2041\nStrong's: a co-laborer, i.e. coadjutor\nKJV: —companion in labour, (fellow-)helper(-labourer, -worker), labourer together with, workfellow.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G4903 (help with), G4904 (companion in labour)\nVariants: συνεργός",
    4905: "συνέρχομαι (synérchomai | soon-er'-khom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2064\nStrong's: to convene, depart in company with, associate with, or (specially), cohabit (conjugally)\nKJV: —accompany, assemble (with), come (together), come (company, go) with, resort.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2064 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: συνέρχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H935 בֹּוא, H1980 הָלַךְ, H2263 חָבַק, H3259 יָעַד, H6298 פָּגַשׁ, H6908 קָבַץ",
    4906: "συνεσθίω (synesthíō | soon-es-thee'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2068 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to take food in company with\nKJV: —eat with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2068 \n\nCognate Group: G1330 (come), G565 (come), G1525 (X arise), G4905 (accompany), G4281 (go before), G4022 (fetch a compass), G2064 (accompany), G1904 (come), G4334 (come), G1831 (come), G3928 (come), G3922 (come in privily)\nVariants: συνεσθίω \nHebrew Equivalents: H398 אָכַל, H1262 בָּרָה",
    4907: "σύνεσις (sýnesis | soon'-es-is)\n\nDerivation: from G4920\nStrong's: a mental putting together, i.e. intelligence or (concretely) the intellect\nKJV: —knowledge, understanding.\n\nSee: G4920 \n\nCognate Group: G801 (foolish), G4907 (knowledge), G4920 (consider), G4908 (prudent)\nVariants: σύνεσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H999 בִּינָה, H1369 גְּבוּרָה, H1847 דַּעַת, H2451 חׇכְמָה, H2940 טַעַם, H4906 מַשְׂכִּית, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    4908: "συνετός (synetós | soon-et'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4920\nStrong's: mentally put (or putting) together, i.e. sagacious\nKJV: —prudent. Compare G5429 .\n\nSee: G4920 \nSee: G5429 \n\nCognate Group: G801 (foolish), G4907 (knowledge), G4920 (consider), G4908 (prudent)\nVariants: συνετός \nHebrew Equivalents: H376 אִישׁ, H995 בִּין, H2450 חָכָם, H2450 חָכָם, H3045 יָדַע, H5082 נְדִיבָה, H6175 עָרוּם, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    4909: "συνευδοκέω (syneudokéō | soon-yoo-dok-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2106\nStrong's: to think well of in common, i.e. assent to, feel gratified with\nKJV: —allow, assent, be pleased, have pleasure.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2106 \n\nCognate Group: G801 (foolish), G4907 (knowledge), G4920 (consider), G4908 (prudent)\nVariants: συνευδοκέω",
    4910: "συνευωχέω (syneuōchéō | soon-yoo-o-kheh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of a presumed compound of G2095 and a derivative of G2192 (meaning to be in good condition, i.e. (by implication) to fare well, or feast)\nStrong's: to entertain sumptuously in company with, i.e. (middle voice or passive) to revel together\nKJV: —feast with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2095 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G801 (foolish), G4907 (knowledge), G4920 (consider), G4908 (prudent)\nVariants: συνευωχέω, συνευωχέομαι",
    4911: "συνεφίστημι (synephístēmi | soon-ef-is'-tay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2186\nStrong's: to stand up together, i.e. to resist (or assault) jointly\nKJV: —rise up together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2186 \n\nCognate Group: G801 (foolish), G4907 (knowledge), G4920 (consider), G4908 (prudent)\nVariants: συνεφίστημι, συνεπίστημι",
    4912: "συνέχω (synéchō | soon-ekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2192\nStrong's: to hold together, i.e. to compress (the ears, with a crowd or siege) or arrest (a prisoner); figuratively, to compel, perplex, afflict, preoccupy\nKJV: —constrain, hold, keep in, press, lie sick of, stop, be in a strait, straiten, be taken with, throng.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G4912 (constrain), G4928 (anguish)\nVariants: συνέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H270 אָחַז, H1121 בֵּן, H2263 חָבַק, H2266 חָבַר, H2388 חָזַק, H3557 כּוּל, H3607 כָּלָא, H3920 לָכַד, H4513 מָנַע, H5674 עָבַר, H5956 עָלַם, H6113 עָצָר, H6279 עָתַר, H6616 פָּתִיל, H6696 צוּר, H6887 צָרַר, H7092 קָפַץ",
    4913: "συνήδομαι (synḗdomai | soon-ay'-dom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4862 and the base of G2237\nStrong's: to rejoice in with oneself, i.e. feel satisfaction concerning\nKJV: —delight.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2237 \n\nCognate Group: G4912 (constrain), G4928 (anguish)\nVariants: συνήδομαι",
    4914: "συνήθεια (synḗtheia | soon-ay'-thi-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4862 and G2239\nStrong's: mutual habituation, i.e. usage\nKJV: —custom.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2239 \n\nCognate Group: G4912 (constrain), G4928 (anguish)\nVariants: συνήθεια",
    4915: "συνηλικιώτης (synēlikiṓtēs | soon-ay-lik-ee-o'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of G2244\nStrong's: a co-aged person, i.e. alike in years\nKJV: —equal.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2244 \n\nCognate Group: G4912 (constrain), G4928 (anguish)\nVariants: συνηλικιώτης",
    4916: "συνθάπτω (syntháptō | soon-thap'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2290\nStrong's: to inter in company with, i.e. (figuratively) to assimilate spiritually (to Christ by a sepulture as to sin)\nKJV: —bury with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2290 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συνθάπτω, συνθάπτομαι",
    4917: "συνθλάω (synthláō | soon-thlah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and thlao (to crush)\nStrong's: to dash together, i.e. shatter\nKJV: —break.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συνθλάω, συνθλάομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1438 גָּדַע, H4272 מָחַץ, H5365 נָקַר, H5422 נָתַץ, H6476 פָּצַח, H7533 רָצַץ",
    4918: "συνθλίβω (synthlíbō | soon-thlee'-bo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2346\nStrong's: to compress, i.e. crowd on all sides\nKJV: —throng.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2346 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συνθλίβω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7533 רָצַץ",
    4919: "συνθρύπτω (synthrýptō | soon-throop'-to)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and θρύπτω (to crumble)\nStrong's: to crush together, i.e. (figuratively) to dispirit\nKJV: —break.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συνθρύπτω",
    4920: "συνίημι (syníēmi | soon-ee'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and ἵημι (to send)\nStrong's: to put together, i.e. (mentally) to comprehend; by implication, to act piously\nKJV: — consider, understand, be wise.\n\nSee: G4862 \n\nCognate Group: G801 (foolish), G4907 (knowledge), G4920 (consider), G4908 (prudent)\nVariants: συνίημι, συνίω, συνίημι/συνίω, σύνιημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2363 חוּשׁ, H3045 יָדַע, H3559 כּוּן, H7200 רָאָה, H7919 שָׂכַל, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    4921: "συνιστάω (synistáō | soon-is-tah'-o), or (strengthened) συνιστάνω (synistánō | soon-is-tan'-o), or συνίστημι (synístēmi | soon-is'-tay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G2476 (including its collateral forms)\nStrong's: to set together, i.e. (by implication) to introduce (favorably), or (figuratively) to exhibit; intransitively, to stand near, or (figuratively) to constitute\nKJV: —approve, commend, consist, make, stand (with).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G4921 (approve)\nVariants: συνιστάω, συνίστημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H631 אָסַר, H2856 חָתַם, H3369 יָקֹשׁ, H3559 כּוּן, H4723 מִקְוֶה, H5647 עָבַד, H5975 עָמַד, H6485 פָּקַד, H6680 צָוָה, H6950 קָהַל, H7971 שָׁלַח",
    4922: "συνοδεύω (synodeúō | soon-od-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3593\nStrong's: to travel in company with\nKJV: —journey with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3593 \n\nCognate Group: G4921 (approve)\nVariants: συνοδεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1980 הָלַךְ",
    4923: "συνοδία (synodía | soon-od-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4862 and G3598 (\"synod\")\nStrong's: companionship on a journey, i.e. (by implication), a caravan\nKJV: —company.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3598 \n\nCognate Group: G4921 (approve)\nVariants: συνοδία \nHebrew Equivalents: H3187 יָחַשׂ",
    4924: "συνοικέω (synoikéō | soon-oy-keh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3611\nStrong's: to reside together (as a family)\nKJV: —dwell together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3611 \n\nCognate Group: G4921 (approve)\nVariants: συνοικέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H1167 בַּעַל, H2992 יָבַם",
    4925: "συνοικοδομέω (synoikodoméō | soon-oy-kod-om-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3618\nStrong's: to construct, i.e. (passively) to compose (in company with other Christians, figuratively)\nKJV: —build together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3618 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: συνοικοδομέω",
    4926: "συνομιλέω (synomiléō | soon-om-il-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G3656\nStrong's: to converse mutually\nKJV: —talk with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3656 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: συνομιλέω",
    4927: "συνομορέω (synomoréō | soon-om-or-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of a compound of the base of 3674 and the base of G3725\nStrong's: to border together, i.e. adjoin\nKJV: —join hard.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3725 \n\nCognate Group: G3618 (build), G2733 (habitation), G3614 (home), G3617 (goodman), G3619 (building), G3616 (guide the house), G2731 (dwelling), G3622 (dispensation), G3615 (they of household), G3613 (habitation), G4925 (build together), G2732 (habitation), G3624 (home), G3611 (dwell), G3620 (edifying), G3621 (be steward), G2026 (build thereon), G3609 (of the house), G3623 (chamberlain)\nVariants: συνομορέω",
    4928: "συνοχή (synochḗ | soon-okh-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4912\nStrong's: restraint, i.e. (figuratively) anxiety\nKJV: —anguish, distress.\n\nSee: G4912 \n\nCognate Group: G4912 (constrain), G4928 (anguish)\nVariants: συνοχή \nHebrew Equivalents: H4692 מָצֹור, H7722 שֹׁוא",
    4929: "συντάσσω (syntássō | soon-tas-so)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5021\nStrong's: to arrange jointly, i.e. (figuratively) to direct\nKJV: —appoint.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5021 \n\nCognate Group: G4912 (constrain), G4928 (anguish)\nVariants: συντάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H1696 דָבַר, H2266 חָבַר, H2963 טָרַף, H3259 יָעַד, H3335 יָצַר, H6485 פָּקַד, H6680 צָוָה",
    4930: "συντέλεια (syntéleia | soon-tel'-i-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G4931\nStrong's: entire completion, i.e. consummation (of a dispensation)\nKJV: —end.\n\nSee: G4931 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: συντέλεια",
    4931: "συντελέω (synteléō | soon-tel-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5055\nStrong's: to complete entirely; generally, to execute (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —end, finish, fulfil, make.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5055 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: συντελέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H1086 בָּלָה, H1214 בָּצַע, H1214 בָּצַע, H1584 גָּמַר, H1639 גָּרַע, H2856 חָתַם, H3605 כֹּל, H3615 כָּלָה, H3772 כָּרַת, H4191 מוּת, H4390 מָלֵא, H5362 נָקַף, H5486 סוּף, H6213 עָשָׂה, H7489 רָעַע, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8552 תָּמַם",
    4932: "συντέμνω (syntémnō | soon-tem'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and the base of G5114\nStrong's: to contract by cutting, i.e. (figuratively) do concisely (speedily)\nKJV: —(cut) short.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5114 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: συντέμνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2782 חָרַץ, H2852 חָתַךְ, H3615 כָּלָה",
    4933: "συντηρέω (syntēréō | soon-tay-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5083\nStrong's: to keep closely together, i.e. (by implication) to conserve (from ruin); mentally, to remember (and obey)\nKJV: —keep, observe, preserve.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5083 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: συντηρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5426 נְתַר, H7011 קַיָּם, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    4934: "συντίθεμαι (syntíthemai | soon-tith'-em-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G4862 and G5087\nStrong's: to place jointly, i.e. (figuratively) to consent (bargain, stipulate), concur\nKJV: —agree, assent, covenant.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: συντίθεμαι, συνεπιτίθημι, συντίθημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2164 זְמַן, H7194 קָשַׁר, H8003 שָׁלֵם",
    4935: "συντόμως (syntómōs | soon-tom'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a derivative of G4932\nStrong's: concisely (briefly)\nKJV: —a few words.\n\nSee: G4932 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: συντόμως \nHebrew Equivalents: H1094 בְּלֹוא",
    4936: "συντρέχω (syntréchō | soon-trekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5143 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to rush together (hastily assemble) or headlong (figuratively)\nKJV: —run (together, with).\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5143 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: συντρέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7521 רָצָה",
    4937: "συντρίβω (syntríbō | soon-tree'-bo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and the base of G5147\nStrong's: to crush completely, i.e. to shatter (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —break (in pieces), broken to shivers (+ -hearted), bruise.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5147 \n\nCognate Group: G4937 (break), G4938 (destruction)\nVariants: συντρίβω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1438 גָּדַע, H1792 דָּכָא, H2040 הָרַס, H2116 זוּרֶה, H2342 חוּל, H2865 חָתַת, H2963 טָרַף, H3782 כָּשַׁל, H3807 כָּתַת, H5062 נָגַף, H5221 נָכָה, H5310 נָפַץ, H6481 פָּצַע, H6760 צָלַע, H7164 קָרַס, H7376 רָטָשׁ, H7429 רָמַס, H7533 רָצַץ, H7665 שָׁבַר, H7673 שָׁבַת, H7857 שָׁטַף, H8045 שָׁמַד, H8074 שָׁמֵם, H8156 שָׁסַע, H8406 תְּבַר",
    4938: "σύντριμμα (sýntrimma | soon-trim'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4937\nStrong's: concussion or utter fracture (properly, concretely), i.e. complete ruin\nKJV: —destruction.\n\nSee: G4937 \n\nCognate Group: G4937 (break), G4938 (destruction)\nVariants: σύντριμμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6094 עַצֶּבֶת, H6481 פָּצַע, H7667 שֶׁבֶר, H7701 שֹׁד",
    4939: "σύντροφος (sýntrophos | soon'-trof-os)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5162 (in a passive sense)\nStrong's: a fellow-nursling, i.e. comrade\nKJV: —brought up with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5162 \n\nCognate Group: G4937 (break), G4938 (destruction)\nVariants: σύντροφος",
    4940: "συντυγχάνω (syntynchánō | soon-toong-khan'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5177\nStrong's: to chance together, i.e. meet with (reach)\nKJV: —come at.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5177 \n\nCognate Group: G4937 (break), G4938 (destruction)\nVariants: συντυγχάνω",
    4941: "Συντύχη (Syntýchē | soon-too'-khay)\n\nDerivation: from G4940\nStrong's: an accident; Syntyche, a Christian female\nKJV: —Syntyche.\n\nSee: G4940 \n\nCognate Group: G4937 (break), G4938 (destruction)\nVariants: Συντύχη",
    4942: "συνυποκρίνομαι (synypokrínomai | soon-oo-pok-rin'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5271\nStrong's: to act hypocritically in concert with\nKJV: —dissemble with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5271 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: συνυποκρίνομαι",
    4943: "συνυπουργέω (synypourgéō | soon-oop-oorg-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of a compound of G5259 and the base of G2041\nStrong's: to be a co-auxiliary, i.e. assist\nKJV: —help together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2041 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: συνυπουργέω",
    4944: "συνωδίνω (synōdínō | soon-o-dee'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G5605\nStrong's: to have (parturition) pangs in company (concert, simultaneously) with, i.e. (figuratively) to sympathize (in expectation of relief from suffering)\nKJV: —travail in pain together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G5605 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: συνωδίνω",
    4945: "συνωμοσία (synōmosía | soon-o-mos-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4862 and G3660\nStrong's: a swearing together, i.e. (by implication) a plot\nKJV: —comspiracy.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G3660 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: συνωμοσία",
    4946: "Συράκουσαι (Syrákousai | soo-rak'-oo-sahee)\n\nDerivation: plural of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Syracuse, the capital of Sicily\nKJV: —Syracuse.\n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: Συράκουσαι",
    4947: "Συρία (Syría | soo-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew origin (H6865)\nStrong's: Syria (i.e. Tsyria or Tyre), a region of Asia\nKJV: —Syria.\nSee: H6865\n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: Συρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H6865 צֹר",
    4948: "Σύρος (Sýros | soo'-ros)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G4947\nStrong's: a Syran (i.e. probably Tyrian), a native of Syria\nKJV: —Syrian.\n\nSee: G4947 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: Σύρος",
    4949: "Συροφοίνισσα (Syrophoínissa | soo-rof-oy'-nis-sah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a compound of G4948 and the same as G5403\nStrong's: a Syro-phœnician woman, i.e. a female native of Phœnicia in Syria\nKJV: —Syrophenician.\n\nSee: G4948 \nSee: G5403 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: Συροφοίνισσα, Σύρα, Συροφοινίκισσα, Φοινίκισσα",
    4950: "σύρτις (sýrtis | soor'-tis)\n\nDerivation: from G4951\nStrong's: a shoal (from the sand drawn thither by the waves), i.e. the Syrtis Major or great bay on the north coast of Africa\nKJV: —quicksands.\n\nSee: G4951 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: σύρτις, Σύρτις",
    4951: "σύρω (sýrō | soo'-ro)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G138\nStrong's: to trail\nKJV: —drag, draw, hale.\n\nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: σύρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2119 זָחַל, H5498 סָחַב, H7857 שָׁטַף",
    4952: "συσπαράσσω (sysparássō | soos-par-as'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4682\nStrong's: to rend completely, i.e. (by analogy) to convulse violently\nKJV: —throw down.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4682 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: συσπαράσσω, συνταράσσω",
    4953: "σύσσημον (sýssēmon | soos'-say-mon)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G4862 and the base of G4591\nStrong's: a sign in common, i.e. preconcerted signal\nKJV: —token.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4591 \n\nCognate Group: G4593 (note), G4953 (token), G2154 (easy to be understood), G4591 (signify), G4592 (miracle), G1978 (notable), G767 (mean)\nVariants: σύσσημον \nHebrew Equivalents: H4864 מַשְׂאֵת, H5251 נֵס",
    4954: "σύσσωμος (sýssōmos | soos'-so-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4983\nStrong's: of a joint body, i.e. (figuratively) a fellow-member of the Christian community\nKJV: —of the same body.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4983 \n\nCognate Group: G4985 (bodily), G4984 (bodily), G4983 (bodily), G4954 (of the same body)\nVariants: σύσσωμος",
    4955: "συστασιαστής (systasiastḗs | soos-tas-ee-as-tace')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4862 and a derivative of G4714\nStrong's: a fellow-insurgent\nKJV: —make insurrection with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4714 \n\nCognate Group: G4955 (make insurrection with), G4714 (dissension)\nVariants: συστασιαστής, στασιαστής",
    4956: "συστατικός (systatikós | soos-tat-ee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G4921\nStrong's: introductory, i.e. recommendatory\nKJV: —of commendation.\n\nSee: G4921 \n\nCognate Group: G4955 (make insurrection with), G4714 (dissension)\nVariants: συστατικός",
    4957: "συσταυρόω (systauróō | soos-tow-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4717\nStrong's: to impale in company with (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —crucify with.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4717 \n\nCognate Group: G4862 (beside), G4776 (sit together), G4855 (spring up with), G4916 (bury with), G4789 (fellow -heir), G4833 (make conformable unto), G4800 (live with), G4957 (crucify with), G4888 (glorify together), G4854 (planted together), G4880 (be dead with), G4821 (reign with), G4891 (raise up together), G4841 (suffer with), G4806 (quicken together with), G4832 (conformed to)\nVariants: συσταυρόω, συσταυρόομαι",
    4958: "συστέλλω (systéllō | soos-tel'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4724\nStrong's: to send (draw) together, i.e. enwrap (enshroud a corpse for burial), contract (an interval)\nKJV: — short, wind up.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4724 \n\nCognate Group: G5288 (draw back), G1291 (charge), G4958 (short), G2689 (apparel), G2687 (appease), G1992 (\"epistle), G1989 (write), G5289 (draw back), G1293 (difference), G4724 (avoid)\nVariants: συστέλλω",
    4959: "συστενάζω (systenázō | soos-ten-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4727\nStrong's: to moan jointly, i.e. (figuratively) experience a common calamity\nKJV: —groan together.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4727 \n\nCognate Group: G4959 (groan together), G4727 (with grief), G4726 (groaning)\nVariants: συστενάζω",
    4960: "συστοιχέω (systoichéō | soos-toy-kheh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4748\nStrong's: to file together (as soldiers in ranks), i.e. (figuratively) to correspond to\nKJV: —answer to.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4748 \n\nCognate Group: G4747 (element), G4748 (walk), G4960 (answer to)\nVariants: συστοιχέω",
    4961: "συστρατιώτης (systratiṓtēs | soos-trat-ee-o'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4757\nStrong's: a co-campaigner, i.e. (figuratively) an associate in Christian toil\nKJV: —fellowsoldier.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4757 \n\nCognate Group: G4747 (element), G4748 (walk), G4960 (answer to)\nVariants: συστρατιώτης",
    4962: "συστρέφω (systréphō | soos-tref'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and G4762\nStrong's: to twist together, i.e. collect (a bundle, a crowd)\nKJV: —gather.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4762 \n\nCognate Group: G4747 (element), G4748 (walk), G4960 (answer to)\nVariants: συστρέφω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1980 הָלַךְ, H2342 חוּל, H3648 כָּמַר, H3950 לָקַט, H6887 צָרַר, H6908 קָבַץ, H7194 קָשַׁר",
    4963: "συστροφή (systrophḗ | soos-trof-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4962\nStrong's: a twisting together, i.e. (figuratively) a secret coalition, riotous crowd\nKJV: —+ band together, concourse.\n\nSee: G4962 \n\nCognate Group: G4747 (element), G4748 (walk), G4960 (answer to)\nVariants: συστροφή \nHebrew Equivalents: H5475 סֹוד, H5712 עֵדָה, H6887 צָרַר, H7194 קָשַׁר, H7195 קֶשֶׁר",
    4964: "συσχηματίζω (syschēmatízō | soos-khay-mat-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4862 and a derivative of G4976\nStrong's: to fashion alike, i.e. conform to the same pattern (figuratively)\nKJV: —conform to, fashion self according to.\n\nSee: G4862 \nSee: G4976 \n\nCognate Group: G4976 (fashion), G3345 (transfer), G4964 (conform to)\nVariants: συσχηματίζω, συσχηματίζομαι",
    4965: "Συχάρ (Sychár | soo-khar')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7941)\nStrong's: Sychar (i.e. Shekar), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Sychar.\nSee: H7941\n\nCognate Group: G4976 (fashion), G3345 (transfer), G4964 (conform to)\nVariants: Συχάρ, Σιχάρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7941 שֵׁכָר",
    4966: "Συχέμ (Sychém | soo-khem')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H7927)\nStrong's: Sychem (i.e. Shekem), the name of a Canaanite and of a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Sychem.\nSee: H7927\n\nCognate Group: G4976 (fashion), G3345 (transfer), G4964 (conform to)\nVariants: Συχέμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H7926 שְׁכֶם, H7927 שְׁכֶם",
    4967: "σφαγή (sphagḗ | sfag-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G4969\nStrong's: butchery (of animals for food or sacrifice, or (figuratively) of men (destruction))\nKJV: —slaughter.\n\nSee: G4969 \n\nCognate Group: G4968 (slain beast), G4967 (slaughter), G4969 (kill), G2695 (slay)\nVariants: σφαγή \nHebrew Equivalents: H2026 הָרַג, H2028 הֲרֵגָה, H2719 חֶרֶב, H2873 טָבַח, H3589 כִּיד, H6993 קֶטֶל",
    4968: "σφάγιον (sphágion | sfag'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of G4967\nStrong's: a victim (in sacrifice)\nKJV: —slain beast.\n\nSee: G4967 \n\nCognate Group: G4968 (slain beast), G4967 (slaughter), G4969 (kill), G2695 (slay)\nVariants: σφάγιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2113 זְוָעָה, H2874 טֶבַח, H5071 נְדָבָה",
    4969: "σφάζω (spházō | sfad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to butcher (especially an animal for food or in sacrifice) or (generally) to slaughter, or (specially), to maim (violently)\nKJV: —kill, slay, wound.\n\nCognate Group: G4968 (slain beast), G4967 (slaughter), G4969 (kill), G2695 (slay)\nVariants: σφάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2026 הָרַג, H2076 זָבַח, H2873 טָבַח, H7819 שָׁחַט, H8158 שָׁסַף",
    4970: "σφόδρα (sphódra | sfod'-rah)\n\nDerivation: neuter plural of σφοδρός \nStrong's: (violent; of uncertain derivation) as adverb; vehemently, i.e. in a high degree, much\nKJV: — exceeding(-ly), greatly, sore, very.\n\nCognate Group: G4968 (slain beast), G4967 (slaughter), G4969 (kill), G2695 (slay)\nVariants: σφόδρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H3190 יָטַב, H3966 מְאֹד, H4390 מָלֵא, H5704 עַד, H5704 עַד, H7690 שַׂגִּיא",
    4971: "σφοδρῶς (sphodrōs | sfod-roce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from the same as G4970\nStrong's: very much\nKJV: —exceedingly.\n\nSee: G4970 \n\nCognate Group: G4968 (slain beast), G4967 (slaughter), G4969 (kill), G2695 (slay)\nVariants: σφοδρῶς",
    4972: "σφραγίζω (sphragízō | sfrag-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4973\nStrong's: to stamp (with a signet or private mark) for security or preservation (literally or figuratively); by implication, to keep secret, to attest\nKJV: —(set a, set to) seal up, stop.\n\nSee: G4973 \n\nCognate Group: G2696 (seal), G4973 (seal), G4972 (seal up)\nVariants: σφραγίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2856 חָתַם, H5640 סָתַם, H8552 תָּמַם",
    4973: "σφραγίς (sphragís | sfrag-ece')\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from G5420\nStrong's: a signet (as fencing in or protecting from misappropriation); by implication, the stamp impressed (as a mark of privacy, or genuineness), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —seal.\n\nSee: G5420 \n\nCognate Group: G2696 (seal), G4973 (seal), G4972 (seal up)\nVariants: σφραγίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H2368 חֹותָם, H2397 חָח",
    4974: "σφυρόν (sphyrón | sfoo-ron')\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative probably of the same as σφαῖρα (a ball, \"sphere\"\nStrong's: compare the feminine σφῦρα, a hammer); the ankle (as globular)\nKJV: —ancle bone.\n\nCognate Group: G2696 (seal), G4973 (seal), G4972 (seal up)\nVariants: σφυρόν, σφυδρόν \nHebrew Equivalents: H8455 תֹּותָח",
    4975: "σχεδόν (schedón | skhed-on')\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of the alternate of G2192 as adverb\nStrong's: nigh, i.e. nearly\nKJV: —almost.\n\nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G2696 (seal), G4973 (seal), G4972 (seal up)\nVariants: σχεδόν",
    4976: "σχῆμα (schēma | skhay'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from the alternate of G2192\nStrong's: a figure (as a mode or circumstance), i.e. (by implication) external condition\nKJV: —fashion.\n\nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G4976 (fashion), G3345 (transfer), G4964 (conform to)\nVariants: σχῆμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6596 פֹּת",
    4977: "σχίζω (schízō | skhid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to split or sever (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —break, divide, open, rend, make a rent.\n\nCognate Group: G4977 (break), G4978 (division)\nVariants: σχίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1234 בָּקַע, H7121 קָרָא",
    4978: "σχίσμα (schísma | skhis'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4977\nStrong's: a split or gap (\"schism\"), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —division, rent, schism.\n\nSee: G4977 \n\nCognate Group: G4977 (break), G4978 (division)\nVariants: σχίσμα",
    4979: "σχοινίον (schoiníon | skhoy-nee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of σχοῖνος \nStrong's: (a rush or flag-plant; of uncertain derivation); a rushlet, i.e. grass-withe or tie (generally)\nKJV: —small cord, rope.\n\nCognate Group: G4977 (break), G4978 (division)\nVariants: σχοινίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2256 חֶבֶל, H2256 חֶבֶל, H5364 נִקְפָּה",
    4980: "σχολάζω (scholázō | skhol-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4981\nStrong's: to take a holiday, i.e. be at leisure for (by implication, devote oneself wholly to); figuratively, to be vacant (of a house)\nKJV: —empty, give self.\n\nSee: G4981 \n\nCognate Group: G4977 (break), G4978 (division)\nVariants: σχολάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7503 רָפָה",
    4981: "σχολή (scholḗ | skhol-ay')\n\nDerivation: probably feminine of a presumed derivative of the alternate of G2192\nStrong's: properly, loitering (as a withholding of oneself from work) or leisure, i.e. (by implication) a \"school\" (as vacation from physical employment)\nKJV: —school.\n\nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G4977 (break), G4978 (division)\nVariants: σχολή \nHebrew Equivalents: H328 אַט",
    4982: "σώζω (sṓzō | sode'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a primary σῶς (contraction for obsolete σάος, \"safe\")\nStrong's: to save, i.e. deliver or protect (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —heal, preserve, save (self), do well, be (make) whole.\n\nCognate Group: G4991 (deliver), G4990 (saviour), G4992 (salvation), G4982 (heal)\nVariants: σώζω, ἐκσῴζω, σῴζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H247 אָזַר, H1272 בָּרַח, H2421 חָיָה, H2620 חָסָה, H3045 יָדַע, H3467 יָשַׁע, H4422 מָלַט, H5337 נָצַל, H6299 פָּדָה, H6403 פָּלַט, H7200 רָאָה, H7682 שָׂגַב, H7725 שׁוּב, H7804 שְׁזַב, H7999 שָׁלַם, H8300 שָׂרִיד",
    4983: "σῶμα (sōma | so'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G4982\nStrong's: the body (as a sound whole), used in a very wide application, literally or figuratively\nKJV: —bodily, body, slave.\n\nSee: G4982 \n\nCognate Group: G4985 (bodily), G4984 (bodily), G4983 (bodily), G4954 (of the same body)\nVariants: σῶμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H216 אֹור, H1320 בָּשָׂר, H1472 גְּוִיָּה, H1480 גּוּפָה, H1655 גֶּשֶׁם, H2428 חַיִל, H2945 טַף, H5038 נְבֵלָה, H5315 נֶפֶשׁ, H6297 פֶּגֶר, H7607 שְׁאֵר, H7607 שְׁאֵר",
    4984: "σωματικός (sōmatikós | so-mat-ee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G4983\nStrong's: corporeal or physical\nKJV: —bodily.\n\nSee: G4983 \n\nCognate Group: G4985 (bodily), G4984 (bodily), G4983 (bodily), G4954 (of the same body)\nVariants: σωματικός",
    4985: "σωματικῶς (sōmatikōs | so-mat-ee-koce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4984\nStrong's: corporeally or physically\nKJV: —bodily.\n\nSee: G4984 \n\nCognate Group: G4985 (bodily), G4984 (bodily), G4983 (bodily), G4954 (of the same body)\nVariants: σωματικῶς",
    4986: "Σώπατρος (Sṓpatros | so'-pat-ros)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4982 and G3962\nStrong's: of a safe father; Sopatrus, a Christian\nKJV: —Sopater. Compare G4989 .\n\nSee: G4982 \nSee: G3962 \nSee: G4989 \n\nCognate Group: G4985 (bodily), G4984 (bodily), G4983 (bodily), G4954 (of the same body)\nVariants: Σώπατρος",
    4987: "σωρεύω (sōreúō | sore-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from another form of G4673\nStrong's: to pile up (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —heap, load.\n\nSee: G4673 \n\nCognate Group: G2002 (heap), G4987 (heap)\nVariants: σωρεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2846 חָתָה",
    4988: "Σωσθένης (Sōsthénēs | soce-then'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4982 and that of G4599\nStrong's: of safe strength; Sosthenes, a Christian\nKJV: —Sosthenes.\n\nSee: G4982 \nSee: G4599 \n\nCognate Group: G2002 (heap), G4987 (heap)\nVariants: Σωσθένης",
    4989: "Σωσίπατρος (Sōsípatros | so-sip'-at-ros)\n\nDerivation: prolongation for G4986\nStrong's: Sosipatrus, a Christian\nKJV: —Sosipater.\n\nSee: G4986 \n\nCognate Group: G2002 (heap), G4987 (heap)\nVariants: Σωσίπατρος",
    4990: "σωτήρ (sōtḗr | so-tare')\n\nDerivation: from G4982\nStrong's: a deliverer, i.e. God or Christ\nKJV: —saviour.\n\nSee: G4982 \n\nCognate Group: G4991 (deliver), G4990 (saviour), G4992 (salvation), G4982 (heal)\nVariants: σωτήρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3444 יְשׁוּעָה, H3467 יָשַׁע, H3468 יֶשַׁע",
    4991: "σωτηρία (sōtēría | so-tay-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a derivative of G4990 as (properly, abstract) noun\nStrong's: rescue or safety (physically or morally)\nKJV: —deliver, health, salvation, save, saving.\n\nSee: G4990 \n\nCognate Group: G4991 (deliver), G4990 (saviour), G4992 (salvation), G4982 (heal)\nVariants: σωτηρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H3444 יְשׁוּעָה, H3468 יֶשַׁע, H6413 פְּלֵיטָה, H7961 שָׁלֵו, H7965 שָׁלֹום, H8454 תּוּשִׁיָּה, H8668 תְּשׁוּעָה",
    4992: "σωτήριον (sōtḗrion | so-tay'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of the same as G4991 as (properly, concretely) noun\nStrong's: defender or (by implication) defence\nKJV: —salvation.\n\nSee: G4991 \n\nCognate Group: G4991 (deliver), G4990 (saviour), G4992 (salvation), G4982 (heal)\nVariants: σωτήριον, σωτήριος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8002 שֶׁלֶם",
    4993: "σωφρονέω (sōphronéō | so-fron-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G4998\nStrong's: to be of sound mind, i.e. sane, (figuratively) moderate\nKJV: —be in right mind, be sober (minded), soberly.\n\nSee: G4998 \n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: σωφρονέω",
    4994: "σωφρονίζω (sōphronízō | so-fron-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G4998\nStrong's: to make of sound mind, i.e. (figuratively) to discipline or correct\nKJV: —teach to be sober.\n\nSee: G4998 \n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: σωφρονίζω",
    4995: "σωφρονισμός (sōphronismós | so-fron-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G4994\nStrong's: discipline, i.e. self-control\nKJV: —sound mind.\n\nSee: G4994 \n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: σωφρονισμός",
    4996: "σωφρόνως (sōphrónōs | so-fron'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G4998\nStrong's: with sound mind, i.e. moderately\nKJV: —soberly.\n\nSee: G4998 \n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: σωφρόνως",
    4997: "σωφροσύνη (sōphrosýnē | so-fros-oo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from G4998\nStrong's: soundness of mind, i.e. (literally) sanity or (figuratively) self-control\nKJV: —soberness, sobriety.\n\nSee: G4998 \n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: σωφροσύνη",
    4998: "σώφρων (sṓphrōn | so'-frone)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G4982 and that of G5424\nStrong's: safe (sound) in mind, i.e. self-controlled (moderate as to opinion or passion)\nKJV: —discreet, sober, temperate.\n\nSee: G4982 \nSee: G5424 \n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: σώφρων",
    4999: "Ταβέρναι (Tabérnai | tab-er'-nahee)\n\nDerivation: plural of Latin origin\nStrong's: huts or wooden-walled buildings; Tabernæ\nKJV: —taverns.\n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: Ταβέρναι, ταβέρναι, Ταβέρνη, Τρεῖς~Ταβέρναι",
    5000: "Ταβιθά (Tabithá | tab-ee-thah')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (compare H6646)\nStrong's: the gazelle; Tabitha (i.e. Tabjetha), a Christian female\nKJV: —Tabitha.\nSee: H6646\n\nCognate Group: G4998 (discreet), G4995 (sound mind), G4994 (teach to be sober), G4996 (soberly), G4997 (soberness), G4993 (be in right mind)\nVariants: Ταβιθά, Ταβηθά \nHebrew Equivalents: H6646 צְבִיָּה",
    5001: "τάγμα (tágma | tag'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5021\nStrong's: something orderly in arrangement (a troop), i.e. (figuratively) a series or succession\nKJV: —order.\n\nSee: G5021 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάγμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1714 דֶּגֶל, H2416 חַי, H7273 רַגְלִי",
    5002: "τακτός (taktós | tak-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G5021\nStrong's: arranged, i.e. appointed or stated\nKJV: —set.\n\nSee: G5021 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τακτός \nHebrew Equivalents: H7600 שַׁאֲנָן",
    5003: "ταλαιπωρέω (talaipōréō | tal-ahee-po-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5005\nStrong's: to be wretched, i.e. realize one's own misery\nKJV: —be afflicted.\n\nSee: G5005 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταλαιπωρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5753 עָוָה, H7703 שָׁדַד",
    5004: "ταλαιπωρία (talaipōría | tal-ahee-po-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5005\nStrong's: wretchedness, i.e. calamity\nKJV: —misery.\n\nSee: G5005 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταλαιπωρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4113 מַהֲמֹרָה, H4285 מַחְשָׁךְ, H5136 נוּשׁ, H7588 שָׁאֹון, H7667 שֶׁבֶר, H7701 שֹׁד, H7722 שֹׁוא",
    5005: "ταλαίπωρος (talaípōros | tal-ah'-ee-po-ros)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5007 and a derivative of the base of G3984\nStrong's: enduring trial, i.e. miserable\nKJV: —wretched.\n\nSee: G5007 \nSee: G3984 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταλαίπωρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7703 שָׁדַד",
    5006: "ταλαντιαῖος (talantiaîos | tal-an-tee-ah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G5007\nStrong's: talent-like in weight\nKJV: —weight of a talent.\n\nSee: G5007 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταλαντιαῖος",
    5007: "τάλαντον (tálanton | tal'-an-ton)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of the original form of τλάω (to bear\nStrong's: equivalent to G5342); a balance (as supporting weights), i.e. (by implication) a certain weight (and thence a coin or rather sum of money) or \"talent\"\nKJV: —talent.\n\nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάλαντον \nHebrew Equivalents: H374 אֵיפָה, H3603 כִּכָּר",
    5008: "ταλιθά (talithá | tal-ee-thah')\n\nDerivation: of Chaldee origin (compare H2924)\nStrong's: the fresh, i.e. young girl; talitha (O maiden)\nKJV: —talitha.\nSee: H2924\n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταλιθά, θάβιτα, ταλιθα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2924 טָלֶה",
    5009: "ταμεῖον (tameîon | tam-i'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter contraction of a presumed derivative of ταμίας (a dispenser or distributor\nStrong's: akin to τέμνω, to cut); a dispensary or magazine, i.e. a chamber on the ground-floor or interior of an Oriental house (generally used for storage or privacy, a spot for retirement)\nKJV: —secret chamber, closet, storehouse.\n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταμεῖον \nHebrew Equivalents: H618 אָסָם, H2315 חֶדֶר, H4200 מֶזֶו, H8432 תָּוֶךְ",
    5010: "τάξις (táxis | tax'-is)\n\nDerivation: from G5021\nStrong's: regular arrangement, i.e. (in time) fixed succession (of rank or character), official dignity\nKJV: —order.\n\nSee: G5021 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάξις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1700 דִּבְרָה, H2073 זְבוּל, H4164 מוּצַק, H4264 מַחֲנֶה, H4546 מְסִלָּה, H4725 מָקֹום, H6467 פֹּעַל, H7093 קֵץ, H8452 תֹּורָה",
    5011: "ταπεινός (tapeinós | tap-i-nos')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: depressed, i.e. (figuratively) humiliated (in circumstances or disposition)\nKJV: —base, cast down, humble, of low degree (estate), lowly.\n\nCognate Group: G5011 (base), G5012 (humbleness of mind), G5014 (humiliation), G5013 (abase)\nVariants: ταπεινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H34 אֶבְיֹון, H779 אָרַר, H1790 דַּךְ, H1792 דָּכָא, H1800 דַּל, H4134 מוּךְ, H6013 עָמֹק, H6041 עָנִי, H6800 צָנַע, H7326 רוּשׁ, H8217 שָׁפָל, H8482 תַּחְתִּי",
    5012: "ταπεινοφροσύνη (tapeinophrosýnē | tap-i-nof-ros-oo'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5011 and the base of G5424\nStrong's: humiliation of mind, i.e. modesty\nKJV: —humbleness of mind, humility (of mind, loneliness (of mind).\n\nSee: G5011 \nSee: G5424 \n\nCognate Group: G5011 (base), G5012 (humbleness of mind), G5014 (humiliation), G5013 (abase)\nVariants: ταπεινοφροσύνη",
    5013: "ταπεινόω (tapeinóō | tap-i-no'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5011\nStrong's: to depress; figuratively, to humiliate (in condition or heart)\nKJV: —abase, bring low, humble (self).\n\nSee: G5011 \n\nCognate Group: G5011 (base), G5012 (humbleness of mind), G5014 (humiliation), G5013 (abase)\nVariants: ταπεινόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1086 בָּלָה, H1790 דַּךְ, H1792 דָּכָא, H1792 דָּכָא, H1809 דָּלַל, H2151 זָלַל, H3013 יָגָה, H3512 כָּאָה, H3665 כָּנַע, H3766 כָּרַע, H4134 מוּךְ, H5307 נָפַל, H6030 עָנָה, H6030 עָנָה, H6087 עָצַב, H7326 רוּשׁ, H7743 שׁוּחַ, H7817 שָׁחַח, H7843 שָׁחַת, H8213 שָׁפֵל",
    5014: "ταπείνωσις (tapeínōsis | tap-i'-no-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5013\nStrong's: depression (in rank or feeling)\nKJV: —humiliation, be made low, low estate, vile.\n\nSee: G5013 \n\nCognate Group: G5011 (base), G5012 (humbleness of mind), G5014 (humiliation), G5013 (abase)\nVariants: ταπείνωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1792 דָּכָא, H6040 עֳנִי, H6115 עֹצֶר, H8216 שֶׁפֶל",
    5015: "ταράσσω (tarássō | tar-as'-so)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: to stir or agitate (roil water)\nKJV: —trouble.\n\nCognate Group: G5015 (trouble), G5017 (stir), G5016 (trouble)\nVariants: ταράσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H926 בָּהַל, H943 בּוּךְ, H1104 בָּלַע, H1204 בָּעַת, H1238 בָּקַק, H1607 גָּעַשׁ, H1804 דָּלַח, H1949 הוּם, H1993 הָמָה, H2196 זָעַף, H2287 חָגַג, H2342 חוּל, H2490 חָלַל, H2560 חָמַר, H2730 חָרֵד, H3648 כָּמַר, H3766 כָּרַע, H3857 לָהַט, H3943 לָפַת, H4102 מָהַהּ, H4116 מָהַר, H4127 מוּג, H4171 מוּר, H4833 מִרְפָּשׂ, H5074 נָדַד, H5307 נָפַל, H5503 סָחַר, H5753 עָוָה, H5791 עָוַת, H6206 עָרַץ, H6244 עָשֵׁשׁ, H6471 פַּעַם, H6504 פָּרַד, H6670 צָהַל, H6789 צָמַת, H7043 קָלַל, H7264 רָגַז, H7280 רָגַע, H7489 רָעַע, H7493 רָעַשׁ, H7515 רָפַשׂ, H7812 שָׁחָה, H7817 שָׁחַח, H7997 שָׁלַל, H8074 שָׁמֵם, H8269 שַׂר",
    5016: "ταραχή (tarachḗ | tar-akh-ay')\n\nDerivation: feminine from G5015\nStrong's: disturbance, i.e. (of water) roiling, or (of a mob) sedition\nKJV: —trouble(-ing).\n\nSee: G5015 \n\nCognate Group: G5015 (trouble), G5017 (stir), G5016 (trouble)\nVariants: ταραχή G5430 \nHebrew Equivalents: H1091 בַּלָּהָה, H1205 בְּעָתָה, H2189 זַעֲוָה, H2427 חִיל, H2470 חָלָה, H2649 חִפָּזֹון, H2787 חָרַר, H3766 כָּרַע, H4079 מִדְיָן, H4090 מְדָן, H4103 מְהוּמָה, H5792 עַוָּתָה, H7407 רֹכֶס, H7465 רֹעָה",
    5017: "τάραχος (tárachos | tar'-akh-os)\n\nDerivation: masculine from G5015\nStrong's: a disturbance, i.e. (popular) tumult\nKJV: —stir.\n\nSee: G5015 \n\nCognate Group: G5015 (trouble), G5017 (stir), G5016 (trouble)\nVariants: τάραχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1091 בַּלָּהָה, H4103 מְהוּמָה, H5916 עָכַר",
    5018: "Ταρσεύς (Tarseús | tar-syoos')\n\nDerivation: from G5019\nStrong's: a Tarsean, i.e. native of Tarsus\nKJV: —of Tarsus.\n\nSee: G5019 \n\nCognate Group: G5015 (trouble), G5017 (stir), G5016 (trouble)\nVariants: Ταρσεύς",
    5019: "Ταρσός (Tarsós | tar-sos')\n\nDerivation: perhaps the same as ταρσός (a flat basket)\nStrong's: Tarsus, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Tarsus.\n\nCognate Group: G5015 (trouble), G5017 (stir), G5016 (trouble)\nVariants: Ταρσός",
    5020: "ταρταρόω (tartaróō | tar-tar-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from Τάρταρος (the deepest abyss of Hades)\nStrong's: to incarcerate in eternal torment\nKJV: —cast down to hell.\n\nCognate Group: G5015 (trouble), G5017 (stir), G5016 (trouble)\nVariants: ταρταρόω",
    5021: "τάσσω (tássō | tas'-so)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary verb (which latter appears only in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to arrange in an orderly manner, i.e. assign or dispose (to a certain position or lot)\nKJV: —addict, appoint, determine, ordain, set.\n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H270 אָחַז, H1713 דָּגַל, H1980 הָלַךְ, H3259 יָעַד, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5414 נָתַן, H5975 עָמַד, H6286 פָּאַר, H6680 צָוָה, H7560 רְשַׁם, H7725 שׁוּב, H7760 שׂוּם, H7896 שִׁית",
    5022: "ταῦρος (taûros | tow'-ros)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word (compare H8450, \"steer\")\nStrong's: a bullock\nKJV: —bull, ox.\nSee: H8450\n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταῦρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H46 אָבִיר, H504 אֶלֶף, H4806 מְרִיא, H6499 פַּר, H7794 שֹׁור",
    5023: "ταῦτα (taûta | tow'-tah)\n\nDerivation: nominative or accusative case neuter plural of G3778\nStrong's: these things\nKJV: —+ afterward, follow, + hereafter, X him, the same, so, such, that, then, these, they, this, those, thus.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταῦτα, οὗτος",
    5024: "ταὐτά (tautá | tow-tah')\n\nDerivation: neuter plural of G3588 and G846 as adverb\nStrong's: in the same way\nKJV: —even thus, (manner) like, so.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G846 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταὐτά G5437",
    5025: "ταύταις (taútais | tow'-taheece), and ταύτας (taútas | tow'-tas)\n\nDerivation: dative case and accusative case feminine plural respectively of G3778\nStrong's: (to or with or by, etc.) these\nKJV: —hence, that, then, these, those.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταύταις, οὗτος G4047",
    5026: "ταύτῃ (taútēi | tow'-tay), and ταύτην (taútēn | tow'-tane), and ταύτης (taútēs | tow'-tace)\n\nDerivation: dative case, accusative case and genitive case respectively of the feminine singular of G3778\nStrong's: (towards or of) this\nKJV: —her, + hereof, it, that, + thereby, the (same), this (same).\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταύτῃ, οὗτος G4047",
    5027: "ταφή (taphḗ | taf-ay')\n\nDerivation: feminine from G2290\nStrong's: burial (the act)\nKJV: —X bury.\n\nSee: G2290 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταφή \nHebrew Equivalents: H2590 חָנַט, H6912 קָבַר, H6913 קֶבֶר",
    5028: "τάφος (táphos | taf'-os)\n\nDerivation: masculine from G2290\nStrong's: a grave (the place of interment)\nKJV: —sepulchre, tomb.\n\nSee: G2290 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάφος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6913 קֶבֶר",
    5029: "τάχα (tácha | takh'-ah)\n\nDerivation: as if neuter plural of G5036 (adverbially)\nStrong's: shortly, i.e. (figuratively) possibly\nKJV: —peradventure(-haps).\n\nSee: G5036 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάχα",
    5030: "ταχέως (tachéōs | takh-eh'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5036\nStrong's: briefly, i.e. (in time) speedily, or (in manner) rapidly\nKJV: —hastily, quickly, shortly, soon, suddenly.\n\nSee: G5036 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταχέως \nHebrew Equivalents: H4116 מָהַר, H4120 מְהֵרָה, H7280 רָגַע",
    5031: "ταχινός (tachinós | takh-ee-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G5034\nStrong's: curt, i.e. impending\nKJV: —shortly, swift.\n\nSee: G5034 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταχινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4116 מָהַר",
    5032: "τάχιον (táchion | takh'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter singular of the comparative of G5036 (as adverb)\nStrong's: more swiftly, i.e. (in manner) more rapidly, or (in time) more speedily\nKJV: —out (run), quickly, shortly, sooner.\n\nSee: G5036 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάχιον, ταχέως \nHebrew Equivalents: H4116 מָהַר, H4120 מְהֵרָה, H7280 רָגַע",
    5033: "τάχιστα (táchista | takh'-is-tah)\n\nDerivation: neuter plural of the superlative of G5036 (as adverb)\nStrong's: most quickly, i.e. (with G5613 prefixed) as soon as possible\nKJV: —+ with all speed.\n\nSee: G5036 \nSee: G5613 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάχιστα, ταχέως",
    5034: "τάχος (táchos | takh'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5036\nStrong's: a brief space (of time), i.e. (with G1722 prefixed) in haste\nKJV: —+ quickly, + shortly, + speedily.\n\nSee: G5036 \nSee: G1722 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τάχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3288 יְעָף, H4116 מָהַר, H4116 מָהַר, H4120 מְהֵרָה, H7281 רֶגַע",
    5035: "ταχύ (tachý | takh-oo')\n\nDerivation: neuter singular of G5036 (as adverb)\nStrong's: shortly, i.e. without delay, soon, or (by surprise) suddenly, or (by implication, of ease) readily\nKJV: —lightly, quickly.\n\nSee: G5036 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταχύ, ταχύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H213 אוּץ, H4116 מָהַר, H7138 קָרֹוב, H7280 רָגַע",
    5036: "ταχύς (tachýs | takh-oos')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: fleet, i.e. (figuratively) prompt or ready\nKJV: —swift.\n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ταχύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H213 אוּץ, H4116 מָהַר, H7138 קָרֹוב, H7280 רָגַע",
    5037: "τέ (té | teh)\n\nDerivation: a primary particle (enclitic) of connection or addition\nStrong's: both or also (properly, as correlation of G2532)\nKJV: —also, and, both, even, then, whether. Often used in composition, usually as the latter participle.\n\nSee: G2532 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τέ",
    5038: "τεῖχος (teîchos | ti'-khos)\n\nDerivation: akin to the base of G5088\nStrong's: a wall (as formative of a house)\nKJV: —wall.\n\nSee: G5088 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τεῖχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2346 חֹומָה, H2742 חֲרוּץ, H4694 מְצוּרָה, H7023 קִיר, H7791 שׁוּר, H8510 תֵּל",
    5039: "τεκμήριον (tekmḗrion | tek-may'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of τεκμάρ (a goal or fixed limit)\nStrong's: a token (as defining a fact), i.e. criterion of certainty\nKJV: —infallible proof.\n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: τεκμήριον",
    5040: "τεκνίον (tekníon | tek-nee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G5043\nStrong's: an infant, i.e. (plural figuratively) darlings (Christian converts)\nKJV: —little children.\n\nSee: G5043 \n\nCognate Group: G5043 (child), G5044 (bring up children), G5041 (bear children), G5042 (childbearing), G5040 (little children)\nVariants: τεκνίον",
    5041: "τεκνογονέω (teknogonéō | tek-nog-on-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5043 and the base of G1096\nStrong's: to be a child-bearer, i.e. parent (mother)\nKJV: —bear children.\n\nSee: G5043 \nSee: G1096 \n\nCognate Group: G5043 (child), G5044 (bring up children), G5041 (bear children), G5042 (childbearing), G5040 (little children)\nVariants: τεκνογονέω",
    5042: "τεκνογονία (teknogonía | tek-nog-on-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5041\nStrong's: childbirth (parentage), i.e. (by implication) maternity (the performance of maternal duties)\nKJV: —childbearing.\n\nSee: G5041 \n\nCognate Group: G5043 (child), G5044 (bring up children), G5041 (bear children), G5042 (childbearing), G5040 (little children)\nVariants: τεκνογονία",
    5043: "τέκνον (téknon | tek'-non)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5098\nStrong's: a child (as produced)\nKJV: —child, daughter, son.\n\nSee: G5098 \n\nCognate Group: G5043 (child), G5044 (bring up children)\nVariants: τέκνον \nHebrew Equivalents: H319 אַחֲרִית, H1004 בַּיִת, H1121 בֵּן, H1248 בַּר, H1755 דֹּור, H2945 טַף, H3205 יָלַד, H3206 יֶלֶד, H5768 עֹולֵל, H6529 פְּרִי, H6631 צֶאֱצָא",
    5044: "τεκνοτροφέω (teknotrophéō | tek-not-rof-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5043 and G5142\nStrong's: to be a childrearer, i.e. fulfil the duties of a female parent\nKJV: —bring up children.\n\nSee: G5043 \nSee: G5142 \n\nCognate Group: G5043 (child), G5044 (bring up children)\nVariants: τεκνοτροφέω",
    5045: "τέκτων (téktōn | tek'-tone)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5098\nStrong's: an artificer (as producer of fabrics), i.e. (specially), a craftsman in wood\nKJV: —carpenter.\n\nSee: G5098 \n\nCognate Group: G5043 (child), G5044 (bring up children)\nVariants: τέκτων \nHebrew Equivalents: H2796 חָרָשׁ",
    5046: "τέλειος (téleios | tel'-i-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5056\nStrong's: complete (in various applications of labor, growth, mental and moral character, etc.); neuter (as noun, with G3588) completeness\nKJV: —of full age, man, perfect.\n\nSee: G5056 \nSee: G3588 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τέλειος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8002 שֶׁלֶם, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8503 תַּכְלִית, H8549 תָּמִים",
    5047: "τελειότης (teleiótēs | tel-i-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G5046\nStrong's: (the state) completeness (mentally or morally)\nKJV: —perfection(-ness).\n\nSee: G5046 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελειότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H8538 תֻּמָּה, H8549 תָּמִים",
    5048: "τελειόω (teleióō | tel-i-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5046\nStrong's: to complete, i.e. (literally) accomplish, or (figuratively) consummate (in character)\nKJV: —consecrate, finish, fulfil, make) perfect.\n\nSee: G5046 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελειόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3615 כָּלָה, H3634 כָּלַל, H4390 מָלֵא, H6213 עָשָׂה, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8552 תָּמַם",
    5049: "τελείως (teleíōs | tel-i'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5046\nStrong's: completely, i.e. (of hope) without wavering\nKJV: —to the end.\n\nSee: G5046 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελείως",
    5050: "τελείωσις (teleíōsis | tel-i'-o-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5448\nStrong's: (the act) completion, i.e. (of prophecy) verification, or (of expiation) absolution\nKJV: —perfection, performance.\n\nSee: G5448 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελείωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H4394 מִלֻּא, H8002 שֶׁלֶם",
    5051: "τελειωτής (teleiōtḗs | tel-i-o-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G5048\nStrong's: a completer, i.e. consummater\nKJV: —finisher.\n\nSee: G5048 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελειωτής",
    5052: "τελεσφορέω (telesphoréō | tel-es-for-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5056 and G5342\nStrong's: to be a bearer to completion (maturity), i.e. to ripen fruit (figuratively)\nKJV: —bring fruit to perfection.\n\nSee: G5056 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελεσφορέω",
    5053: "τελευτάω (teleutáō | tel-yoo-tah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed derivative of G5055\nStrong's: to finish life (by implication, of G979), i.e. expire (demise)\nKJV: —be dead, decease, die.\n\nSee: G5055 \nSee: G979 \n\nCognate Group: G5053 (be dead), G5054 (death)\nVariants: τελευτάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1478 גָּוַע, H4191 מוּת, H5307 נָפַל",
    5054: "τελευτή (teleutḗ | tel-yoo-tay')\n\nDerivation: from G5053\nStrong's: decease\nKJV: —death.\n\nSee: G5053 \n\nCognate Group: G5053 (be dead), G5054 (death)\nVariants: τελευτή \nHebrew Equivalents: H319 אַחֲרִית, H4191 מוּת, H4194 מָוֶת",
    5055: "τελέω (teléō | tel-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5056\nStrong's: to end, i.e. complete, execute, conclude, discharge (a debt)\nKJV: —accomplish, make an end, expire, fill up, finish, go over, pay, perform.\n\nSee: G5056 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1585 גְּמַר, H3319 יְצָא, H3615 כָּלָה, H5486 סוּף, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6775 צָמַד, H6945 קָדֵשׁ, H7999 שָׁלַם",
    5056: "τέλος (télos | tel'-os)\n\nDerivation: from a primary τέλλω (to set out for a definite point or goal)\nStrong's: properly, the point aimed at as a limit, i.e. (by implication) the conclusion of an act or state (termination (literally, figuratively or indefinitely), result (immediate, ultimate or prophetic), purpose); specially, an impost or levy (as paid)\nKJV: —+ continual, custom, end(-ing), finally, uttermost. Compare G5411 .\n\nSee: G5411 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τέλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3615 כָּלָה, H4371 מֶכֶס, H4522 מַס, H5331 נֶצַח, H5490 סֹוף, H5704 עַד, H7093 קֵץ, H8548 תָּמִיד",
    5057: "τελώνης (telṓnēs | tel-o'-nace)\n\nDerivation: from G5056 and G5608\nStrong's: a tax-farmer, i.e. collector of public revenue\nKJV: —publican.\n\nSee: G5056 \nSee: G5608 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελώνης",
    5058: "τελώνιον (telṓnion | tel-o'-nee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of G5057\nStrong's: a tax-gatherer's place of business\nKJV: —receipt of custom.\n\nSee: G5057 \n\nCognate Group: G5047 (perfection), G5046 (of full age), G5056 (+ continual), G5051 (finisher), G2005 (accomplish), G5052 (bring fruit to perfection), G4930 (end), G3838 (+ in wise), G4931 (end), G5050 (perfection), G5048 (consecrate), G5055 (accomplish), G5049 (to the end)\nVariants: τελώνιον, τελωνεῖον",
    5059: "τέρας (téras | ter'-as)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a prodigy or omen\nKJV: —wonder.\n\nCognate Group: G5059 (wonder)\nVariants: τέρας \nHebrew Equivalents: H4159 מֹופֵת, H6382 פֶּלֶא, H8047 שַׁמָּה, H8540 תְּמַהּ",
    5060: "Τέρτιος (Tértios | ter'-tee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: third; Tertius, a Christian\nKJV: —Tertius.\n\nCognate Group: G5059 (wonder)\nVariants: Τέρτιος",
    5061: "Τέρτυλλος (Tértyllos | ter'-tool-los)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Tertullus, a Roman\nKJV: —Tertullus.\n\nCognate Group: G5059 (wonder)\nVariants: Τέρτυλλος",
    5062: "τεσσαράκοντα (tessarákonta | tes-sar-ak'-on-tah)\n\nDerivation: the decade of G5064\nStrong's: forty\nKJV: —forty.\n\nSee: G5064 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τεσσαράκοντα, τεσσεράκοντα",
    5063: "τεσσαρακονταετής (tessarakontaetḗs | tes-sar-ak-on-tah-et-ace')\n\nDerivation: from G5062 and G2094\nStrong's: of forty years of age\nKJV: —(+ full, of) forty years (old).\n\nSee: G5062 \nSee: G2094 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τεσσαρακονταετής, τεσσερακονταετής",
    5064: "τέσσαρες (téssares | tes'-sar-es), or neuter τέσσαρα (téssara | tes'-sar-ah)\n\nDerivation: a plural number\nStrong's: four\nKJV: —four.\n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τέσσαρες",
    5065: "τεσσαρεσκαιδέκατος (tessareskaidékatos | tes-sar-es-kahee-dek'-at-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5064 and G2532 and G1182\nStrong's: fourteenth\nKJV: —fourteenth.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G2532 \nSee: G1182 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τεσσαρεσκαιδέκατος",
    5066: "τεταρταῖος (tetartaîos | tet-ar-tah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G5064\nStrong's: pertaining to the fourth day\nKJV: —four days.\n\nSee: G5064 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τεταρταῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7243 רְבִיעִי",
    5067: "τέταρτος (tétartos | tet'-ar-tos)\n\nDerivation: ordinal from G5064\nStrong's: fourth\nKJV: —four(-th).\n\nSee: G5064 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τέταρτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H702 אַרְבַּע, H7243 רְבִיעִי, H7256 רִבֵּעַ",
    5068: "τετράγωνος (tetrágōnos | tet-rag'-o-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G5064 and G1137\nStrong's: four-cornered, i.e. square\nKJV: —foursquare.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G1137 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετράγωνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7250 רָבַע",
    5069: "τετράδιον (tetrádion | tet-rad'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of τέτρας (a tetrad\nStrong's: from G5064); a quaternion or squad (picket) of four Roman soldiers\nKJV: —quaternion.\n\nSee: G5064 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετράδιον",
    5070: "τετρακισχίλιοι (tetrakischílioi | tet-rak-is-khil'-ee-oy)\n\nDerivation: from the multiplicative adverb of G5064 and G5507\nStrong's: four times a thousand\nKJV: —four thousand.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G5507 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετρακισχίλιοι",
    5071: "τετρακόσιοι (tetrakósioi | tet-rak-os'-ee-oy), or neuter τετρακόσια (tetrakósia | tet-rak-os'-ee-ah)\n\nDerivation: plural from G5064 and G1540\nStrong's: four hundred\nKJV: —four hundred.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G1540 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετρακόσιοι",
    5072: "τετράμηνον (tetrámēnon | tet-ram'-ay-non)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G5064 and G3376\nStrong's: a four months' space\nKJV: —four months.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G3376 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετράμηνον, τετράμηνος",
    5073: "τετραπλόος (tetraplóos | tet-rap-lo'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5064 and a derivative of the base of G4118\nStrong's: quadruple\nKJV: —fourfold.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G4118 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετραπλόος, τετραπλοῦς",
    5074: "τετράπους (tetrápous | tet-rap'-ooce)\n\nDerivation: from G5064 and G4228\nStrong's: a quadruped\nKJV: —fourfooted beast.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G4228 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετράπους \nHebrew Equivalents: H929 בְּהֵמָה, H2416 חַי",
    5075: "τετραρχέω (tetrarchéō | tet-rar-kheh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5076\nStrong's: to be a tetrarch\nKJV: —(be) tetrarch.\n\nSee: G5076 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετραρχέω, τετρααρχέω, τετρααρχέω (some edd. τετραρχέω)",
    5076: "τετράρχης (tetrárchēs | tet-rar'-khace)\n\nDerivation: from G5064 and G757\nStrong's: the ruler of a fourth part of a country (\"tetrarch\")\nKJV: —tetrarch.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G757 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τετράρχης, τετραάρχης",
    5077: "τεφρόω (tephróō | tef-ro'-o)\n\nDerivation: from τέφρα (ashes)\nStrong's: to incinerate, i.e. consume\nKJV: —turn to ashes.\n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τεφρόω",
    5078: "τέχνη (téchnē | tekh'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5088\nStrong's: art (as productive), i.e. (specially), a trade, or (generally) skill\nKJV: —art, craft, occupation.\n\nSee: G5088 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τέχνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H2451 חׇכְמָה, H4639 מַעֲשֶׂה, H5656 עֲבֹדָה",
    5079: "τεχνίτης (technítēs | tekh-nee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G5078\nStrong's: an artisan; figuratively, a founder (Creator)\nKJV: —builder, craftsman.\n\nSee: G5078 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τεχνίτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H542 אָמָן, H2672 חָצַב, H2796 חָרָשׁ",
    5080: "τήκω (tḗkō | tay'-ko)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to liquefy\nKJV: —melt.\n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τήκω, τήκομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1234 בָּקַע, H1671 דְּאָבֹון, H2215 זָרַב, H3615 כָּלָה, H4127 מוּג, H4198 מָזֶה, H4529 מָסָה, H4549 מָסַס, H4743 מָקַק, H5140 נָזַל, H5413 נָתַךְ, H7817 שָׁחַח, H8552 תָּמַם",
    5081: "τηλαυγῶς (tēlaugōs | tay-low-goce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from a compound of a derivative of G5056 and G827\nStrong's: in a far-shining manner, i.e. plainly\nKJV: —clearly.\n\nSee: G5056 \nSee: G827 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τηλαυγῶς, δηλαυγῶς",
    5082: "τηλικοῦτος (tēlikoûtos | tay-lik-oo'-tos), or feminine τηλικαύτη (tēlikaútē | tay-lik-ow'-tay)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G3588 with G2245 and G3778\nStrong's: such as this, i.e. (in (figurative) magnitude) so vast\nKJV: —so great, so mighty.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G2245 \nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5065 (fourteenth), G5073 (fourfold), G5070 (four thousand), G5069 (quaternion), G5067 (four), G5062 (forty), G5064 (four), G5074 (fourfooted beast), G5075 (tetrarch), G5072 (four months), G5066 (four days), G5063 (forty years), G5071 (four hundred), G5076 (tetrarch), G5068 (foursquare)\nVariants: τηλικοῦτος",
    5083: "τηρέω (tēréō | tay-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from τερός (a watch\nStrong's: perhaps akin to G2334); to guard (from loss or injury, properly, by keeping the eye upon; and thus differing from G5442, which is properly to prevent escaping; and from G2892, which implies a fortress or full military lines of apparatus), i.e. to note (a prophecy; figuratively, to fulfil a command); by implication, to detain (in custody; figuratively, to maintain); by extension, to withhold (for personal ends; figuratively, to keep unmarried); by extension, to withhold (for personal ends; figuratively, to keep unmarried)\nKJV: —hold fast, keep(- er), (pre-, re-)serve, watch.\n\nSee: G2334 \nSee: G5442 \nSee: G2892 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: τηρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5341 נָצַר, H6845 צָפַן, H6965 קוּם, H7912 שְׁכַח, H8085 שָׁמַע, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    5084: "τήρησις (tḗrēsis | tay'-ray-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5083\nStrong's: a watching, i.e. (figuratively) observance, or (concretely) a prison\nKJV: —hold.\n\nSee: G5083 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: τήρησις",
    5085: "Τιβεριάς (Tiberiás | tib-er-ee-as')\n\nDerivation: from G5086\nStrong's: Tiberias, the name of a town and a lake in Palestine\nKJV: —Tiberias.\n\nSee: G5086 \n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: Τιβεριάς",
    5086: "Τιβέριος (Tibérios | tib-er'-ee-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: probably pertaining to the river Tiberis or Tiber; Tiberius, a Roman emperor\nKJV: —Tiberius.\n\nCognate Group: G5084 (hold), G3906 (observe), G4933 (keep), G5083 (hold fast), G3907 (obervation), G1301 (keep)\nVariants: Τιβέριος",
    5087: "τίθημι (títhēmi | tith'-ay-mee)\n\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form of a primary θέω (théō | theh'-o)\n (which is used only as alternate in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to place (in the widest application, literally and figuratively; properly, in a passive or horizontal posture, and thus different from G2476, which properly denotes an upright and active position, while G2749 is properly reflexive and utterly prostrate)\nKJV: —+ advise, appoint, bow, commit, conceive, give, X kneel down, lay (aside, down, up), make, ordain, purpose, put, set (forth), settle, sink down.\n\nSee: G2476 \nSee: G2749 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: τίθημι, τίθημι/τιθέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H622 אָסַף, H935 בֹּוא, H1777 דִּין, H1875 דָּרַשׁ, H3322 יָצַג, H3332 יָצַק, H3427 יָשַׁב, H3455 יָשַׂם, H3559 כּוּן, H3772 כָּרַת, H4171 מוּר, H5060 נָגַע, H5117 נוּחַ, H5148 נָחָה, H5181 נָחַת, H5307 נָפַל, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5414 נָתַן, H5437 סָבַב, H5446 סְבַל, H5810 עָזַז, H5927 עָלָה, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6485 פָּקַד, H6680 צָוָה, H6965 קוּם, H6979 קוּר, H6983 קֹושׁ, H7311 רוּם, H7412 רְמָה, H7561 רָשַׁע, H7737 שָׁוָה, H7760 שׂוּם, H7896 שִׁית, H8104 שָׁמַר, H8371 שָׁתַת, H8628 תָּקַע",
    5088: "τίκτω (tíktō | tik'-to)\n\n\nDerivation: a strengthened form of a primary τέκω (tékō | tek'-o)\n (which is used only as alternate in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to produce (from seed, as a mother, a plant, the earth, etc.), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —bear, be born, bring forth, be delivered, be in travail.\n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: τίκτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2029 הָרָה, H3205 יָלַד, H4422 מָלַט",
    5089: "τίλλω (tíllō | til'-lo)\n\nDerivation: perhaps akin to the alternate of G138, and thus to G4951\nStrong's: to pull off\nKJV: —pluck.\n\nSee: G4951 \nSee: G138 \n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: τίλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4803 מָרַט",
    5090: "Τιμαῖος (Timaîos | tim'-ah-yos)\n\nDerivation: probably of Chaldee origin (compare H2931)\nStrong's: Timæus (i.e. Timay), an Israelite\nKJV: —Timæus.\nSee: H2931\n\nCognate Group: G3866 (committed unto), G3908 (allege), G3331 (change), G4286 (purpose), G114 (cast off), G5087 (+ advise), G2007 (add unto), G4388 (purpose), G115 (disannulling), G4369 (add), G3346 (carry over), G1936 (laying on)\nVariants: Τιμαῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2931 טָמֵא",
    5091: "τιμάω (timáō | tim-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5093\nStrong's: to prize, i.e. fix a valuation upon; by implication, to revere\nKJV: —honour, value.\n\nSee: G5093 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τιμάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1921 הָדַר, H3365 יָקַר, H3513 כָּבַד, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6186 עָרַךְ, H8254 שָׁקַל",
    5092: "τιμή (timḗ | tee-may')\n\nDerivation: from G5099\nStrong's: a value, i.e. money paid, or (concretely and collectively) valuables; by analogy, esteem (especially of the highest degree), or the dignity itself\nKJV: —honour, precious, price, some.\n\nSee: G5099 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τιμή \nHebrew Equivalents: H1926 הָדָר, H1935 הֹוד, H1952 הֹון, H3366 יְקָר, H3513 כָּבַד, H3519 כָּבֹוד, H3701 כֶּסֶף, H4242 מְחִיר, H4377 מֶכֶר, H5797 עֹז, H6187 עֵרֶךְ, H7613 שְׂאֵת",
    5093: "τίμιος (tímios | tim'-ee-os), including the comparative τιμιώτερος (timiṓteros | tim-ee-o'-ter-os), and the superlative τιμιώτατος (timiṓtatos | tim-ee-o'-tat-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5092\nStrong's: valuable, i.e. (objectively) costly, or (subjectively) honored, esteemed, or (figuratively) beloved\nKJV: —dear, honourable, (more, most) precious, had in reputation.\n\nSee: G5092 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τίμιος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2656 חֵפֶץ, H3368 יָקָר, H6337 פָּז, H7350 רָחֹוק",
    5094: "τιμιότης (timiótēs | tim-ee-ot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G5093\nStrong's: expensiveness, i.e. (by implication) magnificence\nKJV: —costliness.\n\nSee: G5093 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τιμιότης",
    5095: "Τιμόθεος (Timótheos | tee-moth'-eh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5092 and G2316\nStrong's: dear to God; Timotheus, a Christian\nKJV: —Timotheus, Timothy.\n\nSee: G5092 \nSee: G2316 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: Τιμόθεος",
    5096: "Τίμων (Tímōn | tee'-mone)\n\nDerivation: from G5092\nStrong's: valuable; Timon, a Christian\nKJV: —Timon.\n\nSee: G5092 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: Τίμων",
    5097: "τιμωρέω (timōréō | tim-o-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a comparative of G5092 and οὖρος ouros (a guard)\nStrong's: properly, to protect one's honor, i.e. to avenge (inflict a penalty)\nKJV: —punish.\n\nSee: G5092 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τιμωρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7921 שָׁכֹל",
    5098: "τιμωρία (timōría | tee-mo-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5097\nStrong's: vindication, i.e. (by implication) a penalty\nKJV: —punishment.\n\nSee: G5097 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τιμωρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4112 מַהֲלֻמָּה, H6365 פִּיד, H8504 תְּכֵלֶת",
    5099: "τίνω (tínō | tee'-no)\n\n\nDerivation: strengthened for a primary τίω (tíō | tee'-o)\n (which is only used as an alternate in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to pay a price, i.e. as a penalty\nKJV: —be punished with.\n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6064 עָנַשׁ",
    5100: "τὶς (tìs | tis)\n\nDerivation: an enclitic indefinite pronoun\nStrong's: some or any person or object\nKJV: —a (kind of), any (man, thing, thing at all), certain (thing), divers, he (every) man, one (X thing), ought, + partly, some (man, -body, - thing, -what), (+ that no-)thing, what(-soever), X wherewith, whom(-soever), whose(-soever).\n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τὶς, τις",
    5101: "τίς (tís | tis)\n\nDerivation: probably emphatic of G5100\nStrong's: an interrogative pronoun, who, which or what (in direct or indirect questions)\nKJV: —every man, how (much), + no(-ne, thing), what (manner, thing), where (-by, -fore, -of, -unto, - with, -withal), whether, which, who(-m, -se), why.\n\nSee: G5100 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τίς \nHebrew Equivalents: H349 אֵיךְ, H639 אַף, H834 אֲשֶׁר, H834 אֲשֶׁר, H1116 בָּמָה, H2009 הִנֵּה, H2088 זֶה, H3642 כָּמַהּ, H4069 מַדּוּעַ, H4100 מָה, H4100 מָה, H4100 מָה, H4100 מָה, H4310 מִי, H5704 עַד, H6030 עָנָה",
    5102: "τίτλος (títlos | tit'-los)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a titulus or \"title\" (placard)\nKJV: —title.\n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τίτλος",
    5103: "Τίτος (Títos | tee'-tos)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin but uncertain significance\nStrong's: Titus, a Christian\nKJV: —Titus.\n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: Τίτος",
    5104: "τοί (toí | toy)\n\nDerivation: probably for the dative case of G3588\nStrong's: an enclitic particle of asseveration by way of contrast; in sooth\nKJV: —(used only with other particles in the comparative, as G2544, G3305, G5105, G5106, etc.)\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G2544 \nSee: G3305 \nSee: G5105 \nSee: G5106 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τοί G5520",
    5105: "τοιγαροῦν (toigaroûn | toy-gar-oon')\n\nDerivation: from G5104 and G1063 and G3767\nStrong's: truly for then, i.e. consequently\nKJV: —there-(where-)fore.\n\nSee: G5104 \nSee: G1063 \nSee: G3767 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τοιγαροῦν, τοίγε",
    5106: "τοίνυν (toínyn | toy'-noon)\n\nDerivation: from G5104 and G3568\nStrong's: truly now, i.e. accordingly\nKJV: —then, therefore.\n\nSee: G5104 \nSee: G3568 \n\nCognate Group: G5091 (honour), G5092 (honour), G5093 (dear), G5094 (costliness)\nVariants: τοίνυν",
    5107: "τοιόσδε (toiósde | toy-os'-deh)\n\nDerivation: (including the other inflections); from a derivative of 5104 and G1161\nStrong's: such-like then, i.e. so great\nKJV: —such.\n\nSee: G1161 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: τοιόσδε",
    5108: "τοιοῦτος (toioûtos | toy-oo'-tos)\n\nDerivation: (including the other inflections); from G5104 and G3778\nStrong's: truly this, i.e. of this sort (to denote character or individuality)\nKJV: —like, such (an one).\n\nSee: G5104 \nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: τοιοῦτος",
    5109: "τοῖχος (toîchos | toy'-khos)\n\nDerivation: another form of G5038\nStrong's: a wall\nKJV: —wall.\n\nSee: G5038 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: τοῖχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1447 גָּדֵר, H2346 חֹומָה, H2434 חַיִץ, H3796 כֹּתֶל, H7023 קִיר",
    5110: "τόκος (tókos | tok'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5088\nStrong's: interest on money loaned (as a produce)\nKJV: —usury.\n\nSee: G5088 \n\nCognate Group: G5109 (wall), G3319 (among), G3320 (middle wall)\nVariants: τόκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H5386 נְשִׁי, H5392 נֶשֶׁךְ, H8432 תָּוֶךְ",
    5111: "τολμάω (tolmáō | tol-mah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from τόλμα (boldness\nStrong's: probably itself from the base of G5056 through the idea of extreme conduct); to venture (objectively or in act; while G2292 is rather subjective or in feeling); by implication, to be courageous\nKJV: —be bold, boldly, dare, durst.\n\nSee: G5056 \nSee: G2292 \n\nCognate Group: G662 (be very bold), G5112 (the more boldly), G5113 (presumptuous), G5111 (be bold)\nVariants: τολμάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3947 לָקַח, H4390 מָלֵא",
    5112: "τολμηρότερον (tolmēróteron | tol-may-rot'-er-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of the compound of a derivative of the base of 5111 (as adverb)\nStrong's: more daringly, i.e. with greater confidence than otherwise\nKJV: —the more boldly.\n\nCognate Group: G662 (be very bold), G5112 (the more boldly), G5113 (presumptuous), G5111 (be bold)\nVariants: τολμηρότερον, τολμηρός, τολμηροτέρως",
    5113: "τολμητής (tolmētḗs | tol-may-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G5111\nStrong's: a daring (audacious) man\nKJV: —presumptuous.\n\nSee: G5111 \n\nCognate Group: G662 (be very bold), G5112 (the more boldly), G5113 (presumptuous), G5111 (be bold)\nVariants: τολμητής",
    5114: "τομώτερος (tomṓteros | tom-o'-ter-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative of a derivative of the primary τέμνω (to cut\nStrong's: more comprehensive or decisive than G2875, as if by a single stroke; whereas that implies repeated blows, like hacking); more keen\nKJV: —sharper.\n\nSee: G2875 \n\nCognate Group: G662 (be very bold), G5112 (the more boldly), G5113 (presumptuous), G5111 (be bold)\nVariants: τομώτερος, τομός",
    5115: "τόξον (tóxon | tox'-on)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5088\nStrong's: a bow (apparently as the simplest fabric)\nKJV: —bow.\n\nSee: G5088 \n\nCognate Group: G662 (be very bold), G5112 (the more boldly), G5113 (presumptuous), G5111 (be bold)\nVariants: τόξον \nHebrew Equivalents: H827 אַשְׁפָּה, H2671 חֵץ, H7198 קֶשֶׁת",
    5116: "τοπάζιον (topázion | top-ad'-zee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative (alternately) of τόπαζος \nStrong's: (a \"topaz\"; of uncertain origin); a gem, probably the chrysolite\nKJV: —topaz.\n\nCognate Group: G662 (be very bold), G5112 (the more boldly), G5113 (presumptuous), G5111 (be bold)\nVariants: τοπάζιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H6337 פָּז, H6357 פִּטְדָה",
    5117: "τόπος (tópos | top'-os)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a spot (general in space, but limited by occupancy; whereas G5561 is a large but participle locality), i.e. location (as a position, home, tract, etc.); figuratively, condition, opportunity; specially, a scabbard\nKJV: —coast, licence, place, X plain, quarter, + rock, room, where.\n\nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τόπος, Πόντος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1004 בַּיִת, H3027 יָד, H3653 כֵּן, H3733 כַּר, H4057 מִדְבָּר, H4161 מֹוצָא, H4349 מָכֹון, H4437 מַלְכוּ, H4583 מָעֹון, H4725 מָקֹום, H4999 נָאָה, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H5116 נָוֶה, H5159 נַחֲלָה, H5519 סָךְ, H5977 עֹמֶד, H6279 עָתַר, H8478 תַּחַת",
    5118: "τοσοῦτος (tosoûtos | tos-oo'-tos)\n\nDerivation: from τόσος (so much\nStrong's: apparently from G3588 and G3739) and G3778 (including its variations); so vast as this, i.e. such (in quantity, amount, number of space)\nKJV: —as large, so great (long, many, much), these many.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G3739 \nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τοσοῦτος",
    5119: "τότε (tóte | tot'-eh)\n\nDerivation: from (the neuter of) G3588 and G3753\nStrong's: the when, i.e. at the time that (of the past or future, also in consecution)\nKJV: —that time, then.\n\nSee: G3753 \nSee: G3588 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τότε",
    5120: "τοῦ (toû | too)\n\nDerivation: properly, the genitive case of G3588\nStrong's: sometimes used for G5127 ; of this person\nKJV: —his.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G5127 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τοῦ, ὁ G3836",
    5121: "τοὐναντίον (tounantíon | too-nan-tee'-on)\n\nDerivation: contraction for the neuter of G3588 and G1726\nStrong's: on the contrary\nKJV: —contrariwise.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G1726 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τοὐναντίον, τὸ ἐναντίον",
    5122: "τοὔνομα (toúnoma | too'-no-mah)\n\nDerivation: contraction for the neuter of G3588 and G3686\nStrong's: the name (is)\nKJV: —named.\n\nSee: G3588 \nSee: G3686 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τοὔνομα, τὸ ὄνομα",
    5123: "τουτέστι (toutésti | toot-es'-tee)\n\nDerivation: contraction for G5124 and G2076\nStrong's: that is\nKJV: —that is (to say).\n\nSee: G2076 \nSee: G5124 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τουτέστι, τουτέστιν G4047, G5542",
    5124: "τοῦτο (toûto | too'-to)\n\nDerivation: neuter singular nominative or accusative case of G3778\nStrong's: that thing\nKJV: —here (-unto), it, partly, self(-same), so, that (intent), the same, there(-fore, -unto), this, thus, where(-fore).\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τοῦτο, οὗτος",
    5125: "τούτοις (toútois | too'-toice)\n\nDerivation: dative case plural masculine or neuter of G3778\nStrong's: to (for, in, with or by) these (persons or things)\nKJV: —such, them, there(-in, -with), these, this, those.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τούτοις, οὗτος",
    5126: "τοῦτον (toûton | too'-ton)\n\nDerivation: accusative case singular masculine of G3778\nStrong's: this (person, as objective of verb or preposition)\nKJV: —him, the same, that, this.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τοῦτον, οὗτος",
    5127: "τούτου (toútou | too'-too)\n\nDerivation: genitive case singular masculine or neuter of G3778\nStrong's: of (from or concerning) this (person or thing)\nKJV: —here(-by), him, it, + such manner of, that, thence(-forth), thereabout, this, thus.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τούτου, οὗτος",
    5128: "τούτους (toútous | too'-tooce)\n\nDerivation: accusative case plural masculine of G3778\nStrong's: these (persons, as objective of verb or preposition)\nKJV: —such, them, these, this.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τούτους, οὗτος",
    5129: "τούτῳ (toútōi | too'-to)\n\nDerivation: dative case singular masculine or neuter of G3778\nStrong's: to (in, with or by) this (person or thing)\nKJV: —here(-by, -in), him, one, the same, there(-in), this.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τούτῳ, οὗτος",
    5130: "τούτων (toútōn | too'-tone)\n\nDerivation: genitive case plural masculine or neuter of G3778\nStrong's: of (from or concerning) these (persons or things)\nKJV: —such, their, these (things), they, this sort, those.\n\nSee: G3778 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τούτων, οὗτος",
    5131: "τράγος (trágos | trag'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5176\nStrong's: a he-goat (as a gnawer)\nKJV: —goat.\n\nSee: G5176 \n\nCognate Group: G5117 (coast)\nVariants: τράγος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6260 עַתּוּד, H6842 צָפִיר, H8495 תַּיִשׁ",
    5132: "τράπεζα (trápeza | trap'-ed-zah)\n\nDerivation: probably contracted from G5064 and G3979\nStrong's: a table or stool (as being four-legged), usually for food (figuratively, a meal); also a counter for money (figuratively, a broker's office for loans at interest)\nKJV: —bank, meat, table.\n\nSee: G5064 \nSee: G3979 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: τράπεζα \nHebrew Equivalents: H3899 לֶחֶם, H6341 פַּח, H7607 שְׁאֵר, H7979 שֻׁלְחָן",
    5133: "τραπεζίτης (trapezítēs | trap-ed-zee'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G5132\nStrong's: a money-broker or banker\nKJV: —exchanger.\n\nSee: G5132 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: τραπεζίτης",
    5134: "τραῦμα (traûma | trow'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from the base of τιτρώσκω (to wound\nStrong's: akin to the base of G2352, G5147, G5149, etc.); a wound\nKJV: —wound.\n\nSee: G2352 \nSee: G5147 \nSee: G5149 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: τραῦμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2483 חֳלִי, H2490 חָלַל, H5424 נֶתֶק, H6481 פָּצַע",
    5135: "τραυματίζω (traumatízō | trow-mat-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5134\nStrong's: to inflict a wound\nKJV: —wound.\n\nSee: G5134 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: τραυματίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2342 חוּל, H2490 חָלַל, H6481 פָּצַע",
    5136: "τραχηλίζω (trachēlízō | trakh-ay-lid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5137\nStrong's: to seize by the throat or neck, i.e. to expose the gullet of a victim for killing (generally, to lay bare)\nKJV: —opened.\n\nSee: G5137 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: τραχηλίζω, τραχηλίζομαι",
    5137: "τράχηλος (tráchēlos | trakh'-ay-los)\n\nDerivation: probably from G5143 (through the idea of mobility)\nStrong's: the throat (neck), i.e. (figuratively) life\nKJV: —neck.\n\nSee: G5143 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: τράχηλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1621 גַּרְגְּרֹות, H1627 גָּרֹון, H6203 עֹרֶף, H6676 צַוַּאר",
    5138: "τραχύς (trachýs | trakh-oos')\n\nDerivation: perhaps strengthened from the base of G4486 (as if jagged by rents)\nStrong's: uneven, rocky (reefy)\nKJV: —rock, rough.\n\nSee: G4486 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: τραχύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H386 אֵיתָן, H7406 רֶכֶס",
    5139: "Τραχωνῖτις (Trachōnîtis | trakh-o-nee'-tis)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5138\nStrong's: rough district; Trachonitis, a region of Syria\nKJV: —Trachonitis.\n\nSee: G5138 \n\nCognate Group: G5132 (bank), G5133 (exchanger)\nVariants: Τραχωνῖτις",
    5140: "τρεῖς (treîs | trice), or neuter τρία (tría | tree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: a primary (plural) number\nStrong's: \"three\"\nKJV: —three.\n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρεῖς, Τρεῖς ταβέρναι, Τρεῖς",
    5141: "τρέμω (trémō | trem'-o)\n\nDerivation: strengthened from a primary τρέω (to \"dread\", \"terrify\")\nStrong's: to \"tremble\" or fear\nKJV: —be afraid, trembling.\n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρέμω",
    5142: "τρέφω (tréphō | tref'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (properly, τρέφω\nStrong's: but perhaps strengthened from the base of G5157 through the idea of convolution); properly, to stiffen, i.e. fatten (by implication, to cherish (with food, etc.), pamper, rear)\nKJV: —bring up, feed, nourish.\n\nSee: G5157 \n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρέφω \nHebrew Equivalents: H398 אָכַל, H1431 גָּדַל, H2110 זוּן, H2342 חוּל, H2421 חָיָה, H3557 כּוּל, H4770 מַרְבֵּק, H7200 רָאָה",
    5143: "τρέχω (tréchō | trekh'-o)\n\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb (properly, θρέχω\nStrong's: compare G2359); which uses δρέμω (drémō | drem'-o)\n (the base of G1408) as alternate in certain tenses; to run or walk hastily (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —have course, run.\n\nSee: G2359 \nSee: G1408 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τρέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1750 דּוּץ, H7323 רוּץ",
    5144: "τριάκοντα (triákonta | tree-ak'-on-tah)\n\nDerivation: the decade of G5140\nStrong's: thirty\nKJV: —thirty.\n\nSee: G5140 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τριάκοντα",
    5145: "τριακόσιοι (triakósioi | tree-ak-os'-ee-oy)\n\nDerivation: plural from G5140 and G1540\nStrong's: three hundred\nKJV: —three hundred.\n\nSee: G5140 \nSee: G1540 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τριακόσιοι",
    5146: "τρίβολος (tríbolos | trib'-ol-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5140 and G956\nStrong's: properly, a crow-foot (three-pronged obstruction in war), i.e. (by analogy) a thorny plant (caltrop)\nKJV: —brier, thistle.\n\nSee: G5140 \nSee: G956 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τρίβολος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1863 דַּרְדַּר, H2757 חָרִיץ, H6791 צֵן",
    5147: "τρίβος (tríbos | tree'-bos)\n\nDerivation: from τρίβω (to \"rub\"\nStrong's: akin to τείρω, τρύω, and the base of G5131, G5134); a rut or worn track\nKJV: —path.\n\nSee: G5131 \nSee: G5134 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τρίβος \nHebrew Equivalents: H734 אֹרַח, H1870 דֶּרֶךְ, H4546 מְסִלָּה, H4570 מַעְגָּל, H5410 נָתִיב, H7635 שָׁבִיל",
    5148: "τριετία (trietía | tree-et-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5140 and G2094\nStrong's: a three years' period (triennium)\nKJV: —space of three years.\n\nSee: G5140 \nSee: G2094 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τριετία",
    5149: "τρίζω (trízō | trid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to creak (squeak), i.e. (by analogy) to grate the teeth (in frenzy)\nKJV: —gnash.\n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τρίζω",
    5150: "τρίμηνον (trímēnon | trim'-ay-non)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G5140 and G3376 as noun\nStrong's: a three months' space\nKJV: —three months.\n\nSee: G5140 \nSee: G3376 \n\nCognate Group: G4274 (forerunner), G5143 (have course), G1408 (course)\nVariants: τρίμηνον, τρίμηνος",
    5151: "τρίς (trís | trece)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5140\nStrong's: three times\nKJV: —three times, thrice.\n\nSee: G5140 \n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρίς",
    5152: "τρίστεγον (trístegon | tris'-teg-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G5140 and G4721 as noun\nStrong's: a third roof (story)\nKJV: —third loft.\n\nSee: G5140 \nSee: G4721 \n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρίστεγον, τρίστεγος",
    5153: "τρισχίλιοι (trischílioi | tris-khil'-ee-oy)\n\nDerivation: from G5151 and G5507\nStrong's: three times a thousand\nKJV: —three thousand.\n\nSee: G5151 \nSee: G5507 \n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρισχίλιοι",
    5154: "τρίτος (trítos | tree'-tos)\n\nDerivation: ordinal from G5140\nStrong's: third; neuter (as noun) a third part, or (as adverb) a (or the) third time, thirdly\nKJV: —third(-ly).\n\nSee: G5140 \n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρίτος, τρίτον",
    5155: "τρίχινος (tríchinos | trikh'-ee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G2359\nStrong's: hairy, i.e. made of hair (mohair)\nKJV: —of hair.\n\nSee: G2359 \n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρίχινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8181 שֵׂעָר",
    5156: "τρόμος (trómos | trom'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5141\nStrong's: a \"trembling\", i.e. quaking with fear\nKJV: —+ tremble(-ing).\n\nSee: G5141 \n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τρόμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H367 אֵימָה, H4172 מֹורָא, H4288 מְחִתָּה, H5892 עִיר, H6343 פַּחַד, H7461 רַעַד, H7461 רַעַד, H7578 רְתֵת",
    5157: "τροπή (tropḗ | trop-ay')\n\nDerivation: from an apparently primary τρέπω to turn\nStrong's: a turn (\"trope\"), i.e. revolution (figuratively, variation)\nKJV: —turning.\n\nCognate Group: G5154 (third), G5140 (three), G5153 (three thousand), G5151 (three times), G5152 (third loft)\nVariants: τροπή",
    5158: "τρόπος (trópos | trop'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5157\nStrong's: a turn, i.e. (by implication) mode or style (especially with preposition or relative prefix as adverb, like); figuratively, deportment or character\nKJV: —(even) as, conversation, (+ like) manner, (+ by any) means, way.\n\nSee: G5157 \n\nCognate Group: G5159 (suffer the manners), G5158 (as)\nVariants: τρόπος \nHebrew Equivalents: H834 אֲשֶׁר, H834 אֲשֶׁר, H1697 דָּבָר, H2940 טַעַם, H6040 עֳנִי",
    5159: "τροποφορέω (tropophoréō | trop-of-or-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5158 and G5409\nStrong's: to endure one's habits\nKJV: —suffer the manners.\n\nSee: G5158 \nSee: G5409 \n\nCognate Group: G5159 (suffer the manners), G5158 (as)\nVariants: τροποφορέω, τροφοφορέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5375 נָשָׂא, H5375 נָשָׂא",
    5160: "τροφή (trophḗ | trof-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5142\nStrong's: nourishment (literally or figuratively); by implication, rations (wages)\nKJV: —food, meat.\n\nSee: G5142 \n\nCognate Group: G5162 (nurse), G5160 (food)\nVariants: τροφή \nHebrew Equivalents: H400 אֹכֶל, H1715 דָּגָן, H2964 טֶרֶף, H3899 לֶחֶם, H4202 מָזֹון",
    5161: "Τρόφιμος (Tróphimos | trof'-ee-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G5160\nStrong's: nutritive; Trophimus, a Christian\nKJV: —Trophimus.\n\nSee: G5160 \n\nCognate Group: G5162 (nurse), G5160 (food)\nVariants: Τρόφιμος",
    5162: "τροφός (trophós | trof-os')\n\nDerivation: from G5142\nStrong's: a nourisher, i.e. nurse\nKJV: —nurse.\n\nSee: G5142 \n\nCognate Group: G5162 (nurse), G5160 (food)\nVariants: τροφός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3243 יָנַק",
    5163: "τροχιά (trochiá | trokh-ee-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G5164\nStrong's: a track (as a wheel-rut), i.e. (figuratively) a course of conduct\nKJV: —path.\n\nSee: G5164 \n\nCognate Group: G5162 (nurse), G5160 (food)\nVariants: τροχιά \nHebrew Equivalents: H4570 מַעְגָּל",
    5164: "τροχός (trochós | trokh-os')\n\nDerivation: from G5143\nStrong's: a wheel (as a runner), i.e. (figuratively) a circuit of physical effects\nKJV: —course.\n\nSee: G5143 \n\nCognate Group: G5162 (nurse), G5160 (food)\nVariants: τροχός \nHebrew Equivalents: H212 אֹופָן, H1534 גַּלְגַּל, H4173 מֹורַג",
    5165: "τρύβλιον (trýblion | troob'-lee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a bowl\nKJV: —dish.\n\nCognate Group: G5162 (nurse), G5160 (food)\nVariants: τρύβλιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3709 כַּף, H7086 קְעָרָה",
    5166: "τρυγάω (trygáō | troo-gah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of τρύγω (to dry) meaning ripe fruit (as if dry)\nStrong's: to collect the vintage\nKJV: —gather.\n\nCognate Group: G5162 (nurse), G5160 (food)\nVariants: τρυγάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1219 בָּצַר, H1869 דָּרַךְ, H2490 חָלַל, H2554 חָמַס, H6168 עָרָה, H7114 קָצַר",
    5167: "τρυγών (trygṓn | troo-gone')\n\nDerivation: from τρύζω (to murmur\nStrong's: akin to G5149, but denoting a duller sound); a turtle-dove (as cooing)\nKJV: —turtle-dove.\n\nSee: G5149 \n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: τρυγών \nHebrew Equivalents: H8447 תֹּור",
    5168: "τρυμαλιά (trymaliá | troo-mal-ee-ah')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of τρύω (to wear away\nStrong's: akin to the base of G5134, G5147 and G5176); an orifice, i.e. needle's eye\nKJV: —eye. Compare G5169 .\n\nSee: G5169 \nSee: G5134 \nSee: G5147 \nSee: G5176 \n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: τρυμαλιά \nHebrew Equivalents: H2288 חֲגָו, H4492 מִנְהָרָה, H5357 נָקִיק, H5595 סָפָה",
    5169: "τρύπημα (trýpēma | troo'-pay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the base of G5168\nStrong's: an aperture, i.e. a needle's eye\nKJV: —eye.\n\nSee: G5168 \n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: τρύπημα, τρῆμα",
    5170: "Τρύφαινα (Trýphaina | troo'-fahee-nah)\n\nDerivation: from G5172\nStrong's: luxurious; Tryphæna, a Christian woman\nKJV: —Tryphena.\n\nSee: G5172 \n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: Τρύφαινα",
    5171: "τρυφάω (trypháō | troo-fah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5172\nStrong's: to indulge in luxury\nKJV: —live in pleasure.\n\nSee: G5172 \n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: τρυφάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5727 עָדַן, H6026 עָנַג",
    5172: "τρυφή (tryphḗ | troo-fay')\n\nDerivation: from θρύπτω (to break up or (figuratively) enfeeble, especially the mind and body by indulgence)\nStrong's: effeminacy, i.e. luxury or debauchery\nKJV: —delicately, riot.\n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: τρυφή \nHebrew Equivalents: H134 אֶדֶן, H8588 תַּעֲנוּג, H8597 תִּפְאָרָה",
    5173: "Τρυφῶσα (Tryphōsa | troo-fo'-sah)\n\nDerivation: from G5172\nStrong's: luxuriating; Tryphosa, a Christian female\nKJV: —Tryphosa.\n\nSee: G5172 \n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: Τρυφῶσα",
    5174: "Τρωάς (Trōás | tro-as')\n\nDerivation: from Τρός (a Trojan)\nStrong's: the Troad (or plain of Troy), i.e. Troas, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Troas.\n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: Τρωάς, Τρῳάς",
    5175: "Τρωγύλλιον (Trōgýllion | tro-gool'-lee-on)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Trogyllium, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Trogyllium.\n\nCognate Group: G5167 (turtle-dove), G4058 (dove)\nVariants: Τρωγύλλιον G5590, G5591",
    5176: "τρώγω (trṓgō | tro'-go)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from a collateral form of the base of G5134 and G5147 through the idea of corrosion or wear\nStrong's: or perhaps rather of a base of G5167 and G5149 through the idea of a craunching sound; to gnaw or chew, i.e. (generally) to eat\nKJV: —eat.\n\nSee: G5167 \nSee: G5149 \nSee: G5134 \nSee: G5147 \n\nCognate Group: G5176 (eat)\nVariants: τρώγω",
    5177: "τυγχάνω (tynchánō | toong-khan'-o)\n\nDerivation: probably for an obsolete τύχω (for which the middle voice of another alternate τεύχω (to make ready or bring to pass) is used in certain tenses\nStrong's: akin to the base of G5088 through the idea of effecting; properly, to affect; or (specially), to hit or light upon (as a mark to be reached), i.e. (transitively) to attain or secure an object or end, or (intransitively) to happen (as if meeting with); but in the latter application only impersonal (with G1487), i.e. perchance; or (present participle) as adjective, usual (as if commonly met with, with G3756, extraordinary), neuter (as adverb) perhaps; or (with another verb) as adverb, by accident (as it were)\nKJV: —be, chance, enjoy, little, obtain, X refresh…self, + special. Compare G5180 .\n\nSee: G5180 \nSee: G5088 \nSee: G1487 \nSee: G3756 \n\nCognate Group: G1783 (intercession), G5177 (be), G5241 (make intercession for), G1793 (deal with)\nVariants: τυγχάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4672 מָצָא",
    5178: "τυμπανίζω (tympanízō | toom-pan-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5180 (meaning a drum, \"tympanum\")\nStrong's: to stretch on an instrument of torture resembling a drum, and thus beat to death\nKJV: —torture.\n\nSee: G5180 \n\nCognate Group: G1783 (intercession), G5177 (be), G5241 (make intercession for), G1793 (deal with)\nVariants: τυμπανίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H8427 תָּוָה",
    5179: "τύπος (týpos | too'-pos)\n\nDerivation: from G5180\nStrong's: a die (as struck), i.e. (by implication) a stamp or scar; by analogy, a shape, i.e. a statue, (figuratively) style or resemblance; specially, a sampler (\"type\"), i.e. a model (for imitation) or instance (for warning)\nKJV: —en-(ex-)ample, fashion, figure, form, manner, pattern, print.\n\nSee: G5180 \n\nCognate Group: G499 (figure), G5296 (form), G5179 (en-ample)\nVariants: τύπος, τυπικῶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H6754 צֶלֶם, H8403 תַּבְנִית",
    5180: "τύπτω (týptō | toop'-to)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (in a strengthened form)\nStrong's: to \"thump\", i.e. cudgel or pummel (properly, with a stick or bastinado), but in any case by repeated blows; thus differing from G3817 and G3960, which denote a (usually single) blow with the hand or any instrument, or G4141 with the fist (or a hammer), or G4474 with the palm; as well as from G5177, an accidental collision); by implication, to punish; figuratively, to offend (the conscience)\nKJV: —beat, smite, strike, wound.\n\nSee: G3817 \nSee: G3960 \nSee: G4141 \nSee: G4474 \nSee: G5177 \n\nCognate Group: G5180 (beat)\nVariants: τύπτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2505 חָלַק, H5062 נָגַף, H5221 נָכָה",
    5181: "Τύραννος (Týrannos | too'-ran-nos)\n\nDerivation: a provincial form of the derivative of the base of G2962\nStrong's: a \"tyrant\"; Tyrannus, an Ephesian\nKJV: —Tyrannus.\n\nSee: G2962 \n\nCognate Group: G5180 (beat)\nVariants: Τύραννος, Τύραννοσ1",
    5182: "τυρβάζω (tyrbázō | toor-bad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from τύρβη (Latin turba, a crowd\nStrong's: akin to G2351); to make \"turbid\", i.e. disturb\nKJV: —trouble.\n\nSee: G2351 \n\nCognate Group: G5182 (trouble), G2350 (make ado), G2351 (tumult)\nVariants: τυρβάζω, θορυβάζω",
    5183: "Τύριος (Týrios | too'-ree-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5184\nStrong's: a Tyrian, i.e. inhabitant of Tyrus\nKJV: —of Tyre.\n\nSee: G5184 \n\nCognate Group: G5182 (trouble), G2350 (make ado), G2351 (tumult)\nVariants: Τύριος",
    5184: "Τύρος (Týros | too'-ros)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6865): Tyrus (i.e. Tsor), a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Tyre.\nSee: H6865\n\nCognate Group: G5182 (trouble), G2350 (make ado), G2351 (tumult)\nVariants: Τύρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6865 צֹר",
    5185: "τυφλός (typhlós | toof-los')\n\nDerivation: from, G5187\nStrong's: opaque (as if smoky), i.e. (by analogy) blind (physically or mentally)\nKJV: —blind.\n\nSee: G5187 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: τυφλός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5787 עִוֵּר",
    5186: "τυφλόω (typhlóō | toof-lo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5185\nStrong's: to make blind, i.e. (figuratively) to obscure\nKJV: —blind.\n\nSee: G5185 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: τυφλόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5787 עִוֵּר",
    5187: "τυφόω (typhóō | toof-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5188\nStrong's: to envelop with smoke, i.e. (figuratively) to inflate with self-conceit\nKJV: —high-minded, be lifted up with pride, be proud.\n\nSee: G5188 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: τυφόω, τυφόομαι",
    5188: "τύφω (týphō | too'-fo)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to make a smoke, i.e. slowly consume without flame\nKJV: —smoke.\n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: τύφω, τύφομαι",
    5189: "τυφωνικός (typhōnikós | too-fo-nee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5188\nStrong's: stormy (as if smoky)\nKJV: —tempestuous.\n\nSee: G5188 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: τυφωνικός",
    5190: "Τυχικός (Tychikós | too-khee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5177\nStrong's: fortuitous, i.e. fortunate; Tychicus, a Christian\nKJV: —Tychicus.\n\nSee: G5177 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: Τυχικός",
    5191: "ὑακίνθινος (hyakínthinos | hoo-ak-in'-thee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G5192\nStrong's: \"hyacinthine\" or \"jacinthine\", i.e. deep blue\nKJV: —jacinth.\n\nSee: G5192 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: ὑακίνθινος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8476 תַּחַשׁ, H8504 תְּכֵלֶת",
    5192: "ὑάκινθος (hyákinthos | hoo-ak'-in-thos)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: the \"hyacinth\" or \"jacinth\", i.e. some gem of a deep blue color, probably the zirkon\nKJV: — jacinth.\n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: ὑάκινθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8476 תַּחַשׁ, H8504 תְּכֵלֶת",
    5193: "ὑάλινος (hyálinos | hoo-al'-ee-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G5194\nStrong's: glassy, i.e. transparent\nKJV: —of glass.\n\nSee: G5194 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: ὑάλινος",
    5194: "ὕαλος (hýalos | hoo'-al-os)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the same as G5205 (as being transparent like rain)\nStrong's: glass\nKJV: —glass.\n\nSee: G5205 \n\nCognate Group: G5186 (blind), G5185 (blind)\nVariants: ὕαλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2137 זְכוּכִית",
    5195: "ὑβρίζω (hybrízō | hoo-brid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5196\nStrong's: to exercise violence, i.e. abuse\nKJV: —use despitefully, reproach, entreat shamefully (spitefully).\n\nSee: G5196 \n\nCognate Group: G5195 (use despitefully), G1796 (do despite unto), G5196 (harm), G5197 (despiteful)\nVariants: ὑβρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1343 גֵּאֶה, H1343 גֵּאֶה, H5937 עָלַז, H7043 קָלַל",
    5196: "ὕβρις (hýbris | hoo'-bris)\n\nDerivation: from G5228\nStrong's: insolence (as over-bearing), i.e. insult, injury\nKJV: —harm, hurt, reproach.\n\nSee: G5228 \n\nCognate Group: G5195 (use despitefully), G1796 (do despite unto), G5196 (harm), G5197 (despiteful)\nVariants: ὕβρις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1344 גֵּאָה, H1347 גָּאֹון, H1466 גֵּוָה, H2087 זָדֹון, H3944 לָצֹון, H5947 עַלִּיז, H7312 רוּם",
    5197: "ὑβριστής (hybristḗs | hoo-bris-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G5195\nStrong's: an insulter, i.e. maltreater\nKJV: —despiteful, injurious.\n\nSee: G5195 \n\nCognate Group: G5195 (use despitefully), G1796 (do despite unto), G5196 (harm), G5197 (despiteful)\nVariants: ὑβριστής \nHebrew Equivalents: H1341 גֵּא, H1343 גֵּאֶה, H7312 רוּם",
    5198: "ὑγιαίνω (hygiaínō | hoog-ee-ah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G5199\nStrong's: to have sound health, i.e. be well (in body); figuratively, to be uncorrupt (true in doctrine)\nKJV: —be in health, (be safe and) sound, (be) whole(-some).\n\nSee: G5199 \n\nCognate Group: G5198 (be in health), G5199 (sound)\nVariants: ὑγιαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7965 שָׁלֹום, H7999 שָׁלַם",
    5199: "ὑγιής (hygiḗs | hoog-ee-ace')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G837\nStrong's: healthy, i.e. well (in body); figuratively, true (in doctrine)\nKJV: —sound, whole.\n\nSee: G837 \n\nCognate Group: G5198 (be in health), G5199 (sound)\nVariants: ὑγιής \nHebrew Equivalents: H2416 חַי, H2421 חָיָה, H7965 שָׁלֹום",
    5200: "ὑγρός (hygrós | hoo-gros')\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5205\nStrong's: wet (as if with rain), i.e. (by implication) sappy (fresh)\nKJV: —green.\n\nSee: G5205 \n\nCognate Group: G5198 (be in health), G5199 (sound)\nVariants: ὑγρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3892 לַח, H7373 רָטֹב",
    5201: "ὑδρία (hydría | hoo-dree-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G5204\nStrong's: a water-jar, i.e. receptacle for family supply\nKJV: —water-pot.\n\nSee: G5204 \n\nCognate Group: G5198 (be in health), G5199 (sound)\nVariants: ὑδρία \nHebrew Equivalents: H3537 כַּד",
    5202: "ὑδροποτέω (hydropotéō | hoo-drop-ot-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5204 and a derivative of G4095\nStrong's: to be a water-drinker, i.e. to abstain from vinous beverages\nKJV: —drink water.\n\nSee: G5204 \nSee: G4095 \n\nCognate Group: G5198 (be in health), G5199 (sound)\nVariants: ὑδροποτέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4325 מַיִם",
    5203: "ὑδρωπικός (hydrōpikós | hoo-dro-pik-os')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5204 and a derivative of G3700 (as if looking watery)\nStrong's: to be \"dropsical\"\nKJV: —have the dropsy.\n\nSee: G5204 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G5198 (be in health), G5199 (sound)\nVariants: ὑδρωπικός",
    5204: "ὕδωρ (hýdōr | hoo'-dore), and genitive case, ὕδατος (hýdatos | hoo'-dat-os)\n\n, etc.\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5205\nStrong's: water (as if rainy) literally or figuratively\nKJV: —water.\n\nSee: G5205 \n\nCognate Group: G5204 (water)\nVariants: ὕδωρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H4325 מַיִם, H4726 מָקֹור, H5140 נָזַל",
    5205: "ὑετός (hyetós | hoo-et-os')\n\nDerivation: from a primary ὕω (to rain)\nStrong's: rain, especially a shower\nKJV: —rain.\n\nCognate Group: G5204 (water)\nVariants: ὑετός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1652 גָּשַׁם, H1653 גֶּשֶׁם, H4306 מָטַר, H4306 מָטַר, H4325 מַיִם, H7722 שֹׁוא, H7858 שֶׁטֶף",
    5206: "υἱοθεσία (huiothesía | hwee-oth-es-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed compound of G5207 and a derivative of G5087\nStrong's: the placing as a son, i.e. adoption (figuratively, Christian sonship in respect to God)\nKJV: —adoption (of children, of sons).\n\nSee: G5207 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: υἱοθεσία",
    5207: "υἱός (huiós | hwee-os')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a \"son\" (sometimes of animals), used very widely of immediate, remote or figuratively, kinship\nKJV: —child, foal, son.\n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: υἱός \nHebrew Equivalents: H376 אִישׁ, H1004 בַּיִת, H1121 בֵּן, H1247 בַּר, H2233 זֶרַע, H3206 יֶלֶד, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה",
    5208: "ὕλη (hýlē | hoo-lay')\n\nDerivation: perhaps akin to G3586\nStrong's: a forest, i.e. (by implication) fuel\nKJV: —matter.\n\nSee: G3586 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: ὕλη \nHebrew Equivalents: H3121 יָוֵן, H5521 סֻכָּה, H8068 שָׁמִיר",
    5209: "ὑμᾶς (hymâs | hoo-mas')\n\nDerivation: accusative case of G5210\nStrong's: you (as the objective of a verb or preposition)\nKJV: —ye, you (+ -ward), your (+ own).\n\nSee: G5210 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: ὑμᾶς, σύ",
    5210: "ὑμεῖς (hymeîs | hoo-mice')\n\nDerivation: irregular plural of G4771\nStrong's: you (as subjective of verb)\nKJV: —ye (yourselves), you.\n\nSee: G4771 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: ὑμεῖς, σύ",
    5211: "Ὑμεναῖος (Hymenaîos | hoo-men-ah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from Ὑμήν (the god of weddings)\nStrong's: \"hymenæal\"; Hymenæus, an opponent of Christianity\nKJV: —Hymenæus.\n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: Ὑμεναῖος, Ὑμέναιος",
    5212: "ὑμέτερος (hyméteros | hoo-met'-er-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5210\nStrong's: yours, i.e. pertaining to you\nKJV: —your (own).\n\nSee: G5210 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: ὑμέτερος",
    5213: "ὑμῖν (hymîn | hoo-min')\n\nDerivation: irregular dative case of G5210\nStrong's: to (with or by) you\nKJV: —ye, you, your(-selves).\n\nSee: G5210 \n\nCognate Group: G5206 (adoption), G5107 (such)\nVariants: ὑμῖν, σύ",
    5214: "ὑμνέω (hymnéō | hoom-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5215\nStrong's: to hymn, i.e. sing a religious ode; by implication, to celebrate (God) in song\nKJV: —sing a hymn (praise unto).\n\nSee: G5215 \n\nCognate Group: G5214 (sing a hymn), G5215 (hymn)\nVariants: ὑμνέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל, H2167 זָמַר, H3034 יָדָה, H3045 יָדַע, H7442 רָנַן, H7892 שִׁיר",
    5215: "ὕμνος (hýmnos | hoom'-nos)\n\nDerivation: apparently from a simpler (obsolete) form of ὑδέω (to celebrate\nStrong's: probably akin to G103 ; compare H5667); a \"hymn\" or religious ode (one of the Psalms)\nKJV: —hymn.\n\nSee: G103 \nSee: H5667\n\nCognate Group: G5214 (sing a hymn), G5215 (hymn)\nVariants: ὕμνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1984 הָלַל, H5058 נְגִינָה, H7892 שִׁיר, H8416 תְּהִלָּה, H8605 תְּפִלָּה",
    5216: "ὑμῶν (hymōn | hoo-mone')\n\nDerivation: genitive case of G5210\nStrong's: of (from or concerning) you\nKJV: —ye, you, your (own, -selves).\n\nSee: G5210 \n\nCognate Group: G5214 (sing a hymn), G5215 (hymn)\nVariants: ὑμῶν, σύ",
    5217: "ὑπάγω (hypágō | hoop-ag'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G71\nStrong's: to lead (oneself) under, i.e. withdraw or retire (as if sinking out of sight), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —depart, get hence, go (a-)way.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G5217 (depart)\nVariants: ὑπάγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1980 הָלַךְ",
    5218: "ὑπακοή (hypakoḗ | hoop-ak-o-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5219\nStrong's: attentive hearkening, i.e. (by implication) compliance or submission\nKJV: —obedience, (make) obedient, obey(-ing).\n\nSee: G5219 \n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: ὑπακοή \nHebrew Equivalents: H6038 עֲנָוָה",
    5219: "ὑπακούω (hypakoúō | hoop-ak-oo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G191\nStrong's: to hear under (as a subordinate), i.e. to listen attentively; by implication, to heed or conform to a command or authority\nKJV: —hearken, be obedient to, obey.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G191 \n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: ὑπακούω \nHebrew Equivalents: H238 אָזַן, H3985 מָאֵן, H5401 נָשַׁק, H6030 עָנָה, H7181 קָשַׁב, H7760 שׂוּם, H8003 שָׁלֵם, H8085 שָׁמַע",
    5220: "ὕπανδρος (hýpandros | hoop'-an-dros)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G435\nStrong's: in subjection under a man, i.e. a married woman\nKJV: —which hath an husband.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G435 \n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: ὕπανδρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8478 תַּחַת",
    5221: "ὑπαντάω (hypantáō | hoop-an-tah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and a derivative of G473\nStrong's: to go opposite (meet) under (quietly), i.e. to encounter, fall in with\nKJV: —(go to) meet.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G473 \n\nCognate Group: G5222 (meeting), G2658 (attain), G5221 (meet)\nVariants: ὑπαντάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7136 קָרָה",
    5222: "ὑπάντησις (hypántēsis | hoop-an'-tay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5221\nStrong's: an encounter or concurrence (with G1519 for infinitive, in order to fall in with)\nKJV: —meeting.\n\nSee: G5221 \nSee: G1519 \n\nCognate Group: G5222 (meeting), G2658 (attain), G5221 (meet)\nVariants: ὑπάντησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H6440 פָּנִים, H7121 קָרָא",
    5223: "ὕπαρξις (hýparxis | hoop'-arx-is)\n\nDerivation: from G5225\nStrong's: existency or proprietorship, i.e. (concretely) property, wealth\nKJV: —goods, substance.\n\nSee: G5225 \n\nCognate Group: G5222 (meeting), G2658 (attain), G5221 (meet)\nVariants: ὕπαρξις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1952 הֹון, H3426 יֵשׁ, H4735 מִקְנֶה, H7399 רְכוּשׁ",
    5224: "ὑπάρχοντα (hypárchonta | hoop-ar'-khon-tah)\n\nDerivation: neuter plural of present participle active of G5225 as noun\nStrong's: things extant or in hand, i.e. property or possessions\nKJV: —goods, that which one has, things which (one) possesseth, substance, that hast.\n\nSee: G5225 \n\nCognate Group: G5222 (meeting), G2658 (attain), G5221 (meet)\nVariants: ὑπάρχοντα, ὑπάρχω",
    5225: "ὑπάρχω (hypárchō | hoop-ar'-kho)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G756\nStrong's: to begin under (quietly), i.e. come into existence (be present or at hand); expletively, to exist (as copula or subordinate to an adjective, participle, adverb or preposition, or as an auxiliary to a principal (verb)\nKJV: —after, behave, live.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G756 \n\nCognate Group: G5222 (meeting), G2658 (attain), G5221 (meet)\nVariants: ὑπάρχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H202 אֹון, H205 אָוֶן, H1961 הָיָה, H3426 יֵשׁ, H3885 לוּן, H4735 מִקְנֶה, H7399 רְכוּשׁ",
    5226: "ὑπείκω (hypeíkō | hoop-i'-ko)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and εἴκω (to yield, be \"weak\")\nStrong's: to surrender\nKJV: —submit self.\n\nSee: G5259 \n\nCognate Group: G5222 (meeting), G2658 (attain), G5221 (meet)\nVariants: ὑπείκω",
    5227: "ὑπεναντίος (hypenantíos | hoop-en-an-tee'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G1727\nStrong's: under (covertly) contrary to, i.e. opposed or (as noun) an opponent\nKJV: —adversary, against.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G1727 \n\nCognate Group: G5222 (meeting), G2658 (attain), G5221 (meet)\nVariants: ὑπεναντίος \nHebrew Equivalents: H340 אָיַב, H6862 צַר, H6965 קוּם, H8130 שָׂנֵא",
    5228: "ὑπέρ (hypér | hoop-er')\n\nDerivation: a primary preposition\nStrong's: \"over\", i.e. (with the genitive case) of place, above, beyond, across, or causal, for the sake of, instead, regarding; with the accusative case superior to, more than\nKJV: —(+ exceeding, abundantly) above, in (on) behalf of, beyond, by, + very chiefest, concerning, exceeding (above, -ly), for, + very highly, more (than), of, over, on the part of, for sake of, in stead, than, to(-ward), very. In the comparative, it retains many of the above applications.\n\nCognate Group: G5228 (above)\nVariants: ὑπέρ, ὑπερεγώ",
    5229: "ὑπεραίρομαι (hyperaíromai | hoop-er-ah'-ee-rom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G5228 and G142\nStrong's: to raise oneself over, i.e. (figuratively) to become haughty\nKJV: —exalt self, be exalted above measure.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G142 \n\nCognate Group: G5228 (above)\nVariants: ὑπεραίρομαι, ὑπεραίρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5375 נָשָׂא, H5674 עָבַר, H5927 עָלָה",
    5230: "ὑπέρακμος (hypérakmos | hoop-er'-ak-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and the base of G188\nStrong's: beyond the \"acme\", i.e. figuratively (of a daughter) past the bloom (prime) of youth\nKJV: —+ pass the flower of (her) age.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G188 \n\nCognate Group: G5228 (above)\nVariants: ὑπέρακμος",
    5231: "ὑπεράνω (hyperánō | hoop-er-an'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G507\nStrong's: above upward, i.e. greatly higher (in place or rank)\nKJV: —far above, over.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G507 \n\nCognate Group: G5228 (above)\nVariants: ὑπεράνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4606 מֵעָל, H5921 עַל, H5945 עֶלְיֹון",
    5232: "ὑπεραυξάνω (hyperauxánō | hoop-er-owx-an'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G837\nStrong's: to increase above ordinary degree\nKJV: —grow exceedingly.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G837 \n\nCognate Group: G837 (grow), G5232 (grow exceedingly)\nVariants: ὑπεραυξάνω",
    5233: "ὑπερβαίνω (hyperbaínō | hoop-er-bah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and the base of G939\nStrong's: to transcend, i.e. (figuratively) to overreach\nKJV: —go beyond.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G939 \n\nCognate Group: G3845 (transgress), G5233 (go beyond), G531 (unchangeable), G3847 (breaking), G3848 (breaker)\nVariants: ὑπερβαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1801 דָּלַג, H3254 יָסַף, H5472 סוּג, H5674 עָבַר",
    5234: "ὑπερβαλλόντως (hyperballóntōs | hoop-er-bal-lon'-toce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from present participle active of G5235\nStrong's: excessively\nKJV: —beyond measure.\n\nSee: G5235 \n\nCognate Group: G5234 (beyond measure), G5236 (abundance), G5235 (exceeding)\nVariants: ὑπερβαλλόντως",
    5235: "ὑπερβάλλω (hyperbállō | hoop-er-bal'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G906\nStrong's: to throw beyod the usual mark, i.e. (figuratively) to surpass (only active participle supereminent)\nKJV: —exceeding, excel, pass.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G5234 (beyond measure), G5236 (abundance), G5235 (exceeding)\nVariants: ὑπερβάλλω",
    5236: "ὑπερβολή (hyperbolḗ | hoop-er-bol-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5235\nStrong's: a throwing beyond others, i.e. (figuratively) supereminence; adverbially (with G1519 or G2596) pre- eminently\nKJV: —abundance, (far more) exceeding, excellency, more excellent, beyond (out of) measure.\n\nSee: G5235 \nSee: G1519 \nSee: G2596 \n\nCognate Group: G5234 (beyond measure), G5236 (abundance), G5235 (exceeding)\nVariants: ὑπερβολή",
    5237: "ὑπερείδω (hypereídō | hoop-er-i'-do)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G1492\nStrong's: to overlook, i.e. not punish\nKJV: —wink at.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G1492 \n\nCognate Group: G5234 (beyond measure), G5236 (abundance), G5235 (exceeding)\nVariants: ὑπερείδω, παροράω, ὑπεροράω, ὑπερεῖδον \nHebrew Equivalents: H4604 מַעַל, H5956 עָלַם",
    5238: "ὑπερέκεινα (hyperékeina | hoop-er-ek'-i-nah)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and the neuter plural of G1565\nStrong's: above those parts, i.e. still farther\nKJV: —beyond.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G1565 \n\nCognate Group: G5234 (beyond measure), G5236 (abundance), G5235 (exceeding)\nVariants: ὑπερέκεινα",
    5239: "ὑπερεκτείνω (hyperekteínō | hoop-er-ek-ti'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G1614\nStrong's: to extend inordinately\nKJV: —stretch beyond.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G1614 \n\nCognate Group: G1614 (cast), G5239 (stretch beyond), G1616 (X instantly), G1618 (without ceasing)\nVariants: ὑπερεκτείνω",
    5240: "ὑπερεκχύνω (hyperekchýnō | hoop-er-ek-khoo'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and the alternate form of G1632\nStrong's: to pour out over, i.e. (passively) to overflow\nKJV: —run over.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G1632 \n\nCognate Group: G1614 (cast), G5239 (stretch beyond), G1616 (X instantly), G1618 (without ceasing)\nVariants: ὑπερεκχύνω, ὑπερεκπερισσοῦ, ὑπερεκπερισσῶς, ὑπερεκχύννω, ὑπερεκχύννομαι, ὑπερεκχύν(ν)ω",
    5241: "ὑπερεντυγχάνω (hyperentynchánō | hoop-er-en-toong-khan'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G1793\nStrong's: to intercede in behalf of\nKJV: —make intercession for.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G1793 \n\nCognate Group: G1783 (intercession), G5177 (be), G5241 (make intercession for), G1793 (deal with)\nVariants: ὑπερεντυγχάνω",
    5242: "ὑπερέχω (hyperéchō | hoop-er-ekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G2192\nStrong's: to hold oneself above, i.e. (figuratively) to excel; participle (as adjective, or neuter as noun) superior, superiority\nKJV: —better, excellency, higher, pass, supreme.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G5247 (authority), G5242 (better)\nVariants: ὑπερέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H117 אַדִּיר, H553 אָמַץ, H5736 עֲדַף, H6186 עָרַךְ, H8133 שְׁנָא",
    5243: "ὑπερηφανία (hyperēphanía | hoop-er-ay-fan-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5244\nStrong's: haughtiness\nKJV: —pride.\n\nSee: G5244 \n\nCognate Group: G5247 (authority), G5242 (better)\nVariants: ὑπερηφανία \nHebrew Equivalents: H1215 בֶּצַע, H1347 גָּאֹון, H2087 זָדֹון, H7312 רוּם, H7588 שָׁאֹון",
    5244: "ὑπερήφανος (hyperḗphanos | hoop-er-ay'-fan-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G5316\nStrong's: appearing above others (conspicuous), i.e. (figuratively) haughty\nKJV: —proud.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G5247 (authority), G5242 (better)\nVariants: ὑπερήφανος, ὑπερλίαν \nHebrew Equivalents: H1343 גֵּאֶה, H1364 גָּבֹהַּ, H2086 זֵד, H3887 לוּץ, H6184 עָרִיץ, H7293 רַהַב, H7312 רוּם",
    5245: "ὑπερνικάω (hypernikáō | hoop-er-nik-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G3528\nStrong's: to vanquish beyond, i.e. gain a decisive victory\nKJV: —more than conquer.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G3528 \n\nCognate Group: G3527 (Nicanor), G3534 (victory), G3528 (conquer), G3529 (victory), G5245 (more than conquer)\nVariants: ὑπερνικάω",
    5246: "ὑπέρογκος (hypéronkos | hoop-er'-ong-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G3591\nStrong's: bulging over, i.e. (figuratively) insolent\nKJV: —great swelling.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G3591 \n\nCognate Group: G3527 (Nicanor), G3534 (victory), G3528 (conquer), G3529 (victory), G5245 (more than conquer)\nVariants: ὑπέρογκος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1419 גָּדֹול, H6382 פֶּלֶא, H7185 קָשָׁה",
    5247: "ὑπεροχή (hyperochḗ | hoop-er-okh-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5242\nStrong's: prominence, i.e. (figuratively) superiority (in rank or character)\nKJV: —authority, excellency.\n\nSee: G5242 \n\nCognate Group: G5247 (authority), G5242 (better)\nVariants: ὑπεροχή \nHebrew Equivalents: H1364 גָּבֹהַּ, H6967 קֹומָה",
    5248: "ὑπερπερισσεύω (hyperperisseúō | hoop-er-per-is-syoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G4052\nStrong's: to super-abound\nKJV: —abound much more, exceeding.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G4052 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: ὑπερπερισσεύω",
    5249: "ὑπερπερισσῶς (hyperperissōs | hoop-er-per-is-soce')\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G4057\nStrong's: superabundantly, i.e. exceedingly\nKJV: —beyond measure.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G4057 \n\nCognate Group: G5248 (abound much more), G4050 (abundance), G4057 (exceedingly), G5249 (beyond measure), G4052 (abound), G4051 (abundance), G4053 (exceeding abundantly above)\nVariants: ὑπερπερισσῶς",
    5250: "ὑπερπλεονάζω (hyperpleonázō | hoop-er-pleh-on-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G4121\nStrong's: to superabound\nKJV: —be exceeding abundant.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G4121 \n\nCognate Group: G4121 (abound), G5250 (be exceeding abundant)\nVariants: ὑπερπλεονάζω",
    5251: "ὑπερυψόω (hyperypsóō | hoop-er-oop-so'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G5312\nStrong's: to elevate above others, i.e. raise to the highest position\nKJV: —highly exalt.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G5312 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὑπερυψόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5927 עָלָה, H6184 עָרִיץ, H7311 רוּם",
    5252: "ὑπερφρονέω (hyperphronéō | hoop-er-fron-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5228 and G5426\nStrong's: to esteem oneself overmuch, i.e. be vain or arrogant\nKJV: —think more highly.\n\nSee: G5228 \nSee: G5426 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὑπερφρονέω",
    5253: "ὑπερῷον (hyperōion | hoop-er-o'-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of G5228\nStrong's: a higher part of the house, i.e. apartment in the third story\nKJV: —upper chamber (room).\n\nSee: G5228 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὑπερῷον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5950 עֲלִילִיָּה",
    5254: "ὑπέχω (hypéchō | hoop-ekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G2192\nStrong's: to hold oneself under, i.e. endure with patience\nKJV: —suffer.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2192 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὑπέχω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5375 נָשָׂא, H5445 סָבַל",
    5255: "ὑπήκοος (hypḗkoos | hoop-ay'-ko-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5219\nStrong's: attentively listening, i.e. (by implication) submissive\nKJV: —obedient.\n\nSee: G5219 \n\nCognate Group: G5218 (obedience), G1522 (hear), G1873 (hear), G191 (give audience), G3878 (neglect to hear), G5219 (hearken), G5255 (obedient), G3876 (disobedience), G189 (audience)\nVariants: ὑπήκοος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4522 מַס, H5647 עָבַד, H8085 שָׁמַע",
    5256: "ὑπηρετέω (hypēretéō | hoop-ay-ret-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5257\nStrong's: to be a subordinate, i.e. (by implication) subserve\nKJV: —minister (unto), serve.\n\nSee: G5257 \n\nCognate Group: G5257 (minister), G5256 (minister)\nVariants: ὑπηρετέω",
    5257: "ὑπηρέτης (hypērétēs | hoop-ay-ret'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and a derivative of ἐρέσσω (to row)\nStrong's: an under-oarsman, i.e. (generally) subordinate (assistant, sexton, constable)\nKJV: —minister, officer, servant.\n\nSee: G5259 \n\nCognate Group: G5257 (minister), G5256 (minister)\nVariants: ὑπηρέτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H3596 כִּילַי, H5650 עֶבֶד",
    5258: "ὕπνος (hýpnos | hoop'-nos)\n\nDerivation: from an obsolete primary (perhaps akin to G5259 through the idea of subsilience)\nStrong's: sleep, i.e. (figuratively) spiritual torpor\nKJV: —sleep.\n\nSee: G5259 \n\nCognate Group: G879 (fall asleep), G1798 (dream), G1853 (X out of sleep), G5258 (sleep), G1797 (dream), G1852 (awake out of sleep)\nVariants: ὕπνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2377 חָזֹון, H2472 חֲלֹום, H3463 יָשֵׁן, H8142 שֵׁנָה, H8142 שֵׁנָה",
    5259: "ὑπό (hypó | hoop-o')\n\nDerivation: a primary preposition\nStrong's: under, i.e. (with the genitive case) of place (beneath), or with verbs (the agency or means, through); (with the accusative case) of place (whither (underneath) or where (below) or time (when (at))\nKJV: —among, by, from, in, of, under, with. In the comparative, it retains the same general applications, especially of inferior position or condition, and specially, covertly or moderately.\n\nCognate Group: G879 (fall asleep), G1798 (dream), G1853 (X out of sleep), G5258 (sleep), G1797 (dream), G1852 (awake out of sleep)\nVariants: ὑπό",
    5260: "ὑποβάλλω (hypobállō | hoop-ob-al'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G906\nStrong's: to throw in stealthily, i.e. introduce by collusion\nKJV: —suborn.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G906 \n\nCognate Group: G879 (fall asleep), G1798 (dream), G1853 (X out of sleep), G5258 (sleep), G1797 (dream), G1852 (awake out of sleep)\nVariants: ὑποβάλλω",
    5261: "ὑπογραμμός (hypogrammós | hoop-og-ram-mos')\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5259 and G1125\nStrong's: an underwriting, i.e. copy for imitation (figuratively)\nKJV: —example.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G1125 \n\nCognate Group: G4270 (before ordain), G1121 (bill), G1124 (scripture), G1123 (written), G5261 (example), G1125 (describe)\nVariants: ὑπογραμμός",
    5262: "ὑπόδειγμα (hypódeigma | hoop-od'-igue-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5263\nStrong's: an exhibit for imitation or warning (figuratively, specimen, adumbration)\nKJV: —en-(ex-)ample, pattern.\n\nSee: G5263 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: ὑπόδειγμα",
    5263: "ὑποδείκνυμι (hypodeíknymi | hoop-od-ike'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G1166\nStrong's: to exhibit under the eyes, i.e. (figuratively) to exemplify (instruct, admonish)\nKJV: —show, (fore-)warn.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G1166 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: ὑποδείκνυμι, ὑποδεικνύω, ὑποδείκνυμι/ὑποδεικνύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2324 חֲוָא, H3046 יְדַע, H3384 יָרָה, H5046 נָגַד, H5608 סָפַר, H7200 רָאָה, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    5264: "ὑποδέχομαι (hypodéchomai | hoop-od-ekh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G1209\nStrong's: to admit under one's roof, i.e. entertain hospitably\nKJV: —receive.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G1209 \n\nCognate Group: G5262 (en-ample), G323 (shewing), G1166 (shew), G3856 (make a public example), G322 (appoint), G1165 (make a shew)\nVariants: ὑποδέχομαι",
    5265: "ὑποδέω (hypodéō | hoop-od-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G1210\nStrong's: to bind under one's feet, i.e. put on shoes or sandals\nKJV: —bind on, (be) shod.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G1210 \n\nCognate Group: G5266 (shoe), G5265 (bind on)\nVariants: ὑποδέω, ὑποδέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5274 נָעַל",
    5266: "ὑπόδημα (hypódēma | hoop-od'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5265\nStrong's: something bound under the feet, i.e. a shoe or sandal\nKJV: —shoe.\n\nSee: G5265 \n\nCognate Group: G5266 (shoe), G5265 (bind on)\nVariants: ὑπόδημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H5274 נָעַל",
    5267: "ὑπόδικος (hypódikos | hoop-od'-ee-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G1349\nStrong's: under sentence, i.e. (by implication) condemned\nKJV: —guilty.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G1349 \n\nCognate Group: G5267 (guilty)\nVariants: ὑπόδικος",
    5268: "ὑποζύγιον (hypozýgion | hoop-od-zoog'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G5259 and G2218\nStrong's: an animal under the yoke (draught-beast), i.e. (specially), a donkey\nKJV: —ass.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2218 \n\nCognate Group: G5267 (guilty)\nVariants: ὑποζύγιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H860 אָתֹון, H2543 חֲמֹור",
    5269: "ὑποζώννυμι (hypozṓnnymi | hoop-od-zone'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G2224\nStrong's: to gird under, i.e. frap (a vessel with cables across the keel, sides and deck)\nKJV: —undergirt.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2224 \n\nCognate Group: G5267 (guilty)\nVariants: ὑποζώννυμι",
    5270: "ὑποκάτω (hypokátō | hoop-ok-at'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G2736\nStrong's: down under, i.e. beneath\nKJV: —under.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2736 \n\nCognate Group: G5267 (guilty)\nVariants: ὑποκάτω \nHebrew Equivalents: H4295 מַטָּה, H8478 תַּחַת",
    5271: "ὑποκρίνομαι (hypokrínomai | hoop-ok-rin'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G5259 and G2919\nStrong's: to decide (speak or act) under a false part, i.e. (figuratively) dissemble (pretend)\nKJV: —feign.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2919 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: ὑποκρίνομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H6030 עָנָה",
    5272: "ὑπόκρισις (hypókrisis | hoop-ok'-ree-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5271\nStrong's: acting under a feigned part, i.e. (figuratively) deceit (\"hypocrisy\")\nKJV: —condemnation, dissimulation, hypocrisy.\n\nSee: G5271 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: ὑπόκρισις",
    5273: "ὑποκριτής (hypokritḗs | hoop-ok-ree-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G5271\nStrong's: an actor under an assumed character (stage-player), i.e. (figuratively) a dissembler (\"hypocrite\"\nKJV: —hypocrite.\n\nSee: G5271 \n\nCognate Group: G5273 (hypocrite), G505 (without dissimulation), G5272 (condemnation), G4942 (dissemble with), G5271 (feign)\nVariants: ὑποκριτής \nHebrew Equivalents: H2611 חָנֵף",
    5274: "ὑπολαμβάνω (hypolambánō | hoop-ol-am-ban'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G2983\nStrong's: to take from below, i.e. carry upward; figuratively, to take up, i.e. continue a discourse or topic; mentally, to assume (presume)\nKJV: —answer, receive, suppose.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2983 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: ὑπολαμβάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H270 אָחַז, H935 בֹּוא, H1802 דָּלָה, H1819 דָּמָה, H2803 חָשַׁב, H3498 יָתַר, H6030 עָנָה, H7475 רַעְיֹון",
    5275: "ὑπολείπω (hypoleípō | hoop-ol-i'-po)\n\nDerivation: from G5295 and G3007\nStrong's: to leave under (behind), i.e. (passively) to remain (survive)\nKJV: —be left.\n\nSee: G5295 \nSee: G3007 \n\nCognate Group: G353 (receive up), G4301 (come aforehand), G2638 (apprehend), G4356 (receiving), G3880 (receive), G1949 (catch), G354 (taking up), G5274 (answer), G4355 (receive), G423 (blameless), G2983 (accept), G3335 (eat), G3336 (taking)\nVariants: ὑπολείπω \nHebrew Equivalents: H680 אָצַל, H1633 גָּרַם, H3322 יָצַג, H3498 יָתַר, H5736 עֲדַף, H5800 עָזַב, H5975 עָמַד, H7604 שָׁאַר, H7662 שְׁבַק, H7760 שׂוּם",
    5276: "ὑπολήνιον (hypolḗnion | hoop-ol-ay'-nee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed compound of G5259 and G3025\nStrong's: vessel or receptacle under the press, i.e. lower wine-vat\nKJV: —winefat.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G3025 \n\nCognate Group: G5276 (winefat), G3025 (winepress)\nVariants: ὑπολήνιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3342 יֶקֶב",
    5277: "ὑπολιμπάνω (hypolimpánō | hoop-ol-im-pan'-o)\n\nDerivation: a prolonged form for G5275\nStrong's: to leave behind, i.e. bequeath\nKJV: —leave.\n\nSee: G5275 \n\nCognate Group: G5276 (winefat), G3025 (winepress)\nVariants: ὑπολιμπάνω, ἀπολιμπάνω",
    5278: "ὑπομένω (hypoménō | hoop-om-en'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G3306\nStrong's: to stay under (behind), i.e. remain; figuratively, to undergo, i.e. bear (trials), have fortitude, persevere\nKJV: —abide, endure, (take) patient(-ly), suffer, tarry behind.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G3306 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: ὑπομένω, ἀπομένω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2342 חוּל, H2442 חָכָה, H2934 טָמַן, H3176 יָחַל, H3427 יָשַׁב, H3557 כּוּל, H4102 מָהַהּ, H6960 קָוָה, H6965 קוּם",
    5279: "ὑπομιμνήσκω (hypomimnḗskō | hoop-om-im-nace'-ko)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G3403\nStrong's: to remind quietly, i.e. suggest to the (middle voice, one's own) memory\nKJV: —put in mind, remember, bring to (put in) remembrance.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G3403 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: ὑπομιμνήσκω, ὑπομιμνῄσκω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2142 זָכַר",
    5280: "ὑπόμνησις (hypómnēsis | hoop-om'-nay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5279\nStrong's: a reminding or (reflexively) recollection\nKJV: —remembrance.\n\nSee: G5279 \n\nCognate Group: G364 (remembrance), G5280 (remembrance)\nVariants: ὑπόμνησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H8416 תְּהִלָּה",
    5281: "ὑπομονή (hypomonḗ | hoop-om-on-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5278\nStrong's: cheerful (or hopeful) endurance, constancy\nKJV: —enduring, patience, patient continuance (waiting).\n\nSee: G5278 \n\nCognate Group: G5278 (abide), G3887 (abide), G5281 (enduring), G1696 (continue), G4037 (wait for), G3306 (abide), G3438 (abode)\nVariants: ὑπομονή \nHebrew Equivalents: H4723 מִקְוֶה",
    5282: "ὑπονοέω (hyponoéō | hoop-on-o-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G3539\nStrong's: to think under (privately), i.e. to surmise or conjecture\nKJV: —think, suppose, deem.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G3539 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: ὑπονοέω",
    5283: "ὑπόνοια (hypónoia | hoop-on'-oy-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5282\nStrong's: suspicion\nKJV: —surmising.\n\nSee: G5282 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: ὑπόνοια \nHebrew Equivalents: H7475 רַעְיֹון",
    5284: "ὑποπλέω (hypopléō | hoop-op-leh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G4126\nStrong's: to sail under the lee of\nKJV: —sail under.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G4126 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: ὑποπλέω",
    5285: "ὑποπνέω (hypopnéō | hoop-op-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G4154\nStrong's: to breathe gently, i.e. breeze\nKJV: —blow softly.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G4154 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: ὑποπνέω",
    5286: "ὑποπόδιον (hypopódion | hoop-op-od'-ee-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G5259 and G4228\nStrong's: something under the feet, i.e. a foot-rest (figuratively)\nKJV: —footstool.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G4228 \n\nCognate Group: G3559 (admonition), G5282 (think), G2657 (behold), G3539 (consider), G1270 (thought), G2133 (benevolence), G1271 (imagination), G3540 (device), G4307 (providence), G279 (impenitent), G4306 (provide), G1425 (hard to be understood), G2132 (agree), G453 (fool), G5283 (surmising), G3563 (mind), G3340 (repent), G1771 (intent), G454 (folly)\nVariants: ὑποπόδιον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1916 הֲדֹם",
    5287: "ὑπόστασις (hypóstasis | hoop-os'-tas-is)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5259 and G2476\nStrong's: a setting under (support), i.e. (figuratively) concretely, essence, or abstractly, assurance (objectively or subjectively)\nKJV: —confidence, confident, person, substance.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G2476 \n\nCognate Group: G5287 (confidence)\nVariants: ὑπόστασις \nHebrew Equivalents: H2465 חֶלֶד, H3351 יְקוּם, H3666 כִּנְעָה, H4241 מִחְיָה, H4613 מׇעֳמָד, H4673 מַצָּב, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H5324 נָצַב, H5475 סֹוד, H7551 רָקַם, H8431 תֹּוחֶלֶת, H8499 תְּכוּנָה, H8615 תִּקְוָה",
    5288: "ὑποστέλλω (hypostéllō | hoop-os-tel'-lo)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G4724\nStrong's: to withhold under (out of sight), i.e. (reflexively) to cower or shrink, (figuratively) to conceal (reserve)\nKJV: —draw (keep) back, shun, withdraw.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G4724 \n\nCognate Group: G5288 (draw back), G1291 (charge), G4958 (short), G2689 (apparel), G2687 (appease), G1992 (\"epistle), G1989 (write), G5289 (draw back), G1293 (difference), G4724 (avoid)\nVariants: ὑποστέλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1481 גּוּר, H3607 כָּלָא, H6075 עָפַל, H6440 פָּנִים",
    5289: "ὑποστολή (hypostolḗ | hoop-os-tol-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5288\nStrong's: shrinkage (timidity), i.e. (by implication) apostasy\nKJV: —draw back.\n\nSee: G5288 \n\nCognate Group: G5288 (draw back), G1291 (charge), G4958 (short), G2689 (apparel), G2687 (appease), G1992 (\"epistle), G1989 (write), G5289 (draw back), G1293 (difference), G4724 (avoid)\nVariants: ὑποστολή",
    5290: "ὑποστρέφω (hypostréphō | hoop-os-tref'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G4762\nStrong's: to turn under (behind), i.e. to return (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —come again, return (again, back again), turn back (again).\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G4762 \n\nCognate Group: G5288 (draw back), G1291 (charge), G4958 (short), G2689 (apparel), G2687 (appease), G1992 (\"epistle), G1989 (write), G5289 (draw back), G1293 (difference), G4724 (avoid)\nVariants: ὑποστρέφω \nHebrew Equivalents: H235 אָזַל, H1765 דָּחַף, H6437 פָּנָה, H7725 שׁוּב",
    5291: "ὑποστρώννυμι (hypostrṓnnymi | hoop-os-trone'-noo-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G4766\nStrong's: to strew underneath (the feet as a carpet)\nKJV: —spread.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G4766 \n\nCognate Group: G5288 (draw back), G1291 (charge), G4958 (short), G2689 (apparel), G2687 (appease), G1992 (\"epistle), G1989 (write), G5289 (draw back), G1293 (difference), G4724 (avoid)\nVariants: ὑποστρώννυμι, ὑποστρωννύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3318 יָצָא, H6428 פָּלַשׁ",
    5292: "ὑποταγή (hypotagḗ | hoop-ot-ag-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5293\nStrong's: subordination\nKJV: —subjection.\n\nSee: G5293 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ὑποταγή",
    5293: "ὑποτάσσω (hypotássō | hoop-ot-as'-so)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G5021\nStrong's: to subordinate; reflexively, to obey\nKJV: —be under obedience (obedient), put under, subdue unto, (be, make) subject (to, unto), be (put) in subjection (to, under), submit self unto.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G5021 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ὑποτάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1696 דָבַר, H1747 דּוּמִיָּה, H1826 דָּמַם, H3533 כָּבַשׁ, H4911 מָשַׁל, H5414 נָתַן, H6399 פְּלַח, H7286 רָדַד, H7760 שׂוּם, H7896 שִׁית",
    5294: "ὑποτίθημι (hypotíthēmi | hoop-ot-ith'-ay-mee)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G5087\nStrong's: to place underneath, i.e. (figuratively) to hazard, (reflexively) to suggest\nKJV: —lay down, put in remembrance.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G5087 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ὑποτίθημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5186 נָטָה, H5414 נָתַן, H6293 פָּגַע, H7760 שׂוּם",
    5295: "ὑποτρέχω (hypotréchō | hoop-ot-rekh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G5143 (including its alternate)\nStrong's: to run under, i.e. (specially), to sail past\nKJV: —run under.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G5143 \n\nCognate Group: G1296 (instrumentality), G657 (bid farewell), G5021 (addict), G813 (unruly), G4367 (bid), G5292 (subjection), G1297 (commandment), G2004 (charge), G5293 (be under obedience), G506 (disobedient), G814 (disorderly), G1299 (appoint), G5001 (order), G812 (behave self disorderly), G392 (set in order)\nVariants: ὑποτρέχω",
    5296: "ὑποτύπωσις (hypotýpōsis | hoop-ot-oop'-o-sis)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5259 and a derivative of G5179\nStrong's: typification under (after), i.e. (concretely) a sketch (figuratively) for imitation\nKJV: —form, pattern.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G5179 \n\nCognate Group: G499 (figure), G5296 (form), G5179 (en-ample)\nVariants: ὑποτύπωσις",
    5297: "ὑποφέρω (hypophérō | hoop-of-er'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G5342\nStrong's: to bear from underneath, i.e. (figuratively) to undergo hardship\nKJV: —bear, endure.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G499 (figure), G5296 (form), G5179 (en-ample)\nVariants: ὑποφέρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3201 יָכֹל, H3557 כּוּל, H3559 כּוּן, H4672 מָצָא, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6901 קָבַל",
    5298: "ὑποχωρέω (hypochōréō | hoop-okh-o-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5259 and G5562\nStrong's: to vacate down, i.e. retire quietly\nKJV: —go aside, withdraw self.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G5562 \n\nCognate Group: G499 (figure), G5296 (form), G5179 (en-ample)\nVariants: ὑποχωρέω",
    5299: "ὑπωπιάζω (hypōpiázō | hoop-o-pee-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5259 and a derivative of G3700\nStrong's: to hit under the eye (buffet or disable an antagonist as a pugilist), i.e. (figuratively) to tease or annoy (into compliance), subdue (one's passions)\nKJV: —keep under, weary.\n\nSee: G5259 \nSee: G3700 \n\nCognate Group: G5299 (keep under)\nVariants: ὑπωπιάζω, ὑποπιάζω",
    5300: "ὗς (hŷs | hoos)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a hog (\"swine\")\nKJV: —sow.\n\nCognate Group: G5299 (keep under)\nVariants: ὗς \nHebrew Equivalents: H2386 חֲזִיר",
    5301: "ὕσσωπος (hýssōpos | hoos'-so-pos)\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (H231)\nStrong's: \"hyssop\"\nKJV: —hyssop.\nSee: H231\n\nCognate Group: G5299 (keep under)\nVariants: ὕσσωπος \nHebrew Equivalents: H231 אֵזֹוב",
    5302: "ὑστερέω (hysteréō | hoos-ter-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5306\nStrong's: to be later, i.e. (by implication) to be inferior; generally, to fall short (be deficient)\nKJV: —come behind (short), be destitute, fail, lack, suffer need, (be in) want, be the worse.\n\nSee: G5306 \n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὑστερέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1639 גָּרַע, H2308 חָדַל, H2637 חָסֵר, H4102 מָהַהּ, H4513 מָנַע",
    5303: "ὑστέρημα (hystérēma | hoos-ter'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5302\nStrong's: a deficit; specially, poverty\nKJV: —that which is behind, (that which was) lack(-ing), penury, want.\n\nSee: G5302 \n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὑστέρημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2642 חֶסְרֹון, H2818 חֲשַׁח, H4270 מַחְסֹור",
    5304: "ὑστέρησις (hystérēsis | hoos-ter'-ay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5302\nStrong's: a falling short, i.e. (specially), penury\nKJV: —want.\n\nSee: G5302 \n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὑστέρησις",
    5305: "ὕστερον (hýsteron | hoos'-ter-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of G5306 as adverb\nStrong's: more lately, i.e. eventually\nKJV: —afterward, (at the) last (of all).\n\nSee: G5306 \n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὕστερον, ὕστερος \nHebrew Equivalents: H314 אַחֲרֹון",
    5306: "ὕστερος (hýsteros | hoos'-ter-os)\n\nDerivation: comparative from G5259 (in the sense of behind)\nStrong's: later\nKJV: —latter.\n\nSee: G5259 \n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὕστερος \nHebrew Equivalents: H314 אַחֲרֹון, H707 אָרַג, H3610 כִּלְאַיִם, H5927 עָלָה",
    5307: "ὑφαντός (hyphantós | hoo-fan-tos')\n\nDerivation: from ὑφαίνω to weave\nStrong's: woven, i.e. (perhaps) knitted\nKJV: —woven.\n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὑφαντός \nHebrew Equivalents: H707 אָרַג, H707 אָרַג, H2803 חָשַׁב, H3610 כִּלְאַיִם, H5927 עָלָה, H7551 רָקַם",
    5308: "ὑψηλός (hypsēlós | hoop-say-los')\n\nDerivation: from G5311\nStrong's: lofty (in place or character)\nKJV: —high(-er, -ly) (esteemed).\n\nSee: G5311 \n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὑψηλός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1116 בָּמָה, H1343 גֵּאֶה, H1361 גָּבַהּ, H1364 גָּבֹהַּ, H1433 גֹּדֶל, H2388 חָזַק, H4060 מִדָּה, H4175 מֹורֶה, H5186 נָטָה, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5797 עֹז, H5945 עֶלְיֹון, H7311 רוּם",
    5309: "ὑψηλοφρονέω (hypsēlophronéō | hoop-say-lo-fron-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5308 and G5424\nStrong's: to be lofty in mind, i.e. arrogant\nKJV: —be highminded.\n\nSee: G5308 \nSee: G5424 \n\nCognate Group: G5304 (want), G5305 (afterward), G5303 (that which is behind), G5306 (latter), G650 (defraud), G5302 (come behind)\nVariants: ὑψηλοφρονέω",
    5310: "ὕψιστος (hýpsistos | hoop'-sis-tos)\n\nDerivation: superlative from the base of G5311\nStrong's: highest, i.e. (masculine singular) the Supreme (God), or (neuter plural) the heavens\nKJV: —most high, highest.\n\nSee: G5311 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὕψιστος \nHebrew Equivalents: H4791 מָרֹום, H5945 עֶלְיֹון",
    5311: "ὕψος (hýpsos | hoop'-sos)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5228\nStrong's: elevation, i.e. (abstractly) altitude, (specially), the sky, or (figuratively) dignity\nKJV: —be exalted, height, (on) high.\n\nSee: G5228 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὕψος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1116 בָּמָה, H1347 גָּאֹון, H1363 גֹּבַהּ, H1433 גֹּדֶל, H4605 מַעַל, H4791 מָרֹום, H6967 קֹומָה, H8443 תֹּועָפָה",
    5312: "ὑψόω (hypsóō | hoop-so'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5311\nStrong's: to elevate (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —exalt, lift up.\n\nSee: G5311 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὑψόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1347 גָּאֹון, H1361 גָּבַהּ, H1396 גָּבַר, H1431 גָּדַל, H4754 מָרָא, H5186 נָטָה, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5937 עָלַז, H6186 עָרַךְ, H6286 פָּאַר, H6509 פָּרָה, H7235 רָבָה, H7311 רוּם, H7311 רוּם, H7442 רָנַן, H7679 שָׂגָא, H7682 שָׂגַב",
    5313: "ὕψωμα (hýpsōma | hoop'-so-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5312\nStrong's: an elevated place or thing, i.e. (abstractly) altitude, or (by implication) a barrier (figuratively)\nKJV: — height, high thing.\n\nSee: G5312 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: ὕψωμα",
    5314: "φάγος (phágos | fag'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5315\nStrong's: a glutton\nKJV: —gluttonous.\n\nSee: G5315 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: φάγος",
    5315: "φάγω (phágō | fag'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (used as an alternate of G2068 in certain tenses)\nStrong's: to eat (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —eat, meat.\n\nSee: G2068 \n\nCognate Group: G5312 (exalt), G5310 (most high), G5251 (highly exalt), G5311 (be exalted), G5313 (height)\nVariants: φάγω, ἐσθίω",
    5316: "φαίνω (phaínō | fah'-ee-no)\n\nDerivation: prolongation for the base of G5457\nStrong's: to lighten (shine), i.e. show (transitive or intransitive, literal or figurative)\nKJV: — appear, seem, be seen, shine, X think.\n\nSee: G5457 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φαίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H215 אֹור, H974 בָּחַן, H1540 גָּלָה, H1961 הָיָה, H2095 זְהַר, H2224 זָרַח, H2734 חָרָה, H2734 חָרָה, H2734 חָרָה, H5308 נְפַל, H5869 עַיִן, H7136 קָרָה, H7200 רָאָה",
    5317: "Φάλεκ (Phálek | fal'-ek)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6389)\nStrong's: Phalek (i.e. Peleg), a patriarch\nKJV: —Phalec.\nSee: H6389\n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: Φάλεκ \nHebrew Equivalents: H6389 פֶּלֶג",
    5318: "φανερός (phanerós | fan-er-os')\n\nDerivation: from G5316\nStrong's: shining, i.e. apparent (literally or figuratively); neuter (as adverb) publicly, externally\nKJV: —abroad, + appear, known, manifest, open (+ -ly), outward (+ -ly).\n\nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φανερός \nHebrew Equivalents: H974 בָּחַן, H1540 גָּלָה, H3045 יָדַע",
    5319: "φανερόω (phaneróō | fan-er-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5318\nStrong's: to render apparent (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —appear, manifestly declare, (make) manifest (forth), shew (self).\n\nSee: G5318 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φανερόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1540 גָּלָה",
    5320: "φανερῶς (phanerōs | fan-er-oce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5318\nStrong's: plainly, i.e. clearly or publicly\nKJV: —evidently, openly.\n\nSee: G5318 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φανερῶς",
    5321: "φανέρωσις (phanérōsis | fan-er'-o-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5319\nStrong's: exhibition, i.e. (figuratively) expression, (by extension) a bestowment\nKJV: —manifestation.\n\nSee: G5319 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φανέρωσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H224 אוּרִים",
    5322: "φανός (phanós | fan-os')\n\nDerivation: from G5316\nStrong's: a lightener, i.e. light; lantern\nKJV: —lantern.\n\nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φανός",
    5323: "Φανουήλ (Phanouḗl | fan-oo-ale')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6439)\nStrong's: Phanuel (i.e. Penuel), an Israelite\nKJV: —Phanuel.\nSee: H6439\n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: Φανουήλ \nHebrew Equivalents: H6439 פְּנוּאֵל",
    5324: "φαντάζω (phantázō | fan-tad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5316\nStrong's: to make apparent, i.e. (passively) to appear (neuter participle as noun, a spectacle)\nKJV: —sight.\n\nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φαντάζω, φαντάζομαι",
    5325: "φαντασία (phantasía | fan-tas-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5324\nStrong's: (properly abstract) a (vain) show (\"fantasy\")\nKJV: —pomp.\n\nSee: G5324 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φαντασία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2385 חֲזִיז",
    5326: "φάντασμα (phántasma | fan'-tas-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5324\nStrong's: (properly concrete) a (mere) show (\"phantasm\"), i.e. spectre\nKJV: —spirit.\n\nSee: G5324 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φάντασμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H2384 חִזָּיֹון",
    5327: "φάραγξ (pháranx | far'-anx)\n\nDerivation: properly, strengthened from the base of G4008 or rather of G4486\nStrong's: a gap or chasm, i.e. ravine (winter-torrent)\nKJV: —valley.\n\nSee: G4486 \nSee: G4008 \n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: φάραγξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H663 אֲפֵק, H1516 גַּיְא, H4095 מַדְרֵגָה, H4569 מַעֲבָר, H5158 נַחַל, H6010 עֵמֶק",
    5328: "Φαραώ (Pharaṓ | far-ah-o')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (H6547)\nStrong's: Pharao (i.e. Pharoh), an Egyptian king\nKJV: —Pharaoh.\nSee: H6547\n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: Φαραώ \nHebrew Equivalents: H6547 פַּרְעֹה",
    5329: "Φάρες (Pháres | far-es')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H6557)\nStrong's: Phares (i.e. Perets), an Israelite\nKJV: —Phares.\nSee: H6557\n\nCognate Group: G5319 (appear), G5325 (pomp), G5320 (evidently), G5322 (lantern), G2015 (appearing), G5321 (manifestation), G5326 (spirit), G5316 (appear), G2014 (appear), G1718 (appear), G5324 (sight), G5318 (abroad), G2016 (notable)\nVariants: Φάρες, Φαρές \nHebrew Equivalents: H6538 פֶּרֶס, H6557 פֶּרֶץ",
    5330: "Φαρισαῖος (Pharisaîos | far-is-ah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (compare H6567)\nStrong's: a separatist, i.e. exclusively religious; a Pharisean, i.e. Jewish sectary\nKJV: —Pharisee.\nSee: H6567\n\nCognate Group: G5330 (Pharisee)\nVariants: Φαρισαῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6567 פָּרָשׁ",
    5331: "φαρμακεία (pharmakeía | far-mak-i'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5332\nStrong's: medication (\"pharmacy\"), i.e. (by extension) magic (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —sorcery, witchcraft.\n\nSee: G5332 \n\nCognate Group: G5330 (Pharisee)\nVariants: φαρμακεία, φάρμακον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3785 כֶּשֶׁף",
    5332: "φαρμακεύς (pharmakeús | far-mak-yoos')\n\nDerivation: from φάρμακον (a drug, i.e. spell-giving potion)\nStrong's: a druggist (\"pharmacist\") or poisoner, i.e. (by extension) a magician\nKJV: —sorcerer.\n\nCognate Group: G5330 (Pharisee)\nVariants: φαρμακεύς G5759, G5760 \nHebrew Equivalents: H3785 כֶּשֶׁף",
    5333: "φάρμακος (phármakos | far-mak-os')\n\nDerivation: the same as 5332\nKJV: —sorcerer.\n\nCognate Group: G5330 (Pharisee)\nVariants: φάρμακος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2269 חֲבַר, H2748 חַרְטֹם, H3784 כָּשַׁף",
    5334: "φάσις (phásis | fas'-is)\n\nDerivation: from G5346 (not the same as \"phase\", which is from G5316)\nStrong's: a saying, i.e. report\nKJV: —tidings.\n\nSee: G5346 \nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G5330 (Pharisee)\nVariants: φάσις",
    5335: "φάσκω (pháskō | fas'-ko)\n\nDerivation: prolongation from the same as G5346\nStrong's: to assert\nKJV: —affirm, profess, say.\n\nSee: G5346 \n\nCognate Group: G5330 (Pharisee)\nVariants: φάσκω \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר",
    5336: "φάτνη (phátnē | fat'-nay)\n\nDerivation: from πατέομαι (to eat)\nStrong's: a crib (for fodder)\nKJV: —manager, stall.\n\nCognate Group: G5336 (manager)\nVariants: φάτνη \nHebrew Equivalents: H18 אֵבוּס, H723 אֻרְוָה, H1098 בְּלִיל, H7517 רֶפֶת",
    5337: "φαῦλος (phaûlos | fow'-los)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: \"foul\" or \"flawy\", i.e. (figuratively) wicked\nKJV: —evil.\n\nCognate Group: G5336 (manager)\nVariants: φαῦλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H191 אֱוִיל, H3886 לוּעַ, H5557 סָלַף, H5766 עֶוֶל",
    5338: "φέγγος (phéngos | feng'-gos)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to the base of G5457 (compare G5350)\nStrong's: brilliancy\nKJV: —light.\n\nSee: G5457 \nSee: G5350 \n\nCognate Group: G5336 (manager)\nVariants: φέγγος \nHebrew Equivalents: H216 אֹור, H1242 בֹּקֶר, H3313 יָפַע, H3852 לֶהָבָה, H5051 נֹגַהּ, H5105 נְהָרָה",
    5339: "φείδομαι (pheídomai | fi'-dom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: to be chary of, i.e. (subjectively) to abstain or (objectively) to treat leniently\nKJV: —forbear, spare.\n\nCognate Group: G5336 (manager)\nVariants: φείδομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H2347 חוּס, H2550 חָמַל, H2550 חָמַל, H2820 חָשַׂךְ, H3938 לָעַט, H4263 מַחְמָל",
    5340: "φειδομένως (pheidoménōs | fi-dom-en'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from participle of G5339\nStrong's: abstemiously, i.e. stingily\nKJV: —sparingly.\n\nSee: G5339 \n\nCognate Group: G5336 (manager)\nVariants: φειδομένως",
    5341: "φελόνης (phelónēs | fel-on'-ace)\n\nDerivation: by transposition for a derivative probably of G5316 (as showing outside the other garments)\nStrong's: a mantle (surtout)\nKJV: — cloke.\n\nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G5336 (manager)\nVariants: φελόνης, φαιλόνης",
    5342: "φέρω (phérō | fer'-o)\n\n\nDerivation: a primary verb (for which other, and apparently not cognate ones are used in certain tenses only; namely, οἴω (oíō | oy'-o); and ἐνέγκω (enénkō | en-eng'-ko)\n\nStrong's: to \"bear\" or carry (in a very wide application, literally and figuratively, as follows)\nKJV: —be, bear, bring (forth), carry, come, + let her drive, be driven, endure, go on, lay, lead, move, reach, rushing, uphold.\n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φέρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H935 בֹּוא, H2734 חָרָה, H2986 יָבַל, H3051 יָהַב, H3287 יָעֵף, H3318 יָצָא, H3381 יָרַד, H3557 כּוּל, H3559 כּוּן, H3947 לָקַח, H5066 נָגַשׁ, H5068 נָדַב, H5086 נָדַף, H5130 נוּף, H5312 נְפַק, H5375 נָשָׂא, H5414 נָתַן, H5591 סַעַר, H5674 עָבַר, H5844 עָטָה, H5927 עָלָה, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6437 פָּנָה, H7725 שׁוּב, H7857 שָׁטַף, H7971 שָׁלַח",
    5343: "φεύγω (pheúgō | fyoo'-go)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to run away (literally or figuratively); by implication, to shun; by analogy, to vanish\nKJV: —escape, flee (away).\n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φεύγω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1272 בָּרַח, H5074 נָדַד, H5080 נָדַח, H5127 נוּס, H5264 נָסַס, H5307 נָפַל, H6207 עָרַק, H8300 שָׂרִיד",
    5344: "Φῆλιξ (Phēlix | fay'-lix)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: happy; Phelix (i.e. Felix), a Roman\nKJV: —Felix.\n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: Φῆλιξ",
    5345: "φήμη (phḗmē | fay'-may)\n\nDerivation: from G5346\nStrong's: a saying, i.e. rumor (\"fame\")\nKJV: —fame.\n\nSee: G5346 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φήμη \nHebrew Equivalents: H8052 שְׁמוּעָה",
    5346: "φημί (phēmí | fay-mee')\n\nDerivation: properly, the same as the base of G5457 and G5316\nStrong's: to show or make known one's thoughts, i.e. speak or say\nKJV: —affirm, say. Compare G3004 .\n\nSee: G5457 \nSee: G5316 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φημί \nHebrew Equivalents: H559 אָמַר, H5001 נָאַם",
    5347: "Φῆστος (Phēstos | face'-tos)\n\nDerivation: of Latin derivation\nStrong's: festal; Phestus (i.e. Festus), a Roman\nKJV: —Festus.\n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: Φῆστος",
    5348: "φθάνω (phthánō | fthan'-o)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to be beforehand, i.e. anticipate or precede; by extension, to have arrived at\nKJV: —(already) attain, come, prevent.\n\nCognate Group: G5348 (attain), G4399 (prevent)\nVariants: φθάνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H553 אָמַץ, H1692 דָּבַק, H3013 יָגָה, H4291 מְטָא, H5060 נָגַע",
    5349: "φθαρτός (phthartós | fthar-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G5351\nStrong's: decayed, i.e. (by implication) perishable\nKJV: —corruptible.\n\nSee: G5351 \n\nCognate Group: G5351 (corrupt), G1311 (corrupt), G862 (not corruptible), G5256 (minister), G5349 (corruptible), G90 (uncorruptness), G1312 (corruption), G861 (immortality), G2704 (corrupt)\nVariants: φθαρτός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4937 מִשְׁעֵן",
    5350: "φθέγγομαι (phthéngomai | ftheng'-gom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G5338 and thus to G5346\nStrong's: to utter a clear sound, i.e. (generally) to proclaim\nKJV: —speak.\n\nSee: G5346 \nSee: G5338 \n\nCognate Group: G5351 (corrupt), G1311 (corrupt), G862 (not corruptible), G5256 (minister), G5349 (corruptible), G90 (uncorruptness), G1312 (corruption), G861 (immortality), G2704 (corrupt)\nVariants: φθέγγομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1696 דָבַר, H5042 נָבַע, H6030 עָנָה, H7580 שָׁאַג, H7878 שִׂיחַ, H8608 תָּפַף",
    5351: "φθείρω (phtheírō | fthi'-ro)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from φθίω (to pine or waste)\nStrong's: properly, to shrivel or wither, i.e. to spoil (by any process) or (generally) to ruin (especially figuratively, by moral influences, to deprave)\nKJV: —corrupt (self), defile, destroy.\n\nCognate Group: G5351 (corrupt), G1311 (corrupt), G862 (not corruptible), G5256 (minister), G5349 (corruptible), G90 (uncorruptness), G1312 (corruption), G861 (immortality), G2704 (corrupt)\nVariants: φθείρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1238 בָּקַק, H2254 חָבַל, H5034 נָבֵל, H5127 נוּס, H7843 שָׁחַת",
    5352: "φθινοπωρινός (phthinopōrinós | fthin-op-o-ree-nos')\n\nDerivation: from derivative of φθίνω (to wane\nStrong's: akin to the base of G5351) and G3703 (meaning late autumn); autumnal (as stripped of leaves)\nKJV: —whose fruit withereth.\n\nSee: G5351 \nSee: G3703 \n\nCognate Group: G5351 (corrupt), G1311 (corrupt), G862 (not corruptible), G5256 (minister), G5349 (corruptible), G90 (uncorruptness), G1312 (corruption), G861 (immortality), G2704 (corrupt)\nVariants: φθινοπωρινός",
    5353: "φθόγγος (phthóngos | ftong'-gos)\n\nDerivation: from G5350\nStrong's: utterance, i.e. a musical note (vocal or instrumental)\nKJV: —sound.\n\nSee: G5350 \n\nCognate Group: G5351 (corrupt), G1311 (corrupt), G862 (not corruptible), G5256 (minister), G5349 (corruptible), G90 (uncorruptness), G1312 (corruption), G861 (immortality), G2704 (corrupt)\nVariants: φθόγγος \nHebrew Equivalents: H6957 קַו",
    5354: "φθονέω (phthonéō | fthon-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5355\nStrong's: to be jealous of\nKJV: —envy.\n\nSee: G5355 \n\nCognate Group: G5355 (envy), G5354 (envy)\nVariants: φθονέω",
    5355: "φθόνος (phthónos | fthon'-os)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to the base of G5351\nStrong's: ill-will (as detraction), i.e. jealousy (spite)\nKJV: —envy.\n\nSee: G5351 \n\nCognate Group: G5355 (envy), G5354 (envy)\nVariants: φθόνος",
    5356: "φθορά (phthorá | fthor-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G5351\nStrong's: decay, i.e. ruin (spontaneous or inflicted, literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —corruption, destroy, perish.\n\nSee: G5351 \n\nCognate Group: G5355 (envy), G5354 (envy)\nVariants: φθορά \nHebrew Equivalents: H1238 בָּקַק, H2256 חֶבֶל, H5034 נָבֵל, H7845 שַׁחַת",
    5357: "φιάλη (phiálē | fee-al'-ay)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a broad shallow cup (\"phial\")\nKJV: —vial.\n\nCognate Group: G5355 (envy), G5354 (envy)\nVariants: φιάλη \nHebrew Equivalents: H3563 כֹּוס, H4219 מִזְרָק, H6170 עֲרוּגָה, H7083 קֶסֶת",
    5358: "φιλάγαθος (philágathos | fil-ag'-ath-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G18\nStrong's: fond to good, i.e. a promoter of virtue\nKJV: —love of good men.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G18 \n\nCognate Group: G19 (goodness), G865 (despiser of those that are good), G17 (them that do well), G5358 (love of good men)\nVariants: φιλάγαθος",
    5359: "Φιλαδέλφεια (Philadélpheia | fil-ad-el'-fee-ah)\n\nDerivation: from Φιλάδελφος (the same as G5361), a king of Pergamos\nStrong's: Philadelphia, a place in Asia Minor\nKJV: —Philadelphia.\n\nSee: G5361 \n\nCognate Group: G19 (goodness), G865 (despiser of those that are good), G17 (them that do well), G5358 (love of good men)\nVariants: Φιλαδέλφεια",
    5360: "φιλαδελφία (philadelphía | fil-ad-el-fee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5361\nStrong's: fraternal affection\nKJV: —brotherly love (kindness), love of the brethren.\n\nSee: G5361 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: φιλαδελφία",
    5361: "φιλάδελφος (philádelphos | fil-ad'-el-fos)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G80\nStrong's: fond of brethren, i.e. fraternal\nKJV: —love as brethren.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G80 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: φιλάδελφος",
    5362: "φίλανδρος (phílandros | fil'-an-dros)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G435\nStrong's: fond of man, i.e. affectionate as a wife\nKJV: —love their husbands.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G435 \n\nCognate Group: G5362 (love their husbands), G5363 (kindness)\nVariants: φίλανδρος",
    5363: "φιλανθρωπία (philanthrōpía | fil-an-thro-pee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5364\nStrong's: fondness of mankind, i.e. benevolence (\"philanthropy\")\nKJV: —kindness, love towards man.\n\nSee: G5364 \n\nCognate Group: G5362 (love their husbands), G5363 (kindness)\nVariants: φιλανθρωπία",
    5364: "φιλανθρώπως (philanthrṓpōs | fil-an-thro'-poce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from a compound of G5384 and G444\nStrong's: fondly to man (\"philanthropically\"), i.e. humanely\nKJV: —courteously.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G444 \n\nCognate Group: G5362 (love their husbands), G5363 (kindness)\nVariants: φιλανθρώπως",
    5365: "φιλαργυρία (philargyría | fil-ar-goo-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5366\nStrong's: avarice\nKJV: —love of money.\n\nSee: G5366 \n\nCognate Group: G5362 (love their husbands), G5363 (kindness)\nVariants: φιλαργυρία",
    5366: "φιλάργυρος (philárgyros | fil-ar'-goo-ros)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G696\nStrong's: fond of silver (money), i.e. avaricious\nKJV: —covetous.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G696 \n\nCognate Group: G5362 (love their husbands), G5363 (kindness)\nVariants: φιλάργυρος",
    5367: "φίλαυτος (phílautos | fil'-ow-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G846\nStrong's: fond of self, i.e. selfish\nKJV: —lover of own self.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G846 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φίλαυτος",
    5368: "φιλέω (philéō | fil-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5384\nStrong's: to be a friend to (fond of (an individual or an object)), i.e. have affection for (denoting personal attachment, as a matter of sentiment or feeling; while G25 is wider, embracing especially the judgment and the deliberate assent of the will as a matter of principle, duty and propriety: the two thus stand related very much as G2309 and G1014, or as G2372 and G3563 respectively; the former being chiefly of the heart and the latter of the head); specially, to kiss (as a mark of tenderness)\nKJV: —kiss, love.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G2372 \nSee: G25 \nSee: G2309 \nSee: G1014 \nSee: G3563 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H157 אָהַב, H5401 נָשַׁק, H7453 רֵעַ",
    5369: "φιλήδονος (philḗdonos | fil-ay'-don-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G2237\nStrong's: fond of pleasure, i.e. voluptuous\nKJV: —lover of pleasure.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G2237 \n\nCognate Group: G5369 (lover of pleasure), G2237 (lust)\nVariants: φιλήδονος",
    5370: "φίλημα (phílēma | fil'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5368\nStrong's: a kiss\nKJV: —kiss.\n\nSee: G5368 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φίλημα \nHebrew Equivalents: H5390 נְשִׁיקָה",
    5371: "Φιλήμων (Philḗmōn | fil-ay'-mone)\n\nDerivation: from G5368\nStrong's: friendly; Philemon, a Christian\nKJV: —Philemon.\n\nSee: G5368 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: Φιλήμων",
    5372: "Φίλητος (Phílētos | fil-ay-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G5368\nStrong's: amiable; Philetus, an opposer of Christianity\nKJV: —Philetus.\n\nSee: G5368 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: Φίλητος",
    5373: "φιλία (philía | fil-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5384\nStrong's: fondness\nKJV: —friendship.\n\nSee: G5384 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλία \nHebrew Equivalents: H157 אָהַב, H160 אַהֲבָה, H1730 דֹּוד, H7453 רֵעַ",
    5374: "Φιλιππήσιος (Philippḗsios | fil-ip-pay'-see-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5375\nStrong's: a Philippesian (Philippian), i.e. native of Philippi\nKJV: —Philippian.\n\nSee: G5375 \n\nCognate Group: G5375 (Philippi), G5374 (Philippian)\nVariants: Φιλιππήσιος",
    5375: "Φίλιπποι (Phílippoi | fil'-ip-poy)\n\nDerivation: plural of G5376\nStrong's: Philippi, a place in Macedonia\nKJV: —Philippi.\n\nSee: G5376 \n\nCognate Group: G5375 (Philippi), G5374 (Philippian)\nVariants: Φίλιπποι",
    5376: "Φίλιππος (Phílippos | fil'-ip-pos)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G2462\nStrong's: fond of horses; Philippus, the name of four Israelites\nKJV: —Philip.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G2462 \n\nCognate Group: G5375 (Philippi), G5374 (Philippian)\nVariants: Φίλιππος",
    5377: "φιλόθεος (philótheos | fil-oth'-eh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G2316\nStrong's: fond of God, i.e. pious\nKJV: —lover of God.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G2316 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλόθεος",
    5378: "Φιλόλογος (Philólogos | fil-ol'-og-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G3056\nStrong's: fond of words, i.e. talkative (argumentative, learned, \"philological\"); Philologus, a Christian\nKJV: —Philologus.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G3056 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: Φιλόλογος",
    5379: "φιλονεικία (philoneikía | fil-on-i-kee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5380\nStrong's: quarrelsomeness, i.e. a dispute\nKJV: —strife.\n\nSee: G5380 \n\nCognate Group: G5379 (strife), G5380 (contentious)\nVariants: φιλονεικία, φιλον(ε)ικία",
    5380: "φιλόνεικος (philóneikos | fil-on'-i-kos)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and νεῖκος (a quarrel\nStrong's: probably akin to G3534); fond of strife, i.e. disputatious\nKJV: —contentious.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G3534 \n\nCognate Group: G5379 (strife), G5380 (contentious)\nVariants: φιλόνεικος, φιλόν(ε)ικος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2388 חָזַק",
    5381: "φιλονεξία (philonexía | fil-on-ex-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5382\nStrong's: hospitableness\nKJV: —entertain stranger, hospitality.\n\nSee: G5382 \n\nCognate Group: G3579 (entertain), G5382 (given to hospitality), G5381 (entertain stranger), G3578 (lodging), G3580 (lodge strangers), G3581 (host)\nVariants: φιλονεξία, φιλοξενία",
    5382: "φιλόξενος (philóxenos | fil-ox'-en-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G3581\nStrong's: fond of guests, i.e. hospitable\nKJV: —given to (lover of, use) hospitality.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G3581 \n\nCognate Group: G3579 (entertain), G5382 (given to hospitality), G5381 (entertain stranger), G3578 (lodging), G3580 (lodge strangers), G3581 (host)\nVariants: φιλόξενος",
    5383: "φιλοπρωτεύω (philoprōteúō | fil-op-rote-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5384 and G4413\nStrong's: to be fond of being first, i.e. ambitious of distinction\nKJV: —love to have the preeminence.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G4413 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλοπρωτεύω",
    5384: "φίλος (phílos | fee'-los)\n\nDerivation: properly, dear, i.e. a friend\nStrong's: actively, fond, i.e. friendly (still as a noun, an associate, neighbor, etc.)\nKJV: —friend.\n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φίλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H157 אָהַב, H441 אַלּוּף, H2269 חֲבַר, H3045 יָדַע, H4828 מֵרֵעַ, H7453 רֵעַ, H7965 שָׁלֹום, H8003 שָׁלֵם",
    5385: "φιλοσοφία (philosophía | fil-os-of-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5386\nStrong's: \"philosophy\", i.e. (specially), Jewish sophistry\nKJV: —philosophy.\n\nSee: G5386 \n\nCognate Group: G5386 (philosopher), G5385 (philosophy)\nVariants: φιλοσοφία",
    5386: "φιλόσοφος (philósophos | fil-os'-of-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G4680\nStrong's: fond of wise things, i.e. a \"philosopher\"\nKJV: —philosopher.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G4680 \n\nCognate Group: G5386 (philosopher), G5385 (philosophy)\nVariants: φιλόσοφος \nHebrew Equivalents: H825 אַשָּׁף",
    5387: "φιλόστοργος (philóstorgos | fil-os'-tor-gos)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and στοργή (cherishing one's kindred, especially parents or children)\nStrong's: fond of natural relatives, i.e. fraternal towards fellow Christian\nKJV: —kindly affectioned.\n\nSee: G5384 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλόστοργος",
    5388: "φιλότεκνος (philóteknos | fil-ot'-ek-nos)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G5043\nStrong's: fond of one's children, i.e. maternal\nKJV: —love their children.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G5043 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλότεκνος",
    5389: "φιλοτιμέομαι (philotiméomai | fil-ot-im-eh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from a compound of G5384 and G5092\nStrong's: to be fond of honor, i.e. emulous (eager or earnest to do something)\nKJV: —labour, strive, study.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G5092 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλοτιμέομαι",
    5390: "φιλοφρόνως (philophrónōs | fil-of-ron'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5391\nStrong's: with friendliness of mind, i.e. kindly\nKJV: —courteously.\n\nSee: G5391 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλοφρόνως",
    5391: "φιλόφρων (philóphrōn | fil-of'-rone)\n\nDerivation: from G5384 and G5424\nStrong's: friendly of mind, i.e. kind\nKJV: —courteous.\n\nSee: G5384 \nSee: G5424 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιλόφρων, ταπεινόφρων \nHebrew Equivalents: H8213 שָׁפֵל",
    5392: "φιμόω (phimóō | fee-mo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from φιμός (a muzzle)\nStrong's: to muzzle\nKJV: —muzzle.\n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φιμόω, κημόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2629 חָסַם",
    5393: "Φλέγων (Phlégōn | fleg'-one)\n\nDerivation: active participle of the base of G5395\nStrong's: blazing; Phlegon, a Christian\nKJV: —Phlegon.\n\nSee: G5395 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: Φλέγων",
    5394: "φλογίζω (phlogízō | flog-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5395\nStrong's: to cause a blaze, i.e. ignite (figuratively, to inflame with passion)\nKJV: —set on fire.\n\nSee: G5395 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φλογίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2760 חָרַךְ, H3857 לָהַט, H3947 לָקַח",
    5395: "φλόξ (phlóx | flox)\n\nDerivation: from a primary φλέγω (to \"flash\" or \"flame\")\nStrong's: a blaze\nKJV: —flame(-ing).\n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φλόξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H784 אֵשׁ, H2131 זִיקָה, H3827 לַבָּה, H3851 לַהַב, H3852 לֶהָבָה, H7008 קִיטֹור, H7632 שָׁבִיב, H7957 שַׁלְהֶבֶת",
    5396: "φλυαρέω (phlyaréō | floo-ar-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5397\nStrong's: to be a babbler or trifler, i.e. (by implication) to berate idly or mischievously\nKJV: —prate against.\n\nSee: G5397 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φλυαρέω",
    5397: "φλύαρος (phlýaros | floo'-ar-os)\n\nDerivation: from φλύω (to bubble)\nStrong's: a garrulous person, i.e. prater\nKJV: —tattler.\n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φλύαρος",
    5398: "φοβερός (phoberós | fob-er-os')\n\nDerivation: from G5401\nStrong's: frightful, i.e. (objectively) formidable\nKJV: —fearful, terrible.\n\nSee: G5401 \n\nCognate Group: G5387 (kindly affectioned), G5384 (friend), G5377 (lover of God), G5369 (lover of pleasure), G5383 (love to have the preeminence), G2705 (kiss), G5370 (kiss), G5373 (friendship), G5367 (lover of own self), G5368 (kiss)\nVariants: φοβερός \nHebrew Equivalents: H366 אָיֹם, H1763 דְּחַל, H3372 יָרֵא, H4172 מֹורָא",
    5399: "φοβέω (phobéō | fob-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5401\nStrong's: to frighten, i.e. (passively) to be alarmed; by analogy, to be in awe of, i.e. revere\nKJV: —be (+ sore) afraid, fear (exceedingly), reverence.\n\nSee: G5401 \n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: φοβέω, φοβέομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H1481 גּוּר, H1672 דָּאַג, H1763 דְּחַל, H2342 חוּל, H2730 חָרֵד, H2865 חָתַת, H3025 יָגֹר, H3372 יָרֵא, H3372 יָרֵא, H5128 נוּעַ, H6206 עָרַץ, H6342 פָּחַד, H6399 פְּלַח, H7264 רָגַז, H7493 רָעַשׁ, H7760 שׂוּם",
    5400: "φόβητρον (phóbētron | fob'-ay-tron)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of G5399\nStrong's: a frightening thing, i.e. terrific portent\nKJV: —fearful sight.\n\nSee: G5399 \n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: φόβητρον, φόβητρον/φόβηθρον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2283 חָגָא",
    5401: "φόβος (phóbos | fob'-os)\n\nDerivation: from a primary φέβομαι (to be put in fear)\nStrong's: alarm or fright\nKJV: —be afraid, + exceedingly, fear, terror.\n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: φόβος \nHebrew Equivalents: H367 אֵימָה, H1763 דְּחַל, H2730 חָרֵד, H2844 חַת, H3374 יִרְאָה, H6184 עָרִיץ, H6343 פַּחַד, H7413 רָמָה",
    5402: "Φοίβη (Phoíbē | foy'-bay)\n\nDerivation: feminine of φοῖβος (bright\nStrong's: probably akin to the base of G5457); Phœbe, a Christian woman\nKJV: —Phebe.\n\nSee: G5457 \n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: Φοίβη",
    5403: "Φοινίκη (Phoiníkē | foy-nee'-kay)\n\nDerivation: from G5404\nStrong's: palm-country; Phœnice (or Phœnicia), a region of Palestine\nKJV: —Phenice, Phenicia.\n\nSee: G5404 \n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: Φοινίκη",
    5404: "φοῖνιξ (phoînix | foy'-nix)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a palm-tree\nKJV: —palm (tree).\n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: φοῖνιξ, φοῖνιξ1, φοῖνιξ/φοίνιξ, φοίνιξ",
    5405: "Φοῖνιξ (Phoînix | foy'-nix)\n\nDerivation: probably the same as G5404\nStrong's: Phœnix, a place in Crete\nKJV: —Phenice.\n\nSee: G5404 \n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: Φοῖνιξ, Φοῖνιξ2",
    5406: "φονεύς (phoneús | fon-yooce')\n\nDerivation: from G5408\nStrong's: a murderer (always of criminal (or at least intentional) homicide; which G443 does not necessarily imply; while G4607 is a special term for a public bandit)\nKJV: —murderer.\n\nSee: G5408 \nSee: G443 \nSee: G4607 \n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: φονεύς",
    5407: "φονεύω (phoneúō | fon-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5406\nStrong's: to be a murderer (of)\nKJV: —kill, do murder, slay.\n\nSee: G5406 \n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: φονεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2026 הָרַג, H2763 חָרַם, H5221 נָכָה, H7523 רָצַח",
    5408: "φόνος (phónos | fon'-os)\n\nDerivation: from an obsolete primary φένω (to slay)\nStrong's: murder\nKJV: —murder, + be slain with, slaughter.\n\nCognate Group: G5400 (fearful sight), G5399 (be afraid), G5401 (be afraid)\nVariants: φόνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1818 דָּם, H2719 חֶרֶב, H6297 פֶּגֶר, H6310 פֶּה, H7523 רָצַח",
    5409: "φορέω (phoréō | for-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5411\nStrong's: to have a burden, i.e. (by analogy) to wear as clothing or a constant accompaniment\nKJV: —bear, wear.\n\nSee: G5411 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φορέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3254 יָסַף",
    5410: "Φόρον (Phóron | for'-on)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: a forum or market-place; only in comparative with G675 ; a station on the Appian road\nKJV: —forum.\n\nSee: G675 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: Φόρον, φόρον, Ἀππίου~Φόρον, Ἀππίου Φόρον",
    5411: "φόρος (phóros | for'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5342\nStrong's: a load (as borne), i.e. (figuratively) a tax (properly, an individual assessment on persons or property; whereas G5056 is usually a general toll on goods or travel)\nKJV: —tribute.\n\nSee: G5342 \nSee: G5056 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φόρος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1094 בְּלֹוא, H1980 הָלַךְ, H4060 מִדָּה, H4522 מַס, H6064 עָנַשׁ",
    5412: "φορτίζω (phortízō | for-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5414\nStrong's: to load up (properly, as a vessel or animal), i.e. (figuratively) to overburden with ceremony (or spiritual anxiety)\nKJV: —lade, by heavy laden.\n\nSee: G5414 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φορτίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7809 שָׁחַד",
    5413: "φορτίον (phortíon | for-tee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G5414\nStrong's: an invoice (as part of freight), i.e. (figuratively) a task or service\nKJV: —burden.\n\nSee: G5414 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φορτίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H4853 מַשָּׂא, H5520 סֹךְ",
    5414: "φόρτος (phórtos | for'-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G5342\nStrong's: something carried, i.e. the cargo of a ship\nKJV: —lading.\n\nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φόρτος G5846",
    5415: "Φορτουνᾶτος (Phortounâtos | for-too-nat'-os)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: \"fortunate\"; Fortunatus, a Christian\nKJV: —Fortunatus.\n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: Φορτουνᾶτος, Φορτουνάτος",
    5416: "φραγέλλιον (phragéllion | frag-el'-le-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative from the base of G5417\nStrong's: a whip, i.e. Roman lash as a public punishment\nKJV: —scourge.\n\nSee: G5417 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φραγέλλιον",
    5417: "φραγελλόω (phragellóō | frag-el-lo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed equivalent of the Latin flagellum\nStrong's: to whip, i.e. lash as a public punishment\nKJV: —scourge.\n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φραγελλόω, φλαγελλόω",
    5418: "φραγμός (phragmós | frag-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G5420\nStrong's: a fence, or inclosing barrier (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —hedge (+ round about), partition.\n\nSee: G5420 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φραγμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1447 גָּדֵר, H1448 גְּדֵרָה, H4189 מֹושְׁכָה, H4881 מְשׂוּכָה, H5823 עָזַק, H6556 פֶּרֶץ",
    5419: "φράζω (phrázō | frad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G5420 through the idea of defining\nStrong's: to indicate (by word or act), i.e. (specially), to expound\nKJV: — declare.\n\nSee: G5420 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φράζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2244 חָבָא, H3384 יָרָה",
    5420: "φράσσω (phrássō | fras'-so)\n\nDerivation: apparently a strengthening form of the base of G5424\nStrong's: to fence or inclose, i.e. (specially), to block up (figuratively, to silence)\nKJV: —stop.\n\nSee: G5424 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φράσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H331 אָטַם, H5472 סוּג, H5480 סוּךְ, H5526 סָכַךְ, H5640 סָתַם, H7515 רָפַשׂ",
    5421: "φρέαρ (phréar | freh'-ar)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a hole in the ground (dug for obtaining or holding water or other purposes), i.e. a cistern or well; figuratively, an abyss (as a prison)\nKJV: —well, pit.\n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φρέαρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H875 בְּאֵר, H953 בֹּור, H1460 גֵּו",
    5422: "φρεναπατάω (phrenapatáō | fren-ap-at-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5423\nStrong's: to be a mind-misleader, i.e. delude\nKJV: —deceive.\n\nSee: G5423 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φρεναπατάω",
    5423: "φρεναπάτης (phrenapátēs | fren-ap-at'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G5424 and G539\nStrong's: a mind-misleader, i.e. seducer\nKJV: —deceiver.\n\nSee: G5424 \nSee: G539 \n\nCognate Group: G5411 (tribute), G4376 (offering), G1313 (differing), G4851 (be better for), G399 (bear), G5413 (burden), G5412 (lade), G5414 (lading), G5342 (be), G2018 (add), G4374 (bring), G5409 (bear), G1308 (be better)\nVariants: φρεναπάτης",
    5424: "φρήν (phrḗn | frane)\n\nDerivation: probably from an obsolete φράω (to rein in or curb; compare G5420)\nStrong's: the midrif (as a partition of the body), i.e. (figuratively and by implication, of sympathy) the feelings (or sensitive nature; by extension (also in the plural) the mind or cognitive faculties)\nKJV: —understanding.\n\nSee: G5420 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρήν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3685 כְּסִיל, H3820 לֵב, H4486 מַנְדַּע",
    5425: "φρίσσω (phríssō | fris'-so)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary verb\nStrong's: to \"bristle\" or chill, i.e. shudder (fear)\nKJV: —tremble.\n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρίσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3735 כָּרָא, H5568 סָמַר, H5590 סָעַר",
    5426: "φρονέω (phronéō | fron-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5424\nStrong's: to exercise the mind, i.e. entertain or have a sentiment or opinion; by implication, to be (mentally) disposed (more or less earnestly in a certain direction); intensively, to interest oneself in (with concern or obedience)\nKJV: —set the affection on, (be) care(-ful), (be like-, + be of one, + be of the same, + let this) mind(-ed), regard, savour, think.\n\nSee: G5424 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρονέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2450 חָכָם, H7919 שָׂכַל",
    5427: "φρόνημα (phrónēma | fron'-ay-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5426\nStrong's: (mental) inclination or purpose\nKJV: —(be, + be carnally, + be spiritually) mind(-ed).\n\nSee: G5426 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρόνημα",
    5428: "φρόνησις (phrónēsis | fron'-ay-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5426\nStrong's: mental action or activity, i.e. intellectual or moral insight\nKJV: —prudence, wisdom.\n\nSee: G5426 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρόνησις \nHebrew Equivalents: H999 בִּינָה, H1847 דַּעַת, H2451 חׇכְמָה, H3820 לֵב, H6195 עׇרְמָה, H7307 רוּחַ, H7922 שֶׂכֶל, H8394 תָּבוּן",
    5429: "φρόνιμος (phrónimos | fron'-ee-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G5424\nStrong's: thoughtful, i.e. sagacious or discreet (implying a cautious character; while G4680 denotes practical skill or acumen; and G4908 indicates rather intelligence or mental acquirement); in a bad sense conceited (also in the comparative)\nKJV: —wise(-r).\n\nSee: G5424 \nSee: G4680 \nSee: G4908 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρόνιμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H995 בִּין, H2450 חָכָם, H4209 מְזִמָּה, H6175 עָרוּם, H8394 תָּבוּן",
    5430: "φρονίμως (phronímōs | fron-im'-oce)\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5429\nStrong's: prudently\nKJV: —wisely.\n\nSee: G5429 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρονίμως",
    5431: "φροντίζω (phrontízō | fron-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5424\nStrong's: to exercise thought, i.e. be anxious\nKJV: —be careful.\n\nSee: G5424 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φροντίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1672 דָּאַג, H2803 חָשַׁב, H3372 יָרֵא, H6342 פָּחַד",
    5432: "φρουρέω (phrouréō | froo-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G4253 and G3708\nStrong's: to be a watcher in advance, i.e. to mount guard as a sentinel (post spies at gates); figuratively, to hem in, protect\nKJV: —keep (with a garrison). Compare G5083 .\n\nSee: G4253 \nSee: G3708 \nSee: G5083 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρουρέω",
    5433: "φρυάσσω (phryássō | froo-as'-so)\n\nDerivation: akin to G1032, G1031\nStrong's: to snort (as a spirited horse), i.e. (figuratively) to make a tumult\nKJV: —rage.\n\nSee: G1031 \nSee: G1032 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρυάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7283 רָגַשׁ",
    5434: "φρύγανον (phrýganon | froo'-gan-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a presumed derivative of φρύγω (to roast or parch\nStrong's: akin to the base of G5395); something desiccated, i.e. a dry twig\nKJV: —stick.\n\nSee: G5395 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φρύγανον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2738 חָרוּל, H7110 קֶצֶף, H7179 קַשׁ",
    5435: "Φρυγία (Phrygía | froog-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: probably of foreign origin\nStrong's: Phrygia, a region of Asia Minor\nKJV: —Phrygia.\n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: Φρυγία",
    5436: "Φύγελλος (Phýgellos | foog'-el-los)\n\nDerivation: probably from G5343\nStrong's: fugitive; Phygellus, an apostate Christian\nKJV: —Phygellus.\n\nSee: G5343 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: Φύγελλος, Φύγελος, Φύγελος/Φύγελλος",
    5437: "φυγή (phygḗ | foog-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5343\nStrong's: a fleeing, i.e. escape\nKJV: —flight.\n\nSee: G5343 \n\nCognate Group: G5430 (wisely), G877 (folly), G5428 (prudence), G5429 (wise), G5424 (understanding), G5427 (mind), G878 (fool), G5426 (set the affection on)\nVariants: φυγή \nHebrew Equivalents: H1272 בָּרַח, H4498 מָנֹוס, H5127 נוּס",
    5438: "φυλακή (phylakḗ | foo-lak-ay')\n\nDerivation: from G5442\nStrong's: a guarding or (concretely, guard), the act, the person; figuratively, the place, the condition, or (specially), the time (as a division of day or night), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —cage, hold, (im-)prison(-ment), ward, watch.\n\nSee: G5442 \n\nCognate Group: G5438 (cage), G5441 (keeper), G5442 (beward), G5439 (imprison)\nVariants: φυλακή \nHebrew Equivalents: H615 אָסִיר, H821 אַשְׁמֻרָה, H3608 כֶּלֶא, H4115 מַהְפֶּכֶת, H4307 מַטָּרָא, H4525 מַסְגֵּר, H4685 מָצֹוד, H4931 מִשְׁמֶרֶת, H5341 נָצַר",
    5439: "φυλακίζω (phylakízō | foo-lak-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5441\nStrong's: to incarcerate\nKJV: —imprison.\n\nSee: G5441 \n\nCognate Group: G5438 (cage), G5441 (keeper), G5442 (beward), G5439 (imprison)\nVariants: φυλακίζω",
    5440: "φυλακτήριον (phylaktḗrion | foo-lak-tay'-ree-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a derivative of G5442\nStrong's: a guard-case, i.e. \"phylactery\" for wearing slips of Scripture texts\nKJV: — phylactery.\n\nSee: G5442 \n\nCognate Group: G5438 (cage), G5441 (keeper), G5442 (beward), G5439 (imprison)\nVariants: φυλακτήριον",
    5441: "φύλαξ (phýlax | foo'-lax)\n\nDerivation: from G5442\nStrong's: a watcher or sentry\nKJV: —keeper.\n\nSee: G5442 \n\nCognate Group: G5438 (cage), G5441 (keeper), G5442 (beward), G5439 (imprison)\nVariants: φύλαξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H6697 צוּר, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    5442: "φυλάσσω (phylássō | foo-las'-so)\n\nDerivation: probably from G5443 through the idea of isolation\nStrong's: to watch, i.e. be on guard (literally of figuratively); by implication, to preserve, obey, avoid\nKJV: —beward, keep (self), observe, save. Compare G5083 .\n\nSee: G5443 \nSee: G5083 \n\nCognate Group: G5438 (cage), G5441 (keeper), G5442 (beward), G5439 (imprison)\nVariants: φυλάσσω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1961 הָיָה, H2095 זְהַר, H2421 חָיָה, H5341 נָצַר, H5425 נָתַר, H6113 עָצָר, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6487 פִּקָּדֹון, H6822 צָפָה, H8085 שָׁמַע, H8104 שָׁמַר",
    5443: "φυλή (phylḗ | foo-lay')\n\nDerivation: from G5453 (compare G5444)\nStrong's: an offshoot, i.e. race or clan\nKJV: —kindred, tribe.\n\nSee: G5453 \nSee: G5444 \n\nCognate Group: G5443 (kindred)\nVariants: φυλή \nHebrew Equivalents: H524 אֻמָּה, H1004 בַּיִת, H1471 גֹּוי, H3816 לְאֹם, H4138 מֹולֶדֶת, H4294 מַטֶּה, H4940 מִשְׁפָּחָה, H7626 שֵׁבֶט",
    5444: "φύλλον (phýllon | fool'-lon)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5443\nStrong's: a sprout, i.e. leaf\nKJV: —leaf.\n\nSee: G5443 \n\nCognate Group: G5443 (kindred)\nVariants: φύλλον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5929 עָלֶה, H6074 עֳפִי",
    5445: "φύραμα (phýrama | foo'-ram-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a prolonged form of φύρω (to mix a liquid with a solid\nStrong's: perhaps akin to G5453 through the idea of swelling in bulk), mean to knead; a mass of dough\nKJV: —lump.\n\nSee: G5453 \n\nCognate Group: G5443 (kindred)\nVariants: φύραμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4863 מִשְׁאֶרֶת, H6182 עֲרִיסָה",
    5446: "φυσικός (physikós | foo-see-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G5449\nStrong's: \"physical\", i.e. (by implication) instinctive\nKJV: —natural. Compare G5591 .\n\nSee: G5449 \nSee: G5591 \n\nCognate Group: G5446 (natural), G5447 (naturally), G5449 (kind)\nVariants: φυσικός",
    5447: "φυσικῶς (physikōs | foo-see-koce')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5446\nStrong's: \"physically\", i.e. (by implication) instinctively\nKJV: —naturally.\n\nSee: G5446 \n\nCognate Group: G5446 (natural), G5447 (naturally), G5449 (kind)\nVariants: φυσικῶς",
    5448: "φυσιόω (physióō | foo-see-o'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5449 in the primary sense of blowing\nStrong's: to inflate, i.e. (figuratively) make proud (haughty)\nKJV: —puff up.\n\nSee: G5449 \n\nCognate Group: G5448 (puff up), G5450 (swelling)\nVariants: φυσιόω",
    5449: "φύσις (phýsis | foo'-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5453\nStrong's: growth (by germination or expansion), i.e. (by implication) natural production (lineal descent); by extension, a genus or sort; figuratively, native disposition, constitution or usage\nKJV: —(man-)kind, nature(-al).\n\nSee: G5453 \n\nCognate Group: G5446 (natural), G5447 (naturally), G5449 (kind)\nVariants: φύσις",
    5450: "φυσίωσις (physíōsis | foo-see'-o-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5448\nStrong's: inflation, i.e. (figuratively) haughtiness\nKJV: —swelling.\n\nSee: G5448 \n\nCognate Group: G5448 (puff up), G5450 (swelling)\nVariants: φυσίωσις",
    5451: "φυτεία (phyteía | foo-ti'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5452\nStrong's: trans-planting, i.e. (concretely) a shrub or vegetable\nKJV: —plant.\n\nSee: G5452 \n\nCognate Group: G5448 (puff up), G5450 (swelling)\nVariants: φυτεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H4302 מַטָּע, H5193 נָטַע",
    5452: "φυτεύω (phyteúō | foot-yoo'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5453\nStrong's: to set out in the earth, i.e. implant; figuratively, to instil doctrine\nKJV: —plant.\n\nSee: G5453 \n\nCognate Group: G5448 (puff up), G5450 (swelling)\nVariants: φυτεύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5193 נָטַע, H5341 נָצַר, H6213 עָשָׂה, H8362 שָׁתַל",
    5453: "φύω (phýō | foo'-o)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: probably originally, to \"puff\" or blow, i.e. to swell up; but only used in the implied sense, to germinate or grow (sprout, produce), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —spring (up).\n\nCognate Group: G5448 (puff up), G5450 (swelling)\nVariants: φύω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5193 נָטַע, H5927 עָלָה, H6509 פָּרָה, H6779 צָמַח",
    5454: "φωλεός (phōleós | fo-leh-os')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivative\nStrong's: a burrow or lurking-place\nKJV: —hole.\n\nCognate Group: G5448 (puff up), G5450 (swelling)\nVariants: φωλεός",
    5455: "φωνέω (phōnéō | fo-neh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5456\nStrong's: to emit a sound (animal, human or instrumental); by implication, to address in words or by name, also in imitation\nKJV: —call (for), crow, cry.\n\nSee: G5456 \n\nCognate Group: G4859 (consent), G2019 (cry), G4857 (concord), G4858 (music), G4856 (agree together), G5455 (call), G5456 (noise)\nVariants: φωνέω, ἐμφωνέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1898 הָגָה, H1898 הָגָה, H5375 נָשָׂא, H6850 צָפַף, H7121 קָרָא, H7891 שִׁיר, H8628 תָּקַע",
    5456: "φωνή (phōnḗ | fo-nay')\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G5316 through the idea of disclosure\nStrong's: a tone (articulate, bestial or artificial); by implication, an address (for any purpose), saying or language\nKJV: —noise, sound, voice.\n\nSee: G5316 \n\nCognate Group: G4859 (consent), G2019 (cry), G4857 (concord), G4858 (music), G4856 (agree together), G5455 (call), G5456 (noise)\nVariants: φωνή \nHebrew Equivalents: H565 אִמְרָה, H1600 גָּעָה, H1697 דָּבָר, H1995 הָמֹון, H2201 זַעַק, H3956 לָשֹׁון, H4687 מִצְוָה, H5100 נְהָמָה, H6310 פֶּה, H6618 פְּתַלְתֹּל, H6963 קֹול, H8193 שָׂפָה, H8467 תְּחִנָּה, H8643 תְּרוּעָה",
    5457: "φῶς (phōs | foce)\n\nDerivation: from an obsolete φάω (to shine or make manifest, especially by rays\nStrong's: compare G5316, G5346); luminousness (in the widest application, natural or artificial, abstract or concrete, literal or figurative)\nKJV: —fire, light.\n\nSee: G5316 \nSee: G5346 \n\nCognate Group: G5462 (light), G2017 (give light), G5457 (fire), G5458 (light), G2020 (begin to dawn), G5460 (bright), G5461 (enlighten), G5459 (day star)\nVariants: φῶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H216 אֹור, H5043 נֶבְרְשָׁא, H5051 נֹגַהּ, H5094 נְהִיר, H5216 נִיר",
    5458: "φωστήρ (phōstḗr | foce-tare')\n\nDerivation: from G5457\nStrong's: an illuminator, i.e. (concretely) a luminary, or (abstractly) brilliancy\nKJV: —light.\n\nSee: G5457 \n\nCognate Group: G5462 (light), G2017 (give light), G5457 (fire), G5458 (light), G2020 (begin to dawn), G5460 (bright), G5461 (enlighten), G5459 (day star)\nVariants: φωστήρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H2096 זֹהַר, H3974 מָאֹור",
    5459: "φωσφόρος (phōsphóros | foce-for'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5457 and G5342\nStrong's: light-bearing (\"phosphorus\"), i.e. (specially), the morning-star (figuratively)\nKJV: —day star.\n\nSee: G5457 \nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G5462 (light), G2017 (give light), G5457 (fire), G5458 (light), G2020 (begin to dawn), G5460 (bright), G5461 (enlighten), G5459 (day star)\nVariants: φωσφόρος",
    5460: "φωτεινός (phōteinós | fo-ti-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G5457\nStrong's: lustrous, i.e. transparent or well-illuminated (figuratively)\nKJV: —bright, full of light.\n\nSee: G5457 \n\nCognate Group: G5462 (light), G2017 (give light), G5457 (fire), G5458 (light), G2020 (begin to dawn), G5460 (bright), G5461 (enlighten), G5459 (day star)\nVariants: φωτεινός",
    5461: "φωτίζω (phōtízō | fo-tid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5457\nStrong's: to shed rays, i.e. to shine or (transitively) to brighten up (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —enlighten, illuminate, (bring to, give) light, make to see.\n\nSee: G5457 \n\nCognate Group: G5462 (light), G2017 (give light), G5457 (fire), G5458 (light), G2020 (begin to dawn), G5460 (bright), G5461 (enlighten), G5459 (day star)\nVariants: φωτίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H215 אֹור, H224 אוּרִים, H3384 יָרָה, H5050 נָגַהּ, H5102 נָהַר, H5214 נִיר, H7200 רָאָה",
    5462: "φωτισμός (phōtismós | fo-tis-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G5461\nStrong's: illumination (figuratively)\nKJV: —light.\n\nSee: G5461 \n\nCognate Group: G5462 (light), G2017 (give light), G5457 (fire), G5458 (light), G2020 (begin to dawn), G5460 (bright), G5461 (enlighten), G5459 (day star)\nVariants: φωτισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H216 אֹור, H3974 מָאֹור",
    5463: "χαίρω (chaírō | khah'-ee-ro)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to be \"cheer\"ful, i.e. calmly happy or well-off; impersonally, especially as salutation (on meeting or parting), be well\nKJV: —farewell, be glad, God speed, greeting, hall, joy(- fully), rejoice.\n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαίρω \nHebrew Equivalents: H157 אָהַב, H1523 גִּיל, H3190 יָטַב, H5937 עָלַז, H7442 רָנַן, H7797 שׂוּשׂ, H7965 שָׁלֹום, H8055 שָׂמַח",
    5464: "χάλαζα (chálaza | khal'-ad-zah)\n\nDerivation: probably from G5465\nStrong's: hail\nKJV: —hail.\n\nSee: G5465 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χάλαζα \nHebrew Equivalents: H68 אֶבֶן, H1259 בָּרָד",
    5465: "χαλάω (chaláō | khal-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5490\nStrong's: to lower (as into a void)\nKJV: —let down, strike.\n\nSee: G5490 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2118 זָחַח, H6186 עָרַךְ, H7971 שָׁלַח",
    5466: "Χαλδαῖος (Chaldaîos | khal-dah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: probably of Hebrew or (H3778)\nStrong's: a Chaldæan (i.e. Kasdi), or native or the region of the lower Euphrates\nKJV: —Chaldæan.\nSee: H3778\n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: Χαλδαῖος, χαλδαῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3778 כַּשְׂדִּי",
    5467: "χαλεπός (chalepós | khal-ep-os')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G5465 through the idea of reducing the strength\nStrong's: difficult, i.e. dangerous, or (by implication) furious\nKJV: —fierce, perilous.\n\nSee: G5465 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλεπός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3372 יָרֵא",
    5468: "χαλιναγωγέω (chalinagōgéō | khal-in-ag-ogue-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5469 and the reduplicated form of G71\nStrong's: to be a bit-leader, i.e. to curb (figuratively)\nKJV: —bridle.\n\nSee: G5469 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλιναγωγέω, χαλινόω",
    5469: "χαλινός (chalinós | khal-ee-nos')\n\nDerivation: from G5465\nStrong's: a curb or head-stall (as curbing the spirit)\nKJV: —bit, bridle.\n\nSee: G5465 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλινός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4964 מֶתֶג, H7448 רֶסֶן",
    5470: "χάλκεος (chálkeos | khal'-keh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5475\nStrong's: coppery\nKJV: —brass.\n\nSee: G5475 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χάλκεος, χαλκοῦς, χάλκοῦς \nHebrew Equivalents: H5153 נָחוּשׁ, H5178 נְחֹשֶׁת",
    5471: "χαλκεύς (chalkeús | khalk-yooce')\n\nDerivation: from G5475\nStrong's: a copper-worker or brazier\nKJV: —coppersmith.\n\nSee: G5475 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλκεύς \nHebrew Equivalents: H2793 חֹרֶשׁ, H2796 חָרָשׁ, H6884 צָרַף",
    5472: "χαλκηδών (chalkēdṓn | khal-kay-dohn')\n\nDerivation: from G5475 and perhaps G1491\nStrong's: copper-like, i.e. \"chalcedony\"\nKJV: —chalcedony.\n\nSee: G5475 \nSee: G1491 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλκηδών",
    5473: "χαλκίον (chalkíon | khal-kee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive from G5475\nStrong's: a copper dish\nKJV: —brazen vessel.\n\nSee: G5475 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλκίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H5518 סִיר, H7037 קַלַּחַת",
    5474: "χαλκολίβανον (chalkolíbanon | khal-kol-ib'-an-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G5475 and G3030 (in the implied mean of whiteness or brilliancy)\nStrong's: burnished copper, an alloy of copper (or gold) and silver having a brilliant lustre\nKJV: —fine brass.\n\nSee: G5475 \nSee: G3030 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλκολίβανον",
    5475: "χαλκός (chalkós | khal-kos')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from G5465 through the idea of hollowing out as a vessel (this metal being chiefly used for that purpose)\nStrong's: copper (the substance, or some implement or coin made of it)\nKJV: —brass, money.\n\nSee: G5465 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαλκός \nHebrew Equivalents: H5154 נְחוּשָׁה, H5178 נְחֹשֶׁת, H7037 קַלַּחַת",
    5476: "χαμαί (chamaí | kham-ah'-ee)\n\nDerivation: adverb perhaps from the base of G5490 through the idea of a fissure in the soil\nStrong's: earthward, i.e. prostrate\nKJV: —on (to) the ground.\n\nSee: G5490 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαμαί",
    5477: "Χαναάν (Chanaán | khan-ah-an')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H3667)\nStrong's: Chanaan (i.e. Kenaan), the early name of Palestine\nKJV: —Chanaan.\nSee: H3667\n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: Χαναάν, Χανάαν, χανάαν \nHebrew Equivalents: H3667 כְּנַעַן",
    5478: "Χανααναῖος (Chanaanaîos | khan-ah-an-ah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G5477\nStrong's: a Chanaanæan (i.e. Kenaanite), or native of gentile Palestine\nKJV: —of Canaan.\n\nSee: G5477 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: Χανααναῖος, Χαναναῖος, χαναναῖος",
    5479: "χαρά (chará | khar-ah')\n\nDerivation: from G5463\nStrong's: cheerfulness, i.e. calm delight\nKJV: —gladness, X greatly, (X be exceeding) joy(-ful, -fully, -fulness, -ous).\n\nSee: G5463 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαρά \nHebrew Equivalents: H1524 גִּיל, H4234 מָחֹול, H7440 רִנָּה, H7442 רָנַן, H7814 שְׂחֹוק, H8057 שִׂמְחָה, H8342 שָׂשֹׂון",
    5480: "χάραγμα (cháragma | khar'-ag-mah)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5482\nStrong's: a scratch or etching, i.e. stamp (as a badge of servitude), or scupltured figure (statue)\nKJV: —graven, mark.\n\nSee: G5482 \n\nCognate Group: G5480 (graven)\nVariants: χάραγμα",
    5481: "χαρακτήρ (charaktḗr | khar-ak-tare')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5482\nStrong's: a graver (the tool or the person), i.e. (by implication) engraving ((\"character\"), the figure stamped, i.e. an exact copy or (figuratively) representation)\nKJV: —express image.\n\nSee: G5482 \n\nCognate Group: G5481 (express image)\nVariants: χαρακτήρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H6867 צָרֶבֶת",
    5482: "χάραξ (chárax | khar'-ax)\n\nDerivation: from χαράσσω (to sharpen to a point\nStrong's: akin to G1125 through the idea of scratching); a stake, i.e. (by implication) a palisade or rampart (military mound for circumvallation in a siege)\nKJV: —trench.\n\nSee: G1125 \n\nCognate Group: G5481 (express image)\nVariants: χάραξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3733 כַּר, H4674 מֻצָּב, H4685 מָצֹוד, H4692 מָצֹור, H5550 סֹלְלָה",
    5483: "χαρίζομαι (charízomai | khar-id'-zom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G5485\nStrong's: to grant as a favor, i.e. gratuitously, in kindness, pardon or rescue\nKJV: —deliver, (frankly) forgive, (freely) give, grant.\n\nSee: G5485 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαρίζομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H5414 נָתַן",
    5484: "χάριν (chárin | khar'-in)\n\nDerivation: accusative case of G5485 as preposition\nStrong's: through favor of, i.e. on account of\nKJV: —be-(for) cause of, for sake of, +…fore, X reproachfully.\n\nSee: G5485 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χάριν",
    5485: "χάρις (cháris | khar'-ece)\n\nDerivation: from G5463\nStrong's: graciousness (as gratifying), of manner or act (abstract or concrete; literal, figurative or spiritual; especially the divine influence upon the heart, and its reflection in the life; including gratitude)\nKJV: —acceptable, benefit, favour, gift, grace(- ious), joy, liberality, pleasure, thank(-s, -worthy).\n\nSee: G5463 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χάρις \nHebrew Equivalents: H1420 גְּדוּלָה, H2505 חָלַק, H2580 חֵן, H2617 חֵסֵד, H2896 טֹוב, H7356 רַחַם, H7522 רָצֹון",
    5486: "χάρισμα (chárisma | khar'-is-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5483\nStrong's: a (divine) gratuity, i.e. deliverance (from danger or passion); (specially), a (spiritual) endowment, i.e. (subjectively) religious qualification, or (objectively) miraculous faculty\nKJV: —(free) gift.\n\nSee: G5483 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χάρισμα",
    5487: "χαριτόω (charitóō | khar-ee-to'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5485\nStrong's: to grace, i.e. indue with special honor\nKJV: —make accepted, be highly favoured.\n\nSee: G5485 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χαριτόω",
    5488: "Χαῤῥάν (Charrhán | khar-hran')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H2771)\nStrong's: Charrhan (i.e. Charan), a place in Mesopotamia\nKJV: —Charran.\nSee: H2771\n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: Χαῤῥάν, Χαρράν, χαρράν \nHebrew Equivalents: H2771 חָרָן",
    5489: "χάρτης (chártēs | khar'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5482\nStrong's: a sheet (\"chart\") of writing-material (as to be scribbled over)\nKJV: —paper.\n\nSee: G5482 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χάρτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H4039 מְגִלָּה",
    5490: "χάσμα (chásma | khas'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from a form of an obsolete primary χάω (to \"gape\" or \"yawn\")\nStrong's: a \"chasm\" or vacancy (impassable interval)\nKJV: —gulf.\n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χάσμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H6354 פַּחַת",
    5491: "χεῖλος (cheîlos | khi'-los)\n\nDerivation: from a form of the same as G5490\nStrong's: a lip (as a pouring place); figuratively, a margin (of water)\nKJV: —lip, shore.\n\nSee: G5490 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χεῖλος \nHebrew Equivalents: H561 אֵמֶר, H3027 יָד, H3895 לְחִי, H6310 פֶּה, H8193 שָׂפָה",
    5492: "χειμάζω (cheimázō | khi-mad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5494\nStrong's: to storm, i.e. (passively) to labor under a gale\nKJV: —be tossed with tempest.\n\nSee: G5494 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χειμάζω, χειμάζομαι",
    5493: "χείμαῤῥος (cheímarrhos | khi'-mar-hros)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5494 and G4482\nStrong's: a storm-runlet, i.e. winter-torrent\nKJV: —brook.\n\nSee: G5494 \nSee: G4482 \n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χείμαῤῥος, χείμαρρος, χειμάρρους",
    5494: "χειμών (cheimṓn | khi-mone')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of χέω \nStrong's: (to pour; akin to the base of 5490 through the idea of a channel), meaning a storm (as pouring rain); by implication, the rainy season, i.e. winter\nKJV: —tempest, foul weather, winter.\n\nCognate Group: G5479 (gladness), G4796 (rejoice in), G2168 (thank), G2169 (thankfulness), G5485 (acceptable), G5486 (gift), G5463 (farewell), G5484 (be- cause of), G2170 (thankful), G5487 (make accepted), G884 (unthankful), G5483 (deliver)\nVariants: χειμών \nHebrew Equivalents: H1653 גֶּשֶׁם, H5638 סְתָו",
    5495: "χείρ (cheír | khire)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the base of G5494 in the sense of its congener the base of G5490 (through the idea of hollowness for grasping)\nStrong's: the hand (literally or figuratively (power); especially (by Hebraism) a means or instrument)\nKJV: —hand.\n\nSee: G5494 \nSee: G5490 \n\nCognate Group: G5499 (made by hands), G886 (made without hands), G5495 (hand), G5498 (handwriting), G5497 (some to lead by the hand), G5496 (lead by the hand), G5500 (choose)\nVariants: χείρ \nHebrew Equivalents: H2651 חֹפֶן, H3027 יָד, H3225 יָמִין, H3581 כֹּחַ, H3709 כַּף, H8168 שֹׁעַל, H8420 תָּו",
    5496: "χειραγωγέω (cheiragōgéō | khi-rag-ogue-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5497\nStrong's: to be a hand-leader, i.e. to guide (a blind person)\nKJV: —lead by the hand.\n\nSee: G5497 \n\nCognate Group: G5499 (made by hands), G886 (made without hands), G5495 (hand), G5498 (handwriting), G5497 (some to lead by the hand), G5496 (lead by the hand), G5500 (choose)\nVariants: χειραγωγέω",
    5497: "χειραγωγός (cheiragōgós | khi-rag-o-gos')\n\nDerivation: from G5495 and a reduplicated form of G71\nStrong's: a hand-leader, i.e. personal conductor (of a blind person)\nKJV: —some to lead by the hand.\n\nSee: G5495 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G5499 (made by hands), G886 (made without hands), G5495 (hand), G5498 (handwriting), G5497 (some to lead by the hand), G5496 (lead by the hand), G5500 (choose)\nVariants: χειραγωγός",
    5498: "χειρόγραφον (cheirógraphon | khi-rog'-raf-on)\n\nDerivation: neuter of a compound of G5495 and G1125\nStrong's: something hand-written (\"chirograph\"), i.e. a manuscript (specially, a legal document or bond (figuratively))\nKJV: —handwriting.\n\nSee: G5495 \nSee: G1125 \n\nCognate Group: G5499 (made by hands), G886 (made without hands), G5495 (hand), G5498 (handwriting), G5497 (some to lead by the hand), G5496 (lead by the hand), G5500 (choose)\nVariants: χειρόγραφον",
    5499: "χειροποίητος (cheiropoíētos | khi-rop-oy'-ay-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G5495 and a derivative of G4160\nStrong's: manufactured, i.e. of human construction\nKJV: —made by (make with) hands.\n\nSee: G5495 \nSee: G4160 \n\nCognate Group: G5499 (made by hands), G886 (made without hands), G5495 (hand), G5498 (handwriting), G5497 (some to lead by the hand), G5496 (lead by the hand), G5500 (choose)\nVariants: χειροποίητος \nHebrew Equivalents: H457 אֱלִיל",
    5500: "χειροτονέω (cheirotonéō | khi-rot-on-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a comparative of G5495 and τείνω (to stretch)\nStrong's: to be a hand-reacher or voter (by raising the hand), i.e. (generally) to select or appoint\nKJV: —choose, ordain.\n\nSee: G5495 \n\nCognate Group: G5499 (made by hands), G886 (made without hands), G5495 (hand), G5498 (handwriting), G5497 (some to lead by the hand), G5496 (lead by the hand), G5500 (choose)\nVariants: χειροτονέω",
    5501: "χείρων (cheírōn | khi'-rone)\n\nDerivation: irregular comparative of G2556\nStrong's: from an obsolete equivalent χέρης (of uncertain derivation); more evil or aggravated (physically, mentally or morally)\nKJV: —sorer, worse.\n\nSee: G2556 \n\nCognate Group: G5499 (made by hands), G886 (made without hands), G5495 (hand), G5498 (handwriting), G5497 (some to lead by the hand), G5496 (lead by the hand), G5500 (choose)\nVariants: χείρων",
    5502: "χερουβίμ (cheroubím | kher-oo-beem')\n\nDerivation: plural of Hebrew origin (H3742)\nStrong's: \"cherubim\" (i.e. cherubs or kerubim)\nKJV: —cherubims.\nSee: H3742\n\nCognate Group: G5502 (cherubims)\nVariants: χερουβίμ, Χερούβ, Χεροῦβ, Χερουβίμ \nHebrew Equivalents: H3742 כְּרוּב",
    5503: "χήρα (chḗra | khay'-rah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a presumed derivative apparently from the base of G5490 through the idea of deficiency\nStrong's: a widow (as lacking a husband), literally or figuratively\nKJV: —widow.\n\nSee: G5490 \n\nCognate Group: G5503 (widow)\nVariants: χήρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H490 אַלְמָנָה, H1565 גַּלְמוּד",
    5504: "χθές (chthés | khthes)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: \"yesterday\"; by extension, in time past or hitherto\nKJV: —yesterday.\n\nCognate Group: G5503 (widow)\nVariants: χθές, ἐχθές \nHebrew Equivalents: H8543 תְּמֹול",
    5505: "χιλιάς (chiliás | khil-ee-as')\n\nDerivation: from G5507\nStrong's: one thousand (\"chiliad\")\nKJV: —thousand.\n\nSee: G5507 \n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χιλιάς \nHebrew Equivalents: H505 אֶלֶף",
    5506: "χιλίαρχος (chilíarchos | khil-ee'-ar-khos)\n\nDerivation: from G5507 and G757\nStrong's: the commander of a thousand soldiers (\"chiliarch\"; i.e. colonel\nKJV: —(chief, high) captain.\n\nSee: G5507 \nSee: G757 \n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χιλίαρχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H441 אַלּוּף, H8269 שַׂר",
    5507: "χίλιοι (chílioi | khil'-ee-oy)\n\nDerivation: plural of uncertain affinity\nStrong's: a thousand\nKJV: —thousand.\n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χίλιοι",
    5508: "Χίος (Chíos | khee'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Chios, an island in the Mediterranean\nKJV: —Chios.\n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: Χίος, χίος",
    5509: "χιτών (chitṓn | khee-tone')\n\nDerivation: of foreign origin (H3801)\nStrong's: a tunic or shirt\nKJV: —clothes, coat, garment.\nSee: H3801\n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χιτών \nHebrew Equivalents: H899 בֶּגֶד, H3801 כְּתֹנֶת, H3801 כְּתֹנֶת, H4055 מַד, H4598 מְעִיל",
    5510: "χιών (chiṓn | khee-one')\n\nDerivation: perhaps akin to the base of G5490 (G5465) or G5494 (as descending or empty)\nStrong's: snow\nKJV: —snow.\n\nSee: G5490 \nSee: G5465 \nSee: G5494 \n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χιών \nHebrew Equivalents: H7950 שֶׁלֶג, H8517 תְּלַג",
    5511: "χλαμύς (chlamýs | khlam-ooce')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a military cloak\nKJV: —robe.\n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χλαμύς",
    5512: "χλευάζω (chleuázō | khlyoo-ad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative probably of G5491\nStrong's: to throw out the lip, i.e. jeer at\nKJV: —mock.\n\nSee: G5491 \n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χλευάζω, διαχλευάζω",
    5513: "χλιαρός (chliarós | khlee-ar-os')\n\nDerivation: from χλίω (to warm)\nStrong's: tepid\nKJV: —lukewarm.\n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χλιαρός",
    5514: "Χλόη (Chlóē | khlo'-ay)\n\nDerivation: feminine of apparently a primary word\nStrong's: \"green\"; Chloë, a Christian female\nKJV: —Chloe.\n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: Χλόη, χλόη",
    5515: "χλωρός (chlōrós | khlo-ros')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5514\nStrong's: greenish, i.e. verdant, dun-colored\nKJV: —green, pale.\n\nSee: G5514 \n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χλωρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2682 חָצִיר, H3418 יֶרֶק, H3836 לָבָן, H3892 לַח, H6168 עָרָה, H6212 עֶשֶׂב, H7879 שִׂיחַ",
    5516: "χξϛ (chx stigma | khee xee stig'-ma)\n\nDerivation: the 22nd, 14th and an obsolete letter (G4742 as a cross) of the Greek alphabet (intermediate between the 5th and 6th), used as numbers; denoting respectively 600, 60 and 6\nStrong's: 666 as a numeral\nKJV: —six hundred threescore and six.\n\nSee: G4742 \n\nCognate Group: G5507 (thousand), G5506 (captain), G5505 (thousand)\nVariants: χξϛ, χιϚ’, χξϚ’ G5940, G5947, G5953",
    5517: "χοϊκός (choïkós | kho-ik-os')\n\nDerivation: from G5522\nStrong's: dusty or dirty (soil-like), i.e. (by implication) terrene\nKJV: —earthy.\n\nSee: G5522 \n\nCognate Group: G5517 (earthy)\nVariants: χοϊκός",
    5518: "χοῖνιξ (choînix | khoy'-nix)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a chœnix or certain dry measure\nKJV: —measure.\n\nCognate Group: G5517 (earthy)\nVariants: χοῖνιξ \nHebrew Equivalents: H1324 בַּת",
    5519: "χοῖρος (choîros | khoy'-ros)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a hog\nKJV: —swine.\n\nCognate Group: G5517 (earthy)\nVariants: χοῖρος",
    5520: "χολάω (choláō | khol-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5521\nStrong's: to be bilious, i.e. (by implication) irritable (enraged, \"choleric\")\nKJV: —be angry.\n\nSee: G5521 \n\nCognate Group: G5517 (earthy)\nVariants: χολάω",
    5521: "χολή (cholḗ | khol-ay')\n\nDerivation: feminine of an equivalent perhaps akin to the same as 5514 (from the greenish hue)\nStrong's: \"gall\" or bile, i.e. (by analogy) poison or an anodyne (wormwood, poppy, etc.)\nKJV: —gall.\n\nCognate Group: G5517 (earthy)\nVariants: χολή \nHebrew Equivalents: H3939 לַעֲנָה, H4845 מְרֵרָה, H7218 רֹאשׁ",
    5522: "χόος (chóos | kho'-os)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5494\nStrong's: a heap (as poured out), i.e. rubbish; loose dirt\nKJV: —dust.\n\nSee: G5494 \n\nCognate Group: G5517 (earthy)\nVariants: χόος, χοῦς",
    5523: "Χοραζίν (Chorazín | khor-ad-zin')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: Chorazin, a place in Palestine\nKJV: —Chorazin.\n\nCognate Group: G5517 (earthy)\nVariants: Χοραζίν, Χωραζίν, χοραζίν",
    5524: "χορηγέω (chorēgéō | khor-ayg-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5525 and G71\nStrong's: to be a dance-leader, i.e. (generally) to furnish\nKJV: —give, minister.\n\nSee: G5525 \nSee: G71 \n\nCognate Group: G5525 (dancing), G5524 (give)\nVariants: χορηγέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2110 זוּן, H3557 כּוּל",
    5525: "χορός (chorós | khor-os')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a ring, i.e. round dance (\"choir\"\nKJV: —dancing.\n\nCognate Group: G5525 (dancing), G5524 (give)\nVariants: χορός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1974 הִלּוּל, H2256 חֶבֶל, H4234 מָחֹול, H4246 מְחֹלָה",
    5526: "χορτάζω (chortázō | khor-tad'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5528\nStrong's: to fodder, i.e. (generally) to gorge (supply food in abundance)\nKJV: —feed, fill, satisfy.\n\nSee: G5528 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χορτάζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H7646 שָׂבַע",
    5527: "χόρτασμα (chórtasma | khor'-tas-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5526\nStrong's: forage, i.e. food\nKJV: —sustenance.\n\nSee: G5526 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χόρτασμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4554 מִסְפֹּוא, H6212 עֶשֶׂב",
    5528: "χόρτος (chórtos | khor'-tos)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: a \"court\" or \"garden\", i.e. (by implication, of pasture) herbage or vegetation\nKJV: —blade, grass, hay.\n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χόρτος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1877 דֶּשֶׁא, H2682 חָצִיר, H6212 עֶשֶׂב, H7179 קַשׁ",
    5529: "Χουζᾶς (Chouzâs | khood-zas')\n\nDerivation: of uncertain origin\nStrong's: Chuzas, an officer of Herod\nKJV: —Chuza.\n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: Χουζᾶς, χουζᾶς \nHebrew Equivalents: H4596 מְעִי, H4671 מֹץ, H6083 עָפָר",
    5530: "χράομαι (chráomai | khrah'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of a primary verb (perhaps rather from G5495, to handle)\nStrong's: to furnish what is needed; (give an oracle, \"graze\" (touch slightly), light upon, etc.), i.e. (by implication) to employ or (by extension) to act towards one in a given manner\nKJV: —entreat, use. Compare G5531 ; G5534 .\n\nSee: G5531 \nSee: G5495 \nSee: G5534 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χράομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H113 אָדֹון, H1167 בַּעַל, H3190 יָטַב, H6213 עָשָׂה, H6743 צָלַח, H7592 שָׁאַל",
    5531: "χράω (chráō | khrah'-o)\n\nDerivation: probably the same as the base of G5530\nStrong's: to loan\nKJV: —lend.\n\nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χράω, κίχρημι \nHebrew Equivalents: H3867 לָוָה",
    5532: "χρεία (chreía | khri'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5530 or G5534\nStrong's: employment, i.e. an affair; also (by implication) occasion, demand, requirement or destitution\nKJV: —business, lack, necessary(-ity), need(-ful), use, want.\n\nSee: G5534 \nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χρεία \nHebrew Equivalents: H2818 חֲשַׁח, H2819 חַשְׁחוּת, H6878 צֹרֶךְ",
    5533: "χρεωφειλέτης (chreōpheilétēs | khreh-o-fi-let'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of G5531 and G3781\nStrong's: a loan-ower, i.e. indebted person\nKJV: —debtor.\n\nSee: G5531 \nSee: G3781 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χρεωφειλέτης, χρεοφειλέτης, χρεοφειλέτης/χρεωφειλέτης",
    5534: "χρή (chrḗ | khray)\n\nDerivation: third person singular of the same as G5530 or G5531 used impersonally\nStrong's: it needs (must or should) be\nKJV: —ought.\n\nSee: G5530 \nSee: G5531 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χρή",
    5535: "χρῄζω (chrḗizō | khrade'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5532\nStrong's: to make (i.e. have) necessity, i.e. be in want of\nKJV: —(have) need.\n\nSee: G5532 \n\nCognate Group: G5528 (blade), G5526 (feed), G5527 (sustenance)\nVariants: χρῄζω",
    5536: "χρῆμα (chrēma | khray'-mah)\n\n\nStrong's: something useful or needed, i.e. wealth, price\nKJV: —money, riches.\n\nCognate Group: G5537 (be called), G5536 (money), G5538 (answer of God)\nVariants: χρῆμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H1697 דָּבָר, H3701 כֶּסֶף, H4242 מְחִיר, H5223 נָכֶה, H7399 רְכוּשׁ",
    5537: "χρηματίζω (chrēmatízō | khray-mat-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5536\nStrong's: to utter an oracle (compare the original sense of G5530), i.e. divinely intimate; by implication, (compare the secular sense of G5532) to constitute a firm for business, i.e. (generally) bear as a title\nKJV: —be called, be admonished (warned) of God, reveal, speak.\n\nSee: G5536 \nSee: G5530 \nSee: G5532 \n\nCognate Group: G5537 (be called), G5536 (money), G5538 (answer of God)\nVariants: χρηματίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1696 דָבַר, H7580 שָׁאַג",
    5538: "χρηματισμός (chrēmatismós | khray-mat-is-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G5537\nStrong's: a divine response or revelation\nKJV: —answer of God.\n\nSee: G5537 \n\nCognate Group: G5537 (be called), G5536 (money), G5538 (answer of God)\nVariants: χρηματισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H4853 מַשָּׂא",
    5539: "χρήσιμος (chrḗsimos | khray'-see-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G5540\nStrong's: serviceable\nKJV: —profit.\n\nSee: G5540 \n\nCognate Group: G5539 (profit), G5540 (use)\nVariants: χρήσιμος, χρήσιμον \nHebrew Equivalents: H1215 בֶּצַע, H6743 צָלַח",
    5540: "χρῆσις (chrēsis | khray'-sis)\n\nDerivation: from G5530\nStrong's: employment, i.e. (specially), sexual intercourse (as an occupation of the body)\nKJV: —use.\n\nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5539 (profit), G5540 (use)\nVariants: χρῆσις \nHebrew Equivalents: H7592 שָׁאַל",
    5541: "χρηστεύομαι (chrēsteúomai | khraste-yoo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from G5543\nStrong's: to show oneself useful, i.e. act benevolently\nKJV: —be kind.\n\nSee: G5543 \n\nCognate Group: G5542 (good words), G5541 (be kind), G5544 (gentleness), G5543 (better)\nVariants: χρηστεύομαι",
    5542: "χρηστολογία (chrēstología | khrase-tol-og-ee'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a compound of G5543 and G3004\nStrong's: fair speech, i.e. plausibility\nKJV: —good words.\n\nSee: G5543 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G5542 (good words), G5541 (be kind), G5544 (gentleness), G5543 (better)\nVariants: χρηστολογία",
    5543: "χρηστός (chrēstós | khrase-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G5530\nStrong's: employed, i.e. (by implication) useful (in manner or morals)\nKJV: —better, easy, good(-ness), gracious, kind.\n\nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5542 (good words), G5541 (be kind), G5544 (gentleness), G5543 (better)\nVariants: χρηστός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2896 טֹוב, H3368 יָקָר, H3477 יָשָׁר",
    5544: "χρηστότης (chrēstótēs | khray-stot'-ace)\n\nDerivation: from G5543\nStrong's: usefulness, i.e. morally, excellence (in character or demeanor)\nKJV: —gentleness, good(-ness), kindness.\n\nSee: G5543 \n\nCognate Group: G5542 (good words), G5541 (be kind), G5544 (gentleness), G5543 (better)\nVariants: χρηστότης \nHebrew Equivalents: H2896 טֹוב, H2898 טוּב",
    5545: "χρῖσμα (chrîsma | khris'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5548\nStrong's: an unguent or smearing, i.e. (figuratively) the special endowment (\"chrism\") of the Holy Spirit\nKJV: —anointing, unction.\n\nSee: G5548 \n\nCognate Group: G5545 (anointing), G5580 (false Christ), G5546 (Christian), G500 (antichrist), G5547 (Christ), G5548 (anoint)\nVariants: χρῖσμα, χρίσμα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4888 מִשְׁחָה",
    5546: "Χριστιανός (Christianós | khris-tee-an-os')\n\nDerivation: from G5547\nStrong's: a Christian, i.e. follower of Christ\nKJV: —Christian.\n\nSee: G5547 \n\nCognate Group: G5545 (anointing), G5580 (false Christ), G5546 (Christian), G500 (antichrist), G5547 (Christ), G5548 (anoint)\nVariants: Χριστιανός, χριστιανός",
    5547: "Χριστός (Christós | khris-tos')\n\nDerivation: from G5548\nStrong's: anointed, i.e. the Messiah, an epithet of Jesus\nKJV: —Christ.\n\nSee: G5548 \n\nCognate Group: G5545 (anointing), G5580 (false Christ), G5546 (Christian), G500 (antichrist), G5547 (Christ), G5548 (anoint)\nVariants: Χριστός, χριστός \nHebrew Equivalents: H1325 בַּת",
    5548: "χρίω (chríō | khree'-o)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to G5530 through the idea of contact\nStrong's: to smear or rub with oil, i.e. (by implication) to consecrate to an office or religious service\nKJV: —anoint.\n\nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5545 (anointing), G5580 (false Christ), G5546 (Christian), G500 (antichrist), G5547 (Christ), G5548 (anoint)\nVariants: χρίω \nHebrew Equivalents: H3251 יָסַךְ, H4886 מָשַׁח, H5480 סוּךְ",
    5549: "χρονίζω (chronízō | khron-id'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5550\nStrong's: to take time, i.e. linger\nKJV: —delay, tarry.\n\nSee: G5550 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρονίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H309 אָחַר, H954 בּוּשׁ, H3186 יָחַר, H3462 יָשֵׁן, H4900 מָשַׁךְ",
    5550: "χρόνος (chrónos | khron'-os)\n\nDerivation: of uncertain derivation\nStrong's: a space of time (in general, and thus properly distinguished from G2540, which designates a fixed or special occasion; and from G165, which denotes a particular period) or interval; by extension, an individual opportunity; by implication, delay\nKJV: —+ years old, season, space, (X often-)time(-s), (a) while.\n\nSee: G2540 \nSee: G165 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρόνος \nHebrew Equivalents: H319 אַחֲרִית, H2161 זָמַם, H3117 יֹום, H3453 יָשִׁישׁ, H4801 מֶרְחָק, H5704 עַד, H5704 עַד, H6256 עֵת, H6471 פַּעַם, H7093 קֵץ, H7281 רֶגַע, H8447 תֹּור",
    5551: "χρονοτριβέω (chronotribéō | khron-ot-rib-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from a presumed compound of G5550 and the base of G5147\nStrong's: to be a time-wearer, i.e. to procrastinate (linger)\nKJV: —spend time.\n\nSee: G5550 \nSee: G5147 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρονοτριβέω",
    5552: "χρύσεος (chrýseos | khroo'-seh-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5557\nStrong's: made of gold\nKJV: —of gold, golden.\n\nSee: G5557 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρύσεος, χρυσοῦς \nHebrew Equivalents: H2091 זָהָב, H6337 פָּז",
    5553: "χρυσίον (chrysíon | khroo-see'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G5557\nStrong's: a golden article, i.e. gold plating, ornament, or coin\nKJV: —gold.\n\nSee: G5557 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρυσίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H2091 זָהָב, H2742 חֲרוּץ, H3800 כֶּתֶם, H6337 פָּז",
    5554: "χρυσοδακτύλιος (chrysodaktýlios | khroo-sod-ak-too'-lee-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5557 and G1146\nStrong's: gold-ringed, i.e. wearing a golden finger-ring or similar jewelry\nKJV: —with a gold ring.\n\nSee: G5557 \nSee: G1146 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρυσοδακτύλιος",
    5555: "χρυσόλιθος (chrysólithos | khroo-sol'-ee-thos)\n\nDerivation: from G5557 and G3037\nStrong's: gold-stone, i.e. a yellow gem (\"chrysolite\")\nKJV: —chrysolite.\n\nSee: G5557 \nSee: G3037 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρυσόλιθος \nHebrew Equivalents: H8658 תַּרְשִׁישׁ",
    5556: "χρυσόπρασος (chrysóprasos | khroo-sop'-ras-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5557 and πράσον (a leek)\nStrong's: a greenish-yellow gem (\"chrysoprase\")\nKJV: —chrysoprase.\n\nSee: G5557 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρυσόπρασος",
    5557: "χρυσός (chrysós | khroo-sos')\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the base of G5530 (through the idea of the utility of the metal)\nStrong's: gold; by extension, a golden article, as an ornament or coin\nKJV: —gold.\n\nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρυσός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2091 זָהָב, H2742 חֲרוּץ",
    5558: "χρυσόω (chrysóō | khroo-so'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5557\nStrong's: to gild, i.e. bespangle with golden ornaments\nKJV: —deck.\n\nSee: G5557 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρυσόω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2645 חָפָה, H6823 צָפָה",
    5559: "χρώς (chrṓs | khroce)\n\nDerivation: probably akin to the base of G5530 through the idea of handling\nStrong's: the body (properly, its surface or skin)\nKJV: —body.\n\nSee: G5530 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χρώς \nHebrew Equivalents: H216 אֹור, H1320 בָּשָׂר",
    5560: "χωλός (chōlós | kho-los')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: \"halt\", i.e. limping\nKJV: —cripple, halt, lame.\n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χωλός \nHebrew Equivalents: H6455 פִּסֵּחַ",
    5561: "χώρα (chṓra | kho'-rah)\n\nDerivation: feminine of a derivative of the base of G5490 through the idea of empty expanse\nStrong's: room, i.e. a space of territory (more or less extensive; often including its inhabitants)\nKJV: —coast, county, fields, ground, land, region. Compare G5117 .\n\nSee: G5117 \nSee: G5490 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χώρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H127 אֲדָמָה, H217 אוּר, H386 אֵיתָן, H776 אֶרֶץ, H4082 מְדִינָה, H4725 מָקֹום, H7704 שָׂדֶה, H8478 תַּחַת",
    5562: "χωρέω (chōréō | kho-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5561\nStrong's: to be in (give) space, i.e. (intransitively) to pass, enter, or (transitively) to hold, admit (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —come, contain, go, have place, (can, be room to) receive.\n\nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χωρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1004 בַּיִת, H2388 חָזַק, H3201 יָכֹל, H3557 כּוּל, H5375 נָשָׂא",
    5563: "χωρίζω (chōrízō | kho-rid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5561\nStrong's: to place room between, i.e. part; reflexively, to go away\nKJV: —depart, put asunder, separate.\n\nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χωρίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H910 בָּדָד, H914 בָּדַל, H4185 מוּשׁ, H6504 פָּרַד",
    5564: "χωρίον (chōríon | kho-ree'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G5561\nStrong's: a spot or plot of ground\nKJV: —field, land, parcel of ground, place, possession.\n\nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χωρίον \nHebrew Equivalents: H3754 כֶּרֶם",
    5565: "χωρίς (chōrís | kho-rece')\n\nDerivation: adverb from G5561\nStrong's: at a space, i.e. separately or apart from (often as preposition)\nKJV: —beside, by itself, without.\n\nSee: G5561 \n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χωρίς",
    5566: "χῶρος (chōros | kho'-ros)\n\nDerivation: of Latin origin\nStrong's: the north-west wind\nKJV: —north west.\n\nCognate Group: G5550 (+ years old), G5549 (delay), G5551 (spend time)\nVariants: χῶρος",
    5567: "ψάλλω (psállō | psal'-lo)\n\nDerivation: probably strengthened from ψάω (to rub or touch the surface\nStrong's: compare G5597); to twitch or twang, i.e. to play on a stringed instrument (celebrate the divine worship with music and accompanying odes)\nKJV: —make melody, sing (psalms).\n\nSee: G5597 \n\nCognate Group: G5567 (make melody), G5568 (psalm)\nVariants: ψάλλω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2167 זָמַר, H5059 נָגַן",
    5568: "ψαλμός (psalmós | psal-mos')\n\nDerivation: from G5567\nStrong's: a set piece of music, i.e. a sacred ode (accompanied with the voice, harp or other instrument; a \"psalm\"); collectively, the book of the Psalms\nKJV: —psalm. Compare G5603 .\n\nSee: G5567 \nSee: G5603 \n\nCognate Group: G5567 (make melody), G5568 (psalm)\nVariants: ψαλμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H2156 זְמֹורָה, H4210 מִזְמֹור, H5035 נֶבֶל, H5059 נָגַן, H5748 עוּגָב, H7692 שִׁגָּיֹון, H7892 שִׁיר, H8416 תְּהִלָּה",
    5569: "ψευδάδελφος (pseudádelphos | psyoo-dad'-el-fos)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and G80\nStrong's: a spurious brother, i.e. pretended associate\nKJV: —false brethren.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G80 \n\nCognate Group: G5361 (love as brethren), G80 (brother), G81 (brethren), G5360 (brotherly love), G79 (sister), G5569 (false brethren)\nVariants: ψευδάδελφος",
    5570: "ψευδαπόστολος (pseudapóstolos | psyoo-dap-os'-tol-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and G652\nStrong's: a spurious apostle, i.e. pretended pracher\nKJV: —false teacher.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G652 \n\nCognate Group: G651 (apostleship), G649 (put in), G5570 (false teacher), G1821 (send), G652 (apostle), G3992 (send)\nVariants: ψευδαπόστολος",
    5571: "ψευδής (pseudḗs | psyoo-dace')\n\nDerivation: from G5574\nStrong's: untrue, i.e. erroneous, deceitful, wicked\nKJV: —false, liar.\n\nSee: G5574 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G5580 (false Christ), G5578 (false prophet), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G5583 (liar), G5577 (false witness), G893 (that cannot lie), G5581 (falsely so called), G5574 (falsely), G5582 (lie), G5579 (lie), G5571 (false), G5573 (speaking lies)\nVariants: ψευδής \nHebrew Equivalents: H391 אַכְזָב, H3576 כָּזַב, H3576 כָּזַב, H3577 כָּזָב, H3585 כַּחַשׁ, H7723 שָׁוְא, H8267 שֶׁקֶר",
    5572: "ψευδοδιδάσκαλος (pseudodidáskalos | psyoo-dod-id-as'-kal-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and G1320\nStrong's: a spurious teacher, i.e. propagator of erroneous Christian doctrine\nKJV: —false teacher.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G1320 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G2085 (teach other doctrine), G1322 (doctrine), G3547 (doctor of the law), G1317 (apt to teach), G1320 (doctor), G2567 (teacher of good things), G1321 (teach), G1319 (doctrine), G1318 (taught)\nVariants: ψευδοδιδάσκαλος",
    5573: "ψευδολόγος (pseudológos | psyoo-dol-og'-os)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and G3004\nStrong's: mendacious, i.e. promulgating erroneous Christian doctrine\nKJV: —speaking lies.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G3004 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G5580 (false Christ), G5578 (false prophet), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G5583 (liar), G5577 (false witness), G893 (that cannot lie), G5581 (falsely so called), G5574 (falsely), G5582 (lie), G5579 (lie), G5571 (false), G5573 (speaking lies)\nVariants: ψευδολόγος",
    5574: "ψεύδομαι (pseúdomai | psyoo'-dom-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of an apparently primary verb\nStrong's: to utter an untruth or attempt to deceive by falsehood\nKJV: —falsely, lie.\n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G5580 (false Christ), G5578 (false prophet), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G5583 (liar), G5577 (false witness), G893 (that cannot lie), G5581 (falsely so called), G5574 (falsely), G5582 (lie), G5579 (lie), G5571 (false), G5573 (speaking lies)\nVariants: ψεύδομαι, ψεύδω",
    5575: "ψευδομάρτυρ (pseudomártyr | psyoo-dom-ar'-toor)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and a kindred form of G3144\nStrong's: a spurious witness, i.e. bearer of untrue testimony\nKJV: —false witness.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G3144 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: ψευδομάρτυρ, ψευδόμαρτυς",
    5576: "ψευδομαρτυρέω (pseudomartyréō | psyoo-dom-ar-too-reh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from G5575\nStrong's: to be an untrue testifier, i.e. offer falsehood in evidence\nKJV: —be a false witness.\n\nSee: G5575 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: ψευδομαρτυρέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H6030 עָנָה",
    5577: "ψευδομαρτυρία (pseudomartyría | psyoo-dom-ar-too-ree'-ah)\n\nDerivation: from G5575\nStrong's: untrue testimony\nKJV: —false witness.\n\nSee: G5575 \n\nCognate Group: G3143 (take to record), G3142 (to be testified), G3144 (martyr), G1957 (testify), G4303 (testify beforehand), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G3141 (record), G5577 (false witness), G4828 (testify unto), G3140 (charge), G1263 (charge), G2649 (witness against), G4901 (also bear witness)\nVariants: ψευδομαρτυρία",
    5578: "ψευδοπροφήτης (pseudoprophḗtēs | psyoo-dop-rof-ay'-tace)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and G4396\nStrong's: a spurious prophet, i.e. pretended foreteller or religious impostor\nKJV: —false prophet.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G4396 \n\nCognate Group: G4394 (prophecy), G4395 (prophesy), G4397 (of prophecy), G4396 (prophet), G5578 (false prophet), G4398 (prophetess)\nVariants: ψευδοπροφήτης \nHebrew Equivalents: H5030 נָבִיא",
    5579: "ψεῦδος (pseûdos | psyoo'-dos)\n\nDerivation: from G5574\nStrong's: a falsehood\nKJV: —lie, lying.\n\nSee: G5574 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G5580 (false Christ), G5578 (false prophet), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G5583 (liar), G5577 (false witness), G893 (that cannot lie), G5581 (falsely so called), G5574 (falsely), G5582 (lie), G5579 (lie), G5571 (false), G5573 (speaking lies)\nVariants: ψεῦδος \nHebrew Equivalents: H391 אַכְזָב, H3577 כָּזָב, H3585 כַּחַשׁ, H4820 מִרְמָה, H8267 שֶׁקֶר",
    5580: "ψευδόχριστος (pseudóchristos | psyoo-dokh'-ris-tos)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and G5547\nStrong's: a spurious Messiah\nKJV: —false Christ.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G5547 \n\nCognate Group: G5545 (anointing), G5580 (false Christ), G5546 (Christian), G500 (antichrist), G5547 (Christ), G5548 (anoint)\nVariants: ψευδόχριστος",
    5581: "ψευδώνυμος (pseudṓnymos | psyoo-do'-noo-mos)\n\nDerivation: from G5571 and G3686\nStrong's: untruly named\nKJV: —falsely so called.\n\nSee: G5571 \nSee: G3686 \n\nCognate Group: G5581 (falsely so called), G3686 (called), G3687 (call), G2028 (call)\nVariants: ψευδώνυμος",
    5582: "ψεῦσμα (pseûsma | psyoos'-mah)\n\nDerivation: from G5574\nStrong's: a fabrication, i.e. falsehood\nKJV: —lie.\n\nSee: G5574 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G5580 (false Christ), G5578 (false prophet), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G5583 (liar), G5577 (false witness), G893 (that cannot lie), G5581 (falsely so called), G5574 (falsely), G5582 (lie), G5579 (lie), G5571 (false), G5573 (speaking lies)\nVariants: ψεῦσμα",
    5583: "ψεύστης (pseústēs | psyoos-tace')\n\nDerivation: from G5574\nStrong's: a falsifier\nKJV: —liar.\n\nSee: G5574 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G5580 (false Christ), G5578 (false prophet), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G5583 (liar), G5577 (false witness), G893 (that cannot lie), G5581 (falsely so called), G5574 (falsely), G5582 (lie), G5579 (lie), G5571 (false), G5573 (speaking lies)\nVariants: ψεύστης \nHebrew Equivalents: H3577 כָּזָב",
    5584: "ψηλαφάω (psēlapháō | psay-laf-ah'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5567 (compare G5586)\nStrong's: to manipulate, i.e. verify by contact; figuratively, to search for\nKJV: —feel after, handle, touch.\n\nSee: G5567 \nSee: G5586 \n\nCognate Group: G5572 (false teacher), G5580 (false Christ), G5578 (false prophet), G5576 (be a false witness), G5575 (false witness), G5583 (liar), G5577 (false witness), G893 (that cannot lie), G5581 (falsely so called), G5574 (falsely), G5582 (lie), G5579 (lie), G5571 (false), G5573 (speaking lies)\nVariants: ψηλαφάω \nHebrew Equivalents: H1659 גָּשַׁשׁ, H3237 יָמַשׁ, H4185 מוּשׁ, H4959 מָשַׁשׁ",
    5585: "ψηφίζω (psēphízō | psay-fid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from G5586\nStrong's: to use pebbles in enumeration, i.e. (generally) to compute\nKJV: —count.\n\nSee: G5586 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: ψηφίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H5608 סָפַר",
    5586: "ψῆφος (psēphos | psay'-fos)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5584\nStrong's: a pebble (as worn smooth by handling), i.e. (by implication, of use as a counter or ballot) a verdict (of acquittal) or ticket (of admission); a vote\nKJV: —stone, voice.\n\nSee: G5584 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: ψῆφος \nHebrew Equivalents: H2687 חָצָץ, H2808 חֶשְׁבֹּון, H6864 צֹר",
    5587: "ψιθυρισμός (psithyrismós | psith-oo-ris-mos')\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of ψίθος (a whisper\nStrong's: by implication, a slander; probably akin to G5574); whispering, i.e. secret detraction\nKJV: —whispering.\n\nSee: G5574 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: ψιθυρισμός \nHebrew Equivalents: H3908 לַחַשׁ",
    5588: "ψιθυριστής (psithyristḗs | psith-oo-ris-tace')\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5587\nStrong's: a secret calumniator\nKJV: —whisperer.\n\nSee: G5587 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: ψιθυριστής",
    5589: "ψιχίον (psichíon | psikh-ee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive from a derivative of the base of G5567 (meaning a crumb)\nStrong's: a little bit or morsel\nKJV: —crumb.\n\nSee: G5567 \n\nCognate Group: G4785 (number with), G4860 (reckon), G5586 (stone), G5585 (count)\nVariants: ψιχίον, ψίξ",
    5590: "ψυχή (psychḗ | psoo-khay')\n\nDerivation: from G5594\nStrong's: breath, i.e. (by implication) spirit, abstractly or concretely (the animal sentient principle only; thus distinguished on the one hand from G4151, which is the rational and immortal soul; and on the other from G2222, which is mere vitality, even of plants: these terms thus exactly correspond respectively to the Hebrew H5315, H7307 and H2416)\nKJV: —heart (+ -ily), life, mind, soul, + us, + you.\n\nSee: G5594 \nSee: G4151 \nSee: G2222 \nSee: H5315\nSee: H7307\nSee: H2416\n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψυχή \nHebrew Equivalents: H376 אִישׁ, H2416 חַי, H3820 לֵב, H3824 לֵבָב, H5315 נֶפֶשׁ, H7307 רוּחַ",
    5591: "ψυχικός (psychikós | psoo-khee-kos')\n\nDerivation: from G5590\nStrong's: sensitive, i.e. animate (in distinction on the one hand from G4152, which is the higher or renovated nature; and on the other from G5446, which is the lower or bestial nature)\nKJV: —natural, sensual.\n\nSee: G5590 \nSee: G4152 \nSee: G5446 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψυχικός",
    5592: "ψύχος (psýchos | psoo'-khos)\n\nDerivation: from G5594\nStrong's: coolness\nKJV: —cold.\n\nSee: G5594 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψύχος, ψῦχος \nHebrew Equivalents: H7120 קֹר, H7136 קָרָה",
    5593: "ψυχρός (psychrós | psoo-chros')\n\nDerivation: from G5592\nStrong's: chilly (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —cold.\n\nSee: G5592 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψυχρός \nHebrew Equivalents: H7119 קַר",
    5594: "ψύχω (psýchō | psoo'-kho)\n\nDerivation: a primary verb\nStrong's: to breathe (voluntarily but gently, thus differing on the one hand from G4154, which denotes properly a forcible respiration; and on the other from the base of G109, which refers properly to an inanimate breeze), i.e. (by implication, of reduction of temperature by evaporation) to chill (figuratively)\nKJV: —wax cold.\n\nSee: G4154 \nSee: G109 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψύχω, ψύχομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H6979 קוּר, H7849 שָׁטַח",
    5595: "ψωμίζω (psōmízō | pso-mid'-zo)\n\nDerivation: from the base of G5596\nStrong's: to supply with bits, i.e. (generally) to nourish\nKJV: —(bestow to) feed.\n\nSee: G5596 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψωμίζω \nHebrew Equivalents: H398 אָכַל, H939 בּוּזָה, H1262 בָּרָה",
    5596: "ψωμίον (psōmíon | pso-mee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive from a derivative of the base of G5597\nStrong's: a crumb or morsel (as if rubbed off), i.e. a mouthful\nKJV: —sop.\n\nSee: G5597 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψωμίον",
    5597: "ψώχω (psṓchō | pso'-kho)\n\nDerivation: prolongation from the same base as G5567\nStrong's: to triturate, i.e. (by analogy) to rub out (kernels from husks with the fingers or hand)\nKJV: —rub.\n\nSee: G5567 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ψώχω",
    5598: "Ω (Ō | o'-meg-ah)\n\n\nStrong's: the last letter of the Greek alphabet, i.e. (figuratively) the finality\nKJV: —Omega.\n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: Ω, Ὦ1, Ὦ",
    5599: "ὦ (ō | o)\n\nDerivation: a primary interjection\nStrong's: as a sign of the vocative case, O; as a note of exclamation, oh\nKJV: —O.\n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ὦ, ὦ2, ὼ",
    5600: "ὦ (ō | o), including the oblique forms, as well as ἦς (ēs | ace); ἦ (ē | ay)\n\n; etc.\n\nDerivation: the subjunctive of G1510\nStrong's: (may, might, can, could, would, should, must, etc.; also with G1487 and its comparative, as well as with other particles) be\nKJV: —+ appear, are, (may, might, should) be, X have, is, + pass the flower of her age, should stand, were.\n\nSee: G1510 \nSee: G1487 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ὦ, εἰμί",
    5601: "Ὠβήδ (Ōbḗd | o-bade')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H5744)\nStrong's: Obed, an Israelite\nKJV: —Obed.\nSee: H5744\n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: Ὠβήδ, Ἰωβήδ \nHebrew Equivalents: H5744 עֹובֵד",
    5602: "ὧδε (hōde | ho'-deh)\n\nDerivation: from an adverb form of G3592\nStrong's: in this same spot, i.e. here or hither\nKJV: —here, hither, (in) this place, there.\n\nSee: G3592 \n\nCognate Group: G1374 (double minded), G5591 (natural), G5590 (heart), G3642 (feebleminded), G404 (refresh), G403 (revival)\nVariants: ὧδε \nHebrew Equivalents: H1988 הֲלֹם, H2008 הֵנָּה, H2088 זֶה, H3541 כֹּה, H6311 פֹּה",
    5603: "ᾠδή (ōidḗ | o-day')\n\nDerivation: from G103\nStrong's: a chant or \"ode\" (the general term for any words sung; while G5215 denotes especially a religious metrical composition, and G5568 still more specially, a Hebrew cantillation)\nKJV: —song.\n\nSee: G103 \nSee: G5215 \nSee: G5568 \n\nCognate Group: G5603 (song), G103 (sing)\nVariants: ᾠδή, ὤδή \nHebrew Equivalents: H1902 הִגָּיֹון, H4210 מִזְמֹור, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H5058 נְגִינָה, H7692 שִׁגָּיֹון, H7892 שִׁיר",
    5604: "ὠδίν (ōdín | o-deen')\n\nDerivation: akin to G3601\nStrong's: a pang or throe, especially of childbirth\nKJV: —pain, sorrow, travail.\n\nSee: G3601 \n\nCognate Group: G5604 (pain), G5605 (travail in)\nVariants: ὠδίν \nHebrew Equivalents: H2256 חֶבֶל, H2256 חֶבֶל, H2342 חוּל, H2427 חִיל, H3205 יָלַד, H4856 מַשֹּׂא, H6735 צִיר",
    5605: "ὠδίνω (ōdínō | o-dee'-no)\n\nDerivation: from G5604\nStrong's: to experience the pains of parturition (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —travail in (birth).\n\nSee: G5604 \n\nCognate Group: G5604 (pain), G5605 (travail in)\nVariants: ὠδίνω \nHebrew Equivalents: H2029 הָרָה, H2254 חָבַל, H2342 חוּל, H6887 צָרַר",
    5606: "ὦμος (ōmos | o'-mos)\n\nDerivation: perhaps from the alternate of G5342\nStrong's: the shoulder (as that on which burdens are borne)\nKJV: —shoulder.\n\nSee: G5342 \n\nCognate Group: G5604 (pain), G5605 (travail in)\nVariants: ὦμος, ὼμος \nHebrew Equivalents: H3802 כָּתֵף, H6654 צַד, H7926 שְׁכֶם",
    5607: "ὤν (ṓn | oan), including the feminine οὖσα (oûsa | oo'-sah); and the neuter ὄν (ón | on)\n\nDerivation: present participle of G1510\nStrong's: being\nKJV: —be, come, have.\n\nSee: G1510 \n\nCognate Group: G5607 (be), G1510 (am)\nVariants: ὤν, εἰμί",
    5608: "ὠνέομαι (ōnéomai | o-neh'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice from an apparently primary ὦνος (a sum or price)\nStrong's: to purchase (synonymous with the earlier G4092)\nKJV: — buy.\n\nSee: G4092 \n\nCognate Group: G5607 (be), G1510 (am)\nVariants: ὠνέομαι",
    5609: "ὠόν (ōón | o-on')\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: an \"egg\"\nKJV: —egg.\n\nCognate Group: G5607 (be), G1510 (am)\nVariants: ὠόν, ᾠόν, ὤόν \nHebrew Equivalents: H6098 עֵצָה",
    5610: "ὥρα (hṓra | ho'-rah)\n\nDerivation: apparently a primary word\nStrong's: an \"hour\" (literally or figuratively)\nKJV: —day, hour, instant, season, X short, (even-)tide, (high) time.\n\nCognate Group: G5610 (day)\nVariants: ὥρα \nHebrew Equivalents: H4022 מֶגֶד, H4150 מֹועֵד, H4999 נָאָה, H5732 עִדָּן, H6256 עֵת, H8160 שָׁעָה",
    5611: "ὡραῖος (hōraîos | ho-rah'-yos)\n\nDerivation: from G5610\nStrong's: belonging to the right hour or season (timely), i.e. (by implication) flourishing (beauteous (figuratively))\nKJV: —beautiful.\n\nSee: G5610 \n\nCognate Group: G5610 (day)\nVariants: ὡραῖος \nHebrew Equivalents: H1926 הָדָר, H2530 חָמַד, H2896 טֹוב, H3302 יָפָה, H3303 יָפֶה, H4999 נָאָה, H5273 נָעִים, H8209 שַׁפִּיר",
    5612: "ὠρύομαι (ōrýomai | o-roo'-om-ahee)\n\nDerivation: middle voice of an apparently primary verb\nStrong's: to \"roar\"\nKJV: —roar.\n\nCognate Group: G5610 (day)\nVariants: ὠρύομαι \nHebrew Equivalents: H7580 שָׁאַג",
    5613: "ὡς (hōs | hoce)\n\nDerivation: probably adverb of comparative from G3739\nStrong's: which how, i.e. in that manner (very variously used, as follows)\nKJV: —about, after (that), (according) as (it had been, it were), as soon (as), even as (like), for, how (greatly), like (as, unto), since, so (that), that, to wit, unto, when(-soever), while, X with all speed.\n\nSee: G3739 \n\nCognate Group: G5610 (day)\nVariants: ὡς, ὡσάν",
    5614: "ὡσαννά (hōsanná | ho-san-nah')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H3467 and H4994)\nStrong's: oh save!; hosanna (i.e. hoshia-na), an exclamation of adoration\nKJV: —hosanna.\nSee: H3467\nSee: H4994\n\nCognate Group: G5614 (hosanna)\nVariants: ὡσαννά \nHebrew Equivalents: H3467 יָשַׁע, H4994 נָא",
    5615: "ὡσαύτως (hōsaútōs | ho-sow'-toce)\n\nDerivation: from G5613 and an adverb from G846\nStrong's: as thus, i.e. in the same way\nKJV: —even so, likewise, after the same (in like) manner.\n\nSee: G5613 \nSee: G846 \n\nCognate Group: G5614 (hosanna)\nVariants: ὡσαύτως",
    5616: "ὡσεί (hōseí | ho-si')\n\nDerivation: from G5613 and G1487\nStrong's: as if\nKJV: —about, as (it had been, it were), like (as).\n\nSee: G5613 \nSee: G1487 \n\nCognate Group: G5614 (hosanna)\nVariants: ὡσεί",
    5617: "Ὡσηέ (Hōsēé | ho-say-eh')\n\nDerivation: of Hebrew origin (H1954)\nStrong's: Hosee (i.e. Hoshea), an Israelite\nKJV: —Osee.\nSee: H1954\n\nCognate Group: G5614 (hosanna)\nVariants: Ὡσηέ, Ὡσηέ/Ὡσῆε \nHebrew Equivalents: H1954 הֹושֵׁעַ",
    5618: "ὥσπερ (hṓsper | hoce'-per)\n\nDerivation: from G5613 and G4007\nStrong's: just as, i.e. exactly like\nKJV: —(even, like) as.\n\nSee: G5613 \nSee: G4007 \n\nCognate Group: G5614 (hosanna)\nVariants: ὥσπερ",
    5619: "ὡσπερεί (hōspereí | hoce-per-i')\n\nDerivation: from G5618 and G1487\nStrong's: just as if, i.e. as it were\nKJV: —as.\n\nSee: G5618 \nSee: G1487 \n\nCognate Group: G5614 (hosanna)\nVariants: ὡσπερεί",
    5620: "ὥστε (hṓste | hoce'-teh)\n\nDerivation: from G5613 and G5037\nStrong's: so too, i.e. thus therefore (in various relations of consecution, as follow)\nKJV: —(insomuch) as, so that (then), (insomuch) that, therefore, to, wherefore.\n\nSee: G5613 \nSee: G5037 \n\nCognate Group: G5614 (hosanna)\nVariants: ὥστε",
    5621: "ὠτίον (ōtíon | o-tee'-on)\n\nDerivation: diminutive of G3775\nStrong's: an earlet, i.e. one of the ears, or perhaps the lobe of the ear\nKJV: —ear.\n\nSee: G3775 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: ὠτίον, ὠτάριον \nHebrew Equivalents: H241 אֹזֶן",
    5622: "ὠφέλεια (ōphéleia | o-fel'-i-ah)\n\nDerivation: from a derivative of the base of G5624\nStrong's: usefulness, i.e. benefit\nKJV: —advantage, profit.\n\nSee: G5624 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: ὠφέλεια \nHebrew Equivalents: H1215 בֶּצַע, H2974 יָאַל",
    5623: "ὠφελέω (ōpheléō | o-fel-eh'-o)\n\nDerivation: from the same as G5622\nStrong's: to be useful, i.e. to benefit\nKJV: —advantage, better, prevail, profit.\n\nSee: G5622 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: ὠφελέω \nHebrew Equivalents: H247 אָזַר, H2974 יָאַל, H5382 נָשָׁה",
    5624: "ὠφέλιμος (ōphélimos | o-fel'-ee-mos)\n\nDerivation: from a form of G3786\nStrong's: helpful or serviceable, i.e. advantageous\nKJV: —profit(-able).\n\nSee: G3786 \n\nCognate Group: G3775 (ear), G5621 (ear), G1801 (hearken)\nVariants: ὠφέλιμος",
